GIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: It's likely, in a couple of weeks, they will seek criminal indictments to key players, maybe even the former president himself. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, I haven't seen anything that makes me think the president committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 What's come out so far has generally been known before. There are a few things that were new. I think it's legitimate to explore whether or not there was a plan to use violence to stop the vote. And if there was a plan, was the president involved?\par \par \uc2 I haven't seen anything up to now that makes me think he was. And I personally doubt it. But I think that's -- those are legitimate questions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To FOX News' Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with the very latest -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 A taped deposition from former Attorney General Bill Barr was central to the hearing this morning. Barr testified that allegations about voter fraud and voting machines were -- quote -- "idiotic." Still, claims of fraud got through to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARR: I saw absolutely zero basis for the allegations, but they were made in such a sensational way that they obviously were influencing a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 I thought, boy, if he really believes this stuff, he has lost contact with -- he has become detached from reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The committee said the president simply ignored what people close to him said about the election results. That stirred the emotions of those loyal to the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): Directly told the president over and over again they were false. These were his people. This was Trump world telling the president that what he was saying was false.\par \par \uc2 And he continued to say it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The committee played tape from Trump official \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 . He alleged that a drunk \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  caught the ear of the former president election night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP 2020 CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: He was definitely intoxicated.\par \par \uc2 There were suggestions by -- I believe it was Mayor Giuliani to go and declare victory and say that we had won it outright.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Two members of the committee, Jamie Raskin and Adam Schiff, believe there's enough for prosecutors to bring indictments.\par \par \uc2 Two more hearings this week. On Wednesday, the panel examines the push for states to send in alternative electoral votes, Thursday, the pressure campaign on former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 At the U.S. Southern border, the massive migrant caravan disbanding, but organizers say that won't change where they're heading.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Bill Melugin has the very latest -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, thousands of Haitian migrants have started gathering in the Mexican city of Reynosa directly behind us across the river, this as the Biden administration loses in court yet again, a federal judge throwing out one of their key immigration policies.\par \par \uc2 Those stories and much more coming up right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To the U.S. Southern border now.\par \par \uc2 The massive migrant caravan breaking up, but thousands are still heading up to the border.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' own Bill Melugin is live from Hidalgo, Texas, with the very latest -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Charles, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Huge numbers of migrants have already made it to the U.S.-Mexico border. That includes thousands of Haitians who are literally behind us on the river here in the Mexican city of Reynosa. We will show that video to you right now. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 We went into Reynosa yesterday to shoot this video. What you're looking at are thousands of Haitians who are waiting outside of a local migrant shelter there in Reynosa, a single-file line, almost as far as the eye can see in some areas, more than 1,000 Haitians waiting in that line hoping to get into that shelter.\par \par \uc2 There are already several hundred other Haitians living inside of that shelter. And most of these Haitians did not just come here from Haiti just now. They left Haiti years ago. They have been living in South America, predominantly in Chile and Brazil. Now they have decided is a good time to try to come and make it to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The director of that shelter, though, is exhausted, telling us the numbers he's dealing with are overwhelming. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PASTOR HECTOR SILVA, MIGRANT CAMP DIRECTOR (through translator): This is the result of people thinking the borders open. We are not equipped to handle this capacity and tend to thousands and thousands of families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And, Charles, you mentioned that caravan off the top.\par \par \uc2 What you're looking at here is video from one of our contacts in Ciudad Acuna. That is the Mexican city directly across from Del Rio. These are busloads of migrants from that caravan who started arriving in Ciudad Acuna late yesterday afternoon, again, hundreds of them in these busloads.\par \par \uc2 What was interesting, though, is the Mexican authorities stopped them. They did not let them cross into the U.S. They detained them, and they physically took them elsewhere away from the border. We will keep our viewers updated on what happens with that.\par \par \uc2 And last thing we'd like to mention is, as you take a look at this video we shot over the weekend -- we embedded with Texas DPS here in the Rio Grande Valley looking for runners. As you look at several young men arrested, we want to tell you about a court case that the Biden administration lost over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge just threw out one of their key immigration policies, which was DHS announced last year that they were going to severely limit who ICE can and cannot target for deportation. You will remember they said ICE can only go after national security threats, public safety risks and recent border crossers, with Secretary Mayorkas saying that just being in the country illegally is not grounds for removal.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live, a federal judge disagreed with that policy. It is now thrown out. He ruled that ICE was not being able to do its job. He ruled that DHS policy was illegal. He is giving the federal government one week to potentially appeal that order before it takes effect.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bill, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, with gas prices and crime ramping up, meet the Republican hoping to take California Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A red wave in the bluest of blue states?\par \par \uc2 California Republican State Senator Brian Dahle will face off with Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  in November. Many are saying it's a long shot. But with gas prices and crime on the rise, Dahle says folks are ready for change.\par \par \uc2 And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, we did reach out to Governor Newsom. We have yet to hear back from him.\par \par \uc2 Listen, long shots, this is what politics are for, right? I think it was Howard Cosell who says that's why we play the game. Why do you think you're going to win this election?\par \par \uc2 S