gy.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. That's exactly right, energy syntax.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you, nice to see you in person too.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the former \uc2 Obama\uc2  official who says that President Biden's foreign policy, gaffes are dragging down America standing on the world stage. He wrote this very controversial piece for a former \uc2 Obama\uc2  official, we're going to talk to him next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The President welcoming leaders from Latin America today to a three day regional summit in which countries are expected to put a big focus on immigration but the heads of eight countries including Mexico, Honduras, El Salvador, Guatemala, are skipping the event in Los Angeles. Instead, they sent lower level cabinet officials, which has raised questions about the influence that America has in our own backyard.\par \par \uc2 My next guest recently wrote an op-ed in "USA Today" titled Biden's careless comments are hurting his presidency and diplomacy. He needs a reset, wrote, Brett Bruen, who joins me now. Former White House Director of Global Engagement under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . He's now president of the Global Situation Room. Brett, good to have you with us today. You say in this piece that used to cringe when the President now president, vice president at the time went to the podium. So why did you decide that you had to speak out as someone who was a part of the \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden administration?\par \par \uc2 BRETT BRUEN, GLOBAL SITUATION ROOM PRESIDENT: Well, look, I think these are very polarized partisan times. But national security requires us to speak up if we see something that is heading off the rails and especially something as repeated as the problems that this team has had on the foreign policy front, it is necessary to say we have to course 