 Putin from office. You had better have a plan. You better be able to back it up. And we have not seen that, on repeated occasions from this White House.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And as we've seen in the frustration that we've heard is happening from the President that he doesn't like it when the White House then walks it back or changes what he said. He said, you know, he's the president, you know, who do you think needs to go in this national security team?\par \par \uc2 BRUEN: So last summer, with the reckless retreat from Afghanistan, I said, this ultimately falls on the National Security Adviser. But now we're seeing with this Summit of the Americas, just an absolute, unmitigated diplomatic disaster. And that is not just the National Security Adviser, it's on the whole team, whether it's the cabinet level, whether it's those who are responsible for Latin America and other regions.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, you take something like this decision not to invite Cuba, Nicaragua, Venezuela, that ought to have been taken six months ago, we ought to have done the groundwork to prepare Mexico, Guatemala, Honduras, and others for this reality. You don't do it days before. And the kind of run on the networks is these countries aren't showing up?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, the American people were told that we're going to have this much more favorable face on the world stage if President Biden was elected. You know, it appears that you feel like we're in a more unstable position, perhaps.\par \par \uc2 BRUEN: I think we've got a process problem. And quite frankly, this team is not acknowledging the errors. And if they can't acknowledge them, they can't fix them, which is why we are continuing to make the same mistakes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see if we changes. We've heard some rumblings that changes might be coming. We'll see if there in the security team. Brett, thank you very much. Brett Bruen, good to have you with us today, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So the new plan for the Biden ministration to keep tensions with China under control as they expand their military power in a big way. A new secret base in Cambodia, put there by China. General Jack Keane is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a question of setting the guard rails with China. That is how a senior U.S. defense official is characterizing one of the goals set by Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  and his first face to face with his Chinese counterpart. This is a very important meeting in Singapore. It's slated for tomorrow comes as the Chinese and Cambodian officials have broken ground on a contentious naval port expansion project on the Gulf of Thailand.\par \par \uc2 With that we bring in retired four star general Jack Keane, former Vice Chief of Staff of the Army now chairman of the Institute for the Study of War and Fox News's senior strategic analyst. General, always good to see you. So when I hear that there's a secret base being built in Cambodia by the Chinese, the influence is growing and that entire area. What does it mean? How concerned should we be?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, well, China certainly has stated what their objectives are. And President Xi has been quite clear about it these last 10 years. He wants to dominate and control the Indo-Pacific region in this space as part of that strategy. He also wants to replace the United States as the world's global leader. And to be able to do that he has to project power beyond the region into other parts of the world. And I think we'll see many other bases being built as a result of it. He has one in Djibouti. He has one in Tajikistan that has to do with Afghanistan, just across the border.\par \par \uc2 Keep an eye on the radicals, I think what's that about. But we'll see bases in South America. We'll see them in the east coast of Africa, in the Solomon Islands, I suspect. And look, the United States has a global power, it 800 bases and facilities in 70 countries. And we don't look for territorial control, what we want is influence and to protect the United States national interests and that of our allies, by making certain that the global economy is thriving, that it's moving on the sea lanes of communication, and we want to ensure that continues that's in our national interest. It's in our allies national interest.\par \par \uc2 China, I think, as their bases expand, and they will expand because, as I said, they want to be a global power. They're more interested also in the same things we are in terms of OSI lane staying open and maintaining a vibrant economy, but they're also interested in control. And that's very different than what the United States is doing.\par \par \uc2 Now many of our bases have small radar site facilities. But a lot of them are very flush bases in Europe and the Middle East and in the Pacific, which we're all very familiar with and it's taken us, you know, decades to be able to attain that kind of base strategy that we have in support of our overall strategy in terms of what the United States wants to do with maintain peace and stability in the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, I think about World War II, and we saw the expansion of Nazi Germany into northern Africa, you saw the expansion of Japan and the Indo-Pacific and the Solomon Islands, places like that, names that people don't talk about every day. But when you look in history books, those are the places that you're going to find where the Japanese expanded and took over. Are there parallels to the expansion that we're seeing now?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, most definitely. China's presence in South America and Africa economically is rather significant. And, and we've talked about it here in terms of the mineral extraction and mining and extraction, rare minerals, et cetera. They're going to protect their interest levels. And that's why the military bases are going to be there. And this expansion will continue. It's a natural evolution of what has taken place with China, but our interest and their interests are very different.\par \par \uc2 And as a result of it, we talked about guardrails in the introduction there, what we're trying to avoid is while our national interests are different, and we are obviously competing with them, and we're trying to actually contain some of their ambitions, because it steps on national interest, there's potential here for conflict. I mean, right now sitting in the Indo-Pacific region, Martha, I mean, most of us who watch this, on a close basis, believe we're in a window of vulnerability, because we do not have the military deterrence there that we're used to having. And it's eroded as a result of the 911 wars and decreased defense budgets.\par \par \uc2 And it's a serious deficiency. They have 355 ships going to 460. We have 298 going to 280. They out gun us with airpower, they outgun us with offensive missiles. And they have an extraordinary strategy to hold back the United States military in the event of a conflict over Taiwan. So we have to catch up. And we have to be honest with the American people about the seriousness of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we'll see how, you know, what the seriousness is of Secretary Austin's keeping those guardrails up in this meeting that he has with his Chinese counterpart, and what he has been authorized to say to them in this situation. We just talked in the last segment about the security operation at the White House and how forceful it is or is not. General, thank you. We'll see what happens in that meeting. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Great talking to you, Martha. Good to see you in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nice to see you in person. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 So more than a dozen people, this is an extraordinary story, are now cancer free after they all took the same experimental drug, amazing development that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great development for these cancer patients, 14 of them now in remission after they all took the same experimental drug. The results are from a small trial, but so far it has a 100 percent success rate. Fox Business correspondent Gerri Willis has more for us from New York. Hi, Gerri.\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. That's right. Good news about cancer, oncologist, and patients alike cheering the results for this new experimental drug that eradicated early stage rectal tumors in 100 percent of the patients tested. Now, results of the six month trial were made public Sunday at the annual meeting of Clinical Oncology in Chicago. The drug called get this, Dostarlimab.\par \par \uc2 It's one of the new class of cancer meds you've heard about them immunotherapies, they use the body's own immune system to fight cancer. Now it's notable the trial was very small, just 14 patients participating, but none needed planned follow up care like chemo, radiation, surgery. And while the group was diverse, all the patients had the same abnormality in their cancer cells, which prevented the body from repairing mutations. It's called mismatch repair deficiency.\par \par \uc2 Now of course, the patients will continue to be closely monitored to make sure the tumors don't recur or spread. But the study's authors say it could be utilized for patients with other tumor types like pancreas, stomach, bladder, if those patients have the same abnormality I described. Now, when the study's author Andrea Churchik (ph) presented her findings Sunday morning, the room broke into applause, gasps, and tears emerging as the audience read the findings 100 percent clinical complete response. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is incredible and very encouraging. I love the photos of all of those people who went through that trial. And I encourage everybody to read their individual stories because they are really so inspiring, just like you are. Gerri, thank you. Gerri Willis, always good to see you my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So coming up on this week's episode of The Untold Story podcast I'm joined by Laura Stack the bestselling author of quote, "The Dangerous Truth About Today's Marijuana: Johnny Stack's Life and Death Story." Laura's 19-year- old son Johnny died by suicide in 2019 after becoming psychotic from dabbing high THC marijuana concentrates. She has formed a nonprofit Johnny's ambassadors to shed light on this whole issue. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA STACK, ORATOR: Our children are being used like a giant science experiment on their brains, Martha. And if we wait another 60 years like we did with big tobacco to sound the alarm that this stuff is not only addictive but destructive, we are going to lose generations of our young people to mental illness, to depression to anxiety to bipolar to schizophrenia. And I know Martha 100 percent if marijuana hadn't been here in Colorado, my son would still be with me today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Look, I encourage all parents \uc2 Google\uc2  dabbing, take a look at what this is make yourself aware of what's going on. That is THE STORY for this Thursday. I'll see you tonight, Brett and I anchor special coverage of the hearings on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220610ei6900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SEAN HAN