ng, a full-on Hollywood multimedia extravaganza with, of course, Hollywood creative license to embellish, omit as desired, and they've even procured professional fake news production help from a former ABC executive of, oh, "Good Morning America" and "Nightline". They've gone all out for this.\par \par \uc2 And it turns out this is the same executive who reportedly spiked the ABC News report that would have exposed Jeffrey Epstein many years ago. We'll have more on that later in the program.\par \par \uc2 Now, this upcoming hearing on Thursday aims to do one thing and that is excite their Democratic base, vilify \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and all of his supporters yet again. In other words, it's like a third impeachment.\par \par \uc2 And just last week, Trump adviser \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , he was thrown in handcuffs and leg shackles at an airport for refusing to participate in the sham hearing. He was allegedly thrown in the same jail cell used for Reagan's assassin John Hinckley.\par \par \uc2 Now this after being charged with two misdemeanor process crimes for not cooperating with House Democrats, Navarro would have gladly and this is the normal course of business for something not that serious, he would -- if they would have called his lawyer say turn yourself in at this location at 8:00 a.m., and he would have done it easily posted bail as is customary with minor offenses such as the one he's being charged with.\par \par \uc2 But he was never given that opportunity. Instead, Democrats use law enforcement as a political weapon to intimidate and embarrass Navarro, like they did with Manafort and \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  and George Papadopoulos and so many others. Now, there's no respect for our federal government or no aspect of it that Democrats will not weaponize.\par \par \uc2 Now, this upcoming made for TV, you know, fully produced congressional hearing is no exception. This is where it gets interesting. Despite all the hysteria, the truth is clear and if the committee had any real intention of getting to the truth and preventing this from ever happening again, which should be their goal.\par \par \uc2 I condemn this in real time on my radio show. We condemned it right here on this program that night, like we condemned all the riots in the summer of 2020 that were not mostly peaceful. Now, they would not be turning to this into another hoax impeachment. Of course, they've had a predetermined outcome sadly from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Knowing the dangers associated with large protests, it turns out that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  approved up to 20,000 National Guard troops that would bolster the Capitol well in advance of the rally on January 6th. He did it on January 4th.\par \par \uc2 My prediction is, is that they will lie by omission, the committee will, keep that critical fact out of the circus presentation that they, you know, are creating as we speak, their Hollywood show that they're going to put on. They won't focus on once the president as required by law signed off on up to 20,000 troops being called up. That was his role.\par \par \uc2 Then the jurisdiction went to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who's in charge of security for the House, along with the sergeant of arms and the D.C. Mayor Bowser who refused to call up the guard. Again, that that is their call as required by law.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did his part, and if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really wanted this breach of the Capitol, why would he have authorized up to 20,000 National Guard troops.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are multiple documents and statements to back all this up and the January committee has all of this, but they won't be including it on Thursday. By the way, that would include this letter from Mayor Bowser declining National Guard support prior to January. These documents from a recent inspector general's report that details how Trump's Department of Defense planned for January and they planned appropriately.\par \par \uc2 And they responded for calls for assistance appropriately and they did not delay or obstruct the response to the January 6 riot.\par \par \uc2 Now, we also reached out to Biden's defense secretary, \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 . He was in a high level role at the DOD during January 6. He's yet to provide us with a comment. They said they wouldn't get back to us tonight. Hopefully, we will be able to report tomorrow what their answer is.\par \par \uc2 But don't forget right here on this program, the DOD chief of staff, Department of Defense chief of staff Kash Patel, along with the acting Department of Defense Secretary Chris Miller, they will join us in a minute, along with the White House chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and president Trump himself -- four of the five people in the room on January 4th, all of them have confirmed what we know to be true, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did request and as required by law approved and authorized up to 20,000 guard troops to be called up by Pelosi and Bowser should they need them and want them. He saw this as a potential -- a potentially dangerous situation. Why wouldn't he after coming off the riots of the summer of 2020?\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Did you authorize calling up the guard and then it became -- the chain of command went to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and to the mayor of D.C., Muriel Bowser? Did you as required by law authorize that?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: One hundred percent and attested to by many people and they turned it down. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  turned it down.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FORMER DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Mayor Bowser's written refusal, the communications between the leader of the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  and their chain of command to the DOD refusing our request to allow National Guard's men and women to stage on January 4 and 5 before January 6.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Did you both ask for the National Guard to be called up?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Without a doubt, Sean. We've made that very clear not just once but on numerous occasions we wanted to make sure that there was plenty of National Guard on the ready in case there was some kind of violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I had a meeting with President Trump on the 3rd of January concerning some international threats and at the very end, he asked if there were any requests for national guard support.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What was the president's response to you with regard to the request made by Mayor Bowser?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fill it and do whatever was necessary to protect the demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, Kash Patel and the former Acting Defense Secretary Chris Miller will join us in a second.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, none of what you just saw I predict will be played by the January 6 Committee. They don't care about the truth. Sadly, they're not even looking for ways to better secure the Capitol and protect our elected leaders, which we must do. They're just smearing Trump again and again and again and attempting to cement their power in D.C.\par \par \uc2 According to reports, we have Democrat \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  now wants to use the January 6 Committee to abolish the Electoral College so major population centers New York and California and New Jersey and Illinois, they can rule the country with an iron fist in perpetuity.\par \par \uc2 Now, clearly, if you're actually interested in the truth about January 6, and not just using it as a political weapon and you want to protect all elected officials, you're better off watching this program.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction is the author of "The Plot to Kill the King", by the way, number one best-selling children's book in the country, former chief of staff to the acting defense secretary, Kash Patel, is with us, along with former Acting Defense Secretary \uc2 Christopher Miller\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Okay. My understanding is this, there were five of you in the room. President Trump, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , both of you and General Milley, is that correct, Mr. Secretary?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MILLER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Yeah, that's exactly correct, Sean. Hey, first off, if you don't mind, thanks for taking this on. I can't help but think years ago tonight if we were alive, Kash and I would have been on the front lines. You would have been there along with Edward R. Murrow and Walter Cronkite taking back Europe from the Nazis and this is our front line now.\par \par \uc2 So thanks for taking this on and being serious about it. There hasn't been enough serious conversation. And this is an exclusive first time Kash and I have been on together because we've been separated to make sure there was an appearance of collusion or anything like that. So thanks for taking this on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, all -- so the four of you that I've had an opportunity to interview now confirm that on January 4th in the Oval Office, that you heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  authorize up to troops, Kash Patel, two days before January 6 even happened. Tell us about that meeting.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yeah, it's not one of those -- thanks again, Sean, like Chris said for having us especially on D-Day to talk about this serious matter. It's not one of those meetings you forget. The Secretary of Defense Chris Miller, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , the chief of staff to the president United States of America, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , and the president of the United States himself in the Oval Office talking about some of the most serious national security threats we were facing and then we pivoted to setting up for January 6 and Mr. Trump unequivocally authorized up to 20,000 national guardsmen and women for us to utilize should the second part of the law the requests come in. But those requests never did, as you highlighted.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me -- let me be very clear. Both of you said this under oath, under the threat of a penalty of perjury to the committee?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Absolutely, Sean. And to be clear, Kash brought it up best, the meeting was one -- it was one of the serious kind of heavy meetings I've been in and it was about a foreign threat that was directed towards the United States, which obviously we can't talk to you for about -- for fear of, you know, ended up in jail.\par \par \uc2 And so, I want to -- I kind of want to like be clear, the president -- as we're leaving -- he says, hey, one more thing, then we all sat back down and we discussed what was going on in January 6.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to -- because I think that's important to bring up so that the opposition doesn't get this idea that this was the purpose of the meeting. The president was doing exactly what I expect commander in chief to do, any commander in chief to do. He was looking at the broad threats against the United States and he brought this up as -- on his own. We did not bring it up.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. And, Kash, the only other person in the room was General Milley. Now, I've had a source tell me and we've been reaching out to his office -- hopefully, we'll hear back from him. I don't think he's avoiding -- avoiding us in any way, and that General Milley, one -- I had somebody tell me that he signed a document that attests to the very thing that you and the defense secretary and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and the president have already confirmed. Have you heard of such a memo or memorandum that exists that is in the hands of the January 6 committee?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yeah, thanks, Sean. So let me be clear, \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  also testified under oath as Secretary Miller and myself, and we've always been consistent that the president authorized it. If \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , it's my belief, had said anything otherwise, that committee, including Adam Schiff, would have leaked that information because it would advance their narrative.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the memo you speak of, the National Guard reports in through the chain of command. That is through the secretary of defense and the secretary of the army. There's a delegation memo that was \uc2 Christopher Miller\uc2  was smart enough to craft in order to save time so that we would not have to go through bureaucratic gymnastics should the requests come in. That memorandum was executed by the office of the secretary of defense, the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff and the secretary of the army who controls the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 I hope that memorandum is in the hands of the January 6 committee, along with the slew of email traffic between the offices I just mentioned outlining and with particularity the request from the president of the United States for up to 20,000 National Guardsmen and the denial of that request by Mayor Bowser and the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  and other federal agencies. Those are the documents that a congressional committee of oversight who cares about the truth, those are the documents that should be put forth before the American public, though I doubt they will on Thursday in this Hollywood bonanza that they're attempting to bury the facts.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I want the public the -- I want the public to understand this. Mr. Secretary, once the president makes that authorization and once that is communicated to the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Mayor Bowser who wrote down -- she denied in writing that she even wanted the guard to be called up, it is not the president's authority to call up the guard on his own. He can only as required by law authorize it, but it's in their jurisdiction, their total call. He has no say in that. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The president could mobilize the National Guard but he made the decision to support the local law enforcement and political leaders. So what you said is exactly right, typically, the governors -- I want to highlight, Sean? We reiterated numerous times to all of the cabinet secretaries, as well as the political leadership in Washington, D.C. that we would provide -- the president had authorized any National Guard support or any military support, I want to be clear that's just not National Guard, any military support that they requested. We got one request from Mayor Bowser which we fulfilled uh in fall and --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Mr. Secretary, isn't this consistent with what the president did in the summer of 2020 and we had this -- we had these local authorities butting heads? The president would authorize the use of the guard. They wouldn't call up the guard, but he left it in the hands of local authorities in almost every instance?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I wasn't there for that event. I can only speak about the 6th of January and the events leading up to it, and I can say definitively, President Trump offered any and all support requested by suitable officials within Washington, D.C., federal, state -- well, it's not state, but federal and local.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, Kash Patel, let me ask you. We're told by the committee chairman, it was reported that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is off limits. That means no testimony, no text messages, no emails, nothing from the sergeant of arms, nothing from Mayor Bowser.\par \par \uc2 Is it and is it not true that the mayor herself in writing declined the use of the guard that Trump authorized? Is that true? All of that true?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: All of that's true and that's not how you run a congressional investigation. I know from my days of running the Russia-gate investigation.\par \par \uc2 But let's put all this documentation aside, Sean. Let's put the memos and the delegations aside and the authorizations aside, and look at the proof this way -- had the president not authorized 20,000 National Guardsmen and Women, then when we did employ and deploy and mobilize 15,000 National Guardsmen and Women on January 6th, that would have been an unlawful command from the secretary of defense, the chairman of the joint chiefs of staff, the secretary of the army and the general of the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 Do you think we executed an unlawful command, that question is posed to America in the January 6 committee? Of course, not. They want it both ways because they want the political narrative to defeat the facts but it's just not true. We acted appropriately like the inspector general's report stated and President Trump had the foresight to issue the authorization for the National Guard's men and women.\par \par \uc2 Those are the hard facts that a congressional investigation is diluting with their politicization of our national security apparatus.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you both. Mr. Secretary, I'll start with you.\par \par \uc2 To me, it's pretty -- it's commonsensical. If those troops that the president authorized were called up, up to 20,000 of them, isn't it -- isn't it pretty easy to conclude that January 6 as it unfolded would not have happened? That 20 -- 10 -- 20,000 troops would have stopped any breach of the Capitol. So the fact that this is totally and completely being ignored by the committee to me speaks volumes.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts? Would that have stopped what happened that day? I believe it would have.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: A little different view. My firm belief is that if we would have sent even one national guard troop up the Capitol Hill before the onset of the riot, we would have been accused, President Trump and I and the military and Kash Patel would have been accused of fomenting a coup against the elected officials on Capitol Hill. So I think it's important to recognize that the United States military, there were 10,000 police officers on duty that day, all of our briefings and all of our coordination with them, they said we can handle up to -- here's a headline for you --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: -- they said they can handle up to one million demonstrators. They're like, we got this. We got this. So we -- we're very comfortable with the request based on their threat assessment of what they can handle.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I have less than a minute. I doubt they're going to focus on the president's words that many of you will now peacefully and patriotically march to the Capitol so your voices will be heard.\par \par \uc2 We'll give you the last word, Kash Patel. So now this is going to be a Hollywood production with Hollywood creative license to ignore what is clearly the most relevant facts in the case and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  authorizing this days earlier. What does it tell you about the committee and about the show that they're going to put on for the American people missing pertinent information? We have about 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yeah. Sure, Sean. So what they're doing is they're politicizing the national security of our country. They're forcing individuals like me who served for 16 years to spend hundreds of thousands of dollars in legal fees that should never occur. They're attacking people with political subpoenas and vendettas and then crying about them when this Justice Department doesn't get it.\par \par \uc2 You know what they should do, Sean, they should release every single document they have in their holdings. Chris and I have seen them. We want them out. And above that, they should release every transcript, mine, General Milley's, Secretary Miller's, everybody's transcript to show the American people that we took an oath to abide by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 We took an oath to testify that day about January 6 and they can read in black and white what we did and how we acted appropriately and how the president's authorization was unequivocal. They don't want to put that on the front lines but I'm happy to come on your show and tell the world about it anytime you want.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you. We'll have you back. Secretary Miller, thank you. Kash Patel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Biden administration continues to brush off high gas prices as they move on with their radical green agenda. We'll explain. Governor Rick Perry gives his reaction, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Now, the Biden team's energy policy is falling apart in every front tonight because as gas prices hit one record after another, failed far left Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  judge says that Biden isn't to blame for this. No, it's those greedy oil companies. Okay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: We also know that the price of gasoline is not set by a dial in the Oval Office, and when an oil company is deciding hour by hour how much to charge you for a gallon of gas, they're not calling the administration to ask what they should do. They're doing it based on their goal of maximizing their profits. It's been very striking right now to see these oil companies who have become almost ridiculously profitable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now despite these obvious and undeniable failures, the Biden team is hell-bent on pursuing the green new deal fantasies of theirs, which would destroy the economy, is destroying the economy almost instantaneously.\par \par \uc2 For example, Biden is now invoking the Defense Production Act, not to lower gas prices but to ramp up clean energy production.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, former energy secretary, Texas Governor Rick Perry.\par \par \uc2 You know, so Buttigieg first says, buy electric cars, then he blames -- he's out there saying, oh, well, get used to our gas prices until we are energy independent only with clean energy and now he's out there -- let's see blaming oil companies and blaming everybody but themselves, you are the energy secretary, did the policy shift from Biden, from Trump to Biden, caused the problems we'll have at the pump?\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY, FORMER U.S. ENERGY SECRETARY: Now, well, the simple answer is yes. But you know, the question I have for uh the transportation secretary is what's the emergency in the renewable industry that you would put the DPA to work? And this is a section of our power industry that produces at max about 20 percent. So the idea that they're going to go use the DPA in the solar and in the wind side of things is just beyond me.\par \par \uc2 If you really wanted to make a difference, what you would have done is sent the message early on in this administration -- listen, we're going to -- we're going to be a partner with the energy industry. But what they did is they sent the clear message of we don't like the fossil fuel industry, we don't like the nuclear industry, and actually, when you look at it, the wind and the solar people out there, they've got their own challenges as well because of the not in my backyard mentality that you see in a lot of these places when you start putting in big wind farms or you start putting in big solar facilities, the local community stops them.\par \par \uc2 So if you don't support fossil fuels and if you don't support nuclear energy, you are going to be in a fool's errand to try to fuel this country and certainly the world. So they're getting it wrong in so many different ways. It's -- it is stunning.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Governor, Mr. Secretary, you know about energy and you know about politics. So Democrats think that they can mitigate their losses in November by talking about abortion, about guns, about January 6th, then use the usual playbook Republicans are racist and sexist and homophobic. But every single day, every American, it's -- it smacks them right in the face when they fill up their gas tank and every item they buy in every store the