 panel market, and again, avoids unleashing all domestic energy possibilities, including fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich begins our coverage live from the North Lawn tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 That's right. The president is invoking that wartime law to try to boost domestic manufacturing of solar panels. But one manufacturer says it is a waste of taxpayer money and falls far short of delivering a policy to sustain the industry.\par \par \uc2 And there's even more blowback over his decision to lift tariffs on some imports.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): In a new clean energy push, President Biden is pausing tariffs on solar panel imports from four Southeast Asian countries, after a Commerce Department investigation over whether companies there, circumvented tariffs on Chinese-made goods stalled projects in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): How is this not a gift to Chinese solar manufacturers who may of whom operate with forced labor and are subsidized by the Chinese Communist Party?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Today's announcement is about one country in one country alone, and it's about the United States.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): The White House says the tariff exemptions don't apply to any materials imported from China. But critics are sounding off. A spokesperson for the pro tariff group Coalition for Prosperous America, tweeted in part, "It's time to call this for what it is: Bill Back Beijing.\par \par \uc2 And First Solar, an American manufacturer of solar panels said it was deeply disappointed with the announcement, and "Directly undermines American solar manufacturing by giving unfettered access to China's state subsidized solar companies for the next two years."\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House couldn't say which emergency the president's using to invoke the wartime law.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: This is a very important part of the president's agenda in getting to that clean energy. This is a way that we felt that we can act to get moving in that way.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The push for sustainable energy comes as gas prices continue to break records. Today, $0.25 higher than just a week ago. Averaging $4.86 for a gallon of regular.\par \par \uc2 According to a new ABC News poll, eight in 10 voters say inflation is their top concern, and only 28 percent approve how the president is handling it. Biden taking fire from both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): So I support everything the president is doing. But I don't think it's enough that my constituents don't think it's enough.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He takes no responsibility for his economic malpractice, because that's what this is. He's missed the diagnosis for over a year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The White House says the president is doing everything he can to bring gas prices down. But he won't be lobbying Saudi Arabia to boost oil production if he meets with the Crown Prince next month.\par \par \uc2 That meeting is under consideration despite the country's record on human rights abuses, a bar that Biden upheld when it comes to the summit he's hosting this week, denying entry to three Latin American countries and thereby causing Mexican president to back out of the whole plan. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, today is the 78th anniversary of D-Day. The battle that turned the tide in World War II. It's 6:03. Eastern, nothing from President Biden or the White House today on the anniversary of D-Day.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Nothing from the president or from the White House, and it's actually a second year in a row for that. And it is not like this White House doesn't issue statements frequently. On May 31st, they gave a proclamation on National Ocean Month. Also a proclamation on National Homeownership Month, and a proclamation on Great Outdoors Month.\par \par \uc2 This is something that all the previous past presidents Bush, \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and Trump, all acknowledged in some form during their first year in office. But two years in a row, no statement from President Biden, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's quite something. The chairman of Joint Chiefs of Staff was at Normandy. We should point that out. We'll have something from that a little bit later in the show. But interesting, no statement. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A caravan of nearly 10,000 migrants making their way towards the U.S. southern border tonight, and more are joining them along the way. This is border agents there, work to control the already massive numbers they're dealing with on a daily basis at the border.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has the story from the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Chanting liberty in Spanish, a large migrant caravan stretching some three miles long departs southern Mexico, undeterred by bad weather.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ROSAN ANGELA MARQUEZ, VENEZUELAN MIGRANT (through translator): This, for us is nothing. It can rain, it can thunder nobody will stop us.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Mexican National Guard line the road not stopping their march. Organizers say it's nearly 10,000 strong, with 80 percent of the migrants from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 JOSE GOMEZ, VENEZUELAN MIGRANT (through translator): You can't live in that country anymore.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Caravan leader, Luis Villagran, tells Fox News, they have a message for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 LUIS VILLAGRAN, CARAVAN ORGANIZER (through translator): We also want to tell President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to see, to sit down with the Latin America countries, to see what is happening.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: It's