n Friday may have been planning multiple assassinations after finding a list of powerful political targets.\par \par \uc2 That's raising concerns here in Washington as we await a monumental abortion ruling, of course, from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gillian Turner has details tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Officials tell Fox News, shooting suspect, Douglas Gouda had a hit list found at the murder scene.\par \par \uc2 It included Wisconsin Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAUL\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL OF WISCONSIN: Appears to be related to the judicial system. Those who may have other -- and other targets have been notified of that.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The victim is 68-year-old John Roemer, a former Juneau County judge who retired from the bench in 2017. He was found Friday zip tied to a chair and fatally shot.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): This is the really the most extreme concern of all that a former party or defendant is going to search you out and meet out his own form of retribution.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  assigned to protect the 2,700 federal judges nationwide say they've monitored more than 4,500 threats levelled against judges and prosecutors last year alone.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: Turn up you incubator.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now, angry protests against conservative Supreme Court justices in the aftermath of the leaked abortion draft opinion are once again raising fresh questions about their safety.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): One of those decisions will be the Dobbs case, involving the fate of Roe vs. Wade. And that's going to tell us a lot about what it will look like outside the Supreme Court over the next few months.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But congressional efforts have so far stalled. Efforts to fund protection for the families of the justices and keep judges' personal information like home addresses from public databases.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Well, the Supreme Court's internal investigation into the leak of the draft opinion which could overturn Roe v. Wade has intensified, we are learning. Officials are now having law clerks turn over their personal cell phone records and sign affidavits, a ruling, Bret, from the court on row could come any day. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring an Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton. He's a member of the Senate Judiciary and intelligence committees. Senator, thanks for coming back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): Thanks for having me on again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to ask you about guns in a second, but I want to start with our lead story about that executive action by President Biden on solar panels, reducing tariffs and initiating the Defense Production Act on climate areas.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: That's a good question, Bret. I'm not sure what the emergency is here. Other than he has a deep division in the Democratic Party and the solar industry, between people who make solar panels in America, and those who buy them.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not willing to protect American manufacturers in the green energy industry, then, he is not willing to protect any traditional industry from China's economic crimes. Especially, since it seems that a lot of the parts and inputs, if not the panels themselves are made in the Xinjiang Province, where China uses slave labor from religious and ethnic minorities.\par \par \uc2 But more fundamentally, Bret, when gas is approaching $5 a gallon on average across the country -- $7 and $8 in some states. The fact that the Biden White House is focused on solar energy, as opposed to getting gas prices under control, really tells you where their priorities are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think is at the heart of that? I mean, he says he wants to do everything to bring gas prices down. But obviously, they're not doing everything they can be doing. And this executive action proves that.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yes, they're not. It's not a priority, because it's not a priority for Democratic politicians to bring gas prices down.\par \par \uc2 Well, this exactly what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in the campaign that we're going to phase out in resources like oil, and gas, and coal. So, for Democrats who have these green New Deal fantasies, $5 gallon gas is not an accident. It's not an unintended consequence. It's very much the intended consequence. They want gas to be high because they want you out of your pickup truck or your minivan and want you to get into electric vehicles, or scooters or mass transit, or whatever it is that they ride around town in Washington.\par \par \uc2 But that's not the way most Arkansans live, it's not the way most Americans live.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, talks on Capitol Hill on the Senate side in particular about coming to some conclusions, some compromise on guns, mental health, school security. Here is senators Manchin and Murphy. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): We know we can do something that would have prevented this. Raising the age, making sure that the age, at least gives us a chance to work with that person seeing and evaluating and have a little maturity to them. And the second thing is that we know that the red flag laws do work as long as due process.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): The House is going to act this week. My hope is in the following weeks, the Senate will act as well. Progress, I hope, will show many of our Republican holdouts that the sky doesn't fall, if they support common sense anti-gun violence measures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, are you for raising the age to buy an A.R.-15 to 21?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, Bret, even the liberal Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals in California has already said that's unconstitutional. I don't think that we should be infringing on the rights of law abiding citizens because of gun criminals.\par \par \uc2 You just had, earlier a few moments ago, news from Philadelphia and the terrible shooting you had over the weekend there. It is a simple fact the vast majority of gun crimes in this country are committed by serious, violent felons often involved in the drug trade.\par \par \uc2 That's why I've got legislation that would impose mandatory minimums on felons in possession of a firearm at all, that would increase penalties for felons who use a gun or who have a career of committing crimes using firearms.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Democrats have traditionally oppose these measures, because it cuts against their desire to reduce mandatory minimums to reduce the prison population. But if we want to get at the vast majority of gun crimes, that's a good place to start.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But if there is a compromise to be had, and if it's not, you know, we're going to take guns or we're going to somehow exclude or infringe on the Second Amendment. And it's to raise an age or increase a background check for an A.R.-15 or the like, why not get in that conversation?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, we don't know what the compromise will be. First, we don't know what legislation might look like. I'll, of course, evaluate any proposal, but I'll evaluate it with a view to the Second Amendment rights of law abiding citizens, and also what would actually work once we know all the facts.\par \par \uc2 The facts that have come out of Uvalde for the last two weeks have been changing somewhat. We may have the basic contours, but there's still more that we need to learn to ensure that we have a proposal that would actually solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 In addition, some of the proposals that you've discussed or that we heard in those clips, are more appropriately addressed at our state capitol level by our state legislatures, rather than here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You wrote a letter to the White House about their effort to potentially reduce student loan debt, federal student loan debt, but you have a concern about ethics.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Yes, Bret. At a time when the president is talking about canceling $10,000 of student loan debt for any American who has it, he has numerous White House aides who actually have student loan debt, who are working on this matter.\par \par \uc2 In violation of his own ethics guidance, that's inappropriate. More fundamentally, he doesn't have the legal authority to cancel student loan debt and it's deeply unfair.\par \par \uc2 What about all those kids who went to college and worked during college and then taking out loans, or they pay their loans back?\par \par \uc2 Or the vast majority of Americans who don't have a college degree who work as waiters and waitresses, or truck drivers. Or have a small lawn care business? If they didn't taken out loans, and now we're going to ask them to repay the loans of people who did go to school and have agreed to repay them. That is deeply unfair, the president shouldn't do it. He certainly shouldn't have aides who are going to benefit from it working on the matter.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I always like to ask you questions about foreign policy, but I don't have a lot of time, I want to end with this, January 6 committee is doing primetime hearings starting Thursday night, your thoughts of this as we get ready to hear whatever they're going to bring to the table in their investigation?\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Well, I've seen a lot of reports recently say that this committee, which I think is illegitimate on the House own rules and resolution that created it is now geared towards things like