r \uc2 Meantime, in L.A., Los Angeles's progressive District Attorney George Gascon is under fire again tonight overcharges in a hit and run case over last summer. The juvenile driver's criminal past calling into question why he was back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin has the story from Los Angeles tonight. And a warning here, some of the images in this report may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Harrowing video out of Venice, California last August shows a 16-year-old driver with no license in a stolen car mowing down a mother walking with her baby in a stroller, leaving them with minor injuries. The juvenile then tries to flee the scene before a Good Samaritan uses his truck to stop him.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has learned that teen was on felony probation at the time for spiking a girl's drink in 2019 and he was in violation of his probation. Despite that criminal history, on Friday, the team was sentenced to serve just five months in diversionary camp, which prosecutors tell us is a little tougher than summer camp. This after L.A. District Attorney George Gascon's office opted to file less serious felony charges in accordance with his progressive policies.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to Fox News, Gascon's office said the light sentence was appropriate, telling us in part "The sheriff's department agreed with the felony charges that were filed. We stand by the purpose of the juvenile justice system to rehabilitate young people".\par \par \uc2 But L.A. Sheriff Alex Villanueva immediately pushed back tweeting: "We were never consulted as we were not the investigating agency".\par \par \uc2 Gascon's office then admitted to us the sheriff's department had no involvement and they would have to correct their statement.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Women can't walk their babies down the street in Gascon's L.A. and he keeps doubling down on policies and then, he lies to the media.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And the mom who was run over is blasting Gascon's handling of the case and the weak sentence, telling Fox in part, someone with a criminal record tried to kill me and my son. And George Gascon thinks that five months of camp is a sufficient punishment.\par \par \uc2 Gascon and his staff are highlighting their incompetence in their complete disregard for victims.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, the second campaign to recall L.A. D.A. George Gascon has now surpassed half a million signatures. That is the most ever collected for any petition here in L.A. County. They need about 70,000 more by the July 6th deadline to officially get the recall to qualify on the ballot. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live in L.A. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, just down the way -- up the way rather, San Francisco District Attorney \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2  will face a recall election tomorrow. Polls on the recall race suggest the progressive prosecutor is in real jeopardy of losing his seat.\par \par \uc2 Critics say Boudin is not prosecuting criminals aggressively enough and is putting the safety of San Francisco residents at risk.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democrats ramp up the pressure on Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  over the Capitol riot probe.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight, Fox 5 in San Diego where the Navy identifies a fighter jet pilot killed in a training crash. Friday Lieutenant Richard Bullock was killed when his F-18 Super Hornet went down during a routine training mission.\par \par \uc2 The cause of the crash remains under investigation. Military officials say recovery efforts are ongoing at the crash site.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Anchorage, Alaska. One of the big stories there tonight. The Iditarod sled dog is found safe after disappearing from a checkpoint in the race three months ago.\par \par \uc2 The Trail Committee says the French musher will return to pick up his dog Leon and then head back to France. They found him.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway, way outside, from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some Democrats are growing frustrated with the Justice Department even after the indictment of the former Trump aide \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , who, by the way, is on \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 's show tonight. Democrats are urging Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to pursue more prosecutions over the January 6th Capitol riot. FOX News is told they may get what they want. Correspondent David Spunt has the story from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Multiple sources inside the Justice Department tell FOX News nothing is off the table when it comes to charging other high-profile names in the House January 6th committee probe with contempt of Congress. Former Trump adviser \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  told a judge his two misdemeanor counts were unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 PETER NAVARRO\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP AIDE: I let them know that at the Justice Department. There was no reason on God's good earth for what they did. That did not have to happen.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Hours after Navarro's Friday court appearance, members of the House January 6th committee learned the Justice Department would not indict former Trump chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and social media director Dan Scavino. Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  and Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  expressed concern, saying in a statement, "We find the decision to reward \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and Dan Scavino for their continued attack on the rule of law puzzling. We hope the Department provides greater clarity on this matter."\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has yet to give specifics. Later this summer, former Trump aide \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  will head to trial for ignoring the House committee.\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: There is no way for us to really decipher what makes the difference between Navarro, Bannon, and Meadows. There could be different facts on the ground in terms of their level of compliance with the subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: On Thursday night, the committee will make its case to the American people in prime time. Former ABC News president James Goldston has been tapped to bring previously unseen material into the home of millions expected to watch the hearing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And Bret, just hours ago, five men, including the leader of the Proud Boys, had their charges upgraded to seditious conspiracy. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the DOJ. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in FOX News senior political analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening, what do you make of this Thursday prime time event and what may come out of it?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Let's be clear about this committee. While it is bipartisan, it is bipartisan on paper only because all the members of the committee, including the two Republicans, were appointed by the Democrats, by \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , in fact. And she was careful to choose the two Republican member who could be counted on to agree to the conclusions reached by her as articulated by Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  when this was just getting underway. That's the congressman from Mississippi, the democrat who is heading the committee who said that we came, quote, critically close to losing our democracy on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 So there you have the position where the committee's leaders have already reached, as has the speaker of the House, so this is very much a partisan hearing, which is not to say that interesting facts won't come out and that they won't make an interesting presentation in prime time. But look, this is only partially a hearing to determine the facts. It is as much as anything else an effort to give the Democrats an issue that they can run on when nearly all the other issues that people care about are working against them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To that point, we just heard mentioned James Goldston, the former president of ABC News, has agreed to join the committee as a soon to be announced adviser producing Thursday's special as a blockbuster event. The broadcast will be filmed with never-before-seen surveillance footage of the January 6th Capitol riot, over 1,000 interviews and depositions with witnesses and a variety of other explosive documents and tips. FOX News is told Goldston will have a hand in all the hearings this month, but he will be particularly involved in the efforts by the committee to make its prime time hearing Thursday and another one later this month, quote, TV friendly. What about that?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, that makes -- well, I'm old enough to remember the mother of all monumental congressional hearings, and that, of course, would the Watergate hearings. But they were not held in prime time, and they were about a president who was still in office. So this is a hearing about the activities of a president and his followers, a president who is now a year- and-a-half removed from office after an effort to upset the election results that not only failed but turned out to be really, while they caused a riot, as an effort to overthrow an election, they were a fiasco.\par \par \uc2 And I suspect that a great many of the American people have moved on from this and don't really care that much about it anymore, which is why this TV professional, former president of ABC News, has been brought in to try to make the production as dramatic as possible. They may succeed. I question how long people will care about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Very quickly, Brit, and maybe I'm making too big of a deal out of this, but second year in a row for this White House, this president not to commemorate in some way the anniversary of D-Day? It's kind of weird, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 HUME: I'm surprised. It seems to me an easy call to make. The president hasn't been shy about commemorating military events or the heroism of American soldiers, which was certainly on full display on the D-Day invasion which came close to failing, and only by remarkable prowess and heroism did it finally succeed. It was an amazing event, and great books have been written about it, movies made about it. It's one of the most memorable events in American military history. I'm at a loss to describe why the president wouldn't take note of it today. Surprising.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It seems like low hanging fruit. Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  issues a new warning to the west as Ukrainian forces go on the offensive in the country's east. A live report from Kyiv next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  survived a no confidence vote a few hours ago, securing enough support from his conservative party to remain in office as the U.K. prime minister. The vote taking place because of revelations that he and his staff repeatedly held boozy parties that flouted the COVID restrictions they imposed on others.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, overseas also Russia issuing new threats to the west if it provides Ukraine with long-range rockets, which the Biden administration has already pledged to do. It comes as Russian forces strike Kyiv for the first time in more than a month, and Ukrainian forces make successful counterattacks in the east. Senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  reports tonight from the Ukrainian Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Renewed fierce street-to-street fighting as Ukrainian forces, nearly driven from the last city they controlled in eastern Luhansk region, have battled their way back to controlling Sievierodonetsk. Russia has focus resources there, trying to capture the area before Ukraine receives rocket launchers with better range and accuracy than the artillery Ukraine is currently using.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  made a visit to the front lines, thanking the soldiers and encouraging civilians to hold on.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Take care of yourselves. This is very, very important, because without you, there is no Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Both the U.S. and Great Britain have promised multiple launch rocket systems to Ukraine. With a potential of turning the tide, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  threatened to escalate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): If they are supplied, we are draw appropriate conclusion and use our means of destruction strike at those objects we have not struck.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Russia said western weapons were the target of yesterday's airstrikes in Kyiv. Cruise missiles hit warehouses where Ukrainians say train cars are repaired. As reporters were given a tour of the target, there was no visible evidence of weapons.\par \par \uc2 SERHIY LESHCHENKO, PEOPLE'S DEPUTY OF UKRAINE: This facility is totally peaceful, and everyone can come and see it.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: From strikes on civilian housing to evidence of executions and mass graves, Ukraine's prosecutor general is pursuing thousands of war crimes cases. Ukraine is using websites and apps to crowdsource evidence of war crimes, and the public is responding. Prosecutors are sorting through more than 50,000 videos alleging war crimes.\par \par \uc2 IRYNA VENEDIKTOVA, UKRAINE PROSECUTOR GENERAL: We see now unprecedented volume of crimes. That's why people want to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And as the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  targets Putin allies, a warrant has been issued to seize the property of oligarch Roman Abramovich. Specifically, the government wants to seize a gulf stream jet worth $60 million and a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  787 worth $350 million. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  live in Kyiv. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's green energy push amid surging energy costs, and where is gun control legislation on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, security officials are working to identify the gunman who killed at least 50 people, five-zero, at a Catholic church in southwestern Nigeria Sunday. Authorities says the assailants opened fire on worshipers both inside and outside the building in a coordinated attack before escaping the scene. Officials say explosives were also set in that assault.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Halifax, Canada. One of the big stories there tonight, a crewless robotic boat attempting to retrace the 1620 voyage of the Mayflower finally reaches North America. That vessel docked in Halifax, Nova Scotia, on Sunday instead the Massachusetts coast, but it made the trip.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's the emergency in the --\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: This is just a step to get to a place where we do have a clean energy arsenal. And so this is a very important part of the president's, this is a very important part of the president's agenda in getting to that clean energy?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): I'm not sure what the emergency is here other than he has a deep division in the Democratic Party, in the solar industry between people who make solar panels in America and those who buy them. If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not willing to protect American manufacturers in the green energy industry, then he is not willing to protect any traditional industry from China's economic crimes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, talking about that executive action and the Defense Production Act for solar panels in the midst of all of that is going on. Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of things that happen all the time, Mara. This seems a little out of step, does it not?\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: I think you have got a lot of clashing priorities here. The president wants to help the transition to clean energy, so he wants more solar panels in the U.S. The solar panel industry in the U.S. isn't producing enough right now.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, he also wants to punish China for violating trade rules. Well, does he want to lift the tariffs to get more products into the United States, or does he want to punish China? This is a really -- I think there are just clashing priorities here, and he has decided that his top priority right now is getting more solar panels into the U.S. even if it means importing them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting, just going back in the way back clock, June 21st, 1979, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," Guy, "President Carter observed that the gasoline shortage had disconcerted many. He said with dismay that some few Americans have reached a state of panic. The president's solar power plan calls for the nation to obtain 20 percent of its energy from solar power and other renewable sources by the year 2000. The president said he was committed to spend more than $1 billion in the fiscal year 1980 to stimulate solar and other renewable forms of energy."\par \par \uc2 In other words, other presidents have chosen at a moment when gas prices are spiking to go after solar. This one happens to be President Carter.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Everything old is new again. And this is one of several comparisons being made between the current president and that particular former president. Indeed, there was a "Politico" story out just yesterday reporting that President Biden is privately seething about his approval numbers and how people are comparing him to \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . When outcomes are this bad, the Carter comparisons may be apt, and they are certainly going to arise.\par \par \uc2 As for this particular issue on solar power and solar panels and these tariffs, it seems a little bit in the weeds for many Americans. It also seems a little bit in the weeds for the White House press secretary, who was asked pretty simple questions, what's the emergency here to justify it, and her answers were, let's say, less than persuasive in terms of persuading me that she really has any idea what they're doing. And that might not be her fault. It might be the administration's fault, because a lot of this feels a bit incoherent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Morgan, just broadly, Guy mentioned the tariffs. But this potentially is really good for China, not just on solar panels, which they have a lot of, but other items as well. We heard the commerce secretary say this weekend that there might be more tariffs lifted on China.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: Yes, and to go back what Mara said about China and solar panels, remember, just last year this administration banned solar panels that are made in China. Why? Because they mostly come from the Xinjiang region, which where know slave labor is happening, and genocide as declared by President Trump and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . Secretary Blinken said in his confirmation hearings and at the beginning of his tenure as secretary of state that this administration would lead with human rights.\par \par \uc2 But when you are potentially making it easier to import solar panels that are made through slave labor, that is not putting human rights first. And also, if we are going to relieve any sort of tariffs on China, what deal are we negotiating? What is the Biden team getting for it? We should not continue a foreign policy where we relieve tariffs on China, relieve sanctions pressure on Iran and allow their oil to be bought as well, without getting anything for it in return. That's my big problem. It's like, where is the negotiation here?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Meantime, on Capitol Hill, the gun talks continue in earnest, we are told. Here is Senators Murphy and Toomey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2 , (D-CT): Obviously, we are trying to convince Republicans, and so I can only do so much to get my Republican colleagues to jump on board. I don't think there is any reason why we can't come to agreement by the end of the week, but I'm not interested in setting arbitrary deadlines.\par \par \uc2 SEN. PAT TOOMEY, (R-PA): I've been advocating for expanding background checks. By the way, I think encouraging states to have some kind of red flag laws could make sense as long as there's adequate due process. So there are things we could do that would be constructive that are consistent with Republican values.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So there is a bipartisan group of senators. We have confirmed among them, Senators Cornyn, Tillis, Sinema, Murphy. We saw Toomey on there earlier, Manchin has weighed in. It seems like a growing list. Mara, is this different this time?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Boy, I would hate to say that because we have been here before. And we have never gotten over the finish line to get gun safety legislation. But this time they are working with a pretty -- a set of pretty modest improvements. In there also might be a raise in the age buying an AR-15 from 18 to 21. That kind of law has been signed by \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  when he was the governor of Florida before he was a senator, so that has been done.\par \par \uc2 There is an air of optimism around this, but since we have gotten this far in the past and not had a successful piece of legislation, I don't want to predict what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Well, I think that all the action is in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . What happened in the house and those debates and those votes are irrelevant. Those provisions are going nowhere in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . I think this speech last week from President Biden was emotional at times, divisive at times, partisan at other times, perhaps overall counterproductive, but again, largely, irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 The central question is can this group hammer out some set of proposals, however, modest, that could get 10 Republican senators to join. That's it. That's the math. And I think it's possible. I wouldn't bank on it just yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , I want to end where we started, and that is the economic situation. "Wall Street Journal" polling on the economy, opportunity for most people to achieve the American dream, generally optimistic, 38 percent, generally pessimistic, 61 percent. State of the U.S. economy, excellent or good, 17 percent, poor or not so good, 83 percent. And the present financial situation, pretty well satisfied, 14 percent, more or less satisfied, 51 percent, not satisfied at all, 35 percent. They don't even ask the right track, wrong track, which has been really upside down in every poll we have seen. This is the time that they have to do something to change the equation ahead of November, right?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: And I haven't seen any proposals to that effect. Listen, I live in Tennessee here in real America where people don't make money off of government contracts, and people are hurting. People really are making decisions about filling up their gas tank, which could be over $100 to fill it up completely, and filling up their grocery cart. And that's what I think the administration is missing in terms of tone and perspective.\par \par \uc2 The policies, referring to that "Politico" piece that Guy was talking about earlier in the segment, the policies that they are talking about running on in November are January 6th, are abortion, and are guns. So, I'm not seeing anything that Biden's political team is putting out to actually address the pain at the pump, the pain at the grocery store, the pain in everyday lives for Americans. And until the Biden team realizes that they are doing worse than Trump in the polling because people's lives are simply not as good today as they were two years ago, they are going to continue to see their polls decline.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, as we mentioned at the top of the show remembering D-Day, 78 years ago today more than 150,000 allied troops carried by nearly 7,000 naval vessels stormed the beaches of Normandy, France. Officially it was named Operation Overlord. It was the largest amphibious invasion in military history. Roughly 4,400 allied soldiers were killed on those beaches, including 2,500 Americans. Thousands more were wounded.\par \par \uc2 This year, crowds gathered in Normandy as they do every year to honor, this time, several dozen World War II veterans in attendance, and all those brave men who gave the ultimate sacrifice to liberty Europe from Nazi tyranny. Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , spoke at Omaha Beach today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS: Here beaches the Normandy 78 years ago, ordinary people from all walks of life came together to serve the cause of liberty. Early in the morning, on 6 June, as the German defenders on these blocks looked out to sea, they bore witness to the first plumes of black smoke as 700 allied ships disembarked 137 allied soldiers from several different countries that would storm the beaches of normed and relentlessly turn back the Nazi tide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was the battle that almost didn't happen. We will never forget that moment 78 years ago today.\par \par \uc2 Fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220607ei6600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 America Needs Affordable Diesel And A Reliable Air Industry To Function Properly; Gascon Has Made L.A. A Dangerous Place To Live; Legislation Would Outlaw Most Firearms In The U.S.; Final Exam; Cringey Virtue Signaling From Corporations For Pride Month\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Clay Travis, Bill Melugin, Kevin Corke, Abby  Hornacek, Jimmy Failla \par \uc2 8,107 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm doing the project at the moment and I would just like to know what your favorite animal is. Also, could you find out from Bill Hemmer if he likes tigers? He loves tigers, and my favorite animal is a koala bear.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is up next.\par \par \uc2 And always remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 Let's say just for the sake of a mind experiment that you're the Secretary of Transportation for the United States of America. That's a big job. But what would you do all day? Well, that's a pretty easy one because every Secretary of Transportation has the same two concerns, the overriding concerns, and despite what they may tell you, neither one of these concerns has anything to do with systemic racism.\par \par \uc2 So your first concern would be the price of diesel fuel. Diesel fuel is what makes transportation possible in this country. Virtually everything you buy in America arrived by diesel engine, our entire trucking fleet runs on diesel, so does every single freight locomotive in the United States.\par \par \uc2 So if diesel prices get too high, transportation -- and you're the Secretary of Transportation -- stops. The price of nearly everything becomes unaffordable. So inflation is directly connected to transportation and transportation requires diesel.\par \par \uc2 It turns out the diesel fuel prices are the key to a lot of the American economy, and you would know that.\par \par \uc2 Your second concern if you were Transportation Secretary would be domestic air travel. This is a huge country, it's far too big a country not to have reliable commercial flights from one place to another. Businesses depend on the airlines and so do families.\par \par \uc2 For generations, they've been able to depend on the airlines. America has had the most efficient air service in the world. Planes land on time, they don't crash. So maintaining that system of air travel is probably the top concern of the United States Transportation Department.\par \par \uc2 So that is what you would be worried about if you were the Secretary: Diesel and airlines. And yet remarkably, in less than a year and a half, under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , both of these things, fuel costs and air travel have gone dramatically in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 The price of diesel has now hit its highest price ever recorded in history. The national average is up 70 percent since just last year, it's now $5.53 a gallon. That's terrible for truckers, but it's also terrible for you, it means you're paying much more for everything you buy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Here on I-70, a lot of big trucks making the cross country hauls, but a major increase in diesel prices. With the numbers ticking higher and higher with no end in sight, it is hitting them hard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You pay out a lot more because you're doing the same amount of work and mileage, but you're paying a lot more at the pump.\par \par \uc2 If you've got it or use it or wear it or whatever, a truck touched it one time or another.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: So whether or not you have to buy diesel fuel, the price hike affects your goods, groceries, and services.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You pay for it one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The cost of a gallon of diesel fuel rose by more than a dollar in just Biden's first year as President, that was the largest annual increase in over a decade. Why is this happening? Well, because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made it happen. This was intentional.\par \par \uc2 It's a product of his clampdown of domestic energy production and especially of his lunatic war against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the war that both parties seem to be in favor of, but that is crushing the United States by the sanctions of Russia that have been especially counterproductive.\par \par \uc2 So back in February, you may remember that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  pledged to destroy the Russian economy. Let's make them poor, or is this just in retaliation for installing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as President.\par \par \uc2 So what happened next? Well, four months later, the opposite has happened. The ruble is now at a four-year high. Thanks, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Russia has the largest gold reserves on Planet Earth. Meanwhile, in this country, you can't afford gasoline.\par \par \uc2 Oh, so the opposite from what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  predicted happened. What's his explanation for that? Explanation? Sorry. It was an Act of God. I can't do anything about it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are in a situation where, you know because of a war in Ukraine, gas prices and food prices are extremely high.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot going on right now, but the idea we're going to be able to now click a switch to bring down the cost of gasoline is not likely in the near term, nor is it with regard to food.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, there's nothing we could do. Oh, well, you could push to an end of the war in Ukraine, which Biden is single handedly prolonging. You could force your client state, Ukraine, to come to the table, both countries Russia and Ukraine want to do that. The only reason this war continues is because the White House is forcing him to continue.\par \par \uc2 And then by the way you could stop shutting down energy pipelines. You can stop canceling oil drilling. But of course, Biden doesn't plan to do that, it would help America too much. Instead, he is pleading with Venezuela for oil, and then pleading with the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 Wasn't it just the other day that the United States was energy independent? Yes, but now, we're begging dictatorships to sell us oil. But it won't be enough.\par \par \uc2 Industry officials are predicting shortages very soon, quote, "I wouldn't be surprised to see diesel being rationed on the East Coast this summer, said \uc2 John Catsimatidis\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 United Refining Company\uc2 , quote: "Right now, inventories are low, and we may see a shortage in the coming months."\par \par \uc2 What would that look like here? It would be ugly. And again, it has worked to Russia's advantage. Even "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " was forced to acknowledge that this week, quote: "China and India could be protected from some of the burden of higher oil prices because Russia is offering them discounted oil. Russia has become the second biggest oil supplier to India."\par \par \uc2 So how is this a good thing for the United States? No, it's the opposite. It is a disaster. And this is the first crisis you would expect the Secretary of Transportation to be on top of.\par \par \uc2 We can't afford gasoline, but Russia now has so much oil, it is selling it at a discount to our main global adversary China. That seems bad. But it's only one crisis that the Transportation Secretary has on his plate.\par \par \uc2 The second is domestic air travel. It has become totally unreliable, tried to fly anywhere recently? It's like traveling in India 1985. You have no idea if the planes could arrive or leave, no one will give you a straight answer on that and there is nothing you can do.\par \par \uc2 It is falling apart, and anyone who has tried to go anywhere, particularly this weekend can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABC REPORTER: Since Friday, we've seen more than 2,500 flights canceled in to and out of the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Delta seeing the most blaming weather and air traffic control, vendor staffing and increased COVID case rates.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  reporting staffing shortages at its air Traffic Control Center in Jacksonville, one of the busiest in the nation, saying in a statement, "Weather was a factor, but staffing has been a challenge."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, staffing has been a challenge. Why is that? Maybe because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  forced the airlines to fire employees that didn't want his ineffective shot, and so the system fell apart, there aren't enough people to run it. Airlines can no longer provide on-time flights.\par \par \uc2 So guess what rich people are doing? Well, rather than work to fix the system, they are just opting out completely, they're flying private. According to latest earnings report from the charter airline company, \uc2 Wheels Up\uc2 , in March of 2020, it had 6,300 active members. That number has now nearly doubled to 12,400, and it's not just \uc2 Wheels Up\uc2 . That's the trend across the industry.\par \par \uc2 According to private jet data provided by airports, there were total in April of 2019 of 389,000 private jet flights in this country. Again, April 2019. In April this year, there are more than 475,000 because everyone is so much richer after the COVID lockdowns, no, because if you can afford to fly private and get out of our crumbling commercial air system, you will.\par \par \uc2 But if you can't afford it, good luck getting where you're going. You're probably going to spend some unscheduled time in an airport.\par \par \uc2 For the first three months of 2022, major airlines in this country recorded a cancellation rate that is 50 percent higher than it was for the same period in 2019, fifty percent higher. Good work, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the first quarter of this year, the Department of Transportation received more than 13,000 complaints about airline services, that's up 330 percent from the first quarter of 2019. What a disaster.\par \par \uc2 And not only are you unlikely to get where you're going on time, you're also going to pay a lot more for your ticket. The average price of a domest