had originally reported that Ramos smoked pot. Then mysteriously the reference was missing from the online editions. Now, why is that? Was it bad information? Or is this the pro marijuana bias that we've become accustomed to? That's so powerful because billions are on the line with it nationwide.\par \par \uc2 So is this story now irrelevant? We reached out to The Times on this, but they have not responded.\par \par \uc2 Now, the American people are hearing a lot about AR-15s and background checks. But they also deserve to hear about this as well. Respected medical studies for years now have demonstrated that pot use, especially among teens, can trigger psychosis and increase the chance that a young person will develop violent behaviors.\par \par \uc2 The Association of Mature American Citizens write, "A 2017 study identified marijuana as the single most common, correlative variable in otherwise diverse populations and circumstances surrounding the association of violence and marijuana. Reported links exist to shooters in Parkland, El Paso, Dayton, Aurora-Colorado, Tucson, Chattanooga, Sacramento, and perhaps elsewhere."\par \par \uc2 Now, a friend of the Dayton shooter Connor Betts told authorities that he was a gun owner and that he had done hard drugs with bats as well as marijuana and acid four or five times a week from 2014 to 2015. Former New York Times reporter Alex Berenson has written extensively on this subject.\par \par \uc2 "A 2012 paper in the Journal of Interpersonal Violence, examining a federal survey of more than 9000 adolescents found that marijuana use was associated with a doubling of domestic violence in the U.S."\par \par \uc2 Then there's this, the National Library of Medicine. "Recent illicit and medical marijuana is of much higher potency and more likely to cause violent behavior. High potency marijuana is a predictable and preventable cause of tragic, violent consequences."\par \par \uc2 My question tonight is why isn't there a national conversation happening right now about this? Why aren't reporters doing more to demand information on the potential drug use in this case and others, both illicit drugs and prescription drugs?\par \par \uc2 Antidepressants have long been known to be a problem for some young people. Or are we all just going to pretend that Joe confiscating all the nine millimeters, or Beto running a gun buyback program is going to heal and protect our youth? Is that what we're hoping?\par \par \uc2 You see, I'd rather operate in the real world. And that world is a lot more complicated than the anti Second Amendment, anti-gun activists would suggest. Whenever there is a societal, cultural, familial and spiritual deficit, bad things are going to rush in to fill it. Guns, gangs, drugs, then there'll be harm to others, or self harm. And until we're honest about all of this, young people will continue to be pawns in a political game. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Dr. Russell Kamer, who is the Medical Director of Partners in Safety. Dr. Kamer, thanks for joining us tonight. Again, it was initially reported that he was a user. In fact, he got angry that his grandmother and his mother didn't want him to use marijuana.\par \par \uc2 But again, we don't know more. It was just mysteriously taken out of an article in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . But why aren't people in general not talking more about the pot psychosis, violent behavior connection?\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL KAMER, PARTNERS IN SAFETY MEDICAL DIRECTOR: Well, that's a very good question, Laura. What we found in the study is it's very clear that the use of the high potency marijuana is strongly associated with the development of psychosis. And this is clear. But why aren't we talking about it?\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm going to blame myself, I'm going to blame my generation of baby boomers, who have some nostalgic view of marijuana that it was some weak drug. and it used to be a simple puff of woodstock weed wasn't that dangerous. But today's products are very high THC.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I didn't even think that marijuana could be associated with psychosis up until a few years ago. We were not taught that in medical school. But we see now with the high potency, that it's very clear. My colleagues in Colorado, where they're sounding the alarms that was one of the first states to legalize, it's practically a daily occurrence that kids come into the emergency rooms and florid cannabis induced psychosis.\par \par \uc2 Well, the studies on this are from various different journals, studying even men in China and then thousands in the United States. And it's so, so shocking. I know Alex Berenson wrote the book that kind of blew it out of the water.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that the medical community is well aware of. Yet, you get the sense that the billions of dollars on the line are more important than our kids. And what's happening especially to young men in the United States, who are frequent users of this high potency, THC that's now in marijuana products sold legally in dispensaries across the United States.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this at the very least needs a serious national conversation.\par \par \uc2 KAMER: Definitely. We are witnessing the beginning of a new addiction for profit industry. Tobacco, alcohol, even the opiates are fading away, and now the money shifting over to the new industry. And this shapes the conversation. Even in the medical field, there are many of my colleagues who are not aware that this current marijuana is much more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, in fact, a lot of parents kind of roll their eyes like, Oh, my kid got caught smoking a joint, or big deal, or edibles. And then you hear stories of parents doing the pot with their kids. And I guess that's the cool thing to do in some communities. It's like they have no clue.\par \par \uc2 But it's - I'm doing this topic tonight. I'm going to probably get a lot of hate mail because of it. Because it's so serious. And yet, people like Beto, and I'm not trying to drag you in a political conversation, Dr. Kamer. But they're fine with going to - do a run on the guns. And I guess you can argue that if that's what you want to do, try to get rid of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 But yet completely oblivious to what legalization of marijuana has done and is doing to an entire generation of Americans with violent consequences.\par \par \uc2 KAMER: It's very scary what's going to happen. We have just seen the beginning. Because even here in New York, where it's legalized, the pot shops haven't opened yet. And the damage happens when the pot shops open, when more marijuana is used. We've seen traffic fatalities that have gone up dramatically.\par \par \uc2 It's clear that fatalities have gone up 20 percent. If the nation - if marijuana is legalized nationwide, we predict that there'll be at least 7000 excess traffic fatalities a year. Yet these things are ignored.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Violence, psychosis, schizophrenia, traffic deaths. Other than that, it's just wonderful. Dr. Kamer, thank you for joining us. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Now, one week ago off duty \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2  agent, Jacob Albarado, he started his day at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas. He was at an award ceremony for his eight-year-old daughter, Jada. She's a second grader at the school.\par \par \uc2 Well, shortly after the ceremony ended, he went to get a haircut. Well, that's when all hell broke loose. At 11:41 a.m., he got a text from his wife Trisha, a fourth grade teacher at the same school. There's an active shooter, help. Love you. He immediately jumped up, started looking for a gun. The barber had one. This was a shotgun, and together they raced toward the school.\par \par \uc2 When Jacob arrived, he saw the chaos unfolding. And he knew he needed to get inside to bring his family and anyone else he could to safety. Jacob is here with me now exclusively and I'll let him pick up from there. Jacob, what happened once you got to the school? How'd you get in?\par \par \uc2 JACOB ALBARADO, CBP AGENT WHO RAN INTO TX SHOOTING: I was trying to get to my wife's room. My wife's been working there for seven going on, eight years. And I know the majority of the law enforcement there. So I just announced who I was and made my way towards my wife's room.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And what did you see when you went it?\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: As I went in, I just saw a whole bunch of kids running out - running off campus, jumping through the windows, cops breaking windows, and it was a complete chaos pretty much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And you saw your daughter and I know you gave her a big hug.\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: Yes. I mean, I had spoken with my wife on the phone. My wife had told me that she had already left her room. She was across the street at the funeral home. And from that point on, I went to go look for my daughter.\par \par \uc2 Now, on CNN today, Jake Tapper had a secret service agent on talking about this issue. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: When off duty CBP Customs and Border Protection Agency agent, who was able to go inside the school on the day of the shooting, other parents and other loved ones were standing outside the school at the time. Some of them even reportedly being handcuffed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN WACKROW, FORMER SECRET SERVICE AGENT: This is a clear example of the complete breakdown of the incident command structure. The fact that the public was allowed to get so close to this critical incident site is shocking to me. It breaks all these protocols. We have so many unanswered questions right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Jacob, I don't think you, the parents and the protocol issues are the overall problem here. But the implicit criticism from Tapper, you're probably not be surprised to hear that.\par \par \uc2 Maybe it's slight criticism, maybe I'm making more of it than it should be. But that you got to get in there and that the other parents didn't. And you got to make sure your kid was ok, even though you are helping others get out. Do you have a response to that?\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: Once again, I mean, it was just complete chaos. Everybody was concerned for their child. Thankfully, like I said, I was off duty. I've been born and raised in Uvalde for 42 - going on 42 years. It's a small community, everyone pretty much knows who I am.\par \par \uc2 And like I said, my wife works there. So they all know who I was. So I was able to get on campus. And like I said, I wasn't just trying to save my child. I was trying to get as many people out of there as I could.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jacob, the community is in such deep, immeasurable, inconsolable grief. But I've been reading about how important the churches have been. The Catholic Church and others have been as a comfort to all the families, the grieving, the surviving, and the children who we think about tonight.\par \par \uc2 What have you seen about the community coming together after this unimaginable evil?\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: It's amazing. Everyone's given a helping hand. It's - you would never think such people would come out of the woodworks and help out. Everyone, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , everyone. We're getting food trucks from all over the place. Barbecue trucks coming in from Alabama feeding people, knowing that everyone's hurting. It's just - it's amazing how much people have been helping out Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jacob, how is your daughter doing and how is your wife doing?\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: My daughter is fine for the most part. She still has friends that have passed. My wife is the one that's really affected because she was right there by the shooter. The shooter bypassed her room and shut up the rooms on the opposite side and my wife lost some of her close friends.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jacob, if I had been you and I had gotten the same call, I would have done the exact same thing that you did. Probably not as well as you did it, but I would have done the exact same thing that you did.\par \par \uc2 And I'm thankful that you ran into that building, maybe other parents could have run in and help. It's could have, should have, would have on everything,. But I'm thankful that you ran in and did what you could. And our prayers are just so strong and fervent for the entire community. And we just - I mean, there are no words. But please extend our concern and our prayers to your wife and to all those around you. And thank you for your work for the Border Patrol as well.\par \par \uc2 ALBARADO: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Up next. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has Americans feeling more worried now than during the height of COVID, Yes. Plus, could something uniquely terrible about California go nationwide? What is that? I'm going to explain it in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Long road trips, remember those? Beach time, visiting your family, your friends. Summer's supposed to be a break when American families get to go on vacation after a very hectic school year. But now with gas pricing more than double what it cost on Election Day and 2020, recession lurking, grocery bill surging, Americans are understandably freaking out.\par \par \uc2 Now, a new survey by Gallup shows just how worried Americans are. Only 14 percent rate the economy as excellent or good. Who are those 14? 39 percent say, it's fair; 46 percent say poor.\par \par \uc2 Now, just to put that into perspective, only 32 percent rated the economy as poor when everything was shut down at the height of COVID. That was in April of 2020. Now, think about all the peace and prosperity that America enjoyed back in the summer of 2019 during Trump. We had low gas prices, high consumer and investor optimism, rising wages, no