ave veered off their core missions of stable prices reining in inflation and maximizing employment. Those are their missions. The Treasury Department's core mission is to make sure that the U.S. economy is strong and healthy. And you know what, frankly, Treasury and the Fed have fallen down on all of those jobs, and the people suffering the most are the American people.\par \par \uc2 We have been screaming for well over a year that inflation was taking root, that it was going to get deeply entrenched, and then it was going to be incredibly painful for the American people, but also incredibly painful to dislodge inflation, as we saw and the early 1980s. This is a very painful process to get rid of it once it takes root. And now we are in this situation, you have the president, the treasury secretary, and the chairman of the Fed all saying whoops-a-daisy, our bad. We told you for a year it was going to be transitory, but guess not. No there's no relief in sight.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Monica, we said this months ago. Brian Deese, the council of economic advisers, he should be gone. I don't know why we had to hear from him today. Yellen, I would not hire her to cat-sit, let alone run the Treasury Department, OK. She should be gone. And Powell gets reupped as chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . So there is no accountability for completely - sorry -- screwing over the American pillared people. Zero accountability. If you're a basketball coach you would have been fired.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: That's right. There is only accountability, Laura, if you're a Republican or a conservative or President Trump. If you're a Democrat in office, all you do is fail up, and you fail up because in large part you've got the protection of the press, their Pretorian Guard, that just covers for them and makes excuses for them and doesn't cover these stories the way of the American people are experiencing them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No one has lost their job.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: I look at the Treasury -- no one has lost their job. I look at what Treasury and the Fed do almost every day, Laura. They have veered off onto climate change and social justice experiments and things like that rather than focusing on keeping the United States economy on an even keel.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, we know there's no accountability. There must be accountability, and it can't wait another moment, but we won't hold our breath there. Monica, thank you.\par \par \uc2 OK, here is the clue for tonight's wit's end segment that is going to be revealed at the end of the show. What theatrical fashion choice has the CCP upset? Tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle. We'll see if you get it right at the end of the show.\par \par \uc2 Biden gets some mental help from an unlikely source. Maybe he needs it. Raymond Arroyo has the details, "Seen and Unseen" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we unpack the stories behind the headlines. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, Biden has a pretty tough midterm season ahead with, I guess it's three quarters of American saying that we are on the wrong track as a country.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Meanwhile, Laura, his approval numbers are lower than Trump's. A new NBC report reveals, quote, "Biden is rattled by his sinking approval ratings. Biden is unhappy about a pattern that has developed inside the West Wing. He makes a clear succinct statement only to have aides rush to explain that he actually meant something else. The so-called cleanup campaign, he has told adviser, undermines him and smothers the authenticity that fueled his ride" -- or "rise" rather.\par \par \uc2 Now, that clear and succinct authenticity, Laura, was on display today when the president met with the New Zealand prime minister.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Prime Minister, welcome to the White House. We last see each other -- it's a pleasure to see you in person. I understand that your leadership has taken on a critical role on this global stage. And you know, and we want to be, I want to work with you on that effort. You know, the history goes back a long way, a long way. And I want to, by the way, I recognize New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh. What?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I've got to tell you, this was a pitiful display. And this went on and on, we just can't run it all. But when you have a president this bound to his notes, and he still rambles incoherently like this --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It conveys such a lack of leadership and strength at a time when the world is crying out for it, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I'm also wondering why the prime minister was wearing her coat the entire time. It was like she wanted to run out.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I had the same thought. Very Columbo of her, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I know. It's like she wanted to get out of there as soon as possible. It was 95 degrees in Washington today, by the way. Maybe it was cold in that Oval.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But Biden also said he was relying on the New Zealand prime minister's guidance on climate change, gun control, and combating extremism. It's a remarkable thing to see the U.S. seek the guidance of a country that locked down their population for years, outlawed guns, and has an economy in full collapse, but here we are, Laura. And it's disturbing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You forgot one thing. And is poised to be a puppet of China. So that's all good stuff for Biden.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. Well, and he's complaining about the staff rushing in and kind of changing things around or reframing what he just said. But when you have a president that is doing this kind of threatening war with China over Taiwan, threatening to go to war with Russia, they've got to clean this up. It's a matter of national security. And here we are. And as you noted for months, Laura, when the president runs into policy difficulties, he calls in the celebrities. When Biden was having trouble getting young people to take the COVID vaccine, he gave Olivia Rodrigo a driver's license to promote the shots. Now he wants to highlight his work to stem Asian hate crimes. So he met with the boy band BTS today at the White House, so it only stand to reason given Biden's cognitive challenges that he would turn to another celebrity for assistance. Poor Selena Gomez got roped into a White House visit on healthcare, or mental care, that at times felt more like a Biden intervention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you for all you are doing to lift the burden off of people who have problems with their mental health, who feel lost and not sure what to do, and sometimes they are ashamed to acknowledge they have anything that's bothering them.\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, U.S. FIRST LADY: It doesn't matter what age you are, I think mental health is really a crucial issue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It starts with people standing up, telling their stories, whether they are soldiers in the military, leaders like the president and the first lady. We want people to not suffer in silence.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, well, I guess it's OK, OK to ask for help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It really is OK to ask for help, even if you get angry when aides occasionally correct your misstatements, or snap at the gentlest queries from reporters, help is always available. I guess that's a good message. I'm not sure, Laura, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the best pitchman for mental health, but there he is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the whole thing was like a cry for help. It was a cry for production help, they need better music underscoring that, Raymond. That was the worst music. Selena Gomez looks like she would rather be back with, wasn't it Justin Bieber. Who did she date? I forgot. One of those cutie pies.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, as I watch this, the thing that kept coming to mind is what other celebrities is he going to call on? When he looks at the Hispanic numbers, I guess J-Lo is going to make an appearance. And night the Biden administration just asked an appeals court to allow them unilaterally to impose a mask mandate on air travelers. They're trying to get that mask mandated place tonight. So he may call \uc2 Tom Cruise\uc2  before this is over, Laura. We'll check in.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The mask mandate is back, unbelievable. Raymond, Republican.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't watch us live, set your series record on your DVR so you don't miss any episodes of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE every weeknight at 10:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 But up next, two-tiered justice on display in a D.C. circuit courtroom today, plus, whatever happened to that leak at the Supreme Court. We have a breaking update on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SUSSMANN\uc2 , FORMER HILLARY CLINTON CAMPAIGN LAWYER: I told the truth to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the jury clearly recognized that with their unanimous verdict today. I'm grateful to the members of the jury for their careful and thoughtful service. Despite being falsely accused, I'm relieved that justice ultimately prevailed in my case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Only in Washington does a sleazy Clinton lawyer admit he lied to the feds, get off scot-free, and then gloat about it to a cheering press corp.\par \par \uc2 I want to recap what transpired at this trial. Sussmann's own defense was structured around the fact that he lied to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about being a Democrat operative, but since the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knew that he was lying, it was no harm, no foul. Outside of Sussmann's malfeasance, remember, this was the trial that we learned that Hillary Clinton approved of the dissemination of that disinformation that was meant to undermine our trust in the presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 So how could this courtroom be so easily duped? One of the jurors is the parent of a classmate of Sussmann's daughter. Get this, the judge is married to disgraced former \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent \uc2 Lisa Page\uc2 's defense attorney. So the next time you hear all the caterwauling from the elite media in the political class about the tragic and misguided trust in D.C. officials, look no further than this predictable travesty.\par \par \uc2 And four weeks after the leak of \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's draft opinion on the Roe versus Wade case, officials of the court announced unprecedented measures are going to be taken to find the person or persons response for that leak. According to CNN, law clerks are being asked to provide cellphone records and sign affidavits. Some clerks are apparently so alarmed over the move, particularly the request for private cell data, that they have begun exploring whether to hire outside counsel.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Carrie Severino, president of Judicial Crisis Network, also former clerk, as am I, for Justice Thomas. Carrie, it seems like this took four weeks.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, FORMER THOMAS SUPREME COURT CLERK: It's kind of funny because those are exactly some of the things we were saying right off the bat. Hey, let's look at cell phones, let's get affidavits, because I'm really glad to hear they're doing the affidavits, because I think that's key.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why is that critical, Carrie?\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: If they swear under penalty of perjury that they didn't have something to do with this, and then later it turns out they did, that in itself, regardless of whether there's underlying crimes there, that in of itself would constitute a federal crime. And what's worrying me is we are getting toward the end of the term. They should have been fired weeks ago, and they still haven't been. But even if then this isn't resolved finally until after they're gone, that is a clear consequence they are going to face on top of hopefully a lot of --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Beyond that, the person who did this, or persons who did this, is still at the court, potentially compromising other cases or doing something else that is untoward or unethical. By the way, Joan Biskupic, the CNN reporter who broke the story, said this about law clerks looking to hire attorneys. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOAN BISKUPIC, CNN LEGAL ANALYST: If they obtain counsel, will new suspicions come to them. But as we all know, our lives are on the cell phones. This has nothing to do with, from their point of view, whether they had talked to journalist, but rather all the kinds of personal information, images, data, texts, that are on our personal cell phones.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Reaction to that? Would they be worried? Do they think the marshal's office is interested in looking at their photos? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Photo stream. I would hope the marshal's office, it sounded for me, and Joan would probably know better since she's doing the reporting on this, that we're talking about a list of who have your called? And that doesn't include your apps, your photo stream, et cetera, everything. But again, I am just relieved that we are finally getting to this. It is so important, and for John Roberts' own legacy, to have this now resolved is just outrageous. This person needs to be brought to justice. You don't want to leave and get like a $400,000 clerk bonus for someone who has undermined that whole institution, because just imagine you can remember how crazy this time of year is anyway.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's so busy.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: And now they've got this where you don't know who you can trust going on at the same time. It's really undermining.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I've got to say, since this happened, I haven't heard anything out of the court. Even people who are worried, I haven't heard one thing -- not that I ever hear anything -- but I haven't even heard that they're worried or their upset, or people -- I haven't heard anything. Anything. So maybe it scared people sufficiently.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: I hope they are scared straight, because we can't have our nation's highest court where you can't trust that this is going to remain confidential. That is a major part of the process working effectively, and I worry that it's going to take a long time before you have trust --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It never would have happened under Justice Rehnquist, and it did not under the chief that I clerked under. Carrie, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Our "Wit's End" segment is next, but remember, here is the clue. Wet theatrical fashion choice has the CCP very upset? Hurry up, tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle. We'll see if you get it right, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What theatrical fashion choice has the CCP upset? Chuck tweeted "A ban on platform shoes?" Janna wrote "Masks." What? Howard guessed "Tight pants." Here is the big reveal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No turning back now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Having any fun yet?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, a lot of you actually got this one right because "Top Gun" added the Taiwanese flag back on Maverick's jacket after originally taking it off in previous trailers. Thank goodness. And guess what, they smashed Memorial Day weekend records. I haven't seen it yet. I'm going to. Let that be a lesson to Hollywood. Don't go woke, because you might go broke. Fantastic movie by all accounts. The 80s were awesome.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally, remember, get USA made Freedom Matters gear @IngrahamAngle. Proceeds for this month, last today, Shoreline Soup Kitchens and Pantries. Gutfeld next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220601ei5v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Crime Fears; Will Any Gun Measures Pass in Congress?; Border Surge; Supreme Court Leak Investigation; Uvalde Massacre Investigation Continues; President Biden Meets with Federal Reserve Chairman\b0\par\par\uc2 Dan Springer, David Lee Miller, Aishah Hasnie, Griff Jenkins,  Hillary Vaughn, Jeff Paul, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,373 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: President Biden sitting down with the Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at the White House today, the president saying inflation is a top priority, while defending the Fed's independence.\par \par \uc2 The meeting coming just weeks after President Biden appointed Powell to a second term, as just the White House is making a push for more spending.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, and this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Hillary Vaughn is at the White House with the very latest -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president today says the Fed is a very big part of his plan to fight inflation. But even though the president was face to face with the Fed chair today, he says that he is still staying hands off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My plan is to address inflation. It starts with a simple proposition. Respect the Fed. Respect the Fed's independence, which I have done and will continue to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Republicans say even Biden's fix for inflation, though, raising interest rates, isn't an economic cure without its own side effects.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): It's put \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  in a box. They're going to have to raise interest rates. And when they raise interest rates, that makes it harder to buy a home.\par \par \uc2 That's going to usually make the stock market go down, as we have seen with the last few rate increases, and that's going to affect people's 401(k). So \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  doesn't have a lot of options now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Republicans say the best option they think to get inflation under control is to get government spending under control.\par \par \uc2 But the White House today is not second-guessing any past spending.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Do you guys think that any part of inflation this year is because of President Biden's spending plans, or is it all Putin's fault?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, what I can say is, we are -- and Brian just spoke to this. We are at a historic place when it comes to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Now we're going to a place where it's -- we're going into transition, where we're going to see an economy that's more stable, that's more steady. So that's because of the American Rescue Plan that we -- that the president signed into law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Charles, more spending could be on the way, because Senator Joe Manchin is reportedly open to a skinny version of Build Back Better and is in negotiations with Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  over a clean and climate energy package, about $300 billion in tax incentives for clean energy, with the caveat that it is paid for, and that it pays off some of the debt.\par \par \uc2 But to do that, that would mean tax hikes -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hillary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the president vowing to get inflation under control, but will more government spending actually make matters worse?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, market watcher Larry Glazer.\par \par \uc2 Larry, it's -- listen, we have got here -- listen, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  pumped in trillions of dollars. Both administrations pumped in trillions of dollars, although many say that last almost $2 trillion, it's what sparked this runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 LARRY GLAZER, MAYFLOWER ADVISORS: You know, I'm sure, when the Fed chairman and the president met, they addressed the elephant in the room. And that's that 40-year high in inflation.\par \par \uc2 It's creating a crushing burden on working families. And I'm sure the Fed chairman reminded the president he needs to remain independent, can't appear political. But I'm sure he also reminded the president that $30 trillion worth of national debt isn't the Fed's fault, right?\par \par \uc2 It's Washington's fault. And more of that spending is creating more inflation, which is creating more volatility in the economy, higher gas prices, higher food prices, gas up more than 50 percent since last year on Memorial Day, lower 401(k)s and also lower president's approval rating.\par \par \uc2 So there are certain tools that are the Washington tools, certain tools that are the Fed's tools. Unfortunately, it's not fair to put Washington's problems on the Fed, because \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 's crystal ball is as cloudy as Washington's. He puts his pants on one leg at a time.\par \par \uc2 When he went to "Top Gun," the movie, this weekend, he knew how expensive those flights were. Everyone's complaining to him about high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: And he can't do anything about it if Washington keeps spending.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Larry, also, what really worries me is the notion that the Fed maybe might make a convenient scapegoat if we do trip into recession. Some people believe we're already in one, especially if you own a small business.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, I don't see where the White House has pivoted at all. Are you shocked that, even during that press conference, the White House remaining steadfast that nothing is -- they have done has contributed to this inflation crisis?\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: You know, Charles, it is pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 And if you look back 40 years ago, when we saw inflation at these levels, there was another Fed chairman in place. And he said to the president, if you let him do what I need to do to beat inflation, you will be a one-term president. And, sure enough, that was the case.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that's the conversation that's going on in the White House today. i think everybody's got to take some personal responsibility. There is a consequence to spending money excessively, and families are seeing it at the grocery store and the gas pump.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, they're the ones paying for Washington's access. We have $90,000 worth of debt per person that we are paying for every day and we're going to inherit to the next generation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: And that is a burden that keeps on giving.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Larry, stand by, because we have got Austan Goolsbee. He's going to join now, former \uc2 chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  under President Obama.\par \par \uc2 Austan, again...\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, FORMER CHAIRMAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... a lot of people -- a lot of people are concerned that extra spending, that extra money, listen, on one hand, you get these incredible accomplishments that you get to brag about.\par \par \uc2 But on the other hand, you have got to live with the idea that economics 101, too much money chasing too few goods might have sparked something that we can get out of now.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, the only thing I would say about that is you're seeing highest inflation in 40 years in Germany, in Japan, in U.K. in a bunch of places where they didn't pass the stimulus that they passed in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: So we do got to weigh off how much of this is from too much demand, but how much of this is coming from too little supply.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, with that in mind, I'm glad you brought that up, because, with that in mind, those other niches don't have something else in common with us, the amount of crude oil under our feet and the ability to get to it.\par \par \uc2 If we really wanted to, we could drive oil prices into the dirt. So is the White House sacrificing the middle class right now for a greater agenda of climate change?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, my take on what you're calling the climate change agenda is, that has nothing to do with why the price of oil worldwide went to $125 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, no, no, no, the price of oil in America. I'm not caring -- I don't care about the world right now, Austan.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It's a world commodity, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm saying, it is a world commodity, but we could drill more.\par \par \uc2 There's a ton of it under our feet.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: OK, but we're...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: If we unleashed our industry, would not the -- would not oil prices go down?\par \par \uc2 You're saying we can't bring oil prices down in America? You know we can, Austan. Come on.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, we're going to set the record for oil production in the United States this year, and last year was the second highest year that we have ever had.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And we could do even more, if we really cared about Americans...\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: And we could do more, and it would have a modest...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Raising than taxes -- rather than raising taxes -- are we going to raise taxes? Are you going to try to raise -- is the Biden administration's on actually trying to raise taxes in the midst of all of this?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I mean, they -- you have seen them say they want tax relief for middle-income people, and they would like high-income people to pay more. Do you consider that a tax increase?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A tax increase is a tax increase.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring Larry Glazer in.\par \par \uc2 Larry, again you're watching this every day from a market perspective. You see the damage that's going on. Now, to a degree, listen, the Fed wants it. I guess the White House accepts that we have to bring in the so-called wealth effect.\par \par \uc2 And it is really crushing the housing market. That's what I'm really concerned about, because more Americans have homeownership than own stocks. If they tinker with this thing, I mean, how bad could it get if we really - - listen, you want to cool housing prices, but what if we derail them?\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: You know, Charles a year ago, the Fed talked about inflation in a transitory capacity. And we all know that wasn't the case.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is much worse than they expected. We also know that excessive government spending contributes to high prices. And that's something that we're seeing at the pump. We're seeing -- and it's also bad policy contributes to higher prices.\par \par \uc2 So it's a rough combination. It's a toxic recipe that we're dealing with. The financial markets are becoming unnerved because they have lost confidence in Washington's ability to navigate this. They want to see policy. They want to see agenda and they want to see a plan. And they haven't seen that from Washington.\par \par \uc2 And they have certainly haven't seen it from the Fed either. So I think everybody's trying to restore confidence to the process. They're failing. And, as a result, American families are paying the price and they're suffering the burden under this.\par \par \uc2 Look, more than 50 percent of families live paycheck to paycheck in this country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: That's an awfully tough situation to be and when you have gas -- wheat is up 40 percent. You have got gas up 50 percent and you have got rent with the highest increase on record.\par \par \uc2 That's not a working family agenda. We need to change that agenda to support working families, because they're the ones that pay the burden right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, yes, we have got a full-screen right now, Austan, of gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 Again, the president released crude from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. That did nothing to curb it. In fact, all we do now is we have less supply in case there's a real emergency. Other than pointing the finger at oil companies, is there a plan to bring prices down?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don't speak for the administration. It strikes me that, for sure, the war in Ukraine and the massing of troops, which they announced that he was massing troops in December of last year, that marks the spot where the prices start going through this roof.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, the spot was marked on the day after the election.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices went up the next day after the election, when Biden won, and they have never stopped going up, because it's -- the markets are smart.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, that's not true, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know the market is looking ahead.\par \par \uc2 When the president of the United States-elect says, I'm going to destroy the oil companies, the crude prices go up, and they haven't stopped coming down.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: That's not what he said.\par \par \uc2 Prices went up and then came down about almost 25 percent until -- in December. December 12, I think, or 11, is when they announced they were massing troops in Ukraine. And then it...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Are you confident, Austan, that we will be able to skirt recession, that we will be able to get by without going into an official recession?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I'm not confident.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of that depends on what happens with the virus. If we get a resurgence of a new variant of the virus, I think for sure we'd have a recession. And I'm not sure. If the Fed overtightens, I think we could also have a recession. So I'm not confident.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Charles, I would add that families are far more concerned about inflation right now than they are about COVID in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I think, if you look at -- China has been offline. Their demand has been taken out of the global energy market.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: When they come back online, it is only going to drive energy prices higher.\par \par \uc2 The biggest concern right now is demand destruction. If you slow the economy because people can't afford to drive, they can't afford to buy things, it is going to lead to a slowing economy. That's not a recipe you want to end with at the end of the summer.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. I think that's true.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me give you the last word, Austan.\par \par \uc2 We did have some news out that Senator Manchin may be receptive to a so- called skinny version of Build Back Better, I don't think that would include tax -- adjustments in taxes. I don't know that he's been favorable to that. But he wants some sort of payoff.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is that -- again, the additional spending, when a lot of folks on Wall Street are saying it's $2.5 trillion right now in excess of cash sitting around that's exacerbating the recession and inflation issue, is that the right medicine at this time?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Well, I feel like that's kind of mixing a few things.\par \par \uc2 If you thought that excess spending is what caused the inflation, all that matters is the delta from last year when you're doing the accounting. And so it's not going to go up $2 trillion from what it was last year. Demand is going down. We are on path to have the biggest decrease in the deficit in the history of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So it's -- spending is falling, not going up. The Build Back Better argument is not really about inflation, because that's a bill that is spread over 10 years and is paid for.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: So I kind of think that's a -- that's a red herring. We should argue about that on its own merits.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, let's give some props to the Trump taxes for that deficit going down. We are bringing in records amount of money on the tax side, on the tax side.\par \par \uc2 When President Trump's tax bill went through...\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I think you should go check the numbers, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... we were told that corporations and rich people would be taxed less.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: No, Charles, you need to go check the numbers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And we're setting record after record on receipts.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, I love talking to you, Austan, but we're out of time, my man. Please, let's do it again real soon.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Good to see you again, man.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And I hope all of this gets better for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 Larry, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: By the way, let's take a quick look at the corner of Wall and Broad, stocks limping to end this month, after seeing a big rally last week. It wasn't enough, though, for May to get into the green, although the Dow and S&P were up slightly, the Nasdaq falling 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to go live to Uvalde, Texas, for the latest in the investigation into that school shooting just one week ago today.\par \par \uc2 Plus, why is the Justice Department now getting involved?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, it's been one week since the horrific school massacre in Uvalde, Texas, and devastated parents are searching for answers as they mourn their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 Now the Justice Department saying it's investigating why officers at the scene waited almost an hour to storm the classroom where the gunman barricaded himself.\par \par \uc2 To Jeff Paul, who's live on the ground in Uvalde -- Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, as this memorial behind us here at the city center continues to grow, this community is just beginning to say goodbye to the 21 people who were killed last week.\par \par \uc2 And they will continue to do so for the next two weeks. And they -- there really won't be a day until mid-June, where there won't be some sort of visitation or memorial service, one of the first ones today being of that of Amerie Jo Garza, just one of the 19 kids killed at Robb Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 And at her memorial service, she is being remembered not only as an honor roll student, but for her bravery. Her family says she was one of the several students who tried calling police during the attack.\par \par \uc2 Then there's Maite Rodriguez, who's also being remembered today, giving, charismatic, courageous, intelligent, just some of the words her family uses to describe their little angel, an anime watcher whose favorite color was green, wore Converse every single day, and loved all animals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LYDIA CARRASCO, UVALDE, TEXAS, RESIDENT: The consolation that the parents can happen is that their angel is in heaven, and it's a good place for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now, while the community is incredibly sad, they're also increasingly getting angry over the handling of this shooting.\par \par \uc2 We know the Department of Justice is now going to be reviewing law enforcement response, especially why it took more than 45 minutes for officers to get inside that classroom and kill the shooter.\par \par \uc2 There's also talks, Charles, from lawmakers here about knocking down the school, Robb Elementary School, at least the structure, and building a new one in time for next school year in the fall -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you, Jeff, very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to get right to former Deputy Assistant Attorney General John Yoo.\par \par \uc2 And, John, what will the DOJ be looking for in this investigation?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Charles, it's interesting what Justice is doing here.\par \par \uc2 They're not conducting a criminal investigation. They're not looking into, as we see in other kinds of situations, whether police used force illegally. Instead, what they're doing is they're operating through a relatively obscure office of the Justice Department that's involved with grants-making to local police departments.\par \par \uc2 And what they're going to do who is, they're going to try to do what we call an after-action report. They're going to look at what happened, what mistakes were made, try to get the facts on the ground straight, because we're still seeing lots of conflicting stories about when police were on the scene, when they could have gone into the school. Why did they wait so long to get a Border Patrol unit there?\par \par \uc2 And then maybe we can learn some lessons. Hopefully, there will be recommendations about what can be done to stop these terrible school shootings.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, what makes it really tougher, also, Chief Pete Arredondo, in December of last year took a course at Southwest University, a junior college, school barricaded. It's on this sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 He also took the same course in August 25 of 2020. And then, on June 10 of 2019, he took a 16-hour course on terrorism response, with a specific focus on active shooter.\par \par \uc2 Golly, it's -- he was -- he had the preparation. So where do you go from there? If the police chief has taken all of these courses, he has been entrusted with this job, I mean, where do we go from here as a society who we want to send our kids and grandkids to school and feel safe?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Charles, it's a tough question we all should wrestle with.\par \par \uc2 I think -- looking backwards on it, I think the chief made a mistake when he decided that the situation had moved from an active shooter, when you do want to get people in the building and rush into the rooms and try to stop the shooting, and had moved to someone who was no longer a threat to the children, was more barricaded inside the room and would be harder to stop.\par \par \uc2 I think, clearly, now we know that was a mistake, because you were getting phone calls from the children in the classroom with the -- with Ramos, the shooter, still shooting.\par \par \uc2 So we can look back and say he made an error. Now, whether he knew, the sheriff -- the police chief knew all those calls were being made, all that information was available, something that the Justice Department will find out.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what we can do here, obviously, we can spend more money training officers on these kinds of things. I also think we could spend more time talking about how to secure schools. And, of course, we're going to have to look at tougher types of gun control, but a gun control that's consistent with the Second Amendment right to -- that all Americans have to keep and bear arms.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Is there any way to test, I guess, for heart?\par \par \uc2 I mean, just I hate to say it, but, again, training is fine. It's fantastic. We need training, I think, more police training with police officers throughout the country, because almost every time we see any kind of sort of incident on tape, it's during the arrest process.\par \par \uc2 I have always said we need more there. We have always talked about the criminal side of this, but how do we test for heart? How do we know, when the time comes, the folks that we have entrusted to take care of us and that we put on a high pedestal because of that will answer the call? There's just no way, huh?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Charles, you're right.\par \par \uc2 The best we can do, I think, is hire people with experience who have gone through this before. This police chief did have a long history in law enforcement, but he hadn't had to face this kind of threat.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOO: And maybe that's what we have to do, unfortunately, maybe look to people who've been military veterans, unfortunately, who have had to face fire under these kinds of circumstances. Maybe those are the best people we could look to for leadership and help in these terrible circumstances.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: John, thank you very much. Always a pleasure talking to you.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, folks, we will be right back right after this.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Coming up: a major update in the search for the Supreme Court leaker. We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 Plus, thieves ransacking a Sephora, as shoppers and employees look on and disbelief. What will it take to stop -- to put a stop to this?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now to that Supreme Court leak investigation that's heating up.\par \par \uc2 Some clerks looking to lawyer up after being asked to turn over cell phone records.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former DOJ prosecutor Jim Trusty.\par \par \uc2 Jim, it's good to hear see that there's movement on this? Where are we right now with the process?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, it's a great question.\par \par \uc2 It seems like we're in a little bit of an ice age for this investigation. I'm worried that the Supreme Court police department is a little overmatched, because they typically focus on security, not on investigative-type material.\par \par \uc2 So I'm a little nervous that it's so quiet. What they're talking about now is trying to collect cell phones or cell phone information, at the very least, from each of the clerks. Remember, each justice has about four clerks. So it's a pretty good pool of people.\par \par \uc2 And we're starting to hear some rumblings that the law clerks or some of the law clerks are considering getting counsel, rather than volunteering any of their personal data to the investigators.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What's interesting to me is that whoever is the leaker was lauded as a hero by many on the left, and that maybe this person, he or she, hasn't been able -- hasn't been persuaded to just out themselves.\par \par \uc2 They're not facing -- would they be facing any real criminal liability?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, I think it's unlikely.\par \par \uc2 I mean -- and you're right. One of my biggest concerns the day this story broke was that they'd be treated as a heroic whistle-blower or a resistance figure, which is really nonsensical. They violated a serious moral code when it comes to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 On the criminal side, it's kind of thin. And that might be one reason why the Supreme Court hasn't fully engaged with law enforcement like the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , because you're really looking for hypertechnicalities when it comes to some sort of theft defense or some sort of intellectual property misuse.\par \par \uc2 There's plenty of stuff that can be wrong that's not necessarily criminally wrong. And this might fall into that category.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the notion, though, that this is the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , that the public has to feel extraordinarily confident?\par \par \uc2 It's already been politicized. It's being ripped apart in the media. And for this to go unpunished, or for than not to be able to find the leaker, wouldn't that do almost irreparable harm to the -- to the -- I'm looking for the right word, but we need the...\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Oh, to the institution, right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... Supreme Court to be able to be that third branch of the government, right?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, it would be -- look, it's already a historic low, from my perspective.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: To have someone break that moral code for political purposes is outrageous.\par \par \uc2 And what I would hope to see -- it's a little bit like a book called "The Blooding" that was about the advent of DNA testing in a little English village in 1983, where everyone lined up, all the men lined up in a murder- rape investigation, and said, take my blood, test this stuff for this new thing called DNA testing, and let's eliminate me from contention. Let's figure out who actually committed this crime.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to see the Supreme Court do the same thing. Those clerks should be lining up, saying, hey, if you want to look at my cell phone, God bless, hurry up and eliminate me, so you can find the person that did something terribly wrong to this institution and to the court.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, with respect to these cell phone records, are these clerks and any other potential suspects, are they obligated to get -- hand over their phones?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: They're probably not obligated unless they got the phones as part of the job.\par \par \uc2 A lot of times, we find out, usually way downstream, that phones that were being used by people like, say, Lisa Page and Peter Strzok at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  were actually government-issued phones. And so you don't have the same privacy right. You have no ability to stop the government from looking at the contents of the phone if they gave it to you.\par \par \uc2 So that's one division. But if it's their personal phones, there's a real good chance that they could fight it until there's sufficient evidence to convince a court that a seizure warrant is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 So, it's uphill, but you want to get people to actually act out of a sense of obligation to the institution, rather than fighting you in court.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Great point.\par \par \uc2 And then, no matter what side of the political aisle you're on, I think we need -- we need to find out how this happened and make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 Jim, thank you very much appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Absolutely. Sure. Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Brazen robbery caught on camera at an L.A. Sephora store. Are lax crime policies to blame?\par \par \uc2 But, first, no break for border agents over the holiday weekend. Why are crossings surging, despite temperatures that are doing the same?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The border surge showing no signs of slowing down, even as we enter the dangerous summer months.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Griff Jenkins is in La Joya, Texas, with very latest -- Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 That heat here in South Texas is just simply oppressive, the heat index 107 degrees, creating a dangerous situation for the migrants, who also have to go through brush that have rattlesnakes and other hazards.\par \par \uc2 And Senator Roger Marshall, a Republican, got a firsthand look at that at the beginning of the Memorial Day weekend. Take a look at this video. This is Senator Marshall on a recent border trip. And he was having to leap into his other job, which is a doctor by trade, treating a migrant woman who is suffering from heat exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, this morning, we saw that the numbers are not slowing down because of the heat. All day long, we saw right here in La Joya groups coming across. You can see in this video these are migrants from Honduras, Guatemala, El Salvador, Nicaragua, Costa Rica, and Peru.\par \par \uc2 CBP sources tell us, Charles, that, over this Memorial Day weekend, there were 4,084 encounters. And that is just in the Rio Grande Valley Sector alone. Now, last week, the Border Patrol chief, Raul Ortiz, tweeting this three-day cap about activity along the entire border with 131 pounds of fentanyl, 26 pounds of heroin, 13 pounds of meth, along with 10 sex offenders apprehended, and one assassination suspect.\par \par \uc2 Now, here in just the RGV, there were four gang members arrested over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Senator -- or Governor of Arkansas Asa Hutchinson says it shows the cartels are in control.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Cartels are controlling the border, vs. law enforcement. And we have got to go after and build cooperation going after reducing the influence of the cartels. We have done that before. Mexico, I think, can step up to the plate to a much greater extent than they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And, Charles, when you talk about the cartel control, here along the border, hundreds of these are found.\par \par \uc2 These are wristbands that came off of migrants this morning. And it says "entregas" in Spanish, which means deliveries. There's a four-digit number on there. It's that organized. That's how much control the cartels have here on our Southern border -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow, supply chain efficiency there.\par \par \uc2 Griff Jenkins in La Joya, Texas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol union president Brandon Judd has been on the front lines of this crisis. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 The efficiency and the brazen, just -- it's over the top right now. And it's amazing because, we're the -- we're the world's preeminent -- preeminent nation. And, obviously, we're choosing not to control our borders. We're not allowing a cartel to dominate our borders. But that's the case, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It is the case.\par \par \uc2 When you look at what's currently going on, it's all about the reward. When these people are crossing the border illegally. They know that they're going to be released into the United States. That's the reward.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we continue to reward people, the cartels are going to be able to go throughout the world. This isn't just Central, South America, Mexico. This is throughout the world. We see people from everywhere, Eastern Europe, Western Africa, from the Asian countries.\par \par \uc2 We see people from everywhere. And it's all about the catch and release. If people are going to be rewarded by being released in the United States, which is what they want -- they want to be here in the United States -- then they're going to continue to come, and the cartels are going to continue to generate billions of dollars of profit. And they're going to be able to continue to pour resources out of the field, creating gaps in their coverage to pass their higher-value products.\par \par \uc2 Charles, you and I have been speaking about this all the way going back to 2017, how these cartels worked\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: We were able to control it under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 This administration gave it completely back to the cartels. And now we have stretches of the border that are completely controlled by criminal organizations.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Brandon, I read a tweet from Congressman \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 . And it struck me. And I wanted to ask you about this.\par \par \uc2 "The threat to America is not Russia. It is our border, and we are under a sustained attack. We need a full immigration moratorium." And he goes on to say we need a deportation czar.\par \par \uc2 And it really strikes me, because we have sent billions of dollars to Ukraine, ostensibly to push back Russia. And both sides of the aisle have said, OK, we can see spending $40 billion, $50 billion, $60 billion, even perhaps more.\par \par \uc2 Why can't there be more unity in Washington, D.C., from both political parties on what \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  just said there, that this is a bigger problem for America than Russia is right now?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Because, right now, the Democrats are simply politicizing this issue. They want open borders. They want to make this what it currently is. That's why we have the situation.\par \par \uc2 This is easily controlled. This is not rocket science. This is something that we have done before. And we can control this. And we can even act within this administration's parameters, the parameters that they set early on. They're just not willing to do that either.\par \par \uc2 I have met with Secretary Mayorkas. I have told him what can be done. They refuse to do what is necessary. We could hold people in our custody, pending a deportation or asylum proceeding. They know, this administration knows that, once we start holding people, rather than releasing them, everybody will stop coming. We will then be able to control illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 And by controlling illegal immigration, we can then go after the profits of the cartels. We can go after the fentanyl. We can go after the cocaine. We can go after the criminal aliens and the aliens from special interest countries. We can protect U.S. citizens if this administration would allow it. They just won't do it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Brandon, of course, you just referenced the fentanyl and the drugs. And it's just absolutely mind-boggling just how bad that's gotten.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Griff had showed us earlier how Republican Senator Roger Marshall came to aid the migrants suffering from heat exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The senator is going to Neil on FOX Business noon tomorrow on "Coast to Coast."\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Brandon, I know we have talked about this for at least six years and longer. Keep up the hard work. It's just mind-boggling it's come to this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, this is one way to empty a shelf, I mean, literally right?\par \par \uc2 Robbers at a Los Angeles makeup store caught on tape. Is it just a case of bad people or bad policy at work? We report, you may want to hide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The massacre in Uvalde motivating both sides of the aisle in D.C. to possibly do something on gun legislation.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill with the latest -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden today pledged at the Oval Office that he will be meeting with Congress when it comes to guns and gun legislation, but, as to who he's wanting to meet and when he's going to meet them, still remains unclear at this hour.\par \par \uc2 Congress, as you know is out this week. And both chambers right now, Charles, are on different tracks when it comes to how to move forward on responding to these mass shootings. So, this morning, we learned the House Judiciary Committee will meet this Thursday, this week, to mark up a sweeping gun reform package that Democrats are calling the Protecting Our Kids Act.\par \par \uc2 It is major. It's massive. It includes measures to raise the minimum age to buy semiautomatic rifles from 18 to 21. It's going to ban new sales and possession of high-capacity ammunition magazine -- magazines, ban new sales of bump stocks, background checks for those ghost guns, and increase federal penalties for gun trafficking, and introduce -- excuse me -- new rules for keeping guns at homes when there's a minor at home.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans are already rebuking this package. Ranking Member Jim Jordan told me today that this package is -- quote -- "a hodgepodge of bills that's an infringement on people's Second Amendment rights." He also claims that none of these bills really would have prevented the tragedies that happened in Texas or in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are bipartisan negotiations, though, under way in the Senate. GOP Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has tasked Senator Cornyn to broker a deal with the Democrats. They're holding a Zoom meeting this afternoon to come up with an agreement on a basic framework with talks that are centered more on mental health and those red flag laws.\par \par \uc2 Now, McConnell, though, was asked about the negotiations today. He remained tight-lipped about whether he's heard of any progress yet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): We have a group led by Senator Cornyn and Senator Murphy on the Democratic side discussing how we might be able to come together to target the problem, which is mental illness and school safety.\par \par \uc2 We will get back at it next week and hope to have a result.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, the key words there, Charles, next week, which is likely when we're -- might see a meeting with the president as well here with Congress -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Aishah, before I let you go, where does Speaker Pelosi stand right now?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Good question.\par \par \uc2 She was just at an event taking some questions. And, of course, gun legislation came up. Of course, Democrats would love to see more expanded background checks. That would be a huge win for Democrats. That's something that the House is definitely pushing.\par \par \uc2 But you have to remember that we're talking about a 50/50 Senate. And even Senators \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  and Blumenthal have already admitted that that is likely not to happen. What's most likely to happen, what they can actually get across the finish line are those red flag laws.\par \par \uc2 And Speaker Pelosi has previously -- previously said in her dear colleagues letter that she supports those bipartisan negotiations happening in the Senate right now.\par \par \uc2 So, really, we're centered on those red flaw laws -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Aishah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, yet another brazen robbery caught on video. Are soft-on-crime policies to blame?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Crime ramping up in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Newly released videos showing the moment a man was randomly stabbed on the Upper East Side Thursday, just one of many recent incidents caught on camera.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' David Lee Miller is live from New York with more -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, it's a video that has left many New Yorkers feeling angry and afraid.\par \par \uc2 A surveillance camera recording shows an attacker with a large knife chase down a 29-year-old man outside a subway station in a mostly residential section of Manhattan. What, if anything, led to the attack, we do not know.\par \par \uc2 The recording shows the attacker, face concealed, getting off an e-bike. He then chases the victim into the street. Police say the victim did not know the person, who repeatedly slashed him in the back and arm. Cops hope the release of the video will lead to an arrest.\par \par \uc2 Police are also asking for the public's help and locating to people who assaulted a 42-year-old man Friday afternoon on the platform of a Brooklyn subway station. Surveillance camera video here shows the victim, whose face has been blurred, being struck numerous times by two men that are now being sought by police.\par \par \uc2 A motive is not clear. An earlier incident may have led to the assault. New York's Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who is under increasing pressure to stop the city's crime surge, met today with other mayors in the state to address gun violence. The mayor called on help from Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: There must be a national approach to this. It must be national solutions that includes social media and how it is not using its communication ability to detect those who are being radicalized, and eventually carrying out radical actions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Only hours before Adam spoke, police say a 29-year-old man was shot in the head outside a Brooklyn deli. It happened about 1:40 this morning. No details about the victim or the shooter have been released.\par \par \uc2 And over the Memorial Day weekend, gun violence, Charles, in the city, it claimed at least three lives -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: David Lee, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All this as, meantime, in California, new video capturing brazen thieves stealing bags of products from a Sephora store over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Dan Springer has those details -- Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, when you watch this video, what strikes you is just how casual these thieves are. They don't seem to be in a hurry at all.\par \par \uc2 They don't seem to, frankly, have a care in the world. It happened Saturday night around 9:00 at the Los Cerritos shopping center in Los Angeles County. The thieves were hooded sweatshirts, but didn't even try to cover their faces as they filled up garbage bags with makeup and other cosmetic products at this Sephora store.\par \par \uc2 They did it right in front of two store employees who just watched and at least one customer who recorded the whole thing on her cell phone and, of course, posted the video on TikTok. By Monday night, the video had more than 1.4 million views.\par \par \uc2 When the shoplifters filled their bags, they just walked out the door and have not been caught. The L.A. County Sheriff's Department says there were multiple robberies at the mall the same night. No word yet from Sephora on the size of the loss, but you can add it to the billions of dollars in merchandise taken in California and across the country.\par \par \uc2 The national Coalition of Law Enforcement and Retail estimates that, in 2019, thieves stole $68 billion worth of goods from retailers, $4 billion in California alone. And surveys indicate the problem has gotten much worse since then.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MICHELIN, PRESIDENT, CALIFORNIA RETAILERS ASSOCIATION: These are folks that do it constantly. These are people that are doing it over and over and over and over again, because they know that they're not going to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: The \uc2 California Retailers Association\uc2  has pushed for laws that would reinstate penalties for misdemeanor theft, things under $950, by the way, everything above that as a felony.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats control the state legislature there, and those bills have gone nowhere -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Dan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, are lax policies to blame?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  Lieutenant Joe Cardinale.\par \par \uc2 Joe, those laws they are talking about, they were removed in Proposition 47 in 2014, where a lot of those felonies became misdemeanors. I have seen videos where folks go into these sorts with calculators knowing they can steel up to a certain amount. And, essentially, nothing happens to him. I mean, how much of a mockery of the system is that?\par \par \uc2 JOE CARDINALE, FORMER \uc2 NYPD\uc2  LIEUTENANT: It's the biggest mockery we have right now, because it's a revolving door justice at its best.\par \par \uc2 And you still have the same -- the same players that are causing this on both ends in the DA's office and on -- with the criminals. And they know they're going to get away with it. They're going to keep doing it. And they're selling these goods out on the street. That's the other side of it, too.\par \par \uc2 So, when they sell them out on the street, and they sell it for a nice low price, they have no need to go back into the store, because it's the real deal. So it's a round-robin effect on the stores, on the consumers. And that's who really pays the price, is the community and the consumers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: Because it's going to reflect on them eventually.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I was shocked to just hear $68 billion, $4 billion in California alone.\par \par \uc2 And prices go up, security costs go up, or they move the stores out of the neighborhoods. It's just -- it's just a no-win situation for society. And it's mind-boggling to me that any law enforcement official or elected official would let this go on as long as they have.\par \par \uc2 I want you to connect the dots to that. Some people say, well, it's just a soft crime. In L.A., almost 400 homicides last year, a 15-year high. Is there a connection when you allow this sort of lawlessness to occur with harder crimes?\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: Absolutely, because they just graduate to the higher crimes.\par \par \uc2 And what happens if somebody tries to stop them, and they use physical force against them? That's going to be the higher crime right there. But, I mean, what's it going to take for them to understand?\par \par \uc2 I mean, security, what security? There is no security. They don't want to put security in because they don't want to challenge them. So everybody comes in unchallenged.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: So it's just ridiculous how they go along with it.\par \par \uc2 And you can thank the people that put these people in power for the elections. You need to change it. And California better wake up, because that $68 billion is going to fall flat on their laps.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Real quick, Joe, you talk about elections and outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Adams came in -- became mayor of New York City with great fanfare about fighting crime. So far, he's blamed guns, he's talking about social media, but nothing actually to deter crime.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts? You got 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 CARDINALE: You know, Charles, get back to the old school of law enforcement in New York City. Put the street crime out there. Let them get the guns off the street. Let them do the job they are paid and trained to do.\par \par \uc2 And stop with the politics that are involved with stop -- holding the police back. Enough of it already. Let the police do their job.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. I mean, that's why he was elected.\par \par \uc2 Joe, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Folks, good news. Neil's back tomorrow, but you can catch me on FOX Business. I'm there every day 2:00 p.m. on "Making Money." I'm going to try and help you as much as I can.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220601ei5v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Visits Uvalde Today to Meet with Families of Shooting Victims; Interview with Rep. Mo Brooks (R-MO); Possibility of Gun Legislation; Sen. Ben Cardin is Interviewed about Gun Legislation; Supreme Court Decisions on Abortion and Gun Control; Biden Signs Executive Action on Law Enforcement Reforms.\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, Casey Stegall, Brit Hume, Juan Williams, Kevin  Corke \par \uc2 9,163 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 President Biden headed to the scene of another mass shooting at the nation looks for answers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): Mourning and frustration in Texas where 19 precious young students and two brave teachers were gunned down at school.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am sick and tired of it. We have to act.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We'll have a live report from Uvalde, where a tight-knit community faces loss and law enforcement face questions about how this happened.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS: The information that I was given turned out in part to be inaccurate, and I'm absolutely livid about that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And gun owners, lawmakers, and former President Trump gather at an \uc2 NRA\uc2  event in nearby Houston with protesters outside.\par \par \uc2 As Washington begins yet another conversation about elusive gun reform legislation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): We're going to try to work through this and see if we can find that common ground.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We'll ask Republican Congressman Mo Brooks, who has an "A" rating from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , and is in a runoff for the Republican nomination for Senate in Alabama, about the divide over gun violence and gun rights.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the president signs an executive order on police reform, two years after the death of George Floyd, as crime across the country rises. We will ask Democratic Senator Ben Cardin about the president's moves and the deadlock in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Then, Pennsylvania's closely watched Republican Senate primary heads to a recount. We'll ask our Sunday panel about the big test for former President Trump's influence.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SMITH (on camera): And hello again from FOX News on this Memorial Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 The president and first lady are on their way to Texas days after 21 people, most of them children, were killed at an elementary school. He'll once again play consoler-in-chief to a community reeling from tremendous loss. The tragedy and other recent mass shootings is sparking renewed calls on Capitol Hill to ramp up gun control measures.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will speak with Congressman Mo Brooks, who is in a runoff for the Republican Senate nomination in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Casey Stegall. He is in Uvalde for us, where the community there is still grieving this morning -- Casey.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Indeed they are. President Biden and the first lady expected to make multiple stops, meeting with multiple victims of Tuesday's senseless act, now deemed the second deadliest school shooting in U.S. history. Nineteen children, mostly fourth-graders, and two adult teachers, were murdered in their own classroom. Police say the 18-year-old shooter managed to get inside Robb Elementary School through a door that had been propped open.\par \par \uc2 When responding officers got on scene, the incident commander decided to wait on the SWAT team instead of engaging with the gunman right away. As a result, 45-plus minutes ticked by, officers in the halls as little kids flooded 911 with pleas for help.\par \par \uc2 Until federal agents arrived, breaching the room and shot and killed the teen gunman as he emerged from hiding.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN MCCRAW\uc2 , \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2  DIRECTOR: From the benefit of hindsight, where I'm sitting now, of course, it was not the right decision. It was a wrong decision, period. There's no excuse for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Officials say the suspect legally purchased of the gun and the ammunition used at a local store days before. Right now, they are poring through his social media, searching for a possible motive.\par \par \uc2 This as the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  is holding its annual conference this weekend down in Houston, more than 270 miles from here. The \uc2 NRA\uc2  event was on the calendar prior to this tragedy. A few keynote figures chose not to attend, like the Texas governor and the Texas lieutenant governor, while Texas Senator Ted Cruz and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were in attendance.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the funerals will begin this week here in the community of this small, tight-knit community, I should say, a week that many students had looked forward to because it was the beginning of their summer vacation -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hearts are broken all over the country as we continue to see that unity grieve there.\par \par \uc2 Casey Stegall, thank you very much, reporting from Uvalde, Texas for us.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Republican congressman and Alabama Senate candidate, Mo Brooks.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome and thank you for joining us here on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. MO BROOKS (R-AL): Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So you heard just a moment ago there, Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas and some other Republican members of Congress had spoken out as well. They're suggesting that perhaps there could be some common ground found here on gun legislation in the wake of this most recent shooting.\par \par \uc2 Would you support any changes, sir, to the current gun laws on the books?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, let's, for emphasis, understand what the Second Amendment is about. The Second Amendment is designed to help ensure that we, the citizenry, always have the right to take back our government should it become dictatorial. That was a great fear of the Founding Fathers and, quite frankly, it's a fear today.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we enjoy un-infringed Second Amendment rights, then we don't really have to worry that much about the government ever becoming dictatorial. But the moment that we take from our citizenry our ability to take our government back is the moment that the ability of dictatorial forces increases to the point where perhaps they will try to implement a dictatorial government at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 So I want to protect the Second Amendment right to bear arms. The amendment is very clear. It says the right to keep and bear arms shall not be infringed. If there are proposals out there that guarantee the rights of the American people to freely exercise their Second Amendment right to bear and keep arms, then I'll consider them.\par \par \uc2 But if you're talking about depriving people of their Second Amendment right to bear arms -- well, first, it's unconstitutional. So you're going to have to address that with a constitutional amendment, and we'll see how Congress and the states react to that kind of measure.\par \par \uc2 But second, it's not the proper way to go if you want to preserve our freedoms. And I say that having been the target of an assassination attempt on the baseball field in Virginia where 170-some-odd bullets were fired over about a seven-minute period of time.\par \par \uc2 So I've been in the middle of one of these things and I was a primary target of the assassin where he had my name, my physical description in his pocket at the time he launched this attack.\par \par \uc2 What we have to do is stop the motivation that causes these criminals, these horrific individuals, to do what they do.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I want to -- I want to dig into that in just a moment, because you did say some things on that this week.\par \par \uc2 But first, to the specific gun laws in your state, sir. The minimum age in your state to buy an AR-15 like the one the school shooter used in Uvalde is 18 years old. There's no waiting period between the time that a firearm is purchased and when it's actually turned over to the buyer. There's no license for the sale of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Gallup research does find that a majority of Americans, 52 percent of them, are in favor of stricter gun laws when it comes to the sale of firearms.\par \par \uc2 So to the majority of Americans who feel that way, you say what?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, I suggest that the polling data that you have does not reflect of the opinions of the American people. I suspect that the people who were polled, by way of example, were not properly explained what the purpose of the Second Amendment right to bear arms is.\par \par \uc2 And I'll use my own history as an example. There are many times when I went to school with a shotgun in my car. Why? Because I just got through duck hunting. There other teenagers my age at that point in time that also brought their weapons to school, and they had been hunters, too, for whatever it is the hunting season was about.\par \par \uc2 Now, back when I was growing up, we didn't have these mass killings, okay? They weren't there. They didn't occur. Or if they did, I certainly was not cognizant of them and they were very, very, very rare, so rare that I cannot recall a single instance in which one of those things occurred during my youth.\par \par \uc2 Today, they are much more common. What's the big difference between when I was growing up and today? The big difference is the decline in the moral values, the decline and a respect for human life.\par \par \uc2 If we teach proper moral values, it would teach respect for human life, if we properly address mental health issues that may somehow or another be associated with all these things, then that is the way to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Okay. So, to that point, this week, you suggested just that, what you believe is behind the rising number of mass shootings in America.\par \par \uc2 You said this: It reflects poorly on liberal policies that encourage out- of-wedlock childbirth, divorce, single-parent households and amoral values that undermine respect for life. Mass killings that are common in America today were, as you just stated, when I grow up, a very rare thing. The way to prevent mass killings, you say, is to restore moral values.\par \par \uc2 I'll dig into that more in just a second, but does that statement, sir, unfairly blame single-parent households in his country for the rise of mass shootings?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : Absolutely not. It blames moral values decline in the United States of America and there are a lot of factors that have contributed to our moral decline.\par \par \uc2 By way of example, all the studies I've ever seen suggest that children who are raised with just one parent around, they don't do as well by the time they become adults. Why? Because it's almost impossible for a single parent to do the kind of job that two parents collectively can do. It's just a numbers game.\par \par \uc2 And there are a lot of single parents that do an excellent job raising their kids, but they are super parents, and I thank them for the effort that they've put forth in producing children who later become responsible adults.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, the data is very clear, those single-parent households, for whatever reason, end up resulting in children who are more likely to be on welfare, who are less likely to get the kind of grades you expect to get in school, or more likely to be involved in drugs, and unfortunately, are more likely to be involved in criminal conduct. That's just the data.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, you're --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : And that's not to say that all single parents do that, okay? That is not the data. We're talking about probabilities and tendencies and 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 percent greater probability that has an effect on society.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, restoring moral values, that's -- you're calling for glacial change there. People want to know what can be done today.\par \par \uc2 Aren't you then making the case for red flag laws that could help these communities identify someone who owns a firearm, who doesn't have respect for human life? They have been adopted in 19 states, but not Alabama. Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : Under the United States Constitution, before you can do something to this kind of individual, you have to show, one, that their mind is off a little bit, and two, that they have engaged in some kind of conduct that would warrant you taking the appropriate kind of action against them -- by way of example, to house them for their own protection or protection of others. It might be non compos mentis -- excuse me, mental capacity type of hearing. There are laws already on the books to take care of those types of issues.\par \par \uc2 The problem is identifying them and identifying them as opposed to the problem associated with misidentifying others. And there has to be a balance. And that's a real challenge for our probate courts around the country and every other governmental body or person who is responsible for determining when someone becomes a risk to themselves and to others. That's a very difficult thing to mind read.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So is it fair to say you could be, if it was written correctly, open to some sort of red flag law in your state?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : We -- I'm not talking about red flag laws. I'm talking about a person who is a danger to others that we already have laws for that are already on the books, okay?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : If you've got some kind of mental issue and you're a danger to yourself or a danger to others, then you already can be subject to court jurisdiction and the appropriate court remedies to ensure that you're no longer a danger to yourself and others. That exists now. That's been on the books in Alabama for decades, and I believe it's been on the books throughout the country for decades.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Several speakers meanwhile, sir, chose not to attend this week's \uc2 NRA\uc2  event in Texas, including Governor Greg Abbott and Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 . The \uc2 NRA\uc2  endorsed you recently.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe it was appropriate to hold that meeting in Texas?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: I'm not going to comment on the meeting in Texas because I'm not a part of it, but I very much appreciate that the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2 , Gun Owners of America, and the National Association of Gun Rights have endorsed me in this \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  race in Alabama over all other comers. That says a lot about who's going to actually fight to defend the Second Amendment right to bear arms, and in Alabama, that's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Onto the Senate primary runoff and the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You're now in a runoff in the Alabama Senate primary against Katie Britt, who I should note we did invite to join us here but she was unavailable. All this is happening after former President Trump decided to un-endorse you, sir, saying you had blown it by going, quote, woke for not talking about what he says was 2020 election fraud.\par \par \uc2 What exactly did the president ask you to do after the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: The president and I had many conversations between September 1st and his decision to un-endorse me that revolved around his being reinstated, revolved around his wanting to rescind the election.\par \par \uc2 And I can understand him wanting to do that, okay? He was robbed, in my judgment, in 2020. In his judgment, he was robbed. And so I can understand that desire.\par \par \uc2 The conflict was, when I would explain, the law does not permit us to do that. Our one shot at reversing the election, our one shot in an election contest under the United States Constitution and federal law, is on January 6th. That's it. That's the final appeal day.\par \par \uc2 So anything we do after that has to focus on winning the 2022 and 2024 elections. We can use what happened in 2020 as a motivation to try and get better election laws, to use as a motivation to win in 2022 and 2024 to save our country. But what is now done in 2020 is irreversible under the United States Constitution and the United States Code.\par \par \uc2 And I'm one of those who believes in law and order. I might not like it that that's the end game, but that is the end game.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And just to go on the record, there has been still no evidence or proof provided that there was any sort of fraud in the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Oh, no, that's wrong. I don't know why you people in the media keep saying that, but that is absolutely false. That is absolutely false. You keep saying every time but that's absolutely false.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What is false, sir?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : You had 150 congressmen and senators who absolutely disagree with you on what you just said.\par \par \uc2 So, what are you calling them? What are you calling them when you say 150 Republican senators and congressmen look at the voter fraud issue and said there was a major problem? What are you calling the Commission on Federal Election reform, a bipartisan committee with \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  on the one hand and James Baker, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , White House chief of staff on the other, back in 2005 who analyzed the systemic weaknesses, systemic flaws in our election system and warned us that elections are going to be stolen if we don't fix these problems and those problems were not fixed?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : So I don't know who's telling you there's no evidence, but that tells me you haven't done your homework. I'm sorry, but that's the way it is.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The courts and the judges -- the courts and the judges that have -- some inside the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: No, no, the courts -- time out. Don't go into that. Don't go into that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. I want to finish up --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : Wait a minute, no, no. I'm getting -- I'm getting the last word on this on because you just made a false statement, okay?\par \par \uc2 The courts are not the final arbiter of who wins federal election contests. Congress is. That is required by the United States Constitution. That is required by the United States Code for congressmen and senators and the president.\par \par \uc2 So don't be surprised the courts don't usurp the power that is authorized in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, let me just quote -- let me just quote the GOP commissioner in Wisconsin in "The Wall Street Journal" today, printed -- I will read it verbate (ph). He said there is no evidence that election fraud is the reason Trump lost in Wisconsin, and that is not for lack of looking.\par \par \uc2 His advice to your party is to pivot away from these conspiracy theories, focus on the issue that affects Wisconsin families and their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 Sir, I want to move on to January 6 and ask you about the committee --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: OK. Then, look at the -- look at the -- look at the judge's opinion, look at the judge's opinion in Pennsylvania that talked about over 2 million illegal ballots cast, that was their court order.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Sir, have you been subpoenaed yet by the January 6th committee?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : Look at the special investigation of a Supreme Court justice who found significant voter fraud at nursing homes in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : Look at the "2000 Mules" documentary that has come out. Look at how many mass mail out of ballots there were across United States for which we have no security.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Okay, and that has been looked at and fact-checked by multiple outlets, including "Reuters", who have debunked that as any sort of proof that there was widespread voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: I'm sorry --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm going to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: January 6th --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : -- fact check at the time (ph) you're absolutely wrong, Sandra. But keep going with that story if you wish.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, thank you, sir. Respectfully, I want to move on to January 6th and the committee.\par \par \uc2 Have you been subpoenaed? Have even served a subpoena as it has been confirmed four other Republican congressmen have?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : I don't know if the committee has issued a subpoena or not, OK, because I haven't been privy to any conversations with them. All I hear are media reports but I can say for a fact that I have not been sub -- that I have not been served with any kind of documentation. And there is no subpoena until it's been served on the person to whom it is directed.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Okay, and you had signaled this week that you're not going to comply unless certain conditions are met, including testifying in public.\par \par \uc2 Should we expect to, if you are indeed served or have been served, to see you testifying in public before this committee?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, I have to get with my colleagues who have been purportedly subpoenaed -- at least they say they have been served. I don't know why I haven't and they -- some of them have.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: I guess it was Kevin McCarthy, he went out of his way to accept service.\par \par \uc2 But I've looked at some of their communications too, the witch hunt committee. And I believe that some of their communications have merit.\par \par \uc2 But the things with me that I require is first it's got to be public, it's got to be something that you at FOX News can have a camera on, so that the American people can see it. No more of this clandestine meeting stuff, secret meeting stuff involving the public's business.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: No more these little leakages, OK?\par \par \uc2 It's got to be in public, it's got to be congressman to congressman. It's going to be limited to issues associated with January the 6th and has to be after the Senate primary is over with. I don't want this witch hunt committee and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  trying to interfere --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : -- with a Republican primary election for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congressman, lastly, looking ahead. If you win this runoff, what is the plan to win in November?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Well, I'm going to beat the Democrats. I have a history of beating Democrats like a drum and I see no reason why this general election in November would be any different.\par \par \uc2 They are socialist. They are dictatorial. They are amoral. They are absolutely the opposite of what we believe in in the state of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 My record against Democrats is 14 wins, zero losses. That's a pretty good record, and I'll defend that against any other comer in this Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 Best I understand, none of the other Republicans running in this race have won even a single election. None of them ever held public office before.\par \par \uc2 I'm the only one that's ever held public office. I'm the only one who's ever beat the Democrats. And unlike in 2017 when we elected a Democrat to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  from ruby red Alabama, if Mo Brooks is the nominee, it ain't happening. And if you want to help, please go to MoBrooks.com.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congressman, thank you very much for joining us today here on "FOX News Sunday". It's always good to speak with you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS\uc2 : My pleasure. Very lively.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will bring our Sunday panel to discuss the debate over gun control and the fate of former President Trump's favorite candidates in these tough midterm battles.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Why do we keep letting this happen? Where in God's name is our backbone, to the courage to deal with it, and stand up to the lobbies? It's time to turn this pain into action.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Now is the time to find common ground. Sadly before the sun had even set on the horrible day of tragedy, we witnessed a now familiar parade of cynical politicians seeking to exploit the tears of sobbing families to increase their own power and take away our constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: President Biden and former President Trump both speaking this week on the politics of gun control after Tuesday's mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 And it is time now for our Sunday group. FOX News senior political analyst Brit Hume, "Politico" congressional correspondent Olivia Beavers, and FOX News political analyst Juan Williams.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you all. Thank you for being here today. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, POLITICO: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. So, Juan, we'll start with you first.\par \par \uc2 There is new momentum for federal reform gun legislation in this country and we know those conditions are under way right now. The Democrats have not been able to succeed in the past to reform gun laws, even after Sandy Hook. Will this time be different?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Sandra, good morning. I'm skeptical that anything can get done. You know, stopping mass murders shouldn't be political, but it clearly is, and nothing was done to Congress after Newtown in 2012 when even more children were slaughtered by a gunman.\par \par \uc2 You know, if the man from Mars landed here from today and saw us so divided that we can't even deal with policies to stop mass murder of children, I think he would just be in disbelief.\par \par \uc2 But you know, right now with Uvalde on our heels and Buffalo, I think there is growing public frustration and as a result, some pressure on the congress, so you may see some marginal steps taken that most likely is a red flag law. You were discussing that with Congressman Brooks, but there's also thoughts about more mental health care resources being given from the government to local government.\par \par \uc2 And then there is some hope for possibility of background checks. Again, the American people overwhelmingly support this, gun owners as well as people who don't own guns. And there is some thought even, but unlikely, of maybe raising the age to 21 because we've seen that very young people, 18 year olds, you know, going into a gun store, buying these assault type weapons, you know, huge amounts of ammunition, body armor, that should raise a lot of concerns.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Brit, both Texas Governor Greg Abbott and Texas Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2  this week canceled there in-person appearances at the \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention in Texas after this shooting. In doing so, Patrick said he didn't want to bring any further pain or grief to these families in Uvalde that are still suffering in this moment. But several high-profile Republicans, including former President Trump, did appear in person.\par \par \uc2 Was that insensitive?\par \par \uc2 HUME: You're asking me, Sandra?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yeah, Brit.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I suppose so. The truth is it's -- you know, if the thing had happened -- if the shooting had happened in Washington, D.C., and the convention were happening somewhere in the same distance away in another state, I don't think it would have had the same -- causes the same reaction.\par \par \uc2 You know, I suppose from appearances' sake that you could say showing up at the \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention was insensitive to the people in Uvalde, but I doubt any of them were paying very much attention to that. These are -- whether you went or you didn't go, it's a political gesture all the way around.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Olivia, to the midterm elections now. Katie Britt and Mo Brooks, with whom we just spoke, are heading to a Senate primary runoff in Alabama. In the last few weeks, he has been surging despite a very public repudiation from former President Trump. What does that say about the power of the Trump endorsement? Is it waning?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's endorsement, I remember asking Jim Jordan about this a couple weeks ago, what happens if, you know, candidates will get his endorsement lose, does that mean that his influence is waning, and he said this is the most important endorsement in the Republican Party, and I think that's a fair thing to say. Is it an end-all, be-all? It does not appear so if you look down in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, when he lost the endorsement, Mo Brooks kept on pushing forward when Katie Britt was focusing on \uc2 Mike Durant\uc2  in this primary and Katie Britt has had a soaring campaign. I think she went for maybe 4 percent of having Republican support in the state when she first launched a campaign to having 44 percent, a 15-point lead over Brooks. So we are seeing this runoff.\par \par \uc2 We'll see if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets involved in this primary race, but clearly it wasn't a kiss of death for Mo Brooks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I want to get to the primary results in Georgia by first bringing back up this "Wall Street Journal" piece from this morning, the Wisconsin GOP's latest meltdown, asking why do Republicans seem so determined to lose the November elections. In it, it says this, quote: This week's primary results in Georgia prove that most Republican voters are ready to stop looking backward at 2020 and start going to get some real work done for their states and their country, time to leave 2020 behind.\par \par \uc2 So, Brit, Governor Brian Kemp overwhelmingly won the Republican primary for governor in Georgia, beating Trump-backed candidate, former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , by more than 50 points, huge margin.\par \par \uc2 Give us your take on what this all means for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his power over the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HUME: He remains a highly influential figure in Republican politics and it may well be true that his endorsement is worth more than any other single individual's endorsement, but the evidence from Georgia is pretty clear. This was the big one for him. He badly wanted to defeat Brian Kemp because he felt Brian Kemp didn't stand up for him when the election was allegedly stolen, and he also wanted to see \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , the candidate -- the incumbent candidate for secretary of state go down. Both men won.\par \par \uc2 As you noted, Kemp won by a huge margin, which suggests to me that is important as a Trump endorsement can be, his influence is starting to wane. People are, as "The Wall Street Journal" suggests, looking ahead and not back.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think that any candidate who wins the nomination and on the Republican Party and goes into the fall election talking about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  senselessly (ph) wants to talk about, which is how the election in 2020 was stolen, is going to find out a very powerful message at a time when so many other issues of greater day-to-day importance to people are crowding that issue well out of sight.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Olivia, finally, to Pennsylvania's Republican Senate primary. It is officially headed for a recount. Dr. Oz currently leads David McCormick by less than a thousand votes there and he's declared himself the presumptive nominee. He did so on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Is Dr. Oz getting ahead of himself here?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: It looks like he's taking a page out of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's playbook, which is exactly what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  advised him to do. You know, this is going -- they're both -- both McCormick and Dr. Oz have hired an army of lawyers and they're playing it out about whether these undated ballots are going to be counted, and we're going to see how that plays out. But right now, you know, Oz is trying to claim that he has the nominee and we just are not clear yet with this -- with this recount.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we're -- we'll find out soon enough.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, many thanks to our panel.\par \par \uc2 We're going to take a quick break right there and we'll see you again shortly.\par \par \uc2 Up next, overshadowed by gun violence. The White House, this week, taking unilateral action on criminal justice reform. We'll bring in Senator Ben Cardin to discuss the moves and state of negotiations on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: President Biden, this week, signed an executive order on police reform aimed to increase accountability and renew trust between law enforcement and communities. It comes two years after the death of George Floyd, and as the U.S. continues to see a spike in crime.\par \par \uc2 In just a moment we'll ask Senator Ben Cardin about these reforms and about the prospect for bipartisan moves on gun legislation.\par \par \uc2 But first to Kevin Corke. He's live in Washington for us with the latest on the talks on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, ABC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, even as the president and first lady travel to Texas to comfort the families of those killed in last week's devastating shooting, back here in Washington, battle lines, as you well know, have been drawn by congressional lawmakers over how best to address gun violence, though I think it's fair to say the sides may actually be closer than ever to finding some common ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): I don't know whether we'll be able to get 60 votes in the Senate, but I'm going to do my best to try.\par \par \uc2 CORKE (voice over): That's the hope from congressional Democrats and even some Republicans, that this time lawmakers from both sides of the aisle will support some measure of, quote, common sense gun legislation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I think there's a sense of, you know, urgency that maybe we didn't feel before.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: As they do so, the White House is hoping the upper chamber will soon confirm the first permanent ATF director in seven years, though the nomination of Steve Dettelbach faces political headwinds. As does the White House on a number of fronts, especially with the out of stock rate for baby formula rising to 70 percent nationwide, up from 45 percent a week ago, and gas prices surging, this holiday weekend in particular, with the price for a gallon of gas nationwide up over $4.59 a gallon, an all-time record high, and migrant boarder crossings reaching numbers never before by Border Patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CORKE: However, there may be just a bit of good news for the White House to report. Of course, we learned on Friday that the Fed's preferred inflation metric actually may have peaked with the annual price change for the personal consumption price index, or PCE, as you well know, Sandra, checking in at 6.3 percent for April. That's actually down from 6.6 percent in March. We'll have to see how May plays out, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, just a little bit of hope that inflation may be topping out.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: Yes. Maybe.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But that's one data point. We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Kevin Corke, reporting live from Washington for us. Kevin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CORKE: You bet.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Joining us now for Maryland, Senator Ben Cardin.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to FOX NEWS SUNDAY, sir. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): Sandra, it's good to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so, Senator, let's start with prospects for reform. So, can Democrats deliver ten Republican senators on any gun reform legislation? Your take.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, the jury's out on that. We've tried on so many different issues and have not been successful even to start debate, whether it -- we deal with gun safety issues or we deal with immigration or we deal even with small business. We're not been able to get ten senators to allow us to even start debates. So, we're going to have to wait to see.\par \par \uc2 I think the urgency is there. I was listening to Senator Cornyn. I really do think that there is a real urgency that we can show that we can act. I'm confident if we can get a bill on the floor on the gun issues and can pass it, it will be the right signal to the American people.\par \par \uc2 It probably won't go as far as I would like to see us go, but it will at least show that Congress can act.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Even some Republicans who have signaled openness to reforms also point out how complicated this really is.\par \par \uc2 Here's Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This man had no criminal record. He shot his grandmother in the face. He lawfully purchased a gun. I don't know how -- I can't tell people that I can think of a law that would have stopped this particular shooting. Are there other things we can do? Yes. Let's see if we can.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Your response to that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, I think we can do some things that will save lives. I think the red flags background checks. These issues will save some lives. I'd like to go further than that. Investing in mental health is good also. But military-style weapons, I think getting them off the street would be very helpful in saving lives.\par \par \uc2 Will it save our lives? Well, no, but America's an outlier in gun violence. We're in a class by ourselves. We have to ask why. And one of the reasons is the fact that it's so easy to get these military style weapons. Particularly an 18-year-old being able to buy that type of weapon, I think it raises questions about America and our laws compared to the rest of the nation -- world.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator -- Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , sir, has blessed some gun reform negotiations, but in the past he's been open to conversations, yet ultimately doesn't support reform bills.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): I don't want to overstate my optimism though. I mean I've been Charlie Brown enough to know that up until now the football has been pulled out from under me every single time. Maybe this time is different.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, when you look at the chances here, is McConnell just going to pull the football out from underneath Democrats again?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, we hope that these bipartisan negotiations will lead to a critical mass of Republicans that will be able to deliver at least ten votes so that we can have a debate on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is known as the great deliberative body, but if we can't even get a bill on the floor to debate, where are we? I mean this is an issue that the American public expects us to act. Enough's enough, Congress needs to act. It needs to take action that can help avoid these types of tragedies.\par \par \uc2 Elementary school children being gunned down? We've got to do something. And clearly the gun safety issues are one, mental health's another. There's a lot of areas that we can make progress and we need to debate that and come together as a body in order to keep our children safer.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: This is also happening in the middle of the Texas gubernatorial race. Take a look at this moment when democratic candidate \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  confronted Texas Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 BETO O'ROURKE: (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Sit down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're out of -- you're out of line and an embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey, get out of our -- get out of our (INAUDIBLE), sir.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Sit down and don't play this stunt.\par \par \uc2 O'ROURKE: Governor Abbott, I have to say something. Because after -- because after El Paso, the time to stop the next shooting is right now and you are doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, he needs to get his (EXPLETIVE DELETED) out of here. This isn't the place to talk this over.\par \par \uc2 O'ROURKE: You are offering us nothing. You have said this is not predictable. This is totally predictable when you choose not to do anything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, you're out of line.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator Cardin, clearly Democrats are frustrated, the country is frustrated, but does that sort of confrontation help matters?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: At this moment, I'm hopeful that Democrats and Republicans will come together and recognize the safety of our children, the safety of our community needs to come first. So that's where we hope that this bipartisan group will be able to come together with some legislative action that will give the public the confidence that we are trying to deal with this issue.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But were you specifically, sir, were you --\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: We've got to listen to each other. We've got to come together as a nation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Were you OK with that confrontation, though?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I didn't see the video in there. I can't see it because of where I am right now.\par \par \uc2 I'm for us being honest with each other and listening to each other and making it clear that our frustration is there, America is an outlier, we need to recognize that, we need to make our communities safer, and we've got to make our schools safe. That's an area where parents send their children and expect it to be a place of safety. So, I know there's a lot of frustration, a lot of anger, a lot of fear that's out there. Let's come together and do something about it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. So the Senate -- the president will be in Texas today, heading there right now, and is pressing for action. But here's White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We leave the mechanics to Senator Schumer. We want to see action. That is what we're calling for. And -- but, again, we leave it to the leadership, the Democratic leadership, to figure out how they're going to move this forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Is that enough for you or should the White House be doing more on its own to get something done here?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I think at this moment we need to have at least ten Republican senators that will allow us to move forward on the Senate debate. Senator Schumer is our point person on that. He's the one that's delegated to get that type of support, working with Senator Connell.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the president's going to be engaged. He's been very engaged. President Biden's been very much engaged in the senate deliberations. I'm sure he will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congress remains, meanwhile, deadlocked on police , sir, and has been for years. But this week the president used his powers to sign an executor order focused on federal law enforcement, which include the creation of a database to help track police officer misconduct. Some police groups celebrated the order as a blueprint for further congressional action, while others are dismissing it as political theater and faltered it for opaqueness.\par \par \uc2 It all comes at a time of spike in crime coast-to-coast in his country and as Republicans hammer your party for being soft on crime and not doing enough to support our police.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, is this going to bring real change or was this just a symbolic photo-op?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: No, I think what the president did is very important to get the data so we know exactly what we're talking about, sharing that information, providing resources to train police officers on sensitivities of communities. I think all that are important best practices, establishing best practices on the use of force. All that are important advancements.\par \par \uc2 Would I prefer the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to take this issue up and pay us sensible reform? Absolutely. But, once again, we could not get 60 votes. We couldn't get the Republicans to join us on a Senate for discussion of this.\par \par \uc2 But I think what the president did, his exact message, and I'm glad to see it's receiving a warm response from our law enforcement community.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I want to finish off with an issue every American cares about in this moment, and that is inflation. Millions are hitting the road for the holiday weekend, sir, and they're paying record high gas prices. Truckers are feeling the pain. Grocery bills are sky-high.\par \par \uc2 What is the plan, sir, to bring these prices down?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, Sandra, I agree. I mean we have a -- American families are confronting price increases. They're having a hard time dealing with their family budgets, Energy costs are way too high. The president has taken some steps to ease that by the release of our reserves. We recognize the international circumstances was a war in Ukraine and energy prices are not determined here in the United States. But we need to do more about it.\par \par \uc2 Our game plan is to deal with the cost (ph) centers that are affecting American families. We're dealing with the cost of health care and prescription drugs. We're hoping to have action in those areas. To deal with the cost of childcare. The president has a plan on that. We hope we'll have cooperation from the Republicans, and we can get some issues that really affect the average American family's budget.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, as sky-high gas prices remain, and it is all -- inflicting a lot of pain on American families. So, this is clearly going to shape up as one of the biggest issues come November elections.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thank you very much for joining us. Senator Cardin, always good to talk to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Good -- have a nice holiday. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And you as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we are back with our Sunday group on one of the biggest Second Amendment cases to head to the Supreme Court in years.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And we're back with our panel now.\par \par \uc2 Juan, to your first.\par \par \uc2 Thanks again to all of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expecting to rule on a huge Second Amendment rights case in just the next few weeks. And it may lead to looser gun laws. It's a challenge to a New York state regulation that requires residents demonstrate a, quote, actual and articulable need to have a concealed weapon in public. Considering that and other recent developments, do you expect, Juan, Democrats to push for either court reforms or for an expanded court?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, I think it's important to note here that that case that you're talking about in New York state, Sandra, has been upheld by lower courts. I think there are seven states, including most American big cities, that are covered by a requirement that you have to prove a need to carry a gun in public at all times.\par \par \uc2 Now, in 2008, the Supreme Court said you can keep a gun at home. They spoke about mental illness and people who were convicted felons being excluded, but they said that's right.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this case, the question is, can you carry a gun on the subway, to a stadium, a sporting event? You know, what about in a government building? And so, you know, I think that there's going to be great frustration -- you mentioned the Democrats -- but I think publicly, if the idea is that people can just carry guns all around, and I think that you're going to see some expression that, you know, you have three Trump justices pushing this court to the right and taking cases like this that seem to divide the country.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'll let Brit respond to that, and also point out that you rewind a couple weeks here, Brit, and the court was already under a lot of scrutiny and pressure after that draft opinion on abortion was leaked. One expert on the court saying, quote, I can't recall the last time the Supreme Court ruled in so many cases likely to spark a strong political backlash.\par \par \uc2 Are we, Britt, seeing a departure from the more incremental record of the \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  court?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think if they overturn Roe v. Wade, we could certainly say that. That, of course, looks like it could happen because of that draft opinion that leaked. But it's not clear that will be the final judgment. And it will be interesting to see what they do in this Second Amendment case. It's a little bit hard for me to believe that the court, having upheld the right to keep and bear firearm as a constitutional right is prepared to go along with the idea that in order to exercise that right you've got to check in with your local government agency to prove that you need it. That will be interesting to see how the court crafts an opinion on that, but I have a feeling that that regulation may be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Oliva, the president signed an executive order on policing this week, timed to mark two years since the death of George Floyd in police custody. This after a high-profile effort to pass criminal justice reforms in the Senate ultimately failed.\par \par \uc2 So, is this order enough to please the progressives who have long hoped for substantive change?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I think that they're going to say that this is a good step, but in terms of incrementalism, they're definitely going to be wanting to push for more changes in police form.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: And so, you know, it's just a start in their mind, but for the time being, they're going be happy that there's just this advancement.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Very interesting stuff. Thank you very much to our panel for joining us on all of that. Thank you very much, and we'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at the best advice commencement speakers had for the class of 2022.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's an end of school year tradition here to share the best moments from commencement ceremonies. This year's graduates heard from athletes, political leaders, and a famous comedian, who shared his confidence in NYU Tisch grads while giving them a pretty memorable nickname.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DWYANE WADE\uc2 , FORMER NBA STAR: Will your journey have some highlights? Yes. Will your journey have some low lights? Yes. And if you continue to build on your discoveries and experiences, if you establish your moments of solitude and allow yourself the space for self-awareness, you will arrive at a place where you can grow.\par \par \uc2 ALLYSON FELIX, TRACK AND FIELD STAR: Your voice has power, and you have to use your voice even if it shakes. There are times when you'll ask for change, and there are times when you'll have to create it. Your life has purpose, so it's important to live a life of purpose.\par \par \uc2 GLENN YOUNGKIN, VIRGINIA GOVERNOR: So here's what I believe. Seek your purpose and you will find happiness. Seek happiness and you will find neither.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Adversity doesn't create character. Adversity reveals character. Men and women, when the challenges come -- and they surely will -- you will be in that moment the person you have been preparing to be in every quiet moment before that.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Graduates, I look at this unsettled world -- and, yes, I then see the challenges -- but I'm here to tell you, I also see the opportunities. The opportunities for your leadership.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I cannot promise you the way will be straight, or the sailing will be easy. But I can promise you that you all have the tools needed to navigate any waters you encounter.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR SWIFT, SINGER AND SONGWRITER: Decide what is yours to hold, and let the rest go. Oftentimes the good things in your life are lighter anyway, so there's more room for them.\par \par \uc2 KEN JEONG, ACTOR AND COMEDIAN: Persistence is my greatest talent. Never give up, never close a door on your life, always persist. I don't define myself by my job. I'm not just a doctor. I'm not just a comedian. I'm not just an annoying overactive. I persisted in annoying the world for decades and the world relented, yo. I'm just me.\par \par \uc2 ADAM SANDLER, ACTOR AND COMEDIAN: You are prepared, Tisches. You've got the goods, Tisches. You've earned the confidence, Tisches! You are the \uc2 NYU\uc2  elite! And you are going to go out there in the real world and kick some creative ass! Can I get a hell yeah!\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hell yeah!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: On that note, our best wishes to the students and parents of the class of 2022.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for us here today. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 I will see you again Tuesday for "AMERICA REPORTS" on the Fox News Channel. That's each weekday at 1:00 p.m. Eastern Time.\par \par \uc2 Have a reflective Memorial Day weekend everyone and a great week. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220529ei5t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Decision To Cancel America's Domestic Energy Production Has Left Us Vulnerable; Small Businesses Getting Crushed In This Economy; Fuel Rationing Could Be In Our Future; Investors Alarmed As Crypto And Stocks Plunge; Treasury Secretary: Banning Abortion Would Be Very Damaging To The Economy; Hooked: More Americans Now Dependent On The Government\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Charles Hurt, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 7,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I appreciate it, brother.\par \par \uc2 So thank you so much for watching "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME." I'm Lawrence Jones. Don't forget to catch my show, "Lawrence Jones Cross Country" tomorrow night 10:00 PM Eastern Time. Until then, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is up next. See you all.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to a Special Edition of TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 When was the last time you heard a Democratic politician propose a solution to a problem -- no matter what the problem is -- that didn't make the Democratic Party more powerful? Ponder that for a moment. Let's say, for example, that you really believe global warming was an existential threat to humanity and that rising carbon dioxide levels were causing global warming. What would you do to fix it?\par \par \uc2 Well, you might think back to 10th grade biology class CO2. CO2, hmm. Don't trees consume huge amounts of CO2 for photosynthesis? Quick \uc2 Google\uc2  check. Why, yes, they do. So, how about we plant a whole lot more trees all across the country and across the world? That seems like a natural solution with lots of upsides. Trees are beautiful. It might help fight global warming.\par \par \uc2 Oh, but no. There's a problem with that idea. Planting more trees might help solve climate change, but it definitely will not enrich your donors. Trees are cheap. It's hard to make money from trees. And worse than that, trees are impossible to regulate. Once you plant trees, they grow by themselves. They do not need Federal help.\par \par \uc2 Trees definitely will not make you more powerful. So instead, here's an idea. Let's invest trillions of dollars of other people's money in batteries and wind farms and electric school buses. That way, Democratic politicians get fingertip control over the entire U.S. economy and all of their friends get rich. Sounds perfect. Let's go with that idea.\par \par \uc2 That's how Democratic politicians think, every one of them. So, it probably should not surprise you that Elizabeth Warren has a brand-new plan for rising gas prices and naturally, it goes without saying, that plan would give Elizabeth Warren a lot more control over you and over the United States.\par \par \uc2 Well, hold on a minute, you may be wondering, why should Elizabeth Warren have control over anything? Elizabeth Warren isn't very bright. She's never created anything. In fact, she's a proven liar. This is a person who exploited the suffering of American Indians to get a job promotion. "The Trail of Tears means I need tenure." Okay, so in every possible way, Elizabeth Warren is mediocre. Yet, and here's the catch -- she's also a U.S. senator and that means she has power.\par \par \uc2 Warren's latest plan to make herself more powerful is called the Price Gouging Prevention Act of 2022. This bill would allow Elizabeth Warren to decide what things cost in the United States. So, let's say you're paying six bucks a gallon for gas (and you may be) the obvious solution to that, in fact, the only solution to that is to make more gasoline because if there is more of something, it will cost less. That's not just basic economics, though it is. It's also a law of nature. It never changes.\par \par \uc2 But Elizabeth Warren wants the opposite. She does not want more gas. In fact, she's worked hard to prevent the United States from producing more gasoline and that's the main reason it's so expensive.\par \par \uc2 What Elizabeth Warren wants instead is control over gasoline and over everything else, and this bill will give it to her. See how this works? And this is why the Biden administration has shut down oil and gas leases in the middle of an energy crisis and not just that, they're crushing refineries.\par \par \uc2 According to one analysis by Hot Air, quote: "Seven American refineries, which formerly processed 806,000 barrels of oil per day, have gone offline. Some simply shut down, while others were being converted to biofuels like ethanol, to meet the demands of the environmental lobby. These losses have left the United States with 124 operating oil refineries. That is 10 percent fewer refineries for gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel that we had in 2016, even as demand has continued to increase." End quote.\par \par \uc2 So, you see where this is going. No more oil or gas for you and that's why you can't afford to fill your tank. Instead, the Biden administration is demanding that states build wind turbines that sound good, but unfortunately don't actually work. Last year, we went to Texas to see this firsthand. Here's a clip from our documentary on the tragic buffoonery of wind power.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: With no chance of being approved near Malibu or Greenwich or in the mountains of Aspen, the development invariably happens in poorer parts of the country. The number one place for wind farms is Texas, but the question is, "What happens when the turbines stop spinning?"\par \par \uc2 KYLE ROTHENBERG, FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDIA: We're in the middle of nowhere in Texas. We're trying to find a place just to sleep tonight. Everything is sold out. There's nothing available.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's now expected to be the largest insurance claim event in Texas history. This winter storm totally overwhelmed the power system in Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I can tell you this morning, nearly 4.2 million Texans still have no electricity.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Texas has the most wind power capacity in the United States, but the Texas turbines, which are not winterized, iced up during a cold weather event in February of 2021. Electricity output collapsed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Texas regulators ordered rolling blackouts Monday as an arctic blast has frozen wind turbines.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COUGER FOX NEWS CHANNEL MEDIA: There is no water in the hotel.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  ORIGINALS" INTERVIEW 1: The Texas grid, like the California grid, is becoming increasingly unreliable and difficult to maintain, difficult to keep that power in a steady basis.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  ORIGINALS" INTERVIEW 2: This 20-year-old turbine is leaking oil. It doesn't look too green to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, the point of an energy grid is to produce and transmit energy. It has only one purpose and that purpose is being sabotaged on purpose by the Biden administration and by Wall Street, in favor of green energy, in favor of windmills that don't actually work.\par \par \uc2 And as a result of that, we're facing the prospect of more blackouts this summer. We