k, and it's crushing everyone. Everybody feels the price at the pump.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So people living in trailers are paying the most for kerosene, a lot of people do heat with kerosene. So have you ever seen an administration set out on purpose to hurt the weakest in our society?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Well, Tucker, it's beyond that. In New York State and in New Jersey, a lot of northeastern states, we are on the climate change agenda, so hardcore. New York State is enacting the Climate Leadership and Community Protection Act, that starts in 2030 and it basically outlaws fossil fuels and forces the electrification of everything.\par \par \uc2 That's less than seven and a half years away. That's all new construction. And if you go to replace your oil or natural gas, propane gas furnace or boiler, you're going to be forced to go electric. The studies have shown it's going to cost $18,000.00 to $50,000.00 for the average homeowner to convert New York State to electric and they want this with clean electric.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why -- we mentioned this before, New York State, about half of our electricity currently comes from fossil fuels, less than five percent comes from wind and solar. We have hydro and nuclear to make up the rest. We just got rid of seven percent of our nuclear power by taking Indian Point in the lower Hudson Valley offline. How are going to get away with removing fossil fuels? This makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 The electric can't handle it. We don't have enough grid, besides the cost to consumers. Last year, Tucker 350,000 people moved out of New York State. As this gets closer, we're going to be driving more people out in New York and businesses.\par \par \uc2 Natural gas is three to five times less expensive than commercial electricity. We just can't do it up here, Tucker. We're draining the state rapidly.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just, I've never seen like it. Mike Taylor. Good luck up there.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes.\par \par