e has always been a strand of isolationism in our party, but it's not anywhere near the dominant view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, really? I think McConnell and \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  should tour the country the debate that $40 billion price tag for Ukraine and town halls in front of all these people and see who ends up winning the audience vote. Anyone take a wager on that? I'll take one.\par \par \uc2 Farcical and irresponsible are two words I'd use to describe their arguments for this proxy war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Single most important thing we can do to push back against potential Chinese aggression is to help the Ukrainians defeat the Russians in this current conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Excuse me. The globalists are responsible for turning China into a global superpower, and the single greatest threat to freedom worldwide. So every stinger missile we give to Ukraine is one that we're not going to be using to defend Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And they want U.S. troops there as well. Oh, yes, they do. We've already upped our numbers in Eastern Europe. And Milley is clearly itching for more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENERAL \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN OF THE JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: My advice would be to create permanent bases, but don't permanently station. So you get the effect of permanence by rotational forces cycling through permanent bases.\par \par \uc2 I believe that a lot of our European allies, they're very, very willing to establish permanent bases. They'll build them. They'll pay for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, they'll pay for them? Another wager I'll take, which takes us to step four. Biden will soon ship even more U.S. jobs overseas, especially to Asia.\par \par \uc2 Now, when U.S. manufacturers can move operations to Asia, this means America is going to become more dependent on Asian supply chains. But Biden, who has been wrong on literally every trade deal he's ever voted on says, "Don't worry, be happy".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you all for joining today for the launch of the Indo-Pacific Economic Framework for prosperity. Nations represent here today are signing up to work toward an economic vision that will deliver for all people, where economic growth is sustainable, and is inclusive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Inclusive growth. Well, they probably think that by bribing these countries with jobs and money, these same countries will rally to our side when China makes its move. But that's just more naive thinking.\par \par \uc2 Every country from Australia to Singapore will end up going with the winner in this economic battle. And if we end up weaker, they're just going to align with China to save themselves. Welcome to reality, kids. That's how it works.\par \par \uc2 Plus, as we learn to the beginning of the pandemic, when we don't make important stuff here, we lose control. And then we open ourselves up for a ton of hurt. So we have shortages and medicines and raw materials, just to name a few.\par \par \uc2 And then step five. After he bankrupts us, helps China, then depletes our munitions and manpower and makes us energy poppers, well, Biden believes we should go to war over Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you willing to get involved militarily to defend Taiwan if it comes to that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's the commitment we made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, how do our geniuses plan to fight a country that after steps one through four, will it become a bigger - had a bigger economy, a bigger industrial base, a bigger army, a bigger Air Force, and a bigger Navy than we'll have?\par \par \uc2 How do they plan to fight a country whose leaders were trained in our universities and who has the best lobbyists in Washington? They could now strategize the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Have they have even thought about what would become of America if we lost a war with China?\par \par \uc2 I think we should be very clear here. That we will not support any more of the establishments wars, or waste any more money on increases in our Pentagon budget, unless we see a real effort to decouple from the CCP. We either have to change policy, take pragmatic new steps, or give up on Taiwan, which would be a complete and utter capitulation.\par \par \uc2 Now, think about President Trump, contrary to all the unfair criticism, he understood that keeping the peace required keeping our strength. Peace through strength, of course, as Reagan said. But now we're back to living in this globalist dream world.\par \par \uc2 But President Xi isn't. He knows how much power we have and how much power he has. And if and when he thinks the balance of power has shifted in China's favor, he's going to move on Taiwan. And the sad fact is, if they have the money, the resources and the minds trained here, they could win.\par \par \uc2 So it's time to tell the establishment, it's time to choose. If you want to help Finland and Sweden and \uc2 NATO\uc2  because of Putin's aggression, ok, that's fine. But let's stop pretending there's a pivot to Asia that matters. You cannot do both. Their plan is to strengthen our enemies, weaken us, then go fight more wars.\par \par \uc2 Trump's plan was to weaken our enemies, strengthen ourselves, and avoid new wars. Make sense, right? It's common sense. Our nation is hurting. You can feel it, right. Our people are pessimistic. It's horrible to see that.\par \par \uc2 They're beginning to think that our political leaders don't know how to turn things around, even if they wanted to. Every week when they look at their family budgets, Americans have to make hard choices. Call me crazy, but I think it's time that Washington started doing the same. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now is \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Secretary of State and Fox News contributor. Mr. Secretary, it's good to see you tonight. A question I posed during the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Right now, given the investment that we have already in Eastern Europe. What about Biden's comment that they tried to walk back, but I think everybody at this point is confused by.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Laura, good evening. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 I heard President Biden's comments today. Deeply confusing. I could not tell you what the Biden administration's policy is with respect to Taiwan today. I don't think anybody can. I'm not even sure President Biden fully understands what it was he said today. We knew this for four years.\par \par \uc2 We knew that if this all began at home, you talked about this in your opening. It begins with a strong American economy. It also begins with a strong American military, not one that's focused on getting every vehicle to be green and every soldier to be part of the diversity and inclusion crowd.\par \par \uc2 It began with a military that could do its primary function, its primary mission. And today, he talked about what we were going to do in Asia. But I must say, there has not been a single action and not a single effort that supports what President Biden talked about.\par \par \uc2 I'm as tough on China as you'll find. I have been sanctioned by the Chinese Communist Party. But it's not about words, Laura. It's about deeds. And this administration hasn't shown the willingness to do what it actually takes to confront the Chinese Communist Party and support the people of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: On the issue of confronting China for what it's doing here in the United States, you all were really serious about this. I remember, you and I talking, what was it, six years ago, or almost about the Chinese students in the United States. And the Department of Justice actually established a China initiative that in February the Biden administration disbanded.\par \par \uc2 It was focused on the espionage program that we thought was happening in a lot of our colleges and universities, because I guess the perceptions that were unfairly painted of Chinese Americans. So (ph) wokefication of even our approach to espionage, Mr. Secretary, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Laura, they're living in a fantasy world. They have walked away from the really good work we did here at home. I've talked about the Chinese Communist Party as being a cancer that's inside the gates. What you and I talked about this six years ago. 360,000 Chinese students studying here.\par \par \uc2 We shuttered the Chinese Consulate in Houston, Texas. They were hurting one of the largest spying operations ever conducted inside the United States of America. And the Trump administration, we closed it down. We denied them the capacity to do what they were trying to do to attend PTA meetings and school board meetings, all the things they were doing here at home to run influence operations inside of our country.\par \par \uc2 This administration just - they called us racist when we did this, Laura, to be honest with you. And the truth is what we were doing is, we were protecting America from this very real threat of the Chinese Communist Party here on America soil. And so if you can't do that, if you can't take on that simple task, your ability to actually confront what \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , I think, has in store for the United States is something - he'll see this weakness, Laura, and he'll drive a truck through it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Mr. Secretary, I need to turn to the border issue, which I know is very concerning to you as well.\par \par \uc2 Fox's Bill Melugin is reporting tonight that it took two weeks for ICE to re-arrest a suspected terrorist, who was released into the U.S. by border patrol agents after he was flagged by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . We know we're vulnerable to the cartels and the drug traffickers. How vulnerable are we are to terrorists, given how open this border is?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: It's a very real challenge, Laura. It's something that we confronted with a great deal of seriousness and determination. It's partly a seriousness factor. It's also just a numbers game, Laura. We have this many people come across the border that are unvetted, unknown, unmarked, when they come across the border. There's no way you can prevent the very real risk you describe.\par \par \uc2 We were always worried about terrorists coming across the border. We've seen what you saw these last couple of weeks. We saw them attempt to do this. When you are an open border's nation, the bad guys will figure out the least expensive way to get through, the least costly way to get through, the least risky way to get through. And today, that's our southern border. This is truly dangerous for the United States.\par \par \uc2 This is an awful lot of ungoverned territory down there, Laura. And we're now allowing ungoverned territory for people all across the world to come to and enter our country without being controlled. To watch them not be able to chase this terrorist down, it's just heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's a dereliction of duty. At some point, you wonder if they want to. I mean, at some point, this becomes the policy. So, Mr. Secretary, thank you for joining us tonight. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is a recession in the United States inevitable?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. Does that mean we don't have problems? We do. We have problems what the rest of the world has. But less consequential than the rest of the world has, because of our internal growth and strength.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's where Biden's head is. Our problems aren't as bad as the rest of the world. That's our bar now, I guess, we're setting, and they're less consequential. Americans, just suck it up. OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We also find ourselves in a situation where we have food shortages, food shortages because of Ukraine. This is going to be a haul. This is going to take some time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining us now, Stephen Miller, former senior adviser to President Trump, founder of America First Legal. Stephen, it sounds like Carter's malaise all over again. It's going to be a long haul, it was going to be a dark, depressing winter in 2021. And talking about food shortages on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER, "AMERICA FIRST LEGAL" FOUNDER: Yes. The big difference, of course, being that we could still feed our babies when Carter was in office. Right now, we have this humiliating spectacle, truly embarrassing, where foreign countries are sending America emergency relief packages so that we can feed our children - all of us grew up watching America send relief packages to poor foreign countries so they can feed their children.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're becoming a third world country.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: It's third world country before our very eyes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Did you see that \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  saying, that we need a Marshall plan for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes. $40 billion, I guess isn't enough.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. So but this is clearly where they're going, right? This is going to be hundreds of billions of dollars over, Lord knows how long. If they get their way, if the people call the military industrial complex, just the Biden administration, combination of Republicans and Democrats, the establishment. They want us deeper into Eastern Europe. And this is going to be a commitment. I see this going on for - could be as long as the Afghan war.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: If not quite a bit longer. But this is always at the 