uc2 HORACE COOPER, PROJECT 21 CO-CHAIRMAN: Well, they absolutely do. And it's costing the state. I mean, remember what the Major League Baseball Association did? You remember, all of the attacks that happened that cause people, particularly those of color in the Atlanta area, who had prepared to play their role in providing their services and goods. That was all ripped right out of their hands, and nothing was done. Where's the apology? We were told Jim Eagle was shutting down votes. And what we're seeing is exactly the--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That is such a great point about major league baseball. And Horace, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  spoke tonight and she tried to spin the record breaking voter turnout. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: The equivalent of saying that more people voting means there's no suppression, is like telling people that if you get in the water, there are no sharks. And more people get in the water, there are fewer sharks. That - there's no correlation there. Voter suppression is about blocking, or impeding certain types of voters from participating in elections. Voter suppression is not about stopping voting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I have to hand it to her, this is what I have to hand to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . She just keeps charging on. There's something kind of admirable about the fact that she can just like -- no, it doesn't matter at all. Like if there were fewer voters, Horace, if there were fewer voters, that would have been better? I'm not even understanding that logic, but maybe I'm not --\par \par \uc2 COOPER: She's on point continually. She has an idea. Let's make sure that we do a slur against the people of Georgia and the people of America, and it doesn't matter what the facts end up being. She repeats it over and over again. I came on your program, and I explained she lost because, in one of the best climates for Democrats in a generation, she went crazy and lost on guns, on taxes, on a lot of the issues that main street Georgians cared about. She's doing it again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, and I said this before on just general commentary on the left in the United States as a whole, but it's really hard to govern a country you loathe and a state that you don't much like. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, (D) GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I'm running for governor because I know that we have to have a conversation about who we are in this state and what we want for each other and from each other. I am tired of hearing about being the best state in the country to do business when we are the worst state in the country to live.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That should lose her the race just right there, not that I thought she had a chance anyway. The worst state in the country. But they think that about America. That's why they like putting all of our efforts in other countries, because they think our country is over. That's kind of what they believe.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: The American dream is critical, not just to the mainstream of America, but in particular to black Americans. A lot of folks forget that when \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  gave his "I Have A Dream" speech, its primary purpose was to demand changes to make it easier for black men in particular to be able to get jobs so they can support their families. When she says that Georgia is a great place to work and a terrible place to live, that's a world salad that I don't understand. But more importantly, most Georgians know getting a good job, being able to provide for your family, that is a place you want to live with you and your family.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Horace, that crime in Chicago, speaking of other issues, this -- \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , how does she have a chance of getting reelected with all that? \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  doesn't want to talk about the crime issue.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: No, they don't want to talk about this escalating problem that they created. We have been sending, since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left Washington, a message. If you're a criminal, it's a free season for you to act. If you want to do something heinous, like what we saw in New York City, it's OK. If you want to continue what's happening in Chicago, there won't be consequences. And if I say something like let's start having accountability, if you have a plan of a mass destruction effect, I don't care if people die or not, you ought to get the death penalty. That will send the right signal. But the progressives can't accept that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, no, no, no. Horace, always great to have you in the studio. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And since the Dems can't figure it out, we sent our own Raymond Arroyo to ask voters what they're really concerned about, and Whoopi may get a new view after this. "Seen and Unseen" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we explore the big cultural stories of the day. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor, Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, you just got back from talking to some voters, I understand.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Laura, I like talking directly to people, and with midterms coming, these are the issues that could drive their votes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You tell me the top three most important topics to you going into these midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Gas, gas, gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The economy right now is really bad for everybody. I have got a family, and to support my family right now, it is costing me a lot more money every month.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have four children. And just worrying about them walking around the city, things that we used to take for granted.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Gas and food. The border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Getting to work safely.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Abortion for sure.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, what's the big three for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would agree with my brother. But definitely abortion issues.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's a big part of your vote?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Prolife first.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That last one was a surprise to me, Laura, but I discovered it's consistent with the latest polling. Monmouth just released a poll of voters' top concerns -- 26 percent said the economy, 25 percent said abortion, followed by healthcare, immigration, and gun control. This electorate is focused on these issues.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The abortion issue cuts both ways. It could be prolife people, and people who are proabortion. So --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, there's a higher interest in this among Democrats who are worried about Roe v. Wade going away. But we'll see what happens in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 Look, speaking of abortion, on Friday, we reported that Archbishop Salvatore Cordileone of San Francisco made a major announcement. And now Sister Whoopi is on the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": The archbishop of San Francisco is calling for Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to be denied receiving communion because of her prochoice stance. This is not your job, dude. That is not -- you can't -- that is not up to you to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: With apologies to Whoopi of Avila, this actually is the archbishop's job. It's canon law. He's called to protect the sacrament of the Eucharist from sacrilege, protect his people from scandal, and importantly here, warn \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  that her soul is in danger due to her support of abortion. So no matter where people are on this issue, the bishop is doing his job. Pastorally, he's trying to lead his flock to salvation. I know Whoopi played a nun, but that doesn't make her a theologian.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the sister act never ends, Raymond, never ends.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What else?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, the Johnny Depp trial took a very strange turn today. Depp was supposed to take the stand. But it looks like Amber Heard's team is cutting their losses. They decided to call experts to testify. One was psychiatrist Dr. David Spiegel who looked a lot like Doc Brown.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He does.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Christopher Lloyd is a shoo-in to play this guy in the made-for-TV movie.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The Doctor, yes, look at that. He was quite a character.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Did you look at that one when you compared the process and speed?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do I have to answer that question, your honor?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You have to answer questions, yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's a news flash for him, apparently. He diagnosed Depp, Laura, with substance abuse disorder, even though he's never met the actor. And what do I always say? Performance matters, Laura. What people see, not what you say. And after a blistering testimony, when you drink your Gatorade like this, people might have questions about your credibility, particularly when you're blaming others, when you're saying others have behavioral problems.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, he's never met Depp and he's never met a comb either, OK?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What's going on there? He does look like the actor in "Back to the Future." All right, what else?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He's definitely met a DeLorean or two, I know it.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, the president was in Tokyo today for a bilat with the Japanese prime minister. It's tonight's slightly tired "Freestylin' Biden."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Joe, where are you? Come here.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: To then reliance -- excuse me, resilience partnership which we announced last year.\par \par \uc2 You know, the two largest democracies -- the two largest economic -- economies --\par \par \uc2 It seems to be the best thing I can do in addition to try to get the - the -- the Middle Eastern countries, including OPEC to raise their production of oil.\par \par \uc2 There's a rapprochement me between China -- excuse me, between the Ukrainians and -- and Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I can't follow any of this. I don't even think he knows what's he's saying. I have to give the president points for originality, though. When he freelances, no one knows what's going to come out, including his staff. Now, when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  speaks, as she did today, she has a way of speaking the way \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  speaks when she's speaking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When we talk about our children for this group, we all believe that when we talk about the children of the community, they are a children of the community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She's always on loop, Laura. She just repeats what she said a second ago. I've never heard anything like it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, Raymond, that's not very nice, Raymond. And it's not nice.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's not nice.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, thank you. "Freestylin' Biden" is still one of my favorite things ever. All right, Ray, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Cleanup in aisle four, or 46. Tonight we bring you a new series, "Walking Back Biden," where the administration struggles to explain that context or to clarify that Joe said what he said but he doesn't actually mean it because it's not U.S. policy. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, when the person sworn in as president of the United States speaks either here or on the world stage, we as Americans kind of presume that he knows what he's saying. We presume that his words represent the actual U.S. position on any issue. After all, he's the guy in charge. But when Biden wonders off the teleprompter, things usually go sideways, which brings us to our new segment, "Walking Back Biden."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would you support sending U.S. troops to Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I will render my advice at the moment in time to the president and the secretary of defense.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: As the president said, our One China policy has not changed. So again, our policy has not changed. The president was clear on the fact that the policy has not changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, we may never know what Biden really made when he said he would respond militarily to China if it invaded Taiwan. But it's not the first time the White House had to do cleanup in aisle 46.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The president delivered a forceful denunciation of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Mr. Biden appeared to call for regime change, although the White House later walked that back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The White House was quick to walk back the president's comments that Putin cannot remain in power.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The White House walked back some of his statements. But that's something that keeps \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  guessing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is that the defense? Then he personally had to walk back his claim that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  was killing people by not cracking down on COVID misinformation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What is your message to platforms like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They're killing people.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  isn't killing people. These 12 people are out there giving misinformation. Anyone listening to it is getting hurt by it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love it when he corrects himself. There he goes, walking. It's kind of like a moonwalk, Michael Jackson. It's becoming a habit. It's not just embarrassing, though. It's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Ben Domenech, FOX News contributor and editor of "The Transom." Ben, it is kind of funny, but it's deadly serious when it comes to these issues.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It would be so funny if we had this mediocre, incoherent president in a time when the world stage was lighter and when there were fewer things that demanded a strong commander-in-chief, someone who actually knew maybe what was going on in the day around him and didn't have to be whispered in the ear, sir, you need to go out there and walk that thing back.\par \par \uc2 The problem, as I see it, is this. He's doing this on some of the most important issues in global affairs. It's not just like he's walking back some detail of a tax negotiation or something with Capitol Hill. This is something that actually matters on the world stage, and he's completely incoherent.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love that argument that, well, he's keeping everyone guessing, including American people.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Remember when that was true of President Trump? Yes, they gave him a lot of compliments of that, too.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They would never let any -- if he said anything that was not like down to the decimal point, they would crucify him.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Look, essentially the White House's role is some version of "The Princess Bride" now. That word he used, it doesn't mean what he thinks it means. It's something different. He was trying to stick to the script. And when it comes to issues like Taiwan, this is too important to get anything wrong about.\par \par \uc2 This is a nation that we have a relationship with that has been very fraught, going up against potentially the most powerful adversary that we have in the world. Everybody's mind is on it given what the Chinese are doing. And here's this former head of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee who seems to be treating it as if he's just another senator ou there just kind of shooting his mouth off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But he's always kind of B.S.-ed his way through life.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: He totally has.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. I hate to say it that way. It's blunt. But he's just kind of --\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: He's just there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He could say, hey, buddy. How are you doing? Good to see you, Xi. Let's go up in the mountain.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: That kind of backslapping approach is fine and good when you are a senator from Delaware, maybe. But when you're the commander-in-chief, we don't have the flexibility to have someone who doesn't know what's actually going on and know the import that his words have.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Ben, you get the sense that there are some on the left who are beginning to see that this is going down the tubes, and they could lose like 100 seats in the House.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Oh, my gosh. They are on the cusp of a total cliff here. They're all worried about it. And what they're looking at is a scenario where you're going from a leadership team in \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who have been around for basically 30 years, to what? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and people like who are in between them and the Squad? It's a really bad scenario for Democrats. They're not ready for it. And it's why they're trying to do crazy things like make Mayor Pete seem like a presidential possibility.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love his, we're going to do like the Berlin Airlift now for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He's on the bike. He's going to on the bike doing it.\par \par \uc2 Ben, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Up next, I can't believe I'm saying this, but I'm starting to miss a certain former Biden administration official. I'll tell you who it is, and I really, really miss this person, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our why we're sad about Psaki moment of the night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: But look, it is, by not, without having a fairer tax code, which is what I'm talking about, then all -- manufacturing workers, cops, it's not fair for them to have to pay higher taxes than the folks that, who are, who are not paying taxes at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What are we looking down at? If only we could circle back. She really was the most competent person the Biden administration had, and now she is gone. So all hell could break loose.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Don't forget, set your DVR, always stay connected with us. Remember, USA made Freedom Matters gear on LauraIngraham.com, all proceeds to go charity, the Shoreline Soup Kitchens and Pantries.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. "GUTFELD!" and the crew is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220524ei5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Progressives Hope Abortion Boosts Primaries; Democrats Battle in New York; Biden's Trip to Asia; Brian Deese is Interviewed about a Recession, Oil, and Baby Formula; Title 42 Stays in Place; Interview with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 ; Road to the Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jessica Tarlov, Karl Rove, Jacqui Heinrich, Alex  Hoff, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 9,517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 As President Biden, elected as uniter, signals that he's done trying to work with Republicans, and former President Trump tallies his wins and losses in the first big round of primaries, the gloves are off.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania still counting votes. And now, we are just two days from big races in Alabama and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): It is the final stretch in Georgia's pivotal Republican governor's race where a former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's pick --\par \par \uc2 DAVID PERDUE\uc2 , GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Together, we can take back our state.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- faces an incumbent embraced by former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R), GEORGIA: Georgians want a governor that's going to be fighting for them today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're on the road to the midterms behind the scenes with both campaigns.\par \par \uc2 And right now, Pennsylvania voters await the outcome of a tight GOP Senate contest.\par \par \uc2 We'll speak with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  about the influence of the MAGA movement and finding unity in November.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president is focused on doing everything in his power to address the Putin price hike.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The White House points fingers elsewhere as the stock market dives, gas prices break records, and parents search for baby formula on empty shelves.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask the president's chief economic adviser Brian Deese about the plan to ease the pain.\par \par \uc2 Then -- the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  says it's preparing for the threat of violence if the Supreme Court delivers a historic ruling on abortion in the coming weeks. We'll ask our Sunday panel how the fight for Roe v. Wade is shaping midterms, and whether it could boost progressive's hopes in Tuesday's runoff in Texas.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): And hello again from FOX News.\par \par \uc2 This morning, there is still no winner in Pennsylvania's Republican Senate race, still too close to call, likely headed for a recount. Even as former President Trump pushes his pick to go ahead and declare victory. That as two southern states brace for their key primaries on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And right now, President Biden is on the ground in Asia. He's on a six-day trip, shoring up U.S. Pacific relations and hoping to show that he's working to break the supply chain backlog -- this against the backdrop of an unpredictable economy here at home.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak live with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  about these hard-fought Republican primaries across the nation, but first, we turn to Alex Hoff live in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, where the Republican Senate race remains in limbo this Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, very much so. Right now, the margin between Dr. Oz and David McCormick is 0.1 percent. So unless that margin grows to half a percent either through mailing or provisional ballots, the Pennsylvania law requires a secretary of state to order a recount.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Friday, the Allegheny County Board of Election swore in a review board. They are now tasked with researching at least 1,900 provisional ballots cast on Tuesday. Oz and McCormick campaign representatives may then challenge ballots. Election officials are promising transparency.\par \par \uc2 DAVID VOYE, ALLEGHENY COUNTY ELECTIOONS DIVISION MANAGER: Every vote will be counted and this is probably the tightest statewide race I've been a part of.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Campaign reps were also in sight at a warehouse in Pittsburgh, where voting data was reed from ten problematic precinct machines.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: We can see victory ahead, and it's all because of you.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: When all the votes are tallied, I am confident we will win.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: While Oz called for party unity, the men who endorsed him, former president Trump, urged him to declare victory Wednesday without all the votes without all the votes cod. Trump also blamed a surprise contender, Kathy Barnette, saying she took votes he feels would have gone to Oz.\par \par \uc2 Like in 2020, mail-in ballots are once again a point of contention. On Friday, a Pennsylvania appeals court ruled in a different case that ballots lacking a written date on the envelope must still be counted. It's unclear how this race will be impacted by the ruling, but McCormick's campaign, which have been slightly favored by mail-in ballots, reached out to companies to make sure they were aware.\par \par \uc2 In response, Oz's campaign released a statement saying his opponent's lawyers are, quote, "following the Democrats playbook."\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, another hotly contested race was called Friday. Progressive Summer Lee won the 12th congressional district primary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Now, Lee was endorsed by House squad members and Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , but her opponents vocalized concern over past statements she made that they called anti-Israel -- Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex, thank you very much. Alex Hoff reporting from Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chairwoman McDaniel, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday". Good to have you here this morning.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , \uc2 RNC\uc2  CHAIRWOMAN: Great to be with you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's talk first about Pennsylvania, the Senate race. It's about 1,100 votes apart between \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and Dr. Oz. And President Trump spoke out on his Truth Social social media site and he said: Dr. Oz should declare victory. It makes it much harder for them to cheat with the ballots that they, quote, just happened to find.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that Dr. Oz should go ahead and declare victory?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I'm not going to speak for Dr. Oz. What I will say is the Republican legislature in Pennsylvania put forward an election integrity bill that Governor Wolf vetoed earlier this year. If that bill had been passed, we would not be in this situation.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is right, and others, that we should not have no excuse absentee voting. This influx of mail-in voting is clearly showing that systems are not ready for that and Pennsylvania is a case of that right now. And I think a lot of this lies at the foot -- the feet of Governor Wolf, why we are having this disaster in Pennsylvania right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But -- so the former president is suggesting that another Republican who is supported by people who used to work in his administration is searching for votes or doing something that is -- that is untoward?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled this year, and the \uc2 RNC\uc2  very much supports, that ballots should not be counted without a date. I think that's the law in Pennsylvania. I think that should be followed.\par \par \uc2 And we certainly do not think that ballots without dates should be counted, because how do you know when they came in? I think that's common sense and that is definitely where the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and the GOP is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, if \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  were to win, will he be supported by President Trump, or is he going to keep saying, you know, that he won in a way that wasn't -- that wasn't fair or that was fraudulent? It seems odd, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I think every Republican is going to be supporting the Republican nominee in these states, including President Trump. The \uc2 RNC\uc2  will be supporting -- we do not want Fetterman, a Democrat socialist, the nominee on the Pennsylvania ticket, to be the senator.\par \par \uc2 We know what's at stake with the balance of power. We see the disaster that our country is going through under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So we're going to rally around. Primaries are difficult. They're challenging, and then we come together afterwards and focus on who we really need to defeat, which is the Democrat.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, "The Wall Street Journal" this week, looking at sort of the bigger picture of all of this, and reminding people of the situation that happened in the runoff in Georgia, in the Senate race there when the GOP lost those two key Senate seats and with it the majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And this is what they write: Angry about his own loss, Mr. Trump told GOP voters not to trust the process. That paved the way for President Biden's $1.9 trillion COVID package, the ensuing 8.5 percent inflation and a liberal Supreme Court justice in waiting.\par \par \uc2 Are they right about that, Ronna?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, I disagree. I mean, President Trump was in Georgia I think the day before the runoff in the Loeffler and Perdue races, down there to turn out the vote, to take back those two Senate seats. The president has sent out many, many emails, and I've seen him calling on people to vote, saying the only vote that won't count is the one you don't cast.\par \par \uc2 So he is absolute where proponent of people getting out of voting because we know what's at stake. We need one seat to win back the Senate and five to win back the House, and the American people are suffering and we need Republicans to win in November.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But when you cast doubt on the process, we know from the polling that happened in Georgia that it made some people in certain districts very wary of participating in it. So whether or not he may have, you know, supported it in terms of robocalls and other things, and coming in the day be far, some of that was sort of baked in a cake with the voters. At least that's what was revealed in the polling there.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, it's still in the Republican Party concerns about election integrity. 2020 was a different election. There was more mail-in ballots than ever, many of the safeguards of voter ID were removed, and you've seen Georgia and Florida and Texas and other states passed comprehensive election reform that require voter ID for absentee ballots.\par \par \uc2 That's just common sense. That's where most Americans are. They agree that we should show our ID to vote, and Democrats don't.\par \par \uc2 And get rid of ballot harvesting and get rid of drop boxes and have same- day voting. These are common sense measures that will ensure integrity in the election, and the problem is, Martha, Democrats want the delta to be longer than ever. They want to vote earlier and then count the ballots as late as possible. So it's like a two-month delta.\par \par \uc2 When you have this type of delay in counting the ballots, it always creates concern with voters.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Republicans have stepped up. The \uc2 RNC\uc2  has built the largest election integrity team across the country heading into 2022, in November, and this is something that's necessary to ensure our voters that we're watching and we're going to make sure it's transparent and fair.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's take a look at Georgia, because a huge governor's race happening there on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And we can take a look at the polls here. We know that there is a very interesting backdrop because you have President Trump endorsing and supporting \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , and he's at 28 percent right now. You can see that slide from 39 in March.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kemp, the incumbent governor, is at 60 percent. He's being supported by President Trump's former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So tell me what you think about the served here for Kemp given Trump's endorsement?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: You know, President Trump has endorsed in 84 races, he's won 81. That's like an A+.\par \par \uc2 Just a note, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not being asked to endorse in any race because no Democrat wants to be seen with him, including \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who is running in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I talked to people in Georgia who love President Trump and like Kemp. I think that's what you're seeing in some of the voters in Georgia, but we are going to see what happens on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The votes haven't been counted, Kemp needs to pass a 50 percent threshold to win the nomination outright and we'll see what happens then.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think it was a mistake for the former president to kind of coax \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  to run for governor?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  is perfectly capable of making his own choices. And President Trump obviously has gotten involved.\par \par \uc2 And the power of his endorsement is astounding. You look at JD Vance, who was down in third place, and he catapulted to first. You look at \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 . You look at these races where he's probably the only person in either party whose endorsement can change the complexion of a race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, I don't doubt that at all. I would guess in most of these cases, the only person whose endorsement is something that most voters even have an awareness of is probably the former vice president.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, it's interesting, because the \uc2 RGA\uc2 , the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2 , is really putting their le behind the candidates who are not backed by President Trump, and they say that in that world, they are doing well.\par \par \uc2 They look at Idaho. They look at Nebraska, where the former president's endorsement did not lead to success in those races. And they poured $5 million into this Georgia race.\par \par \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  saying that he thinks that the former president is on a personal vendetta tour. \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  of Arizona says the GOP should not spend one more moment talking about 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, they see this Kemp surge as proof that what they're doing is working.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: You know, I think the power of incumbency plays into that. The \uc2 RGA\uc2  supports incumbents.\par \par \uc2 I think we're going to get this season and I'm going to be grateful when we do and we really focus on the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have rising inflation. We have a baby formula crisis. We have a border that's surging. We have a drug crisis. We have what's happening in Ukraine. We have gas prices going through the roof.\par \par \uc2 I think in the end, yes, we're going to have some contention through primaries. That always happens. But when the dust settles, Republicans are going to unite, including President Trump, to make sure that we win back the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 We know what's at stake for the American people and we know that if we're fighting or disagreeing among each other, we're hurting our message in talking about with the Democrats are doing and the failure that they've brought to the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's take a quick look at the Alabama Senate race --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and what's going on there.\par \par \uc2 Mo Brooks was originally endorsed by President Trump. Then, President Trump pulled that endorsement and Mo Brooks slid precipitously.\par \par \uc2 But now, you see something else happening. Now he is sort of thin spitting distance of Katie Britt, who is still ahead.\par \par \uc2 What's your -- what's your take on what's going to happen in this Alabama Senate race this week?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, I don't think we're going to get a final say on this Alabama race. You have to get over 50 percent to clinch the nomination. I don't think any of the three candidates are going to get over 50 percent, so we're going to go to a runoff and then we'll fire out who the nominee is going to be in Alabama. I think we're going to keep that seat.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of Republican incumbents who left the Senate this cycle. Alabama is one of them. We need to make sure we hold all of the seats, North Carolina being others, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 And we have to unite as a party, that would be my one message. As these primaries are contentious and you fight so hard to get the nomination, remember what the real goal is and that is us uniting as a party to defeat the Democrats and take back the Senate and the House, because the American people are truly, truly suffering. And we have a vacuum in leadership.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ronna, let me ask about that because some people say, well, you know, the former president shouldn't have weighed in the primary process. He should have stayed on the sidelines. And the same could be said may be of these Republican governors as well. Let the people decide, and then get into this game.\par \par \uc2 Do you -- are you concerned? I think you have said you're concerned that some of this process might hurt your candidates in the general election.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Oh, I'm not surprised that he got involved. Listen, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  stays neutral for a reason. We legally have to stay neutral, but it is helpful because we didn't put our thumb on the scale and we get to bring everybody together after the fact and have a kumbaya moment.\par \par \uc2 But President Trump is always going to get involved in primaries, as is other -- as other candidates across the country. That's just the nature of politics.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I just want to put this on the screen. Madison Cawthorn, obviously, has gotten a lot of attention in this race. He lost his bid to retain his House seat. The president kind of supported him, the former president, last minute in that race.\par \par \uc2 But here's what he just said, Madison Cawthorn: The time for genteel politics as usual has come to an end. It's time for the rise of the new right. It's time for Dark MAGA to truly take command.\par \par \uc2 We have an enemy to defeat, but we will never be able to defeat them until we defeat the cowardly and weak members of our own party. Their days are numbered. We are coming.\par \par \uc2 What you think of that? And what is -- what is "Dark MAGA"?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I don't know what "Dark MAGA" is.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It sounds scary.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: It sounds like the Star Wars thing, like the dark side of the force. I don't know. I don't know what that is.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, it was a very well fought primary. Madison had some issues that came out. He was a rising star in our party, and we need to make sure we retain that seat with Edwards, who defeated him. And Madison did the right thing by conceding.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, something (ph) --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: We need to focus on the Democrats, and I would say this to every Republican. They are the ones in control. They are ones destroying our country, and Republicans who are fighting each other constantly, that is not helping us defeat Democrats in November, and that needs to be the focus.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just a quick last question. Both "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and Peggy Noonan this week said that from what they are seeing out there, that the president is sort of chasing his base and trying to solidify them, and they pointed to that last-minute endorsement of Cawthorn -- rather than leading them.\par \par \uc2 Quick reaction to that before I let you go, Ronna?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah, I disagree. I mean, you look at the rallies he has. You look at the phenomenal fundraising, the juggernaut of fundraising that he's had. You look at his poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 I think the base is really looking to him on a lot of things, and he's going to be critical to help us win in the midterms and help turn out Trump voters who take his lead.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , thank you very much. Good to see you, Chairwoman.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to be with you. You bet.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up next, "FOX News Sunday" is on the road to the midterms as two Republican rivals try to close the deal in a governor's race in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And we'll bring in our Sunday group on more of these key races that are fired up for this week when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "FOX News Sunday" on the Road to the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia this week where the incumbent governor appears to be opening a very big lead ahead of Tuesday's Republican primary. As we showed you earlier, the latest FOX News poll has Governor Brian Kemp at 60 percent, a whopping 32 percent lead over former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 . In fact Kemp has tripled his advantage since March.\par \par \uc2 The race pits Kemp with backing from former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  against \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , who is backed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Mark Meredith spent time with both candidates, who hope to take on Democrats \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  come November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Behind all the smiles, hugs, and handshakes is one of the most bitter political primaries in Georgia's history. Georgia Governor Brian Kemp facing a challenge from his former friend, \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KEMP: It's a tough business. If you want a friend in politics, get you a dog.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Kemp, who leads Perdue in both polling and fundraising, is fending off repeated attacks from former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Brian Kemp is a turncoat, he's a coward, and he's a complete and total disaster.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: President Trump endorsed \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  in February.\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: Mr. President, thank you for coming to Georgia again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Give me \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  over Kemp any day.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: A primary challenge from an established Republican like Perdue was once unthinkable in Georgia, but then came the 2020 election. President Trump accused Kemp of betraying the party by certifying Georgia's 16 electoral votes for President Biden. Before even leaving office, Trump vowed revenge.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I will be here in about a year and a half and painting against your governor, I guarantee you that.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Soon after Georgia went blue in 2020, Perdue lost his seventh seat in a runoff to Democrat \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: Brian Kemp has let us down.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Now, Perdue campaigns heavily on Trump's claim of voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 Do you think relitigating 2020 will get you into the governors mansion?\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: That's not why we're doing this. I said when I announced, this is about truth.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: While Perdue insists his campaign is about more than election integrity, supporters like Richard Giles tell us it's the only reason they are interested in Perdue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My vote probably didn't count because of so much fraud.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Some of \uc2 Perdue\uc2 's former colleagues express shock of her his full embrace of the issue.\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: Somebody is quoting Romney about me? Seriously?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: Yeah. I would discount that totally. Talk to people who really know me. There's nothing changed in my ethos.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Since 2020, Kemp too is focused on election reforms but insists he had no choice but to certify Biden's win.\par \par \uc2 Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is now coming to Kemp's defense, campaigning with them tomorrow ahead of the primary.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON MOYE, GEORGIA REPUBLICAN PARTY EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: We are all fired up with this election coming up.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: We sat down with the executive director of Georgia's Republican Party outside of headquarters. Brandon Moye says this party has learned since its mistakes in 2020, and is focused on building grassroots operations for November.\par \par \uc2 Do truly believe that everybody will get on the same page between now and November?\par \par \uc2 MOYE: I do believe everyone will get on the same page between now and November because again, our main goal is to keep \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  out of the governors mansion.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who lost to Kemp in the gubernatorial race back in 2018, is running unopposed in this year's Democratic primary. She is considered a rising star within her party and is already raising a lot of money to challenge Republicans this fall.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: I would be a better governor because I think about all of Georgia and I know how to get it done.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But to win, Abrams will have to overcome challenges. She's drawn flak for skipping out on a meeting with President Biden when he visited Georgia in January. And she faced widespread criticism for this photo, smiling while elementary students surrounding her remained masked.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: We are getting up every single day to make sure that we're fighting as hard as we can so that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  will not be our governor.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Kemp insists Abrams slip-ups and his own record create a winning combination.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Despite going through a pandemic and where we are now has been incredible. We have the lowest employment rate in the history of our state.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Perdue says if he loses the primary, he'll still support Kemp over Abrams, but whether the former president would eventually play nice remains far less certain.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried at all that Trump will try to spoil your race?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: No, I'm not, because Georgia Republicans are scared to death of \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark Meredith reporting from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And now it's time for our Sunday group. Former Bush White House advisor Karl Rove, "Associated Press" executive editor Julie Pace, and Democratic strategist and co-host of "The Five," Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 Great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Karl, let me start with you, what's your outlook as you look at this very interesting Tuesday governors race, and what's the best measure of how much juice in these races the former president still has?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: First of all, I'm a friend and a supporter of Brian Kemp. We're both are wearing green ties but that is totally coincidence. But I think the polls point to a strong win by Kemp on Tuesday and it's because his record as governor.\par \par \uc2 And what we've learned throughout these contests is if a candidate has a strong record and a strong message, even if they're not endorsed by the former president, they have a chance to win. We saw it in Idaho with Governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 . We saw it in an open race for governor in Nebraska and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 It really comes down to the quality of the candidate and the quality of their message, and in many instances, that's a lot of people who didn't have the president's endorsement to succeed. Even when the president has endorsed, for example, you pointed out he carried JD Vance across the finish line -- no ifs, ands, or buts about it -- because the power of President Trump's endorsement.\par \par \uc2 But even then, 68 percent of the Republican voters said I'm going with somebody else, and Vance won with 32 percent of the votes. It's important, but it's not always determinative.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Julie, you heard \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  moments ago say that President Trump -- former President Trump -- has an almost perfect record. She gave him an A+ for his endorsements.\par \par \uc2 Looking through the lens of the Pennsylvania Senate race, which is likely headed to a recount, what are your thoughts on that? And same question to you about how much juice we are learning the former president has in these races?\par \par \uc2 PACE: Well, I think to your point earlier in the show, Martha, you know, endorsements in general I don't think carry as much weight as they used to, though I think there is one exception, which is I think former President Trump, who is looked at by many people in the Republican Party as a barometer, where should they go with some of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 So I think he can be value added, and certainly we've seen to Karl's point, JD Vance I think being a very prominent example of that. But, you know, that endorsement that he made of Oz in the Pennsylvania primary was seen as pretty risky for many people in the party, and the closeness of that race I think verifies just how risky that was in terms of determining his power in these primaries.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, he will continue to make these endorsements, and I think he's going to have some success, and then he's also going to have some failures here. I do think we are probably pretty early in the overall political process to say what that means for Trump himself though if he does move into 2024 as a candidate for president again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. I would just say obviously the biggest test is going to be the general election and then there will be another look at all of this, right? In terms of will he back this person and then they lost in the general or they won in the general, and it is just such a unique environment where we've never witnessed this before where you have a former president who is essentially everyone that he's going to run again, which just puts this into such unique environment.\par \par \uc2 But, Jessica, as Republicans deal with their own drama, Democrats also have some of their own, and we're looking at this race in Pennsylvania, the house primary race, where Summer Lee was able to just barely beat Steve Irwin. She is considered a progressive. I think looking at this tweet, she kind of lays out where she stands.\par \par \uc2 She says: Instead of thanking black women, black voters, give us the investments we need and deserve, let's redistribute wealth -- she calls for in this and also says to abolish the carceral state and build Black- directed political power.\par \par \uc2 So your thoughts on what we're learning from that race when you look at Summer Lee prevailing over her more moderate candidate?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Summer Lee's victory is no doubt a big boon for the progressive wing of the party and I think we're going to talk about the Texas runoff on Tuesday, because Jessica Cisneros and \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , which if she does pull this off -- it's a rematch of 2020 -- will be another indication that there is some fire behind the progressive wing.\par \par \uc2 But net-net, last week's primaries, were big wins for moderate candidates on the whole. John Fetterman, who beat \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  very decidedly was a \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  supporter and tact way to the middle, sounding like \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  on a lot points, though obviously appearing quite differently and presentation in order to be successful in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But since 2016, this has been the narrative of what's going on with Democrats. I think moderate candidates certainly have the best chance of winning a wider swath of population, but hats off to Summer Lee. It's a terminus victory, and I think that is Democrats continue to argue that we are the big tent party, having her voice alongside other progressives is something that's hugely valuable to us. So, a big congratulations there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Karl, Jessica brings up the \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  race against Jessica Cisneros, who is more progressive, and he, obviously, is more moderate. He's -- he's a pro-life Democrat, which almost doesn't exist in the party anymore. And -- and he's running with this backdrop of fences going up around the Supreme Court and protests happening outside of their homes. Abortion, obviously, is becoming an issue that could -- could work to the benefit of some of these Democrat candidates.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I think \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who's well respected, is in a very tough race and might go down on Tuesday. And with all due respect, Summer Lee is not the only progressive who won. A progressive knocked off an incumbent centrist Democrat congressman in Oregon and -- and -- and liberals for -- you know, not liberals, traditional liberals, but far left members of the Democratic Party won primaries, two of them in North Carolina for safe Democratic seats, and two of them in competitive races in the -- in the state of Oregon. So, this is not just -- Summer Lee's victory is not just an isolated incident. The -- the Democrat left is on the ascendancy, particularly in races for the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 , and that caucus is going to get a lot more squad members and the Democrats in Texas may, on Tuesday, nominate two members who would associate themselves with the squad, one of them against an incumbent Democrat, namely \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 . This is -- the -- the left of the Democratic Party is in ascendancy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hey, let's look at this comment from \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , representative from New York, about this fascinating redistricting that happened in New York where Democrats thought they might be able to pick up some seats in New York. He is looking at the lines here and he says, now they're trying to move the table, drawing a congressional map that robs us of power and takes a sledgehammer to black districts. It's enough to make Jim Crow blush.\par \par \uc2 And, Jessica, what's fascinating about this is some of the matchups that we're going to see in New York now.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to see \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  running against \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 . Some people in Manhattan saying it's eastside versus westside between these two. And take a look at this one. \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  is going to move his district. He'll be running against \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , who's going to, you know, rise from the ashes after his mayoral run, and -- and he wants to have a seat in Congress.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of what's going on here?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, I -- I don't think the \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  is going to be making any major waves in terms of a congressional race. And \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  is someone who is hugely popular. And I think it was smart of him to shift over to this new district rather than stay where he was originally.\par \par \uc2 I'm a native New Yorker. I grew up in a \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  district, lived in \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 's for a little while, and now I'm back in Nadler's. And the sight of the two of them campaigning together -- I don't know if you saw it, but \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  completely dejected, looking, sitting to the side as he listened to \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  discuss her support of Israel, which is an issue that's incredibly important for New Yorkers, as put a lot of people in a very difficult position.\par \par \uc2 I know that all of my friends and colleagues are people who support both \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  and \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2 , who have been great for New York City, and this is going to be incredibly complicated.\par \par \uc2 And another important point about that is what happened in New York's 11th on Staten Island, that the ruling -- the new ruling kept Park Slope, which is a hugely liberal district where \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  actually lives, out of New York 11th seat. So that may make Max Rose, who was trying to mount a comeback, sit out this race in the end.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating. Interesting take.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, panel. We'll see you in a little bit.\par \par \uc2 We'll take a break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Americans are feeling the pressure in stores and at the pump. We're going to bring in the president's chief economic advisor to talk about what they are planning to do to tackle all of this, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: On this Sunday morning, the president is in Asia talking trade, semiconductors, supply chains, while trying to forge stronger ties with our allies in China's backyard.\par \par \uc2 But back home it is the heavy burden of inflation, steep gas prices, and even an unthinkable baby formula shortage that Americans say concerns them most right now.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll ask the \uc2 U.S. National Economic Council\uc2  director, Brian Deese, about how soon we could see some relief.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's turn to Jacqui Heinrich, live in Tokyo, traveling with the president with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is just beginning the second stop of his Asia swing in Tokyo, where trade partnerships are in focus. But his first few days in South Korea showed security concerns are no longer just in the background.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Before departing Seoul, a no-frills message from President Biden to North Korean Leader Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you have a message for Kim Jong-un while you're here?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello. Period.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: New plans to consider expanding joint military exercises on and around the Korean peninsula speaking for themselves. The announcement alongside South Korea's newly elected president comes as Pyongyang plans a possible nuclear or ballistic missile test while Biden's in the region.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not concerned if that's what you're suggesting.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's first trip to Asia as president showing favor for sticks over carrots to contain threats. Behind a response to Russia's war in Ukraine, a thinly veiled message to China with regard to Taiwan, make a move and face global isolation.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Putin's brutal and unprovoked war in Ukraine has further spotlighted the need to secure our critical supply chains so that our economy, our economic and our national security are not dependent on countries that don't share our values.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The trip, billed as an effort to deepen economic ties in the Indo-Pacific shares a clear nexus with security worries. Japan also looking to the U.S. for reassurance over Chinese intrusions in territorial waters, now welcoming Biden to launch a new trade partnership widely seen as a counter to China. The major focus of the framework, reliable supply chains.\par \par \uc2 Most of Biden's presidency has been marked by challenges originating from outside the U.S., but the baby formula shortage stems from problems at home. Biden signed a bill giving more flexibility to buyers using federal assistance programs from Seoul, along with a second bill funding aid for Ukraine. The urgency of both causes prompting the White House to fly the documents from Washington for the president's signature.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And, right now, palates from Germany are in route to Indiana. They're expected to arrive just before 11:00 in the morning Eastern. The White House using the military to move those imports faster with domestic production still not up to speed.\par \par \uc2 Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich reporting from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining us now, Brian Deese, director of the National Economic Council.\par \par \uc2 Brian, welcome back to FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Good to have you here this morning.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: Thank for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to start with the "r" word, because we're hearing a lot more about the possibility of the United States going into a recession. And this comment by \uc2 David Sacks\uc2 , who is a well-known investor, the founder of \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , he gets a lot of attention as a market analyst, really caught a lot of people's eye.\par \par \uc2 Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SACKS\uc2 , PAYPAL FOUNDING COO: From where I sit, the market is really semi-frozen. And this is, again, the worst downturn since the dot com crash. And it looks to me like we're headed into a very serious recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what is your professional opinion as you look at this? Do you agree -- and I could list a number of others who are saying this -- that the United States is headed into a recession in the near term or even over the next six months or so?\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Well, here's where I think we are. Our economy is in a period of transition. We're moving from the strongest economic recovery in modern history to what can be a period of more stable and resilient growth. And while there are absolutely risks with inflation front -- first and foremost, this is what's most important, the United States is better positioned than any other major economy to bring inflation down and address these challenges without giving up all of the economic gains we've made, and that's because of the strength of our recovery.\par \par \uc2 We have the strongest job market in modern history. Americans are getting back to work in jobs with higher pay. And that's meant that Americans can increase their savings, pay down their debt. Businesses are investing. Entrepreneurs are creating new businesses at record rates. And manufacturing is coming back to the United States at record rates as well. All of that progress --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, obviously, those -- those are all good things. But I just want to ask you my -- my question, which is -- and I know recession is -- is a technical term -- but, you know, \uc2 Glenn Hubbard\uc2 , who was a Bush economic advisor, says, you know, whether or not we hit that technical benchmark, Americans feel like they are in a recession. You have \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  saying that she's very concerned about that higher prices are having stagflationary (ph) effects. You've got \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  saying that there's going to be some pain. Unemployment may be up.\par \par \uc2 So I'm asking for your opinion, as the economic advisor at the White House, should people be prepared in the United States that we are or will be in several months heading into a recession?\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Well, what people should know is we are in his period of transition to more stable growth, but people should also take confidence that we are better positioned than any other country to navigate through this and keep our recovery going.\par \par \uc2 Just this week there was an analysis out that said that the United States economy may grow faster this year than China's for the first time in decades. And, again, that's because we have more strength and resilience in our economic recovery than almost anywhere in the world. What we need to do now --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I -- I did see that. They said that the comparison, though, because -- I'm -- I'm sorry to interrupt you, but that -- that they're in the middle of the deepest locked they've had in Covid. And -- and \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  said that that is the reason that we may have a slightly higher growth than -- than China.\par \par \uc2 And with all due respect, I think that Americans feel that they are having -- they're looking at the stock market and they're seeing their savings dwindle and they're making new decisions about whether or not they're going to buy a home or whether or not the tuition payment is going to be a little bit more difficult this year.\par \par \uc2 You know, so I'm just asking, what -- what do you recommend to them in terms of specifics and what they can do to try to whether this a little bit easier as we go through what you're calling a transition?\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Absolutely. And for typical Americans who are driving up to the gas price -- the gas pump or at the grocery store, these prices create real hardship and they also create uncertainty. We understand that. And that's precisely what the president has made very clear that combating inflation is his top economic priority. And what people should know is that we can do this. We know how to do this. First, we need to give the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  the independence to do what it does. It has the tools to combat inflation. Second, we need to reduce costs and make things more affordable for families during this period. So, steps that we can take to reduce the cost of the Internet bills that families pay, or the prescription drug prices that they pay a really important right now. And, third, we need to bring down the federal deficit. Because of the president's policies, we've made a lot of progress on that front, the deficit down $1.5 trillion already this year. But we need to make more progress on that as well. That will help produce price pressures in the economy.\par \par \uc2 If we can do all of those things, than we can build on the historic strengths that we have here in the U.S. economy and we can navigate through to more stable growth that will generate better outcomes for families, because as your segment was just mentioning, we can't go back to a pre- pandemic economy where our supply chains are so vulnerable that something happens overseas and prices go up and people don't have access to things that they need. We need to build a more resilient recovery. We're on the way. We've got to focus on tackling inflation right now, and that's what this president's doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so -- so the -- the biggest factor for most families across the country is gas prices, which you mentioned. And there are -- you know, actually, I just want to play this sound bite from the president because this just came in this morning in South Korea, and here's -- here's what he had to say about the future of gas prices in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thanks to Hyundai, we are being part of this transformative automobile section and accelerating us on the road where we're going to be handing to the United States an all-electric future. And that's what we're shooting for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what does that mean, and all electric future, and how soon? Because I think there are -- there's definitely a -- a contingency in the country which would like to hear the president talk about perhaps doing some things to increase oil and gas supply in the country at least to signal to the markets that we're opening up those spigots a bit, which generally has the impact of starting to lower global oil prices around the country. But that's not what we're hearing from the president right now.\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Well, what we're hearing from the president is we need to distinguish between the short term and the long-term. In the short-term, this president has made clear that we need to increase the supply of oil on the market to blunt the impact of Putin's war in Ukraine and Russian supply coming off the market. So, what the president has done has been to encourage the domestic industry to increase production right now. Not years in the future, but right now. They've committed to increase production by a million barrels a day by the fall. But this president didn't wait and said, we're going to release oil from the strategic petroleum reserve, a million barrels a day between now and then to create a bridge.\par \par \uc2 But, we also know that our car companies and our car industry is moving towards electric vehicles and moving toward an electric vehicle future. We want that transition to happen. We want people to have those choices. It's better for the environment. These cars are -- are fun to drive. People like them. We want more of those cars built in the United States with secure supply chain so that we're not reliant on foreign countries and uncertain supplies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 DEESE: And we want to accelerate that process in a way that will be good for the American consumer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, before I let you go, I just want to ask you one question about the baby formula story, which, obviously, has gotten a ton of attention.\par \par \uc2 So today we have arrivals of baby formula coming from Switzerland through Germany. And I think a lot of people look at this story and they say, that's great, we're really glad there's going to be some relief on the shelves. But on the other hand, how did we get to a point where the United States of America is relying on Switzerland to feed our babies?\par \par \uc2 DEESE: It's -- it's the right question. In the very immediate term, we need to do everything we can to get formula out to the families and the babies that need it. That's why the president has taken this unprecedented step. And, as you mentioned, Operation Fly Formula will have its first flight landing in the U.S. in just a couple of hours. There's about enough formula on that plane, specialty medical grade formula, for about a half a million bottles. That's about 15 percent of the overall national volume this coming week. And we will see additional planes landing over the course of the next couple of days with more formula on it.\par \par \uc2 But you're asking the right question, which is, how did we get to a situation where we have so much market concentration that 90 percent or more of the market is controlled by just three companies. And it goes to this question of supply chain resilience and competition. We need more competition in this market. We need more companies providing their services. And we need to make sure that we never put ourself in a situation where private-sector supply chains can create such risk for American families. So we have some hard questions --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but -- but, Brian, I would just point out that that -- that that situation has existed for a long time. We've known that it was concentrated in -- in one or two companies for a long time. And we also knew, from October and then again in February, that this was coming down the pipeline. Then the president said he's not a mind reader on this issue.\par \par \uc2 So, is there going to be some accountability? You know, who's fault is it? I mean does the -- does the head of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  need to resign from his position, because this is a big deal?\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Look, the short-term issues are important. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  did what it was supposed to do, which was assessed safety and shutdown a facility in the United States. And that was a prudent thing to do because of safety. We're dealing with infants and babies here. Safety has to be paramount.\par \par \uc2 But the broader question is important, which is -- and this is why the president has put a spotlight on the need for more competition, more antitrust enforcement in the United States, because it's not just in the formula industry. We see this in meat packing, for example, where we have too few companies controlling too much of the supply chain and that creates real risks for consumers.\par \par \uc2 So, you have a president now that is saying, we need to pursue competition across the economy in this sector and in others by creating more pathways for small businesses and entrepreneurs to get into these markets, break in, be disruptive, provide services, and create more resilience in that supply chain.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Understood.\par \par \uc2 DEESE: That's an important priority. It's one the president's been on and will stay on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that -- yes, and that's the private sector. But then the public sector also had a responsibility here, which is to make sure that this kind of thing doesn't happen. There were whistle-blowers. So, we'll look forward to see whether or not there is any actual accountability in the government for -- for dropping the ball here. But everyone's glad that those planes are on the way, and we appreciate you giving us an update on that this morning, Brian.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Brian Deese, for joining us. I do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DEESE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up next, our Sunday group is back. White House reaction as the judge lays down his ruling on Title 42 on the United States southern border.\par \par \uc2 And new revelations out from this trial with regards to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's inner circle and what she knew about the Russia collusion narrative.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Back now with our panel for a quick go around.\par \par \uc2 Julie, let's start with you.\par \par \uc2 Much in the news this weekend. This Title 42 will stay in place according to a judge in Louisiana. Obviously, this has been weighing on the White House because there was discussion that as many as 18,000 migrants could cross the border if it was lifted.\par \par \uc2 Your take?\par \par \uc2 PACE: Well, as much as the White House says that they are going to continue to fight to have this policy lifted, there are a lot of Democrats, and probably some in the White House, who are breathing a bit of a sigh of relief because on top of everything else that you were just talking about with Brian Deese, inflation, the baby formula crisis, so many other economic factors, the idea of having a search of the border right now as those midterms draw closer is not exactly what the White House is hoping for in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes,\par \par \uc2 Jessica, your thoughts on them keeping this in place. Is this probably a good thing and a sigh of relief as -- as Julie says?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Julie was being very diplomatic, as it is her job. But I can tell you that Democrats are breathing a sigh of relief that this is at least being, you know, pushed down the proverbial road for a little while. And I -- I don't know how vociferously they're going to be following up on this. This is something that progressives wanted to be lifted, but no one really in the middle. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself was not someone who would look at this and say, oh, we need another few hundred more thousand migrants coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 Karl, with regard to what Brian Deese had to say about inflation of the president's handling of economy, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, if you don't know what the problem is, you can't come up with a solution. And he, obviously, doesn't know what the problem is. The problem was identified last year when the -- when this administration proposed a $1.9 trillion unnecessary Covid stimulus bill. And \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's Treasury secretary, said you're going to throw gasoline on the fire of inflation. And that's exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 And, since then, Brian Deese has been one of the most fervent advocates for making it worse. What you heard him say there, he sort of carefully camouflage it, was, Built Back Better. Let's pass a new bill that supposedly lowers costs by spending lots of government money on free child care, for universal pre-k, pharmaceuticals, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera. Only make it worse. The administration is in bad shape on this issue. A 36 percent approval on the economy, 60 percent disapproval, and, inflation, 26 percent approve, 68 percent disapprove. And that's why policies like the ones that Mr. Deese was talking about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Jessica and Julie, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Panel, we'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that is it for today on this Sunday. Thanks for joining us. I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 You can join me every day on "THE STORY" at 3:00 p.m. Eastern on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week, everyone. We'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220522ei5m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Admin Grappling With Inflation, Recession Fears; Some Experts Warning Of Possible Recession; Dow Falls After Worst Day In Over Two Years; Gas Prices Over $4.00 In Every State For First Time; Fresh Warning Of Recession And Stagflation; Women's March LDR: Summer Of Rage Over Abortion; Senate Approves $40 Billion In Aid To Ukraine; Florida Governor DeSantis Conference On Fentanyl; Families Struggle As Baby Formula Supply Runs Thin; 16-Year-Old Charged In Murder Of Connecticut High School Student\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Aishah Hasnie, Lauren Simonetti, David Asman,  David Spunt, Chad Pergram, Marc Thiessen, Molly Line \par \uc2 8,781 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Though, you're taking away the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's brand, you've got a whole franchise of restaurants that are now going to be called, what?\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: I don't know, but I guess the food will still be you know, delicious and nutritious.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: There you go, as always. It's what my kids telling me. Thanks for joining us. I'm John Roberts. We'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm Gillian Turner in for Sandra Smith. Be sure to set your DVR so you don't miss a minute of "AMERICA REPORTS." And THE STORY with Martha MacCallum starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much. Good to see you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. So, this brutal headline greeted the President of the United States if you happen to see it this morning. Now, it will be his job to try to keep the country on the tracks amid growing warnings that despite the promises of transitory inflation, and a soft landing, the United States appears to be headed toward a potentially deep recession.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SACKS, CRAFT VENTURES CO-FOUNDER: From where I sit, the market is really semi-frozen. And this is, again, the worst downturn since the (INAUDIBLE) crash, and it looks to me like we're heading into a very serious recession.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think we're headed towards recession?\par \par \uc2 LLOYD BLANKFEIN\uc2 , GOLDMAN SACHS SENIOR CHAIR: We're certainly heading. It's certainly a very, very high-risk factor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. OK, so gas prices $4.00 a gallon. That's never happened before where it's that case in every single state in the country. And twice this month, the Dow tumbled more than a thousand points as the markets get closer and closer to full-bear territory here, which is a 20 percent decline from the highs. David Asman and Lauren Simonetti, two of the best brains of Fox Business are here to tell you what this really means and how long this will go on for, maybe. They look into their crystal ball in just a moment. But first, to Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie live where they are dealing with all of this are trying to anyway at the White House. Hi, Aisha.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. That's right. And so, the president unfortunately left this morning for his East Asia trip without really talking about any of these crises across America, and really without offering any new solution. So, this is where we are right now with gas prices. This is the 10th day in a row now that we've broken a record for gas. In fact, every single state in the country now paying more than four bucks a gallon, you can't go anywhere to get it cheaper than that.\par \par \uc2 Households are spending an estimated 5000 a year on gas and it could get worse. \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  warning prices will surpass six bucks nationwide by August. And get this the gas station chain 76, now reportedly reprogramming its pumps in Washington State to sell $10.00 gas, double digits -- it's scary stuff. So, the White House is responding to all of this by saying it released oil from the petroleum reserve already, and then blaming President Putin for the price hike, on inflation, and the stock market woes, the White House passing the buck to the Fed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: You know, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is independent. We leave them to make their own policy decisions. We do not get involved in that, and nothing has changed on how we see the stock market. We do not, that's not something that we keep an eye on every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Martha, that's not something that keep an eye on every day. But perhaps they should as a new Quinnipiac poll reveals that 55 percent of Americans are extremely worried about the rising price of gas and goods, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Aishah, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Fox Business Correspondent Lauren Simonetti, she's the host of "WE'RE MOMMING TODAY!" on Fox Nation; and Fox Business Anchor, David Asman. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX NATION HOST: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Lauren, let me start with you. Because the latest statistics show that Americans are spending about $5,000 a year. So, when you think about you know the average annual income and the chunk that, that is of your entire disposable income, it -- there's a huge ripple effects of that.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Just on gasoline.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: And then, if \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 's prediction actually happens by August, gas is 37 percent more than it is now at $6.20 a gallon nationally, across the nation. I mean, we're really thinking, can we get there? Or do you have demand destruction if you get that high? People are still going to need to go to work. They're going to still need to you know, to go places, they'll cut back, yes, but they're paying close to that in California. So, that's a possibility that hits the lower income people the most.\par \par \uc2 And when we look back, and we start to question, are we in a recession? What caused the recession? What's the clear answer right now? It's the policies from the administration because we have so much energy in this country. If, if the administration would just loosen up and say make Texas feel more comfortable, make North Dakota feel more comfortable, frack, drill, we have the resource, we can certainly have the price of gasoline that we're seeing right now and it's only going up as we head into the summer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, this is from a New York Post op-ed by Steven Hayward, he says: "President Biden and the Democrats seem determined to repeat every policy mistake of the 1970s. And might not, and it might not end until Biden attempts to impose price controls and rationing."\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes, it's crazy. And Lauren's exactly right, it was completely unavoidable -- completely avoidable. This whole thing started with the war on fossil fuels. That's, that's where we really saw the pop happened long before Russia happened. We saw the price of gas even before he was inaugurated, as soon, as he was elected. The week that he was elected, we began to see price of gasoline go up, because everybody knew his, his war on fossil fuels would have these consequences.\par \par \uc2 But you combine that with other policies, for example, keeping people in their homes, paying them to stay at home for much too long a period of time, regulations, increasing regulations dramatically all over for all kinds of things, whether it's road projects for all the stimulus money that went out, and the money itself, obviously, that what happened was we were printing money like crazy that actually began under Trump, but it was it was calming down a lot of those, those pandemic programs were being pulled back.\par \par \uc2 But Biden doubled down on them with his $1.9 trillion plan as soon as he came into office. And a lot of those had nothing to do with COVID, as we know. So, it was it was a completely avoidable situation. There is good news, even though I think we're in a recession right now, what, what we heard from David Sachs, at the beginning, he's a top-venture capitalists, the guy that you had on saying we're definitely in a recession, he knows what he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 He was involved with startups with \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and Pay Pal, the guy knows his stuff. I agree with him. The good news is that we have 11.5 million unfilled jobs right now, because we were coming out of the pandemic. We also have a strong housing market, because there is a dearth of inventory of houses on the market, partly because of the pandemic, because the labor shortage. So, if we, if we get into a difficult recession here, we do have those factors that would mitigate against it being prolonged, the recession itself.\par \par \uc2 But I think we're in a recession, we're going to have to pay the price for these mistakes. And until he changes his policies, and as you and I talked politically, I don't think he's going to do it until the election forces him to do it, and we have a change in Congress. Things are not going to change with regard to the price at the pump.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Lauren, what signs do you see, we just heard from Aishah at the White House. The president is on his way to Asia. This is also an important trip, it's one that, you know, that we need to shore up our allies in that area, we need to show that we have a strong connection with them where, where China's aggression is concerned. But when he gets back from this trip, this is going to still be staring him in the face. There's very limited things that the president can actually do.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Right now, you have to do 180, and he's not willing to do that. I don't think the blame game is working. I don't think that resonates with Americans who can't find formula to feed their babies and can't afford gasoline, which they can't actually find to fill their gas tanks, because that's what makes you go vote.\par \par \uc2 So, we need a different sort of tone coming from the administration yesterday, when the new press secretary had her press conference, and the Dow fell almost 1200 points. She was asked, well, does, does the president looked at this? And her answer was like, no, we, we really don't watch it every day, that's not our sort of thing. How many Americans are invested in the stock market? And that's your nonchalant answer? So, maybe it's being more on the front foot and leading and foreseeing crises before they have - -\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: For example, opening up the spigot of Venezuela, they say we're going to get a million barrels per day of oil from Venezuela, Venezuela only produces 700,000 a day. So, they want to gin up oil production in the dirtiest, most inefficient oil company in the world, which is (INAUDIBLE) in Venezuela, meanwhile, shutting down U.S. oil production, which is done in a much cleaner fashion, in a way that employs millions of Americans, and of course, directly affects consumers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, and for people who look at the, you know, the Keystone Pipeline, for example, and you know, a lot of people say, oh, it wouldn't make a difference, right? It's not actually, but, but it's all about the supply on the market. It's 800,000 barrels a day adding supply to the market.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And it's the clog, it's the clog of diesel in the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . That's one of the reasons Diesel is going through the roof too, and that's affecting everybody.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, you guys. Great to have you here. Lauren Simonetti and David Asman, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, there was nothing there, that from a key witness in the case against ex-Clinton Campaign Attorney \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 , accused of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  when he passed along information about a Trump Russia link that he says he was very concerned about. But as you just heard, one of his you know, the colleagues at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said, you know, we looked into it, it wasn't anything there.\par \par \uc2 But still, they kept pushing through a different of agents who were interested in -- at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , all the way up to the Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 . Marc Thiessen, and Victor Davis Hanson next. And THE STORY exclusive with the executive director of the Women's March, a group vowing to quote have a summer of rage in response to the leaked SCOTUS decision on Roe v. Wade, when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is day one of our feminist future, and it is day one of a summer of rage where we will be ungovernable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Back to the Durham courtroom today, a former Clinton campaign attorneys claim of a possible connection between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and a Russian bank during the 2016 election went all the way to then \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , who you see in the middle box there. That is according to the key witness in the Sussmann trial. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 's former top lawyer James Baker testified that he briefed Comey and his deputy about this tip that came in from Sussmann, and quote, "I think they were quite concerned about it."\par \par \uc2 Victor Davis Hanson is standing by with his take on all of this. But first, to correspondent David Spunt, get us caught up on what's going on today in what is an ever evolving fascinating story inside that D.C. courtroom.\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 It really is fascinating. And listen, there are a lot of names and a lot of details in this case, but at the end of the day, this case is really about just one charge, making a false statement to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 James Baker currently still on the stand. I say that he took the stand late yesterday and he rejoined that testimony this morning at 9:00 a.m. He is expected to go through the rest of the day.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): We have some video of Baker, entering the federal courthouse this morning. The gentleman with a white hair and glasses, getting ready to walk in before his testimony.\par \par \uc2 He was the top attorney at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in September 2016 when defendant \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 , at the time, a Hillary Clinton campaign attorney texted him on a Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 Despite saying they were friends for years, and in "frequent contact", Baker says he was surprised that Sussmann had his personal cell phone number.\par \par \uc2 Sussmann wrote, "Jim, its \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 . I have something time-sensitive and sensitive I need to discuss. Do you have availability for a short meeting tomorrow? I'm coming on my own, not on behalf of a client or company -- want to help the bureau. Thanks."\par \par \uc2 Well, Sussmann went to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  the next day, and told Baker he had evidence of a secret back via a computer server between \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2 , which is connected to the Kremlin and the Trump organization.\par \par \uc2 Baker says that Sussmann warned him \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  was aware of the allegations and with just days away from publishing a front page story.\par \par \uc2 Baker says he never asked Sussmann how The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  got the information. Baker says he made the allegations a high priority because of the impending story.\par \par \uc2 He also told jurors that Sussmann dropped off paperwork and two thumb drives.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigated and Baker says there was nothing there. Martha, he told jurors he wanted to get this off of his desk and get it to agents. He's an attorney.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Also, he specifically was asked multiple times, and I do say, more than a dozen times if \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  was coming on behalf of a client because the government says that he built the Hillary Clinton campaign for that meeting with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sussmann says he was coming on his own as you saw in that text message.\par \par \uc2 Well, there was a transcript from a prior interview done a couple of years ago with Baker, where he used the word, clients, plural when talking about \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But when asked about it multiple times Baker says he misspoke, it was a mistake, and he moved on. Because ultimately, at the end of the day, it is simply about that false statement charge. Martha.\par \par \uc2 Very interesting. David, Thank you. David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Victor Davis Hanson, author of the Dying Citizens, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 , and a National Review Institute fellow.\par \par \uc2 Victor, great to have you with us today. So, you know, over the course of this story, we've heard a lot about James Baker, the lead counsel at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , and Peter Strzok, and \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 , who, you know, desperately said he wanted to make sure the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had this information that they had found, and two thumb drives he dropped off and some documents.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of where these people are sort of positioning themselves right now in this courtroom?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HOOVER INSTITUTION\uc2 : I think they're trying to position themselves that in a 360 degree method, they're going to be challenged by people they used to be friends with and work with.\par \par \uc2 By that, I mean that Mr. Joffe's narratives, and Mr. Sussmann's narratives, and probably, Mr. Baker's narratives, probably Mr. Elias's narratives are mutually contradictory. Because what really happened was Mr. Susmann through a firewall was working for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , as was Mr. Elias, and they coordinated an effort to find some evidence of Russian collusion.\par \par \uc2 They found none. So, they contacted one of the most esteemed cybersecurity experts in the United States, Mr. Joffe.\par \par \uc2 Remember, he had bragged that if \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was going to be elected, he was going to get the top spot. So, they knew he was sympathetic to their cause. He didn't -- he said he would check. He had access to the internal communications coming in and out of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He came back and said, I looked that looks like maybe the \uc2 Alfa-Bank\uc2  is communicating. That was enough for Mr. Sussman, who was -- by the way, Martha, a cybersecurity expert -- lawyer.\par \par \uc2 They went to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and they misled the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , apparently. And then, he went to the CIA.\par \par \uc2 And this is what the key cases, I think, the CIA and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who had known their reputations, especially, Mr. Joffe, and had high regard and work for the past, said this is entirely bogus, and we can't use it.\par \par \uc2 So, that -- the interesting question is how do people with such sterling reputations bring evidence that they insist shows that this fraud -- that was -- that was fraudulent, we know it was fraudulent, it was just spam. But why would they think that was accurate enough to take it to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the CIA, who were sympathetic to them, and had worked with them in the past, and it didn't take long for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the CIA to say this is bogus.\par \par \uc2 So, that begs the question, were they trying to peddle information that they knew was inaccurate? In other words, to fool the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the CIA, at least for a while to investigate it. So, then they could leak to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , and say, look, there's a -- going to a story coming out, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the CIA are investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's communications with an \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, there's so much here about the coordination between all of these players.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANSON: And that -- I think that's what the case is right now boiling down to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And how that story ended up being printed. And we also remember \uc2 James Comey\uc2  going and telling, then-candidate Trump, you know, I just want you to know, there's, you know, some information coming out. And that was often the dossier -- the dirty dossier, as it came to be known, which turned out to be just riddled with holes as well.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the big questions, though, that a lot of people have, Marc Elias, worked very closely with the Clintons for years, is was their awareness in the Clinton office, in the front office, or by \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  of what was going on here?\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was very interesting that Marc Elias yesterday basically tried to say, you know, I hired this Oppo (PH) team, and I made sure that anything that they were handling, basically, it was between, you know, us and them these transactions. I didn't let it go any higher than that.\par \par \uc2 What did you think of that?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: I don't think it's going to be persuasive. He was \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's personal lawyer. He was the DNC lawyer. He was the connection between \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2 . He was the one that got his former Perkin Coie associate \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  involved. And that lead, eventually, to the Fusion GPS, and eventually, Christopher Steele.\par \par \uc2 So their stories are not mutually compatible. And I think, what Mr. Durham, unlike Mr. Mueller, is starting at the bottom. He is not going after \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: He is simply going one step at a time, and seeing if these people will tell the same story, and they're not.\par \par \uc2 But eventually, through GPS, through \uc2 Perkins Coie\uc2 , through the DNC, you get back to the Clinton campaign, and ultimately, Hillary Hillary Clinton himself -- herself.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, that I see it is that you have a very ambiguous judge who was appointed by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , Judge Cooper, and you have a D.C. jury.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: So, I think that there's going to be a lot -- it's going to be very difficult to prove it that Mr. Durham is a very high professional.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they have a very --\par \par \uc2 HANSON: He hasn't leaked.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: He hasn't had press conferences, as he's just the antithesis of the Mueller investigation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, very true.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: So, it's going to be very careful when he's working all the way up, and we'll see what happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see what happens. Thank you, Victor, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Thank, thank, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, as DHS gears up for a possible violent -- for possible violence in response to the draft SCOTUS opinion on abortion, some groups, including the Women's March have said that they look forward to a summer of rage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, WOMEN'S MARCH: It is not an exaggeration to say that this is the worst case scenario come to life.\par \par \uc2 There will be deadly consequences for women. But it is also no exaggeration to say that women will fight back as we always have and meet this moment.\par \par \uc2 We will be ungovernable until this government starts working for us. Ungovernable, ungovernable, ungovernable, ungovernable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Executive Director Rachel O'Leary Carmona, who was -- you just heard there, only on THE STORY, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Keep your bands off our bodies, keep your fans off our bodies, keep your fans off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 Today is day one of an uprising to protect abortion rights. It is day one of our feminist future, and it is day one of a summer of rage, where we will be on governable.\par \par \uc2 I will see you in the streets across America this summer continuing to fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is the head of the Women's March at a rally for abortion rights in Washington on Saturday, promising more protests after the leak of the draft of the Supreme Court opinion that would overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  is preparing for the possibility that violence could happen over the course of the summer.\par \par \uc2 Today, Oklahoma lawmakers passed the strictest abortion ban in the country. It makes exceptions only to save a woman's life and for rape and incest reported to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 I'm joined now by Women's March Executive Director Rachel O'Leary Carmona.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Thank you very much for being with us today.\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, tell me what you mean, Rachel, when you talk about a summer of rage, and what is -- what do you mean when you say we will be ungovernable?\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Well, I think it's fair to say that women are angry. You saw that over the weekend, with over 450 marches, and certainly turnout in excess of 30 to 40,000 in D.C. And so, we're going to that region to action as we have done over the last five years of Women's March, you know, advocacy. And that means taking to the streets, protesting talking to our elected officials, and certainly taking our power to the polls.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But when you say ungovernable, what is -- what does that mean? Are you -- are you at telling people that they need to stay within the law or that they should be outside of the law, when you say ungovernable?\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: I mean, in our opinion, the Supreme Court's leaked draft indicates that the Supreme Court itself is putting women of this country on a crash course with the law.\par \par \uc2 We do know that the legal status of abortions actually doesn't have much of an impact on how many abortions are provided. And so, when women have to make a choice between an unjust law and deciding when and how to have a family, including, you know, no carve-outs or exceptions for issues of rape or incest, or things like that.\par \par \uc2 You know, we are going to make sure that folks get the abortion care that they need, in whatever way that we can, and that means building power electorally, taking our power to the polls, and it means building power locally as we've done for the last five years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me ask you this. You say, vote, and make your voice heard. Does that apply to everyone in the country? Because that's what -- when you overturn Roe v. Wade, you're putting it back to the states, in which case people in those states are, you know, they vote on a referendum or they vote for their, you know, for the people who are representing them. And in some states, people don't want to have abortion legal or in many states, they want it to be 15 weeks or 20 weeks, which is four plus months into a pregnancy. So should those votes be heard as well?\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: I'm really glad that you brought this question up. Because I think that there's a question -- a problem with the way that we talk about this, over 90% of Democrats do not want Roe v. Wade overturned, and that's probably surprising to no one. But also over 50% of Republicans don't either, and over 80% of Independents.\par \par \uc2 And so, when we talk about this, we're not talking about, you know, folks who have been out voted, we're talking about a super majority of Americans whose will is being subverted by --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But why not just let them vote in their states? Why not let them vote? Because you've got 54% who think that they don't want to see any abortion after 15 weeks. And you're right, even in our Fox polls, we show let it stand at 63%. But when you go beyond the question, when you go past the point where you say to people it's been around for 50 plus years, do you think it should stand? I think most people say oh, yes, it's just stand. Then, when you get more specific and you say should someone be allowed to have an abortion after 15 weeks, four plus months of pregnancy, they say, no. That should not be allowed to happen?\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Well, you bring up two questions there and there are two separate questions. The first question is, I think around voting. And we've seen, you know, in many different states across the country, you know, attacks on voting rights and things like that. And so, if we have an opportunity to vote in a fair election where everybody has equal access, I think that, you know, that would be different.\par \par \uc2 But we have to understand the connection between the attacks on voting rights, the attack on abortion rights as part of a broader campaign and strategy. And so, I think that's one piece. But I think the second piece here is that there are --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So wait a second. You're saying that we can't trust any of our voting systems, so we can't allow people to have that choice to vote on it because you have questions. I mean if you're saying --\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: No. I'm saying that the voting rights have been attacked in the same way that abortion rates have been attacked. And so when we're talking about a vote, the piece here is that this legislation, and the decision of Roe v. Wade is being handled in a way that takes the democratically expressed vote out of it. It's going through courts that have been packed by specifically Republican candidates, and it's not representative of a majority of Americans, including majority --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: US courts, you know, when -- we have a justice, right, who's nominated by a president, right? Then, you have senators who come together to advise and consent on that nomination. Those senators are voted by people in states all across the country. And as I said, you say vote and make your Georgia voice heard.\par \par \uc2 So -- well, you can't say vote and make your voice heard, and then say, but -- if people are opposed to the way you want them to vote, that their vote isn't going to count because of other grievances that you have. It's either vote and make your voice heard --\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: I actually think that you can. I think that you can say --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- and allow the American people to do that or not?\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: I think that you can say that we have to go over and above the obstacles that have been put in front of us and the difficulty to vote, and I think that that's very clear. If we were not -- if we were aligned and if we were really serious about letting folks you know, have their voices heard, we could have a day off for voting. We would have more access to voting. We would have mail-in voting or different ways that we know has worked for, you know, other countries, and certainly ways that we saw work during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And so, I don't think it's a serious argument to say that we're going to take this back to the states, specifically on a day where a state like Oklahoma has all but banned abortion starting from the day of conception.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that maybe how people want to vote in Oklahoma. And don't you think if that's how they vote and they choose their elected officials, and they press the lever that says that that's what they want, that they should be allowed to have that representation if that's what the state of Oklahoma wants to vote for.\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: In an election that is not shared gerrymandered elections and that we're not restricted to the --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're either going to say vote and make your voice heard or, you know, either you're in favor of democracy --\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: I'm going to say vote. Everyone should vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Everyone should vote and everyone should make their voice heard. And at the same time, we can recognize that those votes are happening inside of a system that is imperfect, and that specifically targets communities of color and poor people. And, you know, increasingly making it difficult for women to get out and vote so we can hold both of those things to be true, actually. They're not mutually exclusive. We can say everyone should absolutely vote and it's harder for some people to vote than others.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Well, we will see if this happens, what happens when it goes back to the states. And what the American people want state by state, they will have that choice and they will have that option. I hope you'll come back because this is an important conversation and I appreciate you being here, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARMONA: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So as Americans pay more the for gas than ever before, we've been talking about this and retirement accounts tumble the United States' readies billions of dollars for Ukraine, an effort that many people in this country support. We're going to talk about this when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): Inflation roars throughout the land, grocery bills are punishing the working class and poor, and gas prices exceed $5. Even before the pandemic bailout, our country was running a trillion dollar annual deficit just to pay for its routine commitments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So another $40 billion on top of the already sent $11 billion will soon be on its way to Ukraine. The Senate passed that bill today after Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  delayed the vote for a week. He was demanding that an inspector general oversee where the money goes. Marc Thiessen is here but first a congressional correspondent Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. It only takes one senator to gum up the works. And this is why this bill was delayed for a week, \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  filibustered the bill over the IG.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Couldn't Congress simply shift over the $40 billion and not add to the debt? If the defense of Ukraine is really in our national security interest, shouldn't a gift come from our military budget? What about cutting wasteful spending?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The Senate finally OK the bill 86 to 11. All Democrats present voted yes, all nays came from GOP members including Paul. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  pointed to GOP opposition especially from those who say they want to help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (R-NY): It is beyond troubling to see a growing circle of Senate Republicans proudly oppose Ukrainian funding. It appears more and more MAGA Republicans are on the same soft on Putin playbook that we saw used by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wanted the Senate to pass the bill a week ago today, that's because McConnell was secretly planning a weekend meeting with Ukrainian leader \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . McConnell arrived in Kyiv, empty handed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Anyone concerned about the cost of supporting a Ukrainian victory should consider the much larger costs should Ukraine lose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The House and Senate are now synced up this bill goes to President Biden to sign. And I'm told that more money will be needed probably in the fall, Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Chad. Let's bring in Marc Thiessen, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  scholar and Fox News contributor. Good to have you here, Marc. So --\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, I'm interested in what Senator Paul is saying here. Not because I think most people, I think the majority of the country feels very strongly that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  must be defeated in his aggression in Ukraine. But he's saying if this is so important, why don't we carve it out of existing budget and let's make sure that we know where the money is going once it goes there. Because I also think that people should know that when the Pentagon sends weaponry, it lands in Ukraine, and then that's it. We don't know where it goes. We don't know how it's used. That's the process in all of this.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. Well, that's because we don't have American troops on the ground in Ukraine. So if Senator Paul wants to send US troops to Ukraine, which I know he doesn't, then I'm sure we can monitor it much more carefully. Look, the idea of taking it out of the Pentagon budget makes no sense because we need to increase the defense budget in order to deter China. So we shouldn't be taking away from the -- our capability to deter and confront China in order to help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And second, we need -- this is -- the -- very small handful and, by the way, I thought what Senator Schumer said was shameful to try and you know, pull this MAGA Republican stunt when we're doing something that's actually very bipartisan. It's a very small minority of Republicans who oppose this. But they're proposing something that's straight out of the Reagan playbook. This is this is the Gipper strategy. When he came into office in 1980, America just withdrawn from Vietnam, there was no appetite for sending American troops around the world to fight the Soviet expansionism. He had to figure out a way to rollback the Soviet Union without deploying troops. So, we came up with something called the Reagan Doctrine, which recognize that there were freedom fighters around the world who were willing to fight their own wars of liberation. And they just needed American weapons, and training, and intelligence, and help in order to do it.\par \par \uc2 And that strategy worked miracles and brought down the Soviet Union. We rolled back communist regimes from Nicaragua to Angola, and in South Asia and Afghanistan, drove the Soviets out. So this is -- now we have a new generation of freedom fighters in Ukraine who are fighting Russian expansionism. We needed --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it's a noble cause but it seems like whenever anybody sticks their head up and says, what if we do this in a fiscally responsible way, or in this case, everyone just says, oh, well, we can't do that. We can't possibly do that. We have $190 billion in waste and fraud in the COVID program. We have billions of dollars that has been unspent from the COVID program. So, I don't think that it's so horrible to suggest that perhaps there is someplace else in the budget if we feel so strongly about this. And obviously, there's a consensus on it to find that money.\par \par \uc2 This is the other question I have for you. We have been reluctant to allow Ukraine to enter \uc2 NATO\uc2 , right? I mean, that ship has sailed for now because there's a war going on. We understand the Article Five ramifications of that. But here's the President today welcoming Finland and Sweden into this process when Ukraine has been begging to be in \uc2 NATO\uc2  for decades. Here's the President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Today, I'm proud to assure them that they have the full total complete backing of the United States of America. New members joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  is not a threat to any nation. Finland and Sweden's decision to request membership in \uc2 NATO\uc2 , it will be enhanced for all time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if you're watching this in Ukraine, I have to believe you're thinking we have been asking for this for decades. And President Bush wanted to make us a partner in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And, you know, look what happened.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. I was with President Bush in 2008 when he went to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit in Bucharest, and tried to convince the allies to admit Ukraine and Germany opposed it. And so, it didn't go anywhere. If he had succeeded, if we had admitted Ukraine, then this war would have never happened. Because, you know, there's a reason why \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  hasn't invaded Poland, Hungary, the Czech Republic, Lithuania, Latvia or Estonia, because that would be declaring war on America and the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, not just on those countries.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Marc, thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Ukraine was out of that sphere of protection. And so, he felt like he could get --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have to break away, I'm sorry to cut you off, Marc. We just have this breaking news. We want to go to it. My thanks to Marc Thiessen for making those great points.\par \par \uc2 This is Lakeland, Florida where Governor DeSantis is signing legislation to combat the flow of fentanyl from the open southern border. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA GOVERNOR: -- southern border already this year, 6,000 pounds of fentanyl has been seized. And so, if you look at what we've seen recently, we've seen an increase in opioid deaths across the country including here in Florida. According to Florida Medical Examiners Commission, fentanyl cause 5,300 deaths in 2020 more than any other narcotic. And then, while we have some data for the first six months of 2021 only, opioid-related deaths increase another 5.5% and deaths caused by fentanyl increase by almost 10% in the first six months.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is something that's affecting communities all across our state. You talk to sheriffs, you talk to police chiefs, you talk to first responders, and this is something that's all too common. And so, I'm thankful for the legislature to step up and do something about it. We have a whole variety of ways that we're trying to combat this epidemic, but you do need to hold people accountable. And that's what we'll end up doing here today.\par \par \uc2 And so, I am going to -- we're going to hear from some folks here and then I will sign the bill and the law. So first, we're going to have Sheriff Grady Judd from Polk County.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We'll keep a close eye on this. This is a major, major issue in this country. We've lost 100,000 people to fentanyl use over the course of the last year. And Governor DeSantis is trying to block it from getting into Florida. We'll see if he succeeds or if he makes any headway in something that has touched so many lives across this country and does not get enough attention. We're going to stay on it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a New Jersey mother who has gone to great lengths because she had to, to get food for her babies says that no parent should struggle to find formula in the United States of America in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Never soon enough to have food for crying baby.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Millions of families continuing to struggle with the nationwide baby formula shortage. You have seen it on your shelves really pretty much everywhere. There's a toddler and a preschooler who were hospitalized in Memphis as a result of this shortage. And then, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner, Dr. \uc2 Robert Califf\uc2 , face to grilling today over his agency's response to something that they've been aware of since October, when a whistleblower came forward to alleged negligence at the formula plant at the center of all of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT CALIFF\uc2 , FDA COMMISSIONER: I know we have an oversight hearing next week, and we'll be prepared to go into much more detail at that point.\par \par \uc2 REP. ROSA DELAURO, (D-CT): Where did it go? Who read it? What was the chain of command? You apparently don't have any answers to that question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rosa DeLauro digging in on the \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner and just asking what went wrong here and why didn't this whistleblower complaint lead to getting ahead of all of this. The Biden administration is now invoking a wartime tool, the Defense Production Act, rolling out operation fly formula to ramp up deliveries from abroad.\par \par \uc2 My next guest is taking action her own way. Jessica Palaia is a New Jersey mother of two. Jessica, thank you very much for being here. What's been your own experience? Have you had trouble finding formula for your beautiful new baby that we're watching you hold right now?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA PALAIA, NEW JERSEY MOTHER: Hi, everybody. Yes, actually, we have. We -- so my daughter is three months old, we actually experienced the shortage first with my son who's one. And then, when she came along about three weeks later, we went from having nothing to having at least a supply that's going to end -- to the end of the month right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you have organized moms and dads online to try to do your own sort of Defense Production Act to try to move the formula to the places where it's needed. Tell me about that.\par \par \uc2 PALAIA: Yes. So I decided to open this group up because I was a part of other \uc2 Facebook\uc2  groups, but they were from all over the place. And the cost of shipping and gas prices was not helping. So I decided to open a group in the Bergen and Passaic County Area for moms and dads, parents, even caregivers that just needed -- we needed help. We needed to help the babies. That's who we're really trying to help here.\par \par \uc2 And so, I organized this group so that we could share what we had at home if we had extra. We get free samples all the time but sometimes our child doesn't use that specific kind of formula. So at least if we posted pictures, we can give it to somebody in the local area that maybe was on their last can or they had nothing left.\par \par \uc2 And then in addition to this, I was urging people, even friends, neighbors, anybody who was going to the store, if they could just take a picture of what the stores looked like, because the apps and websites were not being accurate. Then at least a mom could go out and know that she was going to get that specific formula there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, when you hear this \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner, and you hear that the plant that was responsible for about 40% of the Abbott, you know, made formulas, that they had a whistleblower back in the fall who said they were concerned about some problems there. And you hear this grilling on Capitol Hill of, you know, why didn't anybody get ahead of this? What do you think about it?\par \par \uc2 PALAIA: It's just -- amazing to me because, you know, in this world, you would think that the easiest thing to get would be baby formula. This is not something that any parent, caregiver should ever have to struggle getting. And to hear that somebody knew about it and did nothing, it's a little terrifying, to be honest, because they're so vulnerable to begin with. And to have to go through this and have to worry about what am I going to feed my child of all things, it's terrifying. It's very -- it's concerning to us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, thank you, Jessica. And good luck to you. We hope this gets resolved in the next few weeks. They say things are going to start moving and we'll certainly be watching it. And we hope that you can continue to find what you need. Good to see you. Thanks, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 PALAIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So they say of this young man that he spread happiness everywhere he went. That was part of the obituary for Jimmy McGrath, a 17- year-old stabbed to death at a party in Connecticut. And now we know who the accused killer is. Correspondent Molly Line reporting live from Shelton, Connecticut. Hi, Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Despite between teenagers from rival prep schools at a massive weekend house party ended in tragedy as you mentioned, one person was killed, three wounded, and now a 16-year- old from Milford, Connecticut is facing charges. And he will be tried as an adult facing murder and assault charges.\par \par \uc2 The victim, 17-year-old James McGrath known, as you mentioned, as Jimmy of Huntington, Connecticut excelled as an athlete. Playing lacrosse and football at Fairfield Prep, a Catholic school where he was a junior. Lacrosse team squared off in the field against nearby Shelton High last night, the crowd honoring Jimmy as his father spoke.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCGRATH, VICTIM'S FATHER: I love (inaudible) made the announcement for supporting (inaudible). So I appreciate everything (inaudible). I appreciate everything in our community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: Documents released today reveal police arrived to a bloody and chaotic scene just before midnight on Saturday with more than 50 teenagers attending a house party in Shelton. Witnesses told detectives three or four people from St. Joseph's High School arrived uninvited. After one stabbing victim reported that he had been jumped earlier that evening in another nearby party.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses said Raul Valle flashed a knife. One witness told police that after the fighting stopped, he saw the suspect later identified via photo array that had stabbed Jimmy McGrath before leaving. Raul's father had hid beneath a suit jacket outside of court today but whispered this apology.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It would be irresponsible for me to make any comment before I had chance to examine all the evidence in the case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: Sorry, that was Valle's attorney. The father of the accused said I'm sorry outside of the courthouse. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Terrible. What an awful, awful story. Thank you, Molly.\par \par \uc2 So, this is called Mission Margraten Plus. It's a nonprofit that takes our World War II heroes to visit the graves at Margraten American Military Cemetery in the Netherlands and to meet some of the Dutch people who adopted and cared for the headstones in this cemetery that stretches a very large expanse, 8,300 American soldiers there. It's a new episode of the Untold Story podcast. I spoke with the co-founders, Roger Kilfoil and Stephanie Falwell. And one of the veterans going on the group, 96-year-old George Ciampa, who is a World War II veteran, fought at the Battle of the Bulge, Normandy Invasion. He told me why this trip is so important to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE CIAMPA, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: I'm going because I want to personally, I mean, personally thank these people. I understand that we've got 100 adopters together on one of the events that we're going to attend with them. And I want to personally shake their hands and thank them for what they're doing. Because it means, it means a lot to me, it really does.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So much gratitude from these European villages. And we thank George Ciampa and we thank you for being here. That's THE STORY. We'll see you tomorrow. Have a good day.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220520ei5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Dr. Oz In The Lead In PA GOP Senate Primary; Americans Can't Afford Biden Agenda Failures\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Ari Fleischer, Mike Huckabee, Leo Terrell \par \uc2 8,255 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And we start with this FOX News alert. The all-important Republican Senate primary race in Pennsylvania still too close to call. You can see 97 percent of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 As it stands, Dr. Oz has about a 1,500-vote lead over \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 . Now, keep in mind that the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, any race decided by 0.5 percent or less automatically triggers a recount. By the way in the history of Pennsylvania no recount has ever turned around the results of the earlier election.\par \par \uc2 We are well within that margin though tonight. Whoever comes out ahead will face off against a fringe far left socialist. His name, right there, John Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we wish his health -- is recovering from a serious stroke. We wish him the best. We'll have a lot more my monologue coming up in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 First, joining us now with analysis on all the latest numbers, he's with Insider Advantage, the pollster Matt Towery. Also, full disclosure somebody I've known for a long time from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 This -- well, you and Robert Cahaly both told me this is a very, very close race. You're both right. You called it. I give you a lot of credit. I've been crunching numbers all day. I've been talking to people all day. I've been checking county websites all day.\par \par \uc2 And I have my belief that worst case scenario, this comes out on Oz's favor. Some people might disagree. What is your take?\par \par \uc2 MATT TOWERY, INSIDER ADVANTAGE: Well, I mean -- you know, there are different opinions out there. My question is this, where are the votes? My experience -- I've been involved before I started just doing polling for news, I was involved in a lot of campaigns -- gubernatorial, Senate, presidential, you name it. I rarely ever saw a vote change that much after you started getting into this level and there were always rumors of giant amounts to be counted and they never materialized.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you some points here while I'm asking this question. For example, since late last night, we counted about another 25,000 votes. And you've seen McCormick's lead -- rather Oz's lead come down. McCormick people ran a great campaign, I understand that and I give a lot of credit to it.\par \par \uc2 My question though is where is the other 25,000, if you're -- if you're thinking these votes proportionate, where are they going to come from? And then I started looking into what I could find.\par \par \uc2 For example, military votes. Everybody said, oh, well there are all these military votes out there. In reality, you can find on publicly accessible websites how many web -- how many military votes were requested. And in reality, there aren't that many. I mean, it's in a thousand, fifteen hundred -- it's low numbers. You can't get --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's also Democrat and Republican.\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: In Philly, my understanding is there were only 127 military requests, that's Democrat and Republican. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Yeah, that's true and of everything I'm going to cover right here, you got to take into account that you're splitting out Republican and Democrat numbers that are being all thrown together right now to make it look like their massive number of votes to be counted in the Republican race. So that's not the case.\par \par \uc2 I'll give you another example. In Allegheny, I made this note, there are locations right now that are still out now. Locations have precincts within them. And within those precincts, if you look at them they split out about half of them are Democratic. So you're not talking about the massive number of votes that everyone thinks are going to come out of there, even if the votes are proportionate.\par \par \uc2 Another example, Lancaster. I know that was a big, big problem yesterday. I noted it very early. I think I texted you and said that's a interesting thing going on there.\par \par \uc2 But right now, there are 4,800 votes that are left out there and the percentage of GOP versus Democrat takes that down to not that many GOP votes left. So when I look at the universe and I've gone down this road many a time, and I've been on the losing side where I thought, whoa, I've got all these votes to come in and they never materialized. I've been on the winning side where I thought all of these votes are going to overwhelm us and they never came in.\par \par \uc2 I'm not saying there aren't more to count and I'm not saying that I could be wrong. But my experience and my intuition tells me from what I can see, there aren't enough votes here to make this a reversal in who's leading. It could take it down more, maybe even to 600, but I don't think it can change the lead.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What about Allegheny County where there's still outstanding votes? \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 's hometown, he did pretty well there. My extrapolation is, is maybe at a maximum you get somewhere near 2,000 votes. If you look -- if you doubled the percentage that Dave had in that particular county and anyone I think by five or six, even if you doubled the remaining votes and gave that advantage to Dave, it still is only a few hundred.\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Yeah, that's the problem. That gets to my point. First of all, we're hearing up votes outstanding and people are mixing Democratic and Republicans. So you have to first winnow down to how many Republican votes are outstanding.\par \par \uc2 Then you have to see what the locations are within those counties. And like I said, specifically with regard to Allegheny, if you've got 38 locations or 37 locations precincts within them, but a good number of those are Democratic so I just don't think the votes are there to reverse the lead in this. I'm not saying that it won't continue to compress as this comes through. That's entirely --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're saying that it looks to you based on looking at the different precincts, different districts, the different counties, that they're running out of runway space in terms of available votes Republican that would likely go in their favor versus say Dr. Oz because there's still outstanding votes in counties where he did very well.\par \par \uc2 TOWERY: Exactly and even if it's proportionate, I just don't think there are enough Republican votes left to be counted to reverse the lead in this race. It will be tight. I'm not saying that it won't compress like I said, but I don't see a way for this to reverse.\par \par \uc2 And when you get to recounts, I did recounts. I mean, I did two recounts with Newt Gingrich, working with him. And I've never seen a recount in which the candidate really the leading candidate really went backwards and in fact in most recounts that I've been involved with, the candidate who's leading actually picks up ballots in an automatic recount, which I believe is what Pennsylvania has.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Matt Towery, thank you for that analysis. We will know more probably in the next day or so.\par \par \uc2 All right, joining us now with more, current Pennsylvania GOP Senate front- runner.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Mehmet Oz is with us.\par \par \uc2 I certainly would want to be in your position, a very close race. We knew going in this would be a tight race.\par \par \uc2 But the most amazing observation I have about this, if you look at the three top leaders, and you look at the percentages of vote, all three ran as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  make America great again conservatives, all three of you.\par \par \uc2 Now, you happen to get the president's endorsement, which obviously paid dividends for you. But what does that say about where the part -- where the people in Pennsylvania are? I think it's very telling.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Oh, the people are very supportive of President Trump. And the more \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's in office, the more they remember those halcyon days of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 But if I can comment on this race, you know, we have covered all counties to ensure that the ballots are correctly counted. This election is ours. We have done well.\par \par \uc2 What I want to do today, though, is to talk about unifying our party, because those three front-runners -- and there are other candidates as well -- all worked their tails off. But we need to unite to take on Fetterman you mentioned earlier.\par \par \uc2 And I am praying that he makes a full recovery. As a heart doctor trained at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , I am optimistic.\par \par \uc2 But this really comes down to what people in Pennsylvania want. People in Pennsylvania have a clear choice. Fetterman is to the left of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's not equal to Biden. He's to the left. So, he supports spending. He supports restricting energy production, which in our commonwealth is a big issue. That drives inflation, number one driver of inflation.\par \par \uc2 I want to liberate energy producers to reduce inflation. Democrats want open borders. This is a real hot topic here. I want to shut down the cartel-run human trafficking operation at the border and also shut down the drugs they're bringing in that makes them even more money.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman wants socialized medicine, like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . We saw what happens when you have one-size-fits-all approaches. It didn't work during COVID. It doesn't work in general. I want private solutions customized to your needs.\par \par \uc2 I'm a doctor. I spent my life studying this. I will take these messages to every corner of the commonwealth. I pledge that, including places Republicans, Sean, don't often go. I have gotten Pittsburgh and Philly to do free clinics. We have taken care of thousands of folks. So I understand the challenges of working with people who feel forgotten.\par \par \uc2 And I got to say -- you know this about me, because the hardest part of this campaign in many ways was getting you to vet me -- that --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 OZ: -- I have my core conservative values, but there's a lot of love in my heart for folks on the other side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 And we will pull this commonwealth together as I campaign into the fall.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I said it, and I meant that. I didn't endorse you because we're friends. And we are friends.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to know that you were America first. I wanted to know you would fight hard as a conservative, a make America great again conservative, a Trump conservative, a save America conservative at this point.\par \par \uc2 Again, you just look at this. You got fully 87 percent of the vote in the primary in Pennsylvania, Republicans, are all claiming the MAGA mantle. That is a profound number.\par \par \uc2 But Dr. Oz, we will watch it. We heard Matt's analysis. We got yours. I did a lot of digging myself. I'm pretty confident in my numbers. But time will tell.\par \par \uc2 We really appreciate you being with us and coming on as often as you did. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 One more --\par \par \uc2 OZ: God bless you for having me on, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You are very welcome.\par \par \uc2 One more quick note about the Pennsylvania primary and I won't belabor this point. Third place finisher Kathy Barnette is now attacking yours truly on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . She's putting out videos blaming me for her loss.\par \par \uc2 Kathy, my first instinct was to put up all of your incendiary tweets again and there's a ton of them, but I really don't need to defend myself. You're the one that really needs to answer the questions about all of your comments and all of your tweets that are.\par \par \uc2 You can lash out at me all you want. You're not the first person. But everything that we revealed is true.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the election we attempted to reach out to you and your campaign for comment over and over and over again. You and your team refuse to get back to us to give us answers.\par \par \uc2 Now maybe you stand by your past comments, maybe you don't. But you refuse to answer our questions. In fact, you only responded to me publicly on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and then I tweeted you back and said I would invite you on for a debate the day before election day, with all the other candidates. I'm on 675 stations nationwide. You never got back to me. That unfortunately would be on you.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think Kathy Barnette is an enormously talented person, with an incredible life story but she will never get elected to statewide office in my opinion unless she is willing and able to answer questions about her past comments, conduct, rhetoric and tweets. I like Kathy Barnette, but without addressing those issues, she is unelectable in Pennsylvania in a general election. She lost in 2020 by 19 points.\par \par \uc2 And make no mistake, this is a must-win Senate race for Republicans, if in fact they have any hope of taking over the Senate. In fact, it's critically important that the GOP regains majorities in both the House and the Senate in the fall. After two years of total Democratic control, this country is falling apart dangerously so.\par \par \uc2 Look at the stock market today. It tanked yet again. Inflation at a 42-year high, interest rates now going up through the roof. This is really crushing everyone in this country, especially the poor, the middle class, our fellow Americans on fixed incomes.\par \par \uc2 And now, top economists are calling stagflation, quote, unavoidable. The CEO of \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  saying there is no question bad days are ahead. Analysts \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , smart people, they're blaming the Biden administration for their inflation. And even far-left "Vox" is blasting Joe's out-of-control spending.\par \par \uc2 And it gets even worse. Today, yet once again, gas prices shooting to the moon and today another brand new record high price for a gallon of gasoline. In fact, every day for the past nine days, gas prices set one new record after another.\par \par \uc2 Now, this year, American families will have to fork over anywhere between $5,200 to $5,700 and going higher, thanks to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's inflation. Two thousand -- by the way, if you drive a car, you're going to pay $2,000 more on average just for gasoline this year over when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the administration likes to blame all of our problems on corporations and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and something they're calling Ultra MAGA. Oh, you mean when gas prices were cheap, the border was under control, where Operation Warp Speed actually worked and we didn't run out of tests after two years, and every other disaster from Afghanistan to the war in Europe.\par \par \uc2 This week, Biden's new press secretary didn't seem to know who to blame or what to say. It was not a good start. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: How does raising taxes on corporations lower the cost of gas, the cost of a used car, the cost of food for ever