o I'm going to wait for nobody. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." I don't like to praise \uc2 Red Sox\uc2  fans on principle, but I will have to give a shout out to this fan. He caught two home runs in the same game, in the same the same inning.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: The same fan?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The same guy, the same game, the same inning. Now, the chances of doing that, and we have the producer calculate this, one in 14 billion, all right.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You have a better chance of winning the lottery, being attacked by a shark, or being struck by lightning. This is the luckiest guy I've ever seen except he's a \uc2 Red Sox\uc2  fan. So, we'll let that slide.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of homeruns, we have Glenn Younkin on the show tonight at 7:00. I didn't think he liked me. But apparently, he's doing the show. We've been trying to get him on for a long time. But we're going to have Youngkin on tonight to react to a lot of hot topics. So, watch at 7:00. Dana Perino.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Both.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Maybe you're respected.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Both.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But you have a great show.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So you're saying he dislikes me and respect me?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm just saying, well, look, you have a great show at 7:00 p.m. so maybe he looks at that and says I'm going to come.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK, all right, it has nothing to do with me personally?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Also, maybe his -- maybe his children are big fans.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, that works.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That happens as well. Hey, Animals Are Great, my edition. This is an elephant who started bellowing when she saw this antelope drowning. And so she sent out an SOS. And so, the zookeeper ran and helped pull the antelope out and save the antelope. She -- this elephant is 61 years old. She's at La Aurora Zoo in Guatemala City. And now she's being honored along with the animal caretaker for their teamwork in saving the antelope.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Animal caretaker, is that a new word?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Like a proper word?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Is that like a proper word?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I don't know what it was before.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Zookeeper?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Zookeeper.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't know. I don't want to offend anybody. Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: I love all five of my children. One is really in the spotlight this week. My dear daughter Simone is graduated from Yale Law School. She's really a great kid. Here we are at the rooftop of the peninsula. We hang out when we can. She's graduating Northwestern. I love my girl. I love her.\par \par \uc2 She survived the terrorist attack in Paris in 2015.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Really that's what she got her interest in foreign affairs. She's already got a clerkship. There she is on my bad boy. That's her mom, Cece. Congrats to Simone. We love you. And I'll be proudly standing there when you graduate.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And O'Reilly's new book Killing the Killers talks about that, her surviving that terrorist attack in Paris. Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm going to do this quickly. Pope Francis has been suffering from strained ligaments in his right knee. He said the thing that is healed him and taken away the pain, Tequila. There you go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (text): Pope Francis! How is your leg? How is your knee?\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS (text): It's being naughty. I need a little bit of tequila for my leg!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You need some tequila?\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: There's always time for tequila. There's Margaritaville somewhere, right? Listen, I always love this one. Promises made and promises kept. Jerome Bettis, after 28 years, went back and got his college degree. It's always nice to see an athlete goes back and finished it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The Bus.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: And I just got a text from Gutfeld saying that I have to push that I'll be in Salt Lake City this week with Greg at a book signing.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: On Saturday.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: On Saturday, yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This Saturday, May 21.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm sorry you had to travel with Greg.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: I don't travel with him.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, good. That should be a policy for everybody.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, that's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next with Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220519ei5i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pennsylvania GOP Senate Primary Too Close To Call\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Bill Hemmer, Bryan Llenas, Alexandria Hoff, Rich  Edson, Leo Terrell, Joe Concha, Geraldo Rivera, Gregg Jarrett, Newt  Gingrich \par \uc2 9,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And this is a FOX News alert. Tonight, we are tracking primary results in five different states, voting is still underway in the states of Idaho and Oregon.\par \par \uc2 But at this hour, the polls are now officially closed in Kentucky, North Carolina and in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, which is suffering a ton of election irregularities we can report at this hour. In Berks County, a judge kept the polls open for an additional hour because of, quote, widespread problems they're having. In Lancaster County, a significant number of mail-in ballots are not able to be scanned electronically because of quote printing problems.\par \par \uc2 We'll get to my monologue in just a moment, but first, joining us live, he is the man. He used to be the Hannity big board. Now, it's the Hemmer big board. He's got the very latest with, what, eight percent of the vote in the Senate race in Pennsylvania and everything else going on tonight.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Okay, I'm going to give you a big spin around here and take the Pennsylvania, Sean, base what we're seeing right now. Again, this is live voting now. So some of this is going to change as we go to the board back here. They'll take it down to North Carolina and show you what's happening there as well.\par \par \uc2 Biggest primary night we've had of the year. We've been watching this now for going back to the 1st of March in the state of Texas. This is where we begin now, Sean.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senate primary with nine percent of the vote in now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , remember, he endorsed Dr. Mehmet Oz right now, about 25 percent of that 9 percent share of the vote.\par \par \uc2 David McCormick, however, off to a pretty good run here, at 32 percent, as you see the vote come in. Kathy Barnette trailing by about 12 points down there at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 Where's the vote still to be tabulated? I can tell you, Sean, if you were to look at the state of Pennsylvania, I'll show you in a moment here. You can draw a T and find out where the Republican voters vote and where the Democrats vote in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 However, this is Pittsburgh. This is Allegheny County. This is the hometown of David McCormick. Well, we're -- okay, there it is. It's the second largest of 67 counties. That gives you an idea of how many votes there are in Allegheny County, in the city of Pittsburgh and you're about well it's still early in that part of the state.\par \par \uc2 Down here in the southeast, this little green county right here, that's the county and the city of Philadelphia. There are a ton of votes here that have yet to be counted. Number one populated county in the entire state out of 67. I was mentioning, where you find votes in the state of Pennsylvania. This is what Pennsylvania did less than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the official winner over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by about 80,000, about 82,000 votes in 2020. If you were to draw this -- you know, a lot of times when you study the state of Pennsylvania, you draw the T. Take away the middle county here, and you can see the red counties across the top and the other red counties that fill in on the sides, and we have seen election after election, Sean, the red counties get redder in Pennsylvania, and the blue counties get bluer.\par \par \uc2 That includes Pittsburgh over here, Erie in the northwest and also the area of Philadelphia down here in the Southeast. Come back to real action now in the Senate race, okay, we're at 10 percent of the vote, sticking pretty much McCormick at 32-1/2 and Oz at 26 percent, and Kathy Barnette who really made a charge in the last days or so of that election trailing right now at about 21 percent.\par \par \uc2 What's happening on the Democratic side for the Senate, John Fetterman is in the hospital. John Fetterman cast a vote today from his hospital room. John Fetterman had a heart procedure today. He has been declared the winner based on the tally so far in "The Associated Press". So he is well on to taking on whoever comes out to win the Republican Senate primary and will we get a winner tonight, will we find out tonight.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the state of Pennsylvania starts counting their ballots after the polls closed and that was just about an hour and four minutes ago. So we got a ways here.\par \par \uc2 Just checking on the Republican race for the gubernatorial primary in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  was endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he is now a leader with about 24, 25 percent of the vote in. So that's a bit of a change as we watch the tallies come in throughout the state.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to take you down to North Carolina and show you what we've seen so far tonight. Popping out here down to the Tar Heel State. This is -- hang on here, okay, this is \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  in Magenta, we'll call it, Sean. He was picked within minutes by "The Associated Press".\par \par \uc2 An early winter endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a year ago, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  was an easy winner in North Carolina tonight.\par \par \uc2 What's happening on the House side? I'm going to draw a little something here for you. If you were to go to the far western reaches of North Carolina, this is Buncombe County right here. That's where Asheville is located and draw a line, this would make up congressional district number 11.\par \par \uc2 This has been hotly watched now especially by Republican observers of this campaign because Madison Cawthorn, a first-time freshman Republican in the House, is in for quite a challenge right now. And I can show you a little bit of what's happening there in CD 11.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Edwards, a state senator, is hanging on here right now over Cawthorn. A number of Republicans in Washington and throughout North Carolina, they did not want Cawthorn to win. They openly campaigned against him and it's - - look at this, 77 percent of the vote now is in, and Chuck Edwards is hanging on right now, with a little less than 35 percent of the tally there and again the 11th congressional district in North Carolina show.\par \par \uc2 You another one here, we're just looking at this. This is congressional district number four in North Carolina. That's the Raleigh area, Sean, and this is -- this is a race we are watching because Nina Elam or Allam, excuse me, she was endorsed by Bernie Sanders. She's a progressive, and she will go on to lose tonight in her bid to take congressional district four and represent the Democrats in November. So, Allam looks like with 99 percent of the vote in there, she will be a loser there in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So the state of the race right now if I were to go back two years ago at the presidential level, you know, you think about Pennsylvania I just showed you there -- well, I mean just remember, Sean, where we were. There we go, boom, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , right? I mean, the tally was so close in North Carolina just like it was in Pennsylvania. So something to track there.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to show you what I can here, we started, what, the first of March, we're in the first week of the war in Ukraine, there was not a lot of ton of attention. That was even the Texas primary, but it was significant too as we watch and we try and track each primary here in the season.\par \par \uc2 And what we tried to do here, Sean, was trying to detect turnout. And so, we chose the two biggest states that have voted so far in the primaries. Ohio is one of them. It was two weeks ago tonight, right? 2022, more than a million Republicans voted, Democrats about 510,000. Now, this is not an apples-to-apples comparison. Every state's different, but they have different levels of interest, depending on whether the Senate's up or whether the governor's race where they got some good congressional races.\par \par \uc2 But watch this, Sean, okay? In 2022, when you compare it to four years ago in 2018, Republicans are up 22 percent, Democrats were down 29 percent in Ohio, all right? Then we went back to March 1st in the state of Texas, 2022, just shy of 2 million Republicans and 1.1 Democrats, right?\par \par \uc2 There's some pretty good races in Texas, too, on both sides. Well, this is what we found out when we compare to 2018, Republicans are up 27 percent, Democrats are up four 4.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, it's not apples to apples in every state, but clearly, the energy and the enthusiasm so far in these primary races on behalf of the Republican Party. So we'll see again how that works out tonight.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing? You still with me?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm still here. I'm watching a great fascination.\par \par \uc2 All right. So the real race tonight, the real drama is going to be in the Senate race in Pennsylvania. We're going to be checking in all throughout the night with you and updating. It looks like it's slightly tightening a little bit.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's getting interesting.\par \par \uc2 All right. Stay with us. Bill Hemmer -- we'll get back to him many times throughout the hour tonight. We might get my monologue in if we have time, we're going to try.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we check in with our very own Bryan Llenas. He is at Oz campaign headquarters tonight.\par \par \uc2 Bryan, what's the feeling there?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sean, good evening. Yeah, I just spoke to the campaign here for Dr. Oz, and they are feeling confident they feel like they have the momentum coming in they are not concerned so far with those early numbers, pointing to the fact that \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  is from the Allegheny County area, as well as the fact that these votes they believe are mostly those early votes, the mail-in ballots that have come in and they're going to wait to see what the turnout is today on primary day when those precincts start coming out.\par \par \uc2 They're particularly interested in looking at the northeastern part of the state. That's where a lot of the blue-collar workers are. We're talking about Wayne and Pike and Monroe Counties, Luzerne, Lackawanna, those areas that's where former President Trump did well, and that's where they believe that Dr. Oz can do well tonight. So that's what we'll be looking for there.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of the former president, it is hard to understate the importance of the former president to the Dr. Oz campaign. As a matter of fact, they said that it's extremely important to the Dr. Oz campaign. In fact, you look at all of the signs in and around Pennsylvania, and they've been -- many have been replaced with signs that say, Dr. Oz and in bold "endorsed by Trump".\par \par \uc2 The polls were not looking great for Oz, leading into about April, but then the president chose and endorsed the doctor, and that is when things started to take a turn at least poll-wise. And when we went out today and we spoke to voters. And we found that most people we spoke to who were in between -- they were really in between Dr. Oz and as well as Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 And those that we spoke to that voted for Dr. Oz said they thought about two things. One, President Trump's endorsement, and the second, electability. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My main purpose was to come out for Oz because Trump supports him and I'm a Trump fan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he's an America first candidate. All the commercials that I had seen I think were not right. I don't believe he's a RINO.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was -- it was a tough choice between Dr. Oz and Kathy Barnette. The one thing -- I want somebody who's going to be electable. Mehmet Oz certainly has the name recognition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: The former president, he took part in a telerally last night. He called in. We're told it was voluntary. He decided he wanted to do everything he can to help Dr. Oz win, so he called in and he told everybody that he is -- that the doctor is a loyal MAGA person, as well as somebody who is strong and a friend of his, and he obviously went against \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 One last thing, you were talking, Sean, about irregularities. We do have an update. So Berks County here in Pennsylvania, they have reported that there's been widespread problems we just got off the phone with them, with their new electronic poll books and now the polls will remain open for another hour.\par \par \uc2 That means that there were longer lines at the check-in. So they had problems with these new electronic poll books so people had to wait longer to get in. There's nothing to do with the actual vote tally, but as a result, the polls now were open until 9:00 p.m., instead of 8:00 p.m. So that's Berks County.\par \par \uc2 Allegheny County, there was a report there that there were issues looking into whether or not they had enough ballots. That there was a report out there that they had ran out of ballots in Allegheny County. We now know that that is no longer the case.\par \par \uc2 They say that they were looking at the situation. Upon further review, those ballots were actually delivered. So that's good news there.\par \par \uc2 And one last issue, we're getting out of Lancaster -- Lancaster County. The Oz campaign tells me here that about votes were affected there with an incorrect -- ballots that were cast with an incorrect election identification number, which made it very difficult to scan those ballots, but they're working on it over time and the Dr. Oz campaign tells us they are not concerned about that county as of now. But we'll keep you updated, Sean, throughout the evening.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thanks so much, Bryan Llenas. We'll be back to you throughout the night. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Alexandria Hoff. She's at Kathy Barnette's campaign headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Alexandria, what's going on there tonight?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, h, Sean. Yeah, a lot of energy here in Elizabethtown. The candidate Kathy Barnette was behind me just minutes ago dancing with supporters. So, it is a lively affair. And you have to look around and think how much last minute planning went into putting on an event like this considering just a month ago, it was largely considered to be a two-man race.\par \par \uc2 But earlier this month, Kathy Barnett had a sudden surge in popularity and really, Sean, it coincided with the leaking of the Supreme Court draft opinion regarding Roe v. Wade right after that Barnette came out revealing that she was actually conceived as a product of rape when her mother was 11 years old, and at that point said she was really poised and should be considered the most pro-life of all candidates.\par \par \uc2 So that gained a lot of momentum there, but with the increased gains, you saw a lot of criticism too and that includes over some past tweets that our critics have deemed homophobic and anti-Muslim. And for that, people within the Republican Party such as former President Trump say that this has made her history makes her largely unappealing to the moderates and independents that a general election would really rely on.\par \par \uc2 So that's something we know that voters have considered. I did speak to a voter earlier today. He was wearing a Trump 2020 shirt and I asked him if he had made up his mind, he said he just did before going in, you know, into the polling location and he said that he ended up casting his vote for Kathy Barnette. He felt that she was the true conservative here.\par \par \uc2 And, Sean, I do also want to talk about because we have been in Lancaster County today that issue that Bryan was just referring to with the ballots the mail-in ballots that had the scanning ID on them that apparently did not properly ingest into the system they couldn't be scanned.\par \par \uc2 We did speak with Barnett's campaign about that. They were concerned about that initially, they wanted to make sure it was being conducted properly and they did tell us that the campaigns have been welcomed inside the county's headquarters to oversee the process of those scanned ballots being transcribed onto new documents so that they can properly be counted -- Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thanks for that report. We appreciate it. We'll be checking back in throughout the evening. Alexandria, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go to Rich Edson. He is at \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 's headquarters campaign headquarters tonight.\par \par \uc2 What's the feeling there? They came out of -- the night with a -- with a significant lead. It seems to be getting closer as the night goes on. How they feel?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They're feeling pretty good here, Sean, and this is the view here from Pittsburgh, from western Pennsylvania, really the only of the top tier parties out here in the western part of the state. You know, \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , he talked an awful lot about President Trump, about President Trump's policies, but he didn't get the nomination. He didn't get the backing from the former president of the United States, but he still said, look, this is a issue set that I have been focused on since my time at West Point, since my time in the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  and he continued to push that.\par \par \uc2 Now, there was a real multi-million dollar fight here between \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and Dr. Mehmet Oz for the last few months in this nomination fight, trying to get on board, trying to make some headway between the two. Kathy Barnette slides right in over the past week and it created real issues here and into the odds campaign.\par \par \uc2 But the message really was from the McCormick folks over the last week or so and even from \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  himself was more of a positive one. He really didn't specific comment on the number of issues that we had seen from Kathy Barnette, some of the previous tweets that she had come out with, some of the questions about her military service. Even as an Army veteran himself, \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  just really refused to go there. It was more of a positive message as that came on.\par \par \uc2 Some of the areas that we've seen some of the early returns good for \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 . They're more of the major cities within Pennsylvania, the Pittsburgh area, and the suburbs around Pittsburgh and cities in eastern Pennsylvania. So they're feeling pretty good about that as well.\par \par \uc2 Also feeling pretty good that if they go up against John Fetterman here, remember, there's another party going on here not too far from where we are in Pittsburgh. That's Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman. Now, he's not there. He had that stroke. He's in the hospital right now, but he's the one who's going to be running according to "The Associated Press" on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 They're feeling pretty good about it here. He's more of a Bernie Sanders type Democrat, but again, there's a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 Also here from the McCormick side, they were hoping that the undecideds would break. A lot of undecideds in the polling really up until the last minute. They were hoping the folks would go their way they're hoping a lot of folks go their way still early on watching and waiting here, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Great report. We appreciate it. We're checking back in throughout the night. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction now, former senior counsel to President Trump, Kellyanne Conway, former White House chief of staff Reince Priebus.\par \par \uc2 Here, before we get into this particular race or any of the specifics of this race, here's an amazing statistic, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came into this night, what, was it 50-something and 1. As of right now tonight, he's 75-1 in terms of people that he's endorsed.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne, that is a pretty -- that is a pretty incredible and impressive record, and I know groups like the Club for Growth, you know, they threw millions into Kathy Barnette's campaign in the final hours of this campaign. They're not the organization that Steve Moore once found it and is not a part of anymore.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: You know, Sean, this is really reflection of what's going on with the two political parties and their heads right now. So everybody wants \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's endorsement. The top three vote getters in the Pennsylvania Senate race right now in the primary, McCormick, Oz and Barnett all made the pilgrimage they want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's endorsement. Oz got it, the other two still were telling their connections to President Trump, to the America First agenda, to their time with him, photos with him.\par \par \uc2 That does not happen on the Democratic side. John Fetterman, the new Democratic nominee, thank God he's recovered, I hope he makes a full recovery from a stroke. God bless you, sir. I'm sure he's watching your show from the hospital, Sean.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went to the state and he was nowhere to be found until the bridge collapse and he showed up in his shorts and sweatshirt. They don't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  campaigning with them. And it tells you the tale of the two parties right now.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman endorsed Bernie Sanders and Fetterman was endorsed by Bernie Sanders last time he ran. This is a man who said abortion any time, fracking never, which brings billions and billions of dollars to Pennsylvania and creates tens of thousands of jobs. So you're looking at a very radical -- you're looking at a radicalization of some of these Democratic nominees.\par \par \uc2 Huge win for President Trump tonight in North Carolina. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is now the Republican nominee for Senate there. It's huge because he was number two or three in the polls for a long time president Trump endorsed him. The club came in for him as well.\par \par \uc2 But here's the deal of what happened there -- Budd won a 17-way primary in 2016, to go to Congress. Little-known congressman is up against a former governor and another congressman and wins that primary tonight with the president's endorsement. It shows you the power of the Trump political currency.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, I mean, I think that's a great analysis. You're right. They all ran as maggot candidates and only one eventually got it. One of the things that happened, the two things that that took place in this race, Reince, that one I don't think is healthy or good for Republicans when they kill each other.\par \par \uc2 I mean the ad war, the amount of money -- for example, I supported Dr. Oz in this race -- is over $40 million. I mean -- and these this was a brutal campaign on all sides and a lot of mud, you know, that flying back and forth. They got pretty ugly at times from my perspective. I never think that helps in the end.\par \par \uc2 And then out of nowhere, what, eight days ago, in comes Kathy Barnette. Nobody had even vetted her and she, you know, comes out of nowhere and then, all of a sudden, we find all of these controversial tweets, all of these controversial statements. I think my favorite though is that she wanted a statue of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  built, and it gave the primary voters very little time to sift through all of this and absorb all of this, and I can't even say confidently tonight that it's fully in the bloodstream in terms of Republican primary voters in Pennsylvania. What are your thoughts on 