ate GOP primary race in the commonwealth of Pennsylvania. It is tightening.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction to all of this is the author of this incredible new book, by the way, "Defeating Big Government Socialism", I never thought it would get this bad this fast but it has, former speaker of the House, FOX News contributor Newt Gingrich.\par \par \uc2 We've got a very tight race. What's fascinating about this race just objectively all three candidates tried to claim the MAGA mantle, all three of them ran as Trump candidates, whether -- and Oz got the endorsement but they all still were clinging to the MAGA mantle. Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIOBUTOR: Well, first of all, I think there's not much room in most of the Republican Party for an anti-Trump candidate. So I think just to survive, people are going to run somewhere in the shadow of Trump mostly hoping for his endorsement which, of course, Dr. Oz got.\par \par \uc2 But I think there's also a big story on the other side. The Democrats in nominating Fetterman are nominating somebody who is a self-described -- Senator Sanders socialist, a man who as chairman of the parole commission released as many criminals as he could and felt badly they couldn't get more criminals out of prison and a man who's promised to raise taxes on virtually every Pennsylvania family.\par \par \uc2 So the choices fall whoever wins in the primary is going to be extraordinarily stark and I suspect the Republicans are going to keep the seat.\par \par \uc2 I think for tonight, I don't know enough to say for sure, if in fact what we're seeing so far is mostly western Pennsylvania, then that would be McCormick's strength and we'll see Oz get stronger and Barnette gets stronger as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 The biggest single county for Republicans in plurality is Lancaster and Lancaster has had a mistake in how they counted the write-in ballots, so they may take a day or two to actually report. It's conceivable that this will take a few hours to sort out but I think uh McCormick's done very well, particularly for not having been endorsed by Trump. Oz has come on strong and that in all fairness, Kathy Barnette in the last two weeks has had an amazing surge from a position that no one would have expected.\par \par \uc2 So whoever we nominate I think is going to be such a clear alternative to Fetterman that we will almost certainly keep the seat this fall.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You don't have a -- do you have a problem with the number of incendiary comments of Kathy. To me -- I would find that in a general election very difficult to overcome but that's only my point of view.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, look, I think -- I think in the general election, if she's the nominee, the question will be who's more extreme and Fetterman has so many things that he's already on the extreme left that it will be a very strange race between two people who each have a lot of things to defend against either McCormick or against Dr. Oz, I think that Fetterman is just toast. Either one of them I think will beat him and beat him pretty handily because the choice will just be so clear.\par \par \uc2 I think of the two frankly, Dr. Oz is stronger in a general election and I think that Trump's nomination as we see the rural counties come in, we'll see what happens. If you'll remember, in Ohio, just a little while ago, as the Republican primary went on, some of the city counties went for somebody else. But the more small towns and the more rural counties all went overwhelmingly for the Trump candidate and I think you may see the same thing happen here as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What does it say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came into the night 58-1 in endorsements, now is he 75-1? That's a pretty good record.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, there's no question that whether you like Trump or dislike Trump as a matter of just history, no modern political leader has dominated his party outside of office on the scale that Trump has. And he has a following in the Republican Party. He's brought in an entire generation of people who were not Republican six or seven years ago who now are part of the Republican Party. It's an amazing achievement.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: A two-point race with 51 percent of the vote in. We've still got a long way to go. Fascinating primary for sure.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Speaker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have more HANNITY next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Election results continue to come in from Pennsylvania. The stakes are high. Too close to call. \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  versus Mehmet Oz. We're going to watch it. We have, what, 53 percent of the vote in?\par \par \uc2 Stay with foxnews.com and the FOX News Channel for continuing coverage. And we'll have more tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Laura Ingraham takes it from here. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220518ei5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Harris Faulkner \par \uc2 1,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: Roe v. Wade and access to reproductive health care including abortion helped lead to increased labor force participation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I was raised by a single mom who worked long hours to keep us out of poverty. I think framing it in the context of labor force participation is - just feels callused to me.\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: There is a spillover into labor force participation. In many cases, abortions are of teenage women, particularly low-income and often black, who aren't in a position to be able to care for children.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'll just simply say that, as a guy raised by a black woman in abject poverty, I'm thankful to be here as a United States senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That's part of an exchange heard around the world, last week, between Republican Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina and Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 . And it struck a cord with millions of people.\par \par \uc2 Now the Senator has written a new op-ed, published today in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." It's titled "Abortion is not the way to help single black mothers".\par \par \uc2 Senator Scott joins me now in an exclusive interview in focus.\par \par \uc2 First of all, welcome.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you very much --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And I want to know what went through your mind as she was saying those words. Translate what you heard and why you felt -- because it was very uncharacteristic to see you at times just lean in so hard -- why did you feel like you needed to do it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I was compelled to do so, to be honest with you. I could not believe my ears. She was responding to a question. So it was completely unprepared, unfiltered. And her response was to, in my opinion, provide a callused approach and a solution, a remedy for blacks living in poverty being abortion.\par \par \uc2 And, as a guy who was raised by a powerful, positive black woman in poverty, in a single-parent household, I know that sometimes broken places is where you find brilliance. I know that sometimes hard work and dedication and perseverance pays off handsomely.\par \par \uc2 You and I both know that the truth of the story is the American journey continues to evolve in the right direct.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And frankly, even Secretary Yellen's words should have been thought through much better, because less than 10 percent of the abortions are teenagers. And so, to put the face of abortion being poor women making a choice so they can increase the labor force participation rate --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Well --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- it's just unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And with what you gave, that statistic, it would be young black girls. I mean, and -- and now you're telling --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: -- that fewer than 10 percent are even part of --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Exactly --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: -- what she's describing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- the larger picture.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I wonder if she knows the facts on that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I got to believe that she did. And unfortunately, it seems like whenever someone needs a poster child of brokenness they go to African Americans. And that's not our story.\par \par \uc2 Our story is a story is the story of victory. Our story is a story of overcoming odds. Our story is frankly triumphing after tragic circumstances. That's the story of who we are.\par \par \uc2 And it's the story that should be told and should be told as a part of the American story that, we as Americans, we are the solution. We shouldn't have fewer Americans. We should have more Americans.\par \par \uc2 You want to increase our labor force participation rate? Choose life.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: All right, I want to get to this. You wrote in your op-ed about the Treasury Secretary. Here's a quote, "We live in a world where words are too often disconnected from the lived experiences of many Americans. Yellen's cold and robotic reference to the issue of life is just the latest example of that." What did you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, couple things. First, when you hear her words and you see my lived experience, you see your lived experience, so many Americans, millions of us, started in poverty. Frankly, they say 97 percent of those who are rich today were either middle class or in poverty two generations ago. My grandfather was picking cotton. He lived long enough to watch his grandson pick a seat in Congress. That tells me that the story --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- continues. The journey becomes more and more beautiful, more and more amazing.\par \par \uc2 But, when you think about the fact that the words that people speak are disconnected from the reality, you hear that inflation is good for people living in poverty. Nothing is further from the truth. You know that baby formula is hard to find, but you knew that several months ago and you did nothing with it.\par \par \uc2 It's almost as if the administration wants us to believe what they are saying as opposed to what we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes. Yes. Hear the words out of their mouth, not what we see them doing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Our lived reality.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And by the way -- by the way, FEMA has back piles of this. Of course, they have a stockpile. If there's a hurricane or a tornado, they've got to have baby formula.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: What if the hospitals run out, because -- because we have a disaster? I mean, it is mind-boggling. That was a good example of that.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to more of your words.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I don't know in the time that I have covered you as a journalist that I've ever known you to write so passionately. I want to get to this. You also wrote in your new op-ed in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," "If abortion is our first and best answer to ensure that women and low-income families can thrive economically, the United States has reached one of its darkest times in our history." Senator.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: There's no doubt that when you think about those words that she spoke and the truth of our existence, we should be leaning into the solution that is our nation and is a story of American evolution.\par \par \uc2 I certainly believe that hard times produces our best results, that frankly, it's --\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Wow, that's for everybody. Did you get that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Amen. Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That's so true. Because there are a lot of people going through hard times right now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That's just it. FAULKNER: Those parents with the deficit of baby formula shortage.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We always have hard times. That's just the truth of our existence. It's always tragedy then triumph. It's always obstacles then opportunities. It's always problems then promise.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that makes me excited about who I am and where we are as a country is that the hardest times I've lived through made a better person and made me more compassionate towards others.\par \par \uc2 So, I hate to hear that those hard times should be eliminated by eliminating of my life. That's a stretch and one that I just have to speak out against.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: You know, when President Trump was in office, you and he worked on opportunity zones. And I really got to do the drilldown on all of that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Can you explain where we are now? Because there was a lot of money spent. What happened to those areas? And it wasn't just people of color. I mean, those benefit -- high tide lifts all boats.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I mean, those communities were touched by the fact that somebody was focusing in on them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: One of the silver linings that we can see now looking back at the pandemic is that pre-pandemic 2019, we saw $29 billion enter into -- private-sector dollars, by the way, go into the poorest zip codes in America. And because of that, we saw the poverty rate hit the lowest ever recorded in the history of this country.\par \par \uc2 That's the kind of insulation you want going into a pandemic. We saw African American unemployment below 6 percent for the first time in the history of the country. We saw wages for the bottom 25 percent grow faster than the top 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, that going into the pandemic actually gave us insulation so that confronting with a global pandemic people without resources had more money, more opportunity than they had seen in their lifetimes.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, women had a 70-year low in unemployment, which means that translating into the pandemic we had more resistance than we would have had, had it not been for the economic success and the policies of the Trump administration going into that pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So, when you talk about the fragility of life and the harshness of circumstances like a global pandemic, we should stand up straight, put our shoulders back and be confident that we shall survive this too -- not quit in the middle.\par \par \uc2 And it's that resiliency that is a part of the DNA of America that we should be focusing on.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Wow, those are powerful word -- not quit in the middle. Some people would say, and -- and some of them are even doctors I've interviewed, that with the lockdowns and the increasing sort of pressure on the American people to solve the pandemic with their own behavior and not feeding us enough of the true science.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: That the changes that we were seeing, that that was quitting in the middle. Your last quick word.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well listen, America's always the solution, not the problem. If we lean in as one American family, we find the greatness that's within us. Unfortunately, pressure typically is necessary to reveal the jewels that we are.\par \par \uc2 And so, whether you are talking about African Americans surviving the last few hundred years of American existence and coming out better, whether you're talking about the brokenness of poverty in rural America, whether you're talking about the harshness and the challenges of the pandemic, the one thing we know about the American family, we are better together, we find solutions, and the greatest of our opportunities don't come from Washington. They come from neighborhoods and garages and libraries and apartments where people come up with the most amazing and remarkable results.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Amen. Senator Tim Scott. Wow. I have to say, again, covering you, we haven't seen you lean in like this. You're passionate about this, and America gets to benefit by hearing your words.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And giving us that -- that thing that most of us know, but we need to be reminded of, that we're so much better than the words of someone who doesn't see our potential.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: So much better than that.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely, Harris.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Senator, thank you for being in focus.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020220517ei5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Left Rushes To Politicize The Buffalo Tragedy; Pennsylvania Primary Election Is Tomorrow\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Leo Terrell, Joe Concha, Lara Trump, Kayleigh  McEnany \par \uc2 8,295 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 It is the night before the all-important primary and coming up, the candidate and -- that I and President Trump endorsed for the Senate, Dr. Mehmet Oz, will be here.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will continue our investigation into his opponent, Kathy Barnette, who is back by the shadowy, swampy, Never Trumper group named Club for Growth. I know they used to be Republican. They're not anymore.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, the mysteries surrounding Kathy Barnette gets stranger and stranger. There are still more questions than there are answers tonight about Barnette's background, her rhetoric, political beliefs, her tweets that are beyond controversial. Whether or not she can even compete in a general election, that's straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to the horrific shooting in Buffalo, New York. Over the weekend, a deranged racist monster opened fire at a supermarket murdering 10 innocent Americans. Now, tonight, we all do pray for all of the victims and their families and the entire city of buffalo everyone impacted by this despicable act of violence.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, yet unfortunately, predictably, many on the left almost immediately started exploiting their pain, their suffering for political gain. In fact after the shooting, it took Democrats just three hours to start blaming Republicans and conservatives and talk show hosts and FOX News. Of course, it's the same terrible smear tactic that liberals have been using for years.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the `90s, you might remember former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  tried to blame \uc2 Rush Limbaugh\uc2  and talk radio for the Oklahoma City bombing. He later said, quote, so the instrument carrying this forward was basically the right wing radio talk show hosts and they understand clearly that emotion was more powerful than reason most of the time.\par \par \uc2 Now, you might remember in 2007, then Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , remember he blamed \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2  for the \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2  massacre. You might remember in 2016, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " blaming the entire Republican Party for the Pulse nightclub. In 2019, Democrats blaming President Trump for the El Paso shooting.\par \par \uc2 And just today, Democrat \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  blaming House Republicans for a shooting in California where apparently a Chinese man opened fire on a Taiwanese congregation. Swalwell referred to the shooter as the GOP's, quote, boy, whatever that means.\par \par \uc2 Now, we can go on and on. Democrats always play the blame game. Unless, of course, it does not benefit them politically. Almost no one in the media mob is covering the 33 people that were shot in Chicago. There are their names right there.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, did you hear their names? Did you hear any reports? Did you hear any outrage? Did you hear any blame?\par \par \uc2 As per usual, this program, we've done this since 2009, we scrolled the names of thousands and thousands of names of people shot in Chicago that you've never heard about. And you might ask, well, why is that? Why every weekend, 30, 40, 60 people shot, we never hear about those people shot?\par \par \uc2 Well, Democrats can't blame Republicans. They can't blame conservatives, and they'd have to look at their policies of let's see, lax on crime, defund, dismantle and no bail laws. So, not a peep from the Democrats about the 10 people shot in Baltimore during a single day. Did any of you hear about the 34 people shot during one week in Los Angeles? Does \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ever talk about the daily violence gripping America's cities because the Democratic Party policies, defund, dismantle, no bail?\par \par \uc2 Now, tomorrow, Biden will pay a visit to Buffalo, frankly rightly so. But keep in mind, he didn't visit Wisconsin last fall after Darrell Brooks murdered six and injured 62 during an attack on a Christmas parade. He didn't visit New York in April after a Black nationalist named Frank James opened fire in a New York City subway, shooting 10. In fact, Democrats didn't play the blame game after either of these attacks.\par \par \uc2 By the way, never mind the 574 riots that, let's see, killed dozens of Americans, injured thousands of cops, caused billions of property damage. Their silence was deafening. They called those protests mostly peaceful. They weren't even close to mostly peaceful.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, after a far-left \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  super fan, and according to "The Daily Caller", Rachel Maddow lover, attempted to massacre Republican lawmakers at a congressional baseball practice, that's the one where Steve Scalise nearly lost his life, and no blame game there either. This was a politically motivated act of violence from a guy who openly declared his love for \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Rachel Maddow.\par \par \uc2 Well, following the shooting, Democrats didn't smear Maddow. They didn't -- they didn't go after \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and nor should they have. No one should be held accountable for the evil actions of a depraved individual whatever their political leanings may or may not be. Everyone's responsible for their own conduct.\par \par \uc2 In fact, right here on this program, when these incidents came up and they turned out to be big supporters of liberals, I have been saying it for years. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me make one thing clear. We believe that every individual, they are responsible for their own actions and their own decisions. I did not blame \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  on this program. I went out of my way not to blame him for the fact that one of his supporters was violent against those ball -- those people playing on the ball field.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, earlier, I said the same thing about \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 . Remember the Unabomber? Radically, far left, environmentally conscious terrorist who was apparently an avid reader of \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 's book "Earth in the Balance" for the unbalanced.\par \par \uc2 But I didn't blame \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  for the psychotic actions of Ted Kaczynski either. We've been remarkably consistent.\par \par \uc2 We condemn all riots. We condemn all violence, and we don't blame people that did not cause people to act this way. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I thought we were always taught by the left never blame the victim. For example, you had the Unabomber in his hut, the \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 's book "Earth in the Balance" was in there. Did \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  inspire the Unabomber? That wouldn't be fair.\par \par \uc2 Or you could take any number of examples where left-wing words do not cause people you cannot say that words are causing people to be violent. But that's the narrative.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: On this show, we condemn all violence. We deplore all acts of hatred, all acts of bigotry. We stand up against racism where we see it. We refuse to exploit horrific loss of life or political gain.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, liberals, the media mobs should be ashamed of themselves. But sadly, they have no shame. They will do anything for power. They'll do anything to lie, smear, besmirch and slander conservatives.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, for the record no one spews more violent rhetoric than the left. In case you need a refresher course, here you go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: You cannot be civil with a political party that wants to destroy what you stand for what you care about.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: But I like to debate this gentleman. And I said, no, I said, if we were in high school, I'd take him behind the gym and beat the hell out of him.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: You got to be ready to take a punch. You got to be ready to throw a punch.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): You get out and create a crowd, and you push back on them. And you tell them they're not welcome.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Everyone should take note of that on both levels, that this isn't -- they're not going to let up and they should not.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I want to tell you, Gorsuch. I want to tell you, Kavanaugh. You have released the whirlwind and you will pay the price.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): Get up in the face of some Congress people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): It does you any good running away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think you need to go back and punch him in the face.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DE NIRO, ACTOR: How dare he say the things he does? Of course, I want to punch him in the face.\par \par \uc2 MADONNA, SINGER: Yes, I have thought an awful lot about blowing up the White House.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY DEPP, ACTOR: When was the last time an actor assassinated a president.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: But Michelle says that you know when they go low, we go high. They go low, we kick them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They go low, we kick them.\par \par \uc2 Imagine just for a second that was rhetoric coming from conservatives or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters or this news channel, what would the reaction in the country be?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with reaction now, FOX News contributor Joe Concha, along with FOX News contributor Leo 2.0 Terrell.\par \par \uc2 Leo 2.0 Terrell, we'll start with you tonight. Very specifically, this double standard has existed for a long time. I'm fed up with it, it's a big lie. They get away with it.\par \par \uc2 I never blame Rachel Maddow. I never blame \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . I never blame anybody for violent actions except -- I didn't blame \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  for the Unabomber. You blame the people that are involved in the actions, but that the left doesn't play fair like that.\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No, they never will. And, Sean, you've done this for years you stroll scroll the names of those Black victims.\par \par \uc2 Let me be very clear -- tomorrow, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to Buffalo to play the white supremacy card because the Democrats exploit Black Americans for one reason, for power. The Black on Black crime, you won't see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  going to Chicago or L.A. or any of these Democratic cities. It's because they use Black Americans for power and control.\par \par \uc2 Do they -- do they help Black Americans? Look at the schools. Look at -- look at the crime, absolutely not. But they played this rhetoric. They played this game.\par \par \uc2 And you know what? It's not working as much. It's not having the same effect that it had years ago, because people are wise to it. That's why you have Blacks and Browns leaving the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 And what they're trying to do now is do whatever they can to create a new base because most people want liberty and freedom. The Democratic elites want that control over people's life. The best example is Leo 2.0.\par \par \uc2 Because when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, if you vote for Trump, you ain't black, and then defunding the police. Black Americans people of all color wants liberty and freedom, and that's what they don't get from the Democrats. The Democrats are only masters of the race card when it's black and white.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Let me bring in the media angle with Joe Concha, probably the best media person on TV today, and you pay very close attention to this.\par \par \uc2 The double standard, I don't -- what -- the examples I use are only the tip of the iceberg here, Joe. You can give many more and go on probably for the next hour giving examples of the hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, and in the end, the focus should be on -- from those in this media -- accountability should begin and end with the person pulling the trigger. And we should mourn the victims.\par \par \uc2 But some of this business completely skipped that part and rushed to judgment before anyone really knew anything about the shooter or his motives. Here are the facts, Sean. The shooter in this case specifically attacked this network and conservatives in general in his crazy manifesto. No matter, because this horrific shooting is being exploited to push broader narratives and agendas.\par \par \uc2 In this case, it's twofold. It's gun control and it's censorship in no particular order. And by playing the blame game and pointing the finger at those not remotely involved, we missed the bigger story. That overall, when you look at it from a macro level, the U.S. murder rate in this country rose to a 25-year high in Mr. Biden's first year.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen cities set homicide records in 2021. And, Sean, there also isn't enough focus on the heroes here either. In that Laguna Beach shooting, a primary care physician, his name was Dr. John Chang, he sacrificed himself to save others. He attacked the gunman and was able to subdue him long enough while injured for other parishioners to come in and hog time before the police arrived.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Chang saved God knows how many lives in that church and throughout his life in this profession. He's an absolute hero and may he rest in peace. Say his name again, Dr. John Chang, hero.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me -- let me go to you, Joe, stay on the media for a second. Then we'll get a comment from Leo, and Leo brought it up.\par \par \uc2 We have scrolled the names of thousands of people that have been shot in the city of Chicago. We started in 2009. That was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's home city and they have not lifted a finger to stop the violence in that city. The names go on every weekend, X number, you know, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, maybe even as high as 70 people shot, 30 people dead, whatever the number is you never hear a word.\par \par \uc2 I thought every life mattered. Why is it we never hear a word from anybody on the Democratic side about fixing violence in cities and towns that have been run by liberal Democrats now for decades?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's accepted as the new the new normal I suppose, Sean. You know, when I mentioned before that 16 cities set homicide records, all of them were run or are run by Democratic mayors, mostly in states that have Democratic governors. So, the narrative suddenly becomes very inconvenient when it comes to reporting these stories.\par \par \uc2 And, yeah, you mentioned the shootings in Chicago. We know what's going to happen this summer as the days go longer and more people are out you're going to hear numbers that rival those that you hear about only in other countries. You say, well, no, that's happening over there. No, it's happening here in Chicago, my favorite city.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, the national media just yawns at this point because I guess they feel it isn't a story because it happened so often, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And they can't blame conservatives and they can't blame FOX News and talk radio and Republicans, Leo.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: They can't because you know who has shot like for the last year since I've been a contributor, systemic racism doesn't exist, except in the minds of Democrats. You see the Democratic playbook is from 1960. You talked about crime in Democratic cities, they run the city but they're still systemic racism, still systemic discrimination, because it's the only card they have.\par \par \uc2 And again, President Biden can drop everything and run to Buffalo, and he's going to play the race card as much as he can to distract us. But the American people are too wise for that game.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Leo, thank you. Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right now to the all-important GOP Senate primary race in the commonwealth of Pennsylvania that will happen tomorrow. Full disclosure, we've been honest, we always are. I endorsed Dr. Oz in the primary. President Trump has endorsed him as well.\par \par \uc2 Recently, another GOP candidate has drawn a lot of late attention after only in the last week to 10 days, rising in the polls seemingly out of nowhere. She hadn't been vetted up to this point.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Barnette is now facing a massive and evolving scandal over her past beliefs, her past comments, her past tweets and her actions and inconsistencies that she seems unwilling to answer. We tried to call her, contact her team and we can't make contact at this point.\par \par \uc2 Look at your screen. Here is Barnette marching alongside, this just broke today, radical Proud Boys during January 6. She claimed she had no