a race of people so that he will go and get a gun, come into that community and kill these people, kill an 86-year-old woman who had just come from visiting her husband at a nursing home, shopping in that Tops store.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I have been into that Tops store in that neighborhood. And to be gunned down this way, it's just pure hatred, pure evil. And there has to be a complete investigation here, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And we need to know how a person for who may have some mental illness was able to get a gun. But I can tell you, Charles, when we talk about mental illness, and we talk about this person gunning down 10 people and murdering 10 people, it's -- the question is, from a legal perspective, is -- when he gunned these people down, did he know the different from right and wrong, not that he was just mentally ill.\par \par \uc2 And when we think about this brave police officer, a police officer who had retired from the police force there in Buffalo, Officer Salter, who was just working there, who tried to take this man down, and because of the armor that he had on, he wound up killing this officer also. Pure evil, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Ted, when these things happen, we always discover -- not always, but, too often, we discover that the person was in the interest of law enforcement, that someone said something, that someone visited him.\par \par \uc2 This particular suspect, the police came to actually visit him when he was in high school. How do we -- and we have had this discussion before. Is there a way to follow up with someone? Is there a way to flag someone when they show these dangerous tendencies, the fantasies of hurting other people, murder/suicide?\par \par \uc2 When the New York State Police have visits your home, should there be some sort of follow-up or follow-through?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Absolutely, Charles, there should be.\par \par \uc2 When a person exhibit this kind of violent behavior, and law enforcement come in contact with it, they need to memorialize that. They need to put it out there somewhere in the law enforcement community that this person exists and his tendencies.\par \par \uc2 It is my understanding, yes, that, in high school, this 18-year-old threatened another high school student, and that, for a day-and-a-half, he may have had some mental treatment. But guess what, Charles? He was permitted to go and buy a gun.\par \par \uc2 And so they're going to have to check that gun dealer to see, under what circumstances and what did he know when he sold that gun or guns, should we say?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Because these guns, from what we have been told, were illegal -- were legally purchased by this 18-year-old.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Ted, there was a 180-page manifesto. There have been social media postings.\par \par \uc2 To what degree should the family and friends be questioned? And at what point is there any sort of liability when you know someone speaks like this over and over again, when they have written a manifesto, when they posted social media things that indicate a violent streak or an urge to commit violence against other people?\par \par \uc2 Is there any time when people who know these things are liable, that they should actually speak up? I know it's tough when it's a family member, but certainly it feels like so many of them could have been stopped if a family member would have said something?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You're absolutely right again, Charles.\par \par \uc2 When you see something, you have to say something. Sometimes, it's -- the most difficult thing is for family members to face the fact that they have another family member who has some forms of mental illness. But it -- but all mentally ill people don't act this way.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Ted...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, if there is a knowledge that there was a manifesto...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm going to cut you off.\par \par \uc2 Here's the pressure.\par \par \uc2 I was going to get you guys in there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: ... turned off.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You need anything, Harry?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (OFF-MIKE) would be good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mayor Brown.\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN, MAYOR OF BUFFALO, NEW YORK: I want to thank the members of the media for joining us.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have an extended press briefing this afternoon. As we begin this new week, our community continues to grieve and heal. We are a strong community. We are rallying around one another, and we will continue to move forward as a strong city of Buffalo, county of Erie, and Western New York region.\par \par \uc2 Late today, we have been made aware of many social media posts going around with possible threats. I want to be clear, Buffalo police and our partnering law enforcement agencies standing here are investigating these social media posts and will prosecute if necessary.\par \par \uc2 And I want to emphasize arrests have already been made. And you will be hear -- hearing more about those.\par \par \uc2 I'm now going to turn things over to Buffalo Police Commissioner Joseph Gramaglia.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH GRAMAGLIA, BUFFALO, NEW YORK, POLICE CHIEF: Thank you, Mayor.\par \par \uc2 So, as I said before, this is a very long investigation. It's going to continue to be a very lengthy investigation. The scene is still being processed. Search warrants have been obtained and executed. And they will still be obtained. There's a lot of digital footprint, electronics that will have to go through, So that process is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 Information has also come as a result of some of this investigation that the individual was here a few months ago back in early March. So, as I said, there's a lot of material to go through, so that we have confirmed now that it appears that individual was here back a couple of months ago in early March.\par \par \uc2 So, at this point, like I said, it's going to be a lengthy investigation. Things will change, information will change as we become aware of that.\par \par \uc2 That's about all I have on the investigation at this point.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAMAGLIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We will now hear from U.S. attorney for the Western District Trini Ross.\par \par \uc2 TRINI ROSS, U.S. ATTORNEY: The federal investigation is continuing.\par \par \uc2 We're working, again, jointly with our state and local law enforcement partners. Regarding the threats that have been going around on the Internet, we have a point-of-contact person in my office to deal specifically with those threats, as we have several prosecutors dealing with the investigation of the crime that took place on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 So the U.S. attorney's office is actively engaged in this investigation. We will continue to be. And we will continue to work with our law enforcement partners and the district attorney's office, DA Flynn, as necessary.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We will now hear from Erie County district attorney John Flynn.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FLYNN, ERIE COUNTY, NEW YORK, DISTRICT ATTORNEY: So I want to touch on a couple topics right here this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And I will piggyback on what the mayor said at the end here. First of all, those of you who are local reporters know my M.O. and how I proceed with talking about investigations and matters during the course of the legal process. Some of you from out of town, let me explain how I work.\par \par \uc2 Once an arraignment has happened, I pretty much shut down, because the legal proceeding has started, and I just do not talk during the course of the legal proceeding. In this matter right here, obviously, this is a very high-profile case. There's a lot of information out there.\par \par \uc2 I made an exception for today. So, today, Monday being the first business day since Saturday, I have done a number of interviews today. I will continue to do some more interviews tonight. I'm here standing right now in front of you. But, after today is over, I am done.\par \par \uc2 And after today is over, the next proceeding that will take place will be the felony hearing. And once the felony hearing is over on Thursday, the matter is going to go, for lack of a better phrase, into grand jury posture.\par \par \uc2 So, once it goes into grand jury posture, the envelope of secrecy now is going to cover everything. And we're pretty much going to shut down all investigative, all prosecution information. So that's kind of the game plan going forward.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you right now that there has been a new development with regard to the prosecution. The -- as all of you know, on Saturday night at the arraignment, the defense attorney made a request for a forensic examination. That request was to the judge that two medical doctors look at my client and evaluate him to determine whether or not we can proceed.\par \par \uc2 Less than 24 hours later, the defense attorney withdrew that request. So, that forensic examination has now been withdrawn. I don't see the need to ask for it myself, so I'm not going to ask for it. The judge has the ability sua sponte, on his own, for those who didn't take Latin, on his own, to order the investigation, a forensic.\par \par \uc2 He did not feel the need to do that. So the mental health forensic part of this has now become a moot point and is now off the table. So we are proceeding now to Thursday's felony hearing. One of two things is going to happen at that felony hearing. The defendant is going to waive the felony hearing or the felony hearing is going to happen on Wednesday, and then we will go forward.\par \par \uc2 So, again, just kind of -- I'm just trying to let everyone know in advance, once that felony hearing happens on 30 -- on Thursday, the -- quote, unquote -- "grand jury investigation" is going to begin, and I will not be able to talk more about anything having to do with this investigation until there has been an indictment on this matter.\par \par \uc2 Again, for those of you out of town, my M.O. is, after an indictment, there will be an arraignment on the indictment. And I will do a press conference on the arraignment on the indictment.\par \par \uc2 But then, once I do a briefing on the arraignment on the indictment, I again shut down again until trial. So, just kind of want to let everyone from out of town how I operate. I have been operating that way for the past five-and-a-half years. I'm going to continue to operate that way, even though, obviously, this is a very high-profile case.\par \par \uc2 The second thing I want to say is that I understand that there is a court of public opinion out there. I understand that there's the narrative out there that there is so much evidence, it's overwhelming, this guy is guilty, this guy did it, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 I understand that court of opinion. I understand emotions are high. I understand the rawness of this matter. However, I do not operate in the court of public opinion. I operate in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 And this defendant is innocent until proven guilty. Let me repeat that. He is innocent until proven guilty. He has only been charged right now with one charge. And that's it. It is an accusation. It is an allegation. And that's all it is.\par \par \uc2 As a prosecutor, I have three communities that I have to protect their rights. I have to protect the rights of the public, the victims, and the defendant. And I have to insure that the defendant has a fair trial here in Buffalo, New York. That is my job, to insure a fair and impartial trial. And I'm going to do everything I can to make sure that that happens.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I want to talk about, and I want to piggyback what the mayor said, there's a lot of chatter out there. There is a lot of threats out there that I'm hearing about and that we're hearing about in law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 One of those threats happened yesterday. A 52-year-old man from Buffalo yesterday allegedly -- and, again, allegedly -- called up a pizzeria at 12:05 p.m. and made threatening comments to the employee, referencing what happened at Tops; 45 minutes later, allegedly, that same individual called up a brewery in downtown Buffalo and made similar threatening comments, again alluding to what happened at Tops.\par \par \uc2 The pizzeria and the brewery called BPD. As usual, BPD did a great job, found out who this guy was, went and arrested him last night. We held him last night, because it was a D felony. And under the New York state bail laws, we can hold someone overnight for a D felony, which we did. We held him overnight, and we arraigned him this morning on a D felony, making a terrorist threat, facing up to seven years in jail.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK, we're hearing from officials in Buffalo, the mayor, U.S. attorney general. And, of course, that's the Erie County district attorney.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring back Ted Williams.\par \par \uc2 And, Ted, a few things struck me. And I want to get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 First, when the mayor was speaking, he talked about these social media posts, these threats. They're investigating them. Erie County, DA John Flynn just alluded to them as well. Copycats in these sorts of cases, how do we guard, how does society guard against the potential for copycats, particularly in the very near-term, immediate future?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, Charles, we have to be very vigilant when it comes to copycats.\par \par \uc2 As you know, just over the weekend here in Laguna Woods, California, we had a shooting at a church. We don't know if there's a nexus. We don't know if this individual at the Laguna Woods, California, church was a copycat. But there are a lot of mentally ill people out there. There are a lot of people out there, unfortunately, who will try to take advantage of a situation like this.\par \par \uc2 And all law enforcement can do is be vigilant. And all the citizens can do are be vigilant also. And if they have information that they can turn over to law enforcement authorities, they should do so.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Ted, the DA mentioned that there was a new development, where the defense initially requested a forensic exam, I guess, in an attempt to perhaps declare whether or not the suspect is mentally capable, if he's mentally fit to be held responsible for these things, but then they dropped the request.\par \par \uc2 You had mentioned early on when we initially spoke during the show about his mental capacity at the time. If the judge doesn't bring this back up, will that not be a factor from here on out?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: If the judge doesn't bring it up, it may not be effective.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you that either the judge or his attorneys are going to revisit the question of mental illness. Somewhere along the line, I can unequivocally tell you there will be a mental evaluation of this individual tab.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 Ted, we got to leave it there, my friend. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Folks, more right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Biden vs. Bezos.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder and billionaire \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  squaring off with President Biden over inflation.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Edward Lawrence has the latest from the White House -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, a tweet from the president started some criticism. Sound familiar? Well, this time, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tweeting out that, if you want to lower inflation, make sure the wealthiest corporations pay their fair share.\par \par \uc2 That sparked a stern response from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder and owner of \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 . Bezos, normally a cheerleader for President Biden in the Biden administration, immediately tweeted back, which prompted the White House deputy press secretary, Andrew Bates, to say: "It's easy to see why the wealthiest people on earth oppose Biden's agenda to help the middle class," adding that: "It's also unsurprising that this tweet comes after the president met with labor organizers, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employees."\par \par \uc2 So, today, Bezos continued the back-and-forth saying: "Look, a squirrel. This is the White House statement about my recent tweets. They understandably want to muddy the topic. They know inflation hurts the neediest the most, but unions aren't causing inflation, and neither are wealthy people. Remember, the administration tried their best to add another $3.5 trillion to the federal spending. But they failed. If they had succeeded, inflation would be even higher than it is today. And inflation today is at a 40-year high."\par \par \uc2 So the new White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , weighed in about 20 minutes ago. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It's not a huge mystery why one of the wealthiest individuals on Earth, right, opposes an economic agenda that is for the middle class, that cuts some of the biggest cost families face, fights inflation for the long haul, right?\par \par \uc2 And that's what we're talking about. That's why we're talking about lowering inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: But Republicans say this is exactly what's wrong with the president's economic policies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): They're not sensitive to the concerns of everyday Americans, everyday Wisconsinites.\par \par \uc2 And until we do that, I fear the situation could get worse, in particular until they change their energy policy, which is stifling domestic production and increasing costs across the board. I think we're going to be in for a rough ride.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And there is no change, of course, from the White House on any of those economic policies -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Edward, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, is Bezos right?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask American Action Forum president Douglas Holtz-Eakin, along with Johanna Maska, a former aide to President Obama.\par \par \uc2 All right, Douglas, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, I think this is more politics than real.\par \par \uc2 If this was really an anti-inflation policy, then they'd recognize that, over the long term, it's counterproductive. It's a tax on savings, investment and growth in the supply side of the economy. They have said repeatedly you need to grow the supply side of the economy. And so this isn't the way to do that.\par \par \uc2 So it's not a real anti-inflation policy. And the litmus test for that, Charles, is that, if you believed this was the best way to control demand, then, when you needed stimulus, you would cut corporate rates. And when they needed stimulus, they did nothing of the sort. They spent $1.9 trillion, and we have got the inflation.\par \par \uc2 So this isn't about inflation policy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: This is just an excuse to run out their policy again.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Johanna, for the most part, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 's \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  friendly to the administration, but they're not the only ones right. Steve Rattner has been critical. \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  has been critical and a whole lot of other folks that you, by the way, worked with under the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 Really critical of these policies.\par \par \uc2 JOHANNA MASKA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: You know, Charles, I was so thrilled to join you on this topic, because \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  accusing someone of misdirection is rich.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a guy who's a tech guy who took a picture of his own genitalia that got out, and he blamed it on the Saudis. This is also a guy who, the day that he increased the minimum wage, he cut profit-sharing.\par \par \uc2 And that is actually very serious. We don't make a middle class, we don't create wealth without profit-sharing. So, absolutely, there needs to be a conversation about how \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is adding to the middle class. And I think he's going to come under more fire, because...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the central topic, though, Johanna?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: ... he literally opened up our markets to China.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the central market, the question here, the inflation question...\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... the administration's efforts...\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Well, and this is...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... that many say backfired, that that $1.9 trillion was essentially unnecessary, and it's -- now we're paying a serious price for it with this wage-price spiral that's devastating most Americans?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: There have been so many politicians who have spent and spent and spent without a plan to actually grow the amount of money that we're spending from. So that has happened time and time again.\par \par \uc2 But here's a guy who should have unique insight on how we change the situation on inflation, because he's literally opened up our American consumers to buying direct from Chinese companies through \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . So he should have a unique vantage point on the supply chain issues that we're dealing with.\par \par \uc2 I think it's fair for the administration to call him out on what he's done to wreck our small businesses in towns like mine in Galesburg, Illinois...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: ... because it's about time that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay its fair shake.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That's a different topic, although I will say, I don't know how many businesses in America have 1.1 million employees. They pay a wage that's far above minimum wage. And people I know who work there are thrilled to have the job.\par \par \uc2 Douglas, let me go back to you and the central question, because listen...\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... it's -- again, it's economics 101.\par \par \uc2 And it's OK if you thought it was going to work, and it didn't work. But I think what Bezos is saying is that the administration continues to go down this path. And if it wasn't for Senator Manchin, we would be in a hell of a lot of trouble as a nation right now.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: I think we see continually attempts to change the story on where the inflation came from.\par \par \uc2 And they don't have a plausible story. And they just keep changing it every day. This is the latest. Mr. Bezos did say, look, it's fair to have a conversation on corporate taxes, but let's not confuse it with the inflation problem, because the reality is, the inflation problem came from the over-stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The way to see that is to recognize one of the most pressing inflation problems is shelter, rents and mortgages and the like. There's no supply chain involved there. Those houses are in America. There's no getting things in from China or anywhere else. And the inflation came from the excessive stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Now it's got to be dealt with by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , but they have a responsibility.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: They did this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Let me bring Johanna back in for the last word here.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the things that Douglas pointed out, also, you have got the issue of gasoline. We have got a ton of crude oil under our feet. We know how to get to it. The administration won't let us. That's another, in my mind, self-inflicted wound that everyone's paying a price for, Johanna.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: We have actually seen the Biden administration open up drilling leases, and I think we have got to make our -- gas has to be more affordable.\par \par \uc2 But the overall question of inflation, I mean, Bret Baier was asking \uc2 Steve Mnuchin\uc2 , aren't we going to see massive inflation because we keep spending and spending? This has been time and time again that the politicians are just spending and spending and spending without a solution.\par \par \uc2 So it's no surprise we have inflation. I wish we would start talking about solutions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So do I. We...\par \par \uc2 MASKA: So, in terms of \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , what he could do, come to the table, talk about solutions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I think he will. I think -- I think that's what he's trying to do.\par \par \uc2 We got to leave it there. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Voters dealing with the hot inflation as midterm primary season heating up, including some key Senate and House contests in North Carolina tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Our own Connell McShane was just down in North Carolina talking to farmers about what is actually going on down there. And he joins us here.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I'll tell you, not only \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  concerned about inflation in America, Charles.\par \par \uc2 As you know, you talk to any kind of business owners, including farmers in North Carolina, it is issue number one ahead of these midterms.\par \par \uc2 This guy Tommy Porter, he's a farmer in North Carolina. He's got cows and pigs, chickens on his farm and the like. And it's just amazing, the price hikes he's dealing with. In fact, he used fertilizer as an example of inflation hitting home for him. Take a listen to our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMMY PORTER, PORTER FARMS: Cost of fertilizer up. Chemicals are up. Fertilizer is up three to four times what it was a year ago.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: I mean, that's crazy. Three to four times?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Three -- $275 gallon tote of generic Roundup, which is a weed killer, a year ago, $1,400, now over $10,000.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Ten thousand.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: So it's just -- everything has gone out of sight.\par \par \uc2 Fertilizer, three to four times what it was a year ago. And it's -- we have nowhere to pass that cost on. Farmers are...\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: That -- I think that's what people miss sometimes when they're talking about or to farmers, is they assume, well, you're paying more, but maybe you're charging more you can pass that along. It's not that simple?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: It's not that -- it's not the case. Farmers are price takers. We're not price makers.\par \par \uc2 The price of cattle is no better than it was a year ago. So I have got three to four times the cost to produce them, and receiving no more for them. And that's not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Not sustainable, Charles, for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 One thing, he's tied into number of contracts.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: So he can't raise the prices the way he might like to because of all these other costs going up.\par \par \uc2 He and other farmers we spoke to are worried about the future. And they did transition into politics in their -- in their argument about that, saying no doubt it's going to affect the way that they're thinking into the fall.\par \par \uc2 And you can see why Republicans are confident in a state like North Carolina, just because you get people like this, they're more likely to show up to vote and to vote for the Republican Party, maybe keep that Senate seat red.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Did he connect the dots on the fertilizer, for instance?\par \par \uc2 We know that a big component is the natural gas.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That gas is something that we have it in abundance.\par \par \uc2 I remember, years ago, when the oil companies were burning it off. They didn't know what to do with it. And now it's up through the roof.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, isn't there something we can do about that?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well, we had a big conversation, just generally speaking, with Tommy about energy independence, just that, when I asked him, hey, what can we do, that was -- his first thing was, well, we shouldn't have cut off the pipeline, for example, the Keystone pipeline.\par \par \uc2 And then he went on to talk about how we're too dependent on other countries in general for energy, not just natural gas, of course, but oil and other -- and other commodities. So he thought energy independence was one thing where the current policies over the last couple of years have made the situation worse, and he'd like to see some change.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, as far as competition...\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... the Biden administration keeps talking about how few meat producers there are and other things.\par \par \uc2 Are they confident, these farmers, that maybe if the government got involved, there'd be more competition, or would they rather them back up?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well, I will tell you one place where competition is hurting, actually, in some cases, which is interesting, there was another farmer we talked to who they have real problems with labor costs, right?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: So their competition is from the low-cost countries, like Mexico, a little bit from Canada as well, but definitely from Mexico, where you're paying people less than 10 bucks a day.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: And they have to pay 14, 15 bucks an hour. And they say there's actually a lot of large multinational companies, American companies, that are doing business over there that are undercutting them on price.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: So that was the other side of this bill.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, good stuff. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: All right, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, folks, a border surge in full swing, as officials wait to see if a federal judge will stop the White House from lifting Title 42.\par \par \uc2 Our Bill Melugin is down in Eagle Pass, Texas, and the clock is ticking -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, it certainly is.\par \par \uc2 And today in Eagle Pass has been one of the most active days we have seen in quite some time. We have had group after group after group crossing illegally, including the single biggest group we have ever seen here in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 That video and a whole lot more coming up right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: If Title 42 goes away, Mayor, what does it mean for you and your town and the area?\par \par \uc2 YOLANDA RAMON, MAYOR PRO-TEM OF EAGLE PASS, TEXAS: Use all the tools that you have, all the tools in the toolbox.\par \par \uc2 And if that's all we have, Title 42, to use it as a tool, then let's please use it. Let's not remove it. Let's continue to keep it there just so that we can put a hold or even probably try to slow down the influx of immigration that's coming in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That is a border Democrat telling the White House to keep Title 42 in place. It goes away in seven days, unless there's a ruling otherwise.\par \par \uc2 To Bill Melugin, who is live in Eagle Pass, Texas, with the very latest -- Bill.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Charles, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 The illegal crossings here in Eagle Pass have been going on all day long. You might be able to see behind me right now, or you take a live look at our FOX drone. We had another group just cross moments ago here in Eagle Pass, a group several dozen in size.\par \par \uc2 A DHS source telling me, over the last 24 hours here in Del Rio Sector, there have been more than 1,200 illegal crossings. And we have been witnessing this sort of thing all day long. And this group is small compared to what we saw earlier.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video we shot three or four hours ago. This was a single massive group of well over 200 illegal immigrants crossing in the exact same spot here in Eagle Pass, most of these migrants from Colombia, Venezuela, Cuba, and Peru. This is the sixth day in a row that we have seen a group like this cross directly on the dot at around 11:00 a.m. local time.\par \par \uc2 We will see a cartel scout on the other side of the river. Minutes later, these large groups will come. And Border Patrol says they have been getting hammered by these big groups. They had three of them in a 10-hour span over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 And this wasn't the only one we had today. Take a look at this piece of video. A few hours before that, in the earlier morning hours, we had this group crossing again in the exact same spot, several dozen in size, again, Colombians, Venezuelans, Cubans, and Peruvians, right in front of an overwhelmed Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 That DHS source telling us Del Rio Sector here since October 1 has had nearly 260,000 illegal crossings. Their numbers right now are up 147 percent over the same time last year. Then take a look at this piece of video we shot over the weekend, again, right here in Eagle Pass.\par \par \uc2 This is private property where they're crossing into. And these illegal immigrants are climbing over a fence on this private property. It's got barbed wire on the top of it. And it's right in front of Border Patrol and right in front of the Texas National Guard. There's razor wire laid out on the riverbank. The migrants didn't know where to go, so they just hopped the fence right onto this private property.\par \par \uc2 It's a pecan orchard with the property owner who comes out and watches this half the time. And she's not very happy about it. Last thing we'd like to show you, take a look at this drone video we shot. Border Patrol has started building this brand-new tent city compound here in Eagle Pass ahead of Title 42's repeal next Monday, one week from today.\par \par \uc2 We're told this is supposed to expand holding capacity by about another 500 people or so, as well as speed up processing. I'm told construction is supposed to finish right before Title 42 drops. But multiple Border Patrol sources tell us they have concerns about this, because they are pulling manpower from stations all across Del Rio Sector to come and staff this facility.\par \par \uc2 And they have concerns that, when Title 42 does drop, that means they're going to have less agents out in the field on patrol because they're going to be doing more paperwork and more processing.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, back out here live, you mentioned it off the top. We are one week out from Title 42 dropping, the situation down here already exploding, and DHS says you can count on it getting a whole lot worse if Title 42 does actually drop.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bill, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's get to read on all of this from Lieutenant Chris Olivarez of the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris, the clock is ticking. I'm really intrigued by that tent city going up, the whole 500 people. From what I have read, there have been 1.8 million or so expulsions since Title 42 went into operations. It doesn't seem like that's going to be enough space to be able to handle the deluge that's expected.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 So what you see right there, of course, what Bill Melugin just reported on about that soft-sided facility there, and, also, what it's doing also, Charles, to take into account, is that it's depleting federal resources. So now you have Border Patrol agents that are going to have staff that facility.\par \par \uc2 And all it's going to mean, Charles, is that it's going to encourage these immigrants to make the journey, to continue to come across into the United States, because they're going to be processed and essentially released into the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, even with Title 42 in play, we have seen on a video that's taking place right there, the mass groups that are coming across, especially there in Eagle Pass, Texas. And that's only less than one mile of an area where we're seeing these groups come across every single day.\par \par \uc2 So right now, what we are focusing on as a state is working with our international partners in Mexico, so that they can take a more aggressive stance on their side by surging resources to contain it and deter those surges, those groups that are coming across the border on the Mexican side.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: How confident are you that that will happen?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Well, Charles, ever since Governor Abbott launched or ever since Governor Abbott initiated those agreements with the governors of Mexico, and we also start those enhanced safety commercial vehicle inspections, we saw what took place at the ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 And we saw that it forced Mexico to surge resources at the border. Now, again, what you're seeing right there, obviously, we know that it's not working, or they don't have the resources there in place.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, with our state leadership, within our agency, we are having consistent conversations with Mexican authorities. And I can tell you we're now -- this kind of week, we will have binational operations throughout the Southern border in Del Rio, Laredo, and other areas of interest, where we can focus on these increased crossings that are taking place.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, earlier today, Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas was talking about how the administration is going to deal with all of this. Take a listen, Chris.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Back in September of last year, we started to plan and prepare for the eventual end of the CDC's use of Title 42.\par \par \uc2 And that is, in fact, what we have done. And I am going to go down to McAllen, Texas, tomorrow -- it's my, I think, ninth or 10th visit to the border -- and explain to local officials exactly what our plans and preparations involve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Chris, first off, you're going to be seeing Secretary Mayorkas when he goes to McAllen.\par \par \uc2 What exactly do you want to hear from him?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: You know, well, Charles, we have been hearing the conversations ever since last year, when this crisis took place, about having a plan in place.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's one thing to have a plan on paper, but you have to effect that plan and take an aggressive stance, take proactive measures, and focus on what the issues are. And that's border security.\par \par \uc2 If this was the case, we wouldn't have saw what we saw last year with the Haitians in Del Rio, when we saw 17,000-plus surge the border all at once. And because what we have been able to do as a state, by providing the surging resources along the border with National Guard, we have been able to deter any other caravans from making their way to the border.\par \par \uc2 So a question I would, have and also I think we should be answering is, when are you going to actually put this plan in place and take a stance, a more aggressive stance, in securing the border, protecting the local smaller border communities, providing more resources to Border Patrol, so they can focus on what they are primarily good at?\par \par \uc2 And that's securing the border, going after criminals, going after drug smugglers. But, right now, that's what needs to be action right now, I believe, Charles, from the secretary.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: And I think, as far as we continue moving forward, as a state, we're going to continue having these conversations with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 But, again, we're having to bear this burden right now, because, again, the federal government is not taking the responsibility of what is taking place right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Do you plan to see Secretary Mayorkas tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: I won't be in the valley, Charles. I'm actually in West Texas right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: I'm actually here, going to cover some other elements are taking place here, a different element that we're seeing here in the West Texas area, as far as border security. So I will not be there.\par \par \uc2 But I'm curious to see what's going to take place and what's going to come out of those conversations.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yeah, you and a whole lot of people, including a lot of Democrats, who are actually elected officials down there. Everyone's concerned.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 Appreciate it, Charles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  deal on hold. Hold on a minute, because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  just said something that might change things up again.\par \par \uc2 And Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  tearing into the Supreme Court leak in a sit- down with Attorney General Yoo -- John Yoo -- with attorney John Yoo. So does he have any idea who the leaker might be?\par \par \uc2 John Yoo is back with us again today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , U.S. SUPREME COURT ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: If someone said that one line of one opinion would be leaked by anyone, and you would say, oh, that's impossible. No one would ever do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock losing all of its gains since \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  disclosed his 9 percent stake in that company last month. But did he just tell everyone that the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  deal is far from over?\par \par \uc2 To FOX Business' s Kelly O'Grady with the latest -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, yeah, this is all coming as he's investigating whether spambots do indeed represent only 5 percent of the platform's users. He speculates it could be at least 20 percent of monetizable users. And you're right. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  just affirmed what the market has been speculating. Negotiating a lower price for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is not out of the question.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we look at where \uc2 Twitter\uc2  closed, it's not much of a surprise, right, down 8 percent and under $40 right now. That's nearly $17 below the share price offer and $15 billion below the deal value. And experts, though, are still bullish on the deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM DRAPER, VENTURE CAPITALIST: I think it'll go at a lower price now that he realizes that a lot of these \uc2 Twitter\uc2  people are really bots.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been like the arbiter of who's allowed to say what. And what are they thinking?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Now, all this is a tweet war is brewing between \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  CEO \uc2 Parag Agrawal\uc2  around the company's willingness to provide transparency about the percentage of bots on the platform.\par \par \uc2 A self-proclaimed lame-duck on the precipice of being ousted, Agrawal claiming the company shared the bot estimation methodology with Musk a week ago, and arguing Elon's external audit is impossible.\par \par \uc2 Musk responding -- quote -- "So how do advertisers know what they're getting for their money? This is fundamental to the financial health of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 But, Charles, as this exchange girls more adversarial, many are questioning if Musk will walk. He would face a $1 billion breakup fee and potentially billions in lawsuits, as he waived due diligence. Regardless, though, I mean, the stock price shows just how much leverage he has at this point.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kelly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now switching gears. As the hunt for the Supreme Court leaker continues, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  now warning it has destroyed trust at the High Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: If someone said that one line of one opinion would be leaked by anyone, and you would say, oh, that's impossible, no one would ever do that. There's such a belief in the rule of law, a belief in the court, a belief in what we were doing, that that was verboten.\par \par \uc2 It was beyond anyone's understanding, or at least anyone's imagination, that someone would do that. And look where we are, where now that trust or that belief is gone forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: His former law clerk John Yoo was the one interviewing Justice Thomas when he made those comments.\par \par \uc2 And John joins me now,\par \par \uc2 John, beyond anyone's imagination is what stuck with me, just something that in just was -- it seems impossible. And yet it happened.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: The Supreme Court has been around for 230 years. It has decided many of the most important cases in our history. This is the first time an opinion has ever leaked from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, the justice is quite clear there in the interview. And I think everyone who served on the court or worked there would know this is a direct attack on the independence and integrity of the Supreme Court, unlike any that's ever happened before.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: John, how does Justice Thomas feel about the notion that it's actually been cheered by a vast -- a large portion of the media has said it was worth the leak, and a lot of people have looked right over it?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Well, as, Charles, you said, it was my honor to interview the justice that day.\par \par \uc2 And I think he couldn't have been clearer. The court in order to do its job, to protect all of our individual rights, to make sure the Constitution works properly, has to be a legal institution. And if you have leaks, if it becomes like Congress or the president, it can't do its job anymore.\par \par \uc2 So anyone who wants to win on Roe, pro-life or pro-choice, shouldn't want to leak, because it's sacrificing the authority of the Supreme Court in the long run as a legal institution that protects our constitutional rights, not as just another political body.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: John, were are we? Where does Justice Thomas -- or do you know, where are we now with respect to finding the leaker?\par \par \uc2 YOO: He didn't say anything about that. And as someone who's -- I have served in all three branches of government. I have seen leaks. I have been leaked against.\par \par \uc2 I have very little faith that the leaker will be caught. Most leaks are investigated. They're never really -- the leaker is very rarely caught. So I don't think that's going to be the solution for the court to rebuild that kind of trust that Justice Thomas is saying is imperative for the functioning of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yet it's heartbreaking, because it feels like it would have to have been sanctioned perhaps by one of Justice Thomas' colleagues.\par \par \uc2 YOO: I hope that's not the case. I don't think Justice Thomas believes that.\par \par \uc2 I could easily see it being one of the young law clerks. I was one of those law clerks once myself many years ago. Those might be the kind of people who would say Roe vs. Wade is so important to me, and they might think it's even so important to the country, that it's worth sacrificing the long-term health of the institution of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But I think anyone with a long-term view, anyone who believes in the rule of law in our country and says the law and the Constitution are different than just politics is going to be saddened by this...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 YOO: ... and hope that the leak will be uncovered.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Has this leak, though, galvanized the justices themselves, putting aside their beliefs and interpretation of the Constitution, as an entity, as a critical part of our democratic republic?\par \par \uc2 Has it galvanized them in their roles?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Well, I think you saw from even that clip you played, Charles, that Justice Thomas is not angry. He's sorry. He feels regret, I think, that this -- that things have come to this.\par \par \uc2 And I think he said pretty clearly and I think even Chief \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said pretty clearly that the court is going to continue on with its mission. They're going to do their best to try to do their job. But, of course, this is throwing the court on to the bonfire of our politics right now, and into the midterm elections as an issue.\par \par \uc2 So it's going to be hard for anybody to normally do their job with the kind of pressure and scrutiny that they're coming under. I hope it doesn't change the outcome of however the justices originally voted...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 YOO: ... because that will weaken the court as an institution.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yeah, the notion of trying to intimidate any court, but particularly the Supreme Court, is really scary.\par \par \uc2 John, great work. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That'll do it for me, folks.\par \par \uc2 Make sure you catch me on FOX Business 2:00 p.m. weekdays, "Making Money," particularly in this market environment. Things are pretty topsy-turvy, but I have got your back.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, here comes "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220517ei5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Gunman Kills 10, Injures 3 in Mass Shooting at Supermarket; Interview with Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  of Oklahoma; Interview with Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  of Colorado\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Alexis McAdams, Lucas Tomlinson, Alex Hoff, Charles  Hurt, Mo Elleithee, Guy Benson, Catherine Lucey \par \uc2 9,345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Mass shooting at a supermarket in New York, and abortion emerges as a flash point in the midterm elections, as Pennsylvania prepares for its turn in the primary spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): A march to the steps of the Supreme Court after a leaked draft opinion signals justices are poised to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): We will stand with the American people, stand with innocent life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While a move by Democrats to pass a bill making a right to an abortion national law fails to pass.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Now the debate shifts to the states. This hour, we'll talk with two state leaders, Oklahoma Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 , where abortion is now banned after six weeks. And Colorado's \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , whose state has expanded abortion with virtually no limits.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a nationwide shortage of baby formula has parents and Washington scrambling for answers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Baby is crying. The baby is hungry. We need to address it right now.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): This administration should be looking at everything and anything. We have heard absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask the Sunday panel about the political fallout over formula.\par \par \uc2 And --\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: The stakes are high. Stakes are high.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary now a three-way race as a conservative candidate suddenly surges.\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: If I lose, you lose.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask Kathy Barnette about her last minute surge and scrutiny from fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And we talk to voters in our road trip across the Keystone State in this week's "Road to the Midterms."\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOFF, FOX NEWS REPORTER: Do you think people are going to take what's happened to their wallet and go to the polls and then try to --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hope they do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We will get to the national debate over abortion in just a minute but first, breaking news.\par \par \uc2 A horrific scene of violence in western New York. Authorities saying a white 18-year-old gunman wearing military gear opened fire on a supermarket in a predominantly Black neighborhood in Buffalo, killing 10 people, wounding three others. The suspect livestreaming it all with a helmet camera.\par \par \uc2 Let's go straight to the scene for the latest from Alexis McAdams -- Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, that's right. A horrible scene here in Buffalo, New York, as this community continues to grieve the loss of so many innocent lives. Investigators say this was a racially motivated attack here in Buffalo, New York. Sources are telling us that the gunman had an online manifesto detailing this attack and his plans that he had posted online.\par \par \uc2 Take a look on your screen. This is a photo of the suspected gunman now given to us just last night by police. Buffalo police say they've identified the gunman as 18-year-old Payton Gendron. Just hours after that shooting, the teen was arraigned on first degree murder charges.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say he drove more than three hours from his small rural hometown down state to carry out the deadliest mass shooting in Buffalo's history.\par \par \uc2 Now, the shooting happened around 2:30 Saturday afternoon at this Tops Grocery Store in Buffalo. You can see that crime scene there. First, police say Gendron shot three people in the parking lot and then 10 more inside. Officers say he was armed with an assault rifle and dressed in tactical gear from head to toe.\par \par \uc2 Investigators tell us of the 13 people shot, 11 were Black.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN GARCIA, ERIE COUNTY SHERIFF: This was pure evil. It was straight up racially motivated hate crime from somebody outside of our community, outside of the city of good neighbors as the mayor said, coming into our community and trying to inflict that evil upon us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Tonight, the suspect will be behind bars and will be back in court on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 President Biden did condemn the shooting in a tweet saying, in part, we must do everything we can to end hate-fuelled domestic terrorism -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alexis McAdams, reporting from Buffalo -- Alexis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, the debate over abortion breaking wide open with the leak of a draft majority opinion that indicates the Supreme Court could soon overturn Roe v. Wade. Pro-choice proponents marching to the steps of the high court this weekend. Democrats retooling their midterm message and now the fight shifting to the halls of Congress and statehouses across the country.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll be joined by two governors whose states are dealing with the issue very differently, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  of Colorado and \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn Lucas Tomlinson at the White House with the latest from the Biden administration -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has called the leak, quote, kind of an infidelity that has shredded the trust among the justices. At rallies across the country, protesters voiced their outrage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Crowds descending on the national mall and in major cities across the country, spurred to action as a direct result of the leaked draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers attacking the court's conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE (D-CA): Now, we all know that this is a crisis moment. The radical Supreme Court justices and right wing extremists are making it clear that they won't stop until a nationwide ban is enacted.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Protesters continue to march in front of the homes of conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans and some Democrats want President Biden's attorney general to stop them.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: It is unlawful. It is against the law in this country to protest a sitting judge.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Democratic leaders tried to make abortion a constitutional right. The 51-49 vote falling well short of the 60 needed to clear a procedural hurdle.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Sadly, the Senate failed to stand in defense of a woman's right to make decisions about her own body.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Red states planning to target the procedure. Blue states rushing to protect abortion rights. In six states, including Colorado, there are no abortion restrictions.\par \par \uc2 A baby formula shortage now the latest problem for the White House, on top of record setting inflation that remains close to a 40-year high. Empty shelves sending parents scrambling, in some cases calling friends and family in other states to check availability.\par \par \uc2 In her final news conference as press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  claims the White House saw this latest crisis brewing.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What I will note is that there has been work ongoing on this for months.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Two weeks from now, House Democrats plan to hold a hearing to discuss the baby formula crisis -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Oklahoma City is Republican Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (R), OKLAHOMA: Thanks, Shannon. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. With the appearance from this leaked draft opinion, if it remains and becomes the majority opinion, that Roe may be going down. You've signed a trigger law that would make it a felony to perform abortion punishable up to 10 years in jail and up to $100,000 in fines.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Maya Bass, who performs abortions there in Oklahoma, says this: These laws are being created by people who have no medical expertise. They're not being created with patient safety or medical outcomes in mind. They're created entirely to control bodies.\par \par \uc2 Your response?\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, my response is I represent 4 million Oklahomans. I don't know how much clearer we can be. We believe life begins at conception and we're going to protect life in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 You know, there were 5,000 -- just in Oklahoma alone, 5,000 unborn children that were killed last year. And we don't believe that in Oklahoma. Other states can do things differently, but we're going to stand for life in the state of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. A group called the Frontier, a journalistic group, has looked into what they say some fact checks on what you're saying about how people in Oklahoma feel about abortion. They cite a \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  study. They say that most recent numbers they have from 2014 found that 51 percent of Oklahoman respondents believed abortion should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 So within the last few years, they say your state was pretty evenly split actually on this issue.\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, some of those -- your -- some of those different facts or those newspapers that you're quoting are not what we find with the people in Oklahoma. These bills, the representatives are elected from all over the state of Oklahoma, probably 80 percent to 90 percent passage in our state. So, I totally disagree with those numbers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's talk about another law that you signed much like in you're your neighboring state Texas has done, allows for private causes of action for anyone who attempts, completes or facilitates an abortion. This could be including someone who pays for one after a heartbeat is detected, roughly six weeks.\par \par \uc2 We've got brand new FOX News polling on this and how people feel about at that six-week mark. About 50 percent they say at six weeks abortion should remain legal.\par \par \uc2 Now, your law as I understand, it has no exemptions for rape or incest. And the argument is a victim may not know at six weeks that she is pregnant.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say to a woman who finds herself in that situation, lives in your state and feels like she's got no options?\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, first off, super compassionate about that. I have daughters, cannot even imagine what that would be like and that hardship.\par \par \uc2 But you have to choose -- that is a human being inside the womb. And we're going to -- we're going to do everything we can to protect life and love both the mother and the child. And we don't think that killing one to protect another is the right thing to do either.\par \par \uc2 And our heart is super compassionate about that. We want the churches, we want all the services, the state, the nonprofits to come around with adoption services and that is -- that's super, super hard. And we're going to do everything we can to help them.\par \par \uc2 But aborting that child, we don't think, is the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ok, let's talk some -- through some of those issues because as you know, it's one of the main critiques of the pro-life position. So, in Oklahoma, to look at the stats of what a woman is facing there with a child, 21.3 percent of children live below the poverty line, 71 percent of SNAP participants are in families with children, and Oklahoma ranked 42nd overall in child wellbeing.\par \par \uc2 A "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " opinion headline puts it this way: The GOP roars about abortion. Then they abandon the children.\par \par \uc2 So what is the plan in Oklahoma to help women if you're advising them to carry through on these pregnancies when they are up against some real challenges?\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, I mean, here's the deal. Is the answer to the socialist Democrat left to abort poor kids? I mean, that's just ridiculous to even kind of quote those types of stats.\par \par \uc2 We have a free market in Oklahoma. We believe that God has a special plan for every single life and every single child, and we want everybody to have the same opportunities in Oklahoma. And aborting a child is not the right answer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask you how this may tie in with a recent Supreme Court decision. The McGirt decision from 2020 says, basically, crimes that are committed that take place on a reservation. They will be -- by Native Americans. They're going to face prosecution in a tribal or federal court. It's not going to be something that the state can prosecute.\par \par \uc2 Now there are questions about, do you think there would be doctors who would say, well, we can get around these state laws, we can perform abortions on reservations. People can come to us if that's the care they want to seek.\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, that's something that a lot of Oklahomans, we've heard the rumblings as well.\par \par \uc2 You know, the tribes in Oklahoma are super liberal. They go to Washington, D.C. They talk to President Biden at the White House. They kind of adopt those strategies.\par \par \uc2 So, yeah, we think that there's a possibility that some tribes may try to set up abortion on demand. They think that you can be 1/1,000th tribal member and not have to follow the state law. And so, that's something that we're watching.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you this, Oklahomans will not take -- will not think very well of that if the tribes try to start up abortion clinics, abortion on demand in eastern Oklahoma because the expansion of tribal lands includes the city of Tulsa now which is a million person MSA.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to talk further of this issue of the tribal lands, the separation there. Nearly half of all Oklahoma land, at least big swaths of it, are part of a reservation. You vetoed a bill that would have required the state to recognize tribal convictions in the same way it acts upon convictions from, say, state municipal courts. It had 96 percent support from your legislature.\par \par \uc2 The Inter-Tribal Council of the Five Civilized Tribes said this: It's unfortunate that governor vetoed the public safety bill. We welcome opportunities to collaborate and work together. Our governor continues to be uncooperative and unwilling.\par \par \uc2 So I want to give you a chance to explain why you vetoed that and what's going on there.\par \par \uc2 STITT: Yeah, first off, that wasn't a bill that I requested. That wasn't a bill that the Department of Public safety requested. That was a tribal bill that they were trying to get across the finish line, and it didn't reciprocate.\par \par \uc2 They were wanting the state of Oklahoma to accept all tribal convictions whether they had jurisdiction or not, but it wasn't reciprocated. They weren't willing to take on the state side as well.\par \par \uc2 And so, we're not going to expand tribal jurisdiction in the state of Oklahoma. We want to have an honest conversation. Really the question should be asked, why were the tribes fighting the state of Oklahoma to protect Native victims in the U.S. Supreme Court Castro-Huerta, we just got before -- argued before the Supreme Court? The tribes fought the state from trying to protect and prosecute a bad guy that wasn't even native to protect the Native victims.\par \par \uc2 So again, we're for law and order in eastern Oklahoma. They're trying to confuse the situation. Oklahomans want to protect and we want to be able to prosecute the crimes in eastern Oklahoma and right now, they're telling us we don't have that right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Governor Stitt, we appreciate your time. Thank you so much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 STITT: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, another state, another perspective. Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Six years before Roe v. Wade became the law of the land, Colorado became the first state to decriminalize abortion. And now, it finds itself on the front lines once more.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from Boulder, Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday", Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D), COLORADO: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. This is what you've recently signed into law, the Reproductive Health Equity Act. It states simply, a pregnant individual has a fundamental right to continue their pregnancy and give birth or to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I don't see any structures related to weeks or trimesters. Does that mean in Colorado now there's a right to abortion up to birth?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: What we did here is we preserved essentially the Roe versus Wade protection in Colorado because fundamentally, Shannon, what we believe is this is not about the government making that decision. It's about women making that decision. And to sort of win this battle, it's about the hearts and minds of women. Not the laws of men.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is there a limit on abortion up through nine months in Colorado?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: It's the same as it has been the last several decades. People face gut-wrenching decisions every day, whether it's rape or incest. They're often having to choose between the life of the mother and child.\par \par \uc2 For the government to insert itself in that conversation between a doctor and a woman, a faith leader and a woman, is simply wrong. I think we need to approach this a different way. I think there's a lot of common ground around reducing unwanted pregnancies, empowering women and men with birth control.\par \par \uc2 And really generating a culture of responsibility about what it means to be a parent.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, and I will take that as a yes, that there is an option for abortion up to birth.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about that because when "AP" polls specifically by trimester frameworks, and they asked people, should it be legal or illegal -- at 80 percent, when we get into the third trimester, people say it should usually be illegal.\par \par \uc2 Even "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " noted in an article updated this May, the United States is one of over a dozen countries that allows abortions for any reason beyond 15 weeks of pregnancy. "The Times" goes on to note that includes North Korea and China.\par \par \uc2 Is that the company that Coloradans wants to be in?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: So, this was on the ballot in Colorado just a year and a half ago, overwhelmingly rejected banning third trimester abortions and that's because, you know, people know how painstaking and difficult that is. When you know that, you know, you might be near birth and you find out your baby doesn't have lungs and won't live for more than a few minutes outside of the womb -- I mean, this is a really difficult thing that women face.\par \par \uc2 And the right answer is, of course, to make sure that we trust people to make that decision and people have the freedom to do that. We don't say big governments are going to insert itself and tell them how to handle something that sadly really doesn't have a right answer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We asked simply, do people think that abortion should be legal or illegal? Just straight up question and our latest FOX News polling, 44 percent said legal, 54 percent said illegal. That's the lowest number we've tracked for legal and it's the first time illegal has polled above 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, there in Colorado, conservative leaders say these are the kind of numbers that are their side.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Hunt, director of the Centennial Institute, says: My contention is the trend is with us. We may lose the battles but we will win the war in the state.\par \par \uc2 They're talking about ballot measures for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about your work being undone in coming elections?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Not particularly. It's been on the ballot, as I said, as recently as a year and a half ago. Voters agreed that the government should not interfere with this basic freedom, this basic decision.\par \par \uc2 It really is in the realm of conscience. It's in the realm of how women understand that there's options. And, by the way, that includes adoption.\par \par \uc2 We implemented free preschool in Colorado. We're making daycare more affordable. We want to make sure that people know about these things.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we want to make sure that we can increase access to birth control and -- for both men and women, and really build that culture of responsibility and also, understanding the importance of consent for both men and women to help reduce the number of unwanted pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A federal law bans picketing and parades outside of the homes of judges in the midst of making decisions and intent of influencing them. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has come out now against these pickets and demonstrations at Supreme Court justices' private homes. One of the top Democrats in the Senate, Dick Durbin, has done as well.\par \par \uc2 What's your position on these demonstrations?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, I certainly don't think that they're effective in persuading judges. In fact, it might further alienate them from a particular cause to be that aggressive.\par \par \uc2 I think our judges, the men and women on the Supreme Court, are really the embodiment of our highest and best judges are very thoughtful about how they look at the information. They look at the record. They make a decision.\par \par \uc2 I've read a number of their decisions over time. And I think we want to keep that in the intellectual realm rather than drag them further into the political realm.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the economy which continues to poll as the top concern for Americans across the board, across party lines, backgrounds, ages, all of that. There in Denver, specifically, they have got an inflationary pain that's above the national average which is already pretty tough for folks, the seventh highest among U.S. metro areas.\par \par \uc2 I want to take a look at some of the numbers that you're dealing with out there. Food prices up over 9 percent, utilities up over 17, and housing about 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 What more do you think the administration could be doing to deal with inflation?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Well, here, we're focused on tax relief, fee relief. We cut property taxes by $750 million. Every Coloradan is going to get a $500 rebate check.\par \par \uc2 What I think that Washington can focus on is, of course, fiscal responsibility, monetary policy. In addition to that, I'd like to see them drop some of the tariffs which add an additional inflationary pressure for consumer products across the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: During COVID, you were a bit of an outlier, and that you took heat from the left. They thought you were a little bit too permissive with -- when it came to things like masks and lockdowns and mandates.\par \par \uc2 Do you regret any of the decisions that you made now that we're closing in on a million COVID deaths here in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: You know, we value freedom in Colorado. We never sugarcoated it. We always gave people the best information we could about the importance of wearing a medical grade mask indoors around others. We did an enormous statewide vaccination campaign and the risks are 10 times less if you're fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, again, it's been a difficult challenge everywhere. I think it's tragic that we've lost a million lives to this pandemic. But without being trusted purveyors of information and making that vaccine widely available, we'd be talking about several million people right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Governor, I've got to ask you something totally random. I saw when we were prepping for the show.\par \par \uc2 You said this about the unsolved JonBenet Ramsey case from 1996, a few days ago. You said: I can say one thing, the killer of JonBenet Ramsey should not rest easily because I'm still optimistic that he or she will be apprehended with technology that exists today in five years or in 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that case will ultimately be solved, Governor?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: We've had several great cold case breakthroughs in Colorado, successfully prosecuted with our Colorado Bureau of Investigations and technology is emerging every day that can take existing evidence and analyze it in new ways.\par \par \uc2 I don't think anybody that committed a capital crime, 10, 20, 30, 40 years ago should rest easy because technology is making improvements and makes it likely to be able to apprehend the suspect. And I'm very optimistic on that front.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Governor, on a wide array of topics, always a pleasure speaking with you. Thanks for being here today.\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, both Democrats and Republicans think they've got a shot at Pennsylvania's open Senate seat. Come Tuesday, we could know a lot more.\par \par \uc2 Plus, one candidate's surprise late surge over big spending rivals is catching many folks by surprise. Kathy Barnette joins us live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "FOX News Sunday" is on the road to the midterms. Today in Pennsylvania where it's a three-way race for the Republican nomination for the Senate seat left open by retiring Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here is the latest FOX News poll showing Mehmet Oz, David McCormick and Kathy Barnette in a statistical dead heat. But it was a much different picture in March with Barnette pulling in just in the single digits.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to enthusiasm, the subgroup of voters who say they're extremely interested in this election, Barnette nearly tops both Oz and McCormick who have collectively outspent her by millions. In just a moment, we will speak with Kathy Barnette about her sudden surge.\par \par \uc2 But, first, Alex Hoff takes us on a road trip across the Keystone State where she catches up with voters and campaigns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID STACHE, BRADDOCK, PA, RESIDENT: This town was so prosperous. There were stores and restaurants. This was like Times Square.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Braddock, Pennsylvania, sits just east of Pittsburgh in what was once known as the Steel Valley.\par \par \uc2 STACHE: But once the mills went, I think a lot of sense of pride of the families went, too.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The Edgar Thomson Steel mill is a rare survivor of the industry's 1980s collapse. While still operational, its days as the town's lifeblood have long gone. Directly across the street is what used to be a prominent car dealership.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You had some experience with that, right?\par \par \uc2 STACHE: My dad bought a car there in the mid-'50s.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): It's now the home of Democratic Senate candidate John Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. JOHN FETTERMAN (D-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: We need to keep making (expletive deleted) in this country.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Fetterman is, shall we say, an outsized political figure in Pennsylvania, a winner, perhaps in part, because he didn't look the part. He spent 13 years as Braddock's mayor and then became lieutenant governor.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Pat's or Geno's, those are more tourist...\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Fetterman has so far held a comfortable lead in the primary race even as his opponent, \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , has called him too progressive. A PAC backing Lamb ran a short-lived ad calling Fetterman a Democratic Socialist.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Does that hurt the party as a whole, him using words like that?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It's Conor's story, he can tell it the way he needs to. You know, neither he nor I are socialists, obviously. But I guess, you know, when, you know, you have, you know, trouble in polls and money, you know, you say things that are desperate.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): The voter demographic in Pennsylvania is nearly identical to the United States as a whole, with the looks and leanings of each region differing greatly. So as the candidates put in the miles, so did we.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): These campaigns, a lot of them have been traveling for like 100 days now. So you want to go to the VFWs, get to the diners, and hope that the local officials along the way will endorse you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Who is introducing?\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): We caught up with Republican Senate candidate David McCormick at a campaign stop outside of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: It's going to be close. So thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: A former hedge fund CEO, McCormick is facing a very tight primary race.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Stakes are high. Stakes are high.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: For the majority of this campaign, McCormick has been polling neck and neck with a pretty distinct character, celebrity heart surgeon Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): When you're making the case to those who are still avid supporters of former President Trump, how are you balancing winning them over still without the endorsement?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: He's very popular, and Mehmet Oz isn't very popular. And the reason he's not popular is because his positions don't line up with primary voters.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Road-tripping to the other side of the state, we would discuss this with Dr. Oz. But first, we paid a visit to someone within a group of increasingly coveted voters. Beth Ann Rosica is a suburban mom.\par \par \uc2 BETH ANN ROSICA, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: So I had been liberal Democrat for 34 years.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But that changed during the pandemic as she felt COVID school closures and mask mandates dealt too heavy of a burden on families. Because of that, she says parents' eyes were opened to policy and curriculum issues.\par \par \uc2 ROSICA: If they had just opened the schools, all of us parents would have just gone back to our jobs and our lives and we would have stopped paying attention.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: She says crime and inflation have also become issues weighing heavily on families.\par \par \uc2 ROSICA: I went to the grocery store, you know, I had three little bags of groceries and it was over $100.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The latest \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll puts inflation at the top of Republican voters' concerns, followed by illegal immigration, which many see as contributing to drug smuggling and worsening a glaring opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 A less than two-hour drive west and the contrasts within the commonwealth become more vivid. Lancaster County is home to the country's largest Amish population. They shy away from showing their faces on camera, but in recent years have broken tradition by showing up to the polls, almost always in favor of Republican candidates. And further west where there are a lot more cars to fill up...\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Just working class people, blue collar people.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: ... rising costs overall begin at the fuel pump.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You have to get diesel which is expensive and so you have to make sure that you can afford your food overall (ph).\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Do you think people are going to take what has happened to their wallet and then go to the polls and try to...\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I hope they do.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Among Democrats, the issue of abortion also suddenly re-emerged as a rallying cry.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You can't just sit back and relax and assume that things will continue on the same way that they have.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think it should be a very concerning moment for all Americans and I'm hoping it will bring people out to the polls.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , 45TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Pennsylvania, we're going to have a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: On the Republican side, a different rallying cry.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Dr. Oz!\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  throwing his weight behind Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Thank you for the endorsement.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I'll probably be voting for Dr. Oz, you know, even though I heard that he is a liberal as far as abortion is concerned.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Back in western Pennsylvania, Oz greeted fans and undecided voters, assuring them that he believes life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 OZ: There are kinds of people who are confused because after $30 million of negative ads have run against you, you can confuse people.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Prior to our sit-down interview, I mentioned that I had felt light- headed early in the week and in true form.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Let me feel your pulse.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Despite a career in medicine and TV, Oz says the former president's backing proves he's the right choice.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Said I am smart, tough, I will never let you down. He wrote that to address the folks who are saying what you're saying, which is, you know, Oz is, you know, Trump-endorsed but is he really the right person? Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But in recent weeks, another serious but far less well-funded Republican contender has emerged, former congressional primary candidate Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): People didn't expect this, these other candidates were too busy in-fighting, and that you've now seen this surge.\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: I haven't been vicious. I've been focused, right? And they're mad now that I've ran a far superior campaign. Little old me. They didn't expect it.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): Off the campaign trail in Pennsylvania's eclectic commonwealth of communities, life still churns and politicians have made promises before.\par \par \uc2 STACHE: There's still manufacturing. It's never going to go back to the '50s and '60s. If it did, that would be one of the greatest miracles of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Alex Hoff, reporting from Pennsylvania. Joining us now, Kathy Barnette, one of the Republican Senate candidates.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Barnette, welcome to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Thank you so much for having me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we showed in the polling, especially among those who are most interested in this race, you are surging at just the right time on a shoestring budget compared to the other two top contenders. But former President Trump has weighed in on this race. He has plenty of good things to say about you but also offers this: "Kathy Barnette will never be able to win the general election against the radical left Democrats. She has many things in her past which have not been properly explained or vetted."\par \par \uc2 And here's what Dr. Oz said to me about that when I asked him Thursday on "Special Report."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OZ: She has raised questions in her own candidacy that she has refused to answer. This is a candidate who lost by 20 points when she ran for Congress 18 months ago. So she's not going to do well in the general election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So I want to give you a chance to answer some of these questions and your critics. Let's start with your military record. You have now released a couple of forms related to your service. Your website, your bio says you served for 10 years. Critics say those forms do not prove that out. Your response?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: That is not true. Those forms do prove just that. I have never -- I have not embellished on my record, on my time in the military. And I'm very grateful that I had the wonderful opportunity to serve our country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Where were you an adjunct college professor?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes, you know, and in fact, let me just make this really short for you and to your viewers. You can go to my website at barnetteforsenate.com, we've placed right there on the front page, you will see my family and around my family is a wheel, and we place there the things you want to know about Kathy Barnette. You know, I've been running this race now for about 13 months. And if you listen to the mainstream media, you would think I crawled from under a rock yesterday. I did not. We've been out here for 13 months. We started off doing about 900 miles a week, then bumped it up to 1,000. Now, these last couple of months, we've been traveling over 1,500 miles every single week. So I have been out here stomping the ground, getting to understand what the issues are in Pennsylvania. And the media was derelict in their duty.\par \par \uc2 I've been in -- I've been in a statistical tie for first place not just one week but for about four weeks. So I've been out here doing the hard work. The numbers have been showing it. No one paid attention to me. They were too obsessed with the two men in this race and didn't bother to take a look at what it is that we were doing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So now you're getting that attention because you have surged, for you, at the right time. So is the information about your work as a professor on the website then now?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: It is.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. When did you move to Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes, we've been -- we've lived here for nine years, going into our 10th year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so now let's...\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I've raised two of my babies here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mm-hmm, it's a great state. As I told you during the commercial, I married into a Pennsylvania family and it's a great place to have a family. So let me ask you about...\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I love it. I love Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... social media posts because you have said some of these are taken out of context. I want to give you a chance to respond to them, then. A tweet in 2014...\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: ... you said "if you love freedom, Islam must not be allowed to thrive under any condition." You've got tweets about former President Obama, one of them in 2016 says "Obama is a Muslim doing Muslim-like things." He has said that he is not, he is a practicing christian. So any context then for those particular tweets?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes, let me just say in almost all of those tweets, you know, especially when you look at the time frame we were living in at that particular time, we had the Obama administration bringing in a lot of Syrian refugees at that time. I was watching the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  director, \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , testify, I believe, in front of the Senate, saying we can vet until the cows come home and we won't know who these people are. And yet, Obama at the time was telling the American people, don't worry about it, we're going to vet everyone. And what we were watching as Americans was very unnerving. And I'm sure the people will remember that. We were looking at the Pulse nightclub shooting. We were watching people take vehicles, weaponize them, and run people down in the street. We were looking at the San Bernadino shooting. We were looking at some very unnerving things that as Americans we thought would never happen here.\par \par \uc2 And at that time, I was hosting a show called "Truth Exchange," and I would have all kind of ideas, and was leaning into helping the public begin to have those conversations. And so those are some of the -- that's the context around a lot of those tweets. The overwhelming majority of the tweets that are now being presented are not even full thoughts. They're not even full sentences. And yet, people take it and they begin to build their own narrative around it. So I can't provide a lot of context because, again, it's almost 10 years ago. That's how far they have to go back to try to find anything on me.\par \par \uc2 What I can say -- although I can't provide a lot of context to that because it's a half thought and everything is not there for me to be able to speak to it. What I can say is that I love my country. And I have always loved my country. And I have always been willing not to blink in the face of difficult discussions. We're facing a lot of difficult discussions. And on those 1,500 miles traveling all around the commonwealth, people feel squeezed and no one is asking me about my tweets. What they're talking about are the illegal immigration, we're on track for 4 million unlawfully present people this year alone. They're talking about inflation. I had someone call me the other day saying they're driving over an hour because they heard a rumor about baby formula milk.\par \par \uc2 We have some very real issues and that is what -- that is the reason why I'm surging and doing so well is because throughout this entire time I've kept the main thing the main thing and that's Pennsylvanians.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. You know that if you win this primary, your Democratic opponent, Mr. Fetterman, is going to bring up these things and more. So they haven't come to light until now because you have done so well and your opponents feel you are a threat. How will you answer him because he will not, you know, pull punches on these many things?\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Yes, yes, you know, listen, you know, people ask me all the time, are you afraid to run against Fetterman? And my response is generally as long as I'm not running while Black, and many of your listeners will know that he is -- he not only accused but he did hold a Black man up with a shotgun. You know, so Democrats have their own issues and I truly believe that as we continue to move throughout this year, the American people, and specifically Pennsylvanians, are going to again begin to prioritize their -- their kitchen table issues above a lot of the other noise that we're hearing right now.\par \par \uc2 People are unnerved. People are just afraid. They -- I think most people know something has gone fundamentally wrong with how our nation is being governed. And while my two primary opponents were spending $60 million attacking themselves, refusing to go to the base, we had seven debates and Mehmet Oz and David McCormick only showed up to two. We had a number of forums and I can only recall them showing up to two. And then while they were attacking one another on television, I was out here with the people.\par \par \uc2 We spent less than $2 million and they're mad. They're mad because I didn't ask for permission to be in this space. I just walked in because this is my country. And our country is in trouble. And I don't believe we have anymore room to elect warm bodies with an R next to their name and say hey, check, Republicans win. We don't win. It's not working out for us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. And as to that incident you refer to with Mr. Fetterman, I confess not familiar with that allegation. I will dig into that and make sure we get some information out.\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: It is there. It is there.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Ms. Barnette, thank you for your time today. We will follow the returns and we'll see what happens for you on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the Biden administration tries to dodge the political fallout from rising gas prices. Now a nationwide shortage of baby formula impacting parents and the political landscape. We'll bring our Sunday group to discuss what's on voters' minds as they head to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: Last year Attorney General Garland's Justice Department was quick to treat the concerned parents of America like potential domestic terrorists. But curiously, I haven't heard any announcement about how the DoJ may handle these intimidation tactics aimed directly at federal judges.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , OUTGOING WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I know that there's an outrage right now, I guess, about protests that have been peaceful to date. And we certainly continue to encourage that outside of judges' homes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The debate over demonstrations at conservative Supreme Court justices' homes, one of the political fault lines from the leak of a draft opinion that indicates the potential overturning of Roe v. Wade. It is time now for our Sunday group. FOX News contributor Charlie Hurt, former DNC Communications Director Mo Elleithee, FOX News contributor Guy Benson, host of "The Guy Benson Show," and Catherine Lucey, who covers the White House for The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. All right, Charlie, what do you make of these protests? And we've had a number of, you know, prominent Democrats come out, including Dick Durbin this week, to say, don't do it.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, well, Dick Durbin's comments were a little late and maybe -- actually, his were fairly forceful. But unfortunately out of the White House they were late or actually kind of nonexistent which is -- reveals a terrible double standard. But the real problem is that it -- of course, it is actually illegal. You're not allowed to do this sort of thing. And so where you have a lot of concern about our institutions, which is a good thing to have, this sort of blithe disregard for a real attack on an entire branch of government trying to work through a very complicated issue is not -- is not good for the country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we got the text of the law, just edited to the relevant parts. It says "whoever with the intent to interfering with, obstructing, or impeding the administration of justice, or with the intent of influencing any judge in the discharge of his duty, pickets or parades in or near a building or residence occupied, or used by such judge, shall be fined under this title or imprisoned not more than one year or both."\par \par \uc2 So, Mo, why isn't the Justice Department getting involved? They get involved in a lot of things but don't seem too interested this time.\par \par \uc2 MO ELLEITHEE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, look, I'm not a lawyer, I'm not a prosecutor. So I don't -- I'm not going to pretend to know why the DoJ is or isn't doing anything. I will say politically I'm against these protests outside of their homes for a number of reasons. One, I just think it's wrong. Number two, I just think it is not going to be successful, right? They're not going to sway any justice. But number three, we spent the last couple of minutes talking about the protests outside of justices' homes instead of talking about the content, instead of talking about the real threat to Roe v. Wade. And if -- and as a supporter of having access to abortion, of Roe v. Wade, I'd much rather be having this conversation than whether or not we are protesting -- where we are protesting.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, so Senate Democrats this week tried to pass something that they kept calling it -- it kept being entitled the codification of Roe v. Wade, but many people think it was far beyond that. The World News Group has a piece entitled "More Radical than Roe," said the proposed law was much more extreme that: abortion on demand for any reason and at any point up until birth. For the first time in our nation's history, Schumer's law would have done away with religious liberty protections for medical providers who object to performing abortions on religious or conscience grounds. It would have also allowed for sex-selective abortions. So even pro-choice Republicans, and they were joined by Joe Manchin, said they cannot go that far.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right. I think that the Senate bill was actually appalling. It goes far beyond anything that really approaches just codifying Roe. A few senators were asked questions about that on the Democratic side. And Mazie Hirono of Hawaii said, let's not get bogged down in the details. Well, the details actually matter quite a lot and the details were disgusting. They're actually quite similar to the bill just recently signed into law by Governor Polis, your guest earlier in the program. He was sort of dancing around your question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What it actually is.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right. The answer was yes, unfortunately. But he didn't really want to spell that out. He did say that this issue, abortion, should come down to the hearts and minds of women, not the laws of men. Well, he's a male governor who signed a bill into law that, according to a new \uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll, flies in the face of 65 percent of what American women believe on this question. So I think that you saw, in these two governors on the program today, very different approaches that represent in each case a pretty small segment of the American electorate and sort of public opinion, whereas many other people, there's a messy middle here on a lot of these issues, that doesn't really seem to be getting a lot of attention from some of the loudest voices.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Catherine, how does the White House then assess this? The president is upside-down on almost every issue that we poll on. The Democrats really need something to rally around for the midterms. Do they feel like this is it?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE LUCEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, certainly Democrats do think that this is potentially a galvanizing issue. They think this motivates women voters, some of those key groups that they think are important, you know, in some of these tough districts in the fall. You know, suburban women. But they are now hoping that this will help, you know, bring people out, you know, get people energized in an election that is looking very, very challenging for Democrats. The president's numbers are really low. People are frustrated by inflation. They're frustrated now about, you know, the shortages of baby formula, for example, which is a real issue for parents.\par \par \uc2 The question is how much this does. You know, what I've heard from a lot of analysts and from pollsters is that Democrats still need an economic message. They need an economic message. They need to be able to talk to people about inflation. They need to be able to show that they're doing things on this. But people do think that this could help on the margins.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and to the economy, seven in 10 Americans told \uc2 Pew Research\uc2 , in a poll released this week, inflation is, quote, "a very big problem for the country today." Mo, as we talked about, I mean, the president is upside-down. Democrats are upside-down on these things. And people vote with their wallets. Do you think it's salvageable, the midterms, this issue with the economy?\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: This is actually a fairly messy election season, I think. And there's so many unknowns that are still coming in, right? How does Roe v. Wade play into this? How does any number of issues play into this? How does yesterday's tragic shooting in Buffalo and the issues that come from that change sort of our collective thinking? Having said all that, yes, inflation is a big problem. It is going to be the number one issue. And that's why you see the president out there over the past week being much more assertive and aggressive in talking about the issue and trying to, number one, say here's what Democrats and my administration are going to try to do; and number two, increasingly drawing a contrast with Republicans, increasingly drawing a contrast with how Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 's tax plan will negatively impact people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Even though no Republicans have embraced it but him.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Right. But you're seeing them start to try to draw a contrast between the two sides. And look, they're even beginning to see how they can try to connect the economic argument to issues like Roe v. Wade. You brought up some great statistics in your interview with the governor of Oklahoma a little while ago. He didn't seem -- he seemed to be caught a little flatfooted by some of those, economic impact of some issues. So I think you're going to see Democrats lean into it a lot more, but they're -- they're facing some serious headwind in...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes?\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: Well, also, I think one of the challenges for the White House, right, is they're trying to, as Mo said, create a choice for voters, say, look, it's us versus the Republicans, here's what Republicans would do. Republicans will raise taxes. But it's typically very, very hard to shift a midterm election from what is a referendum on the party in power.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, especially when we've got this issue of moms who can't find formula, Charles.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Yes. And also, you know, with inflation in particular, you know, the White House also denied that that was a problem for a very long time and tried to -- tried to pretend it away. And the fact that they are grasping at something like Roe v. Wade, I think, also underscores just how desperate they are for an issue that, as you point out, galvanizes things.\par \par \uc2 But go back to something that Mo said I think is very interesting talking about, you know, how you would rather be talking about Roe v. Wade. Well, you know, I'm on the opposite side of this issue. I would rather be talking about the issue as well. And that's really what the beauty of -- if this does, in fact, get overturned, it puts this out for a debate so that...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Back to the states.\par \par \uc2 HURT: ... people -- people's elected legislatures can have these arguments and suss out these differences and arrive at things that -- arrive at decisions at laws that make sense to people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Guy, final word to you.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I would just say, the president gave a speech last week. A lot of blame for inflation. Not a lot of proactive plans. But he did say his policies are helping, not hurting. That was his assertion. Polls show the American people do not believe that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's not their perception of it, anyway. OK, panel, thank you very much. We will see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Big primary challenges coming up this week in North Carolina and Pennsylvania. We will be tracking all of those around the clock. That is it for us today. I'm Shannon Bream. Keep it here at FOX News Channel for the latest on all those primary nights coming up. You can join me weeknights, 12:00 Eastern, 9pm on the West Coast for "FOX News at Night." We've got Supreme Court coverage with opinions coming in the morning. Have a great week, what's left. We'll see you next "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220515ei5f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Kathy Barnette Exposed; Biden Runs Away From Reality On Economic Failures\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Lawrence Jones, Newt Gingrich \par \uc2 8,199 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, the very latest on our economy that is now completely imploding thanks to your president Joey.\par \par \uc2 We also have an update on \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 's crack pipe lie. Remember those crack kits that they had, and they swore up and down that they had no crack pipes in them? Yeah, we've got the evidence to prove it was a total lie.\par \par \uc2 And a shocking report about baby formula that the Biden administration, oh, they're stockpiling it for illegal immigrants in the middle of what is a massive nationwide shortage.\par \par \uc2 Also, Lawrence Jones, LJ, will be here with an exclusive investigation. He actually got planes landing in the dark of night and followed where they were taken to illegal immigrants. And, by the way, nearly got arrested. We'll tell you that whole story and remember you're paying for those flights.\par \par \uc2 But, first, tonight, an important update on Republican Pennsylvania Senate candidate Kathy Barnette. Now, in the last week, out of nowhere, she surged into second place in the polls in Pennsylvania's very crowded Republican Senate primary. Currently, the candidate I endorsed and President Trump endorsed, Dr. Mehmet Oz, is holding on to the number one spot.\par \par \uc2 But it's easy to see why Kathy Barnette is picking up steam and connecting with voters. We've had her on this program in the past. I've had her on my radio show. She's a very impressive person, an incredible life story.\par \par \uc2 But because she was polling so low just until the last week or so, unfortunately, Kathy Barnette has not gone any kind of vigorous, rigorous vetting. That is now proving to be a massive problem in this race. Here's why: in 2020, she was not a serious candidate in her congressional race. She lost to a pretty weak Democrat by 19 points. And now, we are uncovering many troubling comments and tweets that would very likely cause her to lose badly in a general election.\par \par \uc2 This is a very important state. There are so many questions she has never answered and many tweets that likely render her unelectable in Pennsylvania. Now, this is a must-hold Senate seat if Republicans have any chance of taking over the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 This is a tipping point inflection moment for this country. Every one of these races matter. Let me be clear, just because I and many others find her unelectable doesn't mean I want Kathy Barnette cancelled. That's what the left does. Most of these problematic comments and tweets are fairly recent. Meaning, it wasn't done when she was in her 20s or 30s.\par \par \uc2 These are controversial, extremely controversial comments said over and over and over again. So I've got to conclude these are her heartfelt beliefs. Republican voters in Pennsylvania and around the country, you need to know the cold hard truth.\par \par \uc2 Let me put it very simply here. I don't see any scenario where Kathy Barnette can win a general election in Pennsylvania. Let me get specific why: first, in recent years, she engaged repeatedly in some pretty disturbing very controversial rhetoric surrounding gays and lesbians and Muslims, and she makes no distinction for example between Islam and radical Islam, to actually cancel a whole religion. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , she asked her followers to pray for her because, quote, she was about to board the plane 