t how this was a complete charade from the get-go.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are we ever going to get this Durham report? Do we ever get it? We should have had it before the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: He's being thorough but I think we are going to get it. Let's hope it sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Congressman, thank you. Chris, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. We'll take a quick break. We'll back. More HANNITY straight ahead, as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Unfortunately, that is all the time we have left this evening. As always, we thank you for being with us, thank you for making the show possible.\par \par \uc2 We hope you set your DVR so you never, ever, ever miss an episode of HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And don't forget, for all the latest news and information you could ever want, need or trust, it's foxnews.com. We have information about me at hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled, the news continues. Laura is next, "The Ingraham Angle." Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Continues To Blame "MAGA" GOP For Inflation; White House Economic Adviser On Rising Gas, Food Costs; Dem Strategist Slams Students Loan Forgiveness; Rep. \uc2 John Katko\uc2  (R-NY), Is Being Interviewed About Going After Chinese Chemical Companies To Stop Producing The Fentanyl And Precursor Chemicals; Fentanyl Fuels Deadliest Year Ever For Overdoses; DEA: Fentanyl Flowing From China To Mexico TO U.S.; Kirby: Will Let Ukraine Define What Victory Is; Over 40% Of Baby Formula Out Of Stock In The US; Pennsylvania Primary Race Less Than One Week Away; Senator Scott Pushes Back On Yellen's Roe Comments; White House Holding Press Briefing; White House Pressed On Baby Formula Shortage; New Queen Health Concerns After Missed Appearances\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Gillian Turner, Martha MacCallum, Marc Thiessen,  Mark Meredith, Robert Wolf \par \uc2 10,164 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Everybody's on, counting on Powell to see if he can engineer a soft landing for inflation to the economy without crashing the whole thing into the ground, into the ground. Fingers crossed, he could do it.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Better him than us, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Exactly. Thanks for joining us, Gillian. I'll see you tomorrow. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You got it. I'm Gillian Turner. Be sure to set your DVR so you don't miss a minute of AMERICA REPORTS. THE STORY WITH MARTHA MACCALLUM begins right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John and Gillian. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News headquarters in New York. Right now, on THE STORY. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  says that President Biden is having, quote, a good time with his new line of attack against Republicans on inflation. This phrase.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me tell you about this ultra-MAGA agenda. The ultra-MAGA agenda. Under my predecessor, the great MAGA king, the deficit increased every single year he was president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  editorial board, though, writes today about the president's "magical thinking on inflation," as the Post says, "It is wishful thinking that inflation is going to come down by much by election day to show voters he's on top of the problem. Mr. Biden needs to do more than blame someone else's high prices." And with that, we bring in White House Economic Adviser, Jared Bernstein, who joins me now. Jared, thank you very much. It's good to have you with us today. So, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  coming after the president calling it magical thinking in terms of his approach to inflation, what do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: Look, the president has called easing inflationary pressures, fighting this inflationary problem on behalf of household budgets, facing real challenges from these elevated prices, his top domestic priority. And as a member of his economics team, if it's his top priority, it's my top priority. And we are working far from magical thinking or whatever any of those comments, were trying to suggest.\par \par \uc2 We're working aggressively in five different areas to ease inflationary pressures. Those include: reducing energy costs, reducing transportation and logistics snarl ups in the supply chain, reducing kitchen table costs, reducing the budget deficit, the president was stating facts there a second ago, and increasing labor supply. Each one of those are our agenda items that we are focused on with laser like intensity, trying to deliver some easing of pressures to household budgets.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's talk about the White House headline. I mean, I'm sorry, the Wall Street Journal headline says that inflation stays in the heights. The combination of blowout spending and easy money is punishing workers. And we had a $1.9 trillion COVID Relief Bill. And some say, that when you look at the deficit issue, what are the reasons that would naturally come back when you start to back off some of that COVID spending? And that, that is actually what's happening? It's not sort of, you know, a big achievement. It's just sort of the natural course of things once you back off from showering money across the country.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK, so two points. First of all, it talks about, you know, easy money, or whatever it is, you quoted, monetary and fiscal policy. In fact, inflation is highly elevated in every advanced country across the globe. Last seen \uc2 European Union\uc2  inflation --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they all get, a lot of them did the same thing. They had very loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: They did not do the same thing. There were, there was definitely fiscal and monetary policy, but they were all different. But one thing that was the same across every one of those economies was the pandemic. When the President spoke yesterday, I mean, earlier this week about this, he called the pandemic, the first cause of these inflationary pressures; second, on the deficit. There is no question that the deficit is expected to come down this year $1.5 trillion, and it is an historical fact that that is the largest deficit reduction on record.\par \par \uc2 Sure, some of that comes from the fiscal dynamics you described. But a lot of it comes from the underlying strong economy and the revenues that that's generating, revenues are coming in faster than expected, because we have an unemployment rate that's 3.6 percent, 8.3 million jobs created, strong household balance sheets, strong small business job creation, there are a lot of tailwinds in this economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, those are good things. You know, everybody, no matter what side of the fence you may be on, wants to see the economy improve. Everyone wants to see inflation come down. We're just looking at all these different measures that show and even people who are, you know, like \uc2 Steven Rattner\uc2 , for example, has talked about how there was just too much fiscal stimulus and that that's why we have inflation. Now, you've got the Fed, trying to figure out how to get a soft landing out of this, and likely another big hike coming in, in July. Do you think we're going to get another big hike in July? And do you think that that we will get a soft landing?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: We don't talk about Fed policy on a granular basis like that. We're really very much dedicated to an independent federal reserve. I will state and underscoring reporting you just did in that the confirmation of the president's nominees to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is super important. This is the first and foremost institution fighting inflation and the fact that we now have a Federal Reserve with Dr. Lisa Cook, the first black woman ever to be on a \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2  is, I think, a key achievement of Biden- omics trying to make sure that this extremely important global economic institution looks a little bit more like some of the people that it's representing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How does that -- let me ask you, I'm sure she's excellent and well qualified, but how does the fact that it's a diverse pick mean that the economy is going to improve? Why is that relevant?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Totally fair question. The reason it's relevant is because what you need on the Federal Reserve -- but remember, the these are people who, who are pulling some of the most influential levers in the economy. Now, anytime you're doing that, you have to ask yourself, on whose behalf are you pulling those levers? If you're simply focused on the top reaches of the corporate sector, if you're simply focused on the stock market, and you're not paying enough attention to communities that have long been left behind in this economy, then I would argue that you have a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that's not as representative it needs to be. And that's what President Biden has tried to fit here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, you know, people would argue with that that it doesn't matter what color you are to care about communities at the upper and lower reaches of the economy. But let me --\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: President Biden would explain to you that it's extremely important to have a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that looks like and represents all of America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like I said, if she's the most qualified, then absolutely 100 percent. That's who you want the job regardless of her skin color, either way. So, I want to play something for you, because I think this is sort of potent. And we hear a lot about bringing down inflation by sort of giving people you know, cutting the cost of childcare, cutting the cost of other things, to put more money in people's pockets.\par \par \uc2 But I want you to listen to this because I think a lot of people think there's there are other ways to put money in people's pockets and to grow the economy at the same time. This is Paul Begala the other night, I believe this was Friday night, and he says Democrats are getting it wrong. And I'd love to give you an answer to respond, a question to respond.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL BEGALA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: We Democrats have a lab, two labs, actually, secret labs: one in Berkeley, and one in Brooklyn, where we come up with ideas to completely piss off the working class, and it's working wonderfully.\par \par \uc2 What I'd much rather see Democrats do is go back to their roots, which is earn it. We're the party that created the G.I. Bill, nobody called that free college because it wasn't. The guys who got the G.I. Bill earned it. Why don't we have a system where we say, you want to get out of your college debt? Serve your country: Marine Corps, Peace Corps, America, not everybody can carry a rifle, but you can you can measure a kid, and use give two years of service, then you will have earned that and expand it. So, we have community college, job training. We, we need more mechanics, not MBAs. And that's where the Democrats focus ought to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he's saying Democrats are going about it wrong, that people don't want handouts. They want to be in a system where they're earning it. And with regard to the student loan program, in particular, which the president has said he'd like to take away some of that debt, forgiven.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yes, let's talk about that. It's really great question. Let's unpack that. And it takes a few seconds to do so. So, give me a chance here. Earn it. I really liked that commentary. I think that makes a lot of sense. And I also think it's absolutely consistent with, with Biden-omics.\par \par \uc2 Remember, I've worked for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for a long time, at the heart of his model is a labor market that is more welcoming, more inviting, with more chances for occupational upgrading that is getting not just a job, but a better job. And not just for folks at the top of the scale.\par \par \uc2 But for folks who've been long left behind. So, we have an unemployment rate that is just a tick above where it was pre-pandemic. We have more job openings than we've ever seen in this country. The president has overseen 8.3 million in job creation. 545,000 of those jobs have been in the manufacturing sector, again, 8.3 million people back to work, that is America, earning it; and Paul Begala should certainly, I think recognize that if he's talking about that goal. So, I think we're doing very much what he's recommending.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, he's talking about benefits, earning benefits and not just getting a handout from the government.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I'm talking about earning paychecks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, it's separate from the job issue. It's about benefits and additional entitlements and whether or not they should be earned. But thank you very much, Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We certainly hope that the things that you're doing work because the whole country wants to see lower inflation and full employment and all of those good things. So, thank you, Sir, for coming in. I do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: You're welcome, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. So, let's bring in Marc Thiessen, American Enterprise Institute Scholar, and Fox News Contributor. Marc, good to have you with us, as always.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your thoughts on how the White House is approaching, alleviating or trying to take a little bit of the brakes off this inflation surge?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, first of all, I thought that interview was a dumpster fire. I mean, the, the Biden-omics, he particularly used the phrase Biden-omics. I wouldn't want to be coining Biden-omics right now but it's what Biden omics is for most people, the highest inflation in four decades, the highest gas prices on record, the worst labor shortage and supply chain crisis, and we can't find baby formula. That's Biden-omics for the, for the average American.\par \par \uc2 And second of all, this talk about the deficit, they're constantly talking about the deficit. I'm sorry, Americans right now with all the prices problems we're having, they don't care about the federal budget deficit, they care about their personal budget deficit. They care about the deficit in their paychecks because 8.5 percent inflation is eating, is outpacing any wage gains.\par \par \uc2 They care about the deficit and their 401(k)s and retirement plans. They care about the deficit and their gas tanks because they can't afford to fuel up already. They care about the deficit of baby formula on the store shelves. So, focusing on the deficit right now, they're just searching for something positive to say in the, in the numbers looking for the silver cloud in the -- the silver lining in the dark cloud, but all Americans can see it's the dark cloud,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maybe a silver cloud. You know, this ultra --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It'd be nice if we had a silver cloud.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This ultra -- exactly, this ulta-MAGA phrase is getting a lot of attention here. Here's \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  talking about the thinking behind it, here she is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: People have asked, where did he come up with ultra-MAGA? Where did he come up with, what did he call it President Trump? Big, big MAGA, I don't know some -- the king of MAGA? This is all, he is he is having a good time being out drawing the contrast. And I think you will see many, much more of that and, and that I think will be helpful in sharping for the public what the choices are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your thoughts on that Marc?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, so the ultra-MAGA agenda misunderstands the 2020 election, which is that Americans didn't vote against the Trump agenda. They voted against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They love the Trump agenda. In the middle of the pandemic in 2020 Gallup poll, 56 percent of Americans a record said that they were better off now than they were four years ago. That never been that high. Right now, how many -- when you ask how many people say they're better off now than they were before Biden came into office? It's 17 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, people look back at the ultra-MAGA agenda and say, people were at work, there was stuff on the store shelves, the border was secure. The economy was humming and growing. And oh, by the way, people around the world -- our enemies around the world respected us; that they think that mega agenda was pretty good. They just didn't want to re-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That's a big difference.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marc, how long do you think it will take the former president to turn around the ultra-MAGA King and say, I love that title. I'm crazy about it.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Oh, no. Absolutely, he'll love it. He'll embrace it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It won't take long enough. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But also, it makes it makes it by focusing on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you're about to lose a historic election, you're going to have historic losses. If you make it a referendum on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it just becomes not just a defeat for you, but a victory for him. So, you know, you're going to lose, why would you turn it into a referendum on Trump and strengthen him going into 2024? It's just political incompetence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great point, Marc. Always good to see you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marc Thiessen. So, Homeland Security Committee ranking member \uc2 John Katko\uc2 , and Pentagon Spokesperson John Kirby, and a debate that you don't want to miss all of that coming up after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE MILGRAM, ADMINISTRATOR, UNITED STATES DRUG ENFORCEMENT ADMINISTRATION: China is to blame.\par \par \uc2 Chinese chemical companies are the largest producer right now of the precursor chemicals that are being used to make fentanyl. There's a relationship between the Chinese chemical companies and the criminal cartels in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice over): Well, we've been talking about that for a long, long time around here as the United States loses more than 100,000 Americans to drug overdoses.\par \par \uc2 The DEA, it wants China to do more to curb the flow of fentanyl by cracking down on the chemical companies, warning drug cartels will stop at nothing to get the potent synthetic opioid 80 to 100 times stronger than morphine, across this border and into the United States.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2  of New York joins me now. He serves as ranking member on the House Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, good to have you here today. Obviously, that is a tremendous loss, you know, we talk about losing a million people to COVID, we have also lost a much smaller number, but the biggest number ever over 100,000 people to overdose in this country.\par \par \uc2 Is this the way to stop it by going after Chinese chemical companies?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOHN KATKO\uc2  (R-NY): Well, that's one of the things we need to do, Martha, but the Chinese chemical companies that have been producing the fentanyl and precursor chemicals for years.\par \par \uc2 We've cracked on them with -- when they were sending it through the mail. And so, now, they've got a perfect surrogate in the Mexican cartels. And the Mexican cartels are so flush with cash from what's going on with the human trafficking. They're making billions of dollars a month.\par \par \uc2 They're plowing that into their infrastructure and the infrastructure includes producing synthetic drugs and they're perfecting fentanyl. So, I'm -- my fear is that yes, we should crack down on China, of course.\par \par \uc2 But my fear is that the Mexican cartels are developing the ability to do it without the Chinese help anymore. And that's what's really concerning moving forward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, obviously, we see what's going on at the border. And we see the gotaways, and people that we have no idea who came in, or where they went when they got here, people on the terror watch list also coming across the southern border.\par \par \uc2 You know, why? I guess, how is it that we're allowing that to happen with all of the resources that we have?\par \par \uc2 KATKO: It is -- it is a stunning dereliction of duty by the president and this administration. That's coming from the mouth of me, who is one of the most bipartisan members in all the Congress.\par \par \uc2 With the border is wide open, with a number of people flying across this border with record numbers. It's allowing the cartels to make so much money, like I said, but it's also creating the perfect diversion. So, all the people are come -- all the agents are coming off the border to deal with the individuals coming in, in an influx, and that's before Title 42 is revolt which it's going to get much worse.\par \par \uc2 And then, they just walked to drugs and around the other way. And it shows, as you noted for the first time in our nation's\par \par \uc2 In an influx, and that's before Title 42 is revolt, which is going to get much worse. And then they just walked to drugs and around the other way. And it shows, as you noted, for the first time in our nation's history, more than 100,000 people have died of drug overdoses?\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake about it, those drugs are coming across our southern border, absolutely. And those are the things that are killing our kids. And until we understand the connection between the two, it's going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 And the number one killer of people between the ages of 18 to 45 years old right now is drug overdoses.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: That's pretty pathetic.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's incredible. It's bigger than gun violence, it's bigger than car crashes.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- death by overdose. And if you are in a family, or have friends who knows someone, who has taken just the tiniest bit of this poisonous drug, you understand the devastation that comes with that.\par \par \uc2 John Katko\uc2 , thank you very much, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 KATKO: Always.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, Joining me on set is us Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby. John, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We really appreciate you coming to New York. And it's always good to -- we watch your briefings very closely, as you know, here, and it's good to have you anyhow, so, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just a quick segue here on that story. You know, there's some people who look at what's going on in Ukraine and the amount of resources that are going there.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they say, so, how is it that we're so concerned about their border, and not our southern border, when we have all of these -- this loss of life?\par \par \uc2 Kirby: Now, look, I can tell you, President Biden takes border security very seriously. And he understands the importance of proper immigration policies and procedures in place to make the -- to make the border work the way it's supposed to work in terms of our own national security.\par \par \uc2 And we have about 2,500 troops, mostly National Guardsmen are down there right now, to help take the burden off the Border Patrol individuals so that they can do the kinds of actual work to monitor the migrant activities as best they can. So, we're helping them with transportation with communications, and logistical support.\par \par \uc2 And that's a mission that we continue to have the discussion with the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  about, we continue to believe right now. It's a valid mission. And so, we'll continue to help.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You say he cares about it very seriously. I forget the word that you used. But I think a lot of people don't think that.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, look, and I mean, I understand that this is a -- an issue of debate in the country. And I understand that, and I think everybody in the administration understands that the concerns that Americans have, but I can assure you that the president understands the importance of border security. He understands the importance of national security, and border security is folded into that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you didn't -- we wouldn't be continuing to help from a military perspective to take the load off of those border patrol agents down there.\par \par \uc2 With regard to Ukraine, over the first couple of months, and we gave about $11 billion in aid --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- over in different tranches, over that period of time.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of a sudden, it's 4$0 billion on top of that. So, now you're talking $51 billion in aid. And, you know, obviously, I think there's a tremendous amount of support in this country, for Ukraine and for this mission, but I think people look at it and say, so, how many more billions? How long is this going to take? Are we getting ourselves into another endless war here?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, it's there's no way to know how long this is going to last. And we do think it's possible now that the fighting is concentrated in the Donbas region, the eastern part of the country that this could be a prolonged conflict. But we've been very clear that we want to continue to help the people of Ukraine and the Ukrainian Armed Forces defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 And so, that is going to require some additional resources, the president asked for $33 billion, not just in military support, but economic, and humanitarian assistance, looks like the Congress is poised to provide more funds on top of that. We're grateful for that support.\par \par \uc2 And Martha, it's been real bipartisan, I mean, both sides of the aisle understand the importance of the fight that the Ukrainians are in, and how important our ability to help them is, and the capabilities we can send them and the weapons and the systems, which are flowing every day into Ukrainian -- onto the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, look. President Biden is been clear, we're going to help as much as we can, as fast as we can. And I can assure you at the -- at the Pentagon, that's exactly what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, the U.K. Defense Minister Ben Wallace has been speaking out about this situation. He says that Putin is losing. That over time, Putin will lose.\par \par \uc2 And again, I'm asking what does that look like? So, what is -- what is victory? Is the United States in this to win it essentially, as a support vehicle for Ukraine? And what does it look like?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, we're going to let the Ukrainians define what victory is. I mean, is there a country and President Zelenskyy should do that. I think he is been very clear. He wants the Russians out of his country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: He wants his sovereignty respected, he wants his territorial integrity restored, and he has every right to want that. What we're going to do is set him up so that he can achieve that vision of victory that he has.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that we advantage him on the battlefield, which we're doing every day. Also, advantage him at the negotiating table when and if he can actually sit down and have that discussion with Mr. Putin, which he continues to want.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're helping them with all of this weapon and equipment. But now, what's happening is that the long range missiles coming mostly from Russia that are falling in the middle of Ukraine, and we've been seeing this over the last several weeks. So, what happens when Ukraine says this is our problem. It's these missiles that are being launched from inside Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, we need to take out those missile launch systems inside Russia. What would the U.S. response be to that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Will do we still be with them?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I wouldn't get into hypotheticals here. I mean, we, the Ukrainians have a right to defend themselves. And you're right, they are getting struck, not just in the East, but in the central part of the country. And we've even seen some strikes as far west as Lviv again, and down in Odesa.\par \par \uc2 They have a right to defend themselves. We're not -- we're not mandating to them. We're not telling them how they defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think we also understand, and it's not just the United States, but other countries in Europe understand the dangers of escalating this conflict, making it worse than it already is.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have to be mindful of that, too, when we're considering the kinds of things we're doing to help Ukraine and the kinds of things that Ukraine is doing to support itself.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: John Kirby, thank you. Always good to see you. Nice to have you in New York.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Thank you, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Take care.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too.\par \par \uc2 So, Jack Brewer is a former Democrat turned backer of President Trump's pro-life presidency, with a message to one of President Biden's officials, who says that abortion access benefits the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 So, did the administration fail on this baby formula crisis that we have been covering as well? The president is trying to address this today by talking to people at these companies that make it and we're going to tell you what's going on with that, right after this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): The \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America, it seems like it's easier to get a crack pipe in a government funded smoking kit than it is to find baby formula.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And a live look at the White House as we await one of \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 's final briefings. I think tomorrow is her last day so this is either the second last or the last, depending on whether or not to briefs tomorrow which we imagined she probably will. And the issue that is at hand today is this nationwide baby formula shortage. An estimated 40% of top selling brands are out of stock on US shelves, prompting some major retailers to put limits on how much can be purchased. Some Republicans say that the Biden administration's failure to act sooner created this crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): Today House Republicans are here to push for action from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the Biden administration, who should have had a plan for this shortage months ago. Instead, bare shelves Biden has continued to pass the buck. Babies have been put to bed hungry while parents are desperately trying to find alternative formulas that are often difficult to procure. This is not a third world country. This should never happen in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that we bring in James Freeman, assistant editor for the Wall Street Journal Editorial Page and Robert Wolf, former economic adviser to President Obama, founder and CEO of 32 Advisors. He is also a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 I want to start by hearing from some of the moms who are dealing with this situation. Let's listen to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALANA CONTTRILL, MOM STRUGGLING TO FIND BABY FORMULA: It's been a mental struggle. Because as a parent, you just want to provide for your child into your worst nightmare if you're unable to do so.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE NESHEIWAT, MOTHER OF AN 11-MONTH-OLD: Every night, I start to panic whenever the formula is getting low.\par \par \uc2 JENNY ERIKSON, MOM STRUGGLING TO FIND BABY FORMULA: Parents have just been under so much stress in the last two plus years. And you know now on top of everything else, we're having to worry about feeding our baby.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's the truth. Robert, it's kind of difficult to imagine a more a worse PR situation, not to mention the actual medical situation for some of these moms and their babies, then a shortage of baby formula. And I think the congresswoman kind of nailed it when she said this is not a third world country. I mean, this is not the kind of thing that we expect to have to deal with in the United States.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. This is tough to listen to, no question about it, and it's a travesty for parents and moms especially. But just to go through some of the facts. One, in the Michigan plant, they found bacteria. So the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , in one respect, did the right thing because it was dangerous having the recall. Number two, there's no question the supply side and logistics is still difficult in this post-COVID environment. And the three, and let me be clear, I was supportive of Trump NAFTA 2.0 but there were unintended consequences. And one of them was that we were actually became more stringent on importing baby formula, both from an aspect of must have \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval, and there were high tariffs even when it did have \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval. So my view is to allow imports to come in, and I would actually have no tariffs on them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So those are important facts that Robert lays out, James. But I think one of the things that it highlights is that knowing all of that, people have a lack of confidence in government's ability to function. And so, if those were the situations that were coming to the forefront, why, you know, we pay all -- the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has building this, like an entire block large in Washington, DC. So what are they doing in there?\par \par \uc2 JAMES FREEMAN, WSJ EDITORIAL PAGE ASSISTANT EDITOR: I think there are too many of them and they're intervening in ways that are not helpful. I agree 100% with Robert on the idea that we should allow more imports. We should take down those tariff and non-tariff barriers. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  should be better about allowing people to get into this business. They can't scale up quickly because it needs -- it requires a lot of \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals to reconfigure a factory to make baby formula.\par \par \uc2 But totally disagree with Robert on the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's role here. Based on what we know now, they created this crisis when they made that announcement on February 17th, warning consumers not to use the formula from that Michigan plant. They said we're investigating, we found bacteria. They call it a voluntary recall from that point, I'm not sure the company had much choice. Since then, they have not linked that bacteria to the childhood illnesses.\par \par \uc2 The CDC did a genetic test, no match. So this is an \uc2 FDA\uc2  created crisis. It appears, from what we know now, now maybe new information will come to light but this looks to be entirely government made. And we can solve it quickly because of the regulatory and trade barriers Robert was discussing that really impede people from quickly addressing this kind of situation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, yes. And I think it points to -- there's the question of government being able to function and to serve people which is its purpose. And clearly people have been let down in this situation and we hope that they can figure out a way to solve it quickly.\par \par \uc2 Robert Wolf, thank you. James Freeman, thank you. Good to see you both today.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So President Trump reaffirming his backing of Mehmet Oz in the GOP race for the Senate in Pennsylvania after a surge from Kathy Barnette, who is being called "an ultra mega wildcard." The former president says Oz is "the only one who will be able to easily defeat the crazed lunatic Democrat in Pennsylvania," that coming from President Trump, former President Trump. Correspondent Mark Meredith with a preview of this closely watched race which is now days away. Mark, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. It sure is. It's quite a statement too. It's coming down to the wire and Pennsylvania is closely watched Republican Senate primary. There's a new Fox News poll that essentially shows this as a three-way race with a lot of voters still undecided.\par \par \uc2 But as you mentioned, back in April, former President Trump endorsed Dr. Mehmet Oz for the seat currently held by retiring Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 . The endorsement certainly gave Oz a boost but the TV doctor, the multimillionaire, still facing a lot of questions about the validity of his conservative credentials. Oz's lead is also within the margin of error. He's spending a lot of money on ads doing a lot of interviews. I believe he'll be on "Special Report" tonight.\par \par \uc2 We're also watching businessman \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  long considered a strong contender in this race, his numbers seeing a slight drop in recent weeks. Today he's holding events outside of Philadelphia, even campaigning with Texas Senator Ted Cruz. A campaign event with someone like Cruz could help bring out some of these undecided voters give McCormick the boost that he needs.\par \par \uc2 But the other big story right now is conservative commentator Kathy Barnette getting a lot of last minute attention. Polls show her surging and now Trump is trying to prevent a shift from Oz to her campaign. In a statement out today, the former president says, "she has many things in her past which have not been properly explained or vetted, but if she is able to do so she will have a wonderful future in the Republican Party. And I will be behind her all the way." That's quite a statement appears he's trying to hedge his bets there a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Also beyond Pennsylvania, we continue to track all the different battleground states including Ohio. Our political team taking a close look at that Senate contest being vacated by Republican Rob Portman. The race right now looking likely Republicans having a decent shot here, JD Vance, who just won the Buckeye primary a week ago, still in it right now competing against Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . But the latest survey showing Republicans feeling more confident in Ohio. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting to watch. Mark, thank you very much. We had David McCormick on a few days back and Dr. Mehmet Oz will join us tomorrow at 3:00, so stay tuned for that tomorrow. And Republican Senator Tim Scott, with a deeply personal response to Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  for suggesting that access to abortion is beneficial to the US economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: True:\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): True, I'll just simply say that as a guy raised by a black woman in abject poverty. I'm thankful to be here as United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Roe v. Wade in access to reproductive healthcare, including abortion, helped lead to increased labor force participation. It enabled many women to finish school that increase their earning potential. What we're talking about is whether or not women will have the ability to regulate their reproductive situation in ways that will enable them to plan lives that are fulfilling and satisfying for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Senator Tim Scott sort of sat back and listened to that, and he had some own his own personal thoughts on his own life story with regard to these ideas. And then he confronted the Treasury Secretary, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , who says that overturning Roe v. Wade would have very damaging effects on the economy. And here's what Senator Tim Scott had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\\\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'll just simply say that as a guy raised by a black woman in abject poverty. I'm thankful to be here as the United States senator. Telling black teenage moms that there's only one alternative for them, is a depressing and challenging message. I'm simply saying that the experience of so many of us, millions of us in poverty, I conclude, is a reason to be hopeful about what's possible, even for those incredibly powerful positive women making really hard choices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like his mom, he said. Jack Brewer is founder of the jack Brewer Foundation. He was a lifelong Democrat and turned, became Republican in recent years. And Wendy Osefo is professor of Education at \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 . Welcome to both of you, very good to have both of you here.\par \par \uc2 So explain -- when, you know, what are do you take away from that exchange? \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  saying that, you know, it allows women to have lives that are satisfying and fulfilling if they can have abortions, and Tim Scott saying that he is very glad that he was born, even though he grew up in abject poverty.\par \par \uc2 WENDY OSEFO, PROFESSOR, \uc2 JOHNS HOPKINS UNIVERSITY\uc2 : I think the conversation should be one where they both really meet in the middle. You know, what \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is saying is that women should have the choice to decide whether or not they want to have children and Tim Scott is saying that his mom decided to make a choice to have him. And I think that the middle ground conversation there is that about choice. Women should have autonomy and agency over their bodies and no one should tell them whether or not motherhood is or is not for them. And that's what I took away from the conversation.\par \par \uc2 I know many people who are in high income situations and low income situations who have to make the decision whether or not they want to choose motherhood, but that decision is theirs and theirs alone. And we should allow women to be able to make that choice and that's simple.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about -- the Wall Street Journal kind of went through what \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said there about how, you know, allowing abortion, makes the economy stronger and they said