 \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, well said. Wendy, what's your response to that?\par \par \uc2 OSEFO: I think is really disingenuous to make it seem as though abortion is a Black issue. Abortion and the right for a woman to have an abortion is a woman's issue. And you're right, you know, when you lay in bed, you make the decision whether or not to have a child. But let's not put that sole decision on the shoulders of women. That women laid in bed and she laid in bed with a man. So if we're going to put together as a nation stipulations on whether or not to have a child that we should also look at the man's role in this. Why is it that only issues of you --\par \par \uc2 BREWER: Let the man sign up on abortions -- give us the right to sign up on abortions. Don't have abortions without having a having a man saying yes to them. I agree with you. You're right on point.\par \par \uc2 OSEFO: Well, I didn't say that, sir. That's not what I said. But I think it's really important that we can no longer continue to put this squarely on the shoulders of women. If we're going to talk about contraception, let's also talk about contraception for men. So one, no, it's not a Black issue, abortion is not. And then two, it's not just solely on women. We as a country have to have a deeper conversation about what contraception looks like.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. And men and women, obviously, there's two sides of the equation here and both should be involved in this process throughout. I think you both agree on that, at least on that. Wendy, thank you. Jack Brewer, thank you. Wonderful to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: God bless you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 OSEFO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's go to the White House PRESS SECRETARY \uc2 JEN Psaki\uc2 . She is taking some questions from the podium for maybe the second the last time. Tomorrow is her last day. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: -- a number of factors, including conflicting court rulings. So those were the two areas where you've seen the pause. But there was not interest.\par \par \uc2 I would note that if we take a step back and look at what is available out there and look at the supply needed, as you noted at the top of -- as part of your question, leasing and production offshore is a lengthier process. It can take up to 10 years to create supply. So, let's start there.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, of the more than 10.9 million offshore acres currently under lease, industry is not producing on 8.26 million acres. That's about seven point -- 75 -- more than 75 percent that is non-producing. Of the 24.9 million onshore acres under lease, industry is not producing on 12.3 million acres. That's almost 50 percent. And there are also over 9,000 onshore permits that have been approved and are waiting to be used onshore.\par \par \uc2 So, just to reiterate what we expect -- US production is expected to increase by over 1 million barrels a day this year and hit a new record next year, these specific actions were as a result of lack of industry interest and conflicting court rulings. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you. I guess, sort of the bottom line is, you know, on formula. This is a problem. I mean, I think other parents here in the room can probably attest. I, myself, went to three stores this morning, still haven't found what we need. When will parents be able to get the formula they need? What is your best sense of when store shelves will be stocked again?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think it's also important to note that the reason we're here is because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  took a step to ensure that babies were taking safe formula. There were babies who died from taking this formula, so they were doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 We have been working -- this administration has been working for weeks now to address in anticipation of where we thought there could be shortages. We have also seen an increase over the last four weeks of supply available, which hasn't been an increase over the four weeks prior to the recall. That is a good sign.\par \par \uc2 But obviously the steps the President took today are an acknowledgement and a recognition that more needs to be done, that we do not want parents, mothers, families out there to be stressed and worried about feeding their babies. That is why the President today had conversations with the CEOs of \uc2 Walmart\uc2  and Target, why he had conversations with Reckitt and Gerber about efforts to increase production, why we're taking steps to ensure that we are making WIC dollars available to a range of other supply. So, we're working.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing increases over the last couple of weeks. More needs to be done. We're going to cut every element of red tape we can cut. We're going to work with manufacturers. We're going to import more to expedite this as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But if you are a parent who is looking for formula right now, struggling to find what you need, do you have even a rough guess of how long these shortages are going to last? What should parents be bracing for here?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we've already seen an increase in supply over the past couple of weeks. What we are seeing, which is an enormous problem, is hoarding. People hoarding because they're fearful, that is one element of it, and people hoarding because they are trying to profit off of fearful parents. So that is also something we're focused on taking efforts to track and address and look into.\par \par \uc2 But again, more infant formula has been produced in the last four weeks than in the four weeks preceding the recall. We're taking every step to increase that. So, our message to parents is, we hear you, we want to do everything we can and we're going to cut every element of red tape to help address this and make it better for you to get formula on the shelves. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And as you mentioned, this obviously stems from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  taking necessary steps to try and deal with a very serious problem. And as Josh noted, you know, Abbott says that "subject to \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval," they could restart at this site "within two weeks." Do you have a sense of why it may be taking so long to get this factory back up and running or what the holdup is from the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's perspective?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would point you to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . But, again, I'd go back to why this decision was made in the first place, which was to save babies' lives. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is not going to approve manufacturing again unless they are certain of the safety. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When the President spoke with Philippines' President- elect Ferdinand Marcos last night, did they discuss either the dispute with China in the West Philippine Sea or the court, US court judgments against the president-elect?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I'm not aware of those being a part of the discussion, but I can check and see after the briefing if there's more details.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. And then on \uc2 NATO\uc2 , if Finland and Sweden are successful in their attempts to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , that would put the US on the hook, again, for military defending those countries, even potentially with nuclear weapons. What benefit is that to US citizens?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, let me say that we would support, the United States would support a \uc2 NATO\uc2  application by Finland and/or Sweden should they choose to apply. We, of course, will respect whatever decision they make. Both Finland and Sweden are close and valued defense partners of the United States and of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I would note that even without them being members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , our militaries have worked together for many years. We're confident that we can find ways to work with them, address any concerns either country may have about the period now or whatever is required if they were to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I would say, in terms of how this benefits the American people -- I think is sort of what you're asking about, having a strong NATO Alliance, a strong Western alliance, which is a defensive alliance, by the way, is good for our security around the world. And certainly, having a strong partnership with a range of countries, including Sweden and Finland, if they decide to join, should be reassuring to the American people about our own security interests.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And then, finally, when the President visits South Korea next week, does he intend to visit the demilitarized zone? And what's your current assessment of the threat of a North Korean nuclear test?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, on the first question, we're still finalizing details of the schedule for the trip and what it looks like. Obviously, that is a step that is taken by many who visit the region. But I expect we'll have our National Security Advisor here in the briefing room --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is planning to brief tomorrow which will be her last day at the White House as press secretary. She says her plans are not fully formed after that, but we're just going to -- obviously we'll check in on that when that happens tomorrow as \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  taken a lot of questions right now about this baby formula issue, and what the White House is going to be able to do to try to alleviate that for some parents across the country.\par \par \uc2 So before we leave you today, there is on the royal front, another notable absence from Queen Elizabeth, the 96-year-old monarch missed the first garden party of the season at Buckingham Palace today, days after sitting out for the Opening of Parliament, which she has not missed in almost 60 years. Prince Charles stood in for her at that event. Prince William was also in attendance.\par \par \uc2 So we are very glad to be joined today by award winning journalist, editor and author Tina Brown, who joins us now she was awarded the Honor of Commander of the Order of the British Empire by Her Majesty, Queen Elizabeth, for her services to journalism. And her new book, which is fantastic is called "The Palace Papers: Inside the House of Windsor, the Truth and the Turmoil," and it is out now. Tina, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 TINA BROWN, AUTHOR, "THE PALACE PAPERS": Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a great book. It's a big book. I'm about halfway through it. So we're enjoying it and you always get the juiciest details and the best interviews behind the scenes here. But let me just start with that. Obviously, she's not going to be attending these garden parties this year. And there's a lot of concern and speculation about how she's doing.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, she's very frail right now, there's no doubt about it. And, you know, she's the -- it's leaving the country in a kind of perilous state, essentially, because always in the past, the Queen has been there to reassure her composure, her dignity, her gravitas. We're about to enter the 70 year jubilee, you know, celebration of her jubilee. And you know, she's not able to appear a great deal. And it's causing a great deal of sort of national jitters (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, we saw Prince Charles sort of out and about yesterday, I believe. And people were kind of swarming him and saying, you know, how's your mom? And they're also reports that he was somewhat emotional at Parliament, at the Opening of Parliament the other day. And of course, everyone tries to read into those tea leaves, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, I think there is great anxiety. You know, and Prince Charles right now, he's having to step in as the sort of understudy constantly, you know, when his mother is (inaudible) another announcement of something that she can't do. But I think, you know, what I really tried to show in my book that, "The Palace Papers," has been the sort of 25 years of all of this turbulence in the family, all of this drama, but always at the center of it. It could be pulled back together because of Queen Elizabeth II's extraordinary ability to rise above it all and make people feel she could "keep calm and carry on," right?\par \par \uc2 Now, she can't keep calm and carry on and that is making a lot of difference essentially, in how the family dramas unfold. And she's had, you know, two or two or three terrible years, you know. We had the scandals with Prince Andrew, where he essentially had to be cancelled this and the allegations of sexual assault. We've had Harry and Meghan departing the family in a great sort of puff of smoke and a huge amount of drama surrounding it. And then their Oprah interview which caused so much kind of angst.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the loss of Prince Philip the Queen's beloved console, you know, who she is, who died during the pandemic. And this has left her I think, you know, very weakened, really, and it's very poignant to see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed it is. And, you know, you talk about the fact that she's just obviously the center, the driving force of this entire family, this monarchy and keeping it on course, over and over and over again. And you mentioned the Oprah interview, and it's reminiscent in some ways of what we saw from Diana, and you wrote the "Diana Chronicles," which was an excellent. And this sort of picks up where the "Diana Chronicles" ended.\par \par \uc2 But here's a little snippet of the interview that Meghan did, just to remind everybody how explosive this was.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN MARKLE\uc2 , DUCHESS OF SUSSEX: I've advocated for so long for women to use their voice, and then I was silent.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 , TALK SHOW HOST: Were you silent or were you silenced?\par \par \uc2 MARKLE: The later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So they're watching that at Buckingham Palace back then, and what's going through their minds, Tina?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I think what's going through their minds is why was she surprised? I mean, if you marry into the royal family, you're essentially subservient to the monarchy to the crown. And your role is to represent the crown. Your role is to be representing essentially the Queen when you go out on these engagements. It's not to be sounding off about your own opinions. So, yes, of course. I mean, her own opinions were silenced.\par \par \uc2 We don't know what the Queen thinks about anything after 70 years. Nothing, we don't know anything about what the Queen thinks. And, you know, it's served her rather well, you know, her inscrutability. But I think Meghan didn't understand that being a member of the royal family meant essentially, you're not supposed to be, you know, body and forthright --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's not about you. She didn't understand the it's-not-about-you part.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to be doing a podcast with Tina that I hope you all tune in for moments away. But for now we'll say goodbye. That's "The Story" for today, May the 12th. "The Story" goes on. We look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. And Your World gets underway in just a few seconds. Have a great evening, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Chicago's Gangland Encouraged By Dem Policies; The Left Seeks To Hasten America's Decline; Why The Rush To Send $40 Billion To Ukraine?; Washington Spends Recklessly While Americans Suffer; The Biden Blame Game Begins On Formula Crisis; China Turns Oklahoma Farmland Into Drug Farms; Sen. Bill Hagerty (R-TN) Interviewed On U.S. Policy Toward China; Republican Pr