etting us tangled into a proxy or an actual war with Russia, letting China skate, of course.\par \par \uc2 President Xi just must wake up every day and pinch himself that Biden's in the White House. Anyone else find it ironic then when we help make China richer, China just turns around and helps keep Russia afloat by buying more oil and agriculture from Russia. Heck of a job, Joey.\par \par \uc2 For most of the last two decades, the establishment of both parties and their enablers in the press corps, they've consistently placed their own interests ahead of ours. They've gotten richer, their buddies in China have gotten more powerful, and America, the greatest, freest, hardest working country on Earth, has been treated as a piggy bank for the destructive plants.\par \par \uc2 But my friends, the gravy train is coming to an end. Every day, the inflation numbers tell us that the game is up, and that their days of printing money and wasting American resources are running out.\par \par \uc2 This November it's going to be the beginning of a new era, in which the people finally take back their true power, and once more begin steering this country in the right direction. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Florida Congressman Byron Donalds, who serves on the House Budget Committee; and Horace Cooper, Project21 co-chair. Great to see you both in studio.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, you voted against this $40 billion spendoroma. The American people, I think, are really scratching their heads on this. What's going on up there?\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): That's a good question, because even I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Listen, we did $33 billion 2 weeks ago for Ukraine funding with Military equipment. That was two weeks ago. We come back to town this week, and there's a $40 million bill that we have literally two to three hours to read.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: $40 billion.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That just came out of nowhere.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I wish it was $40 million. $40 billion.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: You're right, $40 billion. My apologies. That's how upset I am.\par \par \uc2 So you're looking at this bill, you're still trying to figure out what's in it. At that point, I'm voting no.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Who read it, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Hardly anybody read it, maybe the staff did. But the members didn't have the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Who wrote it?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Your guess is as good as mine. It came out of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's office in the White House.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So lobbyists, I imagine, defense contractors, maybe food distribution company. I don't know. But the American people, Horace, are seeing our country grow weaker, and our families grow poorer day after day.\par \par \uc2 Yet apparently, money that does grow on trees. My mother used to say, it doesn't grow. Apparently, it does grow on trees. And we're just going to send it willy-nilly with literally zero accountability as to where it goes in Ukraine, if it goes there at all.\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, PROJECT 21 CO-CHAIRMAN: Well, my old boss, Dick Armey used to say, you can't be this wrong by accident. This bad behavior, this destructive behavior, it's not a coincidence. People who are not looking out for the American people are making the decisions, and it is time for a day of reckoning.\par \par \uc2 November needs to come and every American, that discussion from those people who are at the gas station, they're regular Americans. And those people are going to show up and hold people accountable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, when we have baby formula, which is something just so essential. We're America. This is the American - this is United States of America, and we're scrounging around for baby food.\par \par \uc2 I lived in the Soviet Union when I was a student. I said, I'll never wait in line for food again, after to wait in line just as a student for little stuff. This is the United States. This is what we're doing now.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: This is the failure of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's failed black America. He's failed white America. He's failed Hispanic America. Why? Because he just stumbles from crisis to crisis. There is no plan, there is no strategy. He does not know what to do.\par \par \uc2 This White House is a mess. The agencies are a mess, and the Democrats rather play politics that actually get down to the business of the American people. Unfortunately, folks, this is the reality of Washington, DC right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, rather than fixing this issue that is really striking at the heart of so many people about baby food formula, the Biden administration is just using - doing the usual gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Today, President Biden spoke with retailers and manufacturers to call on them, to do more, to help families purchase infant formula. What we are seeing, which is an enormous problem is hoarding. People hoarding because they're fearful and we are also - have been also calling on retailers to impose purchasing limits to prevent the possibility of hoarding, because we know that that is an issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why didn't we have hoarding during the Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Absolutely. This is remarkable. Every single one of the terrible results that we see today, they can't be pandemic related. This is policy. President Biden has made it worse. Everything he touches, he has a reverse Midas Touch. He destroys thing. It's not gold. It's a word that I can't say on TV.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But they keep thinking this is a comms problem. This is just to communicate - if they have a - yes, if they have a better backdrop, if they have a better Zoom call, if they have another celebrity come in and try to sell it. But they don't - they don't want to admit, I wish they can, Congressman, this is a policy problem.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has basic - by the way, hinted that they're going to spend more money on Ukraine, or COVID - excuse me, that she said more, more could be on the way. I mean, it's no end in sight for the borrowing and printing.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: As far as they're concerned, there's no problem that can't be solved without massive borrowing. This is the Bernie Sanders plan on steroids, and America is finally seeing it. It is a failure for our country. We have to reverse course.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And I want to get to this new Pew poll out today, guys. 88 percent of Americans believe that violent crime is either a very big or a moderately big problem. 88 percent, and they say, Oh, this is just a state problem. This is a Fox News concern. We've heard that.\par \par \uc2 This is bleeding into suburban America, but devastating urban America. The people who deserve it least, the ones who are struggling the most.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I am sick and tired of this administration standing up and trying to say, how they're looking out for black Americans, how they're looking out for the little guy, how they're looking out for the dispossessed. The truth of the matter is, they have their core constituencies. If you're a defense contractor, if you're a climate crazy, they got you covered. But if you're everybody else, too bad for you.\par \par \uc2 There is a killing filled happening in too many of the cities in America. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just goes out, gets the milk, gets the cookies and takes a nap, no concern whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And there's so much happening with \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2  and San Francisco. Congressman Donalds, you have \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , who believes she should be reelected. Her stellar record - Yes, she's being challenged by some other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But she - when she's asked about this, she's like, I'm not going to dignify your question about why I should be reelected. I mean, looking down at a reporter who asked the question. This is how arrogant and out of touch these people are. But they've been this way for a long time. This is really nothing new. But I think now Americans with less money in their pocket, they're really focusing on it. Final word.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Look, I think the failures that have happened all across America, Democrats are going to see this in November in spades. It's coming for \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , it's coming for everybody else. Don't believe me, go ask the school board members out in San Francisco what happened to them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they're all moving to Florida to your state, Congressman. All right, guys. Great to see you both, Congressman and Horace.\par \par \uc2 And remember, if you can't watch us live, make sure to set a series record on your DVR. Do you still not know how to do that? Oh, boy. All right. You don't want to miss us, every weeknight at 10. It's really fun.\par \par \uc2 Up next in INGRAHAM ANGLE investigation, China is buying up tens of thousands of acres of farmland in Oklahoma. Did you know that? What's going on? And wait until you hear what they're going to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Congress will set up to send $40 billion, as we said, to Ukraine with no strings attached. Some senators are fighting to stop it. One of those men, Senator Bill Hagerty, joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now yesterday, President Biden played farmer in Illinois in a pathetic attempt to show that he cares about the supply chain and farming crisis. But there's something troubling happening to farmland, some 600 miles southwest that deserves attention.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, Chinese nationals have begun buying up tens of thousands of acres of farmland in the state of Oklahoma. While they've been doing so in a number of states, Oklahoma has become their go-to state for these purchases. So what's China's end goal here?\par \par \uc2 Well, according to a statement from the \uc2 Oklahoma Bureau of Narcotics\uc2  to THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, eventually these farms typically become commercial marijuana growing operations. They went on to note that they buy agricultural, or livestock farms, then they turn them into marijuana farms, and then they sublet those - that business to Mexican workers.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Frank Lucas represents Oklahoma's third district where a lot of this is happening. He says, his office has received a number of calls, concerns from constituents voicing real worry about foreign owner of land purchases and how the land is then being used to grow marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Now, he went on to tell us that, "According to the Bureau of Narcotics, there are 8,500 medical marijuana grow licenses in Oklahoma. And of those a shocking 25 percent of them are run by criminal organizations from places like China and Mexico."\par \par \uc2 Now, the state saw the biggest drug bust in its history this past February, where Chinese nationals were among those arrested. Here are officers describing the scope of these operations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONNIE ANDERSON, DIRECTOR OF THE OKLAHOMA BUREAU OF NARCOTICS: Over 500 structures on 80 acres, this is one of the nine that we served a search warrant on this morning. This is pretty good size of drug trafficking organization. It's not - the nine that we did today are all connected. And it's part of this organization that's responsible for moving tons and tons of marijuana out of Oklahoma and to other states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And these aren't just marijuana farms. According to Senator Lankford's office, constituents have mentioned these buyers often aren't doing anything with the land at all. "They just purchase it, and then they just have it."\par \par \uc2 In other words, these Chinese-affiliated buyers want to devalue American farmland in any way they can, or just hold it in their portfolios. How nice.\par \par \uc2 So just how brazen is this? Lankford's spokesperson told us that, constituents have said Chinese parties have knocked on their door to purchase their farmland. In some cases, they were offered a suitcase of cash to make the deal that day.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma bans foreign ownership of farmland, but there's a loophole that allows foreign investors to create a business and they then buy land for that business. A state Senate bill introduced back in February aimed at stopping this. But as far as we can see, it hasn't gone anywhere.\par \par \uc2 But given the economic and the National Security ramifications here, Congress should get involved. A bill to limit purchases of agricultural tracts by China was working its way through the House, but apparently stalled in committee. This is absurd. And it's typical Washington, failing to do what needs to be done as it spends money that we don't have on stuff we don't need. We're going to bring you updates on this as we get them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I hope the Senate can reach an agreement to consider and pass this legislation. Did the Ukrainians need it? We need to do it today. It bears directly on America's national security and vital interests.\par \par \uc2 America cannot wish away global problems that affect us and our allies simply about burying our head in the sand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's what we do on China, morning, noon and night. Well, of course, thanks to Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , who single handedly blocked that Senate from passing the $40 billion Ukraine aid bill today, setting the scene for a fight next week.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bill Hagerty, who plans to vote no on this bill joins me now. Senator Hagerty, great to have you in studio. So there's a rush, rush, rush on this. Why?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): I can't tell you why, Laura, because we have pressing problems right here at home that we're not addressing. You talked about it earlier in this segment. You got people in Memphis that can't find formula for their children; people in Knoxville, they can't gas their tank up because it's too expensive.\par \par \uc2 In my home state of Tennessee, I'm from a flyover state, as they call it here in Washington. We have real problems here. And it precipitates from the Biden administration, the policies that take and basically war in America, collapsing our southern border, standing by as China wages war on our youth, killing more people with fentanyl than any of the thing from the ages of 18 to 45 in America. That's the number one killer. China's fingerprint is on this. And we're standing by and letting it happen. Yet we're rushing to take care of problems overseas.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This total that we're going to be spending in Ukraine, it looks like - I mean, you kind of have to compare it to what we spent in Afghanistan. So we don't declare war, but we declare war on the American people's future by spending money that we don't have, as people are scrounging for baby - the baby formula story. I just - this is like one of the worst things, worse commentaries on the state of America today.\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: Certainly, it is. And I think it just reflects the total out of touch nature of this entire Biden administration. As you say, they'd rather wage war on Americans, criticize Americans and ignore our plight here in America, yet rush to a cause that perhaps \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gets better rating points on. I'm not certain. But it certainly isn't as pressing as what's happening right here in America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ye. Congressman Massey made the comment that it's about the $54 billion would be suspiciously close when you add it all up to the annual amount we spent in Afghanistan. And it's almost like, I guess, he's hinting at the defense contractors, mainly, look - they're in business, they want to make money, I get it. But that's a big chunk of money that is not going to be allocated to a lot of Military interests, if they don't get that money.\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: Well, that money certainly has a place, a better place and a higher use in my view to be dealt with issues right here at home. Again, borrowing money that we don't have to spend, again, on areas of the world where there may be a crisis. I don't think America has anything against Ukraine. We don't want to see them fail. But we have problems right here at home that we need to--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Should Americans die to, say, Finland and Sweden?\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: That's going to be an interesting question that comes up soon. But the difference with Finland and Sweden is--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They are not members in \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: They're not members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but if they join them and actually add to the capacity of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , what \uc2 NATO\uc2  has done in the past is bring people on that are just liabilities for us. So there's a possibility there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When you were watching the reader we did on the Chinese buying up farmland in the United States, they bought \uc2 Smithfield Foods\uc2 , which is 180,000 - 150,000 acres of land and agricultural products, which was eye- raising to me at the time. It was years ago. But now more farmland, some of it for marijuana, isn't it time for Congress to get involved here?\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: It seems like an invasion from China at every point, Laura. And Washington is willing to look the other way every time when it comes to China, yet we are ready to jump on board with other problems that I think are much more -- the biggest problem we've got right now is China.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can you and I go over there and by some land in China?\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: I can assure you that's not possible.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let's see if we can buy 100,000 acres in China.\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: That will absolutely be impossible to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can we ask you to follow-up on this on the Senate side and see what the heck is going on? Because I think America are shocked to hear this.\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: That was new news when, I saw the report tonight. I saw Senator Lankford's office involved in this, so I will reach out to him tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Fantastic. Senator, great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 HAGERTY: Good to be with you, Laura. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, and here is the clue for tonight's "Wit's End" segment. Remember, the answer is revealed at the end of the show. What provocative item did one Catholic school accidentally sell for its students for Mother's Day? Tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle, we'll see if you get it right.\par \par \uc2 But up next, last night we called the hottest race in America, and boy, the Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary has proven to be the case. In moments we are going to be everything you missed, including edited videos and old tweets. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're just five days out from the red-hot Pennsylvania Senate GOP primary, and wow, has that race got dirty in its final days. We continue not to have a horse in this, but because of all the high stakes for all these races ahead of November, we wanted to make sure that you're getting all the facts.\par \par \uc2 Now, a number of doctored videos have been shared in an attempt to smear Kathy Barnette, many suggesting that she is a big supporter of BLM, which she has been denouncing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for years. She addressed what she called the crazy allegations today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE, (R) PENNSYLVANIA U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: I have never supported \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . Look at what \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  has done to our nation. They have divided. They have stoked division. But that's what Marxism does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She made some other controversial comments, and the Barnette fallout will no doubt continue. But the more salient question is this -- with all his fame and with all his celebrity and all the big endorsements, why hasn't Dr. Oz been able to seal the deal with the Pennsylvania voters? Why is that? What is it about him that voters aren't comfortable with?\par \par \uc2 Pro McCormick PACs have dug up disturbing comments he's made about everything from gender transitioning for kids to abortion to police involved shootings. Here he is before he became a Republican Senate candidate buying into a false police shooting narrative.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Shot seven times in the back. The family of Jacob Blake speaks out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He was trying to kill my son.\par \par \uc2 OZ: His devastating injuries.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It hurts to breathe. It hurts to sleep.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Do they think you'll be able to walk again?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He also pulled a knife on a cop along with other things. Joining me now is Jack Brewer, a former \uc2 NFL\uc2  safety turned to CEO who has actually endorsed Kathy Barnette. Jack, again, we want to get all the information here. I think \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  is a strong candidate. Kathy Barnette I don't know as much about, but a lot of controversy about her out there as well. But you say that former President Trump's endorsement of Oz was a mistake. Why do you say that?\par \par \uc2 JACK BREWER, THE BREWER GROUP CEO: Because I don't think he represents what American ideals and conservative principles are. I think he is wishy- washy. I think he will say whatever he can to be elected. I think his money and fame is the reason he got the endorsement, and I think a lot of those that are in power in the media and in corporate America want to see individuals like this. I think this is the old establishment playbook.\par \par \uc2 And I know Kathy Barnette, I know her personally. And to compare these two, it's not even a comparison. If you were hiring someone to run your company or you were hiring someone to oversee your family, it would be someone like Kathy Barnette, not Dr. Oz. I've heard him on the breakfast club and all of these other things. He panders to the woke left. He panders to the ideals that are destructing the poorest of the poor in our nation. He panders to mass abortions. And so whenever --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jack.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: Go ahead, Laura. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We are going to play some of that. But what do you say to the tweets that are getting all the attention, the Kathy Barnette tweets about boarding planes and other things she said that have gotten a lot of heat out of there, what about those?\par \par \uc2 BREWER: I think a lot of that is out of context. First off, they are doctoring up so many different things and trying to smear her. I don't come out talking about Dr. Oz or anybody else, but when you start smearing a woman of God like Kathy Barnette who served in our military, who has been a devout Christian all her life, I have a problem with that.\par \par \uc2 And the tweets that she made, many of them are taken completely out of context. You start talking about Muslims and people are saying she wants to ban Muslims. She was talking about Islamic extremists. In the time period if you go back and look when she puts those tweets out and made those statements, the time period that we were in was a trying time when we were all wondering who was coming over here.\par \par \uc2 And so I think she is a true conservative, we know all that. As a black conservative, it takes a lot to stand up for the MAGA movement --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It does.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: -- stand up for President Trump. You are going to get attacked, your families are going to get attacked. So when she steps out for President Trump like he didn't 16 and in 20 -- listen, I didn't always like President Trump. But he woke my eyes up and he brought in a new perspective on elections, a new perspective on our political process and understanding how the deep state really works. And I think this is an example of the deep state now turning against Kathy Barnette. Republicans and conservatives, we need a black woman like Kathy Barnette representing us, speaking for us. She does speak for America. And I think we are making a huge mistake by slandering her.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, the pile-on is quite something to see. Here is Dr. Oz today responding to the heat that he has gotten.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: What's ironic is that big money groups, never Trump groups, have now come into Pennsylvania with outside money, and they are wrongly supporting Kathy Barnette and putting her in contention. When you're an outsider, the establishment, they come after you with everything they've got. And I've been able to stand on my own two feet very proudly and live up to the promise of my campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jack, your reaction. Dr. O-Z, he's a very well-known person. He has enormous amount of celebrity and star power. And I guess, but he is an outsider, or she is an outsider. It's hard to follow that.\par \par \uc2 BREWER: It's hard to follow what you have a woman who started a campaign with three individuals going out campaigning and talking to people directly. All of a sudden, she is in a statistical tie with someone who is spending $50 million, $60 million. It just doesn't make sense, that logic is not there.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Barnette is resonating with people because of her message, because of her story, a woman who was born off of rape, and all of these other issues we are talking about, she represents everything great about the United States of America. And I'll tell you what, if they continue this, we are going to lose so many strong, black conservatives. If you look across, Laura, the strongest black conservatives in our nation have supported Kathy Barnette, and that is for a reason. These are Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So you disagree with this massive concern that has been raised that she cannot win a general election? Obviously, the Republicans can't lose this seat. This is a seat they should win. Your contention is despite all of this stuff bubbling around, that she can win this Senate seat and go to Washington and fight for America first principles, is that what you're contending?\par \par \uc2 BREWER: I'll telling you, right. You will see historic black women voting for a Republican in Pennsylvania like you've never seen. You will see people like me. I'm a former Philadelphia Eagle. I'll be on the ground campaigning for her. We will have a landslide victory because we will go in and get the minority vote like no other Republican has ever done in the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jack, we are going to be watching this. This is really a three way race right now. A lot of money spent, and it's all pretty much tied up. Jack Brewer, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now, what if everything the experts told you about climate change was wrong? A remarkable finding about hurricanes has the climate alarmists freaking out tonight. We're going to explain it in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You look at how climate change is fueling more powerful storms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We see that when those climate models are run in the future with those higher greenhouse gas concentrations, we see more extreme events of certain types.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ida's powerful winds and massive destruction path is urging us, of course, to examine how climate change is fueling these super storms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, really? Not so fast. According to research from the \uc2 National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration\uc2 , the dramatic reductions in air pollution in North America and Europe during the past 40 years is likely responsible for the steep increase in the number of hurricanes in the Atlantic. The research also shows that in Asia where pollution has increased, there have been hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Steve Milloy, former Trump \uc2 EPA\uc2  transition member and founder of JunkScience.com, as well as Charlie Hurt, FOX News contributor and "Washington Times" opinion editor. Steve, whenever science and facts get in the way, the left change their messaging. What's going on here?\par \par \uc2 STEVE MILLOY, FOUNDER OF JUNKSCIENCE.COM: This is a crazy study. It's not very good science, and it's even bad propaganda, because what it basically says is that \uc2 EPA\uc2  regulations, wind turbines and solar panels and EVs cause hurricanes. That seems to be off message for them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But why? Why would this come out?\par \par \uc2 MILLOY: I don't know. I think that they think that we are all so stupid, we will just believe anything. The study makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Did you read how it was actually conducted? Did you actually --\par \par \uc2 MILLOY: Yes. They just went back in time and did some correlation, cherrypicked data, did correlations, and came up with this -- I don't know what the point of the study is because it doesn't really match with any of their goals. It's not very good science, it's not really science at all. It's junk science. Science would be to use the data they had to make predictions about the future, but they're not really doing that. They just want to draw conclusions.\par \par \uc2 The most active hurricane period in the United States was from 1772 to 1780 during the Revolutionary War, before cheeseburgers, SUVs, coal plants. And where were all the hurricanes? The hurricanes were there, but now they are not. So this study really makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, we had a lot going on on Capitol Hill with the \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administer Gina Raimondo speaking out about what your kids should be doing and worried about. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMUNDO, SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: We believe climate change is an existential threat. So children won't -- forget about clothes on their back. They are not going to be able to have a life if we don't deal with climate change.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My constituents can't forget about the need to put clothes on their kids backs right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, they continue to pour money anywhere and everywhere but into the struggling pockets of Americans.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, OPINION EDITOR, "THE WASHINGTON TIMES": Yes, because this is their religion. And of course, the reason it is their religion is because no matter what the problem is, the solution is always the same. The solution is to send more money to Washington and to give bureaucrats in Washington more power. And that's the purpose of their religion, and that's why whether this study is idiotic or not, or whether it actually makes sense and that the cleaner air is causing more hurricanes -- whatever it is, it doesn't matter. They're going to find a way to use all of it to convince people to send more money to Washington and give more power to bureaucrats in Washington.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Steve, as gas prices, oil prices are soaring, the Biden administration announced yesterday they are going to cancel these oil and gas leases, critical oil and gas leases in Alaska and in the Gulf. And at this point, Tim Kaine, he is actually responding. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE, (D-VA): It strikes me if we are the largest energy producer in the world, and we know that at least transitionally our European allies need energy from sources other than Russia, that us going to Saudi Arabia and say please produce more energy when we're not willing to do it ourselves, I just don't get it. I don't know that there is a coherent strategy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Democrat, he saw that Glenn Youngkin win in that Virginia and suddenly we've got Tim Kaine is making sense here. Steve, this was true sabotage to the American economy.\par \par \uc2 MILLOY: Sure. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that he was doing everything he could to lower gas prices, and he has done the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was a lie.\par \par \uc2 MILLOY: That was a lie. He has done the exact opposite. Not only did he cancel his leases this week, but he's actually in federal appellate court arguing that he doesn't have to do anymore leasing because of climate change. And you throw in the rollback of the Trump reforms of national environmental policy act, and everything else, all the pipeline problems, they're trying to cancel -- they want to be able to cancel pipelines because of climate, he's doing everything he can to discourage oil production.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And what do you of the Democrat revolt, Charlie, on this? They are obviously seeing what everyone else is seeing and feeling. The consumers are extremely upset.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Absolutely. Whether it's inflation or the fact that gas prices have skyrocketed yet again, they are back to their highest levels. They're over $4 a gallon, that is a cheap gallon of gas today. And they are looking at the fact that the entire war in Ukraine was entirely caused by the fact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decided to destroy America's energy independence, which made \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  rich enough to invade Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All, Steve and Charlie, great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Our "Wit's End" segment next. Remember, here's a clue. What provocative item did one Catholic school accidentally sell to its students for Mother's Day? Wow. Hurry up and tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What provocative item did one Catholic school accidentally sell to its students for Mother's Day? Jason tweeted "Individually wrapped ultrathin balloons," uh-oh, Valerie wrote "Birth control pills," and Eileen guessed today's version of mother's little helper. Gummies.\par \par \uc2 Here's the big reveal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Open up the rose. Open it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God. I see it already. Who bought them, the school?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. They just sent out an email saying --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Can you imagine your first grader bringing that home? Well, guess that will get your attention.\par \par \uc2 Remember, USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com. All proceeds for the month of May are going to the Shoreline Soup Kitchens and Pantries in Connecticut. LauraIngraham.com.\par \par \uc2 "GUTFELD!" next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Speaks With Retailers And Manufacturers Of Baby Formula; \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  Effort To Increase Supply; April Producer Prices Signal Lasting Inflation; Mehmet Oz, David Mccormick And Kathy Barnette In Tight Pennsylvania Race; Russian Tension: Moscow Warns Finland Over NATO Membership Bid; Rebuilding Cities: Russian Troops Continue Military Attacks; House Committee Investigating January 6th Riots Asking Congressmembers To Testify; Republicans Calling On Justice Department To Protect Homes Of Supreme Court Justices Due To Continued Protesting; Wildfire In Southern California Damages Mansions; New Images Released Of Black Hole At Center Of Milky Way Galaxy; White House Attends COVID Summit; Biden Administration Attempts To Address Ongoing Concern Over Inflation In U.S. Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jacqui Heinrich, Grady Trimble, Madison Alworth,  Greg Palkot, Chad Pergram, Mike Emanuel, William La Jeunesse, Alexis  McAdams \par \uc2 8,128 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And Hemmer town has been on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  trying to psych me out saying I'm going to win, but I know I'm -- wait, wait, yes, never mind. Maybe I'll lose but you never know.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right. I hope you win. That's -- well, I love Bill too. That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hi, Judge, you know what, you may have to make a deciding judgements on those two? That's a tough one, Dagen versus Bill. We'll be watching.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks, Judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Take care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, growing concerns about the nationwide shortage of baby formula. The White House says President Biden recognizes more needs to be done. It says supplies are increasing but hoarding is now becoming a problem. The president met with suppliers today.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage Grady Trimble in Sturgis, Michigan where a major formula manufacturer is still shut down. We began with White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich and yet another major challenge for the administration. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Well, the pandemics fueled supply chain problems have been creating shortages for baby formula for months, but a voluntary recall from major manufacturer Abbott, which prompted the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to shut down that plant amplified the problem.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are signs the White House should have seen this coming sooner and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  possibly could have done more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): According to Politico, a former employee at Abbott's Sturgis, Michigan plant reported safety concerns to senior \uc2 FDA\uc2  officials in October, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn't interview that whistleblower until December. The plant inspection didn't happen until January 31st. Then, the recall happened February 17th.\par \par \uc2 So, is that timeline acceptable to the White House? And if not, what is the White House doing to correct that at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm sure there will be plenty of time to take a look at if there are any issues that could have been improved here. I don't have any specific analysis of that at this moment in time. What I will notice that there has been work on going on this for months.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House revealed some of that work in a new fact sheet today, telling the \uc2 USDA\uc2  to allow buyers paying with WIC benefits to use them for a wider variety of products, calling on the FTC to crack down on price gouging and ramping up imports from abroad while pushing manufacturers at home to produce more.\par \par \uc2 But while the president has unveiled plans to increase competition in other consolidated markets like meatpacking, so far, it's not on the agenda for formula.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It's a good question. I have not been made aware of that being a concern here. But I'm happy to raise it and see if there's more, I'd tell you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans are pushing Democratic leaders in Congress to move up their timeline.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: House Democrats plan is to have a hearing two weeks from now. I can tell you as a new mom, families and moms and dads and babies can't wait two weeks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The Biden administration is considering invoking the Defense Production Act, but first, trying to ensure it would deliver the intended outcome.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the White House is not issuing guidance for families on what they should do if they run out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is there a hotline that they can call? Should they take a baby to the hospital? What should they do?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I would say those are important public health questions. But what I can report on here, what I can convey to all of you is what we're doing to address exactly that concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the White House says because of their actions, manufacturers have produced more formula in the past four weeks than the four weeks preceding the plant shutdown and that recall, but after Abbott's plant reopens, it's going to take another eight weeks for that formula to reach store shelves and in the meantime, parents who are driving around to different stores searching for it are also paying record high gas prices, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is a tough combination. And Jacqui, congressional Republicans are alleging there's no shortage of formula at the southern border. They say taxpayer dollars are actually using that to supply migrant processing facilities. What have you learned?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, so Republican Congresswoman Kat Cammack of Florida is on the Homeland Security Committee and she says that they are launching a formal inquiry into this after Border Patrol agents sent her pictures and videos showing pallets of unopened formula and diapers at the Donna and Ursula processing facilities.\par \par \uc2 She says that those agents tell her even more shipments are expected in the coming days. So, we plan to ask the White House for their response and more information on this, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll be standing by for that Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, let's take a closer look at what is happening at that troubled \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  facility in Michigan. Fox Business Correspondent Grady Trimble is in Sturgis tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As parents across the country struggled to find baby formula, still no relief.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLARD, MOTHER: One week, I'm thinking to myself, am I not going to have formula to feed my child?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's still scary sometimes thinking that, OK, we have maybe two bottles left and we have to go get formula and we might not find it.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: No word on when one of the country's top formula makers Abbott could restart production at its Sturgis, Michigan plant. Even after it does, it could take up to two months before that product hits shelves.\par \par \uc2 The factory has been shuttered since Abbott issued a voluntary recall in mid-February. Four kids using Abbott's formula got sick, two of them died.\par \par \uc2 The company now says after a thorough review of all available data, there's no evidence to link our formulas to these infant illnesses. This chart shows how the problem got worse after the recall.\par \par \uc2 But even before February, formula shortages were cropping up. Now, nationwide, the supply of baby formula is 43 percent below normal. States in red like Arizona, Nevada, Tennessee and Texas have an out of stock rate of more than 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 RUDOLF LEUSCHNER, ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, RUTGERS BUSINESS SCHOOL: The government can only pay play a coordinating role, retailers might be able to share inventory. Manufacturers might be able to coordinate how they purchase products, and everybody can try to find different ways to raise their capacity.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: Only a handful of companies make almost all of the country's formula. To make up for the factory shut down, Abbott says it's flying in product from Europe. Other companies say they're ramping up production.\par \par \uc2 PETER PITTS, FORMER ASSOCIATE COMMISSIONER OF \uc2 FDA\uc2 : Simply can't turn it up to 11 so to speak and produce more formula. These are very precise manufacturing mechanisms. And changes have to be made with \uc2 FDA\uc2 's OK. You can expedite but you can't rush, rushing causes problems.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE (on camera): Abbott says it's waiting for the go ahead to restart production here. It needs the \uc2 FDA\uc2  it says to do that. We've asked the \uc2 FDA\uc2  why it hasn't given the OK for Abbott to start making formula again and they haven't given us a clear answer, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Once it does get going again and the factory is up and running, the company says it can start making formula within two weeks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Grady Trimble, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed, the Dow lost 104, the S&P 500 fell five, NASDAQ finished ahead seven.\par \par \uc2 Another shot of bad inflation news for the economy since Wall Street on a wild ride and sparks more talk about a recession.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business Correspondent Madison Alworth is in New York tonight. Good evening, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Shannon. We got another round of inflation data today that could show a bumpy road ahead for our economy. The Producer Price Index rising 11 percent year over year in the month of April, its fifth consecutive month of double digit gains.\par \par \uc2 The Producer Price Index measures what suppliers are charging businesses and other customers and generally reflects supply conditions in the economy. It could signal more inflation pressure building.\par \par \uc2 The fear is that more inflation could drive the U.S. into a recession. And that is the tough position that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  sees itself in, trying to thread an economic needle, extinguishing red hot inflation while trying to avoid recession and job loss.\par \par \uc2 But Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  receiving a show of support from the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  today confirming him for a second term on an 80 to 19 vote. Inflation will be the biggest issue for law and policy makers. Prices for necessities like food and energy are surging year over year. It is eating into Americans paychecks and they feel stuck.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm going to pick up my daughter from college, I'm going to probably use all $115 and then come back, fill up and so I can get to work as well.\par \par \uc2 I've already committed to certain things and that's why this is a big shock. I have no choice but to fill up my gas tank and pay this money, there's no other choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: And it's not just Main Street. This year, Wall Street is feeling the hurt. Major indices down double digits for the year. The tech heavy NASDAQ down nearly 30 percent driven by sell offs of blue chip stocks like \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another big tech name in uncertain territory, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the company announcing that they will freeze hiring amid a takeover by billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . They are also laying off some executives. One executive confirming via tweet today that he was let go because they want to take the team in a different direction.\par \par \uc2 Musk \uc2 Twitter\uc2  deal is expected to close this year but we could still be months away from that, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Madison Alworth, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is canceling one of the most high profile oil and gas lease sales pending before the Interior Department. The government is halting the potential to drill for oil in more than one million acres and Alaska's Cook Inlet along with to lease sales in the Gulf of Mexico. The move comes as gasoline prices continued to hit record levels.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. Mehmet Oz who is one of the GOP contenders in the Senate primary in Pennsylvania coming up in just days. Doctor, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR PENNSYLVANIA: Great to be here, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's start with this has gotten to be an incredibly tight race. I want to pull up some Fox News polling brand new on this. It has got you at the top of the pack at 22 percent. McCormick follows you at 20 percent. Barnette at 19 percent. That's essentially within our margin of error.\par \par \uc2 So, I want to give you a chance to hear from your critics and a chance to respond as you all are all neck and neck at this point. Here are some jabs at why you aren't as qualified as they are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLI