ts on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARNETTE: I've seen him on numerous occasions, and specifically at the Breakfast Club saying that my life was nothing more than a corn with electrical currents. I am wondering if the doctor has now since changed his position on that.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: If you go back to that Breakfast Club interview, he'd tax the pro-life for picking fights, he attacks states to put in pro-life legislation as disrupting business. And this is something that you have to deal with Mehmet, because you've got a track record that's completely opposite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so that Breakfast Club interview was a radio interview back in 2019, in which you said that you were pro-life but that you wouldn't interfere with everyone else's stuff is the quote, because it's hard enough to get into life as it is.\par \par \uc2 So, what about the accusation that you have flipped or changed on that position?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I was clear in that interview. And it's the only one that keep talking about that I was against the law from Alabama that would ban abortions and all exceptions.\par \par \uc2 And I'm of the belief as a doctor that if the life of the mother is truly at risk, that will be an exception that is acceptable.\par \par \uc2 And I just think Kathy Barnette doesn't like that. And I as a heart surgeon respect the sanctity of life, I believe life starts at conception. I take care of babies when they're first born, I know this specialness of the baby and nine months earlier, it's still life.\par \par \uc2 But she's raised questions in her own candidacy that she's refused to answer as I'm answering yours right now. She's supported \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and one today statue of President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in place of other structures that are sacred to our country. She attacked the founder of our country, George Washington, as well as attacking President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And what's ironic is big money groups, Never Trump groups have now come into Pennsylvania with outside money. And they are wrongly supporting Kathy Barnette and putting her in contention.\par \par \uc2 And this is a candidate who lost by 20 points when she ran for Congress 18 months ago. So, she's not going to do on the general election, which is why President Trump has over and over again even today, put out a new announcement saying that I'm the one to support because I can win in November. And that's what we need as Republicans and other senator to take the place of the currently retired Republican senator in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 If you want that, you should vote for Dr. Oz which is I think why I'm still leading in the polls.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, they are tight. They are within the margin of error. She is surging and what is a good time for her. I am going to speak with her in a couple of days and we'll ask her those questions that are now bubbling up. Dr. Oz, in the meantime, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 OZ: God bless, take care.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will also have David McCormick on SPECIAL REPORT tomorrow night, but up next look at the death and devastation left behind by Russian troops in one Ukrainian town.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WFXT in Boston as Governor \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  agrees to a $56 million settlement in a lawsuit against Massachusetts over its treatment and care of veterans at the Holyoke Soldiers' Home. During the pandemic, 84 veterans died during the outbreak at the facility. Dozens more veterans were sickened.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in the Twin Cities as severe storms that pushed across Minnesota leave behind damage, downed trees, flooding and power outages. Utility officials said this morning they were working to restore power to some 30,000 customers.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami from WSVN. One of the big stories there tonight, police trying to identify several good Samaritans who stopped to help a woman suffering a medical episode in the middle of a very busy intersection in Boynton Beach.\par \par \uc2 Traffic cameras capture the people springing into action to help the woman. Her daughter says she likely had a reaction to medication. She's doing great now.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pentagon officials confirmed North Korea fired three short range ballistic missiles toward the sea today. It is the latest in a series of demonstrations this year. It came just hours after North Korea confirmed its first official case of Coronavirus since the pandemic began. President Biden is scheduled to visit Japan and South Korea later this month.\par \par \uc2 Finland's leaders have come out in favor of applying to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Sweden could do the same within days. The Kremlin is warning Finland it will be forced to take retaliatory military technical steps.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, residents are returning to the town of Bucha after the Russian occupation left a trail of death and destruction. Here's Senior Foreign Affairs Correspondent Greg Palkot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The shockwave from the war in Ukraine reached further today. Finland saying we're to break decades of neutrality and apply for membership to \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Russia's aggression directly responsible.\par \par \uc2 PEKKA HAAVISTO, FINNISH FOREIGN MINISTER: Russia's invasion of Ukraine has altered the European and Finnish security environment.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: It means Russia would double its shared border with \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries by another 800 miles. Sweden also looks like it will join \uc2 NATO\uc2  ranks. Approval from the alliance is expected. Russia is threatening unspecified actions.\par \par \uc2 DMITRY PESKOV, PRESS SECRETARY, KREMLIN (through translator): \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion does not make our continent more stable and secure.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: And that's not all from Moscow. With U.S. and Western military aid pouring into Ukraine, former Russian President Medvedev warned that could push the conflict into a direct war between Russia and \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And with economic sanctions biting, President Putin his grousing as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, RUSSIA (through translator): These sanctions largely provoke the global crisis.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Russian testing is perhaps a sign all is not well on the battlefield. Russian troops pulling back from Ukraine's second city of Kharkiv, grinding slowly on the Eastern Front, as the Ukrainian fighters in the Mariupol steel mill still hold out amid more signs of Russian military brutality.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . saying today more than 1000 Ukrainian civilians were killed by Russian troops during the occupation of suburbs north of Kyiv including the town of Bucha where locals are returning. Markets are reopening. Buildings are being repaired. The town's mayor sounded determined.\par \par \uc2 ANATOLIY FEDORUK, MAYOR, BUCHA, UKRAINE (through translator): It is our moral obligation to improve the city after the atrocities here, he says.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: But with the scars of Russian horror all around and services and jobs still in short supply, residents aren't so sure.\par \par \uc2 ALEXEY CHERKASHIN, RESIDENT, BUCHA, UKRAINE: Of course we all want to restart here but our feelings are pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): The street that gentleman lives on just about a month ago was littered with bodies, Ukrainian civilian, victims of Russian killing. Actually, that fellow ended on a positive note telling us we will live and we will win. Many here can only hope. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The determination is inspiring, Greg, thank you. Up next, a setback in attempts to provide Supreme Court justices more protection in the wake of the abortion draft leak.\par \par \uc2 But first, beyond our borders tonight, a Chinese passenger jet veers off the runway during takeoff and catches fire. More than 30 people were injured. Video shared by state media shows the left side of the aircraft on fire as people who appear to be passengers head away from the scene.\par \par \uc2 Qatar's Emir arrives in Iran for talks with the Iranian president as efforts to save Tehran's tattered nuclear deal with world powers hit a deadlock. State media says bilateral, regional, and international issues are on the agenda during that visit.\par \par \uc2 Spain says the land borders between its North African enclaves of the Ceuta and Melilla in Morocco will reopen next week. They have been closed for more than two years due to COVID 19 restrictions and tensions between the two countries.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Federal prosecutors are starting a grand jury investigation into the possible mishandling of classified documents that were found at former President Trump's Florida home. That's according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . The report says the probe is examining the role of the Trump administration in the handling of sensitive materials during the final stages of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, House investigators have issued subpoenas to five Republican lawmakers including Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy as they look into last year's Capitol riot. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has the details from Capitol Hill. Hello, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon. It is unprecedented for Congress to issue a subpoena to sitting lawmakers for testimony. Here are the five members who won't cooperate, all from the GOP