enn Youngkin is asking Fairfax County authorities to extend security perimeters around the homes of three justices who live there. The county said no. The commonwealth's attorney general says the Justice Department must step up, too.\par \par \uc2 It's a clear violation of section 1507, and they are trying intimidate witness -- intimidate Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): The Justice Department has prosecuted cases before for people disrupting oral arguments in their workplace at the court. Some experts suggest protesters could also be charged with obstruction of justice for acts aiming to intimidate and influence the Supreme Court justices. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mike, I'm sure this isn't going to wrap up any time soon. We'll continue to follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Some very expensive mansions have sustained heavy damage from a wildfire in southern California. National correspondent William La Jeunesse has the latest from Laguna Niguel. Hello, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Shannon, this house was for sale, seven bedrooms, $10 million. And yet you can see it is a total loss. Despite being very fire resistant, you have a tile roof -- and yet this fire claimed about 20 homes in this subdivision about halfway between San Diego and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 So the fire broke out about 24 hours ago. Blue skies, 65 degrees, 53 percent humidity, the second week in May. And the -- had the support, the Phos-Chek, tankers, helicopters, engine crews, virtually every other driveway. It was not enough as embers entered open windows and attic vents and destroyed these homes from the inside out.\par \par \uc2 Now, what you are looking at is the back of the house. I want to show you the front of it this morning, and why this fire is not over until the last ember is out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So this house was destroyed last night. Firefighters tried to put it out to stop the embers from catching other homes next door on fire. This house appeared to have been saved, but as you can see, the fire has reignited about 12 hours later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So there are about several hundred firefighters here now mopping up and making sure that this fire does not reignite and we have a repeat of what we had yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Now, from here, you can see the ocean, right? That's where the fire began, down there, raced up this hill, driven by about 30 mile an hour winds. The embers shot over this ridge and into the backyards you see over there.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have seen a lot of insurance adjustors here today counting their losses. This is a state where many companies have already stopped underwriting, and they have pulled out. So Shannon, you have to consider this a warning, right? So today we are told this is probably emblematic of what is going to be a very long fire season. In fact, January to April was the driest four months on record in California. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Stunning images. William, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden marking the death of a million Americans in the coronavirus pandemic. The president calling it a tragic milestone. He is urging Congress to sustain funding for additional testing, vaccines, and treatments. The move comes as the White House hosts a global COVID summit.\par \par \uc2 Up next, something you probably have never seen before, a look at this super massive black hole at the center of our galaxy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We are getting our first look tonight at the super massive black hole at the center of our galaxy. It comes from a collection of telescopes around the world.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexis McAdams has the picture tonight. Good evening, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon. That's right, and we're going to show you that picture in a second. Now, scientists tell us tonight that this black hole is 4 million times bigger than the sun. Take a look here on your screen. This is the first look that we just got today of this super massive black hole. It's at the center of earth's Milky Way galaxy. Here you can see that glowing ring of radiation surrounding that darker dish. That's actually the edge of the black hole.\par \par \uc2 The super massive black hole is also called Sagittarius A. The collapsed star is located about 27,000 light years, or 159 quadrillion miles away from earth. So that far away it's practically invisible to optical telescopes. So this collaboration used eight different telescopes spread across the world collecting data from Antarctica to Hawaii. Black holes are extremely dense and have such strong gravity that not even light can escape, dragging in everything from stars to gas to planets. But this black hole taking in less than expected. Researchers calling it a gentle giant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First, we see that only a trickle of material is actually making it all the way to the black hole. If the star were a person, it would consume a single grain of rice every million years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: It's the second black hole ever to be imaged by telescope. Back in 2019, the same group captured this historic but more distant image from a galaxy far, far away. And Shannon, astronomers believe that every galaxy including our own has a black hole at its center, and they're working, as they always are, every single day to better their technology and their telescopes to get a closer and clearer image of these black holes. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I can't even get my mind around those numbers. Alexis, thank you so much for that report.\par \par \uc2 So my colleague Bret Baier recently sat down with former national 