r right now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Kim Strassel, and Jeff Mason on more bad inflation news, and the White House COVID summit.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We inherited an economy on the brink of a great depression. Millions of people losing their jobs, losing their homes, but even more importantly, losing hope.\par \par \uc2 There's more than one way to deal with the impact on a family than what we are doing, and we are going to beat inflation.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: It is really the number one economic issue that faces the administration and the nation.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MCHENRY\uc2 , (R-NC) HOUSE FINANCIAL SERVICES COMMITTEE: What actions are you taking to relieve the pain of families? We have to ask them, what are you actually doing? What does it mean?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's bring in our panel to talk about that, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," and Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Today, just for kicks, I went up to Gallup's polling that they do constantly, talking about what's the most important issue facing the country. And Kim, not surprising today, 35 percent of the respondents had the economy at the top of their list. The administration is scrambling on this no matter what they try it seems at this point.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, the Democrats spent the last week talking endlessly about abortion, clearly hoping to change focus and get Americans' minds on something else. But those polls show that this is a pervasive problem now for them. What's even more worrisome is if you look at the numbers this week, whether it really suggests is that we have got soaring demand, too few workers, and that we're now in what is called a wage-price cycle where companies have to spend more to get workers to stay or come on. They then hike their prices. Then workers demand yet higher wages, and up we go.\par \par \uc2 And if that's the case, the White House is really going to struggle to do anything about this. And then add in that most of what it is doing in that it's cutting back on energy supplies and adding regulations that make companies pull back, this is the exact opposite what you need to be doing to help yourself politically and economically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So to the point of energy supplies, I want to play a little back and forth involving Secretary Yellen, and they gets to domestic production.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY BARR\uc2 , (R-KY) HOUSE FINANCIAL SERVICES COMMITTEE: Did \uc2 FSOC\uc2  consider the substantial risk that is actually now materialized that overregulation of the fossil energy sector, ending Keystone, blocking lease sales on federal lands, constraining supply of energy, has actually undermined financial stability, specifically historic inflation and $4.37 a gallon gas?\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: I think we owe that to the failure of our domestic industry to ramp up production to pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 BARR: I don't think that's what they say. I think they say they can't get approval.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Hugh, there is a lot of back and forth on that. There is the White House calling out the oil companies as bad guys, as people who are profiting off of difficult circumstances, and saying that they aren't using domestic production. They are saying this isn't something that happens overnight. We can't get approval for some of these projects, and there are major investments involved. Where do you come down on this?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I come down that Secretary Yellen actually made me draw back and wonder when she said the production companies are not willing to produce. This very day the administration canceled oil and gas leases in Alaska. Last week they withdrew them in the Gulf of Mexico. Of course, we can go back to Keystone. This is an anti- energy administration. And that, of course, as Kimberley just said, increases pressure. Energy markets are forward-looking, and so long as they see ahead of them obstacles from every department, regulatory impediments from every official with a pen and a bureaucratic sense of mission, they are not going to produce. I just can't actually believe that \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said it's the producers' fault when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is throwing every obstacle in the way he can of lowering gas prices.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Jeff, and it's not just conservatives who have critiques for this administration. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," not a right-leaning publication, says this, "For much of the last year the Biden administration wrongly told the American public that rising prices would be short-lived. When it became clear that inflation would not come down on its own, the White House began a blame game. One of its favorite talking points is to pin inflation on greedy corporations for hiking prices too much. That just doesn't add up." That is from the he editorial board, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "REUTERS": Well, and they are also citing the impact of the war in Ukraine and the ongoing impact of the ongoing pandemic. But "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is certainly right to say that the administration last year said that inflation would be transitory. It did not turn out to be that way. I, in fact, asked President Biden a question about that earlier this week after his speech about inflation. I said, you initially thought this was going to be transitory. That's not the case. What is your projection now? And he didn't want to give a projection, and that's probably because they just don't know.\par \par \uc2 They want it to come down obviously. It is not only an economic liability for this president. It is also a political liability going into the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk COVID. The White House was part of this global COVID conversation today. Here is what the president said about where we are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There is still so much to do. This pandemic isn't over. And with thousands still dying every day, now is the time for us to act, all of us together. We all must do more.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have called upon the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  for $22.5 billion in additional emergency funding to battle COVID.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let me put up another number here, because this is important. while they're asking for that funding and saying this is not over yet, the American people who are worried about COVID and our situation, these numbers have steadily dropped. People worried about getting COVID, back in April of 2020 were at 69 percent. We're now at 17 percent. So, Hugh, how do you convince the average American that we need billions more dollars for something that they seem like they may be coming to terms with as part of daily life?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It cannot be done because the American people don't believe it. Those who want to be vaccinated and boosted are vaccinated and boosted and they are traveling, and they are living their ordinary lice. A COVID summit will do nothing except add cliches to the boosters.\par \par \uc2 They are trying to turn the page away from the economy of the United States. The American people are over COVID, and they are not going to go back to COVID. And I think the administration knows that, but they have got to do something with the president that doesn't let him wander around a microphone in hand in an audience making gaffes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Kim, it is one of the few topics that in all of the polling he is not upside down. He is on immigration, the border, Russia, the economy. But he is starting to make gains on COVID, that the American people are gaining confidence in his ability to handle this.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Well, that seems to be mostly a function, though, of the fact that they are not caring about it as much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Which is your point. That seems to be the main point of this.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, I think one of the problems the administration has here is that, to the extent it focuses on COVID, it does it in a way that tends to really annoy a lot of Americans in that it's all focused on mask mandates on airplanes, other things designed to lock people up, take away their freedoms.\par \par \uc2 When you look at that polling, what it shows here is Americans now want to live with COVID, and if the administration was smart, it would be continuing its efforts, but on things that have more to do with therapeutics, for instance, tests and other things that allow Americans to manage their day-to-day lives with the pandemic alongside of it rather than a disruption on a day-to-day basis.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to make sure we get to the Supreme Court and the efforts to get additional protections for them. Here is a breakdown of reaction to where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The Garland Justice Department is nowhere in sight. The attorney general was quicker to pounce on concerned parents at school board meetings.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: They put barriers up in order to protect the Supreme Court building. This is what we should do around the justices' homes. I think we can both respect First Amendment rights and protect the sanctity of our justices' home.\par \par \uc2 WINSOME SEARS, (R) VIRGINIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: President Biden, you need to tell your attorney general to go and do his job and protect the citizens of the United States of America. Ultimately, sir, this falls on you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Jeff, there is a federal law that appears to be pretty clear in the wording about what happens, the fact that it's against the law to go picket or parade in front of a jurist's home for the intent of influencing their opinion, their decision, as we await the actual original, official opinion from the Supreme Court. The DOJ is taking a lot of the heat for being willing to step in on a number of other things but not necessarily this. What do you make of what they have said so far?\par \par \uc2 MASON: I think this just represents the fact this is such a divisive issue. And it's focused right now, the Republican side is focused on these protests, the Democratic side is focused on what that decision is likely going to be based on the leaked draft. And it's divisive. And there is going to be pressure on both sides.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, if I can also take one second to go back to the previous discussion about COVID, to Kim's point, the administration is doing that which she said they should be doing, and that's why they are asking for money. The ask may be controversial because it's a big number. But they are asking for billions of dollars so that Americans can have vaccines, can have tests, and can have therapeutics this fall.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we'll leave there on all those topics. Panel, thank you very much for your time.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, SPECIAL REPORT salutes a dramatic rescue at a zoo in Guatemala City. Check out this cell phone video capturing the moment an elephant begins trumpeting repeatedly. It's pointing its trunk down into that pool of water where an antelope was drowning. A zoo worker picked up the queues, quickly dove into the water to help that frantic animal get over the ledge to safety. It took a few tries, but eventually that little antelope breaks free, and that story has a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we talk with Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate David McCormick.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream. See you at midnight eastern, 9:00 pacific for FOX NEWS AT NIGHT. But now, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is next. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. Rob Portman (R-OH); Crypto Crash; Will Finland and Sweden Join NATO?; Interview With Former U.S. Special Master for Executive Compensation \uc2 Kenneth Feinberg\uc2 ; Supreme Court Protests; Gas Prices Rising; Wild Swings on Wall Street\b0\par\par\uc2 Gary Kaltbaum, Bill Melugin, Charlie Gasparino, Jack Keane, Phil  Keating, Mike Emanuel, Jeff Flock, Susan Li, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,856 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Doesn't look too bad by the end of the day, the Dow down about 92.5 points. So, you thought we had stabilized.\par \par \uc2 Well, what if I told you for a while we were down more than 600 points? We swung within a 1,000-point range. Get used to it. We're seeing more of it and more signs that inflation, while off its worst levels, is still increasing at an alarming rate.\par \par \uc2 And we have got your covered. Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And here we go again, the volatile swings we have gotten so used to in this market, where stocks can go up, stocks can go down, and have the type of volatility we would sometimes be lucky to see in a matter of weeks play out in a matter of hours.\par \par \uc2 Technology stocks, though, coming back on the notion here that interest rates are coming down. We're going to be taking a look at that on the market side with what's going on, with a stock market that really doesn't have a handle yet what's going on with are Susan Li, and Jeff Flock following the record-setting trending gas prices day after day after day, another one, by the way, today.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Susan -- Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Still searching for a bottom here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We have a new low for stock markets this year, a sixth straight day of losses for the Dow Jones industrials. The S&P 500 is close to bear market territory, down nearly 20 percent from recent peaks. Now, as a result, \uc2 Apple\uc2  has lost its crown as the world's most valuable company to \uc2 Saudi Aramco\uc2 . That's Saudi Arabia's state-controlled oil company.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just \uc2 Apple\uc2 , you have other mega-cap technology giants. They have also gotten a whole lot cheaper this year. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Tesla\uc2  have both lost over 40 percent from their recent records. Now, Wall Street's concerned that interest rates will need to go up sharply and quickly to bring down record inflation.\par \par \uc2 And that means less money in the economy and less money in stock markets. We had a second key read on inflation this morning showing inflation is still coming in higher than forecast. You had April producer prices up 11 percent from a year ago. And that's still near record levels.\par \par \uc2 So, with investors expecting higher interest rates to come, they're trying to raise cash right now in these uncertain times by selling stocks, bonds, and, yes, even cryptocurrencies. And the explosion in the new digital asset class turn into an implosion overnight, $200 billion in value wiped out in 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And that's because confidence was shaken after a crash in a stablecoin that was worth billions of dollars. Now, stablecoins are used by cryptocurrency traders to move their money across different exchanges to buy and sell different cryptocurrencies.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, I will leave you with this. Remember that infamous \uc2 GameStop\uc2  saga last year? Well, we got a revival today with some confusing moves and spikes in \uc2 GameStop\uc2 , AMC and other WallStreetBets favorites. At its peaks this morning, Neil, we had \uc2 GameStop\uc2  rallying 30 percent, halted several times owing to volatility.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't that remind you of deja vu from last year?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Boy, not for the faint of heart, right? Man, oh, man.\par \par \uc2 Susan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Susan Li following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock following the run-up in gas prices day after day after day. We hit another record. He's in Camden, New Jersey, with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: No volatility here, Neil. They're just going up. Yes.\par \par \uc2 I tell you, you got to get President Biden credit. He knows how to stick to his guns. I will tell you why in just a second. But, first, the gas prices, $4.42. That's the new all-time record, up almost 20 cents in the last week, 30 cents in the last month, $1.40 in the last year.\par \par \uc2 The president, administration canceling a possible lease of acreage in Alaska, offshore leases. It would be the Cook Inlet that was canceled, that despite the high gas prices. Like I said, he promised on the campaign trail he would reduce the dependence on fossil fuels. Well, he's sticking to those guns; 10.9 million acres could have been up for auction.\par \par \uc2 The president canceling it, they say, because of lack of interest. Don't know if that's true. But I would point out that the same reason was given in 2007, 2008 and 2011 under both Republican and Democrat administrations, lack of interest for not going forward there.\par \par \uc2 So what do you do if you don't get more oil? Well, \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 , the prime minister of Italy, told President Biden this week that what they ought to do is get a buyers cartel together, sort of like the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cartel, but only buyers, saying, hey, guys, we're not going to pay any more than $60 a barrel or whatever we decide, and we're not going to buy your oil if you won't sell it for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, what if they don't sell it for that? It has been tried in the past, not particularly successful.\par \par \uc2 What are the other options? Well, how about a windfall profits tax? That's been tried in the past as well, as well as price controls on oil. We talked to \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 , our oil guru, about those possibilities. What's he think?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Windfall profit tax is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 First of all, these companies were losing billions of dollars just a year or so ago. And now, if they go to windfall profits, they're going to going to discourage more investment in oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Worth noting, Neil, that those of us of a certain age remember President Nixon and price controls in the '70s. President Carter came along, decontrolled oil in the '80s in return for a windfall profits tax. Neither of them had a whole lot of success -- sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm way too young to remember that, Jeff. I don't know what you're talking about.\par \par \uc2 All right, my friend, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock following all of that.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is here, if you think gas prices are going up, up, up and away, imagine being a trucker in this environment and what it's costing you to fill up with diesel. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's crazy. We went from $200 to fill up the truck to $600, $650. And the work is just dropping off. There will be no work to be had soon.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People aren't spending money building houses, do auditions, swimming pools, things of that. Nothing.\par \par \uc2 We have already seen the work drop off in two to three weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, the irony of today was \uc2 OPEC\uc2  coming out with a statement, you know, the oil-producing guys, saying this is all going to slow activity down in the world.\par \par \uc2 OPEC\uc2  contributed to that. OPEC Plus countries contributed to that by barely raising production. And now they have come to realize that all of this is adding to world angst and slowing the world economy. Now, they went a little too far, got a little too greedy back in the 1970s. Are they on the verge of doing that all over again? And might they regret being a little bit stingy here?\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Gary Kaltbaum, market watcher extraordinaire. We have also got \uc2 Martin Durbin\uc2  with us, the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Martin, end it with you, begin with you on the impact of these higher energy prices, to say nothing of higher prices in general, on businesses large and small across the country. It's not ending, is it?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN DURBIN\uc2 , \uc2 U.S. CHAMBER OF COMMERCE\uc2 : No, and don't expect it to end soon.\par \par \uc2 And it is having an incredible downward -- downward pull on the economy. But things -- there are things that can be done and that the government needs to be doing. Unfortunately, as you mentioned in the lead here, I think the administration just missed an opportunity by canceling new lease sales, both in Alaska and the Gulf of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 But it's -- no question. In addition to the world trying to wean itself off of Russian oil, we're seeing inflation as high as it is, this is exactly the wrong signal to be sending to the markets and the producers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, without even getting into the politics part of it, Gary, bad timing, right? Gas, oil prices rocketing. In the middle of that, you say, all this possible leasing and permits we're going to give you, forget about it, not happening. Bad timing, don't you think?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: If we have a bull market in oil prices, if I want them -- to keep them go going higher, I, as an administration would announce, oh, Alaska, there's a million acres, no more lease there.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just -- that's one -- maybe one thing, but it's the message it is sending to every speculator, every trader and every investor on the oil patch. It is telling them, stay long, don't sell, don't go short, and it ends up prices going higher.\par \par \uc2 And to put a little number to it, every dime move up over one year is about $12 to $13 billion out of the pockets of business and consumers. And we have gone up a heck of a lot of dimes over the last year. And as I have been saying, it is just crushing the consumer. And every day is a bad day that it is not coming down.\par \par \uc2 And I do not think an economy can stand too many more weeks, let alone months, of $104 oil. And you're seeing it in droves as you drive past every gas station right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you raise a good point.\par \par \uc2 And, Martin, I had -- I spoke earlier with \uc2 Terry Duffy\uc2 . the guy who runs the \uc2 CME\uc2 , where they trade all sorts of these commodities, including oil futures and the rest.\par \par \uc2 And he took great exception to the administration fingering the very companies that are trying to get through this for this. I want you to react to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering how you feel about the way some on Capitol Hill have labeled this, that it's not just supply and demand, there's a cabal going on, cornering some markets, particularly when it comes to oil and foodstuffs? What do you say?\par \par \uc2 TERRY DUFFY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, \uc2 CME GROUP\uc2 : Well, here, I will say a couple of things. And I'm very respectful of the institution we call Washington and the office of the president of the United States, but you can't have it always.\par \par \uc2 You cannot give a speech and blame every price increase on Putin out of Russia, and then, in the next thing, you're saying that companies are price gouging. At the same time, we have blockages of supply all over the world. And we have massive demand.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't know how that's price gouging, when you look at what some of these factors are. So I think it's a bit of a political message that's going out from the administration right now. And I think there needs to be more clarity around it. When the president talks about there's only four meat-packers in the United States, he's correct. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Because the rules are so prescriptive to get into that business, government-related, that nobody wants to be in it anymore. It's just not cost-effective to go into these businesses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Martin, if you think about that, what he's saying is something you touched on at the outset here, that you guys, by extension, business in general, have become the bad guys.\par \par \uc2 If not for you, we'd have a much more manageable situation. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I agree. That's a political answer and avoids the real issue, and it avoids the solutions that are there.\par \par \uc2 And we know that the Fed is dealing with the demand side through interest rate hikes. What the administration and Congress could do is deal with the supply side, and that's through energy policy, through trade policy, and really focusing on the things that they can have -- the supply chain, for example, dealing with the supply chain issues.\par \par \uc2 That's what's going to help us long term. And that's the real crux here. These are long-term issues that the administration needs to be playing, long game. This is a long game. And I think this was -- they're showing a lack of imagination, and just missing the opportunity to really set a course here to help the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's making it very hard to get anything bipartisan done in an environment where the finger-pointing has gotten to the point where they're throwing things now.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, I want to thank you both very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Again, the Dow down about 103 points.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we're focusing on other dust-ups, including those protests outside justices' homes that are just starting to heat up. So why is the Justice Department not cracking down?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): The statute is incredibly clear.\par \par \uc2 It basically says, if you are parading or picketing in order to try to influence a judge, then it's punishable with up to a year in prison. That sounds illegal to me. And I just ask the attorney general to enforce the law that's on the books.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, Virginia Governor Youngkin isn't convinced the attorney general is interested in doing that right now.\par \par \uc2 The protests continue, and the justices in their neighborhoods they live on, they continue to have to deal with this day in, day out.\par \par \uc2 Mike Emanuel on where this all stands right now -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is calling on Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to do more, making the case Garland, a former federal judge, should clearly understand the issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Now far left crowds are surrounding justices' private family homes. They want to use intimidation to influence the outcome in a pending case.\par \par \uc2 It should be easy for leaders to condemn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The nine Supreme Court justices met today behind closed doors for the first time since the draft opinion of the abortion case leaked.\par \par \uc2 House Majority Leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  told reporters justices and their families need to be protected, noting: "We're a nation of laws, not of violence or intimidation."\par \par \uc2 The Senate Democratic whip has also been vocal about the protests against these justices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICHARD DURBIN\uc2  (D-IL): I think it's reprehensible. Stay away from the homes and the families of elected officials and members of the court. If we want to bring women and men into this position and accept the responsibility and sometimes the controversy, we have to have reasonable lines drawn to respect their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  and Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  have suggested the protests are OK, as long as they are peaceful.\par \par \uc2 Virginia's attorney general says the protests are a clear violation of federal statute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON MIYARES\uc2  (R), VIRGINIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: They're shutting down private residence, they're shutting down roadways, and they're trying to intimidate witnesses -- intimidate Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: In addition to pressing Attorney General Garland to take action, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin is asking Fairfax County authorities to extend security perimeters around the homes of three justices who live there -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I was thinking too, Michael, forget about the justices who live there. What about all the neighbors and putting up with this commotion just trying to get to and from their homes there?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Yes, no doubt about that. That's got to be an aggravation if you live in the same neighborhood, trying to get to and from your home, trying to get about your daily lives.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, a lot of concern about these justices. And I think you see veteran Democrats like Durbin and Hoyer, who've been around long enough to know that, if the shoe were on the other foot, they would not be pleased. And so they're calling for calm and basically enforcement of this federal statute.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike Emanuel in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Mike. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime here, remember that Florida condo collapse in Surfside, Florida? Ninety-eight people lost their lives, a huge settlement announced today, to the tune of close to a billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 How is that money going to be disbursed?\par \par \uc2 We will ask Ken Feinberg. He's been a key player in many such disbursements going back to 9/11 and much more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: See this image? It could be our future, the very center of the Milky Way galaxy, a black hole sucking in thousands, millions of planets and stars in its path, 27,000 light years away -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL KEATON, ACTOR: And their families will be compensated based on economic value of loss.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED ACTRESS: You're a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 KEATON: And that's exactly what these people need right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was a great movie, Michael Keaton playing the role of Ken Feinberg, the man at the center of dispensing funds to 9/11 victims' families and those associated with them.\par \par \uc2 That was not based on everything being equally divided. It was an unprecedented way to handle that. And it comes at a time right now about a billion dollars have been set up for the victims or the families of victims of that Surfside, Florida, collapse and how that will be disbursed.\par \par \uc2 Ken Feinberg coming up on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, want to go to Phil Keating on the significance of this almost a year after that tragedy -- sir.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 It is very significant. And, also, it happened, it materialized a lot faster than everybody involved thought this would. We're not even a full year since the condo tower behind me, which is no longer there -- it's now a blue sky void -- collapsed in the middle of the night while everybody involved, 98 people, plummeted to their death.\par \par \uc2 And then the half of the building that didn't collapse. well, those people will also, all of them, the survivors and the families, will be splitting up this $1 billion settlement here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Remember, these people died in their sleep, probably waking up for a few seconds in absolute terror. This financial agreement was reached late yesterday afternoon. This is that surveillance video from the building that was right next door, capturing the collapse.\par \par \uc2 This tentative settlement is the result of a class-action lawsuit against the developers of the Champlain Towers South, the insurers, and the building next door.\par \par \uc2 The investigation concluded the building was structurally unsafe, leading to its collapse. The horrific disaster happened nearly a year ago in late June. Engineers who were hired to assess and investigate the structural flaws in that building prior to the collapse, well, they're also part of the class-action settlement.\par \par \uc2 After the deadly collapse, it became known that the building's condo association board, as well as its residents, all knew that major structural and expensive improvements were needed. But the board kept kicking the can down the curb, because the condo owners simply did not want to buck up to $50,000 to $100,000 each needed to do it.\par \par \uc2 Not yet clear is how much each individual unit owner will get from this somewhat unexpected billion-dollar payout. Technically, it's a $997 million payout. But when it's finalized this fall, it's expected to be at least $1 billion, maybe a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Miami-Dade County, as well as the Surfside mayor and some commissioners, were here today to dedicate the banner that's on the construction fence behind me, dedicated to the 98 souls who perished there last June.\par \par \uc2 They had all lamented the fact that, in this most recent legislative session in Tallahassee, the Republican-controlled House and Senate failed to do anything to change the situation for Florida, didn't change any regulations, inspection schedules, or laws for condo towers, which millions of people live in all up and down both sides of the state.\par \par \uc2 But that could happen at a later date -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you don't want to wait for another tragedy for that.\par \par \uc2 Phil Keating in Surfside, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Phil, very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Ken Feinberg right now. Of course, you remember the name. Of course, you remember the man and Michael Keaton, who played him in a very, very good movie chronicling what he had to go through in just dealing with the 9/11 Compensation Fund and making sure everyone was treated equally and accordingly. But, of course, the disbursements of those funds were based on other factors.\par \par \uc2 So, that wasn't even-stevens for everyone. He has since handled, and even before that, a number of other compensation issues across the board.\par \par \uc2 Ken Feinberg with us now.\par \par \uc2 Ken, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 This is a unique case, in and of itself, I mean, for the families and the loved ones of these 98 victims. And I know it goes to condo owners who were not hurt in this collapse. The issue becomes how to disburse those funds. How do you think?\par \par \uc2 KENNETH FEINBERG\uc2 , VICTIM COMPENSATION ATTORNEY: That's right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are two ways you do this. I wasn't involved in this settlement. It's a very significant, important settlement. You could say simply, 98 lost loved ones, divide it into $997 million, equals the same amount for every individual claimant. That's simple. It's straightforward. It will avoid a lot of the divisiveness that you see in a 9/11 Fund or a fund where the stockbroker or the banker's family gets more than the waiter or the busboy or the soldier.\par \par \uc2 So, one way you could allocate this money is simple. Everybody, all lives are equal. If not, you're going to do maybe, perhaps, a 9/11-type tort distribution. The banker's family will get more than the waiter's family. The banker earned more, so the waiter earned less.\par \par \uc2 And you get into this divisive, sometimes, methodology as to dividing up money based on economic harm. That is a perfectly acceptable way of doing things. But it's much more divisive and costly, while some administrator does the math of who gets what.\par \par \uc2 The second issue -- don't ignore the second issue, Neil. You say the loved ones and the families will receive these funds. Who? Who in the family receives these funds? As you know from 9/11 days, the spouse is fighting with the divorced spouse, and the kid doesn't like the brother, and the sister is upset. And the cousin says: I should get the money.\par \par \uc2 If you're distributing millions of dollars to individual next of kin, make sure you have a method, looking at the estate law of Florida, for example. Who gets the funds once they're ready to be distributed? Those are the two big issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And what about the issue of those -- 98 people died out, but the entire building was destroyed and everyone lost their home.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you start dealing with that?\par \par \uc2 FEINBERG: My understanding is, there was $83 million, approximately, set aside all ready to settle property damage claims.\par \par \uc2 I don't think -- if I read correctly what's in the newspapers and what you and others have reported, the $997 million is reserved for death...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 FEINBERG: ... and perhaps -- I don't know this -- physical injury.\par \par \uc2 But the property damage to the various condominiums, I think that's a separate fund with a separate allocation, not involved in the announcement yesterday, if I read it correctly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So when you were dealing with the 9/11 one -- and, of course, you had the Horizon oil spill and others.\par \par \uc2 I mean, how is that decided? I mean, you had various compensation funds set up. This one was a little more challenging, in your case, dealing with the victims of the World Trade Center, who were all over the map, from cooks to top financial brokers and all.\par \par \uc2 That had to tick some people off. Why is this guy and his family getting so much more than my family? How did you deal with that.\par \par \uc2 FEINBERG: Neil -- Neil, you know you -- we have talked about this.\par \par \uc2 In every one of these compensation programs, 9/11, \uc2 BP\uc2 , Surfside, the bombings on Patriots' Day a few years ago in Boston, the bombing of the marathon runners...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 FEINBERG: ... in every case, guaranteed emotional harm, anger, frustration about life's unfairness. Why my brother? Why my wife? Why my father, Mr. Feinberg?\par \par \uc2 Guaranteed one of the most important aspects of any of these compensation programs is dealing with the emotion. And you better give everybody the opportunity to be heard. If you're a claimant and you want to vent about life's unfairness or validate the memory of a lost loved one, let them come in. Give them an opportunity. Let them vent.\par \par \uc2 It's all part of a very important healing process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it had to be very draining for you.\par \par \uc2 But, Ken Feinberg, thank you very, very much. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 FEINBERG: Always. Thanks, Neil. Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, the war in Ukraine and now Finland's move to say, yes, we want in on \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Maybe Sweden could follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Easier said than done -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Finland wants to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and Sweden looking very much doing the same, matter -- maybe in just a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 We support that. Other \uc2 NATO\uc2  nations support that. The only guy who doesn't support it is this fellow \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 General Jack Keane, get his read on all this.\par \par \uc2 General, as you know, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has threatened Finland and Sweden if they even entertain this. Well, hell hath no fury. What do you think he does?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well, certainly,