2 I mean, before the invasion, a little over 20 percent of the people wanted to be a part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and now it's an excess of 80 percent, just a few months later. The country has gone through a complete reversal, I mean, as has the world writ large, in looking at Russia's aggressiveness and their behavior, I mean, invading an independent, sovereign nation, and doing it in such brutal, barbaric fashion that we haven't seen in decades, in terms of genocide and atrocities.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, the world has looked at Russia as an international pariah. And people close to Russia want the protection of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And I think it's clearly understood why.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, General, I want to get your thoughts on maybe what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is thinking of doing here before pulling out, if he ever considers pulling out of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And that is annexing the lands that he's gotten already, and claiming they're his, sort of like what he did with Crimea, but on a larger scale, I can't imagine the Ukrainians saying, all right, that's fine, as long as you go.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, I think that's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 We are tracking the placement of Russian civil administration. People who are normally used to running local governments and administrating to those governments are moving into Mariupol and also moving into Kherson. And I suspect, as opposed to declaring them independent republics, like he did with Donetsk and Luhansk in the Donbass...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: ... he's going to annex them like he has done with Crimea, so they're part of Russia.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, what he's really looking at is owning the entire southern coast of Ukraine. And this -- despite the failures that Russia is undergoing, this would still be a significant gain for Russia, to occupy that southern coast and deny Ukraine access to the sea.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I hope this administration and \uc2 NATO\uc2  are considering is -- before the invasion, Neil, the United States made a serious strategic mistake, in my view, and they asked our maritime naval fleet to leave the Black Sea. Why? Because we didn't want to provoke Putin into an invasion.\par \par \uc2 And I think what needs to happen is, we should begin to escort the cargo ships that need to access the Port of Odessa, which is underneath the control of the Ukrainians, so that they can import and export their goods. To completely shut them down as the Russians are doing is going to be economically devastating to the Ukrainians, because they're so dependent on their exports.\par \par \uc2 As you know, they have a global export market for the agricultural goods, particularly wheat and grain. And the Russians are blockading that. And I think we should escort the cargo ships in and out, much as Reagan and Trump did in the Persian Gulf when the Iranians were causing their mischief.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Controversial, to be sure, for the administration, but I think we have got to step up and help them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, well, they are, that is, Ukraine, the breadbasket for pretty much Europe and a good chunk of the world. So that would be an urgent need, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very, very much. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime, you own anything crypto, Bitcoin, Ethereum, all these other things?\par \par \uc2 Well, chances are you bought it through an exchange called \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 . Will, \uc2 Coinbase\uc2  is in a world of hurt. In fact, a lot of people think it's going to go bankrupt. And if you have gotten any of this stuff through \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , you could be screwed -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Wall Street landscape has so dramatically changed in just a matter of days, \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  filing for Chapter 11 bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The Dow plunging 504 points. That is the biggest one-day point drop since the market reopened after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's a string of people losing their jobs coming out of Lehman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It was the great meltdown. And it kept melting.\par \par \uc2 And the concern was that brokerage houses and exchanges weren't up to the fight to control it or get a handle on it. That was then.\par \par \uc2 Could something like that ever happen again, like even now? With the rocky performance of \uc2 Coinbase\uc2 , the popular exchange used for trading a lot of very -- once very hot and popular crypto investments, including Bitcoin, Ethereum, and a host of others, growing worries that, if it goes down, all those crypto investors who used it to buy this stuff go down as well.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, what do you make of that? Could it happen again?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I remember being outside of Lehman when they were carrying those boxes out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It was a scary time.\par \par \uc2 Here's the thing. What led to the financial crisis, when Lehman imploded, and to a certain extent when Bear imploded first back in March of 2008.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2 , right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But then it was Lehman which touched off the whole thing -- was the notion that every bank on the street had on its balance sheet these toxic assets that were being written down. These are toxic mortgage-based assets.\par \par \uc2 Given the housing crisis, everything had to be marked down. And that meant the banks were insolvent. I don't think we're there yet. But here's where we get kind of close, at least if you believe this theory, and this is just a theory -- I'm not endorsing it -- is that retail right now is so embedded in crypto, that