t only harmed China, but also the U.S. as well as the world."\par \par \uc2 Well, Senator, unfortunately, it looks like Biden and his team, at least the globalists on his team already appear to have bought this logic.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: Laura, you gave some very good and alarming examples that we've seen over the last two years of our very dangerous dependence on China. This IV example is just the latest one, or the active pharmaceutical ingredients we saw early in the pandemic, are simply basic medical and surgical equipment.\par \par \uc2 And that's to say nothing of advanced electronics and technology. He used a very important word as well, decoupling. We need to decouple our economy in strategic and vital sectors from the Chinese economy. We cannot be dependent on the goods that feed us and clothe us and provide us defense and literally save our lives from a Communist Chinese regime that wants to replace us as world's dominant power. It was a mistake to let happen over the last 30 years. We have to unwind it as quickly as we can.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And, Senator, today we learned as all this is bubbling up about the tariffs, removing the tariffs, make it easier for companies to offshore manufacturing and jobs overseas. That same day all this was bubbling up, we learned that the overdose deaths in the United States with a highest on record an overwhelming majority due to fentanyl is, what, it's 70 percent or so. Listen to the DEA administrator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE MILGRAM, ADMINISTRATOR OF DEA: We are so focused right now on stopping the chemicals coming out of the Chinese chemical companies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: China's to blame?\par \par \uc2 MILGRAM: Chinese chemical companies are the largest producer right now of the precursor chemicals that are being used to make fentanyl. There's a relationship between these Chinese chemical companies and the criminal cartels in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You think? So, Senator, China is killing Americans with this fentanyl. And we're going to reward them by lifting the tariffs? Can't make this up.\par \par \uc2 COTTON: There is a direct relationship between the Chinese pharmaceutical industries and these cartels in Mexico. In the past, they've often produced excess supplies and shipped them to the cartels.\par \par \uc2 In more recent years, they've helped the cartels set up their own laboratories, provided them some of the precursors as well. But the upshot of it is, because of Chinese and Mexican drug cartel cooperation, flooding our country with this deadly synthetic drug fentanyl, we have once again set a sad, sad record in overdose deaths in this country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Senator, we really appreciate your speaking out on this issue. And there are few others like you in the Senate. Not enough, we need more. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And tonight only nine days after that draft opinion reversing Roe was leaked by someone or some people at the court, there's been yet another leak.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  tonight describing the situation at the court as a series of disclosures that appear to be happening in almost real-time. Now, they started in an editorial on April 26 in the Wall Street Journal. It expressed concern that Chief Justice Roberts was trying to persuade justices Kavanaugh and Amy Coney Barrett to join him in upholding a Mississippi law, banning most abortions after 15 weeks, but to stop short of overruling Roe outright.\par \par \uc2 "Our guess", the editorial said, "was that Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  would be assigned the majority opinion if the chief did not gain an ally". Good guess. Of course, then the Politico bombshell came just six days after that, where five justices voted to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 And now, another unauthorized disclosure. According to Politico, "None of the conservative justices who initially sided with Alito have to-date