hat they believe is wrong, what they're trying to do is wrong. And we have - that's why I wrote the book on Defeating Big Government Socialism.\par \par \uc2 We have to win the case, that the very base of the Democratic Party is committed to ideas that don't work in the real world. Then, second, we have to be prepared to govern. Not in '25, we have to be prepared to govern in January of 2023. And I think that it's very important.\par \par \uc2 When we took over in '94, we had a governing agenda and we moved it. It was popular enough, by the way, that on the average vote on the key issues, 63 Democrats voted with us. Because back home, people said to them, You got to be for welfare reform; you got to be for the tax cut; you got to be for a balanced budget.\par \par \uc2 And I think if we do it right next year, we can set a pattern, which you see with the Republican governors. I mean, overall, the Republican governors have done a phenomenal job of creating jobs, solving problems, reforming the schools, doing things that really matter to people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. And they didn't wait--\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: House and senate Republicans should do the same.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Newt, to your point about this - the radicalism point and the Democrat party. It's a different Democrat party than when you were there, very different.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  - let me just say this and I'll let you respond, 49 Democrat senators went on the record supporting not just Roe v Wade being codified, no, no, no. Abortion up until birth and removing parental consent for abortions. So much for safe, legal and rare. This is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: I don't know what's happened. I don't know what's happened and I don't know how the Democrats who are up for re election this year, can possibly go home, if their opponents simply outlines what's in the bill they just voted for. Because you're right, it's the most extreme - Ben Domenech called it, the party of death. And I think that that's a fair statement.\par \par \uc2 These people voted today for the most radical position on abortion anywhere on the planet. And when you look at the details of what they voted for, it wasn't Roe v Wade, it was radically more anti-life, and radically more hostile and would basically create an engine of killing babies.\par \par \uc2 And I don't know how these 49 senators are going to go home and defend it, if people have the nerve to take them head on. And don't get involved in some big broad generalization. Take item by item what's in that bill. And I think people will be appalled that their senators voted for this kind of radical position to make it easy to kill babies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was out and about today, Newt. And she made this comment after the abortion vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue. For all who care about this issue, a priority should be to elect pro-choice leaders at the local, the state, and the federal level. Because what we are seeing around this country are extremist Republican leaders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Extremist Republicans wanting to protect pretty unborn babies that could be born and delivered outside the womb and survive and go on to do things, like, I don't know, maybe cure cancer one day. But not according to Kamala.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: She'd never - she would never accept it. And I understand as a sitting vice president why she wouldn't. But I'd be happy to debate her. I think, any Republican senator or House member would be happy to debate her. What she just said is total nonsense.\par \par \uc2 This is the most radical bill ever voted on in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  about abortion. If she wants that to be the position they fight over, that's fine.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, it's great.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Because they're going to be down - they'll be at 23 percent. I mean, it will be amazing. But you see this everywhere. As you pointed out early on, you have this very real problem in terms of baby formula to entirely artificial, it's the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , which is blocking, for example, the use of European baby formulas. So it's creating an artificial shortage.\par \par \uc2 You have the whole problem of people coming across the border in huge numbers. You have the problem of murders, rape, carjackings, robbery in the cities. Every time you turn around, these people are failing now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They're destroying America day-by-day.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: I think, we have a chance in November to really truly turn the tide.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And, Newt, your point, to educate the next generation why this can't continue and what happens if it does, that has got to sink into every Republican. You can't wait to get the White House to educate America. You have go to start doing it now.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: That's right.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And that means pushing policy. Newt Gingrich is the best on this. Newt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have a FOX News alert tonight. Some big Second Amendment news. Out of the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals in California, FOX's Trace Gallagher is in our west coast newsroom, has all the details. Trace, what's up?\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Laura. The Ninth Circuit court was pretty blunt about this, saying California's ban on the sale of semiautomatic weapons to adults under 21 is unconstitutional, a violation of the Second Amendment. The court said the law was, quote here, "An almost total ban on semiautomatic centerfire rifles for young adults." Judge Ryan Nelson then wrote, quoting again, "America would not exist without the heroism of the young adults who fought and died in our revolutionary army. Today we reaffirm that our Constitution still protects the right that enabled their sacrifice, the right of young adults to keep and bear arms."\par \par \uc2 The Firearms Policy Coalition which brought the lawsuit said it's optimistic this ruling will help overturn other age-based gun bans around the country. We should note it was not a total victory for gun rights activists. The lawsuit also tried to block a separate California ban that says adults under 21 who are not in the military or law enforcement must obtain a hunting license before they can purchase a rifle or a shotgun. That ban was put in place back in 2018 in the wake of several school shootings, including at \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  in Florida. Today, the Ninth Circuit said the hunting license requirement was reasonable for increasing public safety.\par \par \uc2 We should also note, the Ninth Circuit has traditionally been among the country's most liberal courts, and just a few years ago today's ruling would have been very unlikely. But the majority opinion of this three-judge panel came from two judges nominated by former President Trump. The dissenting judge was a Bill Clinton nominee. Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Trace, that is fascinating. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 OK, here's the clue for tonight's "Wit's End" segment. Remember, the answer will be revealed at the end of the show. North Korea just banned which western aesthetics? Tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle. We're going to see if you get it right.\par \par \uc2 But up next, a pop star and a Broadway diva expose more than anyone expected, and Biden does as well. "Seen and Unseen," Raymond Arroyo, is next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment tonight where we explore the big cultural stories of the day. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor, Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, we start on Broadway where a diva seems to have lost some of her star quality.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. Following a performance of "Company," Laura, Patti Lupone during a Q and A session with the audience singled out a patron, and she sang a new song that could be called don't cry for me, Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LUPONE, ACTRESS: Put your mouth over your nose. That's why you're in the theater. That's the rule. If you don't want to follow the rule, get the -- out.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 LUPONE: I'm serious. You're sick. You are. You do not respect the people that are sitting around you. You don't pay my salary. Chris Harper pays my salary. Who do you think you are?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The question is, who does she think she is? Look, Patti Lupone, Laura, is one of the greatest performers on Broadway. She is a powerhouse, you and I have both seen her. No entertainer, however, should hector or attack a member of the audience who paid good money to see you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Other than Don Rickles.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The question was wearing a mask, she was screamed at by an actress on stage filled with people not wearing masks, incidentally. This is no way to handle your audience who's come to see you because they love what you do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How much is a ticket to a company? Which originally was produced when, Raymond, 1971?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Like 1974, yes. But this is a revival. People are paying $150 a seat to hear Patti Lupone sing exactly one solo. So at those prices she should be kissing their feet and giving out free Patti Lupone masks out at the door. But at a time, Laura, when people are going to stadiums and churches and restaurants without a mask, I was at a Broadway show a few weeks ago. Everybody had two drinks when they sat down. You want to know why? So they could take the masks off and sip them throughout the performance and get around the mask mandate.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So I don't know who she's doing this for, but it's a bad look for a star like this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's off key for a performer who usually is beautifully on key. All right, what else, Ray?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Theater etiquette, which she's usually a stickler for, this exceeds the bounds.\par \par \uc2 Another star who could benefit, I think, from being unseen is the material girl. Madonna has unveiled a series of NFTs, Laura, those nonfungible tokens, of herself, at least she claims it's herself. The images, which took her a year to create, are so graphic we can't show them here. But Madonna's explanation is probably all you need.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADONNA, SINGER: The opening of each video is essentially me giving birth, whether I'm sitting on a tank in a postapocalyptic city, or I'm in a hospital bed in a very sterile laboratory environment. I showed them. Are they completely weirded out by it? Not often a robot centipede crawls out of my vagina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Papa don't preach.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I almost don't know what to say here, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mama don't preach.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She might have to change that to I might be your yucky star, because you're the yuckiest by far. I have very to add, except she's crossed the border line of relevance and decency, I think.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: God, girls just want to have fun, that was Cyndi Lauper, but girls just want to have NFTs now, I guess. I don't know. Raymond, maybe we need to create an NFT of this, I guess. What's next? Biden almost took a spill boarding Air Force One this morning? No. He did not.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I don't know if you get many takers on that NFT. But Laura, his feet may have avoided disaster, but his tongue sure didn't.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Is Delaware in the house? Anybody from Delaware? Holler loudly man, because I want to see you after this is over.\par \par \uc2 I've been to -- I didn't want to make you stand. I'm sorry. But the whole point of my raising this is, and we're not going to be able to talk much about it today, but conservation.\par \par \uc2 We have no plan, they have no plan to bring down energy prices today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I would make the argument that Biden speaks more truth in his stumbles than when he actually reads the copy cleanly, Laura. And as you were talking about earlier, the president is now pushing to lift those tariffs on China. In an underreported story, Cardinal Joseph Zen of Hong Kong, a tireless voice for freedom and religious rights, he was arrested by the CCP this morning. While, the White House called for his release, Biden said nothing. Ditto for the Pope, which is really distressing. The Vatican, which made a deal with China, the government, permitting Xi to pick bishops, in a statement said they were watching the situation with extreme attention.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How powerful.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Thankfully -- yes. Thankfully Cardinal Zen was released on bail tonight, but they took his passport, and they charge he's colluded with foreign governments due to his support of democracy advocates in Hong Kong. By the way, a new chief executive in Hong Kong was elected. This almost no coverage anywhere.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He wasn't elected.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He got 99 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He wasn't elected. He was placed into power by Xi.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Listen, he got 99 percent of the vote, he was backed by Beijing, and he was the only candidate in the race. But that's the way they run elections Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's a committee.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, and it's another reason to keep the tariffs in place. You can't have this subornation of human rights and religious rights and the rounding up of a saintly man like Cardinal Zen. And surely he'll face --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No, Raymond, Hong Kong freedom -- I'm sorry I'm stepping on you tonight. Hong Kong freedom is gone, crushed. And Pope Francis, God bless him, we need strength from the pontiff. What would JP II do? He'd just say we're watching this. We're going to watch it evolve as he's tortured behind bars, released but tortured in real life. Raymond, this is very disturbing. Thank you for the Patti Lupone shut up and sing update.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Up next, the hottest political race in the country is a Republican primary for the Senate in Pennsylvania. Now, last night, we brought on the surging Kathy Barnette. Tonight, we welcome another top tier challenger, David McCormick. He's here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary is right now the hottest political race in America. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is doing its part to make sure the nearly 26 percent of voters who say they're still undecided less than a week before the election get the chance to see the candidates for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Last night in our Retaking America series, we profiled Kathy Barnette, and boy is the GOP oppo on her flowing. Then there's Dr. Oz, we have our issues with him, but he has an open invitation to come on this show as well.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, we turn to \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , who always finds himself near the top of the heap in Pennsylvania. Dave, great to see you tonight. Why are so many voters, six days out, I know there's already mail-in ballots coming in and there's early voting, but why so many still undecided?\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK\uc2 , (R) PENNSYLVANIA U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, listen, thank you for having me, Laura. I'm traveling across the state meeting every day, and Pennsylvanians are so focused on this. They're angry. They feel like their country is slipping away. And they're zeroed in on the candidates.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's a great thing, and they're looking for someone who can go fight for them in Washington. And they look at me as someone who's battletested, served in the military, grew up in Pennsylvania, created jobs. And I feel momentum on the ground when I do these meetings, and I think you're going to see over the next few days that undecided electorate is really going to begin to zero in on who the believe can lead the fight in Washington. And I believe that will be me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, the Senate has now agreed to lift Trump's tariffs on China. We have a $400 billion deficit with China on trade this year. That's what it's looking like it's going to be. Dave, what about the tariffs? Will you vote, would you support lifting the tariffs on these Chinese goods when they have reneged on their promise to buy $200 billion worth of our agriculture.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: No, I would not support lifting the tariffs. And I would be a leader of a strategy and a posture with China that would even go beyond what President Trump did. And as someone who spent nine years of his life fighting communist ideology, negotiated at the highest levels of our government against China, and then did business around the world, no one has the experience I have to go toe to toe with China.\par \par \uc2 And that's why you have a bunch of strong voices on China like \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , like Ted Cruz, like \uc2 Bob Lighthizer\uc2  who spearheaded President Trump's policies endorsing my campaign. We need somebody in the Senate who really has the experience to go toe to toe with China, and I have that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the criticism of you, Dave, has been that you're in the pocket of China. I know President Trump came hard at you in that rally with Oz last week. And you say to that? Because I know you like Trump. So what gives?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Yes. Listen, I'm a big supporter of President Trump. I've spent my life serving our great country in the military, at the highest levels of government. And even President Trump knows that he asked me to be his deputy defense secretary. So I think my credentials as someone who's strong on China are beyond dispute. And I've done business around the world like President Trump had done in Russia and in China, and that experience is going to make me a much stronger senator.\par \par \uc2 And I know better than anyone that we need to have a strategy that goes toe to toe with China. The economic risks, the national security risks are significant. And we need to have a posture that really brings American leadership to the world and changes our relationship with China, and I can be a leader on that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dave, why do you think Barnette is surging? You and Oz were the two big dogs for the longest time, and then suddenly this woman no one knows much about just suddenly is like in the top three. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, she's getting her day in the sun, but I think what voters are asking themselves, and this race is so important, Laura, you know this better than anybody, but they're trying to find a candidate that, A, has their values, B, can win the general election. This race could not be more important. And then can go to Washington and fight for an America first agenda.\par \par \uc2 And so those are the tests that every candidate has to go through. That's what we're being scrutinized on. And Kathy, I've gotten to know her on the campaign trail. She has a great life story. But she's been tested. Even the last two years she ran for Congress and lost by 20 points. We can't take a risk on this seat.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: We have to win this seat, and we have to go head-to-head with John Fetterman, right, the stakes are so high.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Republicans, they cannot lose this.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: We cannot lose this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You can't take a risk. Now, Dave, just breaking moments ago, Biden's Interior Department, they just announced they are cancelling planned oil and gas lease sales in the Gulf of Mexico and Alaska's Cook Inlet. Very briefly, your response to that? Just broke.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: It's more of the war on energy. It's been a disaster for our country, a disaster for Pennsylvania. It's killed us economically. It's sacrificed our national security, and it's hurt the environment. And it is beyond dispute that the Biden policies have failed. We have to have energy dominance, we can lead the world in energy. It's great for Pennsylvania, it's great for our country. And as the senator from Pennsylvania, this would be my number one agenda item because this is the key to unlocking the potential in Pennsylvania, which is really remarkable.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The steel industry in Pennsylvania, all those workers, they need someone who's going to fight for them. Dave, we're going to be watching this very closely. We really appreciate your joining us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, our "Wit's End" segment is next. Remember, here's the clue. What new aggressive stance has North Korea taken against the west. Hurry up, tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle, we'll see if you get it right, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, what new aggressive stance has North Korea taken against the west? Here are your answers. Jessie tweeted "American movies," ManU wrote "The mullet haircut that I can't grow because I'm bald," and Skipper guessed "Western fashion." Here's the big reveal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everybody's talking about my tight pants. I got my tight pants. I got my tight pants on. Everybody's looking at my tight pants. I got my tight pants. I got my tight pants on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's what they banned. Yes. They love Dennis Rodman, but no tight jeans and dyed hair.\par \par \uc2 "GUTFELD!" next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Inflation Rate Dips But Remains Near 40-Year High; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Acknowledges Frustration Over Soaring Inflation; Senate Blocks Debate On Bill To Codify Roe Versus Wade; House GOP Members Slam Disinformation Governance Board; Chinese Backlash Over State Department Web Site Revision; Price: "We Do Not Support Taiwan Independence"; China Slams United States Over Taiwan Language; Shortages Of Baby Formula Hit States Across U.S.; Illegal Immigrant Arrested For Criminal Trespassing In Texas Sentenced To One Year In Jail; City In Ukraine Previously Occupied By Russia Forces Left Devastated After Their Withdrawal; Senate Votes On Bill To Codify Roe Versus Wade In U.S. Law; Protestors Demonstrate At Houses Of Supreme Court Justices After Leaked Draft Of Decision Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Congressional Republicans Criticize Biden Administration Proposal To Create Disinformation Governance Board\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Jacqui Heinrich, Charles Payne, Brit Hume, Chad  Pergram, David Spunt, Jennifer Griffin, Jonathan Hunt, Trey Yingst, Nate  Foy \par \uc2 7,892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Same awkward where they do on their show 11:00 at night. That's come up on "ONE NATION" Saturday at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: And you know what, everybody should get that book because I'm hooked. Did you see I was in it?\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right but it was really about Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, OK. All right. Well, that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . How are you?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Judge, I want to see you jump out of a perfectly good airplane with a parachute on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: With or without a harness?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: With a harness of course.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, take care.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thanks Judge. Thanks everybody.\par \par \uc2 Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm John Robertson in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, inflation still running rampant through the American economy. The latest numbers show consumer prices in April were 8.3 percent higher than they were a year ago. That number is only slightly below the previous month's figures, which were the highest in more than 40 years. Prices were up two-tenths of a percentage point from March to April.\par \par \uc2 President Biden continues to blame COVID, Russia and the Republicans but not himself or his policies.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, John. Today, the president let up on his rhetoric about ultra-MAGA Republicans at least until he got in front of electrical union workers. And then, he really went off talking about the economy and trying to tie the GOP to all of this.\par \par \uc2 He claimed that a Republican controlled Congress would be worse for your wallet. But earlier today, he sent his deputies to T.V. interviews to tell Americans that he knows how you feel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): April's inflation data is out and it's worse than expected. Although, slightly better than March.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER BOUSHEY\uc2 , COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: You do see the slowdown. So, that is -- that's better than bad news. But I'm a little hesitant to call it good news because we don't know of course where it's going to go in the next few months.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Consumer prices accelerated 8.3 percent in April, down from 8.5 percent the month prior, cooling for the first time in months but still higher than the 8.1 percent Dow Jones economists predicted. Core inflation stripping out, volatile food and energy costs, also higher than expected, rising 6.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: He is acutely aware of these challenges, and that's why he has dispatched his economic team. We are on the case.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President speaking to farmers in Illinois announced new plans to make food more affordable, doubling funding for domestic fertilizer production and expanding eligibility for double cropping insurance and precision farming tools. But he continued to blame Putin for higher prices.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: American farmers understand Putin's war has cut off critical sources of food. Ukraine was the world's largest producer of wheat, and corn and cooking oil.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A new record high for gas prices dealt the president another blow, the national average for a gallon of regular reaching 4.40 up from 2.98 a year ago. The price of diesel even worse. Now, 5.55 a gallon up $2.43 from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): It forces up the price of practically everything that travels on a truck to reach the scene of a store or a supermarket. Inflation on top of inflation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans say the solution is simple.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Turn to Congress and stay -- and say freeze all spending, you've got to budget live within your budget.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But team Biden says more spending on green energy subsidies, elder and child care, affordable housing and electric cars will bring down the cost of living and Republicans in Congress are standing in the way.\par \par \uc2 BOUSHEY: The Republicans don't want to come to the table on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the president just wrapped up his remarks to electrical union workers a few moments ago. He went through a lengthy defense of the American Rescue Plan, which has critics and some economists say exacerbated inflation.\par \par \uc2 The president argued that it helped millions of people put food on the table. But his critics say it cause too many dollars to chase too few goods, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And now everybody is having problems putting food on the table. Jacqui Heinrich for us at the White House. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks took another big tumble today. The Dow down 327, the S&P 500 fell 66, the NASDAQ dropped 373 and change.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume and Fox Business Anchor Charles Payne.\par \par \uc2 You know what, Biden is saying now on the economy and blaming Republican's stance in sharp contrast to what he tweeted out as a candidate back in 2020. Here's what he said on August the 16th.\par \par \uc2 Here's my promise to you, if I'm elected president, I will always choose to unite rather than divide. I'll take responsibility instead of blaming others.\par \par \uc2 Let's take that in two parts. And Charles, let's start with you. First of all, this idea of denying responsibility and laying blame anywhere he can.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: It's heartbreaking. Honestly, I tell you, that's heartbreaking in the way, everyone starts off in this administration. They're interviews is by bragging.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President Biden did it again. I don't understand he's -- they're tone deaf. He's bragging about this and bragging about that and people are suffering.\par \par \uc2 Now, the most recently excuse Ukraine been the breadbasket of Europe is some people watching may be curious, so what does that have to do with our prices?\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you what's hurting us. Natural gas is a key component for fertilizer. When natural gas goes up, the way it's gone up in America, fertilizer prices go up. Guess what else goes up after that? Food prices.\par \par \uc2 Food at home, rose the most -- the most in one year -- in one year since 1980. Think about that for a moment. We saw the fastest pace of food and home prices in 40 years since 1980. He controls that. He can make natural gas prices much lower, but he refuses to do it.\par \par \uc2 You know, not all of it falls on his plate but enough of it that we don't need to have the fastest rate of inflation of any developed nation in the world, we should actually have the slowest.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Charles, I'll get to some of those specific food price spikes in just a moment. But Brit, to you, the second part of that, is this idea of uniting rather than dividing. Let's rewind to yesterday, and what the president said here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know you got to be frustrated. No, I can taste it. But the MAGA Republicans are counting on you to be as frustrated by the pace of progress, which they have everything -- they've done everything they can to slow down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So, Brit, this new idea that the president is floating out there, ultra-MAGA. I remember back in 2016, Hillary Clinton alienated half of America with her so-called basket of deplorables. And I'm wondering why President Biden thinks that that's a good playbook to follow.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think he's trying to rip up the voters in his own party and trying to scare people. Broadly speaking, the MAGA movement as it is associated with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn't terrifically popular with a broad majority of Americans. And it certainly isn't popular with his base.\par \par \uc2 He's obviously has reason to worry about whether his base will be there for him this fall with things going so badly, and people are having such grave doubts about him even from within his own ranks.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's what that is about. I don't think there's any chance it's going to work. You know, he was citing some bill that \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , Florida Republican senator had offered that got practically no Republican support, as if it were the agenda of the Republican Party, which it most certainly is not. I don't think that's going to work either.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, he keeps talking about how we're going to more further spending that was mentioned in the -- in the -- in the package that we just saw.\par \par \uc2 Charles may know of a time when a country was able to spend its way out of an inflationary spiral, but I can't remember one.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, documented inflationary spiral. Let's put up on the screen some of the price increases that we have seen in food. Ground beef up 14.8 percent. Bacon up 17.7. Eggs 22.6. Butter 16. It goes on and on and on. Energy up 30 percent. Fuel oil up 80 percent. Utility gas up 22.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Charles, this is a huge burden that cuts across the entire social strata of America.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Everyone in the country feels it. Every single person. Obviously, some more than others. It's a progressive tax.\par \par \uc2 Back in March, they crunched the numbers, and they said it came out to an average tax of $5,200. Inflation has quickened since then. So, let's probably think it's probably around a tax about $5,500, about $40.00 to $50.00 bucks a month. And Americans are feeling it.\par \par \uc2 It is so debilitating, it is so crushing, but it adds insult to injury when you say, well, this is all about Russia. This is all about ultra-MAGA. This is all about Ukraine being the breadbasket of Europe.\par \par \uc2 No, this is about not unleashing our breadbasket, not unleashing the oil under our feet, putting the climate agenda in front of the American public or front of middle class and poor Americans, it is a despicable thing, and they're going to pay a heavy price for it. But I don't get any satisfaction out of that, people are suffering right now.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, there's no question about that. Brit, when you look back the two elections that I think may be emblematic of where we're headed in November. 2010, after the economic crash, the Democrats lost 63 House seats, six in the Senate in 1992. George H.W. Bush's approval rate went from 89 percent to 29 percent. And he lost the election. How do you think this will be reflected November 8th?\par \par \uc2 HUME: It really is. You can't survive really very well in a very bad economy unless you inherited a bad economy. And everybody knows that the economy that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had was down but it was down because of the lock downs.\par \par \uc2 And that what we're seeing is not -- you know, the job creation that has happened is really nothing but job restoration of jobs that people had and lost during the lockdowns. So, he doesn't really get much credit for that.\par \par \uc2 Think of the -- you know, the conditions in the country is 3.6 percent unemployment ought to be very much in his favor. But when the wage gains and the wages are being wiped out or greatly diminished by inflation, people are not going to be satisfied with that. And it is a crashing issue and the elections you cited are good examples.\par \par \uc2 One more example of when things are bad and the effects they can have is, remember 2006 when the Iraq war was going so badly, and the Republicans lost control of Congress. 41 seats in the House of Representatives are lost. It only takes a few this time for the Democrats to lose control. If that was -- that was the Iraq midterm, this is likely to be the inflation midterm.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, lots to chew over the next six months. Brit Hume, Charles Payne, always good to spend time with you, gentlemen. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks, you too.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The Justice Department finally commenting on the protests by pro- choice activists at the home of some Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 Senators meanwhile held a crucial vote today on whether to begin debate on a bill to codify the Roe versus Wade abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram tells us what happened today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A day of symbolism but the result never in question.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: On this vote the yeas are 49, the nays are 51. The motion is not agreed to.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats held the vote to stoke their base for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has made clear, they're coming after everybody.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats locking in senators.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Stand with women to protect their freedoms, or stand with MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans highlighting Democrats positions.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: The American people need to see what the far left has become.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But abortion drives a splitter between Democrats. Liberals applying pressure on pro-life members of their own party.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): I'm talking to you Joe Manchin, I'm talking to anyone who dares to have the power to do something in this moment.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The pressure failed. Joe Manchin voted no.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): You just can't change around all the time and just try to say wherever the winds are blown is where I'm going to be.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republican senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski support abortion rights, but both voted no.\par \par \uc2 Still, pro-life Democrat Bob Casey voted yes, he doesn't want to change decades of precedent.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): Republicans announcing that they're going to introduce legislation that would impose a six week ban nationwide, I don't support a ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: However, Democrats contend pro-life members are comfortable despite the leftward tilt.\par \par \uc2 REP. DIANA DEGETTE (D-CO): There's always room in our party for people who have all different personal beliefs.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The left is protesting at the San Francisco home of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and showing up in the neighborhoods of Supreme Court justices. It's against the law to pressure a justice on a case.\par \par \uc2 The White House and some Democrats back the demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 REP. RASHIDA TLAIB (D-MI): I welcome it in many ways as long as it's not, you know, violent rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Tonight, the DOJ says Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is directing the \uc2 U.S. marshals\uc2  to help protect members of the Supreme Court, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Another development today, Chad, the Senate confirmed the new member of the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 . What does that mean for big oil?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Well, Vice President Harris broke a tie to confirm Alvaro Bedoya to serve on the FTC Democrats now have more votes on the commission.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  accuses oil companies of price gouging. Schumer believes Bedoya could help in that fight. Democrats hope to pass a bill to allow the government to take on big oil, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Chad Pergram on top of it all on Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are trying to stop the administration's controversial disinformation governance board from ever coming into existence by stripping its funding.\par \par \uc2 An official with the \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2  calls the panel Orwellian and unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has got the latest for us tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The president's Ministry of Truth is just an un-American abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): House Republicans say they have a plan to stop the government's new disinformation governance board by defunding it. Rolled out by the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  late last month, the board is supposed to stop misinformation flowing in from China, Russia and South of the border. Republicans don't buy it.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): We do not allow, we don't trust government bureaucrats, government officials to determine what is true and acceptable speech. That is an anti-American idea.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Critics also have a problem with the board's new director Nina Jankowicz which worked as a disinformation expert at a D.C. think tank before coming on to lead the group.\par \par \uc2 Her online presence is vast with past comments from criticizing the Hunter Biden laptop as a Trump campaign product to videos of her singing about misinformation.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  is behind the bill t