ach. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox29. This is my favorite story of the day.\par \par \uc2 A passenger with no flying experience lands a private plane with the help of air traffic control personnel.\par \par \uc2 The original pilot had apparently suffered a medical emergency. Officials guided the substitute pilot to a safe landing at Palm Beach International Airport.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The war in Ukraine has many in the \uc2 United States government\uc2  concerned about a possible Chinese invasion of Taiwan. New wording on the state department's web site is causing problems with the Chinese government.\par \par \uc2 And national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has got details from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: A tempest in a teapot. That's how the state department would like to characterize China's angry outburst regarding new language on a state department web site describing Taiwan and its relationship to China.\par \par \uc2 Gone is language like, "The United States does not support Taiwan's Independence." The online state department factsheet also dropped the phrase Taiwan is part of China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED\uc2  PRICE, SPOKESPERSON, UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: Our policy towards Taiwan has not changed. The United States remains committed to our long standing One China Policy. We do not support Taiwan independence, and we have repeatedly made this clear both in public and in private.\par \par \uc2 ZHAO LIJIAN\uc2 , SPOKESPERSON, CHINESE FOREIGN MINISTRY (through translator): Now, the U.S. is blurring and hollowing out the one China principle by changing wordings concerning Taiwan. Such an action to seek political manipulation and to upset the status quo in the Taiwan Strait will end up getting 