par \uc2 And one of the people I was focused on was a young American journalist named Austin Tice, a great young American, Georgetown law student, former marine. And he had gone to be a war correspondent in Syria and had been taken hostage back in 2012.\par \par \uc2 And I had to fight my way through the bureaucracy in Washington, to get permission to go to Syria to try and meet \uc2 Bashar al-Assad\uc2 , to plead and negotiate to try and get Austin home. And at one point, I finally got the approval from the U.S. side, and I went to the Middle East, and we sent the letter through channels. And the Syrians wouldn't see me. And then I got a call from Sean. And Sean had filmed a documentary in Syria. He'd heard about Austin's case, and he said, hey, raised his hand, and said let me go to Syria and see what I can do to try to get Austin home.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PENN, ACTOR: You get kind of excited when you can play any small role in something like that. And so it became something that I was looking to do. And then in the case of Austin Tice, the people I talked to said you are going to have to talk to our boss. Would you talk to our boss? And that's when the boss and I got together, and we became friends in that effort.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: You can see the entire interview right now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Josh Kraushaar, Katie Pavlich, and Bill Bennett on a big abortion vote in the Senate, and funding news for the president's disinformation board.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Will this chamber step into the breach and protect the basic right to choose. We cannot allow the whims of MAGA Republicans to bully the rest of the country into submission. I urge my colleagues, take a stand. I urge my colleagues and Americans to fight back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2 , (R-IA): Democrats are using a leaked Supreme Court draft, not even a final decision, as an excuse to push a very radical legislation that goes beyond public opinion or even common sense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: That was debate in the Senate today to advance a measure that would codify Roe v Wade into federal law. It failed, so it will not advance.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Josh Kraushaar is politics editor for "National Journal," Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and former Education Secretary Bill Bennett is with us tonight. So Bill, why don't you start us off. This was never meant to actually advance the matter. It was merely a name-taking exercise. Democrats think that it's going to work in their favor in the November election, but will it?\par \par \uc2 BILL BENNETT, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: It may work a little bit, but they have to frame the argument right. And this fantasy stuff doesn't work. This was, to paraphrase Shakespeare, full sound and fury and ominous warnings, but come to nothing, really amounting to nothing.\par \par \uc2 This is the most difficult, arguably the most difficult issue in American politics, abortion. When you go to the states, there are real issues to be debated. But not the way they were doing it today, and not the way they were doing it on the street outside of the justices' houses.\par \par \uc2 Let me just add that just before on this channel, a couple of hours ago, your governor, the redoubtable Glenn Youngkin, was out saying we will protect these justices, we will have law enforcement there, and we will write the attorney general to do his job and enforce the law to protect them.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, we'll talk more about that in just a second. There was an interesting moment today, Katie, because that vote force Pennsylvania Senator Bob Casey, who is adamantly pro-life. In fact his father, the late governor of the state of Pennsylvania, is the Case in Planned Parenthood versus Casey. He was forced to vote in favor of the measure. And I'm wondering, is that an indication that there's no room to be pro-life in the Democratic Party? And how might that play out in November?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, there is no room in the Democratic Party to be pro-life. They've taken the position that they -- it was rare, they went to rare and safe to have an abortion in the 90s to this extreme measure that they voted on today. And for \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  to put this on the floor for a vote knowing it was going to fail, it was quite interesting given the lack of political traction that Democrats have been able to get on every single issue that voters are concerned about.\par \par \uc2 But I want to talk about the issue of the Democrats using this for the midterm elections. Midterms, of course, are base turnout elections. That's why they're doing. But the truth is that they can continue to argue that this bill was about codifying Roe. That's absolutely not true. This bill was extreme. It was wipe out basically all state laws when it came to restricting abortion. A majority of Americans believe that abortions should be restricted after 15 weeks. It would force religious institutions to conduct the procedure. It also would get rid of consent laws for parental consent, which 70 percent of the country supports.\par \par \uc2 And so for Democrats, they are using this to get out their base, understanding that the rest of the country really is not where they are when it comes to an unlimited number of months, when it comes to abortion in late-term, which the country does not get on board with in any sense of the term, especially on the state level.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Josh, let's talk a little bit about the security issue that Bill Bennett brought up just a second ago. The governors of Virginia and Maryland, Glenn Youngkin and \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , did send a letter to the attorney general asking him to take action. The attorney general apparently has directed the U.S. Marshal Service to help ensure the justices' safety by providing additional support to the marshal of the Supreme Court and the Supreme Court police.\par \par \uc2 But this action out in Virginia and in Maryland, and in Washington, D.C., protesting these justices and their homes, that brings up the specter of what happened to Judge Esther Salas in July of 2020, when a crazed person who found out her address shot and killed her son Daniel. It's not to say that these protests are going to erupt in that, but when you dox Supreme Court justices and you publish, wide and large, their addresses, you leave them open to potential danger here.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes, Democrats are playing with fire here when they refuse to unequivocally condemn this pressure campaign at the Supreme Court justices' own homes, their personal homes, in an attempt to pressure their decision-making on this upcoming case.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, the decision has not been made, and federal law is pretty clear that any such harassment or just undo pressure on judges in particular is against the law. And I've been stunned that you haven't seen clearer rhetoric from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , from the White House, from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 . The Democrats could get some political traction in defending abortion rights, but they can't do it when they sound extreme or they're defending the most extreme elements within their own base.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, on that subject of extreme, a lot of Republicans, Bill, as we switch gears, think that this disinformation governance board is extreme. They're trying to take measure to prevent funding it. \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , the Republican representative from Colorado, said, "I don't think people are fully understanding the severity of what a ministry of truth the Ministry of Truth organized by DHS truly means. This is Stalin level. This is Mao level. This is the hill the die on." Is it the hill to die on, Bill?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: First of all, it's entirely inappropriate in a free country to have something like this. But you would think this thing would be thrown out immediately on the basis of the fact that the woman is being recommended to head the ministry of disinformation has been wrong on everything. She got everything wrong. She disinformed on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . She disinformed on Russian collusion, and so on. This is another big, fat target Democrats have presented for themselves, a real mistake. We don't need it, we don't want it, and God knows, I think we'll be spared it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Of course, we all know now, we are very familiar with that Mary Poppins video that Nina Jankowicz did, but we also have a new tweet that has come to light via "The Washington Examiner" in which she said on November the 1st, 2020, "Trump had not one but two secret email servers to communicate with an influential Russian bank. Unbelievable." Katie, that is disinformation.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: That is disinformation. This is not the only corner of the administration where this disinformation is coming from. The incoming White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , also said that the George election was stolen from \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , and that President Trump wasn't a legitimate president. So there is this very serious question about the Biden administration creating this new board, not to serve as a way to tamp down on disinformation, which used to be a term that was reserved for Homeland Security dealing with outside, foreign influence and information inside the United States, but to go after political enemies inside the country without any recourse because they are not elected, and it's very difficult to get through the bureaucracy and understand what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 And I would note that the DHS secretary can't define what the mission is of the board, and therefore it's rife for abuse.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I wanted to ask Josh about that in the 45 seconds we've got left. The fact that Alejandro Mayorkas knew so little about all of this, what does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Not an encouraging sign, John, that -- it doesn't seem like they vetted Nina Jankowicz, the person who is going to be heading this disinformation committee. And if you can't vet your own staff and you don't see this stuff that's clearly been out there now for the last week, it's a real big red flag. I would expect that if Republicans take back the House in November, if it still exists, by 2023 there is going to be a lot of oversight, and it's not going to last much longer.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, I assume that they know a lot more about it now than they did a week ago. Josh Kraushaar, Katie Pavlich, Bill Bennett --\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Disinformed and uninformed.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Good to see you folks. Thanks for joining us today, have a great evening.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: You, too.\par \par \uc2 All right, finally, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. Wait until you see this. Professional Boxer Terence Crawford's daughter Talaya ran out of her shoe at the start of a 200 meter sprint on Saturday. She went back and put her shoe on. What happened was nothing short of amazing. The seven year old calmly put the sneaker back on, then turned on the afterburners. Talaya demolished the field to the finish line. The video, obviously, is going viral. There is a lesson for every child in America -- never give up, tenacity and strength will get you through.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, Dr. Mehmet Oz will be here. He's running for the Republican nomination for a Senate seat in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Inflation Slows In April, Still Near 40-Year High; Motorists Sound Off On Rising Gas Prices; Ukraine Mounting Counterattacks In Kharkiv; Esper: No Evidence Putin Making Substantial Gains; Former Vice President Pence: No Place For Intimidation Of Judges; Pence On Potential Overturning Of Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Trey Yingst \par \uc2 8,765 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: And he saved his own life.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Imagine if the turbulence were, the guy who was flying the plane is no longer flying the plane.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: My worst nightmare.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'm sure. You, you'd be able to do it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But John, thanks for having me. No, I would not.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, we'll see you again -- see you again tomorrow, Gillian. Thanks for joining us. I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: See you tomorrow. Be sure to set your DVR. And here's Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John and Gillian. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News Headquarters in New York. But go no further than the president's home state of Delaware to take the pulse of this nation as a painful report on surging prices tells folks pretty much what they already knew, meat and milk up 14 percent in the past year, eggs up 22, airfare, electricity used cars all up by double digits, gas once again, hitting an all-time high today. There are the numbers, and then there are the voices of Americans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My vehicle takes about $70.00 to fill up. Before it used to be about $40.00-$45.00.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is insane. That's almost $100 for me to fill my tank.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I say about another month and a half before it starts to like, like really break me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I thought it was going down. But now, it's the same price as my coffee. So, it's like my funds could go elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I try to limit my travels and just go from work to home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Biden can only do by so much. So, I appreciate anything, and everything that he tries to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: With the influx of gas prices also suggest is that President Biden is going to have a similar legacy as \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden, though blaming Putin again today, just about an hour ago, the pandemic and those newly coined ultra-MAGA Republicans for these prices that we're seeing, former Vice President Pence had plenty to say about that. The Supreme Court, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and a whole lot more. But we begin with Wyoming Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , the number three Senate Republican Leader. Senator, always good to have you with us. Thanks for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think there's no better barometer to go different places in the country. Those voices were from Delaware, as I mentioned, but you can tell it's pretty clear what's on people's minds.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Same exact thing in Wyoming, Martha, I saw it this past weekend, people woke up today with a double punch to the gut. One is the highest gas prices of all times, and the other is inflation numbers even worse than the government predicted. And people are in pain, and the president has no idea what to do about it. So, people in Wyoming this weekend, they're having to change the way that they drive, they eat that they live, so there are people who are feeling stressed, they're feeling squeezed.\par \par \uc2 And I used the $100 analogy, which is, I was at the gas station and the guy filled up. He said he had never before paid $100 to fill his truck. Was at the grocery store, the lady had two small bags of groceries, she said, this just doesn't feel like $100 worth of groceries. And $100 is the amount that the economist tells us, American families have to pay more each week, just to keep up with the quality of living of what they had a year ago. That's $5,200 more this year than last year, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, obviously, it's a scramble ahead of the midterms to try to turn this in a better direction on the president's part as he looks at what is looming in November. The Wall Street Journal today, making kind of a humorous analogy, but this is what they said, they said, President Costanza, meaning George Costanza from Seinfeld, takes on inflation.\par \par \uc2 They said, "President Biden on Tuesday tried to get ahead of Wednesday's April inflation report, which we saw this morning, with a speech rehashing his well, well-worn proposals to reduce prices, boost subsidies, raise taxes, increase regulation, he should take Jerry Seinfeld's advice to George Costanza and do the opposite of every policy instinct." And let's remind people what George and Jerry said to each other, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If every instinct you have is wrong, then the opposite would have to be right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I will do the opposite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, that's right. President Biden has mismanaged this economy so badly that people are now trapped in this inflation nightmare in which we're all living in. You know, remember for seven months, he said this was transitory it was going to go away, living in denial of this. Yes, we ought to do the opposite of what the president has done. We shouldn't shut down oil and gas for the Keystone XL pipeline, we should be producing more American energy and we have it he won't let us get it out under out of the ground.\par \par \uc2 The president wants to raise taxes and spend more government money irresponsibly. Well, we ought to stop the reckless spending, and we ought to be making sure that the Trump tax cuts became permanent, so we ought to be doing the exact opposite. But this is a president who will not take responsibility for his own, own mistakes he wants to blame everyone else but he won't look at the man in the mirror and say hey you have a lot of responsibility for the disaster we have right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, speaking of the man in the mirror, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , one of your colleagues on Capitol Hill, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida, called out the president. He says, "Families are suffering, the blame game Biden refuses to take responsibility. It's clear that Biden is the problem. He said, he's incoherent and confused, and needs to resign." And then a reporter in the White House briefing asked the president directly about that question and here's what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He said, and I'm just quoting here, that the best thing, the most effective thing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can do to solve the inflation crisis he created is resign. He's the problem. The senator added later, the senator added later, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is unwell, he's unfit for office. He's incoherent, incapacitated and confused. These are his words. Offering you a chance to respond.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think the man has a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the president kind of laughed it off, uncomfortable moment there to be sure. Do you agree with \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  on the condition of the president?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, I would like to see the president do things that will make the country move ahead in a positive way. And that's focused on jobs, the economy, national security to meet national security, economic security, energy security, border security. We have a lot of crises in this country right now that have been caused by the actions of this President and this administration.\par \par \uc2 We have chaos at the southern border you saw today. 107,000 Americans died from drug overdoses this past year. We don't have any solutions from the administration. We have energy at an all-time high cost today. $2.00 a gallon more than it was the day that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you, Senator, because you kind of sidestepped my question about whether or not you agree with \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  on the President's condition, and you are a doctor. So, are you choosing to not weigh in on what he said about that?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Yes, I haven't had a chance to examine the president on that, and I'll let those two do it between themselves.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And do you also distance yourself from Senator Scott's plans to raise taxes on some of the lower income earners in the country?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: My position has always been to lower taxes. And as I said earlier in this interview, Martha, what I want to do is make permanent the Trump tax cuts which have given us the strongest economy in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  of Wyoming. Thank you very much, sir. Always good to see you. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, more on President Biden's dig at Ultra-MAGA conservatives, a sort of newly coined phrase that \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  says gives it a little extra pop in terms of the damage of the, what they're calling folks there. I'll speak with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  later this hour. And Kellyanne Conway also here as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  co-founder, \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2 , is now weighing in.\par \par \uc2 He says, and he was the head of the company at the time when this decision was made, that it was a mistake to ban former President Trump from the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  platform. This story is heating up. We'll talk to her. And former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  as Russia now claims that Ukraine is plotting to accuse Putin of using weapons of mass destruction, the Former Defense Secretary on the war that is escalating, in Ukraine as we speak when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's get you a live look right now at the Senate floor where any minute of vote will be underway. Senator Schumer has led this effort, he wants all the senators to stand up and say where they would be on basically codifying into law, national law, Roe v. Wade, to make abortion legal across the country. We know now that senator, the Democrats Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, has said that he will vote no on such a measure, he wants it to go back to the States to be decided by individuals, voters in each state how they want their state to, to rule on the question of abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, it's expected to fail because of that, because it's a 50-50 margin in the Senate. And we know mansions going the other way. So, we'll, we'll take you there. We'll keep a close eye on that vote when it gets underway. In the meantime, Russia claiming today that Ukraine is "preparing provocations" to accuse Russia's armed forces of using weapons of mass destruction. That according to Russia's state-run news agency one day after this grim assessment of where this war is right now, from the Director of National Intelligence. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AVRIL MAINES, DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: The uncertain nature of the battle which is developing into a war of attrition, combined with the reality that Putin faces a mismatch between his ambitions and Russia's current conventional military capabilities, likely means the next few months could see us moving along a more unpredictable and potentially escalatory trajectory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That doesn't sound good. Former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  in a moment but first to Foreign Correspondent Trey Yingst for the latest on the ground in Kyiv, Ukraine. Hi Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon, Ukrainian forces said they've launched a successful counter offensive around the city of Kharkiv, reports indicate that some Russian troops were even pushed back over the border into their territory, as fighting does continue in this area.\par \par \uc2 Kharkiv is Ukraine's second largest city and the territorial gains will give the Ukrainians more flexibility when it comes to the renewed fighting in the far east. Still, the civilian death toll continues to rise as evidence is found of Russian atrocities against the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These are our civilian citizens who tried to evacuate. Unfortunately, they were with children. They were shot with an automatic gun. One car was hit with a cannon, which was probably either a tank or an infantry fighting vehicle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: As conflict in the east continues, Ukraine southern Kherson region is currently controlled by Russian forces. And the leadership there says they will ask Moscow to annex the region.\par \par \uc2 Reports to indicate civilians in Kherson have been forcefully taken to camps in Russia, and that basic supplies are lacking in this controlled territory.\par \par \uc2 While the war rages on, Ukraine is trying to pressure Russia on the economic front. Today, the country cut off the flow of some Russian natural gas supplies that normally go to European countries, marking the first time that Russian gas exports have been disrupted by the Ukrainian since this war began.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Now, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary general did speak today and he said that he does not see a peace process developing in the near future. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst, thank you very much, in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  served as defense secretary under President Trump. His new book, A Sacred Oath is out now. And we actually did a podcast that's going to be out tomorrow where we talked at length about a lot of what is in that book.\par \par \uc2 So, good to have you with us today, \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 . Congratulations on the book, and thanks for joining us. I want to focus on this question as a former secretary of defense, and what you think about where this war is right now?\par \par \uc2 Because if it's true that it's we're entering a more escalatory, and highly unpredictable phase, what do you think that says about what we need to be prepared for in the future with this war?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Yes. Well, first of all, Martha, thanks. It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: And let me say this much. I think the assessment that you portrayed from the DNI is a very sober one, and seems accurate to me.\par \par \uc2 Look, for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . It's been a strategic failure to date. He has managed to push Ukraine into the arms of the West even deeper, he has brought more \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces onto his borders. He's unified \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and I hope that soon, \uc2 NATO\uc2  will admit to more countries, Sweden and Finland, and really shore up our north eastern flank of the alliance.\par \par \uc2 And then, not just a strategic failure. But now, at the tactical level, we see that he really cannot consolidate additional gains in the Donbas. It's, they seize one town one day, and the next day, the Ukrainians fight back and retake it. So, it looks like a tactical stalemate as well.\par \par \uc2 So, the question is, where does he go to kind of salvage this foolhardy mission that he's been undergoing now for nearly three months? And what will that push him to? Will he resort to some type of use of weapons of mass destruction? We don't know, but we need to be very careful and keep a close eye on this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, that -- that's the question that I'm really asking here. Because I think it's important to look at the long term, we now have $40 billion, and American investment that the president has now proposed.\par \par \uc2 I just want to point out, $40 billion, and so far, it's been $11 billion over the past, you know, couple of months.\par \par \uc2 And we heard so much about the long war in Afghanistan, and why it was so important to get out of that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I'm not -- I recognize the geopolitical dynamic and the need for Ukraine to defeat \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . But I'm just asking, you know, when you look at this, what is -- what is the end look like? \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  goes, oh, yes, yes. I'm beat. I'm going to go home now and bring all my folks with men, and it's going to be over and peaceful? Do you really think that will happen?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, I don't think that will happen, first of all. Let me say this much. My number one priority as secretary defense was implementing the national defense.\par \par \uc2 And that said that China was our number one strategic adversary. And I still believe that, and number two, right behind them is Russia. And Russia being the more immediate challenge, giving that this is a declining power.\par \par \uc2 The what -- \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  started is not just a war between Russia and Ukraine, but it's a -- it's a war between autocracy and democracy. And so, for that reason, despite the amount of money, look, I think we need to fight this fight. It is -- or support the fight.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainians are doing what we never hoped we'd have to do, which is fight the Russians, and they are beaten them pretty well. And I think we should support that.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, we need to, you know, understand where that money is going, how it's being spent, and make sure it's being used appropriately. We also need to make sure that our allies are also contributing money and material and funding for all this.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, the administration needs to increase the funding for our own defense department.\par \par \uc2 It's inadequate. We keep seeing flat budgets being proposed. And, by the way, we have yet to see a national defense strategy after what? 16 months in office.\par \par \uc2 So, I am concerned about both what's happening in Europe, but also more broadly, the future of the department of defense under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, in the -- under the topic of Ukraine, in your book, you talk about the fact that President Trump met with \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2 , the then- chancellor of Germany, and that she said to him, what are you going to do about Ukraine? Because obviously, this has always been a real sore spot with Russia. They wanted Ukraine back in the fold.\par \par \uc2 And then he said to her, according to your book, what are you going to do about Ukraine? And, you know, encouraged them to do more to build up their military to be stronger on this front.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's interesting looking back at what you wrote about in the book, and now I look at what the new chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  is saying. He is saying we made a mistake. We should have spent more money. We now need to build up our defenses because we fell back.\par \par \uc2 And we should have been stronger members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We should have invested more. So, looking at the situation now and what this chancellor of Germany says, wasn't President Trump, right?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Oh, he was right. I mean, Germany was the weak link in the Alliance. He was right to press them to spend more to live up to their commitments to spend two percent of their GDP on defense. He was right to press them to end \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , and he was right in terms of replying to her -- it was a story he told, I never heard it, but he would tell it about what are they doing to support that?\par \par \uc2 And look, Germany has made a turnaround, they've realized that their policy for so many years was incorrect. They're now coming to spend more money, I hope they will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 But they've been a little bit hesitant to provide material, and do some other stuff. And look, the Germany are -- German army, just hasn't been ready to fight.\par \par \uc2 And so, these are all important points that the president pressed them on.\par \par \uc2 And, as you know, I say in my book, while I think he was right to do that, I don't think he was right to suggest that we would withdraw from the alliance.\par \par \uc2 But on those points, it was spot on. And I think during our tenure, we managed to get a number of \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to live up to their commitments to spend two percent of GDP, and I think that's -- that was a success of the administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Now, that was clearly important to him, and he pressed them on it, and a number of them came forward and actually did it. And that's where we find ourselves now.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , former defense secretary, and we've talked about the book in the podcast, which drops tomorrow. So, thank you for doing that as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Great. Thanks, Martha. Thank --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says that he's proud to have served during the most pro-life administration in American history.\par \par \uc2 What he thinks of new progressive back calls to protest, "six extremist" justices -- six extremist Catholic justices, they call them in a number of instances right after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thanks to your support. And thanks to the leadership of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Life is winning in America again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Keeping an eye on the activity on the Senate floor where at any minute, there will be a vote to codify into national law the right to abortion across the country after the league draft of Roe v. Wade from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 It is not expected to pass. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has said that he will not vote in favor of that. He believes that it needs to go back to the States, to be decided state by state by people placing their votes on this measure across the country.\par \par \uc2 So, we'll keep an eye on it. We'll let you know when that gets underway, and we will monitor the final tally when that is -- when that's happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are we (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: We say, hell, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: You don't care people die.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWD: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And progressive Democrats now tell Fox that they welcome the protests erupting outside the homes of Supreme Court justices and outside some churches across the country in response to that leaked draft opinion from the Supreme Court, indicating that Roe v. Wade could be overturned.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): I have stood and done counter protests in front of abortion clinics, when the right has come to attack, to humiliate and to intimidate people who are exercising their own bodily autonomy.\par \par \uc2 We have seen the MAGA movement, come and attack us here and lead an insurrection that has made some of us afraid for our lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Representative \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , speaking to Hillary Vaughn of our business channel.\par \par \uc2 So, earlier, I spoke with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the founder of the political advocacy group advancing American freedom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's jump right in. What's your reaction to what \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  had to say -- the congresswoman? She is -- she is saying, you know, free speech is free speech. And we've seen other protests on both sides of the aisle. These should be allowed.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, first, let me say that it is my hope, and literally my prayer, Martha that the draft opinion that was leaked a week ago, in the Dobbs case before the Supreme Court becomes the majority opinions, and that we're able to overturn Roe v. Wade and return the question of abortion to the states and the American people.\par \par \uc2 As a pro-life American, I welcome that, and I truly do believe that it's a pathway toward for literally lowering the volume on this issue by returning it to the American people and to their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 But I must tell you that these protests that are taking place outside the private homes of Supreme Court justices are wrong. It is an attempt to intimidate the court, and it's against federal law.\par \par \uc2 And I think to have elected officials, or any Americans hesitating to denounce that, is irresponsible. And I've called on President Biden, called on his administration to speak out against these protests.\par \par \uc2 There is -- there is no place in America for attempting to use intimidation tactics, to change the decisions of judges in our independent judiciary, and that this this situation is no different.\par \par \uc2 And leave aside beyond all of that, these despicable acts of vandalism that have been taking place in pro-life organizations around the country. Look, I want to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I want to ask --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I want -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to ask you about that, because I -- I've watched this debate my whole life as you have. The tenor has changed so dramatically. We saw downtown here in New York, protesters stepping on baby dolls outside of churches, and we see this #ShoutYourAbortion, sort of encouraging people to be proud about it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about how much this debate has changed?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I just remember the days as you do Martha, when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  said he wanted an America where abortion was safe and legal and rare. And now, those days are gone. And what the American people are witnessing now is the extremism of a pro-abortion movement in the country, which I think grieves the hearts of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 62 million unborn children have lost their lives to abortion over the last 50 years. Generations of women, many of whom have experienced abortion have gone through a lifetime of regret. This is a -- this is an issue that is so near and dear into the life of our nation that I truly do believe it belongs to the American people. Roe versus Wade was a usurpation that even the late \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  said was hard to justify, as Roe v. Wade overturned pro-life laws in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 But you're absolutely right. What we're witnessing now is the extremism of the American left and the Democratic Party on the issue of abortion. And you need to look no further than the bill that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is attempting to pass in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , which would nationalize abortion, legalize it all the way up to the moment of birth. And it also would eviscerate conscience protections for doctors and nurses who hold a pro- life view.\par \par \uc2 I think the American people know that the time has come for Roe versus Wade to be overturned, for this issue to be returned to the states and the American people. And allow the American people to prayerfully and carefully and through the Democratic process, restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let me ask you about that specifically, because you, your organization signed an amicus brief in the Mississippi case, which was upholding a state legislature voted ban of no abortion after 15 weeks. Do you still stand by that 15 week ban? Do you approve of that? Is that where you stand?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, we certainly support the Mississippi law. But it's a larger principle here, Martha. I think it's absolutely essential that the American people understand that while the left is saying that repealing Roe versus Wade would ban abortion. The truth of the matter is by repealing Roe versus Wade, we're simply returning the question of abortion to the states and the American people.\par \par \uc2 And Mississippi through its elected representatives in the state legislature and their governor will fashion pro-life laws in keeping with their citizenry. Texas will do the same. Here in Indiana will do the same. And I truly do believe that's the real question here. And I think that draft opinion by Justice Alito makes that so clear, but you're going to continue to hear in the days between now.\par \par \uc2 And when an opinion from the Supreme Court has issued you're going to continue to hear all of this rhetoric about denying women rights, when in fact, what we're doing is empowering the American people to take control of a question of the sanctity of life in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we've got a few more minutes with the former Vice President. We're going to talk ultra-MAGA and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Back now with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , founder of the political advocacy group Advancing American Freedom. Vice President Pence, good to have you back with us today. You know, obviously, we're into the primary cycle of the midterms right now. And one of the biggest questions politically is what kind of impact President Trump is having with the endorsements that he's making?\par \par \uc2 Here's a Pennsylvania poll, 37 percent say that his support for Mehmet Oz, Dr. Oz, influences them towards us, 37 percent say it doesn't matter at all. Do you think that that endorsement in Pennsylvania was a mistake? There are a lot of conservative thinkers who have said it was?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I wouldn't second guess anyone's endorsements in politics. But I do think whether it's Pennsylvania or elsewhere around the country, the men and women that are standing for office for nominations in our party are standing on their own merits. But make no mistake about it. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  remains very popular in our party and even beyond that.\par \par \uc2 I think the agenda of the Trump-Pence administration is looking better and better to more Americans every day as we see inflation at a 40-year high as we see the worst border crisis in history, as we see the disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, as we see gasoline prices that are now nearly doubled since they took office.\par \par \uc2 I think the American people are looking at our agenda of a strong and prosperous America of energy independence of even strong conservatives on our courts. And it's looking better and better every day. And everywhere I go across the country, I hear people saying that they're going to listen to the endorsements of the former President and others, but they want to see that agenda for the Trump-Pence administration back starting this year with the congressional elections and two years from now in all across Washington, D.C. on Pennsylvania Avenue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And obviously Democrats are a bit concerned about what they're seeing in these polls. And now the President has taken to calling people who are conservative or who support some of the things you're talking about ultra-MAGA. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The other path is the ultra-MAGA plant. It's the ultra-MAGA agenda. I never expected the ultra-MAGA Republicans who seem to control the Republican Party now to have been able to control the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You've known former senator and now President Biden for many years, Mr. Former Vice President Pence. What do you think of the man that you see on that screen in here there?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I think yesterday's speech was more about this fall's elections. They're grasping for straws, Martha, come on. I mean, this administration has done more damage to America in a year and a half than any presidency in my lifetime. I mean, we've weakened America on the world stage. We've embolden our adversaries through that disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And beginning with that $2 trillion COVID bill that was passed on a partisan basis over a year ago, they ignited inflation that even today still remains at a 40 year high and gasoline prices. And that's what I'm hearing about all over the place. You know, Martha, one of the nice parts about not being vice president anymore is I get to drive my own car. One of the bad parts is I get to pay for my own gas.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: And I mean, gasoline just hit another record high, $4.40 average, California $6. I mean, Houston yesterday in a police officer I was talking to said to me, hey, we're cutting back on family plans for this summer. I mean, it literally is costing the average American family in other $150 a month. I think they're grasping at straws. I think they're trying to create a straw man.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is this term, ultra-MAGA, whatever that means, look, there's 74 million Americans that stood with us in 2016 and in 2020. And I think that number is only growing as we go into these midterm elections. As Americans know, we can be prosperous. We can be strong. We can have judges who interpret our laws as written and don't legislate from the bench.\par \par \uc2 The American people want all of that back. And that's the train that I think President Biden sees coming down the tracks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to talk to you, sir, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , thank you very much for your time today. See you soon.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's co-founder \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  now admits that it was a mistake to ban former President Trump from tweeting. Former counselor to the president Kellyanne Conway joins us after reacting this way to news that her former boss is now welcome on the site. Quote, love all the people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  who have nothing else to do all day except to tack Trump on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  threatening to leave \uc2 Twitter\uc2  if Trump is back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Live on the Senate floor now the vote underway as Democrats attempt to make law of nationalized right to abortion. And this obviously comes on the heels of that leaked document from the SCOTUS deliberations that we wait to come out to see whether or not indeed they have overturned Roe v. Wade. That decision is expected in a few weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Senate Majority -- Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says quote, it proves the party has been captured by the far left fringe. The fact that they are standing up and taking this vote that is likely to lose, congressional correspondent Chad Pergram, gets us up to speed this afternoon. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. Well, this is a procedural vote. It's two steps actually removed from a straight up or down vote to codify Roe versus Wade. This is an effort to break a filibuster just to start debate on the actual bill.\par \par \uc2 You can filibuster at the beginning, trying to filibuster a bill to start debate and then you can filibuster on the back end as well. This is a filibuster on the front end, which means they're never going to get this bill on the floor because it needs 60 yeas. What is also up in the air is whether or not they will even get 50 yeas on this bill. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , the Democratic senator from West Virginia indicated that he was not for this.\par \par \uc2 However, Bob Casey who historically has been pro-life, he is a rare pro- life Democrat, Democratic senator from Pennsylvania. His father, in fact, was pro-life when he was the governor of Pennsylvania, and was part of that landmark 1992 Supreme Court case, Planned Parenthood versus Casey, imposing some abortion restrictions. Bob Casey has kind of changed his position here.\par \par \uc2 He indicated that he does not want to undo decades of precedent, and he will vote yes to proceed to this bill today. And if it were to come to a final vote, he would vote yes as well. But it's never going to get to that. This vote today is a show vote. This is all about the midterm elections. Listen to Elizabeth Warren, the Democratic senator from Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has made clear, they're coming after everybody. So we need to do this, get it on the board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: And this is why the Democratic and the Republican leaders in the Senate, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and also \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  here, they have made a point about this vote as well. They want to indicate to their basis what this vote is all about. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), MINORITY LEADER: The American people need to see what the far left has become. So I'm glad, I'm glad the Senate will vote today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY), MAJORITY LEADER: Every member of this body will make a choice. Stand with women to protect their freedoms, or stand with MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: So what both sides are trying to do, there are energize their bases to get them to the polls, pro-choice voters, pro-life voters for the midterm elections. And again, as I indicated, they may not even get to 50 votes here. That's because \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , the Democratic senator from West Virginia said that he would be a no. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): Know where you stand is the best thing. You just can't change around all the time and just try to say wherever the winds are blowing is where I'm going to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: So they need 60 votes here, 60 yeas. They are only at 23 right now, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, thank you very much laying it all out there for us, Chad Pergram reporting.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  who founded \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , now on board with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's decision to reverse former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ban, saying quote, I do agree there are exceptions, but generally, permanent bans are a failure of ours and don't work, which I wrote about here after the event. Here's some of the other reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR: I feel like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  just knows that if he himself can escape to space. And the rest of us earthlings will have to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 S.E. CUPP, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: I wonder now if his presence on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  again just kind of becomes noise and really he's just on there if he, you know, decides to go back on to annoy people like us.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP JR\uc2 ., FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP'S SON: They hate that they no longer have a distinct advantage if someone even levels the playing field, that's too much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in Kellyanne Conway, former senior counselor to President Trump and author of "Here's the deal" and Danielle McLaughlin, a liberal commentator, welcome to both of you, great to have you here. You know, Kellyanne, one of the things that I'm really struck by is how quickly the whole tone of this conversation changed, right? Because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  started buying up the stock and then suddenly, it was rumored, oh, maybe he's going to get a majority of the stock. Maybe he's going to try to take it over. And there was all this, you know, shock and people aghast that he would do that. Now, you've got \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  saying, you know, you know what, knocking President Trump off of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , yes, really wasn't a good idea.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER TRUMP SENIOR ADVISER: Yes, and the people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  don't like it because \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is basically -- essentially now a left wing cesspool. It is the incentive there is to be incendiary not to be insightful. It's really not about conversation so much as a disagreement and denigrating people who disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed. He lost his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  handle, yet people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  continued to talk about him. So you would think they would want him back on so they can actually pretend that he's watching their tweets or listening to them.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Martha, we all know especially moms of teens all about social media muscle, we see people out there who aren't particularly compelling conversationalist or very engaging in life are very bold in life, they get that social media muscle they think they can say and do anything they want, including, unfortunately to teenagers.\par \par \uc2 Why is this an issue? It's an issue because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been watching all this from afar. And now he's going to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and he wants to level the playing field. He wants to bring back on all players. Do you know a study by the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  in 2018, said just before those midterms, 92 percent of all tweets were being sent by about 10 percent of users. This is not representative of the country. And those users identified seven and 10 as Democrat or Democrat, leaning independents.\par \par \uc2 I have to laugh finally, of all the people saying, I'm going to get off of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's on. Bye girls, go find something else to do like a hubby or job and stuff --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's the new version of I'm going to, you know, live to Canada. Danielle, what do you think about \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  both saying that they think it was a mistake to knock the former president off the platform?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE MCLAUGHLIN, LIBERAL COMMENTATOR: Well, Martha, you know, I think that you can have two thoughts and two things can be true at once, right? So people like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and people like \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  and are other sort of social media overlords have way too much power over the public discourse. But you can also have the same thought in your mind, which is and choose Kellyanne's word, incendiary.\par \par \uc2 The reason that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was banned, of course, was because of this ongoing incitement in and around January 6th, and this horrific experience that we had in our nation's history of seeing our Capitol building attacked. So we need to be able to have, I think, clear headed conversations about whether a ban is ever appropriate and how long that ban might be.\par \par \uc2 But the idea that the reason that the president, former president was kicked off the platform is not somehow incendiary or extremely problematic. I think that needs debating.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the problem is, you know, you have \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who has a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account. You have the Ayatollah of Tehran, who talks about killing Americans. You have the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leader on there. And the whole idea of it is that it's a town square, that there's public speech that people are allowed to get on there and say what they want.\par \par \uc2 This is from the "Wall Street Journal" editorial board. It's -- the headline is Elon -- Trump, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tempts Trump on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And they write, if Mr. Trump is back in public view, picking fights on an hourly basis and blaming everyone else for his election defeat, he might remind voters why they grew tired of his antics and made him a one-term president. Kellyanne, what do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, first of all, the President has his own platform, it's called Truth Social. And he has said he doesn't intend to go back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That's before \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said he would welcome the president back. Look, I've been asked about President Trump on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  many times. And I read about this in my upcoming book, I say he needs to tweet like we need to eat. It's just about better choices.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes I eat the kale salad. And then I clean off the brownie pan with my youngest by 9:00 p.m. And so I have told the president his best tweets were always when he was informing the public, which head of state was coming and why, which policies were being discussed? What he was doing to surge supplies during COVID and maybe the media weren't covering, he wasn't getting credit for just informing the public. And he'll do that.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Martha, that I think is a very valid point. But we at least have to let him back on the four -- back on the platform if you'd like to be, you miss Nicolas Maduro, he's another one. The Venezuelan dictator who just can say whatever he wants on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And I think those who were happy \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got off of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  didn't realize that they had killed their favorite foil. They got rid of their favorite foil.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had been on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  during the first 15 months of that Biden dministration, they'd be blaming him for more than they blame now that's why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is low in the polls and owns and eats everything. They know it's his -- these are his policies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I asked Danielle the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  about what he thought about it. And I mentioned this to him that, you know, obviously he was the target of some of those tweets on January the 6th, and did he, was he OK, with the President being back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  after that experience? Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I absolutely support the president returning to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And this whole trend beginning really with President Trump of deplatforming conservatives of silencing conservatives has got to come to an end. I mean, the antidote to the woke left is freedom. The antidote to cancel culture is freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick that Danielle.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not deplatformed because he was conservative. It was because of this sort of glorification of violence, which was a violation of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's Terms of Service. And the same as for \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , not because he was a conservative, but because he was being abusive, the same goes for \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , for Alex Jones. So I think to call this cancel culture and to make it political is unfair and it's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It doesn't happen to the Ayatollah or Putin so that argument is sort of shot. When you look at what those folks are saying, it started to look like, you know, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Kellyanne, quick thought from you. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is saying, you know, that it was clearly, you know, there was a bias, a clear bias that leans left and squelches the free speech of people on the right, on this platform. And he says it's not a good business model. He thinks he can triple the business by opening it up. Kellyanne?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Look the greatest entrepreneur of our time, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  wants to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . People should welcome this. He's a genius. And he makes things happen. He also recognizes there are 74 million people in this country who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , less than a year and a half ago -- about a year and a half ago, who were not in the capitol, who should not be deplatformed, should not be canceled, we should feel and he recognizes that voters are first consumers.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I'll say is I think the Dorsey comment is as important as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's revelation because Dorsey is the same guy who took Trump off on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and took "The New York Post" off of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for two weeks because they dare say that 100 Biden's laptop was problematic and authentic. Guess what, 17 percent of voters say they would have switched their vote from Biden to Trump had they known that at the time. So I think there's a little guilt at work here, too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think there is. And I think now you're starting to sense that there's some concern about the Hunter Biden story in some of the closest camps to that situation. So we'll see where that goes. Danielle McLaughlin, Kellyanne Conway, great to have you both here. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have never seen anything like this, that from a CDC official on the record setting number of deaths of more than 100,000 Americans to drug overdose fueled in large part by illicit fentanyl. Some war on drugs over the southern border could increase when the administration lists Title 42 five days from now. Correspondent Nate Foy is live in Eagle Pass Texas after our Fox cameras captured a large group of migrants from Cuba, Venezuela, and Colombia today. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, yes, 150 migrants in that group being processed by border patrol today. But I want to start with the fentanyl threat that you mentioned. We spoke with the medical examiner of Webb County whose jurisdiction covers 11 counties here in south Texas. She had this to say about the threat of fentanyl now versus two years ago. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. CORINNE STERN, WEBB CO. CORONER: We're very, very fortunate in this county prior to 2021 where it was rare for me to see a fentanyl death in this office. And now I would say at least half of my drug overdoses have fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Martha I want to show you that large group that you mentioned that crossed here at Eagle Pass at about noon today. You mentioned these migrants are from Cuba, Venezuela, and Colombia. Now take a look at this video that we just got from Texas DPS. What you're watching is troopers stopping a human smuggling attempt. You can see there's a chase and then eventually the driver is going to get out of the car run away on foot and then change shirts but it doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 Eventually the driver is caught and arrested. Troopers in the Rio Grande Valley sector apprehended eight illegal immigrants. And Martha we spoke with two migrants who were apprehended by border patrol last night. They're from Mexico. They say they spent the past four days on a ranch with no food or water, their entire journey lasting 10 days. They will likely be sent back to Mexico under Title 42. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quite a situation. Nate Foy, thank you very much. Great reporting from Eagle Pass, Texas throughout the last several days, last week or so, thank you, Nate. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So that's THE STORY of this Wednesday, May 11th already, 2022. THE STORY goes on. Great having you with us today. I will see you tonight on "THE FIVE." So hopefully you'll tune in and join us there. And then we'll be back again tomorrow at 3 o'clock. "YOUR WORLD" starts just a couple seconds away. See you later.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Disney\uc2  Earnings and Revenues; Trump Bump; Inflation Woes; Interview With Gov. Glenn Youngkin (R-VA); Interview With Former \uc2 Chrysler\uc2  CEO \uc2 Robert Nardelli\b0\par\par\uc2 Susan Li, Ashley Webster, Chad Pergram, Trey Yingst, Hillary  Vaughn, Madison Alworth, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These prices, they have got to be fixed, because this - - it breaks people's pockets. And we got to pay for living and...\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Inflation is incredible right now. We have to do double work. We can't afford it. The salaries are staying the same. And it's really hard. It's hard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People have to decide how they're going to distribute their income. More of it's going to towards transportation costs and food, OK, but do you have to -- and what about rent? Do you spend it on gasoline?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gasoline has gone way up, and, certainly, food prices too have gone up. Nothing's being done about it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's going to kill the country, these diesel prices.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have no choice but to fill up my gas tank and pay this money. There's no other choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Definitely too much. And it has to change, hopefully soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Fair and balanced, angry and angrier.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World," a more expensive world, and apparently trying to find relief, in that the inflation report we got out today wasn't as high as it was the prior month.\par \par \uc2 But, year over year, it is still in and out of 40-year highs of, a relentless tick-up in prices that goes dramatically beyond the 8.3 percent surge that we did see to growing concerns that everything we eat and touch and buy and plan on is getting pricey and pricier by the hour, by the day, by the week, by the month, amid signs right now that compounding all of this a supply chain crisis that is likely to keep this extending for months, some fear the rest of the year to come.\par \par \uc2 Is that too soon, too fast? We're on it.\par \par \uc2 We're also on this vote going on in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  right now to try to enshrine in law Roe v. Wade. The problem is, the votes probably are not there. But the anger at some justices on the right is still there.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin on his promise to work with both federal and local law enforcement to ensure the safety of Supreme Court justices, whose homes have indeed become the focus of protests and much more. He's joining us later this hour.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the story of this hour, prices you know so well and prices that aren't going down at all.\par \par \uc2 The latest now from Madison Alworth in New York -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, inflation down slightly compared to last month, but prices, they still remain incredibly elevated. And anyone who goes to the grocery store is seeing it.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at where inflation stands today with some of those necessities that you have to get on a weekly basis. Meat, that is up 14 percent. Then you take a look at eggs, 22 percent, milk up over 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 And you talked about the supply chain. It's not just the items being more expensive. It's a matter of getting those items. Perfect example right here, taking a look at the bacon, some of it set further back, some of the shelves just completely empty, styrofoam trying to fill the space where bacon should be.\par \par \uc2 This is all compounding on a problem that we have seen since the start of the pandemic. Pre-pandemic, we were looking at unavailable items from 7 to 10 percent empty shelves. Today, empty shelves are 12 percent of any store. It means people who are able to afford things that are more expensive, like cars, for example, that's another big one with the CPI this -- came out today, 14 percent more expensive for new cars, up over 22 percent more expensive for used cars.\par \par \uc2 Even if someone's ready to buy them, they can't always get them. Dealerships like Lexus at Route 10 used to have a 90 day supply of cars. That's between 300 and 400 cars. Now they have a fraction of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM MAOLI, CEO, CELEBRITY CAR GROUP: Right now, we're sitting with -- and I know you're going to laugh when I tell you this -- we're sitting AT 10, at 10 cars in stock.\par \par \uc2 Customers are coming in. They're buying. What they're buying is, they're buying cars that are coming in, not even knowing when they're going to arrive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: And filling up that car now is also costing Americans more than ever before, a record high set for gas prices, with the national average at $4.40 today.\par \par \uc2 This is after, yesterday, we set a new high, today another high, only more concern that gas prices could continue to raise -- all of this not only impacting the wallet, but that scarcity aspect is so new to Americans. We haven't seen a scarcity like this since the '70s, when we had those long gas lines, but now it's not just gas.\par \par \uc2 It's in the grocery store. It's with your appliances. It's with your cars. And if you can get them, they're all costing you more, so it's an inflation and scarcity double-punch that's affecting all different types of Americans from all different incomes -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Madison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I do remember, Madison, that '70s experience. It was not fun. It was not fun at all\par \par \uc2 Madison, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Hillary Vaughn. She's following the president, what he's saying all about this. He was out in Illinois. A lot of Democrats are trying to rally around him on this, but not all of them.\par \par \uc2 Hillary, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, the president did not directly point blame at Putin or the pandemic for rising prices. But, instead, he compared inflation to a battle that Americans are fighting here at home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Right now, America is fighting on two fronts. At home, it's inflation and rising prices. Abroad, it's helping the Ukrainians defend their democracy and feeding those who are left hungry around the world because Russian atrocities exist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Inflation is eating into every American's budget, but, so far, there has not been much appetite on Capitol Hill to find a bipartisan fix.\par \par \uc2 Even some Senate Democrats I have talked to you today admit that Congress needs to step up and act fast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): We have got to do some things on inflation. We have got some bills to do that. I could toot my own horn because two of them are mine.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Proposals don't help Americans pay the bills today. So what can Congress do? What should the president do to give Americans relief today?\par \par \uc2 Right. SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Right. The president's done what he can. There needs to be both investments by the private sector and bipartisan action on an energy bill.\par \par \uc2 We have had a lot of discussions, but we haven't yet seen action here in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Neil, Republicans continue to say the root cause of this inflation is President Biden's spending.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  yesterday told me he thinks the president should quit because he does not think he is well-equipped to fix this problem -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hillary, I have told you this before.\par \par \uc2 My favorite moment in your reports is you following these guys in the hall.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sure the elevator door opens, they see you and, oh, my God, can I get back in? Click, click. They can't. They can't. You're there. You're relentless.\par \par \uc2 Great stuff, Hillary. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hillary Vaughn on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Bob Nardelli\uc2  joins us right now, the \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , the former CEO there, former \uc2 Chrysler\uc2  CEO.\par \par \uc2 Bob, as you were saying when we were last together, this isn't transitory, it isn't going to be brief. Right now, supply chain problems, if anything, are getting more complicated. Where is all this going?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT NARDELLI\uc2 , FORMER CEO, CHRYSLER: Well, I think Hillary made a good point when she referenced the president said there's two battles, one here in the United States on inflation and the other in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think, Neil, the Ukraine is probably winning right now, as evidenced in the 8.3 percent that we just heard this morning, number one. Number two, a recent survey, Neil, 163 CEOs -- and I see a lot of public CEOs, private CEOs. And this is the worst optimism in 40 years, that they're looking out at the complexity of what they have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 So -- and it's not necessarily a demand issue. I know that the administration keeps saying it's demand problems. But, Neil, you and I both know people aren't going to say, well, I'm going to drive more because gas is only $4.40, or a truckers says, I'm going to drive more because diesel is $5.55.\par \par \uc2 We don't have people going out and having babies because they know that -- it's not we're having more babies is the formula problem. If you just look at supply chain -- and I know you want to talk about that -- is, if you look at a satellite view of the oceans, you could hardly see the water for the sake of container ships, tankers, and so forth out there.\par \par \uc2 This administration said they were going to work 24/7. They have not. Fuel prices. I mean, you don't need a bill. What you need is action, Neil. You need action. You need to reverse what this president did on Inaugural Day and start pumping more oil. That's going to get prices down. A bill's not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 You need to get the ports unloaded.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and, as you know, Bob, the administration has been saying they have opened up some lands and permits and all of that, and the industry isn't taking advantage. You know, the back-and-forth on this defies imagination.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, leaving that aside, if the administration doesn't have a plan to deal with this, and it's relying on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , and judging from another market sell-off today, it seems to be investors' way of saying, this is dicey, and this is going to get dicier. And we don't want to get in the way of this.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that's a sign of complete capitulation. But is it close? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: No, I know people are talking about we have plateaued.\par \par \uc2 And I don't believe that. And they said, even though we have plateaued, it's going to be painful. I think we're going to see an increase in inflation. I deal with this every day with my companies, with CEOs.\par \par \uc2 When they talk about a good first quarter, Neil, and that that's going to carry us, somebody should ask the question, how much of the increase in profitability was from price, how much of it was from margin shift, and how much was really volume out there?\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think -- I don't think this is going to go away. I'm not an optimist. I'm not a pessimist. I was -- learned -- I learned under Jack Welch to be a realist and look at the world the way it is, and then you try to change it.\par \par \uc2 But you have got to acknowledge the problems you have. And this administration is not doing that. When they talked the other day, when he talked the other day about raising taxes on corporate America, that's what caused the offshoring that we lived through a couple of decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Why do you want to punish the men or women that are running these companies that are employing millions of people who are suffering today? When I go on the factory floor, Neil, and I talk to the factory workers, they think we're in a recession already, already, based on food prices.\par \par \uc2 What you reported at the top of your show, food prices, transportation, air travel, there isn't a part of what we live off of that isn't being affected by inflation today. And there is no solution being put on the table that will help remediate this in the short -- and the Fed's -- the history of not quite doing this right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NARDELLI: I mean, you have been around this as long as I have.\par \par \uc2 And the rate increase and the bond sell-off, it's a very, very slippery slope that we're on to try and get the inflation down without driving it into a full -- full recession. And we know, technically, that's two quarters.\par \par \uc2 So, let's see what happens at the end of the second quarter, relative to the technical explanation of a recession, vs. what we're all living each and every day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 We had negative growth, in other words, a contraction, in the first quarter. If we get another one in the quarter we're in, that is the definition of a recession.\par \par \uc2 And, on that, I guess you hope you're wrong on that, Bob, but you have been pretty prescient about a lot of this other stuff.\par \par \uc2 Bob Nardelli\uc2 , the former \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  CEO, former \uc2 Chrysler\uc2  CEO.\par \par \uc2 Again, the sell-off was broad-based to the corner of Wall and Broad today. Too soon to say whether investors are at that point where they're so depressed, so bummed out, so losing money, particularly in once high-flying technology stocks that have lost a better than a trillion dollars in value over the last four trading days, that they give up and say, the hell with it.\par \par \uc2 We're not necessarily at that point. But, sometimes, you can't make up the confluence of events. For example, on the heels of this, we're getting word this weekend there's going to be a super flower blood moon eclipse. It's true. It's when Earth's shadow will cover the moon, which often has a red color during such an event, hence the blood moon nickname.\par \par \uc2 We're told it has nothing to do with the stock market, but, these days, you just wonder.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Relentless and constant, that's how Ukrainian President Zelenskyy sums up the latest air wave of attacks and Russian soldiers advancing on Donbass and elsewhere throughout the country in what has become almost like a Whac-A-Mole situation, where you will never know where they will turn up, that is, the Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 But the president also says it's Ukrainian forces that are surprising them in their resistance.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst now with the latest from Kyiv -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces said they have launched a successful counteroffensive around the city of Kharkiv. Reports indicate some Russian troops were even pushed back across the border into their own territory, as fighting does continue.\par \par \uc2 Kharkiv is Ukraine's second largest city. And the territorial gains will give the Ukrainians more flexibility when it comes to fighting in the far east. Still, the civilian death toll continues to rise, as evidence is found of Russian atrocities against the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROMAN HRYSHCHENKO, COMMANDER OF TERRITORIAL DEFENSE UNIT (through translator): These are our civilian citizens we tried to evacuate. Unfortunately, they were with children. They were shot with an automatic gun. One car was hit with a cannon.\par \par \uc2 It was probably either a tank or an infantry fighting vehicle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: As conflict continues in the east, in Ukraine's southern Kherson region, Russian-installed leadership claims they will ask Moscow to annex the region.\par \par \uc2 Reports do indicate civilians in Kherson have been forcefully taken to camps in Russia, and that basic supplies are lacking in this controlled territory. While the war rages on, Ukraine is trying to pressure Russia on the economic front.\par \par \uc2 Today, in the country, they cut off Russian natural gas supplies that normally go to European countries. This marks the first time that Russian gas exports have been disrupted by the Ukrainians since the war began.\par \par \uc2 Now, where does all of this lead? Well, today, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . secretary-general says that peace talks don't appear to be on the table as of right now -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Trey, thank you for that. Be safe, my friend.\par \par \uc2 To General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2  right now, the former CIA director, retired four- star general.\par \par \uc2 General, very good having you.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, General, is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's endgame here? To take and firmly lock down these regions, Donbass included, claim them as his own and leave?\par \par \uc2 GEN. DAVID PETRAEUS (RET.), FORMER COMMANDER, U.S. CENTRAL COMMAND: I think his ambitions are greater than that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, he can't leave, because the Ukrainians are making their capabilities felt more and more all the time. As Trey reported, there's a counteroffensive ongoing to the east of Kharkiv. Again, it's the second largest city in all of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And they have driven the forces, as he noted, of Russia all the way across the border in some locations. And that could actually threaten the logistical lines of communication that are supporting the offensive in the east. So, that's a problem for Russia. They're having to reposition forces there.\par \par \uc2 I think what we're really seeing is actually an end to the momentum that Russia may have achieved in the south and southeast and the beginning of some momentum for Ukrainian forces, as the enormous amount of weapons systems, ammunition, other supplies and so forth are making their way felt on the Ukrainian side.\par \par \uc2 What the U.S. has provided is extraordinary. And, of course, you also have the U.K. and other \uc2 NATO\uc2  nations providing it. And, of course, as you know, there's a bill on Capitol Hill for almost $40 billion of additional assistance of all types, including military as well.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a sense, I think, that the Ukrainians may actually be able to drive them back in places. So the idea that he would be able to consolidate his gains in the south and southeast and east, I think is probably faulty.\par \par \uc2 This is looking more and more like a quagmire for Russian forces of Putin's own making.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wonder, too, General, what you make of the Russian strategy, it seems, to hit cities that might be getting some of that aid.\par \par \uc2 Odessa comes to mind, other ports cities, where you almost get the sense that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , no matter what Western allies are willing and able and presently getting to Ukraine, will become increasingly difficult, if not impossible, in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Well, it's a huge country.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind that this is a country the size of Texas that originally had 13 million more people than the Texas population. Certainly, he's going after critical infrastructure locations. He's trying to take out railheads, supply depots, fuel storage and a variety of other capabilities in the logistical world that are very important to Ukraine, and having some success in that.\par \par \uc2 But the Ukrainians are amazingly resourceful. They're repairing damage about as quickly as it can be done. And, again, I think there's going to be a sense of shifting of momentum here in the weeks that lie ahead, as this enormous amount of additional weaponry makes its way to the front lines, and as the forces of Putin, which really don't seem to have much in the way of reinforcements, start to fatigue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think, General, in retrospect, the celebration of Victory Day in Russia to herald that moment when they took down the Nazis actually worked against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and reminded Russians, all right, what is going on Ukraine is not that?\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Well, what it certainly did, I think, is, it showed, at the very least, indecision by Putin.\par \par \uc2 He did not declare mobilization. He did not declare war on Ukraine,. He just essentially said, we're going to continue with the special operation without providing any additional forces.\par \par \uc2 So, unless he in the weeks ahead does declare some kind of mobilization, his objectives, his ambitions greatly outstrip his capabilities.\par \par \uc2 As the director of national intelligence yesterday noted, he'd really like to take the entire southern part of the country and cut Ukraine off from the Black Sea entirely. That's beyond his grasp. He can't even get halfway to Odessa. He will do damage to Odessa, as he has been with missiles and rockets and bombs, but very limited in terms of what he can do in terms of ground forces.\par \par \uc2 And, again, I think there could be a rude awakening here as the Russian forces start to feel the brunt of -- again, 90 155-millimeter heavy artillery howitzers, plus nearly 200,000 rounds of ammunition just from the United States alone, that's an enormous amount of capability.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: General Petraeus, thank you very much. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Thanks, Neil. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right back to politics back home and the Trump bump.\par \par \uc2 His batting average is still pretty high, even though a split verdict in two key races, with some very crucial ones coming up -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin is concerned about the safety of Supreme Court justices, as these protests continue and get louder and, he fears, more violent.\par \par \uc2 What he wants to see, like now. He's coming up, like in this show -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, so much for in training Roe v. Wade into legislation and law, a big speed bump and maybe a halt to the process moments ago in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram with the latest on that -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, this was a procedural vote just to start debate on this bill to codify Roe vs. Wade. You need 60 votes to overcome a filibuster. They were well short of that. The vote was 49 yeas, 51 no's.\par \par \uc2 Bob Casey, the pro-life Democratic senator from Pennsylvania, he actually voted to proceed with debate here. He says he's not for ending Roe because he doesn't want to overturn decades of precedent here, but 49-51 in a 50/50, Senate, guess what? \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , the Democrat from West Virginia, he voted with the Republicans on this.\par \par \uc2 So that's why this did not cross that threshold. And, again, they needed 60 votes. So even if it was a 50/50 vote -- you had Vice President Harris presiding over this -- she can't vote in those circumstances, because, per the Constitution, she can only vote to break a tie, and they were well short of that.\par \par \uc2 So this was a show vote today, getting everybody on the record on both sides for the midterms -- Neil,\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chad, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, discussing the Trump bump. He had a split verdict yesterday, but his batting percentage has been very, very high.\par \par \uc2 And I raised the issue with South Carolina Congresswoman Nancy Mace, whether she's worried, because he doesn't support her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Former President Trump is supporting your opponent. In other words, he wants to primary you.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering what you make of that.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Well, we're looking very good in the polls. I'm very excited. I'm cautiously optimistic. We have 31 days to go.\par \par \uc2 I have raised $4.5 million. My opponent has raised less than $300,000 so far this cycle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so, Congresswoman Mace is saying nothing critical of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She doesn't want to risk more of his wrath, but she's saying she's going to survive this primary challenge from the candidate that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has chosen to try to take her down.\par \par \uc2 Kaylee McGhee White joins us right now of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 That's a pattern I noticed among those who are not being supported by President Trump, that they'd rather not further inflame the situation, just hope they survive the battle with the candidate he picked. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE MCGHEE WHITE, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And that's because, as we all know, Trump does -- he has a massive influence over voters still, but yesterday's race in Nebraska and the upcoming race in Georgia and in Pennsylvania, the Senate race, there are all kinds of symptoms that maybe that influence is not as foolproof as we thought.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the candidate that he endorsed in Nebraska did not end up winning that primary election. In Georgia, it also looks like Brian Kemp is going to come out on top. And, in Pennsylvania, Dr. Oz, who Trump also endorsed, is not doing that much better than his rivals.\par \par \uc2 So it's going to be really interesting to see whether Trump's endorsements make that big of a difference in the end.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just wonder, in the case with his gubernatorial candidate choice, of course, he had been brought up on a number of harassment, and worse, charges here. That might have doomed his candidacy, regardless of what former President Trump was pushing.\par \par \uc2 I'm wondering, in Georgia, another race where the former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  is in the battle of his life against the incumbent governor, Brian Kemp, and, in that, he trails by double digits. That might change in a couple of weeks, but it's a mighty climb. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE WHITE: In Georgia, it's particularly tough because voters have already rejected the candidate that Trump endorsed last November. \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  lost his Senate race.\par \par \uc2 And so now he's trying to run for governor again and win back the voters who did not vote for him the last time. So he's already coming at this from a disadvantage. And, again, it's really -- it's really hard to determine how much of an influence Trump's endorsement is going to have over that factor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Kaylee, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Kaylee McGhee White of The Washington Examiner following these races.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia contest, the one pretty much everybody will be watching, is a couple of weeks away still. So we will keep an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping an eye on these protests outside some conservative justices' homes. Why the Virginia governor is now saying, enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Youngkin is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: These protests outside some conservative justices' homes in the Washington, D.C., area have already prompted concern not only for the justices' safety, but for those in the neighborhood as well.\par \par \uc2 Enter Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, who, along with his counterpart in Maryland, Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , just wrote the attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , to say he has a responsibility to stop this sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 Governor Youngkin with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Sir, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I read your letter, and I read the governor's letter, your colleague in Maryland. You want them to take some action to enforce a law that's already on the books, right?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): Yes, this is clear.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this leak was done in order to influence and, oh, by the way to intimidate our justices. And that's exactly what these parades and picket lines and demonstrators are trying to do, is influence and intimidate our justices.\par \par \uc2 It's on the federal books that, in fact, that's punishable with up to a year in prison. And, therefore, Governor Hogan and I have asked the attorney general to do his job and enforce this law. This is just fundamentally wrong to have people showing up at the justices' homes and trying to influence and intimidate them.\par \par \uc2 And so we have taken a two-prong approach in Virginia. First, the state police are at the ready to support local resources and federal resources. We have substantial resources ready to go. Local police, I have asked them to please to create a perimeter around each one of the justices' homes, with support from state police as needed.\par \par \uc2 And then we have asked -- we have asked Attorney General Garland to enforce the law and to make sure that these demonstrators are not allowed to try to intimidate justices, like they're trying to do.\par \par \uc2 It's clearly in the federal statute that this is wrong, and the attorney general needs to enforce it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When you say it's wrong, there's been a debate over whether it's illegal.\par \par \uc2 Now, you hear from the administration and \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  and some others that these are peaceful protests. They get kind of hairy, looking at it myself, Governor.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of that, that nothing illegal is going on?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, the statute is incredibly clear.\par \par \uc2 It basically says, if you are parading or picketing in order to try to influence a judge, then it's punishable with up to a year in prison. That sounds illegal to me. And I just ask the attorney general to enforce the law that's on the books.\par \par \uc2 If people want to demonstrate someplace off away from their home, that's their prerogative. But, again, this is not a final ruling. It's a draft ruling.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And, clearly, these demonstrations are being pulled together to try to influence the final outcome.\par \par \uc2 And that is prohibited, based on federal statute.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Governor, you seemed to intimate at the outset of your remarks that you suspect that this was a liberal who released this. I assume you're talking about a law clerk to one of the liberal justices. Do you think it could have involved a justice his or her self?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, I can't even begin to take any position on who did this.\par \par \uc2 But it's clear that it was done in order to cause chaos and in order to try to influence and intimidate justices. And that's exactly what these demonstrations, with the picketing and the parading and cars going by, which is what's been organized, we believe, for tonight, in front of the justices homes is being done.\par \par \uc2 Again, there is -- this is wrong. I mean, we have justices that have small children. They have neighbors and families. And this is wrong, based on federal statute. And, therefore, it should be stopped.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If the attorney general does nothing in response to your letter, what do you plan to do?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, we -- as I said earlier, we have asked the Fairfax County police and the Fairfax County Board of Supervisors to establish a perimeter safely away from the justices' homes, and, in fact, to try to restrict access for -- from cars and pedestrians that aren't supposed to be there.\par \par \uc2 And we stand ready with the State Police. We have substantial resources ready to support in all cases to make sure that there's no violence and to support the Fairfax County police when they ask us to come in.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment where -- where local resources, state resources and federal resources should be working in collaboration in order to keep our justices and their families safe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, if I could switch to another story where you have made some news, is, you're for suspending the gas tax in Virginia, I believe for six months.\par \par \uc2 And where does that stand?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, there's a moment for common sense and making sure that we keep our justices safe, and also to get the -- get the runaway fuel costs down.\par \par \uc2 And this is a moment where common sense needs to prevail. We see gas prices in Virginia, across the nation at all-time highs. We were up over $4.20 earlier this week. And there's more than -- more than enough funds in the Virginia coffers. We're -- actually have a billion dollars more in our Commonwealth Transportation Fund than we thought we were going to have.\par \par \uc2 And so we can suspend the gas tax for three months, and then slowly bring it back towards the end of the summer...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: ... as we head into the fall and give Virginians a break.\par \par \uc2 It's 26.5 cents a gallon. This is a moment for us to let people get out and about, get out of -- get out of their homes and come out of COVID, visit Virginia. And we can suspend this gas tax because it's money that isn't needed in the system right now. We can then turn it back on in the fall and take up all the projects that we need.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That it would be for a minimum of three months?\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, Governor.\par \par \uc2 It would be a minimum of three months first. Would you extend it if gas prices remained high? What's the plan?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, the primary target here is the summertime.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: We naturally see in crisis -- increases in gas prices during the summer, right at a time when, in fact, people want to get out. And we see all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 And so this is a great time, for the months of the summer, for us to suspend our gas tax, not charge it. Listen, this is a topic that's been supported by Democrats all over the nation. And here in Virginia, we have partisan politics that's in the way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: We can suspend this and take a real shot at the rising inflation that is causing so many Virginians challenges.\par \par \uc2 We can suspend the gas tax. We can eliminate our grocery tax. We can, in fact, declare the largest tax rebate in the history of Virginia. We can double our standard deduction. And, oh, by the way, we can go to work for our veterans and exclude up to $40,000 of their retirement from being taxed.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Virginia has become a much higher cost state than it should be. We have more than enough money in the system. There's an expectation there's more than $14 billion more in the system than we thought. We can take $5 billion of it and provide tax relief, $9 billion of it and invest in education and in law enforcement and mental health.\par \par \uc2 We can bring the cost of living down and invest. We need to get this done in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you don't mind my stepping back, also looking at politics, Governor, Martha MacCallum just had an interview with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the former vice president, who certainly sounds like he is considering running.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to make the leap whether or not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs, but you're getting a signal from other Republicans that they would run again, whether he runs or not.\par \par \uc2 You have been mentioned, with some of the ways and the big ways you have come in and surprised people with your win to become governor and some of the big changes you have made in the few months you have been governor, to be entertaining White House ambitions yourself.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would it make any difference to you if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were running or not?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, let me begin by saying I'm always honored to be included in that great circle of people.\par \par \uc2 I'm new at this job. I have been here for four months. I have got a lot to get done. My focus this year is absolutely to get Virginia moving, but also to support our congressional candidates that are going to be running in Virginia, and a number of governor's races around the country that I think Republican governors can do a lot better job than their Democrat predecessors.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a great opportunity for us across the Commonwealth of Virginia and the country to show that Republican leadership really is the best way.\par \par \uc2 What's going to happen in 2024 is so far away. And we have got immediate problems we have got our focus on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And I just look forward to doing a great job for Virginians and getting Republicans elected this year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  wasn't senator very long before he ran for the White House. Are you saying time in office would decide whether you run for office?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well -- well, as I have said, I am four months in.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And we have got a full plate in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 And I have to tell you, the most important thing I'm focused on right now is getting a budget done. As I have said, we have got tax opportunities. We have got investment opportunities in schools and law enforcement and mental health.\par \par \uc2 And this is a chance in Virginia for us to get moving. I mean, Virginia should be the best state in America. And we plan on having her there. We're going to have the best schools, the best jobs, the best law enforcement. We're going to have a government that goes to work for Virginians, as opposed to telling them what to do all the time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And, meanwhile, we're getting taxes down.\par \par \uc2 So I am so looking forward to delivering in Virginia. And, as I said, I think 2022 is a year where Republican values and leadership will absolutely be the differentiating factor in the future of our country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was brilliantly deflected, Governor, I must say.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you very much for that. We appreciate that. And best of luck with everything.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Great.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me, Neil. It's always a pleasure to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Same here.\par \par \uc2 Governor Glenn Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 All right, the battle between \uc2 Disney\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , advantage Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  might be cracking down on \uc2 Disney\uc2 , but, so far, \uc2 Disney\uc2  is managing to survive this assault, at least the way the company sees it, out with earnings and sales that disappoint a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But when it came to its \uc2 Disney\uc2 + subscription service, growth that was there, and surprisingly there, and that its theme parks continue beating estimates. Revenues from all the theme parks, including Disney World, Disneyland, all the ones abroad, they're doing very well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Ashley Webster on, well, I guess the reverberations that so far have not materialized from that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  crackdown? To be fair, Ashley, that was pretty late in that quarter, right?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: It certainly was.\par \par \uc2 And, also, what we saw in \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and their subscriber numbers going over a cliff kind of put a cold chill through \uc2 Disney\uc2  as well and to see how their subscriptions to \uc2 Disney\uc2 + would fare. No problem, in fact, the stock moving higher in after-hours here, because the number of subscribers beat the estimates for \uc2 Disney\uc2 + by some 2.5 million better than expected.\par \par \uc2 You hinted too, Neil, that both the revenue and earnings for this latest quarter for \uc2 Disney\uc2  did come in a little less than expected. But \uc2 Disney\uc2  parks, the theme parks, showed very strong performance, coming in with $6.6 billion in revenue. That was double last year's performance. Of course, that was hit by COVID restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But I want to get into the battle, the ongoing battle between Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2 . We are told that Governor DeSantis is going to be listening in, in the earnings call, which is going on right now. The question is will \uc2 Disney\uc2  CEO \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  actually talk about what's been happening between the company and Governor DeSantis, the fact that the company came out against an education bill, and the fact that then Florida lawmakers to have diluted \uc2 Disney\uc2 's special district exemption, basically self-rule?\par \par \uc2 We are -- there has been a victory for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of sorts this week, though. A federal judge in Florida threw out a lawsuit from taxpayers who sued saying, you can't do away with \uc2 Disney\uc2 's special tax district because we, the taxpayers, will be on the hook for a lot of extra money. That judge threw that out.\par \par \uc2 So, advantage perhaps to DeSantis there. But we are told by DeSantis aides that he is willing to have constructive talks with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . So, maybe, Neil, a little olive branch is being put out there. And if \uc2 Disney\uc2  accepts, maybe they will sort this whole thing out. But the earnings so far, yes, they missed on the big numbers, but, overall, streaming is doing well and so are the parks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I know a lot of this comes up on the conference call and the guidance...\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and the one that apparently the governor is on.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Yes. We're waiting.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I am curious about one thing, though.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is a sign right now that people still want to have fun. We're going to be exploring that in our final segment coming up here.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Disney\uc2  is proof of that.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: It is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because taking a trip to Disney World, Disneyland ain't cheap.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: It isn't. Well, it's a mortgage these days, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: And you know what?\par \par \uc2 Despite all the calls for boycotts, boycotts never normally work. And I'm not sure it is in this case with \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend. Nothing dopey about that.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: See what I did there? You see? Nothing dopey.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: See, you taught me this, Ashley, how to have play on words.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: I did. I'm sorry I did that, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Well, it's the best I can do.\par \par \uc2 All right, Ashley, thank you very much, my friend, Ashley Webster on all that.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of all things \uc2 Disney\uc2  and people want to have a good time, regardless of the political controversies around it, it is a steady theme we see playing out here. Prices of a lot of stuff are going up, but, apparently, Americans are willing to spend big time on trips, very good for airlines, very good for hospitality, hotel stocks.\par \par \uc2 They have been soaring, their businesses booming. Maybe with the exception of \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 , all the major carriers are looking at record traffic certainly going into this summer. And then we have reports that restaurants, pricey restaurants, at that, usually the kind that Ashley Webster goes to -- they have the tassel on the menu and all that -- anyway, that they're booked solid.\par \par \uc2 Good luck getting a reservation anywhere. It's a trend that right now is the friend for the consumer, because there's certain things that consumers will not skimp on.\par \par \uc2 Enter my next guests to see if they share that view.\par \par \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2  is a senior editor over at \uc2 Travelzoo\uc2 . We have got our own \uc2 Susan Li\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Susan's a big spender, period, by the way.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Aw.\par \par \uc2 And Heather Wheeler, The Krazy Coupon Lady.\par \par \uc2 Susan, I ribbed you good there. What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Americans are saying, look, we know how pricey things are, but there's some things we're not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And trips and fun are two of them.\par \par \uc2 LI: Look, I see you at those white tablecloth dining venues here in New York City, Neil. So, don't hide.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, the Olive Garden?\par \par \uc2 LI: But, yes, I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that was me. That was me. You're right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes with the free garlic bread. No, I'm talking about the three-star Michelin restaurants.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think New York City is really a great example as to the resilience in the consumer. You're paying 20 percent more for airfares. Is that stopping people from going to La Guardia, even Newark to get their trips? No. That's a record level there for airfare prices.\par \par \uc2 Also, groceries, you're paying 10 percent more than last year. That's the highest pace since the 1980s. Same thing for rents. No longer are we looking for those walk-ups. We want doormen here in New York City. And I think that's exactly what's happening across the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 The consumer, regardless of where interest rates are going, they have been locked up for around two years, and they just want to get out there and spend.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, as the New Jerseyan here, young lady, I will let that even Newark fly.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dave, let me ask you, all kidding aside.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of it? I mean, are there some things that people you have talked to, maybe yourself, that, look, you won't -- you won't cheat yourself?\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVELZOO.COM: Well, that's the thing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we were projecting from the beginning of the year a jump in demand and jump in pricing, but those expectations have been shattered. April, the month-to-month airfare is up close to 19 percent. And we expect another 10 percent jump by the time that this month is up.\par \par \uc2 Here's the thing. Travel is not a cost or an expense. It is an investment now. I mean, it is a prerogative for a lot of folks who have not been able to do it at ease over the last couple of years. What's -- what's the payoff on this investment? Well-being, mental health, happiness.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, the price tag, we're willing to pay for that kind of a feeling, if you will, that wellness perhaps that we haven't experienced in a couple of years is definitely higher. And we're prioritizing these day-to-day expenses. We're not seeing cancellations to speak of across the travel industry.\par \par \uc2 New bookings have dipped a little bit in the last few weeks. But at the end of the day, people are ready to go and, in some cases, go big.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Heather, I'm always thinking, you're The Krazy Coupon Lady, but you're savvy as all heck at finding a bargain and a buy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What amazes me is the number of Americans who aren't necessarily hunting for a bargain or a buy. They're going to pay top dollar, if necessary, because they desperately, after two years locked in their homes, want to get out.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER WHEELER, THE KRAZY COUPON LADY: Yes, it's all about -- it's not about telling yourself no. It's about finding a way to afford the things that you want. That's what we talk about all the time at TheKrazyCouponLady.com.\par \par \uc2 And let's take restaurants for example. We're tired of being home. We're tired of cooking every day. There are ways that you can save, though. Instead of doing Uber Eats and \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , where you're paying for like an extra convenience to have it delivered to your home, look for restaurants that offer kids eat free days. These vary a lot by location because so many restaurants are franchised.\par \par \uc2 But just do a quick \uc2 Google\uc2  search for kids eat free near me to find restaurants that offer that. Lots of restaurants, like Applebee's, Chili's, Red Robin and Denny's, all offer kids eat free days.\par \par \uc2 And one of the biggest things that's costing us at restaurants is the beverages. So I say skip the drinks while you're eating out and instead opt for water. That will save you about 20 percent on the cost of your bill.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Whoa. Heather, let's not go nuts here. Let's not go nuts.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WHEELER: Listen, times are tight.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know. I hear you. I hear what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 But, Susan, another thing is, I used to be offended when people would give me the senior discount at restaurants. Now I say, bring it on, bring it on.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And my kids, I don't even invite them anymore. My wife and I go out and say, you kids, look around, hunt in the pantry. See what we have.\par \par \uc2 So I'm wondering if people -- this is something they will not give up on. I suspect it will keep a floor to this slowdown. I'm actually serious about that, because I -- unlike the '70s years experience...\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... we're in a little bit better position than we were then.\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, definitely, because I feel like at least we had some savings cashed up during the last few years of the stimulus checks. But I will tell you this. I just love what The Krazy Coupon Lady was saying.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to say no to myself. I'm not denying myself after these two years. Maybe I won't buy those $600 shoes, but I am still going out to get that soup and to order those Uber Eats, I will tell you that, because that's just the mentality of the consumer these days.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, Gabe, if that is that what's going on, you kind of touched on something interesting near the end of your last remarks, that it's slowing down a little bit.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to put words in your mouth, that those levels aren't being sustained. What are you saying?\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: Yes, the slowdown is in new bookings, especially as we head into those sort of peak summer months, July into August.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: And then we see another little bump as we head towards September and October.\par \par \uc2 That could just be people becoming more flexible with their travel plans. So, that July-August trip now becomes a September-October trip. That doesn't mean that they're canceling or avoiding travel. They may be just becoming a bit more flexible to find some glimmer of hope and a bit of relief on the pocketbook.\par \par \uc2 And there are still some areas of -- some positive areas in the travel industry. Last week, United and \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  engaged in Hawaii airfare wars.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: People were getting $99 fares last week to Hawaii.\par \par \uc2 Cruises are still a pretty big bargain.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A big thing.\par \par \uc2 SAGLIE: So maybe we're looking at places and ways to get there that are different, but we're still going to go.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, any of you need Olive Garden coupons, I have got them.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, it's not just you, Krazy Coupon Lady.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you all very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Want to put that in perspective, because, having lived through the '70s, the backdrop wasn't -- wasn't like that then. So, with all the problems and all the selling, just a little perspective.\par \par \uc2 Try to enjoy yourself.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220511ei5b0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Inflation Failures Pile Up; Incoming White House Press Secretary Has History Of Radicalism And Extreme Rhetoric\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Larry Kudlow, Geraldo Rivera, Joe Concha, Leo  Terrell \par \uc2 8,133 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 We begin with breaking news tonight. Now, we have good news and bad news out of the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 All right. First, the good news, the most pompous and dishonest press secretary in American history, she will be gone at the end of the week. The bad news, her replacement is a full-blown tin foil hat conspiracy theorist who thinks that many of our political opponents are racist and we've got a full HANNITY investigation into \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with this, the big guy \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , his genius new plan to battle inflation. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I agree with what Chairman Powell said last week that the number one threat is the strength in -- in that strength that we built is inflation. Look, I know you got to be frustrated. I know I can taste it.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Translation, in this country, do you take any responsibility to your policies?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think our policies help not hurt. Think about what they say. The vast majority of the -- of the economists think that this is going to be a real tough problem to solve but it's not because of spending.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm screaming at the TV, how have they helped Joe? Now since Joe has clearly lost any ability to communicate because of his cognitive decline, allow me to try to translate for him. There's no plan to curb inflation. By the way, it's not transitory either. There's no plan to lower gas prices. There's no plan to spur on economic growth or as \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported, quote, inside the administration, there is a sense of frustration doom and some say magical thinking about inflation. Magical thinking?\par \par \uc2 We're now on the verge of what is a devastating recession, coupled with record-setting inflation and of course on top of that a supply chain crisis and gas prices, yep, they just hit a brand new record high along with diesel prices.\par \par \uc2 Now, the stock market is plunging right before our eyes. Fortunes, I mean fortunes are being lost by the middle class in this country and people on fixed incomes in this country. That means their pensions, retirement funds, 401ks, they're getting wrecked in the market.\par \par \uc2 There's a nationwide shortage of baby formula and the White House is thinking about the economy in a magical way. Well, it's time for heads to roll. Joe's economic and energy policies, they're nothing but a complete and utter disaster.\par \par \uc2 Biden's inflation is now costing you, the average American, the average American household over $5,200 annually and new numbers out today show the average American is paying $2,000 more per year just for gasoline. But nothing is going to change because the Democratic Party is ruled by socialists that are all part of this religious climate alarmist cult and they will never retreat from their radical agenda. I don't