given out at about 7:00 this morning. And you saw a doubling down on policies that have failed: the green policies that haven't worked, they haven't filled in the gaps left by his, his war on fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 He keeps talking, he kept talking about the Putin price hikes, something that clearly has been shown incorrect because the, the trajectory of not only the gas prices, but inflation in general started as soon as he became president. And we're on the same trajectory, even after Putin's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It's it wasn't that that started the price hikes. The price hikes started way before Russia was under radar. So, he's, he's essentially what I don't understand it makes no economic sense. But it also doesn't make any political sense, because you've seen the polls, he keeps getting worse and worse ratings on handling the economy. So, it doesn't make any economic sense, and it doesn't make any political sense.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDEN: Well, I don't think there's any other option right now politically for this president. And I think maybe the White House and the president realizes that because that was a political speech about an economic topic. Sometimes, when President speak about the economy, you have a number of economic proposals, whether it's, you know, lowering taxes or raising taxes, depending on your point of view. There wasn't much of that today, because once inflation is out of the bottle, and you know, it is right now running the last number, we had eight and a half percent on the consumer level tomorrow, we'll find out that it's probably still above eight percent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are 40-year high rates of inflation. There's not a ton you can do about it if you're a president. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could attack it. They are, they're raising interest rates, what's that going to do? Well, it's going to slow the economy maybe even push us into recession, because that might be the only cure for this right now. So, from the President's point of view, when you don't have many options, you go on the attack against --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I would argue that there's a lot that you can do to cause the inflation --\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Right. You could -- exactly.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: -- exactly what has happened over the past year and a half of all the very fabric poses both on the demand side and on the supply side. Supply side, he was paying people much too long to stay home. He was keeping these COVID policies that should have been ended sooner. On spending policies, of course, he passed essentially a $2 trillion COVID relief plan which was actually more of a social spending plan, then of course the Transportation Act which was bipartisan. But he's been, he's been doing a lot of things that of making the switch been making the situation worse, and primarily on energy policy, which has just been horrendous in terms of his war on fossil fuels. And that's what's causing the prices to go up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's so interesting, because, you know, generally, you think to, get an economy back up and running, you want to provide incentives to businesses, right? You want to make, give them tax breaks, you want to give them things like depreciation, if they buy equipment, you want to get them inspired to employ a lot more people, right? But the solution --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: If you've got a business mind, which you do --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The solutions that we're hearing are not on that side of the equation, they're on like, let's lower prices, let's lower costs to make it a little bit easier for people to absorb inflation. But you know, can they, can they do that? Can they offset that?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Now, you saw a, an administration last year that was trying to stimulate an economy that maybe didn't even need it at the time and gave it too much stimulus that helped to put us in this situation. So, now, you're exactly right, Martha, you're in a position where you don't have as many options. And you come up with ones that, frankly, from an economic perspective, probably won't do much in the way of good.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And he's focusing on the producers again, he was pointing out once again, the meat producers, the meatpackers --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bad corporations --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: The oil companies, et cetera. I mean, rather than rather than providing incentives, he's creating more regulations for these companies that will make prices go even higher because those regulations are passed on his higher prices to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think he got to it in this speech, but one of the details that I read in his plan was that the Congress is going to build a million homes which I thought was an interesting to flood the market with more homes so that you would lower home prices. He didn't get to that one on the list, but I'd love to hear more about that plan when they get to get out there their hammers. They're all going to be. Thank you very much, Connell and David. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 So, bombshell announcement today from this man who seems to stir up headlines almost every day, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , officially revealing whether his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover includes a platform that would include the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Devin Nunez, CEO of Trump Media, Truth Social, and tech, his technology group reacts to this breaking news that's coming up. Also, the Mississippi Governor also a man very much in the news right now, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , is at the heart of the Supreme Court case on abortion because it came from his state and legislation that originated there. Now, he's making new headlines today. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Designating Russia as a state sponsor terrorism, essentially squarely recognize that it's in the company of Syria, Iran, Cuba, North Korea, that's the company it keeps.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): For 20 years he's literally gotten away with murder, now it's time to designate him in a fashion befitting his conduct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, as Ukrainians accused Putin have raining down hypersonic missiles in Odessa today, an expert warn that the war is going to continue to escalate. Both Democrats and Republicans want Russia declared a state sponsor of terror. It would infuriate Putin, no doubt he didn't like being called a war criminal. And it would likely send a message to China as well. Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  joins us on that in moments. But first to Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill, where today we had a unique hearing of the nation's intelligence chiefs, and the biggest warnings that they think the United States is facing right now, largely from Russia, and China. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. Well, the conflict in Ukraine could tell us a lot about China, and also what could happen in Taiwan. That's something to look at here in the coming days here. Now, the \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  Director Scott Berrier, he testified that the war in Ukraine is now a stalemate. The intelligence community has bragged that it correctly predicted that Russia would invade Ukraine, but senators questioned the precision of US intelligence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ANGUS KING (I-ME): The assessment was Ukraine would be overrun in a matter of weeks. That was grossly wrong.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. SCOTT BERRIER, DEFENSE INTELLIGENCE AGENCY DIRECTOR: I think the intelligence community did a great job on this issue, Senator, and we will --\par \par \uc2 KING: \uc2 General\uc2 , how can you possibly say that when we were told explicitly Kyiv would fall in three days and the Ukraine would, would fail, fall in two weeks? You're telling me that was accurate intelligence?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The botched predictions called into question what the U.S. anticipates from China regarding Taiwan, Director of National Intelligence \uc2 Avril Haines\uc2 , in Taiwan, is in a critical period between now and 2030, and the future of Taiwan is linked to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AVRIL HAINES, DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: Whatever lessons China learns coming out of the Russia Ukraine crisis might affect that, timeline as well as, as you indicated, you know, whether or not our capabilities, Taiwan's capabilities, other decisions that will have to be made between now and then that will affect the timeline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There's concern the U.S. could become involved in the war in Ukraine, and that sets the table for China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Do you worry that that Beijing might see an opportunity to invade Taiwan in the very near future? Should the United States get drawn in to an actual conflict to kinetic conflict with Russia?\par \par \uc2 BERRIER: I think that's a remote possibility, China would see that as a window to open to take advantage of that, based on the fact that they, they probably aren't ready to do that right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The House votes tonight on a bill to provide nearly $40 billion in aid to Ukraine. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, thank you very much. Joining now by a Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina. Good to have you here, Senator Graham today.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What really stood out to you from this hearing today? What do you -- it almost feels like sometimes people sort of get used to the war going on in Ukraine, and look away for -- from it for a moment. But there are so many implications here.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, number one, the assessment that what happens in the Ukraine will affect China's decision about Taiwan is dead on.\par \par \uc2 I was in Taiwan in about two weeks ago. So, what happens to Ukraine does affect all the other bad actors. But what I see is bipartisanship breaking out a level that I haven't seen since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Senator Blumenthal and myself, have a resolution, urging the Secretary of State \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  to designate Russia as a state sponsor of terrorism. That would be huge.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian Parliament last week passed a resolution, urging the Congress to urge the Biden administration to designate Russia state sponsored terrorism. I think I want to put it in the supplemental package. It's not just money, for economic stability, more weapons, we need to designate Russia as a state sponsored terrorism.\par \par \uc2 And here's what the ambassador of the Ukraine told the Republicans today at lunch: if he designated Russia as a state sponsor of terrorism, she believed the floodgates would open to other countries helping Ukraine more.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. With regard to this other question about the escalation of this war, \uc2 Avril Haines\uc2 , the DNI said that the war is going to become more unpredictable, and it's going to escalate.\par \par \uc2 And then, there's this exchange with Senator Joe Manchin about whether or not the United States might get drawn in to a boots on the ground situation.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): We reading it now, they have the ability to win if we continue to support, without us being pulled into a land war with them on their own, can they win?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. SCOTT BERRIER, DIRECTOR, \uc2 DEFENSE INTELLIGENCE AGENCY\uc2 : I think that is a -- that is a difficult prediction to make. Right now, I think where the agency is at is a -- is a -- is a prolonged stalemate, should no factor change on either side.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the Russians continue to do what they're doing. And we continue to do what we're doing for the Ukrainians. I see that as a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're now in a situation where we spent $11 billion on the war in Ukraine, and there's a proposal for 40 billion more.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to Americans who look at that and say, what are we getting drawn into here? Again, as the money continues to go, and I think most people understand why. But what about the possibility that we could also be drawn into this with our -- with our own men and women's boots on the ground?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, nobody's suggesting boots on the ground. But if there's a nuclear weapon exploded inside of the Ukraine to break the West will to help Ukraine, that would be an attack on \uc2 NATO\uc2  itself, then, we'd be at war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 You kind of let Putin know what happens if he uses chemical weapons, that would be a no-fly zone, in my view. If he explodes a nuke inside to Ukraine, he radiates half of Europe, then, that's a full blown conflict between Russia and \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is not going to do that. He is having a hard time dealing with the Ukrainians, much less \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here is what I would say to all these hand ringers right now. Have the courage the Ukrainians have, we're providing weapons, economic support, moral support, they're doing the fighting.\par \par \uc2 If Putin wins, there goes Taiwan. If Putin wins, then the map of Europe will be rewritten because he's not going to stop in Ukraine. But if we can stop Putin in Ukraine, and take him down over time, the world is a much safer place.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The outcome of it -- the outcome Ukraine matters to America. If Putin gets away with this, then, all the laws about war crimes become jokes. If he loses, then, I'm convinced, China will take a hard look at what they do in the future.\par \par \uc2 And we need to designate Russia as a state sponsored terrorism right now, because they deserve that designation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , always good to have you here, sir. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, joining me now, Niall Ferguson, senior fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2  at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 , and senior faculty fellow at the Belfer Center for Science and International Affairs at Harvard.\par \par \uc2 Neil, welcome back. Good to have you back with us again today.\par \par \uc2 NIALL FERGUSON, SENIOR FELLOW AT THE HOOVER INSTITUTION AT STANFORD UNIVERSITY: You know, I guess my question at the beginning is sort of a larger one, looking at the situation on the ground right now in Ukraine. Because you have the bombing today of Odesa.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, Putin wants that whole area from Mariupol, all the way through Odessa. But at the same time he's stymied in the east and lost in Kyiv -- in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see this going? How long do you see this going on for?\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: Well, I wish I had the confidence of those who say this is going to be a stalemate or a frozen conflict, a phrase I don't like. I'm not going to disagree with Senator Graham, far be it for me to do that. But I do think we need to recognize that there are some pretty big uncertainties at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Let me just give you a couple. One is that there can't really be a stalemate given the weaponry that we are supplying the Ukrainians with. And the fact that the Russians, because of our sanctions are running out of sophisticated weaponry. I think a stalemate is getting less and less likely.\par \par \uc2 Russia's offensive in the Donbas is showing signs of failing, it's moving extremely slowly. And there is a non-trivial risk that the Russian army, which has already suffered very heavy casualties, and it's really quite demoralized, loses the will to fight a war for which there clearly isn't a great deal of enthusiasm amongst the troops, and indeed, amongst the officers.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that we need to be aware of is that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  not about to resign and go off to retirement in the south of France. His position is extraordinary vulnerable, because this operation has gone so wrong. And he does have options that we don't really have.\par \par \uc2 And the option to escalate to the nuclear level by using -- say a tactical nuclear weapon in the west of Ukraine can't be ruled out completely.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not sure I agree with Senator Graham that we have a very clear way of deterring him from doing that if he's losing the conventional war. So, we need to be aware that there's some big tail risks here. And it's not very obvious to me how we stop this situation, becoming a great deal more unstable than terms like stalemate and frozen conflict imply.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think the -- that they're great points. You have someone -- a leader who is clearly dug in. And he's been talking about the Nazi threat that as he calls it, coming from Ukraine, and the eastern countries on his border for years, and trying to convince people in Russia for years that this is the threat to their country.\par \par \uc2 And now, he's moved forward with it, it's very difficult to see how he sort of gracefully exits this and says, oh, we got a little bit of Donbas, so, we're OK.\par \par \uc2 How about the fact that he was sitting -- much has been made about his posture at this parade and how short his speech was. And the fact that he was sitting there with a little blankie on his lap?\par \par \uc2 And people question his health. They say nobody else was, you know, sort of warmed up with a little blanket at this -- at this parade. What did you make of that whole scene there?\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: Well, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  can no more cheat the grim reaper than the rest of us. But nobody really knows for sure the state of his health. All kinds of rumors have done the rounds for years now.\par \par \uc2 I've even met doctors that have been flown in from the west to see him, but they won't tell me what's up. I think it would be a tremendous relief for the Western world, not just for Ukraine, if Putin's health failed. But I don't think we can base our foreign policy around that scenario.\par \par \uc2 And in any case, he's not a dying man, he's an ageing man, as far as I can see. He's not a crazy man. He's a man who's miscalculated very badly.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we have to assume that he's not about to oblige us by kicking the bucket.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, we are going to have to deal with this crisis, not just for weeks, but for months, and potentially, years to come in. And Martha, there's a kind of analogy here with the Korean War in my mind, which was the hot war that signaled we really were in a Cold War.\par \par \uc2 I think we're in a cold war right now, and this is the hot war that makes us realize it. Only this time, you know, Russia is the junior partner, China is the senior partner.\par \par \uc2 The administration thinks that it's sending a signal to China by inflicting very heavy damage via Ukraine on the Russians. I'm not convinced that that's the message that's being received in Beijing. There seems to be many people very confident that they know how \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  thinks about this, and that he's going to be deterred from acting with respect to Taiwan, because things have gone so badly for Putin in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think we can be completely certain about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fascinating. Yes. And as President Xi has said, that, you know, it's nothing like Ukraine, the relationship with Russia and Ukraine and China and Taiwan, because he said Taiwan is part of China. There's just no comparison. Which is exactly obviously the way that Putin feels about Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Niall, thank you. It's always great to talk to you. Thanks for coming in. Hope we'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 FERGUSON: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  reveals whether President Trump -- former President Trump is welcome back on the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  platform if and when that deal closes.\par \par \uc2 Trump Media and Technology Group CEO Devin Nunez weighs in on that, only on THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 And as leaders in blue states declare themselves an oasis for abortion seekers, spending billions to add more facilities in places like New York and California and Illinois.\par \par \uc2 My next guest made headlines for this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, HOST, CNN: But so just to be clear, you have no intention of seeking to ban IUDs or plan B?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES, (R-MS): That is not what we are focused on at this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): That is Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  on the little talked about side of the story. Prescription pills now account for more than half of all abortions that take place in America as clinics take a backseat to the envelope that comes in the mail.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): While some states that have had some troubles with their economy and homelessness in places like California, New York, where crime levels are high, they see this moment perhaps to take action. Saying that what they will be is an oasis for people who are seeking an abortion, given the pending Supreme Court rules, if indeed they do overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And they will even help women to come to their state as part of these programs for which they're adding billions of dollars -- taking billions of dollars from their budget moving into that effort.\par \par \uc2 So, in moments, Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  who signed the law that triggered, and then went all the way up to this historic legal fight at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 He will join us in moments.\par \par \uc2 But first of correspondent David Lee Miller, with more on this deepening divide between the states. Hi, David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Anticipating that a number of states are going -- that the Roe v. Wade is going to be overturned, a number of states are now taking action to protect access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (voice-over): Here in New York, Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , today, said the state is providing $35 million to support abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 New York lawmakers are also considering a number of bills to safeguard abortion rights. Among them, legislation to protect women who come to New York from other states seeking an abortion. A proposed law would prevent law enforcement from cooperating with investigators from states where abortion is illegal.\par \par \uc2 Governor Hochul says New York is the role model for the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): I'm really proud that you will lead the nation, hopefully, set an example for the other states to realize that all of us have a responsibility to welcome with open arms those who need these services.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: California is also bracing for an increase in out of state women seeking an abortion. Lawmakers there are considering a bill that would allow nurse practitioners without a physician overseeing the procedure to perform abortions. Voters later this year could be asked if abortion rights should be included in California's state constitution.\par \par \uc2 And in Connecticut today, Democratic Governor \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2  signed the first- in-the-nation law that protects patients and healthcare providers from legal action in states where abortion is illegal. The measure is seen as a response to legislation in Texas that allowed anyone to file a civil suit for violating that state's ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And according to a think tank that studies reproductive rights and public policy, if Roe v. Wade is no longer law, 26 states are certain or likely to ban abortion. And that means an estimated 36 million women will be crossing state lines to undergo the procedure. So not exactly the type of tourism a lot of those states ever envisioned. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: True. Thank you very much, David Lee Miller.\par \par \uc2 So with that we bring in Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  of Mississippi whose state law prompted the case that went all the way to the Supreme Court and led to the leaked draft opinion. Governor, it's good to have you with us. Thank you for joining us today. I'd like to start by playing this sound bite from \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , the mayor of Chicago who echoes \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's statements.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D), CHICAGO: If you look around Chicago, we've got a number of states that have the so called trigger laws that would ban abortion upon the reversal Roe. I think the list is long. And Chicago is going to be an oasis. I'm in the Midwest, and we've got to be ready.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Governor, I guess one of the questions, you just heard David Lee Miller's report about how many people would then be traveling across state lines for abortions, would you ban travel for abortions over state lines?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): Well, thank you for having me on Martha. And again, that's a conversation that I can't imagine a mayor of a city or a governor of a state trying to make their place an oasis to have people come there to abort unborn children. And I think that's -- the primary distinction here that must be made is that when you're talking about abortions, there's an unborn child in that womb, an unborn American citizen.\par \par \uc2 And it's just, it's mind boggling to me that these politicians would say this. I think that they are not only misreading the tea leaves, I think they're misreading the political dynamics out there. And they don't recognize that in America today, our abortion laws and what they're trying to do in their cities in their states, make their abortion laws more and more like North Korea and China and less and less like the Western civilized world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So but I think one of the points of this Supreme Court decision, and we don't have it yet, which I would want to point out once again, we don't have it, we just have a leaked opinion from February. But the idea is that it was wrongly decided because this is a state right issue, and that it should be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 So take me through what would happen in Mississippi because you have a trigger laws, so that would mean that if when this decision comes out, there would then that day or the next day, any abortion would be banned in Mississippi, is that correct?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, you are correct that the -- those of us who believe that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided that if the court decides, and again, it's still a big if, because we are talking about a leaked draft opinion, but if a majority of the justices decide to overturn Roe, because they believe that it was wrongly decided as I do, in 1972, you're exactly right, the decision making within go back to the democratic process such that the individual legislatures and the policymaking bodies, those involved in the democratic process in all 50 states would make decisions based upon what they view or their citizens wanted.\par \par \uc2 In a state like Mississippi, you're correct. We do have the cases before the Supreme Court. It is a 15-week ban. But also in 2007, the Mississippi Legislature passed legislation creating a trigger law. I would like to point out that at that time, the Speaker of the House of Representatives was a Democrat, the chairman of the Public Health Committee in the House of Representatives was a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 And so that speaks a little bit to how far Democrats and the Democratic Party has moved to the left on this issue. But that trigger law says that within 10 days, the Attorney General of our state will certify that Roe v. Wade has been overturned and that abortion will be illegal in the state of Mississippi with the exception of rape and the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to ask you about that exception in just a minute but I guess so you're never going to get the 15 weeks, right? I mean that that 15 weeks is where most Americans are. And I realized we're going state by state under this construct. But our recent polling shows 54 percent of people think that 15 weeks is a reasonable time after which to ban abortion, and that was the law that was passed by your state legislature. So why not revert to that, rather than something that happened in 2007?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, because the law that passed in 2007 made it clear that the trigger law, the trigger would come into effect in the event that the court overturns Roe, the 15 week ban, which is before the court so that the court has the ability to decide that the 15 week ban and upholding the 15-week ban is something that they want to do if they choose to go further and do what we asked, which is ultimately overturn Roe, then our trigger law will come into effect.\par \par \uc2 But Martha, to answer your question, why go beyond the 15-week ban? Because there are literally 63 million babies that were aborted over the last 51 years since 1972 in America. And if we go beyond the 15-week ban, then we have the chance to save babies lives. That's the reason we would go beyond that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Governor, I understand that. And I understand your argument, absolutely. I'm just asking, because the whole argument is that then the states will decide, the people will decide on each state. And the people in your state, most recently decided on a 15-week ban. So I'm wondering if they will get what they voted for. Or they will get something that goes back to 2007. And you've -- I think you've made that clear. I have two really quick questions I want to ask you. So you said for the life of the mother and rape are the two exceptions in Mississippi, correct?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: That is correct. In the 2007 bill that passed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's my question, how is incest not rape?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, I think in many instances, it is rape and would be defined as such. Now why the legislature in 2007 made that particular distinction? I can't answer that question, because I was not involved in the legislature at that time. But what I will tell you is in to your previous question, why not give the people in Mississippi the 15-week ban, because remember, the 15-week ban is really out of touch. Even with the Western world, there are 39 out of 42 countries in Europe that have more restrictions on abortion than Mississippi would have if that 15-week ban went into effect.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I understand that. But the question is, yes, I'm just -- I just once again, the point is that each state would decide what the people of that state when they go to the voting booth, and it's a referendum or whatever it is, and in your state what passed was this 15-week ban. So I'm just trying to make sure that I understand how it's all going to work.\par \par \uc2 Just before I let you go, one more question. Would you still allow abortion pills to be sent to people in Mississippi if they chose to use them before that 15-week ban if that was, if there wasn't a total ban in place?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: If they had treatment by a physician, that would be an option for some individuals.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Governor Reeves. Thank you very much. It's great to have you with us today. I appreciate your answering the questions. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Thank you for having me on, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's another question for you. Will former President Trump accept \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's invitation to rejoin \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? He says it was a bad decision that it didn't make any sense. And he says \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  agrees with him. Trump Media and Technology Group CEO \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  reveals the answer to that question, which has been on a lot of people's minds over all of this when we come back.\par \par \uc2 If the president decides to run again, and is, you know, offered back a spot on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , are you saying that he won't accept it?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  is here on the breaking news involving former President Trump and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . But first we want to take you back to the White House briefing room, where moments ago Fox Business correspondent \uc2 Edward Lawrence\uc2  pressed \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  on President Biden's plan to bring down inflation and this is an interesting exchange. So let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The President said today that inflation is his top domestic priority, has that replaced climate change then for this administration as the top priority?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I would say first, that inflation and the rising costs for people across the country has long been the top issue for Pete -- for in every poll we've seen and certainly has been the number one issue for the president for some time. When he came into office, he said that his agenda was going to focus on four major priorities. That includes addressing the climate crisis, of course, and he will continue to do that. But we are certainly aware of that right now, in this moment, that inflation and reducing costs is what is most on the minds of Americans.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So the President was also asked if he took responsibility for any of those policies related to inflation, not just gas prices but inflation. When he took office inflation was 2.5 percent, year over year, it's now 8.5 percent. Invasion didn't happen until about one year into his term. So why doesn't the President take responsibility?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I think the President said today that he's President and that we control all three, obviously Congress, the House, and Senate as well. What is also true is that the pandemic was going on before he took office that fighting this historic pandemic, which has been front and center for his agenda. We know and every economist will tell you this has been the major driver of inflation over the past year plus. And we've continued to fight the pandemic since then.\par \par \uc2 We've also heard Chairman Powell, Secretary Yellen and others convey and we've seen this in nearly every element of data we've seen on inflation since President Putin invaded Ukraine that energy prices account for the majority of inflationary increases over the past several weeks since the war began. So you don't have to take my word for it, that's what economists and data have told us.\par \par \uc2 What our focus is on and you heard the President say this today is not whether inflation is or isn't an issue, we all agree it's an issue. What our plan is and what the President's plan is to address costs and bring down costs is what he outlined this morning. It's a multistep plan. What are we seeing on the other side, we're seeing the plan proposed by Chairman Scott, which would instead raise costs on the American people and sunset or end, whatever you want to call it, Medicare and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, that from evermore in just a short time ago with the Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  at the White House. And former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be able to potentially tweet again, when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  takes over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , which he says probably two or three months away from closing that deal, if indeed it fully closes. He just mentioned that earlier. So he says that the permanent ban after the Capitol riot was quote, morally bad and foolish in the extreme.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, TESLA & SPACEX CEO: I do think that it was not correct to ban \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think that was a mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But the former president told Fox News that he does not plan to tweet and he will stick with his own site, Truth Social. So let's bring in the man who runs that site, Trump Media and Technology Group CEO, \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , who is also the former chairman of the House Intel Committee. Great to have you with us, sir. Thanks for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN NUNES\uc2 , TRUMP MEDIA & TECHNOLOGY GROUP CEO: Great to be with you, as always, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what do you think about what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  had to say there?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Well, look, we're very supportive of what Elon is saying. The question is, will he actually be able to accomplish what he's saying, dealing with all the employees, dealing with all the algorithms. But, you know, the goal has been for us, for President Trump is to give the American people their voice back and open the internet backup. And that's what we're trying to create.\par \par \uc2 I would just add, though, that there's, I think there's an important point here that is lost on people. True Social is wide open right now in the \uc2 Apple\uc2  App Store. And you don't have to ask anyone's opinion. There's no big tech tyrant that can cancel you. You don't have to go to a billionaire to ask permission, your lead on Democrat, Republican, whoever you are. And we're not going to censor you for political speech.\par \par \uc2 So, we are accomplishing what the mission of our company is, which is to get this internet open back up. And we're glad that Elon is making, you know, saying the right things. Let's see if he backs that up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's another statement from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , just a moment ago, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MUSK: I talked with \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  about this. And he and I are of the same mind, which is that permanent bans should be extremely rare. And really reserved for people where they're trying for accounts that are bots, or spam, scam accounts, where there's just no legitimacy to the account at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, with regard to former President Trump, he always said that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was the best way to get his message out, that he reached millions of people in an unfiltered way. He didn't feel like he was misrepresented by someone who was interpreting what he had to say. How many millions of people is he currently reaching on social?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Well, he's got, well, I think almost 2.7 million followers already, and we're only a few weeks old. So, you know, and remember what we're trying to do, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is an old platform. It's a PR wire for the elitist, for blue checkers, for journalists, for fake news, for Hollywood celebrities. What we're doing over at True Social is we're trying to make social media fun again.\par \par \uc2 And the advantage that we have is we have millions of Americans who have been banned or censored on the other platforms. So for example, you know, how are we a fun place? We have these awesome meme makers that make the coolest cartoons you've ever seen. And they've been banned from all the other platforms, so we're a home for them. And they know they can come and establish a home base where we're not cancelable by these other big tech companies, because we built this block by block through social built, block by block, we can't be canceled. That's been our goal.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to be that family friendly, safe place, you're not going to be politically censored. And it's important to note and I mentioned this last week on your show, I think, Martha is that the users have moved on. I mean, where are they really going that right now? TikTok owned by China, likely all that data is going to the Chinese government. We don't know that for sure. But that's where -- there's probably 100 or million more Americans that are on there, a lot of children, a lot of young people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A lot of kids on it, that's for sure. I just want to play this from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 NUNES: \uc2 Instagram\uc2  also, I would say.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, true. This is \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , when she was asked about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  saying that he would reverse the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ban on former President Trump. Here's what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: What I will say, broadly speaking, is that our effort is to of course, make sure that freedom of speech is protected across the country, but that also these platforms are not used for forums for disinformation. And we have seen a history of that not just on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but also on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . The President believes there's more that needs to be done in reforming Section 230 and there's a lot of bipartisan interest in that. So this may be a reminder of the urgency of doing that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I don't think they're too excited about the fact that that \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is saying, yes, I, you know, I think that was a mistake to ban President Trump. And I'm sure, you know, he hopes that President Trump will change his mind and come back to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . What would you say to \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Well, of course, this is what they want, it's they've had a monopoly for years. They own 95 percent of the news. And then they funnel that news, which becomes propaganda, through the tech tyrants at \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . So that, you know, people don't get to actually see what either people that are in the center, center right. And even in many cases, if you disagreed on something involving, let's just say COVID, which there was a myriad of issues that you could disagree with one another on.\par \par \uc2 If you didn't toe, the party line of the left, you are essentially censored. And so that's why it's so important for, you know, what we're doing in True Social is we're giving people their voice back, but the marketplace is really where there's hundreds of millions of people is on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and TikTok. And, you know, we're trying to build out features that aren't not only make fun, you know, make social media fun again, and don't censor, but we're trying to make sure that we have a place that's taking the best attributes of those platforms, so that we can also reach tens of millions of Americans ultimately, where we're a fun safe place to be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I think that everyone would agree that it's healthy to get these networks open. And, you know, the more the merrier and may the best companies win. \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , thank you very much good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 So for the first time in almost 60 years, the now 96-year-old Queen Elizabeth did not attend the State Opening of Parliament today. Prince Charles attended in her place making a historic moment. Prince William next in line, also in attendance today. Buckingham Palace saying that the Queen reluctantly stayed home due to some episodic mobility problems that she's experienced. She has been seen using a cane in recent months. In February, we know that she battled COVID.\par \par \uc2 Neil Sean is U.K. media correspondent and royal reporter. And he watched it all unfold today from up close and personal there. Neil, good to see you this afternoon. What's the significance of this for the Queen and for Charles and William?\par \par \uc2 NEIL SEAN, U.K. MEDIA CORRESPONDENT: Well, good afternoon, Martha. Well, for me really, it was like a window into the future, you know. And what was really somber from my perspective was, you know, the crown just sat there, you know, and then you've got Prince Charles, who looked incredibly emotional, because obviously, he's looking into his future. And it's a future that really nobody wants to see because the Queen has been a mainstay in our lives now for so many years.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's incredible to think, and also just take a moment to think about what it must be like for her majesty having to make that decision. And no doubt watch it back in Windsor on television. You know, it's very difficult for her too. She wouldn't want to have given in so easily. So, you know, a lot of sympathy goes towards our gracious Majesty the Queen as ever.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're watching Prince Charles earlier there, sitting on the throne. And everyone is used to seeing Queen Elizabeth in that position. I think even people who could care less about the Royals don't sort of realize the presence that she's had sort of in the backdrop of everything, every historic event, over the course of all of her time on the throne there. What are we learning about how things are shaping up for this Jubilee celebration, which, you know, I'm sure she hopes she will be able to really be part of?\par \par \uc2 SEAN: I think it's been very difficult, Martha. Over the last year, you know, we've seen a decline in the Queen's mobility. And obviously on the year that really, she needed her mobility. For her obviously, she's had a few setbacks. We are hopeful that she will be at some point involved. But the difficulty is, you know, you can imagine she's a very strong woman wanted to go around, meet all these people, her subjects, her people after 70 years on the throne, that's had to be all scaled back.\par \par \uc2 And let's not also forget that both Camilla and Charles have had to step up. And they know spring chickens, you know. So they've had a lot of work to do in the fact that her majesty has had to step back, that wasn't accounted for. And as we know, we're looking at a slimmed down monarchy in the future. I would suggest to you that perhaps Prince Charles is now wondering whether this was a wise idea, given the amount of work that he and as I say, Camilla had to replace, because of the absence of Her Majesty the Queen. It's been very tough for everybody within the family.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I'm thinking right about this that the Queen mother, Elizabeth's mother, Queen Elizabeth's mother lived to be 101.\par \par \uc2 SEAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do we have any handle on what it is that's causing these mobility issues, how serious it is, or is it just, you know, she's getting old and it's harder to get around?\par \par \uc2 SEAN: Do you know what, Martha, I think it's like any girl, right? You know, if you don't feel your best, you don't want to be seen, you know. And we saw a while back with Princess Anne handing Her Majesty the Queen, a cane. And did you see the look when she gave her the cane as if, I don't want it, you know. She's very, very proud. She wants to be able to move around the way she did. I think it's difficult for her to accept the encroaching age.\par \par \uc2 It's, you know, has proven difficult. All the stories about it being in a wheelchair and sitting in a room in Windsor, not true. She has an active week coming up. But for me, I do think people want to see the Queen literally doing the job and that's going to be difficult moving forward because you have all the abolishing, the monarchy people then stepping in saying, oh, she should abdicate and all that sort of stuff. I don't believe that will ever happen. I think she means it for life and it will be for life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I agree. Neil, thank you very much. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 SEAN: A pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: More of the story right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Before we go we want to welcome a new member to the story team Karen Alexander Hooper is the son of our senior producer Alex Hooper came into the world early this morning. Mom and baby are both happy and healthy and a huge congratulations. He's nine pounds. He's so cute. Little Karen not too little. Congratulations to all. So we're thrilled for them and we wish them a lot of happiness and some rest for mama. So that's THE STORY. We'll see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220511ei5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Update On Attack On Wisconsin Anti-Abortion Group; Stocks Plunge, Americans' Money Increase; White House Briefs As Protests Continue After SCOTUS Leak; Trump Campaigns For Dr. OZ In PA Senate Primary\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Mike Tobin, Bret Baier, Trey Yingst, Mike  Emanuel \par \uc2 9,444 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everyone. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News headquarters in New York breaking right now on THE STORY. We are about to get the very latest in this investigation that's going on in Wisconsin right now, we're going to hear a news conference. You can see that live shot on the left- hand side of your screen, an arson attack over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 On a pro-life groups office. This message was ominously scrawled on the wall there: "If abortions aren't safe, then you aren't either." Former Wisconsin Governor Scott Walker, here exclusively in moments after days of stopping short of condemning protests or marches outside the homes of Supreme Court justices. The White House now says they do condemn this Wisconsin attack and we are about to hear from the White House in the briefing room.\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  will be there and moments will dip and back and forth, as that is ongoing this hour as well. So, here's where this tinderbox of emotion is now, this first clip you're about to see is in front of the home of Chief Justice Roberts. The second is outside a church in downtown New York.\par \par \uc2 OK, so that's we're at. First up, Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  with the background here. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, as you got into it there, we are standing by to hear for the first time from police in Madison, Wisconsin, after this violent attack on the headquarters for an anti-abortion lobbying group. There's evidence of a Molotov cocktail that was thrown through the window of this facility but failed to ignite in there was a fire that was started at another location. But keep in mind all of this happened before 6:00 a.m. on Monday morning, so no one was at the office and no one was hurt. Graffiti at the headquarters. You mentioned that as well. "If abortions aren't safe, then you aren't either." There was an anarchist symbol painted there in the number 1312. That indicates with letters A-C-A-B fall in the alphabet, meaning all cops are expletive. The president of Wisconsin Family Action blames left wing politicians who have tolerated violent and destructive protests and set for the left violence has become the answer for everything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIANNE APPLING, WISCONSIN FAMILY ACTION PRESIDENT: I'm very sad for them that they're so angry and don't have more productive ways to spend their time and their energy rather than trying to harm us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Republican Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson tweeted this attack as abhorrent and should be condemned by all. The White House also weighed in with a statement, "President Biden strongly condemns this attack and political violence of every stripe. The President has made clear through his time in public life, that Americans have the fundamental right to express themselves under the Constitution, whatever the point of view, but that expression must be peaceful and free of violence, vandalism, or attempts to intimidate. The kind of nation comes from the left as well, the are statements from Wisconsin's Democratic governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , and Planned Parenthood as well. Martha, back to you,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mike. Thank you very much. Former Republican Governor of Wisconsin, Scott Walker joins me now. In 2015, he signed a bill banning abortions after 20 weeks in the state. Governor, good to have you here today. You know, I guess your reaction, first of all, to this attack on this pro-life groups' office over the weekend in your state, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT WALKER, FORMER GOVERNOR OF WISCONSIN: What's outrageous and the fact that a Molotov cocktail coming through there didn't immediately spur outrage. It took some time before both the Governor and eventually the president spoke out that we should be speaking out against violence, no matter where it comes from. If a Molotov cocktail had been thrown through the headquarters of Planned Parenthood or through an abortion clinic, I think many of us would have joined in speaking out against that, and would be outraged with the violence but, sadly, is part of a trend we're seeing across the nation. In front of the homes, as you just mentioned, of Supreme Court justices, and even in places of worship, if it was the other way around, I think the outrage would be much larger for many across this nation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think a lot of people look at the governors across the country as the new front line really, if this goes through, as it appears it will the overturning of Roe v. Wade at the Supreme Court when they put out that final decision, it's really going to fall on the governors and the state legislatures. And you, as I mentioned in the intro, signed into law a 20-week ban. Is that where you think this goes? Because a lot of people think that once this can is opened in states like yours might push for a full ban on abortion in your state.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, you're going to go from the court of law, which is where Roe v. Wade is put things for the last nearly half a century to the court of public opinion. And then the last few days you've seen a lot of outrage but much of this pushback has been to divert on to other issues as opposed to protecting the lives of these unborn children. That's really what this is all about, these are babies.\par \par \uc2 We think about yesterday being Mother's Day, it wasn't birthing person's day, it was Mother's Day. And thank God for all those mothers across America and around the world who gave birth to beautiful baby girls and baby boys. That's what we're talking about. We're talking about advocating for people who, living beings, human beings, not a mass of cells, not an organ, but a living human being.\par \par \uc2 We talked a lot about science during COVID, where the science shows us that an unborn baby is a living being. Even the president called it his child the other day. And so, those of us who support protecting innocent lives, need to make that case and do so not only legally, but we need to make it from our heart and show the kind of compassion we have for those babies and their mothers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But I guess, you know, as I said, the question is whether or not what now looks like 15-week ban in Mississippi, the original case that brought all of this, and you have a 20 week ban in Wisconsin. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  said that the Governor of Mississippi was asked you know that he said that trigger law will go right into effect, which would ban all abortions.\par \par \uc2 And then he was asked if he would go further than that to next target the use of Plan B pills, or IUDs. And he said that's not what they're focused on at this time. But that's the argument that you hear from people in the middle of this debate, which I think is where a lot of people are, is that they believe that states will go far further than the 15 or 20-week ban that they've been pushing for.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I think I think that's, because that's where people I think even people in the middle, those of us who are pro-life certainly want to protect every baby out there, every unborn child from conception all the way through to natural death when it comes to protecting the innocent society. But I think the average person who really just doesn't pay a lot of attention to this issue, who believes probably by and large, the polls show that abortion is wrong, but doesn't want to get engaged in this battle.\par \par \uc2 Now is going to be, it's going to move to the state's governor, state lawmakers are going to be engaged in that issue. They're going to be a part of it, as are the voters in terms of choosing who they want in office. But the real extreme are people like Ralph Northam, two years ago, then governor of Virginia, talking about a bill in that state where they were literally going to allow abortion all the way through birth. That's where most Democrats are today.\par \par \uc2 That's the contrast from when a baby is viable, is -- feels pain, comparing that to where most of the states like New York, Illinois, California will do it all the way through birth. That's radical.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Governor Walker, thank you. We got to jump out. Thank you so much for your time, but there's also standing by, and we want to go now to Madison, Wisconsin, where we're getting an update on this arson attack on a pro-life group's office. Let's listen and see what they have discovered.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHON BARNES, MADISON, WISCONSIN CHIEF OF POLICE: My name is Chief Shon Barnes, S-H-O-N B-A-R-N-E-S, I'm the chief of police for the Madison Police Department. I'm joined today by Assistant Chief \uc2 Brian Austin\uc2 ; Madison Fire Assistant Chief, Chase Steadman; Special Agent William Fulton, from the ATF; Supervisory Resident Agent in Charge Peter Jenkins; from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Robert Payne, Assistant Special Agent in Charge FBI Milwaukee division; and Samba Baldeh, who is our State Representatives for District 14 where this incident occurred.\par \par \uc2 I want to start off by saying that there is no room for hate or violence in Madison. In fact, there's no room for hate or violence anywhere in our country. Hate or violence do not advance any cause. And unfortunately, we're investigating the fruits of hate and violence in the form of an arson where a specific nonprofit group was targeted for their beliefs regarding the issue of abortion. We understand that emotions are high on every side of this issue, following the recent news involving the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's what we know thus far. Shortly, after 6:00 a.m. on Sunday, May 8th, a passerby headed to Madison's airport. Notice flames coming from the Wisconsin family action office. The area is very close to the Madison Police Department's North District and our officers arrived to the scene moments before the Madison Fire Department. Our responding officers observed smoke coming from the building and a ground level window which had been breached.\par \par \uc2 Madison Fire Department was able to put out the fire within five minutes after their response. Special thanks to our fire department. Fire investigators located and incendiary device, sometimes referred to as a Molotov cocktail inside the office. We're still working to determine what type of accelerant was used in this device. We collected multiple pieces of evidence on the scene and these items were turned over to our federal partners for processing.\par \par \uc2 As much as we wish we could be able to solve this case today, the forensic process must take time. And we must now wait for those results. While we wait, we are combing the area and searching for digital evidence. We're asking people to call us if they have information regarding this incident. No arrests have been made at this time. We're encouraging anyone with additional information to call our crime stoppers number, that number is: 608266014-6082666014.\par \par \uc2 But most importantly, if you see something, say something. If you can't remember that number, please call 911. if all that fails. Now we have time for a few questions, but please keep in mind that this is an active investigation, and I will not be disclosing any of our investigative leads. Are there any questions for myself?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (INAUDIBLE)-- several devices sets, plural. Received (INAUDIBLE), one Molotov cocktail? Are there multiple severity devices that were published on this building or is it one?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: We found two.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two molotov cocktails.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it believe that that fire was caused by a second device that was working on (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: That's part of our investigative process. We're not that exactly sure if that actually calls it, but that will be part of the investigative process.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chief, do you have any suspect or suspects at this point.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: At this time, no arrests have been made. And we do not have any suspects at this time in custody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have any surveillance video (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Yes, certainly we're combing the area looking for any video evidence or what we call digital evidence. We asked our community that if you've seen something, please give us a call. If you have a business that may be near this area, please give us a call and let us know. So, we can come by and maybe take a look and see if we can find some evidence of who may be responsible for this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For the federal agents, how big of a problem is this at the same level of that the abortion clinics or pro-life groups and targeting the Wisconsin --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At this time, we have no further leads on any things that have occurred of this nature in the state of Wisconsin. This is our first one that we're responding to with our state and local partners and our federal partners, ATF. And again, I mean, we're looking at this from the lens of, this is first amendment protected right. People have their opinions. And when we violate that, and we use a criminal activity or violence and that nature, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and our federal authorities will look at that for all federal statutes in which to investigate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What federal charges may come up after this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is an ongoing investigation, Sir, we're in, we're in communications currently, with the Western District United States Attorney's Office and discussing that as the investigation unfolds.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At this time, sir, no further comment, we would have to take this as the investigation of bolts, and we will use any and all federal statutes available.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, well, we'll monitor this, doesn't sound like they have a lot yet, if anything, really at this point, except that they did announce that there were two Molotov cocktails that were found inside that building, and a fire that was set in that pro-life operation in Wisconsin. So, with that, we bring in "SPECIAL REPORT" Anchor Bret Baier. Bret, good to see you good to be here.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hi, Martha. Great.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, this whole thing feels like something that could get worse. We certainly hope it doesn't. But there's a lot of protests over the weekend. You've got people walking in front of Supreme Court justices' homes. And here's what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  had to say about this issue, because clearly, folks are digging their heels politically on this as well. Here she is over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): This is about something so serious and so personal, and so disrespectful of women. Here we are on Mother's Day, a week where this Court has slapped women in the face in terms of disrespect for their judgment about the size and timing of their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCCALLUM: Slapped women in the face says \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the Supreme Court. This is political fire.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure. And it's emotional. And there are -- we've seen this for years and years decades, really, on this issue. However, it's just interesting how the White House has handled it. The President didn't say anything about the leak. He didn't say anything about putting out the justices addresses or the protests that are going on there.\par \par \uc2 And days later, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  puts out a tweet after this pro-life office in Wisconsin is hit by two Molotov cocktails. I think toning down the emotion is where the leadership should be not ramping it up. And it's such a charged issue that that's not where they are. They see the political upside of perhaps rescuing some Democrats who are in tough races in purple states.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, really raises a great question that you just touched on of where the president actually is on this. He makes a very obvious display of his Catholicism.\par \par \uc2 This would be an opportunity where he could kind of speak to people about his own feelings versus whatever the legal outcome is, in this case. But he has chosen not to do so.\par \par \uc2 And do you think that's because they just feel it's so potent for them politically, that it might give them an advantage in a tough poll environment?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It has to be part of it. It has to be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Otherwise, you would automatically do that. You would talk to the nation, as the leader of the nation, all sides, as he's talked about as a candidate.\par \par \uc2 I do think that there's an interesting thing about this issue in particular, and that how other countries handle it. As opposed to going through the courts, that it goes through the politics of those countries. And there are lawmakers who debate how it should be handled. And for the most part, all of them have some kind of restrictions beyond where even we are. And they've done it through the political process, not through the court system.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's the way we used to do it, right? Before Roe v. Wade. And it looks like that's the way we're going to do it again.\par \par \uc2 Bret, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's good to see you in New York. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the face of a Russian ambassador drips in fake red blood after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  vows victory in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Stunning moments on the left hand side of your screen there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): Glory to our brave Armed Forces! To Russia, to victory! Hurrah!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hurrah!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Stunning scenes over the weekend in Moscow, but some of his comrades may not be following command. We will speak with General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2  next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  trying to justify his unprovoked invasion of Ukraine as he celebrated Victory Day in Moscow, marking the defeat of Nazi Germany.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: You are fighting for the Homeland, for its future, so that no one forgets the lessons of World War II. So that there's no place in the world for executioners, punishers, and Nazis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. So, General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2  is standing by. But first, to foreign correspondent Trey Yingst reporting live from Kyiv. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  spoke today in Moscow but made no major announcements about the war in Ukraine. His remarks came at the annual Victory Day celebrations. That also included a parade through Moscow's Red Square.\par \par \uc2 With more than 11,000 troops marching and military equipment rolling through the Russian capital, Putin made no declaration of victory and did not announce any broader mobilization of forces. He did, however, accuse Ukraine without evidence of planning to retake Crimea and threatening nuclear war out in this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: Preparations were openly under way for another punitive operation in Donbas and an invasion of our historic lands, including Crimea. Kyiv has announced the possible acquisition of nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NATO\uc2  bloc began active military development of the territories adjacent to ours. This was an absolutely unacceptable threat systematically created for us.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): Russian troops and separatist leaders participated in smaller parades and events inside Ukraine in places like the southern cities of Mariupol and Kherson. Holding a large black and orange banner, they walked through the streets of Mariupol, were an estimated 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers are still holed up underneath a steel plant.\par \par \uc2 The facility continues to take fire after Ukrainian officials confirmed all civilians were evacuated over the past several days. These developments come as fighting continues along the eastern front lines in the northern city of Kharkiv, Ukrainian troops have successfully launched a counter offensive that could push the Russians back into their own territory within days. That according to recent analysis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): We are seeing right now in the East, a war of attrition. Russian forces are not successful in their attempts to get deeper into Ukrainian territory, but they do continue to fire missiles into this country. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey, thank you very much, live in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in retired four star General \uc2 David Petraeus\uc2 , former commander of U.S. forces in Iraq and Afghanistan, and former CIA director. He's now a partner at \uc2 KKR\uc2  and Chairman of the KKR Global Institute.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm struck by the contrast of what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  had to say, and on Victory Day in Moscow with the troops parading in front of him, and talking about how Ukraine was about to take over, Crimea, and that they were the aggressor and all of this, and the very difficult P.R. situation that this President Putin finds himself in right now, as he is once again sort of stuck in the mud it appears in Donbas.\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 DAVID PETRAEUS\uc2  (RET), FORMER UNITED STATES AND NATO COMMANDER: Well, I think that captures it very nicely, Martha. Presumably, he must be questioning the domestic support because, of course, he did not declare war or a mobilization.\par \par \uc2 This is still a special operation, certainly to secure that portion of Ukraine that he believes is again, historic and part of Russia and so forth. But he's in a very difficult position.\par \par \uc2 Enormous damage is being done to his economy, his financial system, his inner circle and the business community. In Russia, the casualties have been enormous. The Ukrainians have now captured more Russian tanks than exists in the entire U.K. inventory in their army. So again, this is not going well for him.\par \par \uc2 And as Trey noted, the counter offensive by the Ukrainians Eastern Kharkiv may actually drive the Russians back across the border. And that could continue.\par \par \uc2 So, the big question, I think, now Martha is what happens as all the weapons systems and so forth that the U.S., U.K., and other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries are providing? Can that give the additional capability to the Ukrainians to really expand this counter offensive? Or are we going to be in a somewhat of a frozen war once again, but just with different boundaries than those at the end of 2014?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, we know that humiliation is a real driving force for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , he does not like to be humiliated. And we -- you know, learned of these intelligence stories about U.S. intelligence potentially taking out or giving some information which led to the taking out of some of these generals.\par \par \uc2 This is some video of the Russian ambassador to Poland, who was doused in red paint over the weekend at their victory celebration, obviously, another humiliation and a statement, at least, by these protesters of how they feel about this war.\par \par \uc2 The question is, what will he do? If his troops get pushed back into Russia? Further humiliation for him, what does he resort to if that happens?\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Well, there are several different alternatives. One is, obviously, they finally get serious about negotiations and go to the diplomacy route. The other is, of course, the unthinkable, and that would be to resort to some use of weapons of mass destruction.\par \par \uc2 I think that that is unlikely, I think the crossing of that threshold would take Russia beyond the international pariah status it has right now. And, as you noted, not a good time to be a representative of Russia around the world. But the alternatives become pretty stark for him.\par \par \uc2 And again, he'll see what they can achieve on the battlefield, he clearly is not going to increase the manpower provided that would be the result of mobilization for war. And of course, they're fighting a country in Ukraine, which has mobilized for war. Every able citizen is really helping out in this effort.\par \par \uc2 And again, they're receiving an enormous amount of additional military weapon systems and other capabilities.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General Petraeus, thank you very much. Great to have you here, sir. We'll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 PETRAEUS: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden is now facing a tsunami of economic warning signs as gas prices climbed to near record highs this weekend. Inflation has wiped out people's wage growth, and the market continues under enormous pressure down 630 points.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, that's bad news for people's 401(k)'s across the country, and Americans grow increasingly skittish about the future of their finances.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove will join us with some of his whiteboard wisdom, coming up shortly after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Huh, keeping a close eye on Wall Street, but it's really not a very pretty picture, folks, the Dow is down 550 points right now, S&P is down 118 as the new poll captures the growing concern among Americans when it comes to their money that was worried about paying their normal monthly bills, jumping eight points in the past year to 40 percent in this Gallup survey, and more than half of Americans now say that they're worried about maintaining their standard of living.\par \par \uc2 And look at this gas prices jumping 20 cents in the past month, according to AAA, the national average is now back to an all-time high of $4.33 a gallon that it hit a couple of months ago. And with that, we bring in Karl Rove, former Deputy Chief of Staff for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and co- founder of American Crossroads, and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , former Clinton adviser, and pollster and now chairman and CEO of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2  and Chairman of the \uc2 Harris Poll\uc2 , gentlemen, always good to have both of you with us. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we've seen the numbers here, Karl, people are concerned, obviously, about gas prices creeping higher. 44 percent say that it causes a serious hardship for their family. The president is going to make a big speech tomorrow on the economy and how he hopes to curb inflation, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, CO-FOUNDER, AMERICAN CROSSROADS: Well, he's in deep trouble on this issue and he's in deep trouble because this issue of inflation dominates everybody's view of the economy. In one recent poll, 77 percent of everybody polled said the economy could be seen as poor, Republicans 94 percent, and problematic for the Democrats, 81 percent of independents saw the economy as poor.\par \par \uc2 So, the president would better come up with a better message that he has had thus far, which has seemingly been it's not my fault, it's everybody else's and all we've got to do is spend more money in order to make it go away, which doesn't comport with what American people think.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Mark, you have the market tanking. Obviously, it's not convinced that these changes by the Fed are going to tamp down inflation anytime soon. This is from a CNN reporter, Daniel Dale, what he thinks about President Biden's claim that he has helped at least to bring down the deficit. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL DALE, REPORTER, CNN: Experts I still spoke to still scoffed at the idea that President Biden is personally responsible for having reduced the deficit. In fact, one advocate of deficit reduction, Mark Gold, one of the Committee for Responsible Federal Budget, told me that this claim is almost Bizarro World, a reversal of reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mark, your thoughts on where this all is?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , FORMER CLINTON ADVISER AND POLLSTER: I don't think the President's going to get any credit for bringing down the deficit in this environment. I think -- I think he's got an economic speech and the question is whether or not he's going to say that taxing and spending is going to bring down inflation and improve the economy. If he, is I think that's going to be laughed at. Or does he have something else in mind to see how -- what we did in '96 when we went to a balanced budget strategy, and it had huge economic consequences.\par \par \uc2 Does he have another program here, other than the ones that he's already given? He's going to have to come out with something fundamentally different because I can't argue with Karl's numbers. Inflation is eating up the president's ratings.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Here's the president just a short time ago, talking about this little preview of what we're going to hear tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My top priority is fighting inflation and lowering prices for families and the things they need. Today's announcement is going to give millions of families a little more -- a little more breathing room, help them pay their bills. You know, we work with Democrats and Republicans and businesses and nonprofits to make this happen. And we're going to keep working to fight inflation and lower the cost of -- to all American families for a lot of other things as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, Karl, I mean, the president was supposed to be the great unifier, he's been criticized a lot for not doing that and now I think we're going to hear that he's going to bring down inflation. He wants to bring together Democrats and Republicans, he says.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, but he's got to have a plan that makes sense to the American people. Here's the big problem, where he got a little mini board today, eight and a half percent inflation, four and a half percent wage growth. That means that people are falling behind each and every day. Their wages are going up, but not going up as much as inflation.\par \par \uc2 And as a result of people are getting hurt by this are two groups of people, in particular, people on the lower end of the economic scale, whose wages are not rising as rapidly as everybody else, and who already were living from paycheck to paycheck, to begin with, and those who are on a fixed income.\par \par \uc2 When you have your paycheck sort of flat and wages and inflation is going up to eight and a half percent a year, it's a real problem. So the president, fine, he can go out and talk all he likes, but until he has an answer that comports with what the people feel is in reality, like Clinton did in the mid-90s were going to balance the budget and put our fiscal house in order, the president is not going to do too well with the American people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't expect we're going to hear that but you never know. And that's why we tune in. Thank you very much. \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , always good to see you. Karl Rove, good to see you as well. Thank you very much, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 So, former President Trump's midterm influence is about to face its next big test in the GOP race for who will be the Republican nominee for the Pennsylvania Senate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Under the Trump administration, we had the greatest economy in the history of the world with virtually no inflation. We had no inflation. We had energy, we had cheap gasoline, we had cheap electricity, we -- everything was so good. All they had to do is go to the beach and relax.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is taking some questions and answers here from reporters. Let's listen, and we think she's about to get to some interesting questions here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There will be more opportunities for you all to ask good questions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To other, if I could, real quick. Tomorrow, he's giving a speech on inflation, wondering if you can give us some sense of what more he'll get into.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure. Well, I would note that, as we -- as we announced and previewed a little bit, the president will be speaking to his plan -- his continued plan to continue the fight to address inflation in the coming months. There's a lot of work that has been done to date on this front, whether it was the steps he's taken to address the energy crisis, the release from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, additional steps he's taken on that front, or whether it was the step he's taken, even in recent weeks to fix the family glitch to help more people have access to health care or steps he announced in the last couple of weeks to make E15 available to thousands of people at gas stations across the country.\par \par \uc2 But what you will also hear him talk about tomorrow is the contrast -- and the contrast that his plan and the plan he has been implementing for months now draws with those on the other side of the aisle who has not -- who have not put forward any plan to lower costs for the American people. So it will be his opportunity to lay that all together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And there is growing concern about a persistent supply issue with infant baby formula.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's about a 40 percent shortage right now, major retail is having to limit how much people can buy, especially acute in places like Tennessee, Missouri, and Iowa. This is partly an \uc2 FDA\uc2  issue but it could be a Biden administration issue, just wondering if you guys are planning on taking any steps to help remedy that?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, let me first say, as you know, but -- the \uc2 FDA\uc2  issued a recall to ensure that they are meeting their obligation to protect the health of Americans, including babies who of course, were receiving or taking this formula and ensure safe products are available. That's their job. Ensuring the availability of these products is also a priority for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and they're working around the clock to address any possible shortage.\par \par \uc2 OK. That is -- that is like hearing your name -- your voice on the -- on the answering machine, which I know -- that's a very outdated reference but we've all been there.\par \par \uc2 OK. So what the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is doing, which -- while they are independent, they are part of the administration is they're taking enough number of steps to address that includes working with major infant formula manufacturers to ensure they're increasing production because part of this issue is, of course, making sure they're stuck on the shelves, right.\par \par \uc2 And in working with the industry right now to optimize their supply lines, and product sizes to increase capacity, and prioritizing product lines that are of greatest need. Because obviously, as someone who -- my child has long been out of formula, fortunately, but it's close enough that I remember when you're trying to go to the store and get the specific kind of formula for your age child or whatever their needs are.\par \par \uc2 So, they are -- they are taking steps with that in mind. They're also exercising flexibility and expediting the review of notifications of manufacturing changes that will help increase supply, particularly in the case of specialized formulas. So that applies to that as well for medical needs. And they're also trying to streamline the import entry review process -- processes for already notified infant formula products coming from notified foreign facilities.\par \par \uc2 So, what they're trying to do in the short-hands of it is increased supply but working with a range of manufacturers and what their capacity is to ensure that the kinds of formula that is -- was recalled is where they're able to help ensure it's on the -- on the shelves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It isn't something that you kept in a national stockpile, do you know?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't believe there's a national stockpile of baby formula, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  does -- is not just their responsibility in their view to ensure that we're meeting our obligations to protect Americans, it is also their obligation to take steps to ensure supply can be met when they take these steps. So that is what they're very focused on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's an answering machine? If you could --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Since you're 25, I'll explain it to you after the briefing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Could you say -- just give some general reaction to Putin's speech this morning.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure, I tried to do it a bit at the top. But what I will say is that what we saw President Putin do is give a version of revisionist history that took the form of disinformation that we have seen too commonly as the Russian playbook. Now, what is fortunate is that we are all aware, reporters around the world are aware, Europeans are aware, Americans are aware of the disinformation factory that President Putin and the Kremlin seem to be.\par \par \uc2 But to the suggestion that this war that was prompted by -- directed by President Putin was prompted by Western aggression or Western plans is patently false and absurd. And otherwise, I would say, you know, our view is that we should remember -- and this is why I did this at the top, what this day is actually about, which is something that we've all celebrated, which is the defeat of Nazis in after World War II, something that Russians have celebrated in the streets for many years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Quick question on a totally unrelated topic. Biden has blamed large meatpackers for the relevant meat prices.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And today, \uc2 Tyson Foods\uc2  has another record or very good earnings out that showed its average prices for pizza climbed nearly 25 percent compared to the same quarter last year. I wondered if you will have any reaction to that. Is this the sort of, you know, price gouging that the administration is seeking to stop?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't have any reaction directly to one company's earnings. But what the president's concern is, and what Secretary Vilsack has been here a number of times is concerned about is that the meat industry -- the conglomerates are so small, they're so -- I mean, not small, they're very large, but they're so dominant that they are elbowing out, of course, smaller producers and that they have this capacity and ability to jack up prices and pass those on to consumers when they should not. But I don't have any direct reaction to one company's reports.\par \par \uc2 I am not aware of this issue, and our concern is being addressed, though, so it's an ongoing concern. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Just a little bit more on the Victory Day speech.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Given that he didn't really point to specific victories, and he also didn't signal, you know some kind of upcoming massive escalation --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Or some pathway to ending the war. I'm just wondering if there were any takeaways for U.S. officials, specifically related to the trajectory of the war from that speech.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would say what we're watching closely is what we're seeing on the ground. And what we're seeing on the ground is, you know, the -- right now, a great deal of the war, as you all know, and my colleagues at the Department of Defense have briefed on this, even as recently as this morning has obviously moved more to the east. And that terrain is very different.\par \par \uc2 The Donbas is a lot more flat, it's little small villages. It's very different from what we were seeing in Kyiv. That is a terrain that the Ukrainians know well. And what we look at as we look to the totality of the country, and if we go back to mid-February when President Putin was giving speeches, basically declaring he was going to subsume Ukraine, take over the country, the territorial integrity of the country and go beyond that, is that is exactly not what's happening today. President Putin and the Russians are not marching through Kyiv. They are struggling to fight in other parts of the country. And the Ukrainians are bravely and courageously fighting every day.\par \par \uc2 So, we look at what's happening on the ground, though it is important to note and to call out the revisionist history that we saw in this speech, and the fact that any such statements that we saw it -- we've seen for months from President Putin, that the war was prompted by the West is just patently false and inaccurate. And we can state that too often.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And on the First Lady's trip to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is there anything more you can share?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You talked a little bit about why she wanted to choose Mother's Day to travel to Ukraine. In terms of sort of the security concerns which you've talked about a lot, how are you all able to get to a point where you could feel confident -- the president could feel confident that she would be totally safe?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I'm not going to get into behind-the-scenes security concerns or considerations. Obviously, she would not have gone if we did not feel comfortable with the security arrangements. I will note that she went there, as you said, because -- she wanted to go on Mother's Day because she was thought it was important to show the Ukrainian people that the war has to stop, that the war has been brutal, and that the people of the United States stand with the people of Ukraine. That's why she went yesterday and just show that the hearts of the American people are with the mothers of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 She also of course -- you know, she's been back now and she's had an opportunity to speak with the president and has conveyed -- she said this publicly directly to him what she saw on the ground, the need to support the people of Ukraine. She saw the horrors and the brutality that the people she met had experienced. And I -- you know, that was something she conveyed directly to him. But in terms of security, obviously, we have a range of security considerations we make. And, you know, I'm not going to get into those details from here, though.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just had a quick COVID question.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The warning about the 100 million potential COVID infections by the fall and winter if new COVID funding isn't approved.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dr. Jha has said that this is based on a range of internal and external models. Could the White House point to which specific models were used to get to that number?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There is a range of, as he said, internal-external models. Well, within that range is that number would hit, the point he was making is that we know what we need to do. We have put out an entire playbook to address the pandemic to help a -- preparedness plan to ensure Americans are protected and that we stay on the front foot in our fight against COVID. If we do not take action, we also know that there are -- we know that the virus is going to continue to evolve.\par \par \uc2 And without a staying vigilant and prepared like not having access to life- saving vaccines, testing therapeutics, it is going -- it has the ability to up and our lives. So he is a doctor himself, as you know, a very experienced one, he talks to a range of experts internally and externally. There's a range of models that are out there and this is well within that range. And the point he was making is about the impact if we do not act, if Congress does not act.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would the White House be able to make public --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Jen, can we spread out the questions a little bit? Can you yield to your colleagues, please?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would be happy to but I think you would be polite if you let MJ finish your question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can she yield to her colleagues, please?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Could the White House make public the range of models so that we're able to see how we got to that 100 million infections warning?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We're -- I'm happy to see if there's more specific data we can make available but what I can assure you of is that Dr. Jha is a very experienced public health expert. He talks to a range of officials internally and externally. That's what he's basing it on. Let me go to the back. Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Speaking a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 That means many in Japan are welcoming the upcoming visit. And two quick questions. One, Japan has a population of 130 million but only 39,000 deaths from the virus. I'm wondering if the administration is aware of that and maybe some best practices can be studied while we're there. And second, the -- will there be a reaffirmation of the Security Treaty, especially about the Senkaku Islands? And then I have one quick follow-up.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We'll keep an eye on that. The briefing room with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  taking some questions there. The former President Trump, though, has been working to score another big win at the ballot box because we're now really into the heat of the primary season as we head towards the midterms. President Trump campaigned for Dr. Mehmet Oz in the Republican primary for the open Senate seat as \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  seat in Pennsylvania days after his candidate J.D. Vance won in the GOP Senate primary in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Tuesday's primary results are just the latest proof that we have transformed the face of the Republican Party. Thank goodness. We are now the Working People's Party but we're really the party of everyone. We're the party -- you know what, we're the party of common sense, I think more than anything else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In moments, another Republican candidate for the Pennsylvania Senate seat \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , but first to Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel reporting live from DC. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, the latest Franklin and Marshall poll suggests a red hot race in the GOP primary for the Pennsylvania Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voiceover): Dr. Oz had 18 percent, \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  at 16 percent, Kathy Barnette running 12 percent, and several other candidates in single digits. But here's a key thing to consider when looking at those numbers. 39 percent of those surveyed say they aren't sure who they will vote for and 57 percent who have chosen a candidate say they could still change their minds.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump is clearly trying to play Kingmaker in the Keystone State. He rallied on behalf of Dr. Oz Friday night. Mr. Trump said the GOP is now the working people's party, the party of common sense and putting America first. The Trump-back candidate in Ohio, J.D. Vance won. In Pennsylvania, the former president says McCormick was initially a critic before he decided to run for Senate.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: David came out very strongly against me on numerous occasions. This was before he was going to run and all of a sudden he said boy, I like this guy a lot, including with respect to the disinformation spread about January 6 -- total disinformation.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A spokesman for the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee says the former president's involvement has poured gasoline on this primary race. "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's interjection into Pennsylvania's GOP Senate primary has only escalated the chaos in the race and nasty attacks the Republicans are leveling against each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Pennsylvania primary day is a week from tomorrow and this is clearly a critical stage in this race, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Mike Emanuel, reporting from Washington. We have reached out to all of the Republican candidates who are in this Pennsylvania Senate race and \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  joins us now. Dave, good to have you back on the program. Thanks for being here today. I guess with that sort of setup --\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK\uc2 , PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Hi, Martha. How are you?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hi there. I'm doing well, thanks. Well, you know, what do you say to President Trump and what do you say about what he said there, and what do you say to the voters of Pennsylvania, a large number of whom are still undecided in this race?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, we're -- it's a -- it's a sprint to May 17. And I'm not surprised. I mean, President Trump is very popular in Pennsylvania. His America first policies do great things for Pennsylvania and great things for the country. The problem is Mehmet Oz isn't popular and so his endorsed candidate is not getting momentum.\par \par \uc2 And the reason for that is that he's been liberal -- Hollywood liberal for his entire career and taking a set of positions, whether it's anti-gun or pro-abortion, or pro-transgender transitions or pro-Obamacare that are very different than primary voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And so what's happening now is Pennsylvania voters are zeroing in. I'm running as someone who's battle-tested, a combat veteran, a West Point grad, someone who served in the 82nd airborne and then created jobs in Pennsylvania, and someone who's a seventh-generation Pennsylvanian, someone who grew up in a small town and has a family farm and -- as an outsider.\par \par \uc2 And that story, I believe, is selling and I believe that the people of Pennsylvania are dialing in. And ultimately this race is not about who endorsed Mehmet Oz or who endorsed me. I've had great endorsements from Ted Cruz and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  and \uc2 Bob Lighthizer\uc2  who led to President Trump's China agenda.\par \par \uc2 But those endorsements really just inform the voters that this is someone they should look at. And right now they're looking. And I feel very confident that when they look they're going to zero in on someone who can go to Washington -- first of all, win the -- win the general election, by the way, this is going to be the most significant Senate race in the country. The stakes are so high. And then go to Washington and fight for America's First values and policies. And so I feel good about it but it's going to be a photo finish.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And you saw what happened in Ohio with the president's endorsement of J.D. Vance. He really came up that it was like the Kentucky Derby. He kind of came up the side lane with a surprise win there. Why do you think -- you know, I'm sure you do think that things will be different in Pennsylvania, but you've also got Kathy Barnette to contend with. And the latest Philadelphia Inquirer report says that she has emerged as a likely alternative here, so this is really a split race right now.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, there's a -- there's a number of very capable people running for this seat. I think that's a great validation of our democracy. So many people feel like they want to fix the country. And I think what they're going to -- the questions they're going to ask themselves is who can win the general election because we can't have John Fetterman and his socialist agenda representing Pennsylvania is completely opposite of what Pennsylvanians need right now. But also, who can be a conservative leader in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 And listen, there's no time for on-the-job training. You -- I was listening to your programming to the inflation crisis, the crisis in Ukraine, the risk associated with China, we need somebody who can step into this seat on day one and be capable without on-the-job training of making a huge difference.\par \par \uc2 And so I think those are the two criteria Pennsylvania voters will ask is A, who can win the general election and B, who can carry that conservative agenda to Washington and make a real difference? And I'll be someone who will do that. And I also am an outsider, I don't know anybody anything. I don't anybody anything other than the voters of Pennsylvania, it'd be a great privilege to go to Washington and fight on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, what would you say to President Trump? because I know you wanted his support. We talked about this last time. I mean, everybody wants the support of a former president. Do you think he's going to regret this choice? Because there have been a lot of prominent people who have said that this was a mistake.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, listen, I don't -- I'm going to give the president advice on his endorsements. I think the reality is this -- is this is all politics. And so the fact of the matter is that the people of Pennsylvania are going to have to decide what's right for them. And Mehmet Oz is just someone that doesn't have the profile. He's -- he all these -- positions are in his own words. And the people of Pennsylvania see that.\par \par \uc2 And politics, you know, people are going to say a number of different things about the president standing behind his endorsement. And I know that. You know this is rough and tumble. I've been rough and tumble before as a college wrestler and as a ranger in the 82nd airborne so I knew when I was getting into the ring that you need -- you need to be prepared to be criticized. And I think that's part of what it takes to win this seat and lead in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 , sorry to jump in. We're running out of time. Thank you very much, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: That's OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll be watching all this closely with great interest.\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is THE STORY for today. THE STORY goes on as you know. Thank you so much for being with us today. We look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow. "YOUR WORLD" is next.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Far-Left Agitators Try To Intimidate Supreme Court Justices; Hollywood Lawyer Funding \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's Lifestyle, Report Says\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Sara Sidner, Lara Trump, Kayleigh McEnany \par \uc2 7,972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, mob rule, left-wing intimidation harassment is once again fashionable inside the modern extreme Democratic socialist party after first ignoring and excusing, and in some cases, even openly being supportive of the 574 violent riots in the summer of 2020, dozens dead, thousands of cops, injured billions in property damage people like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  promoting a bail fund to Minnesota praising the LAPD and the defunding efforts there. And then, of course, hypocritically spending every waking moment of every day obsessively condemning the riot on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, ignoring the core issue of why \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Mayor Muriel Bowser failed to make use of the up to 20,000 National Guard troops that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had authorized as required by law two days prior to keep the peace, as also requested by the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  chief.\par \par \uc2 Repeatedly, Democrats are now reversing course, once again and they are supporting acts of political violence. As we speak, far left agitators, they're trying to harass, intimidate, \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices and coerced them into saving Roe v. Wade. It is a disgusting, repulsive, despicable attempt to destroy the independence of our nation's judiciary. And the silence and the tacit approval from the Biden administration and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats, frankly that's equally repulsive.\par \par \uc2 This week, Justice Alito and his family, they were forced to flee their home after rioters published Alito's address and then made plans to show up at his front door. By the way, they showed up. Coming up our own Sara Carter will join us live from the scene of a so-called demonstration that just took place outside of Alito's home.\par \par \uc2 But sadly, it's not just Justice Alito. Over the weekend multiple Supreme Court justices and their families were targeted at their own homes by pro- abortion activists. Let me be clear, this is never okay. All of our public officials, both sides of the aisle, must be safe, must be protected from political violence and intimidation because it's never acceptable.\par \par \uc2 On this show, we condemned all of the rioting, all 574 riots in the summer of 2020. We condemn violence on January the 6th. I did it real time on my own radio show and that night on this show. And now we condemn the less violence against the Supreme Court and pro-life Americans everywhere. Over the weekend in Wisconsin, the headquarters of a pro-life group was attacked.\par \par \uc2 A Molotov cocktail was thrown through a window into an office causing considerable damage. And look at your screen, this threatening message was spray painted on the building, quote, if abortions aren't safe, and you aren't either. A church in Colorado also defaced by pro-abortion vandals and in New York, St. Patrick's Cathedral was surrounded by protesters hurling insults at anyone who went inside. And in Los Angeles, rioters dressed as handmaids stormed into a Catholic church service shouting profanities and pro-abortion rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 And in Washington, D.C., two Catholic churches on Capitol Hill requested extra police presence after one left-wing group threatened to, quote, burn the Eucharist. Now, naturally, many Democrats, their friends, the media mob, they're applauding this kind of violence. I guess they only are -- condemn violence if they can tie it somehow to something Republican or conservative.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  applauded these so- called protesters for using their righteous anger to march and to mobilize. In front of justice's houses? And get this, one reporter explicitly called for more violence tweeting a link to the Wisconsin attack along with the caption, quote, more of this may these people never know a moment of peace or safety until they rot in the ground.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, a wannabe politician in California called for Kavanaugh's home and all of his possessions that they'd be burned in front of him. The rhetoric on TV was even worse. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Violence is always over the line, but the real questions and conversations today about protests outside Supreme Court justice's houses, particularly Justice Kavanaugh. Where do you think that line is?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think for a lot of people, a conversation about civility feels like it misses the mark, when constitutional rights that you believe that you had for over 50 years are about to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean I suppose if the justices don't like the First Amendment, they could just drive to another state where the legislature has determined that the constitutional right to privacy applies, right? I mean, that is what they're telling women to do if they want to control their own bodies, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All of these things that we you know say might happen if abortion gets banned if abortion becomes illegal, they do happen. This isn't a theory. We don't need to speculate. We have actual facts that can inform what happens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If Roe's overturned, abortion's not getting banned and it's not going to be illegal. That is a big lie. We'll get to that in a second.\par \par \uc2 Now, believe it or not, it actually gets worse. One deranged person over at MSDNC saying, she'd like to make love to the Supreme Court leaker and then joyfully abort the fetus. We'll play that disturbing clip coming up.\par \par \uc2 But maybe worst of all, for more than a week, the White House completely utterly refused to condemn what is the stalking and the outright intimidation and doxxing of Supreme Court justices. Listen to \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  repeatedly excusing and supporting the protests at the homes of Supreme Court justices where they have children and they have friends and loved ones and neighbors, really? You support this Joe?\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think the progressive activists that are now planning protests outside some of the justices's houses are extreme?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Peaceful protest? No. Peaceful protest is not extreme.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Some of these justices have young kids but their neighbors are not all public figures. So would the president think a