ge. Every week we're going to hear one of these.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right, but Victor, this is why I started off that the inmates are truly running the asylum. They can't change. They are addicted to fanaticism. They know they're being hurt politically, and yet they go, we're going to do it, we're going to do it. It's going to have a different result. But it's not going to be a different result.\par \par \uc2 And your friend, I know you're very close, \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  and you, speaking of a new model, Victor, I had to get your thoughts on something stunning, at least I thought, that Gates said. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES, \uc2 MICROSOFT CO\uc2 -FOUNDER AND PHILANTHROPIST: During the pandemic, any engagement, sales meeting, meeting with a doctor, having a funeral, people are forced to say, hey, can we do that digitally. and we'll all think do I need to go to that convention or could it be online. I think that is a very positive thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, it's a positive thing for a dweeb who doesn't want to leave his house. It's not a positive thing for the rest of America that actually we have lives and we like being with people. What is going on with old Gates there, VDH?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Well, I think he's made a fortune in China and he's made a fortune in telecommunications. And anything that profits \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  because all of a sudden, a moral issue for everybody else. It's pretty simple. I don't really know him, Laura. He's never come down to here to sell and prune vines with me. I know he's buying a lot of farmland, but I haven't seen him on a track around here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, I will pay a lot of money to see you and \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  on a tractor together. That would be a great moment. Victor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: He's welcome anytime.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 In moments, we're going to introduce you to some of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's new best friends. Plus, we're going to speak to the candidates trying to take her down. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember those so-called principled men and women in the GOP who valiantly stood up to fight against Trump? Thinking people like \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 ? Where are these righteous Republicans now? Well, we might have the answer. Breitbart is reporting that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's reelection campaign is working with a group called People First. Their web site indicates it's affiliated with far left Democrat organizations such as President Biden's campaign arm, Planned Parenthood, \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Harriet Hageman is running to unseat \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in Wyoming, and she joins us now. Harriet, good to see you. I know the primary is coming up, the open primary coming up in August. But \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  really is, she's billed as the principled Republican, only a handful of them left, obviously. And she's passionate about January 6th, investigating that. Are you kind of surprised, then, she hasn't been more vocal about left-wing protests that seem to be getting out of hand against Supreme Court justices?\par \par \uc2 HARRIET HAGEMAN, (R) LIZ CHENEY PRIMARY CHALLENGER: Well, Laura, it's wonderful to see you again, to be back on your program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I have been stunned at her silence when you think about what is actually happened in the last week. I'm a constitutional attorney. I've been practicing law for over 33 years, and I've never seen anything like this. And the people that I have talked to haven't either. This was clearly an attempt to intimidate our Supreme Court justices, to find a way to expose or to try to get them to change, to get someone to change their decision. And also you can see just the rioting and the attacks and the hysteria that you're seeing by the leftists and the radicals out there. It makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 And Wyoming's sitting congresswoman has made no comment whatsoever. And you would think that in light of what she's done related to January 6th that she would have been at the forefront of condemning this. But boy, she hasn't been. I understand she might have in the last few minutes or so, or last hour actually --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, we have it. We have it, Harriet. We searched today for a comment. I think I might have raised it last week, well, where's \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the principled person on what's happening with the justices? We couldn't find anything, so we actually called her office. Now, two hours after we contacted her office, her office sent us a brand-new article noting her new comments, calling for the leaker to be prosecuted at the court. Harriet again, she is of sterling character and judgment, but she wouldn't have released the statement, I don't think, had we not asked.\par \par \uc2 HAGEMAN: I think that's exactly true. She's playing cleanup now, but it also exposes what her real agenda is with the January 6th commission. This doesn't have anything to do with principle, and I would argue she isn't the principled candidate in this race. What the January 6th commission is about, and she stated it very publicly repeatedly, that it is to make sure that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never gets near the Oval Office again. But, again, that isn't why Wyoming sent her to Washington, D.C. That's furthering her agenda and that of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , Adam Schiff, those folks. But it doesn't have anything to do with what Wyoming wants her to do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harriet, they're pummeling you with ads, we don't have to play it. But anyone who has doubts about you should just know you're being attacked by all the right people like the Lincoln Project and all these leftwing groups that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is now getting help from this People First organization. So we're going to be watching this race very carefully. But everyone I know in Wyoming is voting for you, so they've got get out and vote. Harriet, thank you, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Now our "Wit's End" segment is next. Remember, there's a clue. If you sell this item on the New York City subway, you can actually get arrested. Hurry up, tweet me your guesses @IngrahamAngle. We'll see if you get it right. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Selling which item can get you arrested on the New York City subway? Eddie Lopez tweeted "Stolen jewels." What? Joe C. wrote "Big gulp." And Freedom Matrix guessed "Hope in America's elected officials." OK, here is the big reveal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Video arose over the weekend of a fruit vendor in a subway station in Brooklyn was handcuffed by the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 . Is that what you want your police focusing on.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS, (D) NEW YORK CITY: Next day it's propane tanks being on the subway system. Next day it's barbecuing on the subway system. You just can't do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, the woman was selling mangos. How do you go to propane tanks from mangos?\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Freedom Matters on the website, get it.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Sharp Criticism Of Powell Inflation Strategy; Stocks Down As Recession Worries Mount; Esper On Timing Of Putin War On Ukraine; Esper Says Trump Actions Threatened Democracy; \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 's Accomplishments And Mistakes During Donald Trump Administration; Pro-Life Group Targeted And Protests At Justices' Homes; Oversight Officials Estimate At Least $100 Billion In COVID Relief Loan Funding Given For Fraudulent Requests; President Biden To Criticize Republican Plans For Fighting Inflation; Immigration Cases Continue To Increase Along Texas Border With Mexico; Former Trump Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  Says He Does Not Want Former President Trump To Run Again For Office; Pro-Choice Demonstrators Look To Protest At Homes Of Conservative Supreme Court Justices\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Lauren Simonetti, Trey Yingst, Alexandria Hoff, David  Spunt, Nate Foy, Brit Hume, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 7,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Paton has been celebrated as a national hero, and as an army of loyal fans. It makes me want to say, animals are great.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Yes, animals are great. They are so great.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Animals are great. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Again, thank you. Thank you to all the viewers to who put up with me and Greg, for 11 years now.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Happy birthday, Dana.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. We'll see you back here tomorrow. SPECIAL REPORT, it's up next.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Dana, Happy Birthday. It's a great day. It's Dana's birthday. I'm on "GUTFELD!" and Jesse's back.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes. Perfect.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, thanks.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. I'm Bret Baier. We're in New York City tonight. World headquarters for Fox News Channel. And just a few minutes, I will speak with former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  about the war in Ukraine, and his new book, Sacred Oath: Memoirs of a Secretary Of Defense during Extraordinary Times.\par \par \uc2 But we start tonight with the day's headlines.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): There have been no arrests in the weekend fire and vandalism of prominent Wisconsin pro-life lobbying group. President Biden has strongly condemned the attack which comes as pro-choice demonstrators protest outside the homes of some \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol agents tell Fox News they have encountered 1,500 migrants along the southern border in just the past few days. They say five of them are registered sex offenders.\par \par \uc2 A bit later, we will look at the toll being taken by human and drug smuggling in one area.\par \par \uc2 We'll also look at the efforts to find $100 billion stolen by scammers from the COVID Relief loan fund. We're told hundreds of people have been arrested and more suspects are in the pipeline.\par \par \uc2 Fox confirms Democratic appropriators are making a new offer on the Ukraine aid package. The new overall figure is $39.8 billion. This is on top of the $33 billion President Biden initially requested.\par \par \uc2 The additional funding provides $3.4 billion for food and a similar amount for additional military equipment.\par \par \uc2 And stocks continued to plummet today. The Dow lost 654. The S&P 500 fell 132. The NASDAQ dropped 521 today.\par \par \uc2 The economic malaise continues to play out on Wall Street and Main Street tonight fueled by worries over inflation, and what the country is or is not doing to control it.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Lauren Simonetti joins us tonight with the latest. Good evening, Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK REPORTER (on camera): Good to see you, Bret. Unfortunately, the news is not good. Wall Street's five week sell off just entered week number six the market is just screaming red as technical and psychological barriers were tested today.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI (voice-over): All three major averages falling to their lowest levels in more than a year.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost two percent. The S&P fell below that key 4,000 milestone. The tech heavy NASDAQ also taking it on the chin today. It fell below 12,000.\par \par \uc2 Investors are concerned about slowing growth, yes here at home, but also abroad, namely the world's number two economy, China, its exports fell the most in almost two years last month as it pursues a zero COVID policy.\par \par \uc2 Companies are concerned that could exacerbate the supply chain crisis, adding to inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 Add that to the inflation already being seen and felt at home, it's clear markets are not optimistic. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can engineer what we call a soft landing, curbing inflation just enough to avoid recession.\par \par \uc2 The Fed last week raised rates by half a percentage point, but critics think it is not enough and that is why some traders believe markets have taken matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNY POLCARI, MANAGING PARTNER, KACE CAPITAL ADVISORS: If the Fed doesn't do it, then, the market is going to do it. The market is going to get to a place where valuations are just going to then reflect where investors think it should be.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Oil taking a dive today, Bret, down more than $6 a barrel. That was partly tied to China's lockdowns, and also news that the E.U. could soften its sanctions on some Russian oil, both aspects would free up supply.\par \par \uc2 But despite all of that, gas prices jumped again to 433 a gallon just a fraction of a penny shy of the all-time record back in March.\par \par \uc2 So, what comes next for markets in this topsy-turvy week? All eyes on Wednesday. That's when you get the new consumer price data.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI (on camera): Expectations are the April numbers will show inflation slowing just a bit but still above eight percent. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pretty amazing. Lauren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Russia is celebrating Victory Day. The anniversary of its triumph over Germany in World War II. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  drawing parallels between that struggle, and his country's efforts in Ukraine. But Putin did not announce an escalation in the conflict as many feared he might do.\par \par \uc2 Corresponding Trey Yingst is in Kyiv again tonight.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Russian tanks and armored vehicles rolled down the streets of Moscow on Victory Day. As the country commemorates the day Germany surrendered in World War II, Russian President Putin spoke about his war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): You all fighting for the homeland for its future, so that no one forgets the lessons of World War II, so that there is no place in the world for executioners, punishers, and Nazis.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: While the Russian leader didn't make any announcements about broader military mobilization. As some expected, he did falsely accused the Ukrainians of being Nazis and planning to build nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 Poland also held events to commemorate Victory Day. At one memorial in Warsaw, Russia's ambassador to Poland was doused in red paint by anti-war protesters.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the annual celebrations, Ukrainian president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  addressed the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Russia has forgotten everything that was important to the winners of the World War II. But Ukraine and the whole free world will remind them, so that, then, no one will forget.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: His remarks come as Ukrainian forces successfully pushed back a Russian advance around the city of Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe, Russian troops in this area could retreat over the border within days.\par \par \uc2 In the south, Russian troops continue to encircle the Azovstal steel plant, where an estimated 2,000 Ukrainian troops are holed-up underground. Ukrainian officials confirmed over the weekend that all civilians had been successfully evacuated from the plant after being trapped for weeks through humanitarian corridors organized by the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Ukraine's Ministry of Defense says his troops are still defending the facility.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDR MOTUZYANYK, SPOKESPERSON, UKRAINE'S MINISTRY OF DEFENSE (through translator): In the Mariupol direction, the enemy continues to blockade our troops near the Azovstal plant. And with the help of artillery fire and tanks conduct assault operations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Tonight, Russian forces targeted the city of Odesa with missiles as their attempts to take more of southern Ukraine continue. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Trey.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to talk about the situation in Ukraine. And his book is former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His new book is called, A Sacred Oath: Memoirs of a Secretary of Defense During Extraordinary Times.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Bret, great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me start with Ukraine, just hearing today about this victory parade in Russia. And Putin's plans. How do you see this? And how do you see it ending?\par \par \uc2 Well, clearly, it started as a -- as a strategic failure, which is what I've been saying publicly, he has managed to have more \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops move into \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 He's unified Ukraine against him, pushed him more in our orbit, and he's unified the West. So, where this ends up at this point after the first phase clearly fell out, I think is he will try to consolidate his gains in the Donbas. That means expand his footprint in Donetsk and Luhansk.\par \par \uc2 And will he try and kind of sweep the southern coast and link up with Transnistria? We don't know. I don't think he can do that. Because right now, he doesn't seem to be consolidating his gains.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did you ever worry about Russia launching a full scale invasion of Ukraine during your time as defense secretary?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Nothing like this, not -- what we're seeing today. Obviously, he had already done this in 2014 and sees part of the Donbas. So, we were always watching to make sure what he was doing there and in Syria and other places, but not on this type of scale.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President Trump says it's about weakness. Here is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Putin saw this incredible weakness, and that's when he decided to invade Ukraine. He didn't look at Ukraine, when I was there, he would never have done it -- would have never, ever happened 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke to Putin about it a lot. And I told him what would happen if it did, and he understood that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you by that?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I think -- I think that Putin did sense some weakness, given our strategic withdrawal from Afghanistan, which I thought was abysmal. And I think that coupled with maybe the changing of the guard in Germany with \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  leaving, there may have been some things, things that fell into place that Putin thought, if he didn't act now that he would miss this opportunity to do so.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Jon Tester, in a hearing just the other day said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): To say that Putin invaded Ukraine, because of the pull out of Afghanistan is the most bizarre thing I've ever heard. This is absolutely ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, which is it?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, I guess, I don't think he pulled up because of Ukraine. I -- because of Afghanistan, I think that he might have sensed some weakness there, that there was a lack of resolve by President Biden, to really stand firm with our allies in Afghanistan and alongside our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies in \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, there may have been some -- something along those lines, but it's -- I can't point the one thing. I think there were probably a series of things happening, that Putin felt it was time to act.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can the Pentagon be getting aid there quicker?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Probably, I don't know what's going on in the inside. I think that the performance by the administration has been mixed. It was too slow early on and not sufficient.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why we ever took the military option off the table. I don't know why the president said what he said about minor incursions, and I think we should have move forward with providing Ukrainians with the MiGs.\par \par \uc2 But at this point in time, I mean, they're in a better position. The West is in a better position vis-a-vis Putin.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think that the Russian military is a paper tiger? I mean we're not seeing a lot of -- Russia.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I wouldn't say paper tiger. And clearly they have a strategic capability that, that there's been some talk about, but they did not live up to the expectations we thought they would be able to perform on the battlefield. Are you concerned about whether our defense in industrial base is ready for war? There's some people in Washington who say, we're just not producing weapons anymore.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan ism there's a $14 billion backlog, and the weapons, we said, we get them three years ago. I mean, can we handle all of this?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Well, first of all, I don't think the defense budget is sufficient. I thought this was an achievement of the Trump administration to begin rebuilding our military. And what we're seeing right now is inadequate.\par \par \uc2 That said, you know, I worked in the defense industry, and it is an industry prime for efficiency, not for surges in terms of producing Javelin missiles or Stinger, anti-aircraft missiles. So, it takes time to build these things. And we have to anticipate and surge when it comes to production. And that takes -- that takes some time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why did you write this book?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I wrote this book for a few reasons. You know, first and foremost, for history. For the American people to understand what was a very consequential and tumultuous time in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 To learn from it. To learn from things, from times when I succeeded from times when I made mistakes, and from how we kind of made advances with regard to modernizing our military or, you know, Operation Warp Speed, which brought us the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And to talk about the challenges I faced as a cabinet member.\par \par \uc2 But you have many times in this book, where you have issues with President Trump. Something he is doing, something he is saying. Concern, serious concerns you write about in this book? Why didn't you quit?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Because I thought that quitting would result in my immediate dismissal, which would be a fair thing for the president to do. And I would not be there to help shape policy in a favorable direction, to help push back against the bad ideas.\par \par \uc2 And oh, by the way, to help advance the good ideas that we had achieved in the administration, right? Warp Speed, the standing up of Space Force, putting additional assets into Cyber Command. There are a number of accomplishments we made as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you say in page one, can't you just shoot them? Just shoot them in the legs or something? Saying the president had asked you, for the U.S. military to shoot Americans in the streets of the nation's capital?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you said it was surreal.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is what you said in your confirmation hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: You know, at my time in the army, I grew up with this view that if anything, if you're asked to do anything that is illegal or immoral or unethical, then, that would be the point at which you have to consider resignation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And you'd be willing to do that?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are there points in this book where you clearly write that you had crossed that Rubicon? And yet you don't resign?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: No, I never crossed that Rubicon. I said I would never do anything illegal, immoral, or unethical --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you suggest that you were being asked to.\par \par \uc2 I was -- it was being suggested by the president and we successfully pushed back on that. We, being Attorney General Barr, General Milley, myself, and walking back from that notion.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: General Mattis had problems too. He resigned, others have resigned, you felt it was better for you to be in place, and save this criticism for this book.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I thought it was -- I thought my duty was to the country, and it is, and remains.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was better staying in the job, and trying to shape policy to prevent bad things, and advance good things for the nation, rather than just resigning.\par \par \uc2 Look, I could resign on the spot, it would have saved me a lot of grief, a lot of headache. But I thought that would be putting myself before the country, not the other way around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a threat to democracy?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I think that, given the events of January 6th, given how he has undermined the election results, he incited people to come to D.C., stir them up that morning, and fail to call them off. To me, that threatens our democracy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, yes.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I think the answer would that -- what else can you conclude, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, do you think he is going to run for president again?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I don't know. I hope he doesn't. I hope that the Republican base can figure out that while President Trump pushed a lot of traditional Republican ideas, right? Smaller government, less taxes, a stronger military, border security, all those things, that there are other candidates out there that could run that could do it without dividing the people, without creating such tension within the country, and do it by growing the base as well.\par \par \uc2 I think there are candidates out there that can do that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think looking back, you would have done differently?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: You know, I was blessed with a great team, first of all, at the Pentagon, from the senior uniformed leadership to the senior civilian leadership. And I think we, you know, we made a lot of good progress in terms of advancing the National Defense Strategy.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, making headway on Operation Warp Speed, which delivered two vaccines on time. And so, I think there are a lot accomplishments I want to celebrate. You know, I made my share of personal mistakes, if you will, whether it was, you know, the walk across Lafayette Park, things like that.\par \par \uc2 But look, you take for the good with the bad. I know that in my heart, I was serving my country once again as I did as an 18-year-old cadet at West Point. And, again, I think we accomplished a lot at the same time.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The country is divided. And do you see a way to get the country back together? Do you think your book helps in that?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: I think the greatest threat we face as a country is the extreme partisanship coming from both sides of the aisle. And we need to find a way to come back together to work together.\par \par \uc2 Because until we do, it's going to be hard to take on the Chinese, it's going to be hard to address our burgeoning debt. It's going to be hard to address all these other issues.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just trying to give insight into one person's perspective on what I think was a very tumultuous and consequential administration.\par \par \uc2 I think there are a number of Republicans out there that can -- that can become the next president in 2024. Republicans who will not just advance a traditional Republican agenda of lower taxes and a stronger military and border security and things like that, but can do so in a way that not only grows the Republican base, but can bring the country together. That's what we need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is your old classmate from West Point \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  one of them?\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Mike could be. I think we have to see whether Mike is going to run and what positions he'll take and will he be able to chart a path -- a path different from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you for the time.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll have part two of this interview tomorrow. More SPECIAL REPORT after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, an escaped Alabama inmate and a corrections officer who allegedly helped him break free are no longer on the run. They have been apprehended in Evansville, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Marshal Service confirms Casey White is in custody. Vicky White was taken to the local hospital with what appears to be a self-inflicted gunshot wound to the head. This comes after the two were spotted leaving a hotel and a vehicle pursuit followed that, here's officials moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  were in pursuit of a black \uc2 Ford\uc2  pickup. I think it was a \uc2 Ford\uc2  F-150. And Casey White was driving that vehicle. Vicky White was a passenger. During the pursuit, the pickup truck got wrecked. Casey White surrendered. Vicky White has been transported to the hospital for treatment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again, no relation Casey White, Vicky White, but that hunt is over.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there are growing concerns tonight about the shortage of baby formula in the U.S. CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  tell Fox News they are limiting customers to three baby formula products per purchase now.\par \par \uc2 Some people warned the rationing of supplies could lead to safety concerns if parents result to -- resort rather to alternatives to replace in the central need. The shortage has grown out of supply chain issues and a recent recall of some baby formula by a prominent manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  told reporters today the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is working with manufacturers to increase and streamline their supplies.\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice activists are making a major pushback against the leaked \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  draft laying out the possible overturning of the Roe v. Wade decision. President Biden is condemning an arson attack on a Wisconsin pro- life group. And there's also a strong reaction to demonstrations outside the homes of some of the conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff has the latest tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We're certainly not suggesting anyone break any laws.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A day after a Molotov cocktail was thrown into a pro-life organizations office in Wisconsin, the White House issued a statement saying that the president supports freedom of expression but "that expression must be peaceful and free of violence, vandalism or attempts to intimidate".\par \par \uc2 Authorities are investigating the Sunday morning attack on the office of Wisconsin Family Action. Madison police have yet to name a suspect but have acknowledged that the group's anti-abortion stance is what made them a target.\par \par \uc2 SHON BARNES, POLICE CHIEF OF MADISON, WISCONSIN: Hate or violence do not advance any cause.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Increased concern has been raised for Supreme Court justices following the leak of Justice Alito's draft opinion which could overturn Roe v. Wade, a protest at his Northern Virginia home is planned for this evening.\par \par \uc2 Does the president plan to condemn the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion or the doxxing of the justices now that we've seen violence unfold?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I would say that we have been clear and the president's position has long been that we should not see protests that takes the form of violence, that takes the form of vandalism, and that threatens anyone. That has long been his position for his entire career and continues to be his position.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Psaki added that the justices have not been victimized by violence at this point.\par \par \uc2 In a letter sent to colleagues today, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  lauded the protests writing "We have been moved by how so many have channeled their righteous anger into meaningful action."\par \par \uc2 Last week, Senators \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  and Chris Coons introduced bipartisan legislation to increase protection for Supreme Court justices and their families by expanding security around the clock.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): There have been a few snags in getting this bill through as quickly as planned. And in addition, I should mention there is a federal law that prohibits picketing outside of the home of somebody who is involved in a court case if the aim is to influence the outcome of that court case, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new information about where your money really went, when it was supposed to be helping families hurt by job losses during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox New Mexico in Albuquerque as firefighters in the Rocky Mountain Foothills battle the largest fire in the country right now. It's burned about 300 homes. That blaze has taken a hold in rugged areas difficult for firefighters to reach.\par \par \uc2 WFXT in Boston with the sexual misconduct trial of celebrity chef Mario Batali, the woman who accuses him of kissing and groping her while attempting to take a selfie testified today. Batali says the assault never happened and that the accuser has a financial incentive to lie.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Louisville from WDRB. One of the big stories there tonight, reaction to Saturday's massive upset at the Kentucky Derby, 80 to one shot Rich Strike. Stunned the experts with that victory. Rich Strike's victory is the second biggest long shot to win, the first leg of horses -- horse racing Triple Crown and the greatest odds upset since 1913.\par \par \uc2 His owner and trainer tell Fox & Friends they did not think their horse would even be in the race until another entrant was scratched before the deadline.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The lawyer for Dave Chappelle says the comedian wants Los Angeles County District Attorney George Gascon to bring felony charges against the man who allegedly attacked him on stage. He did attack him. Gabriel Colwell tells "The New York Post" the refusal to prosecute the case as a felony is a travesty of justice. Isaiah Lee is charged instead with four misdemeanor counts. Lee is due back in court on May 20th. He's pleading not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have disturbing news about how your tax dollars have been spent and misspent on the program designed to help out people in need during the pandemic. Correspondent David Spunt has a report from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: More than two years into the pandemic, multiple oversight officials estimate that fraudsters stole at least $100 billion in COVID relief loan funding meant to help small businesses survive.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HOROWITZ, PANDEMIC RESPONSE ACCOUNTABILITY: I think people made a conscious decision we are going to get the money out, and if there is fraud, you'll chase it later. And that's just not an acceptable model.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: FOX News sat down with Justice Department Inspector General Michael Horowitz, chair of the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee, a group made of inspectors general overseeing COVID relief fund.\par \par \uc2 HOROWITZ: The Treasury Department has a do-not-pay system. Turned out 57,000 loans were paid out in those first few weeks to individuals who on the do-not-pay list.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: That list includes known fraudsters. Authorities have arrested hundreds, and FOX News is told more are in the pipeline. According to court documents, Richard Ayvazyan and his wife Marietta Terabelian adopted fake identities and secured at least $18 million in paycheck protection program loans. Both on the run, Richard was sentenced to 17 years, his wife six.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CALAMUSA, SMALL BUSINESS OWNER: He selfishly took it literally out of the mouths of babes.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Nancy Calamusa owns a small business that helps feed infants with medical issues. She was denied COVID relief-related loans multiple times and blames fraudster for taking advantage of the system.\par \par \uc2 CALAMUSA: You took it out of my patients' mouths, my pocket, my employees. And that's not OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Michael Horowitz says that his oversight committee is expected to be funded through September, 2025, but may be extended if the caseload before him increases. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in senior political analyst Brit Hume. Good evening, Brit.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your thoughts about this?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think, to a great extent, this was inevitable. Remember, the government almost overnight locked down a huge slice, indeed most of the U.S. economy. Overnight millions of people, tens of millions of people lost their jobs, and many were left without any way to keep going. So the government and Congress were trying to do was to get money into people's hands urgently, as the fast as they could.\par \par \uc2 So the normal safeguards that you would put in place or to use to try to prevent fraud and prevent the money from being use in the wrong way really were set aside, and the money was basically dropped out of airplanes. And the fact that they are estimating, what $100 billion in fraud, I wouldn't be surprised if that number went higher. It all is traceable back to the decision to lock down the economy, which I think in retrospect the collateral damage from which was so severe in all sorts of ways, and this is just yet one other example of it, that I think I feel like I was on the right track when I said it was the worst public policy decision of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you have said that for a long time as this has unfolded. And now, up on Capitol Hill, there is this push for more COVID funding and some reticence from both Republicans and some Democrats to saying wait, let's figure out where everything went.\par \par \uc2 HUME: That's right. And I think the spending level was so staggering that I think it's fair to say that the inflation that we are experiencing was to a great extent a result of that spending. And it's not surprising to me that there is a limited happen appetite for more of it, certainly among Republicans, but even some Democrats as well. I think it was -- inflation was a consequence of it. I think most people can see that. And I guess the president is coming out tomorrow and start talking about inflation, blaming Republicans. But I think he is at a stage now where Republicans for the plans they have or not -- the plans they do have and for the plans they don't have, I suspect he is in a state now where what he needs on inflation is better results, and that political rhetoric isn't going to help him very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To that point, in the president's schedule, Brit, and you have seen these schedules, covered the White House, as did I, they put in what the president is doing. It says on the week ahead, "The president will deliver remarks on his plan to fight inflation and lower costs for working families and contrast his approach with congressional Republicans ultra-MAGA plan to raise taxes on 75 million American families and threaten to sunset programs like Social Security, Medicare, and Medicaid."\par \par \uc2 This is in the schedule, Brit. And you know "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " called that three Pinocchios as far as attacking the Republicans' plan like that. But kind of interesting that they are phrasing it that way.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, I think it's an attempt to try to score some points. Republicans are having a field day with him on inflation and other economic issues. And even though unemployment is strikingly low, jobs are being created, people are back to work, jobs are going begging, in fact, the people's opinions on the condition of the economy are very dark. And I think that's a consequence of inflation. And I'm not sure that attacking the Republicans for plans that that the White House is claiming they have is going to get him very far at this stage. He needs some better results.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we'll take you to a Texas town inland from the southern border that's suffering terrible effects from the illegal immigration crisis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A Haitian citizen has been extradited from Jamaica to the U.S. to face criminal charges related to the assassination of Haitian president \uc2 Jovenel Moise\uc2 . Joseph Joel John is the third individual to be charged and arrested in the U.S. related to that plot. Moise was killed during an attack on his private residence last July.\par \par \uc2 Tonight here in the U.S., we take to a rural Texas area that is enduring the ravages of human and drug smuggling as the border crisis intensifies. Correspondent Nate Foy is in Carrizo Springs, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: New videos tonight show human smuggling attempts in the Del Rio sector. Scenes like these repeated themselves in small towns and communities along the southern border. Migrants pour out of this truck just two weeks before Title 42 is set to end. It's a situation local authorities are struggling to contain even now.\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY MARCIAL GARZA, ZAVALA COUNTY, TEXAS, SHERIFF'S DEPARTMENT: We've seen an uprise in human smuggling cases. In 2019 we estimate about 50 cases. Now we're over 300 cases for 2022 with the same exact man power.\par \par \uc2 FOY: From tunnels like this --\par \par \uc2 GARZA: This is where they stay overnight. They will hide down here. So they are out here all day, and then they get picked up and they're placed in the back of a trunk.\par \par \uc2 FOY: -- into trailers. New pictures tonight show 203 migrants busted by the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 . Smugglers tearing through small communities, putting the innocent people who live there in danger.\par \par \uc2 LETICIA DIAZ, CARRIZO SPRINGS, TEXAS, RESIDENT: It's horrible. It's horrible how the chases start, and all of the sudden somebody gets killed or somebody gets hurt, or our children could get hurt.\par \par \uc2 GARZA: We have seen a risk to our citizens' lives because the pursuits that are occurring within the community, and we have seen uprise in the use of narcotic, drugs such as methamphetamines and heroin.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Ranch owners are forced to pay out-of-pocket for damage during chases.\par \par \uc2 GARZA: A lot of these ranchers are getting their fences torn down, and there isn't much we can do about it. We are a small department, so it's kind of difficult to catch everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, we're just learning tonight a teenager accused of smuggling five migrants was shot at 1:30 this morning during a chase with Kinney County deputies and Texas DPS. That teenager is in a San Antonio hospital. The \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2  are investigating.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Samples extracted from three American tourists who died at a resort in the Bahamas under mysterious circumstances have been sent to a lab in the U.S. Their bodies were found Friday morning at the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort. The couples had been staying in two separate villas. Toxicology results are expected in about a week.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel has reaction to my interview with former defense secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , part one, and thoughts about violence from pro-choice activists.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Authorities in Shanghai tighten antivirus restrictions just as the Chinese city was emerging from a month of strict lockdowns due to the COVID-19 outbreak. Residents in several districts are being ordered again to stay home and are barred from receiving nonessential deliveries.\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland's largest unionist party refuses to return to a power sharing government following its loss in local elections. The Democratic Unionist Party says it will not do so until its demands over post-Brexit customs arrangements are met.\par \par \uc2 And a Nepalese sherpa guide scales Mount Everest for the 26th time. Kami Rita breaks his own record for the most climbs of the world's highest peak. He led a group of Sherpa climbers who fixed ropes along the route so hundreds of others could make their way to the top of the mountain later this month.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are points in this book where you clearly write that you had crossed that Rubicon, and yet, you don't resign.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY: No, I never crossed that Rubicon. I said I would never do anything illegal, immoral, or unethical.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you suggest that you were asked to.\par \par \uc2 ESPER: It was being suggested by the president. And we successfully pushed back on that, "we" being Attorney General Barr, General Milley, and myself, and walking back from that notion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  talking about President Trump, his time his time in the administration, saying he hopes that President Trump does not run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, editor of TheTransom.com, host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and Josh Kraushaar is the politics editor for "National Journal." Ben\par \par \uc2 Josh, what did you make of that? Obviously, there were a lot of -- part two of the interview is tomorrow and gets into some of the more specifics about some of the different operations that he oversaw. But listening to the former defense secretary talk about his time and talk about this book is interesting.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes. Bret, Esper is one of many former Trump officials who have made the argument that they were themselves indispensable for saving American democracy. Clearly there were a lot of other folks of like mind serving in the administration, but the tension between sticking around as secretary of defense. And there was a contradiction there that you pointed out, Bret, and I thought his answer was quite interesting.\par \par \uc2 But I think the biggest political worry a lot of Republicans have if Trump runs for president again is that he won't have those establishment-oriented figures serving with him, that there won't be Espers, there won't be Jim Mattises around him. And that's probably the biggest concern among traditional Republicans and why you are seeing so many of his former advisers going public with these concerns.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And Ben, obviously Trump supporters would say good.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Absolutely they would say good. They would look at someone like Secretary Esper as a problem. Look, this is the latest in a long series of Washington books stretching back for centuries titled, effectively, if only they had listened to me. And it's interesting to see Esper going around and doing these interviews and talking about this today.\par \par \uc2 I actually have a lot of questions about the policy side of things, which I'm looking forward to seeing you ask him about in the second part tomorrow, because when you drive down into specifics, I'm not sure that Secretary Esper thinks that a lot of the things that he is dinging President Trump over are as effective as he believes them to be. In fact, I think that you could see situations where the president had the right of the perspective and that Esper should not have been standing in the way as he was.\par \par \uc2 Yet again, though, this is an example of how the Trump administration was basically eaten by the swamp, devoured by the people who came in and served him, perhaps operating under the idea that they would undermine him at every turn when he did something that they did not like.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we do get into some of those specifics in the interview tomorrow. But this is, Olivia, the second cabinet member who has come out with a book, and asked specifically whether he wanted the former president to run again, said no. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY: I hope he doesn't. I hope that the Republican base can figure out that while President Trump pushed a lot of traditional Republican ideas, right -- smaller government, less taxes, a stronger military, border security, all those things -- that there are other candidates out there, that could run that could do it without dividing the people.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The Make America Great Again movement has to mature. I don't think he should be our nominee, and I will certainly not support him in the primary process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And, Olivia, you cover Capitol Hill. Obviously, there is a lot of public talk about support for the former president, but privately are they more aligned, the Republican caucus, with those sentiments or with the Trump supporters' side?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Yes, Bret, I have talked to Republicans about this asking what they think. The answer that I usually get is that they are interested in Governor DeSantis running. They said that while they really liked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's policies, he created a lot of headaches from his tendencies to shoot from the hip. And that's one of the things that the voters liked about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but for Republican who are trying to do policy, sometimes those tweets arriving at moments when they were least expecting it, which differed from where the party platform had been, caused severe headaches for them, and not to mention the various scandals.\par \par \uc2 So talking to some of the Republicans who are out there saying we support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , we would love for him to run again, they have a different story that they are telling me more privately. And whether or not they are going to support him if he runs again, that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right. We have a little bit of time. But obviously the primary process will tell us a lot about his support across the country.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there is this debate over acceptable forms of protest as you have some pro-choice activists choosing to protest in front of conservative justices' homes. Take a listen to some of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ALLISON, BIDEN CAMPAIGN SENIOR STAFFER: We have free speech, I think as long as people are safe, that the American people have the right to protest how they want.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: There has to be some line in our society that remains inviolate, something we can agree upon collectively where we say enough, that it's wrong. And it has to start with the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Josh, it took a couple days for \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  to send out a tweet saying, obviously not for violence, but for free speech.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, Bret, the extremes are winning out, I think, on both sides. The fact that it took this violence in Wisconsin, then it took a day or two for the president, the president's press secretary, to criticize acts of violence, that was pretty striking. It shows that they have a very loud and extreme base that they are pandering to. And it's a worry, I think, for Democrats who think they may have the winning hand on this issue but are worried about the activists speaking up and being out of the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side. You have -- I think you have a public that's center right on abortion rights. They support regulations. They support 15-week abortions restrictions. But they certainly don't support abortion bans, and they support an exception for rape and incest. And you're now getting pressure from the Republicans in Congress, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  didn't rule out the possibility of an abortion ban. So there are extremes on both sides that both parties are having to deal with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Although he said he would use 60 votes, so he'd have to get through the votes if it was to happen, kind of speculating.\par \par \uc2 And obviously this reaction, Ben, comes after Molotov cocktails being thrown into a pro-life organization in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Look, the level of violence, I think, is unfortunately only likely to increase the longer that we have to wait for this decision. Once it comes down, though, this will move to the state level. And I think people might be surprised to see how quickly those states come to a conclusion -- 37 states have Republican or Democrat documentation in all three branches. And I think that that means this will be internal party discussion that reaches a quick conclusion in most states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You are debt free. You do not owe the college a penny.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you have a balance, you had a balance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about student debt. Just like that, an anonymous donor clears the debt of every 2022 graduate of Wiley College, a small historically black college in Marshall, Texas. Estimated total balances of the 100 graduates $300,000. Awesome moment.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, new FOX polls on the Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary, plus part two of that interview with former defense secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think China in the wake of Ukraine is looking seriously at taking over Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY: I think they have always been looking seriously at taking over Taiwan, and I think they're studying the Russia's successes and failures and trying to figure out how they can do better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. And guess what, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" has Jesse Watters back. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Group Of Protesters Show In Front Of The House Of Some Supreme Court Justices' House And Expressing Their Outrage Over The Leaked Draft But The White House Has No Say Of The Violence Looming In The Streets; A New Gallup Poll Shows That Majority Of Americans Are Increasingly Disappointed Of Rising Commodity Prices, Including Supply Shortage Of Baby Formula, While The Biden Administration Continue To Tout That The Economy Is Doing Great; \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  Is Getting A Left And Right Death Threats After He Bought The Social Media Giant Twitter; \uc2 Russia's Space Agency\uc2  Also Threaten Musk For Providing An Internet Service To Ukrainians; "It's Been Nice Knowin Ya": Musk Says He May Die "Under Mysterious Circumstances;" Holiday Horror: Mystery Surrounding Deaths Of Three Americans In Bahamas; THE FIVE Celebrates Dana's Birthday!\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Jesse Watters, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: We need to get America working like it used to. Senator, thank you so much. I appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Folks, Neil will be back tomorrow but you can catch me 2 p.m. every day on FBN. Making Money, believe me you want to check out the show every day this week. In the meantime, THE FIVE starts now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Jesse Watters, and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 It was a weekend of threats, protests and even arson as critics accused the White House of taking way too long to condemn the potential for violence. Far left activists ramping up their rage against the Supreme Court after the leaked draft opinion on Roe versus wade.\par \par \uc2 Protesters chanting outside the homes of Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on Saturday. Another one is planned for tonight outside the home of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . And a pro-life group headquartered in Wisconsin attacked with two Molotov cocktails. Vandals spray-painting graffiti that reads "if abortions aren't safe, then you aren't either."\par \par \uc2 Police are investing the incident as arson. The White House question over why it waited to speak out against the violence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Does the president plan to condemn the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion or the doxing of the justices now that we've seen violence unfold?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I would say that we have been clear and the president's position has long been that we should not see protests that take the form of violence, that takes the form of vandalism. And that threatens anyone.\par \par \uc2 We have not seen violence or vandalism against Supreme Court justices. We have seen it at Catholic churches. That's unacceptable. The president does not support that. We have seen it at some conservative organizations, we don't support that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  adding her voice to the fight over abortion she's praising protesters using righteous anger to march and mobilize over the end of Roe. And some in the media not concerned about potential threats over Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I think for a lot of people the conversation about civility feels like it misses the mark when constitutional rights that you believe that you had for over 50 years are about to be overturned. The justices have security.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, CO-HOST, ABC: And other justices are saying you have no right to stand and freely protest, peacefully protest outside of our homes. We have a right to privacy. Well, women have a right to privacy as well, and women have a right to privacy with their bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. So now we have our very Jesse Watters, our very own is back, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, people have been anxious to hear what you think about the leaked draft and the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you. Well first, I want to thank everybody for their support and everybody around the table, thank you and thank you for the bourbon. I only had a few drinks before the show today.\par \par \uc2 I know what people are going to say, Watters you went to people's homes. I did. But there's a difference. I went to elucidate not to intimidate. There's one guy with a microphone shows up asks a polite question and then leaves. It's a lot different than having a mob of angry radical activists screaming at the top of their lungs at nighttime while the kids are home. That's different and that's what I want to make clear right now.\par \par \uc2 I might have had my collar up. That was probably the most flagrant thing I did.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Terrible.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The White House gave license to this. Remember last week didn't really condemn it and now you see it's gone full blown crazy outside. I think Alito had to leave his house. He's in an undisclosed location. This is from the same crowd that bailed out the rioters. This is from mad \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 , no relation, who said to get up into people's faces and, you know, Pelosi didn't condemn this, she gave license to it.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to turn the midterms into a referendum on Roe and that's fine because they have nothing else that they've accomplished. And the media is going to bang this drum because they don't want to cover crime or the border or inflation. They have to do this. It has to be a culture war.\par \par \uc2 And so, just like the rage that they stoke over race in the summer of 2020, they are going to stoke the abortion rage this summer. It's going to be rage versus reason. Because we all know it's going back to the states. States can figure it out themselves, that's fine.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is sacred to the left. What that means is this is a religious situation. This is a religious war to the left. Nothing means more to them than this abortion situation. So, if you think a Molotov cocktail or graffiti or surrounding some justice's home is a big deal. That's nothing. Wait until the thing drops this summer, it is going to get so much worse.\par \par \uc2 But we all have to remember this, in this country, I know it's a very tender issue, first trimester people are pretty forgiving about abortion, they are, if you look at the polling. After those first couple weeks though, they don't want anything to do with abortion and everybody needs to focus on how the country feels, and that's how it feels.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: One of the things that they felt, Greg, was this is a quote from administration officials, "tremendous passion," and that, therefore, it was OK and they waited until today to actually say, actually don't commit violence.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Well, not only is it illegal, and I believe in all states, you're also a jerk. You know? Imagine if a producer at The Five decided to change the format and didn't tell you, would you go to Megan's house? Knock? I mean, that's just a -- I mean, when you think about it, personalize it, think about how mentally unstable you are going to somebody's house because you're upset at them for something either political or something to do with your job.\par \par \uc2 No, Greg, this is different, this is about abortion, the Supreme Court. Well, it's exactly the same thing. You go to somebody's house in order to intimidate and threaten them you're a jerk. And any journalist, I don't know who that person was on CNN if she was a journalist not sure or anybody on CNN. How stupid they are.\par \par \uc2 Because if you condone this, like saying like, you know, talking about civility doesn't work, then you're opening the door for that to visit you, your home. We have this I call it mutually assured civility, MAC, that's kind of understood. It's implied that's what we do in life.\par \par \uc2 But once you, like the left does this a lot, they open the door for the stuff and then when the stuff happens, they're like, my God. You just did it. \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  told people to get in people's faces, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  threw out these threats about Kavanaugh, remember that?\par \par \uc2 And then they -- it has, that's the -- then they blame attacks on like Chris Rock on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's like they somehow repeat debunked lies like this is going to lead to the banning of interracial marriage which we know is just laughable. It's easily debunked, but they are under the assumption that no one is actually going to (AUDIO) the opinion and find out it is exactly what they did with the fine people hoax.\par \par \uc2 They want to drum up and rile up people so that they get into a state of a rational fear so they hit the streets and intimidate people. And then you have to spend a couple of years and work backward to go, well, this never happened. This isn't real. This has not happened. It's going back to the states. Abortion isn't being made illegal, blah, blah, blah.\par \par \uc2 Bill Maher was amazing. Did you see when he was talking about it? He was basically learning in real time, literally about abortion. He's like, going most of the people who are pro-life are women. And, you know, abortion laws are far stricter in Europe than they are here.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't understand.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Judge, what about the intimidation factor at like a justice's house. Is that something that one, from the white house, you could say that's wrong, but also from the Justice Department you could say we're going to arrest you.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, if only the attorney general would act against the people who are violating a federal code the way he tried to act against parents of children and schools calling them domestic terrorists. I mean, we would have some sense of equity here but there is no equity because the left traffics in rage.\par \par \uc2 But the sad truth is 18USC Section 1507 it says picketing or parading whoever with the intent of interfering whether obstructing or impeding the administration of justice. And that's what they're trying to do folks. They're trying to obstruct the administration of justice. They don't want this decision to be the final decision.\par \par \uc2 And with the intent of influencing a decision, the decision with a judge and I'm trying to cut through this, resorts to a demonstration in or near a building or residence, and they're talking about judges, can be fined or imprisoned to not more than one year.\par \par \uc2 You know, if Psaki and Biden were so anti, you know, this kind of behavior, why didn't they call out the Department of Justice or the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and say, even with the Molotov cocktail going off in, where was it, in Wisconsin, saying, you know, we're going to send the federal agents out.\par \par \uc2 But I've got to tell you something. I am a Catholic, OK? And there was something that -- and I'm pretty tough. But there was some, the language that they used that was so incredibly offensive to me. And I respect the First Amendment and free speech. Let me be clear on this. But when they say, keep praying because we will be burning your eucharist, stop your rosary and keep praying. We're going to keep coming after this weekend. And then they're singing thank God for abortions, you know?\par \par \uc2 And everything they do is hypocritical because one hand they're saying that this decision is a threat to democracy they're so stupid they don't understand that the Supreme Court has said we're going to send it back to in the states so it can be an issue of democracy, so that you can vote depending upon what you believe.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court didn't say it was unconstitutional, they said let the people decide. And, finally, I think the outrage is even worse when they say, you know, they show the hangers and all that, you know, and that this is how people died, this is how people die.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, I don't want to get into, you know, when does life begin and all that, although you did reference it, Jesse, and I think most people would agree with the first, second and third trimester the way you analyzed it but you want to know, you want to use a hanger? How about you take a picture of an abortion, of a fetus being pulled apart?\par \par \uc2 Let's not go there. Let's try to have an intelligent discussion. And when \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  says we won't be bullied, their response is bully them more. They've tee themself up, the anger, the intent is there. Use the law, use the statute. That's all.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's all good, too. Harold, last word to you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, JR., FOX NEWS CO-HOST: First off, happy birthday --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: -- on a lighter note here. Two things, one, violence has no place in politics. We have seen anti-abortion activists. We've seen those who are opposed to certain things being taught in schools. We now see this nonsense, and it's no longer nonsense when you can't wake up outside your home as a judge or a justice because people are outside threatening you.\par \par \uc2 This one upmanship we have in politics, so it has to stop, the White House and others should call this out just like Republicans should call out violence when it happens or when the language is heated on the other side.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, I happen to think your analysis is right and welcome back.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think we have Casey on top of Roe. And I think the -- I hope the conversation can return to the facts and to the law here and whatever the court decides to do, I hope they don't overturn Roe. I frankly don't want them to give it back to the states because I don't want the patch work, but Judge, if they do, I'm in America, I can live with what the court says.\par \par \uc2 And there's politics behind everything, and that judges and justices and judges are chosen by politicians or by presidents and Democrats, if they're going to want to change this they have to do this at the -- at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 But first and foremost, stop the violence, stop the protesting outside of justices and judges' homes much like I wanted people to stop threatening school board members with violence. It has no place in American politics. This is archaic, it's brutal and people who do it should be arrested.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right. Coming up, President Biden set to blame Republicans for inflation as people run out of common items like baby formula.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: My top priority is fighting inflation and lowering prices for families on things they need. We're going to keep working to fight inflation and lower costs to all American families for a lot of other things as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  trying to turn the political tide with his presidency in peril over inflation. Biden said to bash Republicans as voters grow increasingly frustrated with higher prices. A new Gallup poll shows 52 percent worry about maintaining their standard of living and 40 percent stress over paying their bills.\par \par \uc2 The president also facing backlash over a baby formula shortage. Retailers now limiting how many products customers can buy due to a combination of inflation, supply shortages and product recalls.\par \par \uc2 Jesse, you were heavily medicated during this, a lot of this stuff so I'm interested in where you see this going. He's going to speak tomorrow. Do you have any thoughts?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, the Putin price hike is not working.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, let's find out.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And the White House is a new line of attack. They need to sharpen the contrast with Republicans in the midterms so you are going to see Biden getting hyper partisan and just blaming everything on the right. The country is not buying it. We know we printed and spend way too much money. Fauci shut down the economy and we put all of our supply chain in China, so when we opened back up, boom, the dollar got destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Everybody that has common sense understands that. But Biden could not be in a worse political situation, he can't blame China, he can't blame the Feds, so this is on him right now. He's now having a looming global recession, a stock market that looks like it's kind of shaky, inflation at record high and now there are shortages of not only electricity, Greg, but baby formula.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And let me tell you, baby formula is very expensive. The average family spends $1,500 a year on baby formula. And so you have a baby formula recall happening at the same time as baby formula thievery because you can get a lot of money on the black market. People are making, I think it was like $4,000 a week just stealing baby formula. They have to lock it up like they do toothpaste now at the \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So now they have like operation milk money where the feds have to go and then bust up baby formula rings. It's a really, really sad situation. If you're not breastfeeding, you'll be out of luck.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank God I'm not. Hope Mayor Pete is OK. So, Harold, in the green room you were saying I think it's time that Joe has to resign. I was really surprised you frankly said that. What would be your advice this week before he does resign?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I didn't say that. I think you might have misheard. Look, inflation is what's going to be the center piece of the debate comes fall time for Democrats and Republicans. I think Republicans are right to raise these issues, but they won't win unless they offer solutions. It's easy to say that prices are up. We all know that. We all know we're paying more for things.\par \par \uc2 I give the president more credit this week and I've called balls and strikes on him over the last several weeks. I give them credit for at least traveling outside of Washington and trying to find those examples of where investments are working. The fact that we have a Javelin production plant that he went to, you can like him or not, I think that was the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 The fact that he's going to Ohio to tout the semiconductor chip plant. These are the things that we have to be producing not only to unclog the supply chain, but to ensure that we're able to produce these things here --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How about a baby formula plant?\par \par \uc2 FORD: -- produce (Inaudible) here. And then I would focus on that as well.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Crime, inflation, and the border. That's all