 a bowl of S-word. I think that that kind of sentiment --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Say it. Say it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How many times has Dana cursed on the air? Let's hear.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Pacing people around with a reptile, even going into the wall --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh my gosh. (BLEEP)\par \par \uc2 And also, as a conservative women, you just are like -- you know you're not going to get the glowing profile, so you just get your (BLEEP) done. Great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Let's try something new. I'm going to ask Dana to hold my hand. Dana, if you feel like I'm saying something inappropriate. you squeeze it, and I will then move on to another issue.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Who's watching the kid? You didn't ask me to babysit.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Nap time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg, you're not legally allowed to do that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'll watch him?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dana --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Am I allowed?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, his bedtime is seven. Isn't that past you --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I have something for you to meet.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No way.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes way. We have a new --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my gosh. This is the best surprise ever.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Caroline Sherwood bringing in this new little guy. This is Percy.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Percy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi, Percy.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Percy is seven weeks old. We picked him up in Ohio yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And I think we have another.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, how cure he is.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You guys, wait, what about "ONE MORE THING? Hi, Percy!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He is big.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He's beautiful.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Is this the lemon cake?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Of course it is.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: From across the street. Del Frisco's.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You guys.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Lemon cake.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Come, Percy. I want to see your beautiful face.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You want to go to -- go to the judge.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Hey, Percy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Hi, Peter!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He smells the cake.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow, OK, we haven't had a lemon cake in a while. Wow, you guys, this is a lot. Your birthday is the time to really reflect on all your friends and just a huge amount of gratitude for all of you in THE FIVE. And hi, Percy. Oh, this is your debut. You better make it good.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Would you like to cut the cake or should I?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Let's see. Who's having the lemon cake?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I am.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Peter, come on over.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I know. You got to work. I got to work. OK.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know what, Dana, I have some words of advice.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wait, what about the break?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The words of advice are, everything will be OK.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love that. Hi, Peter.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Great advice, everybody. Happy birthday, Dana!\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Percy, Percy, he's looking --\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'd like to thank Dr. Marc Siegel for shepherding me through this entire back situation process. He's been phenomenal. Thank you so much. Also to his wife ,Luda, who's also been instrumental in getting me healthy. Also, special shout out to my surgeon Dr. Paul McCormick up at Columbia Presbyterian. He did an excellent job and I'm feeling much better. But mostly, thank you to Emma.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Who has been tying my shoes and getting me ready every day. She has been phenomenal. And I love her so much. Thank you so much, Emma.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And thank you to everyone who's taking care of him and he's back at the table and my sister Angie is very happy. I'll go quickly. This weekend, I attended a wonderful wedding. Jeff and Kimmy Fritz and Hope and Ron Morgan said -- wonderful congratulations to Kelsey Fritz and Hamilton Morgan. They got married this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And I knew that Greg would like it because the Fritz had a wedding and I was there and Caroline was there and Layton and Colin and Hudson and Ryan. They did a great job. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The Fritz. All right, tonight we got a great show. It's -- I got Bret Baier, Bret Baier. I got Skunk Baxter. The only guy that's worked on \uc2 NASA\uc2  and in the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, former Doobie Brothers. Rob Long, you know him. Kat Timpf, I think you're familiar with that chick. Great show tonight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, and Judge?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK. So, I noticed that my poodles were having trouble reading and their eyes were tearing. So, the vet said they can't ride in a convertible because it's not good for their eyes. So I use my own medical degree and came up with his brilliant solution. I put goggles on the dog so now they can ride in a convertible or in the back of a pickup truck.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You know what they call those?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, goggles.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Doggles.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Doggles.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: There you go. Harold.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: The dog -- the dog theme continues. Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  awarded a medal to abrade bomb sniffing Jack Russell Terrier named Patron. Patron is credited with sniffing out more than 200 explosive devices since Russia invaded Ukraine. Patriot has been celebrated as a national hero and has an army of loyal fans. It makes me want to say animals are great. Animals are great.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Animals are great. Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Again, thank you. Thank you to all our viewers who put up with me and Greg for almost 11 years now.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Happy birthday, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's it for us. We'll see you back here tomorrow. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New York City Crime Impacting Office Return; Manhunt For Prison Inmate and Corrections Officer Continues; \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Takeover; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  (R-NC); Supreme Court Protests; Inflation Rising; Dow Sinks\b0\par\par\uc2 Alexandria Hoff, Trey Yingst, Keith Kellogg, Jennifer Griffin,  Kelly O'Grady, Susan Li, Madison Alworth, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,080 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When I do drive, I want to know that I'm going to get where I'm going and be able to afford the gas that I need.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: If it hit $5, what would that mean for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Then I probably wouldn't go really anywhere else, just like the food shopping and back home and to work, and that's it. I wouldn't do anything else.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Whatever's on sale, that's what we go for.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: FOX on top of investors and consumers under siege, the Dow hitting its lowest level in more than a year, as consumers hit hard by high gas prices are growing increasingly worried about their finances.\par \par \uc2 A new Gallup poll finding 40 percent are concerned about how they're going to pay their bills, more than half now worried about how they will maintain their standard of living. Today, the fallout for the markets and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "YOUR WORLD."\par \par \uc2 And we have got FOX team coverage, Susan Li on the market and its fallout, and Madison Alworth on consumers who just want out.\par \par \uc2 We began with Susan\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, almost $10 trillion in stock market losses so far this year. So that's half the U.S. economy wiped out in five months.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're looking at the lowest levels in a year on Wall Street, investors selling again today on fresh concerns that China's COVID lockdowns will mean supply disruptions that will keep inflation elevated, at least for some time.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to force the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise rates quickly and sharply, at least in the short term, possibly the most aggressive pace of rate increases since the 1980s. And that means investors are selling off heavyweight technology shares once again, which have outperformed the past two years, but might look expensive, especially in a higher interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not just stocks. The explosion in cryptocurrency prices is also coming back down to reality. You have Bitcoin trading now at less than half of recent record prices. Bitcoin, cryptocurrency has been a popular trade for younger investors. And speaking of which, you know those gains on those infamous meme stocks of AMC and \uc2 GameStop\uc2  that we talked about last year, it's all gone now.\par \par \uc2 Those gains have been wiped out. And you have to remember, Charles, that, at the peak of this year, the average Joe investors accounted for roughly a third of Wall Street trading volume, and that might be disappearing.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, well, the market, no -- there's no place to hide in this market right now.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile -- thanks a lot, Susan.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Americans struggling to just keep up. Gas prices keep moving up. And now drivers are shopping around just to try and save a few bucks.\par \par \uc2 And, well, doesn't FOX Business' Madison Alworth know it. She's been speaking with drivers in Clifton, New Jersey. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Madison, tell us some of these stories.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely, Charles.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have been watching this line at \uc2 Costco\uc2  grow and stay long all day long, people looking to save money wherever they can, as these gas prices continue to soar, people waiting for tens of minutes to get just to the pump. But we're talking about a savings of around 20 cents compared to the state average.\par \par \uc2 So that really does add up, especially if you have a truck or something of that nature. So we have some video that we want to show you, as you can see, these lines stretching really long. The gas here, $4.23 per gallon, that's a good savings when you look at the state average, which is closer to $4.47. The national average, that sits at $4.33.\par \par \uc2 So, with all of that going on, you think, OK, we're back up to that high. Bad news, we're expecting prices to go even higher. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LIPOW\uc2 , PRESIDENT, \uc2 LIPOW OIL ASSOCIATES\uc2 : The matter is, is that we're in for a period of higher energy prices as Europe and the rest of the world tries to wean itself off of Russian oil supplies.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to take several years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Yes, several years. And, right now, people can't wait for that.\par \par \uc2 Like you heard at the top of the show, some of those individuals we spoke to, they're cutting out certain trips, only doing the necessary trips because they just can't afford it. And, right now, we see that national average, $4.33.\par \par \uc2 That is the current high. It was set back in March. The issue is, Lipow is expecting us to add 5 to 10 cents onto the national average this week. If that happens, really when that happens, we're going to see a new national high price for gas -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thanks, Madison.\par \par \uc2 Of course, after that is the summer driving season.\par \par \uc2 Well, Americans worrying about paying their bills, is that a worry heading into the midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 With me now, economist Steve Moore, market watcher Larry Glazer and RealClearPolitics' Susan Crabtree.\par \par \uc2 And, Susan, I got to believe it's a major concern, considering now the urgency factor we seem to be getting from President Biden on this.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN CRABTREE, REALCLEARPOLITICS: That's right.\par \par \uc2 We're going to hear from the president tomorrow. He's going to announce some changes and that he's taking on inflation head on. But I'm not sure he's going to convince the American people to trust him on the issue.\par \par \uc2 You have Democrats grasping for some change in the dynamic of the campaign. But they are really trying to make abortion the campaign issue, but people really vote pocketbook issues. And you have a flurry of polls out there right now that are terrible news for the Biden administration and Democrats heading into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 You had a recent ABC News/\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  survey that said that 68 percent of respondents said they don't like how Biden is handling inflation, and they prefer Republicans to handle the way they're handling -- would handle inflation by a rate of like 50 percent, compared to 31 percent for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So we're seeing some big headwinds on this issue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's interesting, because President Biden keeps saying the same thing over and over again. I feel like he's putting P.R. instead of -- in front of Rx, when it comes to healing us on inflation.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Steve, I want you to take a listen to what President Biden had to say today. He did address inflation earlier. Let's take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My top priority is fighting inflation and lowering prices for families on things they need.\par \par \uc2 We're going to keep working to fight inflation and lower cost to all American families for a lot of other things as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Steve, he's had a lot of opportunities to do that. But he won't go where he can go, particularly starting with energy.\par \par \uc2 He will not release or unleash what we have got under our feet. We keep talking about gasoline crippling the average American out there. He just won't go there.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Well, you're exactly right, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And you look at, obviously, those big increases in gas prices -- and, incidentally, I was in the Midwest this week giving a few speeches. And when I talked about 8.5 percent inflation, Charles, would -- the audience would get angry at me. And they would say, it's not 8.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 They're looking at those gas prices. And they say, look, everything we buy is going up by 15, 20, 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And that's the -- that's the inflation rate that a lot of families are feeling.\par \par \uc2 The other thing about Biden, if you listen to what he's been saying in recent days, in recent weeks, if you notice, Charles, he's always blaming someone else. So, it's the beef producers. Or, yesterday, he said it was the...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The rails.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: The trains, the rail people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And then he says it's the oil companies, the pharmaceutical industry, as if all of a sudden all these companies got greedy.\par \par \uc2 And one other quick point. The thing that I think is really unsettling the market right now -- and, gosh, I don't even want to look at my 401(k) plan -- is that there doesn't seem to be a solution coming from this administration.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , as you know, Charles, last week said, well, we want to repeal the Trump tax cuts, which would only make inflation worse. And then they're still talking about this Build Back Better bill that would add another couple of trillion dollars of spending.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's the reason people are so nervous, because it doesn't look like this administration has any plan, except pointing the finger at other people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of 401(k)s, one of -- the hottest trending thing on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  right now is my 401(k). But, Larry, people are calling it their 101(k).\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, listen, it's sort of a joke, but it's painful as hell. And you should see how angry people are on social media.\par \par \uc2 LARRY GLAZER, MAYFLOWER ADVISORS: You know, Charles, based on the conversations that I have been having with people, whether it's CEOs or 401(k) participants or small business owners, their single biggest concern, including market volatility, is no longer COVID. It's no longer the shutdowns in China.\par \par \uc2 It's not Russia-Ukraine. It's inflation and these rising prices. And they're seeing it spill over into their retirement accounts. And, look, at Mother's Day barbecues all around the country, people were talking about how much it costs to fill the gas tank, how much the groceries cost, how much the Mother's Day cost, right?\par \par \uc2 That's not consumer confidence you want to see. So far, the consumer has held up like a champ, right? They're spending. They want to travel. It's pent-up demand. But for how long? Because the fear is, they're starting to lose confidence. When they go to the barbecue, and they go to the barbershop, and people talk about a recession, are they going to start to retrench?\par \par \uc2 And the fear here is, it bleeds into the regular economy. It hasn't happened yet.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: But when you see this volatility in the financial markets, Charles, you know it's a matter of time, unless the Fed can instill confidence, unless Washington can show policies to people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: And, right now, Washington's not going to bail you out. That's the difference between now and the COVID sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Susan, we're already seeing some cracks in the armor. So, Friday, we learned that consumer credit, what we're using on credit cards jumped the most in a single month in 10 years since December 2010. On Friday, the employment report, the household survey, we lost 353,000 jobs. And, of course, first-quarter GDP was negative.\par \par \uc2 And to Steve's point, every time the White House messages this, they never take the hit. I was reading in The Journal today...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... where Richard Nixon was advised to take the hit early in his tenure. He said no, right?\par \par \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and Volcker took the hit. America went through some pain. They were honest with us. We took a painful thing.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And then we had a decade of golden years. What about the White House?\par \par \uc2 Any chance the White House pivots, Susan, and says, OK, maybe we are responsible for some of this, maybe we will put the climate agenda on the back burner for a moment?\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Well, they haven't seen stood up to their base, their liberal base, so far.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know if there's any reason we're going to see this big change, this big pivot right now. You had -- it took a midterm scorching during the President Biden -- President Clinton years for him to say, OK, we're going to pursue a balanced budget, and we're going to go really lead from the middle here.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: And the Biden administration, that's why he supposedly won.\par \par \uc2 And that's not what we're seeing right now. We're seeing push for Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Let's get Manchin and Sinema on board and more money sloshing around the economy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We have got 30 seconds, Steve.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: It's almost, Charles...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: ... like they're trying to -- what they're trying to do is double down on this failed agenda.\par \par \uc2 And I think the Clinton comparison, because I lived through all those Clinton years. Clinton's first two years in office were a disaster. And they lost big time, the Democrats, but then Clinton did very effectively moved to the middle, Charles, and we got a lot of things done.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We had a booming stock market. We balanced the budget.\par \par \uc2 I don't see any inclination right now whatsoever that the Biden administration will move to a more sensible position. When they talk about more taxes and more spending...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And more spending, yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: ... it's almost like they don't get it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No, we got to...\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: And they need to be serious about fighting inflation, serious about fighting spending, serious about fighting inflation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. They're not serious about that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: That's the way to restore confidence. That's what we need. We need confidence in the financial markets.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Steve made a cogent point here. They really -- it's the climate change agenda first, foremost, and last, whereas Bill Clinton was a brilliant politician, and he listened to the American people.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, folks, protesters marching on some Supreme Court justices' homes, and a pro-life group in Wisconsin coming under attack. What is the White House saying about all of this? And we will have the latest from the briefing, plus reaction from \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Republican \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, what should we make of what Russia did and didn't do on this Victory Day? Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg is here, as anti-war protests get some revenge over there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The scene today in Warsaw, the Russian ambassador to Poland hit with red paint by protesters over the war against Ukraine, this coming without an expected surge by Russia's military on its annual Victory Day holiday celebrating the end of World War II.\par \par \uc2 To FOX's Trey Yingst in Kyiv with the very latest -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  spoke today in Moscow, but made no major announcements about the war in Ukraine. His remarks came as Victory Day celebrations got under way in the capital of Russia. These celebrations included a parade and having soldiers in the streets.\par \par \uc2 There were around 11,000 of them marching, with military equipment rolling through the Russian capital. But Putin made no declaration of victory and did not announce any broader mobilization of forces.\par \par \uc2 He did, however, accuse Ukraine, without evidence, of planning to retake Crimea and threatening nuclear war. Meanwhile, in Poland, a Victory Day memorial was held. Russia's ambassador to Warsaw was doused with red paint by anti-war protesters during that event, the chaotic scene unfolding this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And in neighboring Ukraine, Russian troops and separatist leaders participated in smaller parades and events in places like the occupied southern port cities of Mariupol and Kherson. Holding a large black and orange banner, they walked through the streets of Mariupol, where an estimated 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers are still holed up underneath a steel plant.\par \par \uc2 The facility continues to take fire after Ukrainian officials confirmed all civilians were evacuated over the past several days. These developments come as fighting continues along the eastern front lines. In the northern city of Kharkiv, Ukrainian troops launched a successful counteroffensive that could push the Russians back into their own territory within days, that according to recent analysis.\par \par \uc2 What we're seeing right now, Charles, in the east is a war of attrition. Russian troops are not successful so far in making significant gains into Ukrainian territory, and they continue to fire missiles into Ukraine -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Trey Yingst, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 So, how should we read what Russia did and didn't do on its Victory Day?\par \par \uc2 I want to get right to retire \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Lieutenant General and FOX News contributor Keith Kellogg on all of this.\par \par \uc2 General, there was a lot of speculation going into that -- into the Victory Day parade and speech there was going to be an escalation, that this was going to now...\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET.), FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... be called a war, that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would press the pedal to the metal. What happened?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, Charles. And thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Look, a couple of things happened. And Trey mentioned them briefly as well. Look, the 9 May parade is quite large. It's the victory parade from the victory over the Germans in World War II.\par \par \uc2 And a couple of things happened. Words matter. And I went through the Putin speech and listened to what he said, read it a few times, and said, OK, here's what he said. He said Donbass region five times, never mentioned Ukraine at all, mentioned Kyiv, but didn't mention the country of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And then, what he didn't say, he didn't talk about the mobilization of forces, which he would need to win the war .And here's what -- here's my take on reading between the lines, and I'm probably in a very small minority on this one.\par \par \uc2 I think he realizes he's not going to win this war. And he's trying to look at maybe a way out of this thing, and how can he do it, and kind of say, OK, if I keep the Donbass, that's my idea of success.\par \par \uc2 He's not winning in the Kharkiv region. Look, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported yesterday that Russian forces moving back blew up three bridges. You don't blow up three bridges if you're advancing. You blow up bridges when you're being in retreat and somebody's chasing you.\par \par \uc2 So, maybe what we're looking at is him looking out and saying, there's got to be a way out of this thing, and maybe we can figure a way through it including with the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., because, on last Friday, they voted in the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  to at least look at peaceful ways to celebrate this or to kind of bring this to a conclusion. The Russians did not veto it. That's the first time that's happened.\par \par \uc2 So what I would hope is, maybe we're looking for an interlocutor who can actually get some peace talks going between the Ukrainians and the Russians, because all's what's going to happen is more cities are going to get destroyed, more civilians are going to die, and the Ukrainians are doing exceptionally well.\par \par \uc2 They have got an incredibly charismatic leader in Zelenskyy. They're fighting exceptionally well. They're pushing the Russians back. But I think maybe Putin is thinking, maybe there's an endgame in here, and let's try to figure it out. That's my reading between the lines. I hope I'm right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I like the scenario that you laid out.\par \par \uc2 Some other scenarios that I have read about include that Russia has a history of prolonged warfare, and that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has settled in with the notion that this could take -- and Trey Yingst talked about how long it's -- the fighting is dragging on in the east.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That this could actually be a prolonged, multiyear affair, and then \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would be fine with that.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, but I don't -- here's where I think there's -- that's wrong, Charles, is because we have matched him with artillery and with drones and with heavy systems out there.\par \par \uc2 We're now matching up with what he's what -- he came to the party with, which was a lot of artillery and a lot of missiles. If we do that, it's a zero sum game. He realizes he can't win that fight. Ukraine as a nation will stand.\par \par \uc2 They will -- it will not fall. They have proven that. And I would like to see them at least hold on at the very least Odessa, which gives them access to the Black Sea. So, I think this -- there's maybe a way through this, if we can find somebody that can get these parties talking together, and figure out how to get out of this thing, so we don't see more civilian deaths and keep Ukraine standing as a nation, which it will stand anyway with the way they have been fighting.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What do you make of the -- it's always been curious to me, as someone who's not a military expert, but has heard for years about how powerful and lethal the Russian military is, that it's been this sort of plodding approach, that they never declared war, they have conscripts fighting this war, many generals have died, including a general who didn't -- apparently didn't show up at the Victory Day parade?\par \par \uc2 No one knows where the top guy is right now. What do you make that they never put it in second or third gear?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Well, I don't think they had the capability to do it. It's not in their plan.\par \par \uc2 When I went back and looked at what Gerasimov, who came up with a military plan for Russian modernization, they intended to fight a defensive war, not an offensive war. And they decentralized all of their organizations, what - - you hear the term BTG, battalion tactical groups, so they could never match their forces.\par \par \uc2 They're exceptionally poorly led. They don't have a good command-and- control structure. They have violated about every principle of war that I grew up learning, and they just didn't fight well. And that shocked a lot of people that they couldn't pull it together.\par \par \uc2 And that's reason why they were losing senior officers, not only generals, but regimental commanders, because those are the ones who had to fight forward, because they didn't have that junior leadership, like we have in the American military, to fight through problems.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, General, to your point, it's good that Zelenskyy didn't blink and didn't follow what a lot of Western leaders told him to do at the very beginning. He said, don't give me a ride. I'm staying and we're going to fight.\par \par \uc2 Always appreciate it, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  delivering his annual Victory Day speech under very different circumstances, something Jennifer Griffin asked about during the Pentagon briefing.\par \par \uc2 And she joins us now with the very latest -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Charles, it was not a mission accomplished speech. Putin did not use it to declare all- out war in Ukraine or to call up more conscripts, instead, a subdued alternate reality presented by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , suggesting the war will grind on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): You're fighting for the homeland, for its future, so that no one forgets the lessons of World War II, so that there is no place in the world for executioners, punishers, and Nazis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Of note, during the parade on Red Square, despite the clear skies, there was no air component to the parade.\par \par \uc2 The Russian state television said the Russian warplanes did not participate due to bad weather. More likely it is because most are bogged down in Ukraine, some even shot down.\par \par \uc2 I asked Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby for his reaction to Putin's speech on Red Square.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Then what's your reaction to the words he chose and what he said?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We still heard some of the same bluster, some of the same falsehoods.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine posed no threat, not only no threat to Russia, but no threat to anybody else. So, it wasn't justified. And he talked about the -- ridding the region or of Ukraine of Nazis.\par \par \uc2 You know who's in Ukraine? Ukrainians, not Nazis. I mean, it was just a ridiculous claim.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Ukrainian human rights officials said today that 1.2 million Ukrainians have been forcibly moved to Russia,