stances, it's been nice knowing yes."\par \par \uc2 So, never a dull moment with Elon -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I saw that tweet. But, also, he had an interesting person tweeted back at him. His mom, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: And on Mother's Day too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: See, I think if -- my mom has passed away. But if I had tweeted something like that, she probably would have tweeted, called and did everything else.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So the stock was down. The stock was down 3.7 percent, but Nasdaq was down 4.3 percent. So, so far, that's kind of par for the course. Are you hearing anything else, any scuttlebutt about the deal getting done still?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Yes, so there is suggestions. One researcher was saying, well, I mean, he could move for this to actually be repriced at a lower share price.\par \par \uc2 But what I'm hearing right now is, Wall Street is saying this deal is definitely still getting done. I mean, he's raised so much money from equity investors, debt. I mean, he really wants to own this asset.\par \par \uc2 So, questionable if he could try and get a lower price. That'd be great for him.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: But definitely still seems like this is getting done.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board blinked, didn't they?\par \par \uc2 Kelly, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, forget COVID. The main reason workers are not returning to New York City is crime. The top business leader behind an alarming new study here -- is here, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the latest on the Alabama manhunt. Are officials closing in?\par \par \uc2 And then fierce protests after a Supreme Court draft gets out. Is the White House doing enough?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: A new clue in the manhunt for a murder suspect and a corrections officer charged with helping him escape.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  office now investigating an abandoned vehicle in Evansville, Indiana, believe to be connected to the pair.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Charles Watson is in Florence, Alabama, with the very -- latest.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hey, good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 New development developments this hour. We're learning of a possible Casey White sighting in Evansville, Indiana. Take a look at this photo provided to us by a law enforcement source. It was apparently taken at an Evansville, Indiana, car wash. You see here a large man whose faces partially obscured by a hat.\par \par \uc2 However, if you take a look at the tattoo on his right forearm, it is similar to a tattoo seen in previous photos of Casey White. The U.S. Marshals Service did in fact confirm to FOX News that it is in Evansville looking at a dark blue \uc2 Ford\uc2  F-150 at a car wash.\par \par \uc2 But, at this point, they say they have not found anything inside of that vehicle and they do not have Casey or Vicky White in custody. However, local news outlets in Evansville are reporting the pickup truck does match the description of a vehicle that was stolen in Tennessee, where Vicky and Casey White allegedly abandoned their original getaway vehicle.\par \par \uc2 And speaking up that particular vehicle, the 2007 copper Ford Edge, it is now in the custody of authorities here in Lauderdale County, Alabama. You can see investigators here in this video looking through the windows of the 2007 Ford Edge, taking photos of the partially spray-painted rear end, the front headlights, and the VIN number.\par \par \uc2 We're told the SUV is now at a warehouse, where investigators are combing through it, in hopes of finding some clues that could lead them to the fugitives.\par \par \uc2 And we're also learning this hour, Charles, that Vicky White is facing new charges here in Alabama, second-degree forgery and identity -- identity theft in connection to the purchase of that 2007 Ford Edge. Again, the U.S. Marshals Service is up in Evansville, Indiana, investigating possible leads a possible sighting of Casey White, but, again, Casey and Vicky White not in police custody at this moment, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, as crime surges in New York City, a top business group is warning the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  could see a big long term decline in workers returning to the office.\par \par \uc2 President and CEO of Partnership for New York City \uc2 Kathryn Wylde\uc2  just releasing a new survey on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Kathryn, what are you learning?\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN WYLDE\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, PARTNERSHIP FOR NYC: Well, the first thing we're learning is that the return to office is much slower than anyone had hoped. And that's really retarding our economic recovery, particularly in our Manhattan's Central business districts.\par \par \uc2 And what we're seeing as a big factor in the return to office or big discouragement factor is that people are concerned about safety, about the number of homeless, mentally ill people, whether it's on mass transit or in the streets of the city. So, public safety, not the COVID, not the virus, has become the big issue.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's so interesting you say that, because Politico had a headline just a few days ago, say Democrats clashed over return to office NYC.\par \par \uc2 But they blame COVID. And they blame the people being -- wanting to work in their pajamas. Is the political class listening? I mean, you have come up with this evidence. Business owners are saying the same thing. And if they don't listen, obviously, folks are going to keep leaving.\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: Well, I think that, yes, people are encouraged that the mayor and the governor now have put a major effort on putting more police onto the subways and in the stations.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that means that the folks who are the problem come up to the street level and have to be dealt with there. So there is a big effort to try and deal with the issues. So far, we're not seeing the results. It's going to take some time.\par \par \uc2 We have today about 38 percent of the office workers are back in the office on the average weekday, which is up from where it was a year ago.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: But it's still far less.\par \par \uc2 We had a million people a day coming into Manhattan offices, 80 percent of them riding the subway in 2019. Today, we have only on -- in terms of everyday in the office. It's 8 percent of the office workers who are five days a week.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, and you...\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: So, this is a big change.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. And you say it's going to take time.\par \par \uc2 But here's the problem. We heard anecdotally recently that Broadway ticket sales are down. And they are blaming, in large part, crime. I mean, that's -- Times Square is a big area that attracts a lot of criminal element. You have got small businesses that just can't last that long.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people aren't coming there. And they're not going to the delis for lunch break. It seems very dire to me, a race against the clock.\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: Well, I think that, long term, New York is going to be back as good as ever.\par \par \uc2 But it -- in the meantime, the big challenge is, we have got to deal with these immediate problems, some of which, a large part of which is the result of kind of the stress, anxiety and all the issues associated with the COVID. We have kind of lost a sense of order in the Manhattan business districts.\par \par \uc2 And it's a -- also a catch-22, because, when there aren't a lot of people around, you find a lot more trouble happens. There's a lot more drug use and harassment, bantering with panhandlers.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: So we have got all that going on.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, people already have enough issues in their life because the last two years of pandemic. They don't need to put up with this. So that's really discouraging a return. Again, this is top priority for our new mayor. This is a top priority for our new governor. They are on the case.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Let's hope so.\par \par \uc2 WYLDE: But there's a lot more that needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I was watching Channel 5 the other day, and they had a pop question. How responsible is the mayor for all the crime in the city? And he got like 95 percent.\par \par \uc2 So I hope Gracie Hall is getting the message, because, again, I don't think the city -- it'll come back, but it will just be rich people. And I think a lot of folks who are barely making it can't last that much longer.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for your great work.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, I want to go -- talking about the fallout after the Supreme Court, that draft got out, this after a pro-life group in Wisconsin is attacked and protesters visit some justices' homes. And what is the White House saying about this now?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The protests are not over.\par \par \uc2 Another demonstration expected in a few hours, this outside the home of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . This video was taken just this weekend outside the homes of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What's the White House saying in response?\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Alexandria Hoff has the very latest from the White House -- Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 This has become a whole lot more serious since the -- an attack took place Sunday morning at a pro-abortion -- at a pro-life organization in Wisconsin. So the White House says they condemn that attack. And I wanted to know if the president's condemnation should now go any further. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Does the president plan to condemn the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion or the doxxing of justices, now that we have seen violence unfold?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I would say that we have been clear and the president's position has long been that we should not see protests that takes the form of violence, that takes the form of vandalism, and that threatens anyone.\par \par \uc2 That has long been his position for his entire career and continues to be his position.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Well, around 6:00 a.m. yesterday, the president of the pro-life group Wisconsin Family Action reported to police that someone had thrown a Molotov cocktail into the office. That left the interior charred and the outside was tagged with the phrase, "If abortions aren't safe, then you aren't either." Madison police still searching for a suspect there.\par \par \uc2 As far as the Supreme Court's justices are concerned, last night, a bipartisan bill was introduced aiming to expand security for justices and their families.\par \par \uc2 Of course, another protest, as you mentioned, is planned for Justice Alito's home tonight. Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  offered this on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): ... should be threatening violence against a Supreme Court justice or against any member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 I will be honest, I have had plenty of protests right around the corner from my house over the course of my time in public service. But any threats of violence are beyond the pale.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Now, in a letter sent to Democratic colleagues today, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  applauded the protests so far, saying this -- quote -- "We have been moved by how so many have channeled their righteous anger into meaningful action" -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Alexandria, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So what to make of all this?\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 , who sits on the Judiciary Committee -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SCALIA, SON OF ANTONIN SCALIA: It's a shame that people think that this is a way to try to convince justices.\par \par \uc2 And I think that -- I don't think that it will work on the justices. I think protests like that, if anything, tend to galvanize people in the opinions they have, if they feel like they're being abused by the other side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The son of late Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , Chris Scalia, telling Neil on "Cavuto Live" over the weekend protests outside justices' homes just aren't the answer.\par \par \uc2 My next guest sits on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . And I want to get what he makes of it all, North Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, it's just sort of the -- we heard \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  a moment ago. It was different than what we heard last week, which sounded like a green light from the White House to proceed with these types of protests.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): Yes, we heard it from Speaker Pelosi as well.\par \par \uc2 I think it's inappropriate, it's unsafe, and it's inappropriate for the Supreme Court. Look, I have had multiple protests at my house. I have had them trespass my property. I'm an elected official. The justices of the Supreme Court should be treated differently. They should be treated with respect.\par \par \uc2 And I do agree that I don't believe it changes their opinion, and it may galvanize their decisions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You mentioned Pelosi. And we just heard were Pelosi called it -- suggested that there's a righteous anger, and that that should be fuel for these sorts of protests.\par \par \uc2 Does that -- are you worried that that's pushing the envelope a little bit?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I do.\par \par \uc2 I think it's completely inappropriate. This is somebody who witnessed what happened on January the 6th. Was that righteous anger? I mean, this makes no sense whatsoever. The actions that they're taking are completely counter to the democratic process.\par \par \uc2 If they want to get a policy through, run for office or vote for the people who support you. Hold us accountable. But stay away from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It seems like maybe not only it will galvanize the Supreme Court, but Washington, D.C. This vote that was just mentioned, it seemed like a bipartisan vote to step up protections for \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Yes, we have to step up protection, because now I -- this is laying, I think, the groundwork for saying this, no matter who -- no matter what ideology controls the majority of the Supreme Court, hopefully, that won't happen.\par \par \uc2 But I do see -- it's called political physics. For every action, there's an equal and opposite reaction. This could set a completely different tone going forward, and we have to protect the justices.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, let's switch from political physics to economic physics, if we can...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... because, tomorrow, we're going to hear from President Biden. He's going to lay out his plan to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 And he's set -- he's set to contrast it against the Republican plan. What do you make of it? Because, from what I have heard, what we have read, it feels like more of the same, a lot of spinning and a lot of blame game.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, that's right.\par \par \uc2 Look, the president is -- they're lauding the jobs reports. They're casting their attention away from the fact that so many people have left the work force, that eight million jobs are open, that our productivity is suffering as a result. Inflation is going through the roof.\par \par \uc2 All the gains and wage increases that we have seen over the past 18 months have been eclipsed by spiraling inflation. And now we're headed, I believe, in the direction of a recession. And it's going to hurt hardworking people in North Carolina and across the nation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The worst first-quarter productivity number since 1947.\par \par \uc2 We drew down the most credit since we have since December of 2010. We had first-quarter GDP went down. And from the jobs report, the household survey had 350,000 jobs lost. This seems like an emergency, an economic emergency. Is there going to be a point where the White House accepts a midterm shellacking, or could there actually be some bipartisan effort to make it better?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Look, anything that we could do to get people back to work, to ensure -- to provide some certainty.\par \par \uc2 The biggest problem that we have with this administration is, they're rudderless in terms of economic policy. No responsible business is going to put a major capital expenditure in play right now, not knowing what the regulatory and tax environment looks like.\par \par \uc2 You have people in Congress calling for taxes, calling for more regulations, calling for the very things that prevent productivity from improving.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, apparently, President Biden is going to use a plan that was drafted by Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , and essentially say Republicans want to raise taxes.\par \par \uc2 What is the official Republican line right now? What are Republicans saying, hey, if you vote us in, this is what we would do different?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Well, wait, we voted -- the Jobs and Tax Cuts Act that we pass is one of the primary reasons we have done as well as we have against the headwinds of COVID.\par \par \uc2 This is not the time to raise taxes. This is the time to lower taxes, recognize that working families are suffering, but the job creators also need relief, particularly all the regulatory burdens and uncertainty in this administration.\par \par \uc2 I serve on the Banking Committee, Charles. Every single week, we have a hearing talking about more regulations, more challenges, and ultimately costs that go down to the consumer. I don't believe that the Biden administration has the wherewithal to step back and say, look, we're not going to get the overreach that we ask for.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to get the tax increases that we ask for, but work with reasonable-minded members who will do anything we can to get people back to work.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 You know, I just read where the loan-to-deposit ratio is the lowest in history. We need to get America working like it used to.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you so much. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Folks, Neil will be back tomorrow, but you can catch me 2:00 p.m. every day on FBN, "Making Money." Believe me, you want to check out the show every day this week.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, "THE FIVE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC); Interview with Sen. \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  (D-CT); Investigation into the Leak of the Draft Opinion; Midterm Race in West Virginia for the House; Jill Biden Visits Ukraine; Trump- Backed Candidates in Midterms; Tim Ryan Skips Biden Event\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Lucas Tomlinson, Trey Yingst, Juan Williams, Hillary  Vaughn \par \uc2 9,450 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 An unprecedented Supreme Court leak ricochets through Washington, sparking questions about motive and protests across the nation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The high court looking poised to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Today, questions about who would leak the draft opinion, and calls for protests at conservative justices' homes and Catholic churches, which the White House refuses to condemn.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We certainly encourage people to keep it peaceful and not resort to any level of violence.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll ask Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  about the investigation, the fate of Roe, and the political fallout.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): This is about a woman's right to choose fully. We are not looking to compromise something as vital as this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats push ahead a bill that would legalize abortion nationwide, but they don't have the votes. We'll discuss with Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: It feels even better than I thought it would.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Trump's pick for Ohio Senate surges to win the Republican primary with all eyes now on Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: And the rest of the world is watching us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll bring in our Sunday panel on the primary fights ahead.\par \par \uc2 And we're on the road to the midterms in West Virginia, with an inside look at a deep red battle in the Mountain State.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Someone has to want to work for West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 This Mother's Day, the nation is newly divided over one of the most controversial issues of our time -- the leak of a draft Supreme Court opinion that would overturn constitutional abortion protections in place for nearly 50 years under Roe vs. Wade. The fallout from the stunning revelation opening up new fronts in this year's midterm battlegrounds, raising the questions of public trust in the high court and sparking a wide investigation into who would leak this, why, and why now.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with South Carolina's \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson at the White House with the latest -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, protests have shifted from outside the Supreme Court into the neighborhoods where some of the conservative justices live.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Pro-choice advocates gathered near the homes of Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , hoping they can change minds.\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATORS: You don't care if people die!\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: But Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  saying, quote: We can't be an institution that can be bullied to giving you just the outcomes you want.\par \par \uc2 This Mother's Day, there are even some calls to protest outside Catholic Churches and Catholic advocacy groups want the attorney general to make it clear protests at churches would violate federal law. The White House refusing to condemn the leak or the cause for protests.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The president would want people's privacy to be respected.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The court pressing ahead with an investigation to how the draft was shared with "Politico."\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers slamming the breach.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): Releasing it was an attack against the independence of our federal judiciary.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And Democrats planning a vote on whether to codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Every senator will be held accountable. Reproductive rights will be on the ballot this November.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The president seizing the moment to criticize the MAGA movement and to take aim at a Republican tax proposal ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me tell you about this agenda. It's extreme, as most MAGA things are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Right now, Biden getting ready to embark on his first trip to Japan, and South Korea's president this later month. Topics will include Russia, China, and North Korea -- Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Word today from the U.S. embassy warning Americans in Russia to avoid annual victory day celebrations there as tensions over the war in Ukraine grow.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst has more live on the ground in Kyiv -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, Americans in Russia are being urged to avoid the annual Victory Day parade and similar celebrations at the country's invasion into Ukraine continues. If the U.S. embassy in Moscow releasing a statement saying, quote, given the ongoing tensions, U.S. citizens should avoid large public gatherings. The warning comes as Ukrainian officials worry Russian President Putin will use victory day to launch a larger mobilization of forces and try to finish capturing the city of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 In that southern port city, Russian troops continue to fire on a steel plant housing Ukrainian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Ukraine's deputy prime ministers that all civilians have been evacuated from the facility. Fighting does continue across the eastern Donbas region, where a war of attrition is developing.\par \par \uc2 Now, around Ukraine's second-largest city of Kharkiv, Ukrainian forces have been able to push back a new Russian advance and here in the capital of Kyiv, we have heard air raid sirens throughout the day as this city remains in Russian sites -- Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst reporting from Kyiv, Ukraine -- Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from South Carolina, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, we'll get to the Supreme Court in just a moment, but first, I want to talk about Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You have these intelligence leaks about targeting Russian generals, sinking a Russian warship.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does that make war likely between the U.S. and Russia? In other words, does that leak in the intelligence targeting equal heavy weapons, planes, in the eyes of Putin? How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, no -- you know, Putin winning is a disaster for the United States. That means he's going to move forward further in Europe. He's trying to rewrite the map of Europe.\par \par \uc2 If Putin wins, then Taiwan is probably going to be invaded by China. But if Putin looses, then I think that's a great day for Europe, a great day for the United States.\par \par \uc2 We need to triple down on our willingness to help the Ukraine. We need to pass a $33 billion supplemental package, $20 billion for weapons. We need to work with \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  to gather evidence to prosecute Putin personally.\par \par \uc2 We need a resolution that I have with Senator Blumenthal, a Democrat, to designate Russia as a state sponsor of terrorism. We need to pour on, put more weapons in theater that can strike the Russian military offensively.\par \par \uc2 So I want to triple down on beating Putin. Burns said today, the CIA director, that Putin would double down because he can't lose in Ukraine. Well, I want to triple down because we can't let him win in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Senator, Tom Friedman with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " writes this today.\par \par \uc2 Quote: We need to stick as tightly as possible to our original limited and clearly defined aim of helping Ukraine expel Russian forces as much as possible, or negotiate further withdrawal whenever Ukraine's leaders feel the time is right. Boasting about killing his generals and sinking his ships or falling in love with Ukraine in ways that will get us enmeshed there forever is the height of folly.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I couldn't disagree more. People like that said that we could deter Russia through sanctions. Effort to deter Russia through rhetoric and talking tough and acting weak didn't work.\par \par \uc2 We are in a fight for the future of Europe. \uc2 NATO\uc2  needs to get bigger, not smaller. If Finland and Sweden join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Europe breaks away from dependence on Russian oil and gas and Putin is declared a war criminal, it'd be the best outcome for the world, writ large. China would be less likely to go into Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 So I disagree. I think we should do everything within our power. I'm not asking for troops on the ground, but to make sure that Ukraine can expel Russia and that Putin is a war criminal in the eyes of the world and he's prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Putin must go. I like Tom Friedman, but if you don't understand this, that if Putin is still standing after all this, then the world is going to be a very dark place. China is going to get the wrong signal, and we'll have a mess on our hands in Europe for decades to come.\par \par \uc2 So let's take out Putin by helping Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. So you're saying that that's how this ends, that Putin is removed? Is there any way --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: There's no off-ramp.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's no off-ramp?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: No off-ramp.\par \par \uc2 So, let me tell you why there's no off-ramp. The Ukrainians are not going to give the east to Putin to stop the war. They're going to fight for every inch of their territory.\par \par \uc2 If we push the Ukrainians to give up half the country, then Putin wins. If we back off prosecuting Putin as a war criminal, all the laws on the books become a joke.\par \par \uc2 If we don't get this right, China will certainly invade Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 There is no off-ramp in this war. Somebody is going to win and somebody is going to lose. And I hope and pray and do everything in my power to make sure Ukraine wins and Putin is charged of a state -- state sponsor of terrorism, that designation needs to be given to Russia. He's earned that designation.\par \par \uc2 We need to pour it on when it comes to helping Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing -- at what point do you think Putin is going to move on Moldova or Poland or some other way that draws the West in? Do you think that's inevitable as he gets more desperate?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, Moldova is not part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but I do believe he could use chemical weapons because the people in Mariupol are fighting like tigers. If he uses chemical weapons to subdue the east, that should lead to a no- fly zone by \uc2 NATO\uc2  to restore the international order. If he explodes a nuclear weapon inside of Ukraine to break our will, then to me that would be an attack on \uc2 NATO\uc2  because the radiation would go all over Europe.\par \par \uc2 I could see him getting more desperate, because there is no off-ramp for him.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I think will happen: If we stick with Ukraine, they're not going to give up. Over time, the Russian people will turn on Putin. This war is a disaster.\par \par \uc2 You'll see a parade Monday, but the parade doesn't reflect the Russian military. You see the Russian military getting there ass handed to them on the battlefield in Ukraine. We can win this war on behalf of Ukraine if we help them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Let's turn to the Supreme Court. Many experts have called this leak of a draft opinion a dangerous precedent. How do you see it, and what's the potential political fallout for Republicans, frankly?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, for the court, it's a betrayal of trust. Chief Justice Roberts said it better than I could ever say it. It's a betrayal of trust.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine going to work the next day and looking around at your colleagues, who did this to the court? What will happen if the opinion sticks?\par \par \uc2 And here's what's important. At least in February, there were five votes that led to this draft opinion to repeal Roe v. Wade. I think Roe v. Wade created a constitutional right that doesn't exist in the written Constitution. It's created division from the first day it was decided until now. If it does get repealed, which I hope it will, the issue goes back to the states.\par \par \uc2 But Congress will still stay involved. My Democratic friends are trying to pass a law Wednesday that will allow abortion right up to the day before the baby is delivered. Different states will take different approaches.\par \par \uc2 So the abortion debate will not go away in the country. It will be decided by the people, not a handful of judges.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Will abortion now galvanize -- will abortion now galvanize the left as it has for the right politically? Do you fear that heading into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Single-issue voters seldom decide the outcome of an election. Enthusiasm is important, but here's what I would say: elected officials elected by the people now have a say about abortion, first time since 1973. That process will move forward.\par \par \uc2 I don't believe it's going to change the outcome of the 2022 elections at all. When you go to the grocery store and when you go to a gas station, that's going to remind you the incompetency of the Biden administration. When you look at the southern border, this is not going to take people's eyes off of rocket inflation, broken border, insecure America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just on the substance of the ruling. If it turns out to be what this draft opinion suggests, some senators are now openly questioning whether they were misled in the confirmation hearings.\par \par \uc2 Here is Justice Kavanaugh, and you did some of the questioning, in his confirmation hearing. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , THEN-SUPREME COURT JUSTICE NOMINEE: It's settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court entitled the respect under principles of stare decisis. And one of the important things to keep in mind about Roe v. Wade is that it has been reaffirmed many times.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And this is from Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  this week: If this leaked draft opinion is the final decision and this reporting is accurate, it would be completely inconsistent with what Justice Gorsuch and Justice Kavanaugh said in their hearings and in our meetings in my office.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, you cut off my questioning because I asked Judge Kavanaugh, would you consider challenges to Roe v. Wade? Would you listen to both sides? You would apply the doctrine of stare decisis, but would you shut the party trying to overturn the decision out?\par \par \uc2 This has not been well-accepted by the public. The public has been divided since 1973. Ask your next guest, who's a good friend, does he want the court to reconsider \uc2 Citizens\uc2  versus United regarding campaign finance laws? Would he welcome a revisiting of the Heller decision where the court said the Second Amendment was a personal right?\par \par \uc2 So my liberal friends don't mind the court overturning decisions they don't like. They very much are against overturning decisions that they agree with.\par \par \uc2 So you can't bargain your way into getting onto the court. So any senator who tries to bargain with a nominee, "will you uphold the case I like or overturn the ones I don't" is really doing a disservice to the court.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about -- again, going back to the politics, but Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  saying that it is possible that a federal ban on abortions could happen.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, Wednesday, we're going to take a vote by Senator Schumer, who is insisting we vote, to legalize abortion to the day before birth. That would become the law of the land, but they don't have the votes for it.\par \par \uc2 I have a bill that would outlaw abortion at 20 weeks. We're one of seven nations in the world that allow abortion on demand at 20 weeks, the fifth month in pregnancy. So Congress will continue to debate this issue, the states will finally have control over this.\par \par \uc2 And here's the one common thing: if you don't like the outcome of the abortion debate, now you can kick people out of office who actually vote. Before, you were shut out. You had no avenue.\par \par \uc2 Five judges, six judges, seven judges determined the -- when life begins and how it ends, and I think that was wrong from the start.\par \par \uc2 Now, finally, elected officials have a say about life and the conditions of an abortion. I think that's the way it should be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Graham, thank you. Always good to talk with you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, growing protest across the country outside the court and also outside the homes of conservative justices. We'll talk with Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  about the movement and what the draft ruling means for Democrats in November.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Within days, we could see a procedural vote in the Senate on a bill that would codify Roe versus Wade, or more plainly speaking, attempting to pass a federal law guaranteeing the right to obtain an abortion in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 The plan is in effect an attempt to send the political message ahead of the midterms because Senate Democrats like the votes.\par \par \uc2 Joining us here in Washington, Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First, I want to talk about this draft opinion. The White House hasn't really condemned the leak. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I don't like the way that this decision came out, but the outrage here should be about what's going to happen to women and families in this country, the fact that women and doctors are going to be sent to jail immediately when this opinion becomes a reality, if in the states with trigger laws, all of a sudden, abortion under any circumstances -- even under rape and incest becomes illegal and criminalized.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't love how the decision ultimately was made public, and I hope we figure out what happens, but I think it's interesting that my Republican friends are putting their focus on the process here because they really don't want to talk about the fact that abortion becoming criminalized is something that a sort of small minority of the country supports. Two-thirds of this country is going to oppose an effort by the Supreme Court to fundamentally change the scope of women's health care access in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So -- and just to get process out of the way, are you concerned about the protest outside the homes of justices and what's happening with that? There are some websites that list all of their addresses. There are some tweets calling for assassinations.\par \par \uc2 Should this president be speaking out about that?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, no one should be threatening violence against a Supreme Court justice or against any member of Congress. I will be honest, I've had plenty of protests right around the corner from my house, over the course of my time in public service.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, any threats of violence are beyond the pale.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. The Senate is set for this procedural vote this week on this bill that's failed once before to codify Roe. But several GOP senators -- and you just heard Senator Graham talk about it, narrower compromise. Why rule that out?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, it was not open to any potential pathway to codify Roe versus Wade, to get our nation's laws to where 67 percent of the American public are. And I think it's important that we put senators on record, because let's be clear, Lindsey says this is about the decisions being made at the state level.\par \par \uc2 That's not what's going to happen here. If Republicans get control of the House and the Senate and the White House, which certainly could happen in the next three years, they are absolutely going to pass a national ban on abortion that will affect every single family, every single woman in this country.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is important right now that we get every senator on the record.\par \par \uc2 Do you support a woman's right to make decisions over their own body, or do you think that the government should be in charge? Because this is soon not going to be about state legislature. It's -- pretty soon, this is going to be an effort to pass a federal ban everywhere on abortions in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So how much do you think this ruling, if it stands, affects the midterms for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I think it's going to be a huge turnout push, but not just sort of for Democrats. The big middle of the country, in fact the majority of Republicans in his country oppose the full overturning of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 So, ultimately, I think this is going to push a lot of people to the polls this November that may have otherwise stayed home, because they see that this fight is coming, not just in the state legislatures, but in Washington as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's already having this impact, progressive candidate Jessica Cisneros. She's challenging pro-life Democrat \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  in Texas. And she says Democrats should stop supporting him over this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS (D-TX), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: With the House majority on the line, we have to very much be the deciding vote in the future of productive rights in this country, and we just cannot afford that risk.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You put out a statement this weekend that says: It's time to build upon the movement to elect pro-choice candidates up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, do you kick off pro-life candidates? Should Cuellar leave because he's a pro-life Democrat?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, that's up to the people of Texas. I don't make endorsements in other (ph) states' congressional elections.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, but I mean, your statement did say, it should be pro-choice up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, listen, I -- I support pro-choice candidates. I support pro- choice Democrats. I think we're better off if we have people in Congress that are going to support a woman's right to choose, but that doesn't mean that I'm going around the country endorsing in congressional races in Texas and other places.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democratic candidate for Senate in Ohio, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , and other Democrats also say if this stands, that the Senate filibuster should go away. Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  says: we must end the filibuster to pass it with 50 votes. You said Democrats don't have the votes to end the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 But -- I mean, is this moot because you just don't have them?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yeah, we don't have the votes right now, and frankly, I think the filibuster is fundamentally anti-democratic. I think if you win control of the House, the Senate, and the White House, you probably should have the ability to enact your agenda, frankly, whether the Democratic Party or the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you'd have that same thought in the minority?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I would. And I think it's pretty clear to me that if Republicans get control of the Senate, they will get rid of the filibuster in order to pass a national ban on abortion. They got rid of the filibuster for Supreme Court justices for the explicit purpose of putting on the court judges who would strike down Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think you could naturally assume that they would also change the rules to pass that national abortion ban if they win control of the Senate in this next election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Senator Sanders also says he supports a primary challenge to moderate \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . Do you? Because she's against getting away with -- doing away with the filibuster?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I don't mind having a big tent, right? We can sort of argue these issues inside our party. I don't ever expect that we're going to have every single Democrat in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  supporting the same rule set that I support.\par \par \uc2 I just think we should elect more Democrats. That's how we are going to solve this problem, elect Democrats in this November election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But not pro-life Democrats?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Who are going to support choice and support changing the laws of this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, not that big tent? Pro-choice candidates.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I support pro-choice candidates.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I got it.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: So, that's -- that's my priority.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This week, the president criticized some of the potential policies floated by Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . He called them MAGA, and he talked about the MAGA Movement, Make America Great Again Movement. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history -- in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you agree with that statement and that effort politically? I mean, it has echoes of categorizing a whole bunch of people a certain way.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I mean, I am very concerned about the sort of very quick, hateful, divisive turn that the Republican Party has taken. This effort in Florida to sort of target gay kids in schools, I just think is mean-spirited and something that I had not seen from the Republican Party when I first started out in politics 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm going to interrupt you.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To target gay kids in schools? I mean, the bill is about not talking about sexual identity from K through 3rd grade. That's not targeting gay kids.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, it is, it's absolutely is. It is sending a message to these kids that they are not worthy, that they should be shamed of their identification.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, it says -- it says, Senator, but you don't teach subjects with gender identity.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: You don't think that those kids don't --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Don't the parents have a right --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: You don't think that those kids don't take a message from a -- from a ban on the discussion of their identity suggests that they are not worthy of existence in that school. Half of the kids --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, do you talk to your kindergartner about sexual identity?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Half of kids -- of trans kids in this country have contemplated suicide and that's because of the effort to bully them for their identity by adults in this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: So I absolutely do think that there's a message sent to these kids when you -- when you suggest that a conversation about their identity is somehow threatening the education of kids in our schools.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: With your children, did you talk about sexual identity as a kindergarten -- kindergartener? I mean, you don't talk about the birds and the bees, let alone the birds and the birds at that age. Kindergartener through third grade.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: So, there was an amendment and the Florida legislature to ban sexual education in the early ages. That amendment failed because this wasn't about trying to protect children from a discussion about sex. This was an effort to specifically ban a conversation about topics related to sexual identity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were making a point that you think it's gone too far. I was saying half the country identified at least with that movement, the policies of the make America Great Again Movement, and you think that that's a good, winning thing for the president to be doing politically heading into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Again, I just have seen this turn in the Republican Party where they are not willing to support protections for our democracy. They're willing to install a president in the White House who didn't actually win either the popular or the electoral vote. They seem to be targeting vulnerable kids in a way that they didn't 10 or 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 I do think it's important to sort of talk about this very different element of the Republican Party that exists today that I don't think was the foundation of the party a couple decades ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, student loan forgiveness. This is a big debate. And, you know, you talk about working families. You talk about folks who don't go to college and you're talking about wiping out student debt.\par \par \uc2 There is a former education department lawyer served under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  who wrote: if this issue is litigated, the more persuasive analysis tends to support the conclusion that the executive branch likely does not have the unilateral authority to engage in mass student debt cancellation.\par \par \uc2 Where are you? Does he have the authority and are you for this?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think he has the authority to do it. I would support a more limited forgiveness of debt. I'm one of the few members of the Senate that still has student debt. I don't need my debt forgiven.\par \par \uc2 I also think that this focus on debt excuses the colleges for this dramatic increase in tuition. I sometimes think that our party spends a little bit too much time talking about the debt and not enough time talking about the cost of the degree, because that's where the real problem is. We're going to be in a perpetual cycle of having to forgive debt if college continues to spiral upwards to $100,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 So I think a limited debt forgiveness proposal is legal. I would support it, but I think it's a mistake to put all of our eggs in a basket.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Murphy, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT), SENATE FOREIGN RELATIONS COMMITTEE: Of having to forgive debt if college continues to spiral upwards to $100,000 a year. So, I think a limited debt forgiveness proposal is legal. I would support it. But I think it's a mistake to put all of our eggs in that basket.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Senator Murphy, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group on the Supreme Court leak, the investigation, and how this could further inflame politics of the court.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): This leak at the court is the most egregious breach of trust at the court in the history of our nation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARK WARNER\uc2  (D-VA): I completely condemn the leak. And whoever leaked it, you know, should be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senators on both sides of the aisle condemning the leak of a Supreme Court draft ruling.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" Washington bureau chief Susan Page. Fox News contributor Juan Williams. Former \uc2 RNC\uc2  communications director Doug Heye. And "National Journal's" Josh Kraushaar.\par \par \uc2 Josh, you know, "Politico's" media writer argues that the court will recover from this leak. He sees a silver lining here. Quote, the upside of the leak is grand. The public has gained a new awareness of where a court majority plans to take the nation after a half-century of legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 What's your take?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Well, George Will called it "institutional vandalism" and compared it to the January 6th riots. So that is how much strain the institutions in our country on both left and right are handling it.\par \par \uc2 I think politically speaking, Bret, the bigger impact is going to be on the substance. And you're seeing, you know, Democratic lawmakers starting to get engaged, starting to really run on offense on an issue where they've had a really tough political environment throughout the year.\par \par \uc2 But, look, this is a real blow to the hollowed ground of the Supreme Court that these documents were leaked. I think it matters who leak them. It sounds like the initial instinct that it was someone on the left, but we don't know that. And I think finding out who did it and their political reasons for doing that is also very significant.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan, Republicans say, as it heads into campaigns, it's all about kitchen table issues. They don't see this as the massive issue. However, you, at "USA Today," talked to the Senate minority leader about the possibility at least of a federal ban of all abortions. That's not what would come out of this ruling if it stood. It would go to the states to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": Well, Republicans have been very loathed to talk about the consequences and the next steps that might follow in overturning of Roe v. Wade. But \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  did tell one of my colleagues at "USA Today" that it was possible that they would bring up a federal ban on abortion in the wake of the expected decision from the Supreme Court. And he made news in another way too. He said that he would not carve out an exception to the filibuster in order to pass a federal ban on abortion. But, you know, you hear a lot from other players, Democrats in particular, questioning whether that is the truth, whether, in fact, Republicans would do what Democrats have talked about doing, now unable to do, which is to get rid of the filibuster in order to do what they want to do on the issue of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, I asked Senator Murphy about this effort, this procedural vote that's going to happen. They don't have the votes to lock this in, to codify Roe. Why not take the narrower, moderate legislation that -- from the \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's of the Senate with some kind of, you know, stipulations?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think the key point here is that Senator Graham, Senator Collins, Senator Murkowski, the people who would offer some kind of limited approach, you know, the reality is, these people are desperate at the moment given the political fallout, but also because, you know, you think back, Justice Kavanaugh, Justice Gorsuch, they definitely lied to the Congress, to the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , about as respecting established law, stare decisis. Fifty years -- it's been nearly 50 years of a constitutionally protected right to abortion for women in this country. And I remember Senator Collins got on the floor and hammered Democrats for not respecting what Kavanaugh had said.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there's a lot of fury there and fury to the substance to what Josh was saying, the substance of this, which is that the court is about to take away a constitutionally protect right and turn abortion from a choice into murder. It would be murder in many of these states. One out of every four American women by age of 40 has had an abortion, and you're going to say these people are murderers all of a sudden? I think that's pretty terrible.\par \par \uc2 Right now, in the Fox poll, 63 percent of Americans say leave Roe alone, that includes a quarter of Republicans. So I think that a bill that says, oh, there are going to be loopholes, you can have a Mississippi law, which is at issue in the court, go through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Too many Democrats it seems like bitter -- a bitter pill at this moment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again, the caveat is, we don't know that this is the ruling that will be in the end. This is a draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 But, Doug, clearly some Democrats have hopes that abortion is going to become the issue that propels them, or at least saves some seats in a -- in an environment with recession, inflation, everything else.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST AND DOUGLAS MEDIA FOUNDER: Yes. Look, the conversation Democrats have wanted to avoid for the past six months or even the past year now has been rising costs for not just gasoline but absolutely everything that Americans are paying for, certainly at grocery stores. Rising, violent crime in cities. The situation at the border. This gives them an opportunity to change the topic, but it's Democrats in Washington changing the topics. We'll have to see what races this really plays a role in. Maybe some gubernatorial ones. Pennsylvania potentially. But it doesn't change the dynamic that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is at 41 percent popularity, 39, 43 depending on the poll.\par \par \uc2 When I was at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2010, our magic number for \uc2 Obama\uc2  was 46. We felt if he was at or below that we'd take back the House. Biden is well below that. And this ruling, if it is the ruling, doesn't change the fact that 60 percent or more of the country feel that we're on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan, is there chance, though, that because this becomes the center focus that there is an overshoot here and that people in the middle of the country say this is not something that drives me?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You bet. And the more Republicans talk about their -- what -- what will happen after this decision comes down, if it's this decision, what legislation would follow, in half of the states in our country, that is bad news for Republicans. That is a conversation that Republicans do not want to have because of the risk that it counters concern about things like inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And a conversation that people don't want to have, a lot of moderate Democrats is, late-term abortion restrictions. I asked \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  about it in Ohio. He's not worked out an answer yet.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: There is a lot of nuances on the abortion public opinion. You know, I think Democrats run a risk if they don't favor any restrictions, any regulation at all, especially in a Republican leaning state like -- like Ohio. But the challenge for Republicans is -- Douglas was talking about Pennsylvania. Wisconsin's another state where you have big governors races, were if you elect a Republican with a Republican legislature, they could significantly regulate abortion or even ban it. And that is also outside of where the public is in those swing states.\par \par \uc2 So, both sides have vulnerabilities. They -- they're eager to play to their bases, public opinion on abortion much more to the middle.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think those governors races, to pick up again, what you're hearing from the political strategists on the left, the Democrats, is that in focus groups they -- those issues really matter. We don't know how it plays out in some of these Senate races.\par \par \uc2 And then, secondly, in terms of, you know, the argument in this leak was about enumerated rights. Oh, there's no explicit right to abortion in the Constitution. That, then, on the left, the people are saying, well, no explicit right to gay rights, to contraception, to interracial marriage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I wanted to -- one -- one quick comment. When \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says there's no off-ramp for Putin, he's, obviously, coming from a point of view. It's -- is it shared by a lot of Republicans, do you think?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: It's a growing sense among Republicans in the House and the Senate that there could be no off ramp for Putin.\par \par \uc2 Look, if you're a Republican, tough rhetoric on China and tough rhetoric on Russia are always very safe places to be. So we'll hear more Republicans say things like this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will be interesting.\par \par \uc2 Panel, we have to take a break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're on the ground in West Virginia, where it is Republican versus Republican in a deep red district. Fox's Hillary Vaughn is on the road to the midterms as the campaigns pitch themselves to voters with just days to go.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: FOX NEWS SUNDAY is on the road to the midterms and following an interesting matchup in West Virginia. The state is losing one of its House seats this year due to redistricting. That occurs every ten years based off of census population figures. Under the old map that you see here, there were three House districts represented by three Republican lawmakers. However, under the new map, there are just two districts, leaving two current incumbents to battle over the same seat.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn traveled to the mountain state for an up-close look at this unusual race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 David McKinley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DAVID MCKINLEY\uc2  (R-WV): Yes, let's just make this thing happen here for them.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And Alex Mooney.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEX MOONEY (R-WV): This would have been a good place for my victory party.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Collects on Capitol Hill, but rivals back home.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: We've got two incumbents running against each other.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): Right.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Which is always uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (voice over): Redistricting changes lumped their two districts together. Now they have to face off over one House seat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You will kick his butt. You have to.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: McKinley says he's the only one born and raised in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: That was the sight of my great-great-grandfather's house.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And says Mooney bought a house in the state just to win a House seat.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Can I count on a vote?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Mooney pitches himself as the only true conservative running. He says McKinley sold out, voting for the bipartisan infrastructure bill that landed President Biden a huge legislative win.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: He should be ashamed of himself voting for the bill. We could have killed that bill, come back and passed something we could afford and taken care of the roads and bridges at the same time. Not either or.\par \par \uc2 But he's got to convince Republicans that was a good vote. Good luck, man.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Both candidates have been campaigning with heavy hitters in the GOP.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Alex Mooney! Alex is in a big race.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Mooney campaigning with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Thank you and God bless.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Who was fond of McKinley four years ago.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Great job, David. We like David, right? I like David.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Today, it's Mooney who has the official thumbs up.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: And I never got a chance to talk to Donald, even ask for it. So, I'm OK. I'm OK with that. I got the endorsement of all West Virginians. And that, I think, is more important.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Instead, McKinley is at the trail with Trump's former secretary of state, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , and West Virginia's governor, Jim Justice.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JIM JUSTICE (R-WV): He's the only person I've ever endorsed. I don't get into politics. But in this situation, we can't afford to lose him.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: At the governor's beloved bulldog --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Hello\uc2  (ph), baby.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Is on board too.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE: Baby Dog says right now, vote, vote for David!\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: I got a lick -- I got a lick earlier today.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But it's an endorsement from Democratic Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  that is giving some people pause.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (R-WV): \uc2 David McKinley\uc2  is all about us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: McKinley is asking like he's a rhino, and he -- does his politcking like a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Well, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  endorsed him. Doesn't that change your mind?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That makes a lot of sense.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): Are you a rhino?\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: I'm no more rhino than he is. I have a better voting record with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (voice over): In the final stretch to Election Day, they're both campaigning in their own way. Mooney, on foot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No babies to kiss, but puppies.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: No babies to kiss, just puppies.\par \par \uc2 One thing I'd say, all my door knocking, I've never been bit.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Even going house to house in McKinley's hometown.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've had enough of your opponent.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Anything in particular about him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, he does -- he does things that I don't really care about.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Gotcha. Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you know.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: What about when he voted for the $1.2 trillion Biden non- infrastructure bill?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nyes, that's one of -- that's what really got me the most.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: That's one of them?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: McKinley has a different strategy.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: I'm not going to play politician with you.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: He's focusing on the job he's done and what he's delivered for West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There you go, sir.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And even though his infrastructure vote is getting blowback, he doesn't regret it one bit.\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: The condition of our infrastructure in West Virginia, it's not rhetoric. This is -- we're rated Ds and Fs. For someone to politicize this, that's wrong. This is West Virginia. My vote was for West Virginia, and I'll stand pat on that.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And Mooney isn't ashamed to be West Virginian by choice.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): So why West Virginia? When you first decided to move here, what was -- what was the reasoning? Why did you pick --\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Why am I a West Virginians by choice? I love this state. There's lots of good reasons to live here.\par \par \uc2 West Virginia voters voted me in office to do a job, and I'm running on the job that I've done.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (voice over): Both candidates know who wins Tuesday is almost guaranteed to win the seat in November. They are leaving voters with a last word.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Yes, ma'am, Trump did endorse me.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): What's the one thing you want voters to know about you?\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Well, I guess I'd like to say that President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed me for a reason. That I am a true conservative fighter.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: What is the one thing that you want them to know about you?\par \par \uc2 MCKINLEY: I care about them. I care about them. In my legislation, everything I'm doing, is to help them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hillary Vaughn reporting from West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 This is a "Fox News Alert."\par \par \uc2 Just getting word that the first lady of the United States, Jill Biden, has traveled to Ukraine, being inside the country for about two hours along the border with Slovakia. She met with the first lady of Ukraine, Olena Zelenskyy (ph). And this is, obviously, the first trip for the first lady. It comes just before the big celebration in Russia for their military parade tomorrow -- or rather Monday.\par \par \uc2 We're back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Susan, seems like a big movement. It's not the president going, but it does send a message.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It's a big statement by the Biden administration to send the first lady to the area to have her go over the border into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You know, we've seen a gradual escalation of the Biden administration's willingness to support the effort in Ukraine, to send heavier weapons, to offer more rhetoricals for it because the Ukrainians have delivered in this war.\par \par \uc2 Who would have thought at the beginning of this war that we would get to victory day in Moscow, and Moscow would have no victory to crow about. You know, if -- the Biden administration does not think it's realistic to think about ousting Putin, as \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said to you earlier in this show, but they do think that it is possible to weaken Putin, and that has already happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We just saw pictures of the two first ladies meeting, Juan. Significant move? The president doesn't look like he's going, even though President Zelenskyy has called for him to come to Ukraine today.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  also on the ground in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think these are statements from the west, not only in support of Ukraine, but in defiance of Russia, because there's a risk. The risk is one of these people gets hurt or injured, and that potentially would go beyond escalation. I think we'd be then in World War III.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea that they feel confident and sufficiently comfortable making this step I think is a real strong statement, you know, given what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said to you earlier, Bret, I think that you're seeing now it's both parties and I think across the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance and beyond, Finland and others now saying, this is our stand. This is the world saying it's not good if Russia wins.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But this is the tough part, Josh, and that is, what is the ultimate goal for us? Is it to get Russia out of Ukraine, or is it to get Russia out of Ukraine not including the Dunbar's and Crimea? Where is the line and do we draw it?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, the stakes have become much higher. And this is -- Jill Biden's visit is a sign of confidence I think from the White House that Ukraine can actually win the war. But what happens if there's a stalemate? What happens if this goes on for months if not years? Do we have the wherewithal, the patient's, the bandwidth to commit to, you know, continue to offer military weapons, money, everything else to Ukraine for this effort? I think that's an open question, and we're putting all our chips on the table. It's going to be interesting to see where this goes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Usually foreign policy doesn't play in midterm elections. This may be different depending on where we are come November.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, it's been an emerging bright spot for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who has so much trouble when it comes to assessments of his handling of the economy, of crime, of the border and immigration.\par \par \uc2 I think that the Ukrainians have captured the imagination of the American people by doing something -- you know, it's kind of like it's like a weekend for underdogs, right? In the Kentucky Derby and in Ukraine, the underdogs are doing better than anybody expected, even to the point that we can talk about envisioning a Ukrainian victory over the Russian military? That would be extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, turn to politics. Speaking of underdogs, a lot of the former president 's endorsements are underdogs in primaries coming ahead. We just saw the West Virginia race. We've got the Pennsylvania Senate. Idaho's governor's race. The Georgia governor race.\par \par \uc2 Talk about the stakes here. Obviously you had a big win with J.D. Vance supporting him in Ohio, but what about this stretch coming up?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: May's a very important month for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He has a lot of races where he's made endorsements. And what we saw in Ohio with J.D. Vance is, yes, he won the primary, but he only got a third of the vote. So you had basically two-thirds of Ohio Republicans saying that they didn't support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's person.\par \par \uc2 Now, granted, most of those candidates were running to be as pro-Trumpy as they could be. One of the races I'm looking at that I think is really interesting is in North Carolina. Madison Cawthorn, endorsed by Trump, has a very tough primary on his hands. We talked about support for Zelenskyy in Ukraine earlier. And he made news from saying that Zelenskyy was a thug and it seems every day there's another story about paying your family some corruption, some videos that we probably don't want to look at or talk about. He has a very real race in North Carolina. It's one to watch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. And speaking back to Ohio, I mention \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Had him on the show. One of the things that I ask him was whether he wanted President Biden to campaign for him. I asked it kind of three different ways, but take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I want to be the face and the voice of his campaign at I don't want any distractions. I don't need anybody to prop me up, like J.D. Vance did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean that's the sitting president of the United States here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I don't think he needs to announce in May whether or not he needs Biden coming in. You know, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is a very good candidate for Ohio and, you know, to me he wants this to be a local Ohio contest. He wants this to be Tim versus J.D. And if it's nationalized, then you get a whole different set of dynamics, including the drag potentially from President Trump's low numbers.\par \par \uc2 But I think if you, you know, look at it, you'd have to say, he may want Biden later in the race when he needs to pump up voter turnout in Democratic strongholds like Cleveland or raise money, the president has a real opportunity. But, keep in mind, they have a lot in common. One guy's from Youngstown. Another guy's from Scranton. They're both Catholics, both moderates on the abortion issue we've been discussing. So, I -- there's a lot of commonality and opportunity there as we go down the road in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you were referencing President Biden's low numbers. I put up that 32 percent for J.D. Vance. But if you look at that, you know, it means two-thirds of the GOP primary voters didn't vote for him, so he has to really unite the Republican Party in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, Trump was able to get J.D. Vance over the finish line because there were a lot of other Republican candidates, flawed Republican candidates. The biggest tests are going to be coming up in Georgia, where he's trying to unseat a sitting governor coming up in a lot of other big states in May where the -- Pennsylvania's going to be the biggest one, Dr. Oz on the ballot. He endorsed him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Got -- at a rally over the weekend, but he's facing some pretty fierce headwind in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's it for today. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 A quick programming note. Join me tomorrow for "SPECIAL REPORT" for my sit- down with former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  in his first cable news interview about his new book and his time in the Trump administration. That's tomorrow, 6:00 p.m. Eastern on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week. To Amy, Pat, and Barbie, the moms in my life, Happy Mother's Day. To all you moms out there, enjoy your special day, Happy Mother's Day to you.\par \par \uc2 And we will see you next 