e tampon machines in the boys' bathroom? What do you make of all this?\par \par \uc2 Well, The Associated Press, which not only sets our language guide, but also helps define the way we think, has decided to answer this question in their new guidance on it. Here it is, quote: "Phrasing, like 'pregnant people' should be confined to stories that specifically address the experiences of people who do not identify as women." Okay, so apparently, as long as you're talking about abortion, you can use the word women again, but if not don't.\par \par \uc2 All right, Chadwick Moore has been a journalist for a long time. He deals in words. He is the editor-in-chief of "Outspoken." Chadwick Moore joins us tonight to unravel this mystery from The Associated Press. What does this mean exactly, Chadwick, do you know?\par \par \uc2 CHADWICK MOORE, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, "OUTSPOKEN": Well, I'll tell you what, if you hate women, it's a great time to be alive. You get to laugh at them being humiliated in their sporting events, you get to laugh at them being shocked by men walking around in the buff in their spas and their locker rooms. And you get to demean them by calling them birthing units and uterus-havers and people with cervixes. It's wonderful if you're a misogynist.\par \par \uc2 But what I really want to know is how do you refer to what's the AP guidance on idiotic liberal journalists who wants to alter reality over the fear that maybe four people might get offended? How should we refer to them? I mean, I have some choice words, but this is a family show, so I won't use them right now.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How do you -- right? I mean, how do you do that without using obscenities? That's actually been a problem for us on the show for quite some time. But I love your point about hating women.\par \par \uc2 So we had the #MeToo movement three or four years ago that we are going to respect women, then like 20 minutes after that, it was like, "Shut up, ladies. The men are here and they're now women." Oh, I mean, no one noticed that it seemed like.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Right. And so many women who were champions of the #MeToo movement are silent about this, or maybe they just don't want to be canceled or too fearful of what might come for them.\par \par \uc2 But you're also correct to point out that, wow, suddenly women exist again as of Monday with the Roe v. Wade leak. I wonder, what that could be? And I wonder -- and another thing that also that is happening is the timing.\par \par \uc2 If you remember, around this time last year, it's Mother's Day coming up, we had \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  insert birthing people into his $6 trillion budget proposal and \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  was talking about birthing people as a big news story then, right around this same time.\par \par \uc2 So what is it with Mother's Day that want to get these moms too high on their horses, and remind them that, you know, men can get pregnant, too and have babies?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Basically, the message is, "Shh, men are talking." I am glad women are back. I like women and being a woman is not just wearing lipstick and a dress. It's like more than that, I would say, as a man, I've noticed.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Chadwick Moore, great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we told you about the effort in the name of democracy to prevent voters in Georgia from being allowed to vote for the most popular member of Congress in Georgia, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . Well, a Judge has ruled on that and we will tell you the results, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So here's a newsflash that you already knew. The people who yell about defending democracy hate democracy and do everything they can to stop democracy, which means prevent people from voting for the candidates of their choice. It happened in Georgia recently.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the state fi