cians in both parties speaking out against what is an obvious effort to interfere with, or intimidate the judicial process? Now, remember what Biden promised us, remember, on his inauguration day?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Today, on this January day, my whole soul is in this bringing America together, uniting our people, uniting our nation. We can treat each other with dignity and respect. We can join forces, stop the shouting and lower the temperature.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Did he see what was happening in front of the court, or New York, or see any of that, or is he just tuned out?\par \par \uc2 So where are Biden's calming words now?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, FOX NEWS: Do you think the progressive activists that are now planning protests outside some of the justices' houses are extreme?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Peaceful protest? No. Peaceful protest is not extreme.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: These activists posted a map with the home addresses of the Supreme Court justices. Is that kind of thing this president wants to help your side make their point?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Look, they think the President's view is that there's a lot of passion, a lot of fear, a lot of sadness from many, many people across this country about what they saw in that leaked document. We obviously want people's privacy to be respected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's it. Nothing about going to people's homes. We want the privacy to be seen respected. Spell it out for us. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 President Biden and his party have whipped up the ugliest radicals in their base, and they give lip service to privacy. Oh, please. As we reported last night, conservative justices homes, their locations, they've been shared online. Peter Doocy referenced that. And now