k, saying, she always has been a West Virginia and that this is disenfranchising to the voters. The candidate made an appeal to the Supreme Court to put a stay on Judge Bloom's ruling. And hours ago, they rejected that motion.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is GOP State Senate candidate, Andrea Garrett Kiessling; and her campaign adviser, Greg Thomas.\par \par \uc2 Andrea. OK, your appeal was rejected for the stay. Voting is underway. Where does your fight possibly go from here?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA GARRETT KIESSLING (R) WV STATE SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes, well, thanks for having us on, Laura. Of course, we are very disappointed by the Supreme Court's ruling today. It's just - it's disappointing. We have a lot of expectations in terms of the integrity of elections.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that there was a judge that was - interfered with our elections is concerning. There was a window to contest eligibility of candidates, and that window passed nearly three months ago.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So what's going on? I mean, you have a campaign manager sitting next to you, who would anticipate - you would anticipate everything, right? As a campaign manager, you have to - OK, this roadblock could come, this obstacle could come.\par \par \uc2 But when the voting is already underway, how is this in any way - is common sense, fair? Forget the legal issues that are involved.\par \par \uc2 GREG THOMAS, KIESSLING CAMPAIGN ADVISER: Yes. I mean, I think that's exactly it, Laura. We - obviously, we knew the - Andrea had split time between here and another state over a few years. But there's a window to challenge the stuff. Once we got through that window, we really stepped on the gas on the campaign.\par \par \uc2 And what triggered this challenge is we have one of our opponents in the primary, a very liberal candidate named Josh Higginbotham. He was the only Republican to support the transgender issue kind of stuff here in West Virginia. Once he took a poll and found out that Andrea