ence once again. It's going to spread everywhere.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: When I said good, of course, I'm talking about good that someone is defending Dave Chappelle. I didn't know to the extent that they had to defend him. But that arm was a little bit backwards there, Raymond. But look, it's open season in the eyes of many on the left on Supreme Court justices' privacy. So everything is open season. It's horrific.\par \par \uc2 What else do we have?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: When you weaponize language and said language I disagree with can justify violence, you get to this position. And now it's moved from entertainment, as you just said, to the political. Abortion rights activists now are really upping the ante, calling themselves Ruth Sent Us. And they're not only urging people to visit the homes of what they call six extremist Catholics who set out to overturn Roe, now they're posting videos. And they're encouraging people to storm Catholic churches this weekend in protest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Without this basic right, women can't be free. Abortion on demand and without apology. Without this basic right, women can't be free. Abortion on demand and without apology.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get the -- off our property. Go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I've got to say, I don't like the use of the language, but I like that that seminarian or assistant at the church is pushing back. That's what needs to happen. These people are religious bigots, to begin with, and they now feel empowered. But look, you know more than I, there are federal statutes that protect these houses of worship and the people in them. And you can't intimidate a judge or a justice in this way.\par \par \uc2 But when you cross that threshold and intimidate people who are praying, you've really violated something. And you know what, there's some trespasses that will only be forgiven by God. But the people this weekend, particularly in Catholic churches across the country, they better get ready, have protection and security at the backdoors, and throw these people out with great force.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, as we discussed with Severino and company, this is serious, this is serious business. But Raymond, you hit the faith aspect of this. Forgive those who trespass against us but not against the entire congregation and the sanctity of the church, OK. There's always a new low that the left will hit. When they go low, they go lower, OK? That's the rule. Raymond, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You to. Happy Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And up next, an INGRAHAM ANGLE investigation. This week we were given a tip about two Chinese nationals caught by police in Midland, Texas. But this was just the beginning of the story. The shocking details are next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This week, we received a tip from a Texas congressional office about a disturbing arrest that got no coverage. It was last Monday, and at Kimble County, Texas, interdiction deputy conducted what he thought was just a routine traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 Well, it turned out to be anything but. The vehicle was operated by a U.S. citizen associated with a series of gang-related shootings along the Texas- Mexico border. The front seat female passenger was found to be in deportation legal proceedings, and she was arrested for the possession of narcotics.\par \par \uc2 But here's where it gets interesting. The two rear seat passengers were found to be undocumented alien Chinese high value targets, who were transferred immediately to federal custody. We also learned something even more terrifying, if you can believe it. Undocumented Chinese aliens have been a problem in this area of Texas for a year now, usually sent to the country under the guise of people that are offering aid to Chinese Christians.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the congressman who tipped us off, August Pfluger, member of the Homeland Security and Foreign Affairs Committee. Congressman, first of all, thank you for this information. What does the country need to know about this?\par \par \uc2 REP. AUGUST PFLUGER, (R-TX) HOMELAND SECURITY COMMITTEE: Well, Laura, thanks for having me. And I think the most important thing is, let's look back at the 9/11 Commission, which in the days preceding 9/11 said that the system was blinking red. I think I'm here to tell you that we should be aware in this country right now, with the amount of illegal aliens entering the United States, that the system is blinking red. And that's exactly what happened in Kimble County.\par \par \uc2 And DHS apparently calls these Chinese high value targets, or special interest aliens. This story is not unique. And unfortunately, you're right. It doesn't get the coverage. It's happening all the time. It's egregious. And yet Mayorkas comes to Congress on Capitol Hill last week and says that he has operational control of the border? That is completely false. The border is not secure. All Americans are now waking up to that fact. And we need to be aware that the system is blinking red, and we need to be securing the border and actually doing something about it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, the fentanyl angle with the Chinese national is what comes to mind. But we'll find out more, we hope.\par \par \uc2 I found this exchange, Congressman, with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  to be kind of interesting. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: What is the president's priority? Would it be stopping potential terrorist attacks, or letting these migrants come in?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Again, I think as we noted just a couple of weeks ago when there was reporting about the number of individuals who are on the watch list being stopped at the border, that that that was the Border Patrol doing their jobs. That was the system working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The system is working, Congressman, when we have Chinese nationals sneaking into the country, South Americans, Cubans, people from Nigeria somehow make their way up through South America all the way to the border. But it's working. And that's what Mayorkas said, operational control.\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: Clearly this system is not working. And his definition of "secure" and "operational control" are different than the definition that every sane American uses. You just have to wonder at what point in time, like I mentioned on the 9/11 Commission, that the system was blinking red. That's what's happening right now.\par \par \uc2 Every community, every law enforcement agency, Texans feel abandoned. The administration has abdicated their responsibility. Governor Abbott has had to step in and send Texas DPS troopers. We had a National Guardsman who gave his life on the Rio Grande trying to save somebody who was illegally coming into this country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's outrageous.\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: This is where we are.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's outrageous.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, before you go, will you call for Mayorkas, the DHS secretary, who has this disinformation office, that's his big priority, call for him to resign?\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: Well, there's many of us who have called for him to resign. And the integrity of this issue, the security of our country and the fact that he has now come up with this ministry of truth -- I would encourage him if he's going to look at truth to look at his own statement, calling the border secure, and let's start with that as truth, because we all know that that's not true. It's a false statement. And we deserve better. This is not a partisan issue, and we need to secure the border, because our security, like a 9-11 moment, depends on it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, something that I floated, if Republicans take back control of the House and the Senate, would you be in favor of pursuing, or at least examining an impeachment inquiry based on the lies we've been told about the border, the dereliction of duty on the border, et cetera? Would you at least examine the possibility of impeaching Mayorkas or even the president of the United States over this?\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: Every option is on the table. We need to examine that. And we are going to take back the House. And when we do, there has to be accountability for these actions, especially in a moment where we are calling for the administration to recognize that the system is blinking red as it was in the pre-9-11 days.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, great to see you, Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PFLUGER: Thank you so much, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I have some final thoughts about this wild, wild week, and a new charity recommended some, but I have a new one when we return.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Finally tonight, after this week, some of the things we never thought we would see before, what looks like to be a series of increasing hostilities directed towards Supreme Court justices, leaked opinion. This can't happen, my friends. We're better than this. Our country needs better than this.\par \par \uc2 So hug your family this weekend, fly your flag. Remember how blessed we are as Americans. Remember, Get your USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com. Great totes for Mother's Day and great polo shirts all made in the U.S. This month of May, my proceeds will be donated for Freedom Matters to the Shoreline Soup Kitchens and Pantries. Shoreline Soup is an interfaith service of our wonderful friends in Connecticut, and it provides three meals a day to those who seek them and educates people on hunger and poverty. It's a truly great place with great people and great gear. So get the gear, you help great people feed those in need.\par \par \uc2 Have a wonderful weekend. Remember, thank you for watching. Without you, we're not here. And "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Stocks Fall As Investors Weigh Rate Hike And Job Growth; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Focuses On Economy Amid Supreme Court Fallout; Pro-Choice Group Disrupts Catholic Mass; Border Officials Hold Drills Ahead Of Anticipated Surge; Fact-Checking Alejandro Mayorkas Immigration Policy Statements; Ukraine Leaders Predict Putin Will Escalate Attacks; Videos, Revoked License, And Loaded Gun At Airport; Controversies Surrounding North Carolina Congressman Madison Cawthorn Examined; Some Media Outlets Critical Of Supreme Court Draft Decision Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Energy Expert Interviewed On Ways U.S. Can Reduce Gas Prices; April Jobs Report Indicates Strong Gains For U.S. Labor Market; Protestors Planning Demonstrations Outside Houses Of Conservative Supreme Court Justices Over Possible Roe Versus Wade Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Connell McShane, Peter Doocy, Lauren Green, Nate  Foy, William La Jeunesse, Trey Yingst, Chad Pergram, Bret Baier, Howard  Kurtz \par \uc2 7,930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: And excuse me, let me ask you one more thing. So, how many -- who's on -- who's on your show tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANALYST: You're going to be on my show too.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, thanks for mentioning it. Oh, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  is going to be on but that's it for us tonight. We'll see you back here on Monday night. I hope you have a wonderful weekend. Happy Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF BREAKING NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And good evening. Welcome to Los Angeles. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a solid April jobs report does not calm a very shaky Wall Street. Markets remain negative. Afterward, the economy added more than 400,000 jobs last month, and the unemployment rate remained at a near half century low.\par \par \uc2 Connell McShane of Fox Business joins us from New York. Connell, good evening.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Tracy. A wild week really in the economy and on Wall Street. That jobs report you talked about today did show the labor market is still quite robust in many ways, despite a questionable economic outlook and the volatile stock market.\par \par \uc2 You look at these numbers, the unemployment rate staying where it is at 3.6 percent. In April, 428,000 jobs being added, that marks 12 consecutive months of employers bringing on at least 400,000 workers to their payrolls.\par \par \uc2 But here's the hang up, they'd like to be hiring even more. Last month alone, 363,000 people left the workforce. So, the closely watched labor force participation rate declined, leaving administration officials searching for answers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN WALSH, LABOR SECRETARY: What parts of the country could we do better at? What parts of the country can we get more people engaged in the workforce at. And I think that there's opportunities for us really to look at this thing a little differently.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (voice over): It was another volatile day on Wall Street following this report, the Dow sold off in the morning before roaring back into the green and then turning lower again and closing down on the day.\par \par \uc2 All this volatility aside, the trend in the market over the last month and a half has certainly been lower. The Dow now recording six straight weekly declines. That's the longest streak in three years.\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, PRESIDENT, KALTBAUM CAPITAL MANAGEMENT: It's very simple, the cost of everything has gone up for both the consumer in business and in a very big way. And markets are basically adjusting to those costs. Because as they go up, profits come down, as profits come down, markets come down.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Now, Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  is trying to fight those higher costs for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raising its key interest rate by half a percentage point earlier the week.\par \par \uc2 Now, initially, stocks rallied but then, investors slept on that news and the markets sold off the following day. And that's kind of the way it's been in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE (on camera): Most experts that we speak with are in agreement, this wild ride, it's not over yet, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Somebody's concerned about something. Connell McShane from Fox Business. Connell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here are the numbers for context from Wall Street. The Dow lost 99, the S&P 500 fell 24, NASDAQ dropped 173.\par \par \uc2 Now, the stock market ended unusually turbulent week with its fifth straight weekly decline. For the week, the Dow was off a quarter percentage point, the S&P 500 lost one-fifth. The NASDAQ fell 1-1/2.\par \par \uc2 And now, for the politics behind the jobs numbers and the struggling economy. President Biden is calling on Congress to increase manufacturing in America during a visit to Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But the administration's economic woes had been overshadowed this week by something else. White House correspondent Peter Doocy shows us tonight, Peter, good evening.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Trace. And when that Supreme Court draft opinion about abortion rights was leaked earlier in the week, the White House scrambled to respond and they did but in the days since, the president has been trying to get people to talk about something else like that strong jobs number.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden believes his economic plans are working.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: All of this is going to help address what is on everyone's mind, fighting inflation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But progressive activists want to talk about abortion in the weeks before Roe v. Wade may be overturned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Does the president wants protesters to influence Supreme Court justices so that they uphold Roe?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president believes in peaceful protest. So, I wouldn't say he has a view on that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But minutes later, he did.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Obviously, our preference would be for Congress to codify Roe and of course, for the final opinion not to look like the leaked opinion.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's a slippery slope because there is a law prohibiting certain kinds of protests punishable by up to a year in prison. It's illegal to protest with, "The intent of influencing any judge, juror, witness or court officer in the discharge of his duty, pickets or parades in or near a building housing a court of the United States or in or near a building or residents occupied or used by such judge, juror, witness or court officer".\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  tells Fox News digital: The left tried that tactic on me and my family, terrorizing my wife and baby, it failed. The real story here is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is encouraging it. That makes him no better than the pathetic thugs who engage in this behavior.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, PROFESSOR, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY: Now, these justices are not going to back down. They have the courage of their convictions, and they're not going to yield to a mob.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Senator Elizabeth Warren warns if abortion rights are rolled back, many others could soon follow.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): That ought to make your gay friends nervous because we don't have a long history of protecting equal marriage. We don't have a long history of protecting interracial marriage.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden didn't allude to the fight over abortion in remarks today, trying to keep attention on the economy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've never been more optimistic about America than I am today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The only reason anybody's talking about Roe v. Wade this week is because that draft opinion leaked, and that is a leak that White House officials still have not condemned, still have not said they have any issue with. That is as we look ahead to next week where White House officials are not ruling out an appearance by the president at one of several large pro-abortion rallies scheduled, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We'll look for that. Meantime, Peter, what are we hearing about Mother's Day plans at the White House?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The mother who lives here, the First Lady Jill Biden is on a solo trip overseas. She will be on Sunday with some refugees who have fled Ukraine into Slovakia as President Biden will be here at the White House for Mother's Day celebrating the way only a world leader can, by participating in a virtual meeting of the G7, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: What else would you do? Peter Doocy live for us in the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice activists are planning to protest at several prominent Catholic churches on Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 Religion Correspondent Lauren Green is outside St. Patrick's Cathedral in New York tonight with that part of the story. Lauren, good evening.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, good evening, Trace. Well, you know, here at St. Patrick's Cathedral as with many parishes, Sunday will be a special day to celebrate mothers and motherhood. But activists have something very different in mind.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREEN (voice over): The activist group Ruth Sent Us posted this protest upsetting a Catholic mass, wearing the red capes of "The Handmaid's Tale" T.V. show, they exclaimed the right to abortion and blamed the six Catholics on the Supreme Court for the possible overturning of Roe v. Wade. The group is calling for similar protests of Catholic churches on Sunday Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 The unprecedented leak of a draft of the High Court's opinion has ignited pro-choice rage, including days of protest in front of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The Catholic church has been at the forefront of opposing abortion. The Catholic advocacy group CatholicVote.Org is railing against the Biden administration to enforce the law and stand up against threats from pro- choice protesters and churches. Saying, no religious believer in America should fear for their safety while attending church this Sunday or any day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREEN (on camera): And of course, this Sunday is Mother's Day. Here at St. Patrick's Cathedral, officials told me they are making plans. They won't go into details but say they are working with law enforcement here in New York City to take appropriate steps if necessary, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We'll talk more with the panel about that. Lauren Green live for us in a rainy New York night. Lauren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Howard Kurtz asked the White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  about the abortion controversy. We have his report coming up later this hour.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight we show you a dress rehearsal for an anticipated surge in illegal immigration when the Biden administration ends COVID related asylum rules at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy is in Laredo, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A show of force from authorities on both sides of the Rio Grande in Laredo, National Guard soldiers, \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  troopers and Mexican police all holding joint drills as Title 42 is set to expire.\par \par \uc2 JORGE RODRIGUEZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : I think the sheer presence of air support in the area along with ground troops, it sends a message to both the Mexican government and the migrants who are trying to cross through Mexico that we're ready.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Teams are training for a possible mass migration attempt. Just one mile south a group of 1,500 to 3,000 Haitian migrants are waiting to cross.\par \par \uc2 We are straddling the Rio Grande right now, about a mile south is where you're going to find that group of 1,500 to 3,000 Haitian migrants. I want you to take a look at that right here using Texas DPS resources. You see them just waiting on the other side of the border.\par \par \uc2 The question, what will they do if Title 42 was dropped as scheduled on the 23rd of this month?\par \par \uc2 The violent northeast cartel charges $800 to $1,000 for each person to cross the river. It's unclear how many have that money but Laredo leaders still believe the group is coming.\par \par \uc2 TANO TIJERINA, JUDGE, COMMISSIONERS COURT, WEBB COUNTY, TEXAS: They're waiting, they don't care, they want to come over now. They don't care about Title 42 because they know it's going to be lifted.\par \par \uc2 FOY: In the Del Rio sector, four migrants are back in Mexico tonight after a boat rescue by Mexican authorities near Eagle Pass. Others made it across to be processed by Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : For the month of April, it's going to be historic. And if it's confirmed from what I'm hearing, we're going to see the highest number of encounters in U.S. history for the month of April.\par \par \uc2 It just goes to show you that even what they're trying to do, it's not -- it's not making any type of significant impact along the border right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Trace, Texas DPS source tells me in the past few hours they received new \uc2 Intel\uc2  that 4,000 more Haitian migrants are making their way to Nuevo Laredo, just across the river behind me. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it never stops. Great compelling pictures Nate Foy live for us in Texas. Nate, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, it is sometimes hard to know the facts from the fiction surrounding the border crisis. Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas is tasked with presenting the administration's position but is he presenting the truth? National Correspondent William La Jeunesse takes a look tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: We're not talking about releasing individuals into the United States.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): In fact, DHS often does.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Do you know what released means? Because release -- the release doesn't mean just let go into the United States.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: This statement and others earned DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas three Pinocchios from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: If we do not detain individuals, they're placed on alternatives to detention.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA VAUGHAN, DIRECTOR OF POLICY STUDIES, CENTER FOR IMMIGRATION STUDIES: ATD is another fancy way of saying catch and release.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Most migrants assigned to alternatives to detention self- report to their initial hearings with their whereabouts monitored on a phone. 10 percent wear an ankle bracelet.\par \par \uc2 While ATD isn't new, President Biden tripled its use from the Trump years while emptying immigration jails.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHAN: In March, there were about 61,000 people who were released and about 25,000 of them were put into ATD.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But there is no guarantee they'll show up in court because most immigration proceedings last one to six years. Migrants are off ATD long before their case ends, leaving ICE no idea where migrants go when they don't show up.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: If they do not succeed, we enforce the law and they are removed.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHAN: That is just a flagrant lie.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Judges have ordered more than a million migrants deported who've never left according to DHS figures. Yet, Mayorkas misleads viewers.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: If they do not appear for their immigration enforcement proceedings, they are a priority for enforcement action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Well, that's not accurate unless someone is a violent offender or a national security threat, they are not an ICE priority.\par \par \uc2 Now, critics are concerned about two things. Number one, that the immigration system lacks integrity. Now, at the front end, when you have more false claims of asylum than those that are accurate and at the back end with deportation or removal orders widely ignored coming from a judge. That is why the Secretary got three Pinocchios.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, a lot of people, a lot of false information coming north. William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the battle for a key Ukrainian port city enters what appears to be its final stages. We will have a live report.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 10 in Mobile, Alabama as severe storms and a possible tornado leave major damage to a pair of stores in the downtown area, no injuries reported. More storms expected from South Alabama to coastal Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Fox 13 in Salt Lake City with the funeral service for longtime Utah Senator \uc2 Orrin Hatch\uc2 . He died April 23rd at the age of 88. The senator ended his tenure as the Chamber's longest serving Republican back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Louisville from Fox WDRB. The big story there tonight, preparations for the Kentucky Derby at \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2 . The greatest two minutes in sports kicks off at 6:57 Eastern Time tomorrow evening.\par \par \uc2 Legendary trainer Bob Baffert will not be allowed at the track. He is serving a suspension after his horse that won last year's event failed a post-race drug test.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While at least eight people are dead after a powerful explosion rip through a hotel in Havana, Cuba, the blast tore away large sections of the 19th century structure.\par \par \uc2 Officials now blaming a gas leak. More than a dozen people are said to be missing. Havana's governor says the hotel was in the process of renovations and no tourists were staying there at the time.\par \par \uc2 China has bust Taiwan with the largest contingent of fighter jets and bombers in more than three months. It comes one day after Japan's prime minister visited 10 Downing Street in London, and have had a President Biden's trip to East Asia later this month.\par \par \uc2 While Russian troops continue to try to wrap up their siege of Mariupol in Ukraine, while Ukrainian troops make their last stand and evacuation efforts resume.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Russia forces bombed the Azovstal plant in the Ukrainian city of Mariupol. Inside, Ukrainian civilians and soldiers are trapped in nuclear bunkers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Currently, Russian shelling and assault of Azovstal steel plant do not stop.\par \par \uc2 But civilians still need to be taken out. Women, children, many children who are still there, just imagining this hell.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The United Nations says 500 civilians were able to leave the facility over the past 48 hours, but others still need to be rescued.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials worry, they're running out of time as Russian leadership looks to claim a military victory.\par \par \uc2 DENYS MONASTYRSKIY, MINISTER OF INTERNAL AFFAIRS, UKRAINE (through translator): We understand there is no logic in Putin's actions. He has this thirst for victory. He has the ninth of May, that's upcoming when he needs to bring somebody's head for his people and show he's victorious.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Denys Monastyrskiy, serves as Ukraine's Minister of Internal Affairs. He believes the Russian military will escalate their attacks on Ukraine if a victory can't be claimed by Victory Day next week.\par \par \uc2 This week, the Russian military ran through a rehearsal for their Victory Day celebrations in the capital of Moscow. With fighter jets overhead and troops in the street.\par \par \uc2 Russian President Putin is looking to project an image of strength.\par \par \uc2 This spirit will be larger than the previous one. We will show the powerful strength of our country.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The preparation comes as wartime rhetoric reached new heights this week. With Russia warning \uc2 NATO\uc2  against supplying weapons to Ukraine, and \uc2 NATO\uc2  leadership pushing back.\par \par \uc2 Like the Polish prime minister who called Putin a war criminal.\par \par \uc2 Russian presidential spokesman Dmitry Peskov quickly responded to the statement. "The fact is there has been very, very hostile rhetoric coming out of Poland in recent months," Peskov explained. "And the fact that the territorial integrity of Ukraine can be threatened by Poland also our obvious facts."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Just a few minutes ago, we heard air raid sirens across the Ukrainian capital. Officials here are increasing security measures amid continued tension and ahead of Victory Day on Monday. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Trey Yingst, continuing his exceptional work in the war zone. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, many Republicans wish he would just go away. A look at the man, many consider the GOP's problem child in Congress.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER (voice-over): First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. An explosion rips through a four-story residential building in central Madrid. At least two people were killed and at least 18 injured. Officials now looking at the possibility of a gas leak.\par \par \uc2 Israeli security forces search for two Palestinians suspected of carrying out a stabbing rampage near Tel Aviv. Three Israelis were killed in the assault, Thursday on the country's Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 It is the latest in a series of deadly assaults deep inside the country in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 And a live look at London, one of the big stories there tonight, Buckingham Palace answers one of the biggest remaining questions about Queen Elizabeth II's Platinum Jubilee celebrations.\par \par \uc2 It says Prince Andrew, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2 , and Meghan, the Duchess of Sussex will not be on the palace balcony when monarch greets the public June 2nd.\par \par \uc2 The Queen has decreed that only working members of the royal family will be present. Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER (on camera): While Georgia's Republican secretary of state says GOP Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  can run for reelection. The announcement follows a similar ruling by a judge who says a group of voters challenging her eligibility failed to prove that she engaged in insurrection after taking office.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge is dismissing former President Trump's lawsuit against \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Mr. Trump was challenging his suspension from the platform. He has said he has no plans to rejoin \uc2 Twitter\uc2  if incoming owner \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  reinstates his account.\par \par \uc2 While many Republicans in Congress are expressing frustration with one of their own tonight. That would be North Carolina freshman Congressman Madison Cawthorn, who is making a bit of a habit of putting himself and his colleagues in a series of awkward situations.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has the story from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MADISON CAWTHORN (R-NC): ABC --\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Another day another video, or scandal or eye roll linked to GOP North Carolina Congressman Madison Cawthorn.\par \par \uc2 CAWTHORN: I'm sure that many of you have seen some outlandish stuff the media has been putting out about me over the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's hard to miss. Driving with a revoked license after multiple citations. Police cited Cawthorn twice for showing up at the airport with a loaded gun.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I thought you were arrested for that. Tried to take a gun on a plane, I guess he was -- his member was treated differently.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But wait there's more. Cawthorn alleging fellow lawmakers invited him to cocaine fueled orgies, photos of pictures of Cawthorn in women's lingerie.\par \par \uc2 CAWTHORN: And honestly, I look pretty good in these pictures.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: A video of Cawthorn engaged in a lewd act, another of a male aide groping the congressman.\par \par \uc2 CAWTHORN: This video was just stupid locker room talk taken long before I served in Congress.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: GOP North Carolina Senator \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  is determined to make his fellow Republican a one termer.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS, (R-NC): Absurd to embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What if he wins this primary? What will that mean for the district?\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I'm disappointed for his constituents. And that is why I'm working to avoid that outcome.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Tillis backs GOP state Senator Chuck Edwards in the May 17th primary. Still former President Trump endorses Cawthorn.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I love it because he's never controversial.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BITZER, CATAWBA COLLEGE: I'm not really sure that we've moved to a point where there will be retribution by the voters because of the loyalty and the fidelity to the former president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The GOP fears that controversial candidates like Cawthorn distract from their efforts to win back the House. Trace?\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chad Pergram live for us on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The man charged in this week's attack on comedian Dave Chapelle is pleading not guilty to four misdemeanors. Isaiah Lee is facing charges including battery and possessing a deadly weapon with intent to assault. The Los Angeles County district attorney is refusing to bring felony charges against Lee. The suspect remains jailed. He has been ordered to stay at least 100 yards from Chapelle when he is released.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in Tennessee have found a getaway vehicle for an Alabama corrections officer and inmate who are on the run. The sheriff in Williamson County says there is no sign of the two. A nationwide manhunt is ongoing for Casey White who was awaiting trial in a capital murder case, and Vicky White, no relation, the assistant director of corrections for the jail in Lauderdale County, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 U.S. health officials are looking into more than 100 possible cases of a severe and mysterious liver disease in children. Five deaths have now been reported. About two dozen states have suspected cases. Symptoms include fever, fatigue, loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.\par \par \uc2 The White House is still taking a careful approach to the Supreme Court abortion story. Many members of the media have not been so disciplined. The administration's side is being championed by its soon to be top former spokeswoman. Here is FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: In the midst of a furious debate over the draft Supreme Court ruling that could lead to striking down Roe v. Wade, White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  told FOX News that Republicans are focusing on the unknown leaker as a distraction.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Two-thirds of the public doesn't think Roe should be overturned, so maybe they want to talk more about the leak than they do about whether a woman should have the right to make choices about their own body with their doctors.\par \par \uc2 It is an unpopular position in the country, include among people who may not even identify themselves as Democrats.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Psaki has repeatedly refused to condemn the unprecedented leak, telling reporters the White House has no view on it.\par \par \uc2 With a few exceptions, many hosts, contributors, and yes, on MSNBC and CNN, have decried the potential court ruling to overturn Roe as an outrage and provided a regular platform for the leaders of pro-choice groups.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A giant step backwards for women as free people in America.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, FORMER PLANNED PARENTHOOD PRESIDENT: Your willingness to trade off women and women's rights for people's own personal political views is sickening.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MILLS, "JANE ROE" DAUGHTER: I'm in disbelief, and I can't believe that this is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Some FOX News shows have hosted both pro-life and pro-choice advocates.\par \par \uc2 ALLIE BETH STUCKEY, "RELATABLE" PODCAST HOST: This is the government doing the bare minimum, which is protecting the right of a human being not to be murdered.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Both sides are pushing the controversy well beyond abortion-rights. Media liberals are warning that the court's conservative majority could toss out other laws on same-sex marriage, birth control, and privacy. Media conservatives are talking about respecting the court's independence and protesters demonstrating at conservative justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Abortion is a difficult and uncomfortable subject for public discussion. That's why from leakers to protesters to dark warnings about the future, many in the media are using the draft ruling as a springboard to score ideological points. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Howard Kurtz, Howie, thank you. And you can see Howie's exclusive interview with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , including her comments on leaving the White House, on MEDIA BUZZ. That is Sunday morning, 11:00 eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Up next, tackling your pain at the pump amid rising inflation. \uc2 Toby Rice\uc2  from EQT Energy reveals a solution to lowering gas prices and resolve global energy shortage.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Parents of infants across the U.S. are taking to social media to express their frustration over the country's baby formula shortage. They're posting pictures of empty store shelves hoping to get media coverage and spur political action. The shortage began with ongoing supply chain issues. It has now escalated in recent weeks because of recalls by manufacturer \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  following the deaths of two infants who reportedly consumed formula made at the company's plant in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we look at what the energy sector is doing as the economy grapples with short supplies and high inflation. Here's my colleague Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Trace. Joining us tonight is \uc2 Toby Rice\uc2 , chief executive officer of EQT Energy. Toby, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 TOBY RICE, EQT ENERGY PRESIDENT AND CEO: Happy to be here, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just big picture, what is the energy market, the energy picture when it comes to the U.S. tonight?\par \par \uc2 RICE\uc2 : Clearly there is an energy shortage going on around the world, and America has experienced that same consequences and that results when you have an energy shortage. Right now, what we are seeing here in the United States is energy prices that are significantly higher than they need to be. And that is the bright side, is that these prices are unnecessarily high.\par \par \uc2 So that means there is a solution. And the solution is simple. Let's unleash American energy, bring more supply into this world, and that will have a tremendous impact of lowering energy prices here while also providing energy security to the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  at the White House was asked about the creeping prices of gas particularly, getting to 20 cents from the record. Here is her answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We're going to continue to take steps to reduce the price of gas for the American people. Every option, range of options, remains on the table. And we know that increasing supply and ensuring supply meets the demand on the marketplace is part of that effort. We also watch closely for any attempt at price gouging when oil prices come down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we take a look at the average, the national average gas prices, and it's up there. Toby, they do talk about price gouging a lot. What's your response to how the administration talks about the energy industry that you're in.\par \par \uc2 RICE: I think people talk about price gouging, I think they're looking for scapegoats. We need to be looking for solutions. Prices are set by supply and demand, and the administration is right. One of the solutions to lowering prices is to increase supply. And that is where the focus needs to be, not in vilifying this industry, because we are doing everything we can to do our jobs and lower energy prices for Americans.\par \par \uc2 This industry is coming off a major recession for oil prices were negative. And we're getting back up to speed. And there's a tremendous number of things we could do to help support this industry so that we can continue to do the great work that we do. And one of those things is adding more pipeline infrastructure as a way that will allow us to do more drilling and bring more supply to the market. But without pipeline infrastructure, there's nothing we can do here to add more supply to the market.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you feel like it's an uphill battle to instruct lawmakers who talk about this every day about your industry and about, that maybe it is not just Ukraine and Russia that is the reason for gas going up?\par \par \uc2 RICE: Well, you hear it a lot. Energy is very complex, but let's make it very simple. The United States has a tremendous responsibility and tremendous opportunity to leverage its natural resources that we have here and lower prices for Americans, provide energy security for Americans while also providing energy security for the world. And for us to do that, we just simply need more pipeline infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And this has been a challenge. Now, we have the largest gas field in the world, the Marcellus shale out here in Appalachia. But these pipelines that have been blocked over the years, there's been over seven pipelines blocked. And that is equivalent to about 10 percent of the us supply. And with these pipelines being blocked, we are unable to unlock this resource that we have. Simply put, without pipelines, this Marcellus shale might as well be in Russia, and it's unable to serve the public and serve our allies in Europe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's an important point. We're going to follow this industry, all sides of it. \uc2 Toby Rice\uc2 , chief executive officer of EQT Energy. Toby, thanks.\par \par \uc2 RICE: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trace, I will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the jobs report, the economy, and the hardball abortion tactics following the Supreme Court draft release.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today's job report shows our plans and priorities have produced the strongest job creation in the modern times of the American economy.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN WALSH, LABOR SECRETARY: We all know there is work to be done. The two reports before me, there's a lot going on in the economy. There's a lot of uncertainty at the kitchen table. People are concerned about prices, and certainly, what we are going to do with that number.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Two things can be true. People can have some very important economic tailwinds at their back, helping them face this difficult inflationary environment from a position of strength, and they can be unsettled by inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in our panel, Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 The jobs numbers, you really can't argue with that a lot. The jobs numbers that came out are pretty strong. I want to put this up. This is from Senator Ted Cruz. He tweeted the following, quoting here, "The White House is blissfully peddling the jobs report. The American people can see through their strong economy spin. Inflation is through the roof and wages can't keep up with it." I get, \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , that he is taking a jab at the administration here, but he also makes a fair point because you look at what happened to the markets this week, they were very volatile. And there is a lot up nervous people around these markets.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Trace, the markets are very upset. And Ted Cruz is right on the mark. The president has got every single day between now to November to tell everyone the economy is great and it's the best economy that has ever existed. They are paying $125 at the pump to fill their truck. They know what the grocery bill is, and the 30-year fixed mortgage went back above five percent for the first time since 2010, I believe. It's got to go higher, it's going to go higher. We're going to get a half-point interest rate almost every two months for the next year. It's really impossible for the president to cover the disaster that has unfolded for the American consumer.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And you talk to a lot of realtors around the country, and they are nervous. They are nervous about the housing prices maybe not coming down fast enough, interest prices going up. There's a lot of concern out there.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIR, \uc2 FEDERAL RESERVE\uc2 : We have a good chance to restore price stability without a recession, without a severe downturn, and without materially higher unemployment. I see a strong economy now. I see a very strong labor market.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: So he says that we have a good chance, Leslie Marshall, of not going off the cliff. But he went on later, and he says but we could very well see a slowdown. And you look at what is happening in the economy right now, Leslie, and a slowdown would be fairly devastating for this administration. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 We have a good economy, as you just heard right now. When you see this jobs report and when you see unemployment low, and when you see hourly wages over five percent, 5.5 percent to be exact, from a year ago, an increase of that, that's all good. That's all positive. But we don't hear that, do we? No. We hear the negative.\par \par \uc2 And I've got to say my party, the Democrats, are not using this opportunity to say, hey, this is good news, and the Biden has a right, sorry, Ted Cruz, to be happy about this. Republicans would be doing the same thing. This is what is predictable about whatever party is in power, mine as well. They always look at whatever report, and they go yes, but let's look at the glass half empty, and both parties do it to the other side.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is a concern. We do see interest rates going up. But then again, if you look at the trend, and hourly wage that have increased, that trend is going to slow down, and that actually could help to offset inflation. And when you talk about real estate, sellers are very happy with prices where they're at.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And Matt, but Leslie is kind of putting a friendly spin on there, too. The talks about hourly wages going up 5.5 percent and leaves out the fact that inflation has gone up 8.5 percent. So it's really hard to catch up there.\par \par \uc2 And it seems to me like there is a lot of psychological momentum that we are lacking here. You drive down the street and you see those big giant billboards with five bucks, six bucks a gallon, you walk into a store, you have empty shelves. People feel like the economy is not bouncing on all cylinders.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: That's right. Inflation isn't a concern, Trace. It's the concern. When you ask the American people what they are most concerned about, it is their declining standard of living. And that's what's leading them to assess this economy negatively. That's what's causing the fall in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating, and that's what's leading to rising Republican enthusiasm for the fall.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's a fair assessment.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this and move on, if I can, panel. This whole concept of the Roe v. Wade, it was a big story. It broke late one night this week. I want to play Peter Doocy. He's talking with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  about planned protest outside of the homes of Supreme Court justices., and I will get your response. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think the progressive activists that are now planning protests outside some of the justices' houses are extreme?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Peaceful protest? No. Peaceful protest is not extreme.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R-SC): Are they encouraging open season on our Supreme Court justices? What is going on here. And we know that these protests can turn into riots overnight very quickly.\par \par \uc2 It's almost encouraging this kind of behavior, and it should be discouraged.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We have seen, Leslie Marshall, that, let's face it, these things are not legal. Peter Doocy pointed that at the top of the show tonight. You can't go to a Supreme Court justice's house and hold a protest on their front lawn, nor should you. What are your thoughts on this?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: But you can, the First Amendment says, have a peaceful protest on the sidewalk outside of their home, in the street in front of their home, across the street from their home. And that's what makes our nation great. And one of the foundations, certainly, the First Amendment in our Constitution in this nation.\par \par \uc2 But here's the problem I have, and I get a lot of anger from my side on this. Protesting, wonderful. We've seen women's marches. That's great. But unless we get out and vote in November in the midterms when Democrats don't show up in the numbers Republicans do, it is all for naught. You've got to vote. And showing the people that stayed home, shame on that, because if this draft does become a decision, this is where we are at as a result to people who didn't vote, were apathetic and didn't care, and thought somebody else would do it for us. And that person is gutted in the bag.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Matt, the pro-choice group Ruth Sent Us, referring to \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , said the following here, "Whether you are Catholic for choice, ex-Catholic of other or no faith, recognize that six extremists Catholics set out to overturn Roe, stand at or in a local Catholic church Sunday May 8th," and goes on to hashtag "war on women," "Mothers' Day strike." Protesting inside a church, Matthew.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Trace, we have to reestablish the distinction between the private sphere and the public sphere. It's one thing to have a protest in front of the Supreme Court building if this becomes the final ruling. It's another thing to go into churches, to target people's homes. That is violating the distinction that all civilized societies make between private and public.\par \par \uc2 And it's a reminder that if this opinion becomes the holding decision, it is going to restore abortion law to the states and democratic procedures, that means the rule of law, not mob rule.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Your final thoughts on this, \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , the Catholics, the protesting in Catholic churches.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: I believe it is clear that the court ought to move the issue, the judgment opinion to follow in order to reduce the security threat to the justices, which is real. I live a couple abounds from the baseball field where the Bernie Sanders supporter tried to execute a significant number of members of Congress. And I do not think we should underestimate what this leaker did, and I think that court should -- Tom Cotton suggested it, I think the Senator is right. Issue the judgment, and opinions to follow.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and I think it is notable because you have a lot of Catholic priests and monsignors who are actually prepping and gearing up for what they believe will be people showing up at their church on Mothers' Day, including my parish. It's one of those things that's interest.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Winners and Losers. Let's begin with you, Matt, winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: All right, my winner is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He had a very good week in Ohio, and he is showing the results of his Supreme Court nominees if this decision holds about Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 My loser of the week is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . The war in east Ukraine is not going well. It's going just as badly as the war in Kyiv. And I think the question is, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Leslie, quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Winners, Biden demonstration for the strong jobs report and the low unemployment rate that continues. Losers, Republicans in the state of Louisiana who are trying to push through legislation to make abortion homicide.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Got one winner from you, Hugh. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: There's the pro-life legal groups, and there are dozens of them. I know the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  is the one I've worked with the most. It took them 25, 30 years after the Casey decision, but they are on the brink of a long-sought victory.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Panel, thank you all. We appreciate your time and your comments.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: This is as urgent and as real as it gets.\par \par \uc2 We will vote to protect a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Whoever leaked this out clearly has an agenda that they are trying to be able to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Did I say it's not a political issue?\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. You actually said some call it a political issue. It is not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is not pretty. It's fundamentally destroying this nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our immigration system and particularly our asylum system are outdated.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're really quite precocious.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was not aware of those videos.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The opportunities in the state depend on us fighting and winning against Tim Ryan in November.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to be the face and the voice of this campaign, and I don't want any distractions. I don't need anybody to prop me up like J.D. Vance did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even from the sitting president of the United States of your party, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: These are sideshows.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We see the incredible bravery of the Ukrainian fighters defending their country with everything they have picked.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell me how this ends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): Only with victory. We have no way out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, Bret is back with former defense secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  on the Ukraine war in his first cable news interview promoting his new book. This weekend on FOX NEWS SUNDAY, Bret anchors his exclusive guests will be South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Connecticut Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Trace Gallagher in Los Angeles. Pete Hegseth guests hosts on "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME." Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Group of Radical Protesters Posted a Map with The Home Addresses of the Conservative Justices on the Supreme Court and The White House Didn't Do Anything to Stop a Potential Violence. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  Also Added Fuel to the Growing Outrage in the Streets Over the Leaked Draft and Says That There is More to Come After Roe v. Wade is Overturned; Sunny Hostin of The View Say That She Can't Believe There Are People of Color Who Are on the Republican Side; The Biden Administration and Their Allies in the Media Keep Boasting About How Great the Economy is, But Most Americans Who Feel the Brunt of Inflation and Rising Commodities Believe Otherwise; Dems Boasting About Economy As Inflation Rages; Inflation Nation: U.S. Faces Economic Struggles As Biden Deflects Blame; Backlash Over NYT Piece On Musk 'White Privilege'\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Lawrence Jones, Kayleigh McEnany,  Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Did you want to beer from the homemade thing, processed meats and cheeses, the gift that keeps giving?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Two hundred twenty-five dollars, apparently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Come on, $19.99, just say you know me. Guys, have a great one. We'll see you tomorrow, here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Harold Ford, Jr., Lawrence Jones, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The White House adding more fuel to the fire as far left lunatics threaten to terrorize the Supreme Court justices. A group of radicals posting home addresses of the six conservative leaning justices and encouraging protest following the leak of the draft opinion that could overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has a huge fence around it now and there are growing concerns about security threats. And pro-abortion groups that just show up outside of churches this weekend. Like this protest upsetting a Catholic mass with people wearing the red capes of the handmaiden's TV show. But the White House doesn't seem bothered.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Do you think the progressive that activists that are now claiming protesting outside of some of the justices' houses are extreme?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Peaceful protests? No. Peaceful protest is not extreme.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But this activist posted a map with the home addresses of the Supreme Court justices, is that the kind of thing this president wants to help your side make their point?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Look, I think the president's view is that there is a lot of passion, a lot of fear. A lot of sadness from many, many people across this country about what they saw in that leaked document. We obviously want people's privacy to be respected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And Hillary Clinton is also inflaming passions over the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: And any American says, look, I'm not a woman, this doesn't affect me, I'm not black, that doesn't affect me. I'm not gay, that doesn't affect me. Once you allow this extreme power to take hold, you have no idea who they will come for next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Greg. It appears that the White House, the administration, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  has no interest in discouraging the protest outside the Supreme Court justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD FOX NEWS HOST: First, I would like to point out that Kayleigh is making me think of my days in college because you are a big giant highlighter.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I am. We're still.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I feel like I have to just take you and highlight my speech.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Got to be bright.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So basically, this weekend is like an anti-Mother's Day.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Isn't it incredible that it's so surreal? But I bet you 100 bucks, Judge, that if and when violence erupts the people who lost their collective minds over January 6, in which the only person who died was a protester, Ashli Babbitt at the scene. They'll justify anything in the name of abortion.\par \par \uc2 There's a, you know, what did Jen say? There's a lot of passion and fear. But that's been generated by the Dems who are acting as if abortion is being banned outright. It's just being sent back to the state. The New York claims that it's only about 14 percent decline in abortions, and I know that's sad for people who are really working for that quota. But people can get abortions and they probably will, and that's just the way it works.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, I mentioned this yesterday, there is a filter of evil that the woke operates on. They, when they disagree with you, it's not that you are wrong, it's that you are evil. And that excuses all of their behavior.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, if you are against them, then they can do whatever they want. That's how they justify the -- that's how they justify the riots after Floyd. But the Republicans don't do that, they don't see the other side is evil, they see the other side as wrong. So, they are less likely to get physical, of course there are -- there are extreme, you know, there are exceptions.\par \par \uc2 But the Dems have the memories of goldfish. Don't they know if they normalize this behavior, the right always learns from the left. It wasn't like the tea party just came out of nowhere. I mean, before that you had occupy Wall Street. January 6 actually came after antifa and BLM. Right? And also, the riots.\par \par \uc2 So, if you normalize violence, you are also normalizing violence for the other side. So, it would be best if your political leaders would condemn this, because I hate to tell you, you know, it's -- it always -- we are in this cyclical thing, and Republicans and activists, conservative activist, they have an internal braking system. They don't lose control or destroy things because they are more prone to process, order and orderliness.\par \par \uc2 That's why you don't -- that's why these right-for-life marches are so calm and so peaceful and there is no litter, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I mean, it's like somebody go to climate change protest, there's piles of trash. So, it's like there is -- the right always has a bit more to lose when it comes to the stuff, because you know, they believe in structure. But obviously there's going to be exceptions, but I think that it would behoove the Democrats not to make excuses.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Kayleigh, the --the -- we hear about the rage. And for some reason the left feels not only entitled to rage far more than the right, and I don't know if the right ever does it the way that the left does it, but they feel entitled to act out their rage, that you know, it's OK for them.\par \par \uc2 Well, then, the administration not saying anything about justices or the Supreme Court, their homes with young children. The Catholic mass on Mother's Day. I mean, you know, these people on the right are basically saying, look, since Roe was decided, they're been 63 million abortions. And you know, I said that in a long time period earlier this week, but you know, the people on the right aren't going crazy, but the left feels they are entitled to rage, and there is no sanction, why?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. They are green-lighted by the White House. So, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  got a second bite of the apple, she was asked about this again about the violence and the protest at the Supreme Court justices' homes. Here's what she said during a gaggle. She was asked specifically does the president want protesters to influence Supreme Court justices, so they uphold Roe?\par \par \uc2 Which basically that question verbatim is a crime if you look at the U.S. Code, to influence a justice by protesting at their home? Her response was this. I wouldn't say that the president has a particular view on this. Exact same thing she said about the leak. He doesn't have a particular view on the leak.\par \par \uc2 So unprecedented leaks, no view, protesting at a Supreme Court justices' home, no view. I get \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is leaving, I get that she is going to MSNBC, she needs her streak creds there. I get that they say all kinds of outrageous things, Tiffany Cross said let's quote her exactly, "pick up a weapon and get involved." Nicolle Wallace analogized those oppose COVID restrictions to drone strike domestically on U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  needs a hot take. She's got a hot take here by literally green lighting a crime, and you can look at the statue 18USC 1507, there is also a code of Virginia against protesting at a primary residence. She green- lighted a crime from the podium, she green-lighted a crime in a gaggle today.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Harold, what's your take on it?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You are a green light.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I am a green light.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What is your take on this?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD, JR., FOX NEWS HOST: Well, first, glad to be back around the table. I think there are two things here. First off, no one should ever encourage violence to make your points, your political point.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But isn't -- and I don't mean to interrupt, I think we all agree with that, what about going to Supreme Court justices' homes?\par \par \uc2 FORD: It's wrong, I don't know how much more, but here's what I would caution everyone. This happens across the board. Remember, there were abortion clinics that were blown up by conservatives back years ago, back in the '60s, the '50s and the '60s in the country, Black America in the south were water hosed and violence was by people who had different political views in them.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a history of this. Dissent is important, it's understandable. In fact, it's indispensable, but what is not is violent. The president, the White House should encourage anyone who wants to protest to protest peacefully. If they cross the line, that person or those people should be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Much like I thought parents had every right to go to every school board meeting and say what they needed to say and want to say, but when you threaten a political official, an elected official, you cross the line. Now, the issue here with the protests and the Roe v. Wade decision I think are two different things.\par \par \uc2 If you have an opinion on Roe v. Wade, express yourself, the court will make a determination and decision here. Politics is behind a lot of this. I have a strong opinion about this. I think that people should have the right to choose. I think there should be limitations on abortion in the country, but Roe v. Wade I think has worked, but this court will have an opportunity to weigh in on June and the American people will have to live with that decision and we'll have to live with the politics of it.\par \par \uc2 And hopefully the next election, Democrats will get out and vote for those who share their opinion. And I would encourage no one, please don't cross the line and engage in any violence before any elected official, any judge or any justice.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But Lawrence, the problem is that the president didn't say, don't cross the line, don't do anything violent. When they are talking the language of the left is, you know, we've got to rise up? We've got to let them know, what other language that they were using. That's very forceful active language. It was none of that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS HOST: There's nothing forceful from the President of the United States, there's nothing forceful from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 . And as you remember during the campaign trail, he often talked about his compassion and his religion and him as a man being of the Catholic faith, yet, they're specifically targeting Catholic churches, they haven't said a damn thing about that.\par \par \uc2 To Harold's point, the problem is not protest or evil, even peaceful protests or even the violence, obviously that is wrong, it's the fact that the view of the system, the fact that they feel like protesting a judge should change their position on the -- that is the core of what the Democrats believe, and we are just seeing this at the Supreme Court, but if we remember, this has been happening all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Protesting judges, telling the judges and the jury that they should not listen to the testimony that has been provided for them or read the brief. They want to tear down the system, and I think at its core what the Democratic Party believe today is wrong.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we can talk about protests all day, they shouldn't be protesting judges, because judges should not be influenced by protests. And I think that that is the real issue.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Can I ask Lawrence a question?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Quickly.\par \par \uc2 FORD: On January 6 the people that have protest at the capital, what were they going to undo there?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I don't believe with January 6.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. None of us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: I don't believe --\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : So that's a -- I just want to make sure we're -- I agree with you, I happen to agree with your point about the judge.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Coming up, a huge controversy on The View. A co-host is shock that black people can be conservatives.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, move over, Jill Biden, you are not the only one insulting conservative Americans. After the president attacked the MAGA movement and called it the most extreme political organization in recent U.S. history. I can think of several others more extreme nevertheless. The View is taking his cue and insulting millions of Black and Latino conservatives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, HOST, ABC: I'm not having ethical order of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I have many things that I don't stand by that Trump did. Trump has done things that are racist. I'm a Black woman first, so always understand that, but I do say that as have many conservative values that I will talk to you about. And so, if you look at your network that you're standing behind --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: Are you a Republican?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes. And when you look at your network that you are standing behind you are saying that you look at Chris Cuomo --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: I feel like there is an oxymoron, a Black Republican.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: You feel like it's an oxymoron.\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: I do.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Why? Your friend right here is a Republican.\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: I don't understand how black Republicans and I don't understand Latino Republicans.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Well, here what I --people are recoverable flamenco Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Sunny Hostin never to disappoint. Greg, add it to the list, oxymoron, Neanderthals, deplorable, irredeemable, groups you can't find the country on the map as Don Lemon cackled at the MAGA crowd.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I will always say it is better to be an oxymoron than just a moron, Sunny.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's hard to be the dumbest person on The View. It's like being the smelliest member of antifa, you know. By the way, let's not forget, in terms of the most extreme organizations, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went to the funeral of a leader of the most extreme