anned Parenthood whose founding mission was to exterminate black and brown babies.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And we are not even going to get into the dead bodies in the street today over their little two-year experiment of defunding the police. They say they care about the kids and believe that young people, black and brown should have a quality education, but when we needed teachers the most, they side with the teacher's union.\par \par \uc2 I can go list -- they say they are for black businesses and they want to reform and get rid of redlining, make sure regulation is out of their way. Yet they put the policies in the community to hurt black businesses and brown businesses the worse.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I can go on and on about every single policy position that they hold, that is a total oxymoron, but I won't do that, because I think election day is going to tell us a different story. My only prayer is Republicans do something different this election. I mean, they just serve to you on a silver platter just disgrace. When I go back to my local barbershop, they talk about how the crime and the policies economically is crushing our community. I got to do is talk to these voters and you can win.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. You know, you're exactly right. Lindsey Granger who is the Republican on that panel, Judge Jeanine, shot back at Sunny Hostin and said you want to talk about oxymorons, what about being a Catholic and being pro-choice?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right. Well, I got to tell you, I was very impressed with her, and I think that she makes it very clear that you know, you can't look at Black Americans, Hispanic Americans with a simplistic color chart. If you are this color, you have to think this way. And it's a very condescending and arrogant way of looking at people, it's basically saying, if you're this color, you have to think this way.\par \par \uc2 And you know, how about people who are black, Hispanic, I don't care if you are pink, and you have an individual process that is far more intricate than people who look at your skin color. How about a prosecutor who said, you know what, I'm going to pick a juror today, and I'm going to pick a jury a person of this color because I know they are going to vote this way.\par \par \uc2 That's wrong. That is belittling of that people. And you know, the irony is that these are the people who champion diversity unless, OK, unless it's diversity of thought. If it's diversity of thought, well then you are not allowed in. You are not welcome if you don't agree with us. Shame on them.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Shame on them. Harold, it kind of echoes the language of the White House though, talking about this ultra MAGA agenda, it sounds like something from "The Incredible Hulk," who perhaps I resembled today given my outfit. Nevertheless, I don't think people are going and pumping $90 gas thinking about the ultra MAGA agenda, or going to the grocery stores empty shelves, the ultra MAGA agenda. I don't think it's effective messaging.\par \par \uc2 FORD: You are probably right. Look, this is the cancel culture we live in, people hear an idea that they don't like and they want to cancel it. As long as it is something that echoes what they believe they want to hear.\par \par \uc2 When I was in law school, every Wednesday or Thursday night I got together with a group of friends, a little burger joint on states that went in the university in Michigan. And it was a diverse group of thought and the only rule was you can't hurl a personal insult. We talk about the issues that we've discussed in class and political issues. And it went on for an hour, it went up to three, four, five hours at times when we were really enjoying ourselves in having these debates.\par \par \uc2 You can't cancel Condi Rice from coming to your graduation because you don't agree with her, you can't cancel \uc2 John McCain\uc2  or a Black Republican or a Black Democrat because you don't want to hear them. Our nation is strengthened when we hear these ideas.\par \par \uc2 To your point we want to have this debate. I may not agree with you on everything or Kayleigh on everything. I agree with Greg on a lot of things and the judge makes me agree with her on things. But at the end of the say, you cannot get to a result unless you are willing to engage.\par \par \uc2 Sunny is my friend. I don't know if she meant what she said there.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She meant it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But I hope -- I hope she reflects on it and understands. If that young lady she disagrees with, you're not going to -- you're not going to win her over or the people who believe in her argument by saying that she doesn't exist. You can defeat her argument, undermine her argument, but you can't undermine the person. We need to remove the personal insults and get back to the substance, whether it's on economic issues, business issues, social issues, whatever it may be. That's the only way we're going to be best.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The last thing -- the last thing I'll say is because Harold is a gentleman and he is one that's trying to find common ground, but I think he is under illusion that people like Sunny want common ground. They don't. They want to shame --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I know Sunny. I don't -- I disagree with you there, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And I've known her as well. And I think she's done -- you know them by their fruits, when you are on this panel, she had the same opportunity on national TV to bring people together.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, we might --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: And on the other side, you know what I'm talking about. On her side she takes it to shame black Americans who has a point of view, and by the way, not all of us are on TV as well. There are a lot of conservative black folks at home that either identify as independent or conservative, there's only about 26 percent of Black Americans that identify with liberals, so that's a majority of all of us! That is who she is talking about when she is saying that.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : We should invite her on the show and have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I would love to be --\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: I don't think she would ever come. I'm just surmising that. I don't think --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I've been on shows with her.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We have the lowest rated show if we had her on.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes. Probably true, Greg. All right. Up next, Biden's cronies and the media think the economy is great. And you are just too stupid to realize it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, the Democrats are boasting about Biden's economy, but average Americans is facing a very different reality. Job gains did above the mark of 428,000 added last month. But soaring inflation continues to rise the cost of everything.\par \par \uc2 James Carville says upset Americans don't understand just how good the economy is right now. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think there's an entire picture of an economy here, and I don't think that the people in America are getting - - understanding completely where it is. There are -- there are some bad things, but if we are making these decisions off of the assumption that no jobs are created, that's just not true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Even CNN they believe the analysis is just baloney. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, SENIOR DATA REPORTER, CNN: On the chyron you see dismal. I was going to use the word awful, essentially if you ask Americans how they view the economy is a poor or good, 77 percent say bad, bad, bad. Just 23 percent say, because you got to go all the way back to 2012 to see numbers that bad, and it's not much of a surprise if you ask folks what's the most important problem facing the country right now? What is the top issue? The economy!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yikes. And if rising costs aren't enough of a headache, now the supply chain hitting parents who say they can't find formulas to feed their babies.\par \par \uc2 Kayleigh, you're a mom, do you think this is going to swing women voters to the Republican side based on this issue?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No, absolutely not. I mean, that is a real phenomenon, women who can't afford diapers. I have a friend who helps out a charity to provide diapers, that's how bad the economy is right now. But it's a different story if you go on chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed. The last eight hours \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  has retweeted 19 tweets saying how great the economy is. Just to sample, another strong jobs number, one hell of a graph and it's all true, solid jobs report. You know, at a certain point, the lady doth protest too much, to quote Shakespeare, and that is exactly what \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is doing. Because one number matters, it's the one percent who thinks the economy is excellent.\par \par \uc2 I don't know who the one percent is, maybe \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , but that's the number that matters is what people feel.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Do you think, Harold, it is a communication flow or is it just the reality? They got to deal with what they're working with. They can't spin this number.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: No, you never want to try to tell people that they're doing better than they think that they're doing. I think what James was saying was, we have some fundamentals. And we've got to figure out how to frame a message and then tell the American people, share with American people what we're doing to fix that problem.\par \par \uc2 You touched on one or two of the things. Crime and the border are things we have to get not only a message around, but put a policy in place that the American people are going to have confidence. And I've shared on this show, if I were the president, once a week, I'd be in a city with a DA, Judge. I'd be with the police chief. I'd come back to Washington saying these are the things we have to help cities like the ones I just visited, what they're asking about, what we need cash with, bail reform, more police officers on the street, providing mental health services.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the economy, look, we've got to invest in cities that are going to create new jobs and attract jobs. So, how do you bring more 5G? How do you ensure that we can produce potato chips and semiconductor chips here in America? How do we ensure that we don't fall behind China in producing batteries? If I were the White House, I'd be talking about these things. That's what I interpreted from James.\par \par \uc2 Remember James Carville gave Democrats the greatest sign we had in the 90s. It's the economy, stupid. The White House had to listen.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Judge, it seems like the Democrats have a lot on their plate. I mean, when you're a prosecutor, and you're trying to give someone a break, if they got all these charges, there's only so much you can do to work with them, right?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I don't want to work with them if they have that many charges. But that's another issue. Here's the problem. The problem is an inflation is raging like never before. Inflation has gone up by 8.5 percent, wages have gone up by five percent.\par \par \uc2 This isn't government that is disincentivizing work and disincentivizing jobs, all right. They're incentivizing people to stay home and to not go to work and dismayed students for their loans for no reason when there are 11 and a half million jobs available, and only six million people looking for a job.\par \par \uc2 So, it's the incentivizing and the disincentivizing that this this this government is involved in. And what they're done with the oil