ated community.\par \par \uc2 What scares me in the future is this war and the effects of the war on future food shortages. If we're already having problems right now and we're all in on this thing, man -- and every country is going to feel it, especially the poor ones like Africa. And I think that when you have food shortages, and this is what Dana has explained to me a number of occasions, it usually does lead to more war. A lot of the countries that go to war, go to work because they don't have the food.\par \par \uc2 So, we're having this conflict going on right now which could create severe food shortages, which will then lead to more war. It might be a good time to start figuring out a negotiated settlement before it gets really, really bad. That's my take.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think the biggest story here is the media. And we saw this during COVID. I'm saying that we're all in this together. Americans felt differently and they eventually turned on the media. I think it's going to be the same for this issue as well. Up next, the liberal knives are out for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . The latest desperate attack on the world's richest man. That's next on THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's $44 billion purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and stance on free speech once again landing him in hot water with the media. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is being accused of targeting the world's richest man in a new profile "\uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  grew up in elite white communities in South Africa, detached from apartheid atrocities and surrounded by anti-Black propaganda. He sees his takeover of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as a free speech win, but in his youth, did nothing -- did not suffer the effects of misinformation."\par \par \uc2 Greg, a lot there. As we think about his purchase here, the implications for free speech, but what about this New York Times piece?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's crazy because most people do not read beyond the hea