es this helped him in some ways?\par \par \uc2 You know, it's interesting that you asked that question because that's the first thing I thought when they started with the racist thing. We know, if the left wants to demonize you, they call you a racist. There's no word worse than a racist. It could mean a variety of things, but we all know it's bad.\par \par \uc2 So, when they started with him, and I read part of the article, and it said, you know that he's one of those free speech absolutist. And so, that's -- there's something wrong with one of the pillars upon which this country was started. But I must tell you, this is all an attempt to simply demonize the man. And you know what, my ears are closed. I'm done listening.\par \par \uc2 And his mother had the greatest answer of all. She said, now you're going to blame children for decisions made by a government? He grew up in South Africa where there was apartheid, but he went to his school with black schoolmates. And he spent time with his black friends. His father said that he was aware that there was something wrong with apartheid. I mean, he was a kid. Give me a break.\par \par \uc2 I'm done with them. I'm done with the hate. Let him prove himself through \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Let him do what -- give him a chance to run and win or whatever he's going to do. But I'm done with the hate.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: He seems to be two and O. He's got the battery, he's got people in space. What's your thinking, Kayleigh?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, he's two and O. Don't underestimate \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . Look, the points that you make, Judge and Greg about the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  article -- I have it here. They bury those points in paragraph nine, 11, and 13, as they always do. So, the headline in no way reflects the conclusion about Elon.\par \par \uc2 And the question I would have, it's ironic that this comes on a day where President Biden said, "Even back in the old days when we had real segregationists like James Ea