e talking points.\par \par \uc2 KONST: I did not say that, but I do believe that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Why do you hate women so much? I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I gotten something.\par \par \uc2 KONST: There is -- there is an expression here --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Why do I hate woman? Why do I hate woman?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, Nomiki, Nomiki --\par \par \uc2 KONST: -- about something that's been ruled on.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh, come on. Come on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nomiki, please.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Why do you respect this court more than the previous court? I mean, you just said --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nomiki, that's --\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh, please. I hate woman. I hate my mother, I hate my sisters. I hate woman, please.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nomiki --\par \par \uc2 KONST: OK, stop. I'm not the one angry right now. Look at the angry man.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Please, please.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Stop. OK, you know what? No -- choose --\par \par \uc2 KONST: Why are you so mad? Why are you so mad? It's not affecting your life. It's affecting my life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nomiki, Nomiki, stop. OK?\par \par \uc2 First of all, it's very insulting to say that someone who hates women. OK? There is nothing that Leo has ever said or done on this show that would indicate that he hates women. So, that's a completely unfair thing to say.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KONST: This is not -- this is (INAUDIBLE) other one?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the fact to the matter is -- no this well, it -- you know, there's a large percentage of people in this country who are supportive of this decision. 52 percent -- 54 percent believe that abortion should be banned after 15 weeks, a majority of those who were asked.\par \par \uc2 So, we just have to keep this -- we got to keep this conversation from degrading into, you hate women, or any other form of that. We have to be able to talk about it. And we hope there'll be peaceful protests this weekend, and that no one's church services will be disrupted, because that is certainly disrupting the peace. So, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to all of you for being here. The Governor Huckabee, Leo Terrell, and Nomiki Konst.\par \par \uc2 So, up next, the world's richest man, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is now calling out former President Trump's truth social today, calling it fake news that the former president encouraged him to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, who better to ask about this?\par \par \uc2 Then-Trump Media and Technology Group CEO Devin Nunez who will respond exclusively here.\par \par \uc2 And also, teacher's union boss \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  fesses up to what we have talked about here for two years, the devastating toll of zoom school on our children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: Our kids are in crisis, and we had a mental health crisis before COVID. But for two years of disruption, two years of looking at the screens, two years of not having a normal kind of routine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, so the question becomes what will she do for them now to catch them up, to get them where they need to be? Former Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  response.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So a new study provides proof of what many American families already knew. Remote school was not good for learning. Zoom school widened economic and racial inequality in learning. Kids fell way behind in critical math and reading skills that they need to succeed in the world. This comes from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and they -- it is based on a strong study that says that there is now, "generational learning loss," which is why \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , the president of one of the teacher -- largest teachers' unions in the country is now getting pushed back over these comments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: But for two years of disruption, two years of looking at the screens, two years of not having a normal kind of routine and rhythm. Recovery is really tough. And it's not a shock that a lot of kids, frankly, a lot of adults are still under tremendous stress and anxiety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In a moment, former Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 , but first to Senior Correspondent, Claudia Cowan on this new study. Hi, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. That's right. This new study would seem to confirm what many parents already knew that when zoom school lasted for months on end, kids learned a lot less. Researchers with Harvard Center for Education Policy, drilled down on two years' worth of standardized test scores to see how students fared before and after the pandemic. And compare the findings in districts where schools reopened relatively quickly, mostly in the south and Great Plains to parts of the country where classes remained online in the Northeast, Midwest and West Coast.\par \par \uc2 It found the extended lock downs frequently lobbied for by Teachers Unions had led to a learning loss generation. Just looking at a typical year of math. For instance, students who went back to the classroom lost about 20% worth of learning on average, but those who stayed home for most of that same time period lost the equivalent of about 50% of the math skills they would normally learn in a school year.\par \par \uc2 The study found a greater percentage of school closures happened in poor school districts, essentially widening education inequality, and now vulnerable communities are being impacted by enrollment loss along with learning loss.\par \par \uc2 In Oakland, for instance, seven schools are slated to close for good, sparking protests by parents and teachers and a complaint by the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  that the closures disproportionately affect black students and families.\par \par \uc2 Across the U.S., parents frustrated with the never ending lockdowns switch to private or charter schools or decided homeschooling worked best. Federal aid helped many public schools survived the pandemic, but that money has been spent. And now districts and families are scrambling for long term solutions, Martha, in particular to help millions of these kids catch up again. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a long road. Claudia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joined now by former Education Secretary, \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 . Betsy, always good to have you with us. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 I just want to at the top remind everybody about the other things that we learned, which was that there was a lot of cooperation between the Teachers Unions and the White House that there were meetings there, a lot of discussions about when these schools would be allowed to reopen. And here's the soundbite from \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  when she kept giving all the reasons why her -- the large public schools couldn't go back, while other schools were going back. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: There's no way that you're going to have full time school for all the kids and all the teachers the way we used to have it. And in doing this, not only this retrofitting for ventilation systems, but also for buying the damned masks for the cleaning equipment for the nurses that we're going to need. That's why we have been pushing really hard as to get the money that states need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So they really suffered a lot of these school systems. And other schools went back and they figure their way through all of those things, Betsy, and now we know what the damage is.\par \par \uc2 BETSY DEVOS, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: That's right, Martha. And it's just a shameful that for two years. \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  and her organization and the other teachers union bosses really declined to let kids get back into the classroom. They continued to demand things according to their agenda, not paying attention to the ills that were being imposed on so many kids. And we know as this study has, again, proven the most vulnerable kids are paying the price. It is shameful, and it there is no excuse for it. There were many schools many, many places that students did go back to school counterparts around the world did. And so, the solution frankly, is to put parents in charge of their kids' education as those who could afford it were able to do during the last two years. This is nothing but trying to gaslight the American people today, and you know, parents have a right to be furious with what's going on. They did not sign up to be co-parenting with the government.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, you know, in that story, Claudia Cowan, pointed out that seven schools in that district are shutting down. And we do see parents making their own choice, decisions about where they're going to send their kids, because they did see that the Catholic school was opened, the private school was open, and I'm thinking, why can't my child have that? I pay taxes, you know, I pay into this system. So my question now is what can be done, what can be pushed for from the Department of Education or from the White House to say, let's do right by these children, let's sit with them over the summer, let's make sure that they get caught up. There's not even -- it's not discussed.\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: And the solution, bottom line, is let the resources that are already being spent on each of those children, particularly children from low income families, let those resources be controlled by the parents and let them choose an a setting that's going to work. Let them choose a Catholic school, or another private school or homeschool or a micro school, whatever is best for their children. But the system, led by teachers union bosses have proven they can't do it, they aren't going to catch up, they were granted $190 billion. And they haven't even spent a fraction of that. But the what they're spending it on is not going to catch kids up because there'll be doing more of the same thing and expecting different results. And it won't work. Let parents decide where their kids are going to go to school and how they're going to get their education.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, imagine if they divided up that money and gave it to parents to allow them to make a choice. And also, they need to lift the lid on charter schools and allow them to form so that they can take their $11,000, $15,000, $17,000 that their tax money gives them further, per student and say this is my $15,000 and I want to use it this way this year to educate my child. I'm not telling you anything that you haven't said to us, many times, Betsy, but I hear you. Thank you very much. It's always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up on this story, Trump Media and Technology Group CEO \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  joins us. And there's this issue about fake news now coming from \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . So \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  is going to respond to it moments away because there was this sentiment, statement by him that Trump had encouraged Musk to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Musk says that's fake news. So what happened really? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEVIN NUNES\uc2 , TRUMP MEDIA & TECHNOLOGY GROUP CEO: We're all for it. President Trump, basically before \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought it actually said to go and buy it because, you know, the goal of our company is really to build a community where people are in a family, friendly, safe environment, you know, very much different than \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And that's why we encourage \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to buy it because someone has to continue to take on these tech tyrants.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  calling that fake news that President Trump encouraged him to do this $44 billion \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover. The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO tweeting today, this is false. I've had no communication directly or indirectly with Trump, who has publicly stated that he will be exclusively on Truth Social.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Trump Media &Technology Group CEO \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  responds to this back and forth. But first Fox Business Correspondent Kelly O'Grady. Hi, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yeah, there's been a lot of speculation around what motivated \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to initiate this \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover. He's of course been very vocal about his open Town Square vision, however, who motivated him is even more murky. So reports are surfacing which months it did not confirm or deny that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO was receiving behind the scenes encouragement from a group of influential billionaires, the group including former \uc2 PayPal\uc2  Execs \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  and \uc2 David Sacks\uc2 , investor \uc2 Steve Jurvetson\uc2 , and his brother \uc2 Kimbal Musk\uc2  and other key influences said to be Musk's friendship with \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2 , a vocal challenger to the platform's lack of clarity, since stepping down as its CEO who's called Elon "the singular solution for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Now, reportedly Musk was also growing extremely frustrated by the banning of key accounts and much of the conservative reaction to the bid expressing hopes of the billionaire reinstating former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The Truth Social founder, however, as you said confirmed he will not return to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  regardless of its ownership.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as you said at the top with the New York Post reporting Trump Media & Technology Group CEO \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  claimed the former president urged must've by it. The reports are surfacing that the article must denounce, took those comments out of context. And all this comes as the Truth Social SPAC Digital World Acquisitions Corp is trading down since Musk announced his investment in \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and sits in the high 140s on the app store down from 52 when Musk announced his takeover. It's unclear yet how Musk's acquisition will impact true social in the long run. But the comments section on Elon response to the New York Post article is all a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with jokes of his brush with fake news, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Kelly, thank you very much. So who better to bring in than \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , CEO of Trump Media & Technology Group and former House Intelligence Committee Chair, good to have you with us today. So we you saw that soundbite and in it, you were talking to \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  and you said that's why we encouraged \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to buy it. Because someone has to continue to take on these tech tyrants. So what was that relationship and who did -- was there any discussion directly between President Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about this purchase?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Well, thanks for having me on. There's nothing like a Friday being fake news Friday. I remember during all the Russia hoax controversy and those dark times. We always were waiting for the Friday afternoon fake news bombshell that would then we'd spend three days responding to, and this looks like it's nothing different, Martha.\par \par \uc2 We have publicly said both President Trump and myself, our goal with this company is quite clear. And I, matter of fact, I think I said this on your show last week, we want to open the Internet back up to give the American people and people around the world, their voice back very clearly. So it's not just that interview that I did with \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2 . It's probably three dozen interviews that I have done publicly, many on Fox, Fox Business. And in fact, President Trump was very, very clear before \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  actually bought the company that he likes Elon, he's all for it. He hopes he buys it. That was in a Fox News story.\par \par \uc2 So, look, we actually hope, and since, it's fake news Friday. And I hope they don't take these words out of context. But, you know, we would like it if he would buy \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and most importantly, probably Chinese owned TikTok. So there's a lot more things he could buy, and we'd be a hell of a lot better off with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  owning them, then the tech tyrants that own them now.\par \par \uc2 But with that said, I want to be very clear, Elon got two big issues here, that he has to deal with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . One is he has to make sure that, you know, is he going to be able to get the employees and everybody in line there? And two, is he really going to follow up on his promise. And I think that's the, you know, the key to our company is that we can't be canceled, everybody's safe with us. And that's why we're a growing company, one of the fastest ever in history, Truth Social, is because I think people know when they come to us, there's going to be no issue of us being cancelled by some big tech company.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Got it. So, just to be clear, because this is what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said, there -- you're confirming that there was never any direct or indirect discussion between the former President Trump and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  about the purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Yeah, that's right. And I don't know how anyone who could have got that story, any different. I don't think we've ever --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you were just talking in general terms about what you had said publicly, yeah.\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Yeah, very, very public. And in numerous interview, numerous interviews, so I'm not sure how you go from zero to 100, that somehow you take public statements that we clearly said, and you quickly move that into some realm of that we were having secret talks. I mean, it truly is, Martha. I'm not making fun. This is a fake news Friday bombshell.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Yeah, I mean, and it's just totally fake and fabricated. And I -- and look, and I'm a big fan of The New York Post. I mean, no one was a bigger defender. How the New York Post got censored. I mean, partly how I ended up in this job was because I was so ticked off about how the story by the New York Post, about the Hunter Biden laptop was hell, was clearly censored by \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , et cetera. So, you know, look, I just don't know how the story came about, but hopefully they'll fix it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he responded to it, and you responded to it. So that that seems pretty clear now.\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, to the statement that President Trump has said that he would exclusively be on Truth Social. And then \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said that he said, that's my understanding that the former president wants to be exclusively on Truth Social. If the president decides to run again, and is, you know, offered back a spot-on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Are you saying that he won't accept it?\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Well, look, we -- there's no reason for the president or myself to be back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . So anyone who has made the move, so content creators that have made the move from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and even from \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , which by the way, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  has hundreds of millions of Americans, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  does not. Anyone who's made the move and posts over at Truth Social, they're getting far more engagement, far more engagement, sometimes with a third of the followers getting 10 or 50 times the engagement. I mean, we see it every hour on the hour. And the difference is, is that we're not censoring number one. And two, we don't have fake bot accounts or fake accounts. So a lot of the numbers you see on these other platforms are inflated. When you go to Truth Social, we're 99.9% certain that it's a real person behind that account.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that -- you know what, I mean, that that's a really important part of this whole equation. And I know \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said that's exactly what he wants through new software and technology to make sure that they can eliminate those bots as well. So -- and you say you want to build a community where people can exchange ideas and do it openly and not have to worry about getting cancelled. I think everybody wants to be -- wants that as well. \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , thank you very much. Good to see you on next Friday. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 NUNES: Thanks, Martha. My pleasure. Have a good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. Thank you. You, too. So an exclusive sneak peek of my conversation with fancy like country star Walker Hayes after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Really excited about this, a brand new episode of The Untold Story podcast, country music star Walker Hayes talks with me about going from canceled record contracts and working at \uc2 Costco\uc2  to drinking too much and then finding God through an unlikely friendship. The new book is called, Glad You're Here. And it's co-authored with Craig Allen Cooper.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES, COUNTRY MUSIC STAR: I was dropped from two record labels by the time I met Craig. I had been dropped by two publishing companies by the time I met Craig. I'd really gotten accustomed to rejection. The gift that Craig gave us, made me document that confusion in my heart going why do these people love us so unconditionally? Like where does this come from? What does Craig have that I don't have?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great conversation that we had. And it's going to drop tomorrow, Saturday, which we don't usually do. They usually come out on Thursday. This is special. It's coming out on Saturday. So I hope you'll tune in for that. That is the story for this Friday. Story goes on. Have a great weekend. We'll see on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  to Leave White House Next Week; LA DA Not Charging Chappelle Attacker with Felony; New Bombshells in Durham Probe\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kayleigh McEnany, Joe Concha, Leo Terrell, Gregg  Jarrett, Jason Chaffetz \par \uc2 8,233 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 And tonight, it is official. The most arrogant, dishonest press secretary in modern American history, she will soon be gone. In a little over a week, chief propagandist circle back \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  will step down after reportedly negotiating a big new contract with NBC News, the former home of lying Brian. Anyway, I'm sure Psaki will feel right at home at the conspiracy channel network.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, we're going to give her a proper and very special send-off. Jen, I hope you'll circle back with us and watch later.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, a disturbing story out of Los Angeles. The city's far left district attorney is now refusing to pursue felony charges for the man that viciously assaulted comedian -- I like Dave Chappelle a lot. He's funny as hell. Anyway, but he attacked him while wielding a gun-shaped knife, a fake gun, a real knife, a gun that looked like a knife. We'll show you that in moments.\par \par \uc2 And like many liberal cities, L.A., happily there you can see, happily tolerates violent crime, no bail laws. Oh, let's see, defund, dismantle the police. Oh, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  praised the \uc2 LAPD\uc2  for defunding some of the \uc2 LAPD\uc2 . More straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 First, we do turn to the steps of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  now surrounded by a large chain-link fence with big cement barriers erected overnight to protect the building. Rightly so in my opinion. We need to protect every institution. every monument, every person that serves in this government.\par \par \uc2 This is the right thing to do. Hopefully Democrats have learned something from the 574 riots in the summer of 2020 and the Capitol breach in 2021. By the way still no answers as to why \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Mayor Muriel Bowser didn't use the up to 20,000 troops authorized, required by law by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  two days prior again as required by law. Why didn't they?\par \par \uc2 Now, many people on the left are very, very angry that individual states and voters could soon decide abortion law for their state, overturning Roe v. Wade all the talking points all the lying all the hysteria it's not true it will not outlaw abortion in the United States that is the big lie. That is being spread by the likes of \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  who himself threatened two justices, Justice Gorsuch and Kavanaugh, on the steps of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  screaming, you won't know what hit you -- among other threats.\par \par \uc2 And as we speak, they're threatening to burn our system to the ground. As per usual, Democrats are turning to their favorite political tactic. That's mob violence and intimidation.\par \par \uc2 Now, one liberal group is now organizing an effort to stalk and harass Supreme Court just