mer Speaker of the House and Fox News contributor, Newt Gingrich, author of the upcoming book we can order now on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , 'Defeating Big Government Socialism'.\par \par \uc2 Newt, it seems like people are really feeling the pain now of this, whatever we're going to call. It's not a recession yet. Feels like one to a lot of people. And seeing it for themselves, what liberalism does to America.\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH\uc2 , FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: Well, look, I think every place from going to the gas station to going to the grocery store, which by the way, by September will be a bigger story than gasoline.\par \par \uc2 Food prices are going to go up all summer. On a worldwide basis, they're going to be genuine crises, and hundreds of millions of people are going to be at risk in places like India, the Middle East, Sub Saharan Africa. I think people have no idea how bad the food situation is going to get by this summer, and how expensive it's going to be by September.\par \par \uc2 But in addition, if you've worked hard and saved all your life, I talked to somebody today who knows a great deal about the economy, who had just looked at his 401-K, and realized how much of a hammering they had taken in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 So if you're a retired person relying on a decent stock market, you've got a problem. If you are dealing with the challenge of gasoline, or jobs, because I think that what you and \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  talked about is exactly right. The longer they wait on inflation, the deeper the recession is going to be.\par \par \uc2 We lived through this with \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . It ended up getting called stagflation. And people added up the unemployment rate and the inflation rate and turned it into the misery index by adding the two numbers together. We have a grave danger of being worse off in another year or a year and a half than we were under \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's - this is so staggering, Newt. I mean people are like, Oh, you don't sleep enough. I actually stay up worrying about this, because I think of the people I grew up with, and I grew up as blue collar people. What their lives are right now, they're freaking out. They're really worried and upset and they have a reason to be and a right to be.\par \par \uc2 But, Newt, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair turned MSNBC darling, Michael Steele, he thinks Republicans should be the ones that are worried now. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and a number of leaders in Washington are very worried about what was once a really well paved yellow brick road to winning in November now pockmarked by the Supreme Court narrative, which will transform the playing field even further.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Newt, mixed metaphors aside, should Republicans be concerned?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, first of all, I think when you're on MSNBC, you sound very different than you might have in other conversations. No, I think Republicans have on rising crime rates, as you mentioned a minute ago, on the inflation rates, on the economy in general, on an open border with, frankly, a totally out of touch with reality Secretary of Homeland Security. Every time you turn around, this is an administration, whether it's on education, or it's on regulations, it's an astonishingly bad administration. And the effect is in people's lives.\par \par \uc2 This is the great problem for the Democrats in the news media. You can't say enough things to overcome the cost of gasoline, or the cost of food. In the real world, real people get up every morning, they know it's not working. And no matter what you tell them in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  or NBC, or what the President tries to tell them when he's coherent, which is less and less often, they just don't believe it.\par \par \uc2 And in fact it makes the Democrats look like they're out 