o fight Nazis who are committing genocide against Russian speaking people in the south and east, we're fighting United States and \uc2 NATO\uc2  who are trying to encroach on the security and stability of Russia. This has been a constant theme of his when conflict hasn't been taken.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Full on proxy war in his mind.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And it builds that big narrative to convince the Russian people that Russia was a victim.\par \par \uc2 The second thing, the United States policy. Now, listen, the administration is not going to agree with what I'm going to say. But I think actually, this is what has happened. There is a policy shift.\par \par \uc2 Initially, first couple of weeks, limited support for the Ukrainians in terms of arms and ammunition, very specific, it was more of a push system to them.\par \par \uc2 And based on very good sources that I had, the Biden administration was putting pressure on Zelenskyy to make a deal, take a settlement.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy and his troops did extraordinarily well. And as a result of that, world opinion was changing, public opinion of the United States suddenly very supportive of the Ukrainians. Zelenskyy, one of a kind figure, captured everybody emotionally and psychologically.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration began to shift to help in Zelenskyy defeat the Russians inside of Ukraine actually using the word to attain victory, and then went all out to get ammunition and supplies not just out of the United States, depots, but organized 40 other countries that do much the same.\par \par \uc2 So, the policy, it has shifted.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, why do you think the offensive for the Russians has stalled again, you know, they're putting all this firepower and they're putting all these people in there. And yet, the Ukrainians are repelling in a number of different cities.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, I'll tell you, Bret, looking at it now, after this much time a couple of months, there's systemic problems there that they cannot overcome.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Russia?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: First of all -- yes, Russians. First of all, their generals, they cannot put together a competent campaign plan that's sensible. And they're people are capable of executing properly within their limitations, overly ambitious, again, they're conducting an attack in the Donbass on four separate axis, once again, which they did, entering the country at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is command and control. This centralized old Soviet model of command and control doesn't work well on a fluid battlefield with all the technology that we have on the battlefield today.\par \par \uc2 So what people don't understand, the American military and we're not fighting a battle like that, we put a plan together. And then, our subordinate leaders at the junior level make decisions based on what the enemy is doing. They don't have to ask permission, they just act because they're in a difficult situation.\par \par \uc2 The other thing, they reorganized the Russian military entities, battalion tactical groups that everybody has been hearing about. And doing that, they gave away the regimental brigade headquarters above them, we normally control about five of them, and they're gone.\par \par \uc2 So, they fight these units individually, with no one, providing reconnaissance for them. They don't have the artillery that they should have. And they never mash them together because they don't have the capability to do it, their logistics has failed miserably again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, General, the question I get time and time again, is how does this end? And how does Putin have an off ramp in this thing?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, May 9th may tell us a little bit because that's a celebratory day in Russia of World War II to defeat Nazism. He could begin to take a victory on that day based on the territory owns Mariupol, that is why he's attacking Mariupol. He's wanting to take 