t of Public Safety\uc2 . Troopers in Brownsville chased the truck until it plunges down embankment and splashes into the Rio Grande.\par \par \uc2 Texas DPS troopers confiscate 486 pounds of marijuana. But the driver gets away swimming across the river back into Mexico. With scenes like these, even members of the Biden administration worry more migrants will come if Title 42 ends as scheduled on May 23rd.\par \par \uc2 BLAS NUNEZ-NETO, U.S. CUSTOMS AND BORDER PROTECTION: We must acknowledge that our immigration system and particularly our asylum system are outdated and were not built to contend with the populations and volumes that we are now seeing at our border.\par \par \uc2 FOY: On Capitol Hill today, a Senate committee hearing this warning from the assistant secretary at the \uc2 Administration for Children and Families\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANUARY CONTRERAS, HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES ASSISTANT SECRETARY: We currently have 8,390 children in our care. However, we are planning and preparing for any potential increase in referrals that might result from the termination of Title 42 for adults and family units.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Family units continue crossing the Rio Grande into Roma, Texas, including this group of roughly 20 overnight. Badly needed humanitarian support is coming mostly from charities.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR MICHELLE SILVA, BETHEL MISSION OUTREACH CENTER: It's just crazy. You've got hundreds of people crossing the river, and they've got to process all these people. And so we are just in between to help bring calm and everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, here in Roma, we're told hundreds of migrants cross each and every night. And to give you some perspective, there is a single bus that leaves town every day at 10:00 in the morning. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nate Foy in Roma, Texas. Nate, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at some of the stories beyond our borders tonight. A thick sandstorm blankets Iraq, sending hundreds of people to hospitals with breathing complications. Flight at Baghdad airport suspended for several hours. It was the fifth sandstorm to engulf Iraq within a month.\par \par \uc2 And Hong Kong beaches and pools reopen following Beijing's announcement to ease some of its tough quarantine rules for arrivals from overseas. China has maintained its hardline zero COVID approach but has imposed less burdensome restrictions in Beijing than in other cities such as Shanghai, where millions were placed under strict lockdown.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. When we come back, Daniel Krauthammer joins us to talk about his late father, as you look at the green room there. And we introduce you to this year's winners of the annual Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Memorial Scholarship.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: News, by its nature, is an immediate enterprise, getting it fast for today's audiences. But our late colleague \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  in many appearances over the years on this program was often more focused on the long term, how what happens now will affect future generations. In that spirit, we are so proud here at FOX News to continue honoring his memory by announcing the Grace LaCroy and Emmett Gaffney are the recipients of this year's Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  Memorial Scholarship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some people were born to be journalists. Others find their way through a keen intellect, endless curiosity, and the desire to carve new paths in life. That was \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER\uc2 , SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: You are betraying your whole life if you don't say what you think, and you don't say it honestly and bluntly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: His early ambition was to be a psychiatrist. And despite a diving accident that left him paralyzed, Charles graduated at the top of his class at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 . The journey took him to the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: I thought once I'm in Washington, isn't that where they do politics? One thing will lead to another.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles' regular appearances on our show were a treasured celebration of the power of idea was. His views cut through the noise, Poignant one minute, pointed the next. It was analysis seasoned with experience and a clear-eyed sense of the possible. Critical of political pomposity on the left and right.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: The biggest error that we make is to lose the damn war because we refuse to recognize hot enemy is.\par \par \uc2 If you're a conservative, you should be optimistic.\par \par \uc2 That was the dog that didn't bark.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just a few months ago we inaugurated our redesigned Washington offices and dedicated our new green room to him, a fitting tribute since he often held court in there with guests and younger journalists. After his 2018 passing, we also created a college scholarship for the children of FOX News employees working in partnership with the National Merit Scholarship program.\par \par \uc2 This year's recipients, Emmett Gaffney of New Jersey, whose father Brian heads our documentary unit. Emmett is the editor of his high school newspaper and a pitcher on the baseball team. He hopes to study history in college in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT GAFFNEY, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP RECIPIENT: Part of that intrigue in that is that I don't exactly know what I want to do, I can kind of explore those interests, hopefully, in college, and by this time in about four years, hopefully I will have something lined up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Grace LaCroy of Maryland. Her parents, Lillian and Phil LaCroy, work in our Washington bureau. Grace is an accomplished dancer and a volunteer in her community. She hopes to study psychiatry or speech therapy in college in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRACE LACROY, KRAUTHAMMER SCHOLARSHIP RECIPIENT: Seeing him overcome that much is really going to push me getting this award in his name do my very best in school just to honor his legacy of always helping people and putting so much effort and hard work into everything he does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congratulations. Charles said his father inspired him always to know everything, learn everything. Passing that gift onto the next generation of thought leaders through this memorial scholarship, what better way to honor the memory of Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Daniel Krauthammer, Charles' son, himself a writer. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KRAUTHAMMER, SON: So good to see you too, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's such a great time when we do this every year now, and to see Emmett choose history, at least so far, and Grace choose psychiatry, both things that your dad just loved.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Yes. No, it's incredibly appropriate, I think, in so many ways. My father was so passionate about learning. Not only that, but saw it as a duty, that that's what everyone should do is take it to the fullest. And the fact that Emmett is following history and Grace into psychiatry, I couldn't think of two subjects closer to my father's heart.\par \par \uc2 He would joke sometimes when young students would ask him, how do you get to this point of being a renowned journalist? He's say, well, first you go to medical school.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: But in all seriousness, he thought that that education really taught him something about what matters in life. He saw human suffering. He saw what makes life worth living. And then it was history, I think, that took him out of the hospital. He saw what could put all those things that mattered at risk, which is politics gone wrong. And I think he would be very proud for these two rising students to be following those paths, and to continue the kind of passion and commitment to learning that he put his life towards.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've talked about this. Your father's words cut through the noise here on the panel. And we often find ourselves going back into our archives for things that are happening now to listen to your dad's wisdom on different things. Here he is on Ukraine policy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES KRAUTHAMMER\uc2 , SYNDICATED COLUMNIST: Putin wants the Crimean Peninsula. He has got it. We have to make it plain that he has got to stop where he is.\par \par \uc2 Russia sees America's shrinking defense budget to the point where it becomes irrelevant. If you get Obama, of all people, turning it around, that's a long-term price that the Russian also regret.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He wrote in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," "Obama's foreign policy of denial. No one is claiming that arming Ukraine would have definitively deterred Putin's current actions. But the possibility of a bloody and prolonged Ukrainian resistance to infiltration or invasion would surely alter Putin's calculus more than Obama's toothless sanctions or of empty diplomatic gestures like the preposterous Geneva agreement that wasn't worth the paper it was written on." I walk into people every day who say, man, I just want to know what Charles would think about this moment. I'm guessing that that's pretty close.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: I think. I think about this all the time. I have gone back and read so much of what he wrote in his books, in his columns. And it was, unfortunately, incredibly prescient, that he knew history, he learned the lessons of history. And he saw that when you let a tyrant push and push and you don't push back, they take more.\par \par \uc2 And if look at his writings from 2014 when Putin first took Crimea, back in 2008 even, when he went into Georgia, even all the way back 2007 and 2000 when Putin was just uttering his goals, my father saw it incredible clearly. And he knew the lessons of history going back to the 20th century to the 30s and before that if you don't have a strong free world led by America to push back and uphold the sanctity of the laws we put in place to keep the world peaceful, it instead gets dominated by the likes of Putin and Xi and Iran. And unfortunately, I think, if we had done much of what my father argued years ago we might not be where we are today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, we miss him every day, his words. Please give our best to your mom. I do potentially is a seat at the panel if you ever feel like.\par \par \uc2 KRAUTHAMMER: Thank you, Bret. I would love. We are dealing with a lot in my family. But my mother and I want to thank you and \uc2 Suzanne Scott\uc2  and everyone at FOX for continuing my father's legacy with this scholarship. And we wish all the greatest luck and our congratulations to Emmett and Grace. And we are very excited to see what they do with these paths of learning they have embarked on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's great to see you, too. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on how today's tanking stock markets and tomorrow's job report could be bad omens potentially for Democrats this November.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): The problem is that this government has acted too slow too often. They created the inflation. Now they want to have credit for solving the problem when in fact the problem may actually exacerbate the situation. The Fed does not have enough tools in the toolkit for the economy that we have today.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT NARDELLI\uc2 , FORMER \uc2 HOME DEPOT\uc2  CEO: When they were talking about inflation being transitory, it's like standing outside looking at a tornado and predicting it's not going to come at you. We are already in a recession. So they have to deflect all of the things that are not going so well for this administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We need some Dramamine for the stock markets, and there's some concerns about the U.S. economy long term. "Politico" writes it this way, "Inflation much too high. Powell puts the hammer on price spikes. Rates this year could reach their highest level since before the 2008 Wall Street crash if surging prices continue. That prospect has sent stocks sliding this year and pushed mortgage rates above five percent for the first time in a decade."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters, and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen. Marc, for people looking at the economy, listening to the administration talk about it, there is a disconnect.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Very much so. And first of all, I just miss Krauthammer. And I wish I had gone to medical school so I could be better at this.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Inflation was already eating away everybody's paycheck, and now inflation and the stock market are eating away at their retirement savings. And for this president the list of litany of worsts keeps getting worser. We already have the worst inflation in 40 years, worst gas prices on record, worst crimewave since the 1990s, worst border crisis in American history. Now we can add to that the worst labor shortage in American history, 11.5 record million unfilled jobs in this country, worst mortgage rates in 13 years and worst productivity since 1947. And we're on the cusp of a recession to put a cherry on top. But this is a bad situation for this president, and he is not getting any good news.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff, to that point, in our new FOX News poll, "Compared to two years ago, your family financial situation is," there's that word again, "worse, 44 percent." That is the headwinds that Democrats face as they go to November.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "REUTERS": \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  as governor when he was running for president said it's the economy, stupid. I think that phrase still applies in virtually every political campaign since. And it will certainly apply this year and it will apply in 2024. It's a challenge. It's a big challenge for this president. Inflation is not something that he can just bring down unilaterally. He likes to blame the war in Ukraine and COVID, and I think that economists would back him up in saying that those are certainly having an impact. But he owns the economy. He owns the governing right now. And it's certainly something that Republicans are going to hammer him about in the midterms and after that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy, we talk a lot about different issues, and obviously the abortion issue rose to the top this week with that leaked draft opinion. But it really is the economy.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Right. And we have a very short attention span collectively in our society these days. What you are not going to lose sight of how much you are paying every single day for gas, for groceries, and that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 And to your point about abortion, which is clearly at the forefront of our discussion now, I think the Democrats who might be banking on that to be sort of the savior issue for them, might be some wish casting, some wishful thinking there looking at the new FOX News polling. Yes, most people support retaining Roe versus Wade but also support pretty substantial restrictions on abortion that Democrats typically don't want to talk about. Hence your exchange yesterday with Congressman from Ohio who did not want to really tackle that question that you put to him because it's thorny for the other side of this equation as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of the races we're following closely is the Pennsylvania Senate race. Let's take a listen to the debate from last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have been America first my entire life.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: President Trump endorsed me, calling me America first. He knows what you should know, which is that I love this country.\par \par \uc2 KATHY BARNETTE, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: People naturally pivot toward Democrats who won't solve your problem, but they will cut you a check. And that is how we're on this path where we find ourselves on the road to serfdom. So we need to get America first.\par \par \uc2 CARLA SANDS, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: We know we want to send an America first senator to Washington, not a squish, because we know that's how we are losing our country to the radical left.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: After J.D. Vance's win, you can't get more Trumpy on that stage, I don't think, Marc. This is your race. I asked everyone to pick one. What do you think.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": In the prism of the Ohio Senate race, if I was David McCormick I would be sweating. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  picked somebody who was not a leading candidate, who was in third or fourth place with 10 percent of the vote, and catapulted him over the top into first place. Before Trump -- before Trump's endorsement McCormick was leading Oz by nine points in the March 3 FOX News poll. He is now up 2.5 percent in the RCP average ahead of him. So, it's not the 23 points that J.D. Vance got, but it's a bump. And in a divided field, it could be the decisive factor.\par \par \uc2 MASON: It's worth noting that McCormick is supported by a bunch of former president Trump's former advisers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Including his wife. Jeff, your race?\par \par \uc2 MASON: My race is the Georgia governor's race. Also interesting with the Trump angle, the former president is supporting Senator Perdue against Governor Kemp. Governor Kemp, however, is really way ahead in the polls, both in the primary and in election, or for the general against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . He has got a lot of money. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  also has a lot of money, although she is spending a lot of money. And former Senator Perdue apparently is considering dipping into his own fortune to make up for that cash change. And J.D. Vance 32 percent, Perdue 32 percent. The difference is the field isn't divided.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's right. Your race, Guy?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I'm looking ahead to the general. I'm looking at the Florida governor's race. I don't think that the outcome is necessarily in doubt. I think the incumbent there is in pretty good shape, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . But the margin there could be very interest in a general election statewide, as we start to, dare I say, look even further past 2022. I got my eye on this one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You think DeSantis is lays the groundwork?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I think he might be. I think he is building a reputation and a record. And a big win down in Florida could really put some wind in his sails.\par \par \uc2 MASON: He's absolutely laying the groundwork.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You see the evidence stacking up. And can he do that with former president Trump lurking in Florida.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's Trumpism without the baggage. He is a counterpuncher, but without all the negative.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On this Cinco de Mayo, Deputy Carlos Mutti is spreading some mariachi happiness. The San Diego County sheriff's deputy immigrated with his family to the U.S. from Mexico in 1996. Deputy Mutti says mariachi music lets him give back to the people and the Hispanic heritage in his community. Happy Cinco de Mayo.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the politics of energy and inflation. We'll talk to an industry insider.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Judge Jeanine Pirro hosts JESSE WATTERS PRIME TIME right now. Hey, Judge.\par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Fencing Around SCOTUS Amid Abortion Protests; Supreme Court Launches Probe Of Egregious Leak; DHS: Wasn't Aware OF Jankowics Laptop Comment; Fox At The Border As Migrant Crossing Continue\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Pete Hegseth, David Spunt, Nate Foy, Kelly  O'Grady, David Asman, Jonathan Hunt \par \uc2 8,747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Again, today, what will tomorrow hold? It could be anybody's guess because the market seems to be looking for both good news and bad news.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: And there's the White House briefing room, big questions for that White House right now especially about the Supreme Court justices getting doxed. What will they say? Keep watching. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much to both of you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And it appears that the strategy is sort of this blame President Trump and his supporters. That's what's emerging as Democrats and President Biden in particular, struggle under the weight of some problems that could be too complex to fix in the short term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me tell you about this ultra-MAGA agenda, it's extreme as most mega things are. This mega crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Trump responded to that to Fox just a short time ago saying that in his opinion, MAGA is saving the country. This editorial claims that President Biden might just be trying to do some distracting here. It says Biden's bid to paint the Supreme Court draft opinion as the first salvo in a wholesale remaking of America is just a desperate attempt to change the subject from soaring inflation, a virtually open border and rising crime, the problems every poll shows the public cares about most.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is the world that we live in right now, folks, at the highest court in the land. They are putting up fencing that is deemed to be tall and non-scalable, around our highest court and a group called, Ruth Sent Us, is planning some protests outside the homes of those that they call the six extremist judges, justices, I should say. Justice Alito, who wrote the draft opinion has just dropped out of a public appearance that he was about to make at a judicial conference. Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , who wants clerked at the Supreme Court is standing by and we will speak with him in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, Pete Hegseth, Co-Host of "FOX AND FRIENDS" Weekend; and Richard Goodstein, Democratic Strategist and Former Adviser to Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , both here to take this on. So, Richard, let me start with you. So, you see that editorial in the posts basically saying the president's trying to distract everybody by calling everyone who wouldn't vote for him, extremist -- the most radical political organization in recent history, is that a wise move for someone who was going to bring the country together?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GOODSTEIN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, first of all, I don't think that's exactly what he's trying to do. He -- look, he's got his economic numbers, best job creation in history last year, most small businesses created in history last year, biggest drop in the deficit in history last year, and he's basically trying to call out the theory that was behind this draft Supreme Court decision, which would not just ban abortion, but ban same sex marriage, and would ban use of contraceptives --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Who would said that of any of those things?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That those things aren't in that opinion at all.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: No, but what Justice Alito says is that the privacy is not something that is found in the Constitution, and that's what those Supreme Court decisions for the right of contraceptives are predicated on. So, that's what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to call it out, in addition to banning books, and in other, and basically the --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Don't you think that's going, don't you think that's kind of getting a little crazy here?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Well --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, based on what's in this opinion, when you actually read it, don't you think that's a little much?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Absolutely not. Look, under this opinion, the rapist can sue the rape victim for terminating the pregnancy that he caused. Tell me if that's not totally messed up?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's insane. That's in Justice Alito's opinion? You read that specifically in the opinion?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: No, but what he said is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, then, I would say that you are extrapolating in a way that is unfair.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: No, no -- no, he, the opinion says, if a state has a reason to pass a law, it's a pretty low bar that they're asking, like the state of Texas, to clear and that's the law the state of Texas will pass.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Let's just -- Pete, so this opinion, says that it should, it's not a federal decision, it's a state decision for abortion rights. That's what it says. This is actually something that President Biden was a big proponent of when he was a senator in the United States. He said, you know, Roe v. Wade goes too far. I think this is a decision that should be at the state level. So, what do you make of the larger political effort here to sort of, you know, call people who are MAGA, it's, the most the biggest extremists in the history of American politics?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: What Richard did was build up a giant straw man which is a distraction from exactly what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said. The most extreme movement in American history, he didn't say modern American history, we can -- plenty of extremism there on the left is the MAGA folks. I'm a MAGA folk, am I extreme? Are my parents extreme? Are my high school buddies extreme? Are the guys who work for a living believe borders should be strong, the cops are the good guys, are they extreme?\par \par \uc2 Because that's exactly what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying. And when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have stood up and said you know what, they're not coming after me, they're actually coming after you. I'm just in the way. That's what people hear and that's what they see when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that, because you've actually, if you actually read the draft opinion that was illegally leaked, likely by a progressive.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: I disagree with that, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: You don't know? We shall see. If you, if you actually read it, it is narrowly tailored. And so instead, what the left always does is goes to the extreme examples. We've heard a Democratic politician, prominent one say, the conservatives want to ban biracial marriage. Conservatives don't want gay students to be in classrooms with straight students. That's an absolute, absolute to quote \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , "malarkey." There's nothing to it at all in that opinion, yet you want to fight a straw man, because you just built, you just rattle off some statistics, talk to people who live in America, whether or not the economy right now is better for them. Whether or not --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you -- you know what, I appreciate what both of you are saying here. But I want to -- the question that I actually pose to you is, is it wise? Is it politically wise? Because you've got a lot of people who live in the suburbs, got a lot of independent voters like 70 percent, who are -- who have a disapproval for this president? So, is it wise to do sort of the basket of deplorable, deplorables plan here? Is that smart on the part of this president?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: So, that's not what he said at all. He said, you know, the police are the good guys. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  watched the police get their heads bashed in.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Here we go, back to January 6th. Stop it, we got to the talking points.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: And did nothing on January 6th. And if you disagree, take it up with all the jurors that have found them guilty. If you believe --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Did they say a thing about the rioters, at the BLM protest in 2000. How many of those got locked up?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: And they burned a Biden Headquarter too. So, to lay them at the feet of Democrat is nonsense. OK.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: You're going to lay them in the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: So, the fact of the matter is, Martha, to answer your question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is it politically wise to alienate half the country with that comment, is that wise?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: He's not, he's not accusing those Independents, who don't want to ban abortion, who don't want to raise taxes on tens of millions of people like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 's program does.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Oh, yes, Republicans wants to raise taxes, right.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Who doesn't want to end, who doesn't want to end Medicare and Social Security, like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , a Senate leader does. That's not what those people, those independents, those suburban men and women believe in, they don't believe that abortion should be off the table, that the rapist can sue the victim.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that isn't what the opinion says and I'm not going to let you get away with saying that because the fact of the matter is, that what the opinion says, and I, it bothers me, because, you know, each state will get to decide, which means that the voter goes to the poll and says, you know, how do you vote on the abortion referendum? And I can say, this way or that way, and a bunch of people who work at the Supreme Court don't get to decide. I get to decide for my own self, and for my own state with my vote.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Right. Over a dozen states have already basically passed laws saying no exceptions. Health of the mother, nothing, no rape, no incest, no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (INAUDIBLE) have to approved by the people of the states through vote.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: That's already been done. They've already passed those laws --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: The people voted for those --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: pending Roe being overruled.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: The people voted for those representatives. The people for those - - you're the party that runs around yelling democracy dies in darkness, democracy is the people voting, the closest voting to the people is the most effective for.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: And Martha's question was, is that a way to reach independence? And the answer is, they are freaked out at the notion that those laws could become laws.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: You're the party of defund the police, you're the party of open borders, you're the party of rejecting --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: And you're the party of bashing in police in the capital grounds, OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. OK. OK. No, no, no, you know what? That's not, it's not fair. Nobody wants to -- stop, stop, OK. We're like normal people, OK? We don't want, nobody wants to see a policeman's head bashed in. Whether it's at the Capitol -- I don't want to see police officers in Minneapolis chased out of their own precinct and driven away from their own precinct. I don't want to see that either. So, you know, we, we have a fair forum here. So, that that's the way we're looking at, but I'm going to, I'm going to keep that because I want to see if you're right, OK.\par \par \uc2 When it comes down to vote, I want to see what Independents actually do. And that's what's going to be so fascinating about watching this whole midterm process, because how do they feel? What's more important to them? Inflation or what is happening at Supreme Court? And we will see, we will see what they decide. So, we thank you for being here. We like to have panels that have both sides on them, Richard, so we do appreciate.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Of course. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Peter, as always, thank you. So, while president, the president has called the supporters of state decided abortion law extremists, he would have to wear that label himself for his own view on this matter. As a senator, granted it was long time ago, but I think it's fair to show what he said. He said, "I don't like the Supreme Court decision on abortion. I think it went too far. I don't think that a woman has the sole right to say what should happen to her body."\par \par \uc2 Then, in 1982, President Biden then Senator supported an amendment to let the states overturn Roe v. Wade. He was the only Democrat who voted with Republicans on this. In defending his vote, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  wrote at the time Biden noted that he is a Roman Catholic and said I'm probably a victim or a product, however you want to phrase it, of my background, but he basically said his religious beliefs led him to that conclusion that it would be better for it to go to the States. Now, he says this, which has alarmed some pro-choice advocates yesterday, because he used the word child.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The idea that we're going to make a judgment that we're going to say that no one can make the judgment to abort a child based on a decision by the Supreme Court, I think goes way overboard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a little tough to hear that. But we have the words on the bottom, so I hope you could -- you could put it together. He said, No one should impact the decision of whether or not to abort a child.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  from Missouri, a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , he once clerked for Chief Justice Roberts. So, we have a few things that we want to get to with you, Senator, and thank you very much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, people change their opinions over time, this happens in the world. And it appears that President Biden changed his opinion on this. And you know, you can give latitude for that. But it, it's fair, I think, to point out what the prior opinion was.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Well, and why would now he want to force his opinion on everybody else in America? That's the real question, Martha, because that's what he wants with Roe, he wants to say that there's only one view on abortion, and the Supreme Court is going to control it. And he, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to dictate it. Here -- this is what would happen. If this opinion actually becomes the decision of the court, it would mean that the people get to decide what the abortion laws should be, what the laws protecting life should be in their states. And that's what this is about. That's democracy. And that's what the constitution gives to the people has the right to control this issue and to make up their own minds.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's interesting to note in our polls, 54 percent said that they believe that a 15-week ban is fair, that that was the line that they would say is fair, that's more than half a majority in that polling, just to point that out. And 15 weeks is the number that the Mississippi case was, was all about. So just to mention that. Now, you clerked for Justice Roberts, you see the fences that are going up around the Supreme Court, justices canceling their speaking, public speaking engagements and getting doxed in terms of where they live. What do you want to say about that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Oh, I want to say that this is a threat to judicial independence. I mean, this leak, and I don't know who did it. But I know the leftists are celebrating it. And what they're trying to use it to do, Martha, is to pressure the Supreme Court into changing its opinion, into changing its deliberations. They're trying to undermine the independence and integrity of the court, and to go and threaten justice his families and publish their home addresses and say, we're going to go protest there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Justice Barrett has small children at home. That's wrong. It's disgusting. Unfortunately, it's what these far-left, left activists have become. That's what they do all the time. So, I think it's reprehensible. I think the leak is reprehensible, and I think the attempt to use it to undermine the integrity and independence of the court is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's what the former Attorney General Bill Barr said, when he spoke with us about it yesterday, let's play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, I hope they build it while they're in jail. Because this is a grave and unforgivable sin. And I think it is likely they're going to find out who leaked that, it's a relatively small group of people that could possibly have done that. And I think the full weight of the law has to be brought against them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you agree with him there?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: I do think that if there has been a crime committed Martha, then absolutely, there should be prosecution. If this was a lawyer, by the way, and I suspect it probably was, given who has access, they should be disbarred. Of course, they should be fired. And we'll figure it out. We'll figure out what the circumstances were. But if there were criminal acts committed, of course, they ought to be prosecuted. This is, this is dangerous, and we've got to make sure it doesn't happen again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Chief Justice Roberts just called this leak absolutely appalling. And we know that the marshal at the Supreme Court is the person in charge of it. But yesterday and we're waiting for \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  to come out moments from now, but yesterday she was asked, you know, does the White House object to this leak? Do they believe that it's wrong? And she didn't take that head on? What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: You know, of course, they don't think it's wrong. They're, they're loving this. They're celebrating it because they're trying to use it against the court. They're -- I mean, right now, on Capitol Hill, you've got senators who are threatening the justices who say, we're going to pack the court, threatening to hold hearings, not to hold hearings on the security of the court -- to hold hearings about how they can pressure this court and try to get it to change. I mean, listen, if that's going to be how it is, we may as will elect the justices. If it's going to be referenda on every different decision? I mean, what should happen here is the court should be able to do its job, and the left should quit trying to interfere.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, in the right-hand side of the screen, we have those fences all around. And I think it's dismaying for every American To see the fences that we saw around the Capitol Building and to see these fences now that we're seeing around the Supreme Court. Senator Hawley, thank you. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, Congressman Tony Gonzalez of Texas wants to know why the president wants to release a dam of new migrants on his -- what he says is already overflowing community.\par \par \uc2 And is the U.S. Intel -- is U.S. intelligence making targets of Russian generals?\par \par \uc2 Former deputy supreme commander of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , General Sir Richard Shirreff on that, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Is uniformly unhelpful and irresponsible for the Russians to regard and speak to boast about their nuclear weaponry.\par \par \uc2 There is no reason to bring it to that level.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we know what happened in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): And now, the question is the east, and is Ukraine now winning the war again, in this region.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): As Russian forces struggle in that part of the country, it appears that Ukrainian forces are succeeding in taking back some strategic towns around Kharkiv, which is the second largest city in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Having already saved Kyiv from the Russians, which was obviously an enormous victory for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, could these victories cut off the key supply lines in the Russian effort to take Donbas, which obviously would frustrate them in a very large way in their efforts?\par \par \uc2 And how about this question. Is the United States giving the Ukrainians intelligence and weapons that are winnowing the field of Russian generals? The US will not confirm the number of Russian generals that have been taken out. But the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports, "The smaller version of the switchblade drone, which is now arriving on the battlefield can be used to identify and kill individual soldiers, and could take out a general, sitting in his vehicle or giving orders on the front line." Stunning.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, the Pentagon responded to that report just moments ago. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I won't go any further than what I've said before, certainly, no further than I went today. We do provide them useful intelligence, timely intelligence that allows them to make decisions to better defend themselves against this invasion. And, and I think the less said about that, honestly, the better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, we bring in retired General Sir Richard Shirreff, former deputy Supreme Allied Commander of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sir, good to have you with us. And nice to have you in New York. Welcome.\par \par \uc2 GENERAL SIR RICHARD SHIRREFF, FORMER NATO DEPUTY SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: It's good to be here. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with regard to that report, I mean, I think it's pretty clear why the Pentagon doesn't want to elaborate too much on how much our intelligence is being used to take out Russian generals with this pretty astonishing equipment ability. What do you think about it?\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Well, I share the view of the Pentagon spokesman that clearly, it's not in the interest of the Ukrainians, or the soldiers fighting that battle to be too open about the details of the intelligence. Suffice it to say that the United States and other allies are giving Ukraine huge support and that