e, to all of the people in Russia, were just taking out Nazis, a new version of Nazis that exists in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And now, apparently, Putin has apologized for Lavrov's remark with regard to that. Why do you think he would do that?\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Well, I suspect he is looking at the impact of Lavrov's remarks on a country that has thus far stayed pretty much neutral in this, which is Israel, of course.\par \par \uc2 Because Israel has a lot of citizens who originated in Ukraine and citizens who originated in Russia.\par \par \uc2 And Lavrov's remarks, which are, frankly disgusting, will have -- will have really hit home in Israel. And Putin realizes he can't afford to alienate more nations, more countries than he already has done.\par \par \uc2 But nevertheless, honeyed words from Putin do not hide the fact that his forces, his troops are committing unspeakable atrocities in Ukraine. And we should all never forget that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sir Richard Sherriff. Thank you very much, sir. Very good to have you in New York.\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Great.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, happening right now, another story that we're keeping an eye on here, the actress Amber Heard giving some more dramatic testimony against ex-husband Johnny Depp, accusing him of slapping her, shoving her and kicking her in front of other people on an airplane. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did you bleed from the vagina as well?\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD, AMERICAN FILM ACTRESS: We did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And did you experience any pain later?\par \par \uc2 HEARD: I didn't -- I didn't. I wasn't thinking about that. I was heartbroken. Eventually, I realized that I could be hurt because I was bleeding.\par \par \uc2 But I convinced myself it wasn't broken and that, that -- I that the bottle wasn't broken, or else it would be a lot worse, and the discomfort I was feeling afterwards just paled in comparison to how scared, shocked I was. Scared I just married this man. I just married him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And forgive me for asking this, but I need to just make sure the record is clear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): OK, we're watching this very disturbing testimony of what happened according to Amber Heard between these two individuals. And it just gets, you know, sort of more unbelievable with each thing that we learn here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Correspondent David Spunt, reporting live from the courthouse in Fairfax, Virginia. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Disturbing and incredibly emotional for Amber Heard. She's been on the stand all day today. She was on for three hours yesterday.\par \par \uc2 After today, there is no court tomorrow and no court all next week. Court is in recess. So, the jurors will have about 10 days or so to think, specifically about what she said today. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEARD: At some point, Johnny and I are in a struggle in the living room, and he kind of like, shoves me down on the sofa, and I get up, and I'm trying to get him off of me, and he just whacks me in the face.\par \par \uc2 I remember my nose being swollen, discolored, red, and I took a picture of my face.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Heard said she was a bruised and battered spouse. She told the jury again about what she says were multiple episodes of violence as a result of Depp's temper. During her testimony, Depp continued to look down and continues to look down instead of at the witness stand just feet in front of him.\par \par \uc2 The actor is suing Heard for defamation $50 million, Martha, over a December 2018 \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  op-ed that she authored describing herself as a victim of domestic violence.\par \par \uc2 Depp's name, though was never actually mentioned. He was not arrested or charged as a result of the abuse claims. He denies hitting her. And remember we cannot say this enough, this is a libel trial, meaning, the burden is on Johnny Depp to prove that Amber Heard engaged in actual malice, meaning that she demonstrated a reckless disregard for the truth in her op-ed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): It's a high bar to prove. However, Depp's attorneys, they ultimately believe that he will continue -- convince the jurors, and he will prove it, Amber Heard though, is telling an incredibly emotional story. And Heard team says that her words speak for themselves. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you. David Spunt in Fairfax, Virginia. And a poll out this week is, is tough news really for Democrats because it reveals that a majority of Hispanic voters want Republicans to manage the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Republican Congressman Tony Gonzalez on that. And new calls to impeach the DHS Secretary Mayorkas after a very volatile hearing that just took place on Capitol Hill. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): When the department picked her, did it know that she had said that Mr. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop is Russian disinformation?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: Senator, let me repeat myself and add one other fact, I was not aware of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas talking about the woman who's now running the department's new Disinformation Governance Board, and calling Nina Jankowicz an expert in her field, he said. Republicans on that House Homeland Security Committee want to know exactly what it is that this board will be charged to do. And GOP members of the House Judiciary Committee are asking to hear from -- and ask questions to Jankowicz herself about this position and exactly what it will entail and won't entail.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Mayorkas says that the board will fight disinformation that encourages migrants to try to get into the United States as the feds are about to lift Title 42 COVID restrictions at the border. More on that in a moment when we are joined by Texas Republican Congressman Tony Gonzalez, but first we check in on the border with correspondent Nate Foy, who is in Roma, Texas this afternoon. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Overnight, we saw multiple crossings here in Roma, Texas, including a group of about 20 or 30. They immediately turn themselves into Border Patrol, and we have images to show you of that. But first, I want to show you brand new video today from Texas DPS as they chase a drug smuggler with over 400 pounds of marijuana in the truck.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this new video from Brownsville, which is east of where we are here today. You see the chase starts on rural roads, and then it makes its way onto the grassy area surrounding the Rio Grande. Then you're going to see the truck plunge over the embankment into the water. Texas DPS and Border Patrol calls this a splashdown. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Splashdown. Splashdown. Splashdown.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Now, Martha, what you don't see in this video, notably is the driver being taken into custody. That's because they get out of the truck. It was the plan all along to swim back to Mexico to avoid arrest. However, you do see agents confiscating 486 pounds of marijuana. That's Texas DPS on that bus.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned here in Roma, multiple groups crossing, including this group of 20 to 30 migrants immediately turning themselves into Border Patrol overnight. And the numbers are expected, the DHS estimates that numbers are expected to go up if Title 42, in fact, ends as planned on the 23rd of this month, which is going to put even more pressure on local charities to deal with the humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE SILVA, BETHEL MISSION: Here recently, there's been another increase. Every night there's hundreds of people that come across our border. Just here, I mean, we only really work on one site, so I'm sure there are other sites in Roma. But any given night, there can be up to 200, between 200 to 400 people crossing our rivers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Martha, to give you some perspective here in Roma, there is a single bus that leaves this city every day, at 10:00 AM, that goes to Houston, a single bus. So this city is certainly not built to deal with an influx of migrants like they're seeing right now and certainly not at the estimates that DHS is putting out if Title 42, in fact, ends later this month.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's incredible video, Nate. It really gives you a sense of how dangerous the work is that these border agents do and the situations that they have to put themselves in the middle of rough stuff. Thank you very much, Nate Foy in Roma, Texas for us today.\par \par \uc2 And so, joining me now is Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales of Texas. Congressman, good to have you with us. Welcome back to the program. It's nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you listen to that woman talking about 200 to 400 people coming across the river. I know this is a very familiar scenario to you. So, what are you asking the government of this country to do to make things easier where you live?\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): Yes. Thank you, Martha, for having me on. And it's very simple. The people in my district, the people along the border are asking for one thing. They're asking for help.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm in Pearsall today at the \uc2 Security State Bank\uc2  and I've heard it all day long. You know, I started off in Cotulla, about 100 miles or so from the border. I was in Dilley, I had lunch with the police chief there. And he told me story after story of what is happening. You know, everyday pure chaos. You know, I met with the superintendent at the local school there, same thing. Everybody is living through hell.\par \par \uc2 A few of the stories, though, that aren't making it out. You know, we think of the smugglers, we think of these coyotes, you know, part of this cartel organization, they've expanded. The police chief told me a story, Chief Delgado has told me a story of a 60-year-old woman, a grandmother that was smuggling migrants. He told me another story of a soccer mom that had her 11-year-old child in the front seat while they were smuggling migrants. Why are they doing this? They're doing this because they're getting offered $1,000 a head to bring people over. What is happening is the chaos is spreading and spreading, and spreading and it's all due to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, when you think about this distance information post, right, and that the administration says that it was started under the prior administration, in order to make sure that people were not, you know, that the magnet was not drawing them, and that they didn't have false information. What do you say about that and what do you think about this new version of it under Nina Jankowicz. What are you expecting from it?\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: We should all be terrified. We should be terrified, both DHS and Secretary Mayorkas. They haven't been transparent at all. This entire administration has not been transparent at all. They won't tell us the truth to media. They won't tell us the truth to members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 You know, we all thought, you know, we were sold that this was going to be sleepy Joe and it was going to be a quiet administration, it's been anything. But what it's has been it's been no show Joe. You know, no show in Ukraine, no show along the border, and now they're trying to have -- create this environment where they're going to be basically spying on us in creating this disinformation determining what's disinformation or not.\par \par \uc2 I've spent 20 years as a Navy cryptologist. We fought against this, against the Russians and the Chinese and the Iranians. Those are the kinds of countries that have disinformation campaigns.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know you're calling for the impeachment of the DHS Secretary and Senator Hawley, who I had on earlier, is also on board with that. Why do you believe that that is necessary, and do you think there's any chance that that could actually happen?\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: You know, I think part of it starts with one is solutions. We've identified the problem over and over again. Republicans have come to the border constantly. I just hosted Leader McCarthy and \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  down here as my 10th delegation. We know what the problem is. This is part of the solutions.\par \par \uc2 One of the solutions is ensuring that we have repatriation flights. That we send people back to their country of origin that do not qualify for asylum. Once that happens, all of a sudden, the spigot turns off. So I look at it as whether it's Mayorkas or whether it's somebody else. CBP Commissioner Magnus, he needs to be responsible as well. You know, regardless of who's in the White House, Congress needs to lead again.\par \par \uc2 Title 42 goes away in 18 days. I think there's an opportunity for those Moderate Democrats that have talked about, you know, being supportive and helping the border, now is the time to keep Title 42 in place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. More to talk about but we're going to let you go with that. Thank you very much, Congressman Gonzales. Good to have you today.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, OK. The Dow is not having a good day, to put it mildly, down 