questioning who's behind it all, as you said.\par \par \uc2 So digging in, the list includes everyone from big business to foreign countries, election and human rights watchdog access now counts European governments like Germany and Denmark, \uc2 George Soros\uc2  and even \uc2 Twitter\uc2  itself amongst its donors. Another is Accountable Tech, as a dark money funnel its donors are unclear, but its co-founder is a former foreign policy spokesman for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's campaign.\par \par \uc2 But this isn't a new tactic, it's just a new focus. Another target, of course, is the Supreme Court. White House linked dark money group demand justice and pop-up groups organizing protests aimed at pressuring the justices. And dark money experts say this policy focus is concerning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA ANDERSON, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, HERITAGE ACTION: The dark money spending apparatus has been largely dominated by Arabella Advisors, which is an investment fund. That is basically the front group to \uc2 George Soros\uc2  and his team. Not only is it money that's influencing elections but it's money that's influencing the trajectory of the policy of the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: So Martha, if you follow the funds, Dems are leveraging dark money just as creatively as Republicans, if not more, whether it's policy or business. But I do have to note, even with all this pushback, Elon's quest for an open town square keeps gaining believers. He just secured another 7 billion in equity to fund his takeover today. So there's still momentum there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see. Very, very, very interesting. And I love that he's questioning what's going on out there. Thank you very much, Kelly O'Grady.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys. Talking about questioning what's going on out there. I looked before and I saw the S&P number and I was like, oh, it's getting better. But it's not, OK, because this is the Dow Jones Industrials down 1,300. Tech stocks, in particular, having a very bad day. This is the worst day for the market since June of 2020. And it adds, of course, to the concerns about the economy. You know that more than 77 percent of Americans say they think the economy is in bad shape.\par \par \uc2 CNN says "President Biden is close to the point of no return with Americans when it comes to the economy" after a pair of new polls showed that 77 percent number say the economy is in poor condition under the current White House. So with that, we bring in Fox Business anchor David Asman to join us.\par \par \uc2 So, David, we're about 11 minutes away for close. It's got a long way to go if it's going to bounce back here at the end. What's going on out there?\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Well -- and, by the way, we're looking at Dow Jones that's down almost 4 percent. But NASDAQ is down 5.5 percent. A lot of people if you check in your 401(k), I'm sure Apples in there, other stocks that are on the NASDAQ, so it's terrible. People -- you mentioned that poll, which is very interesting because people's opinions matter. People's sentiments matter with the economy.\par \par \uc2 People stopped buying things if they're not sure they can afford them. And when inflation is so high and when they're now worried about what happens with the economy if there's a recession, all these surplus jobs may go disappear overnight if we have a recession, because businesses won't be around to hire people. So that's why if people stopped buying things that means corporations make less profit. If they make less profit stocks go down. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 Powell yesterday, the head of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2 , said he can get rid of inflation with a soft landing. That means without a recession. The market today is saying we don't believe it. One, we don't believe there'll be a soft landing, two, we're not really sure he can get rid of inflation because it's not only printing money that causes inflation. It's when you have less stuff being made. And we got figures today, productivity numbers today, down 7.5 percent, way more than was expected was expected. It was expected to be less than 1 percent drop.\par \par \uc2 That means people aren't making as much stuff. People are less productive. This is an after effect of the lockdowns. When we turned off the economy and paid people for not working, people came into the workforce again with bad work habits, and they're not producing as much. We see it in this in the service sector when we go to restaurants or the retail sector. We were not satisfied with the service. We're getting a lot of people out there know what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 Why isn't the same thing happened in the factories? Well, it turns out it is. And that's why factories are making less than what they used to make. So we're becoming a less productive country at the same time that price is going up. It's a terrible, terrible combination.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Before I let you go, just a half a minute, what do you think about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 ? And these letters that were sent 26 people banding together from all different entities, you just saw from Kelly, they're coming after him.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, it's scary, not only for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  but what's happening with the justices, you know, to suggest that there should be protests at the houses. That's obstruction of justice because these guys are, and women are deciding on cases that the protesters don't like. It's illegal to do that kind of obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line with Elon, he's doing fine. As we just heard, he's getting more money for his purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , even Prince Allah Willy Alwaleed, who couple of weeks ago was saying he didn't trust Elon to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He's part of that group that's giving him money now. So I think Elon will survive and take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2  despite all the protests.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: An by the way, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is up today. It's one of the only stocks that's up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, David. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Howard Stern, the so called King of All Media, according to him for sure, blasting Hollywood's hypocrisy after the frightening attack on comedian Dave Chappelle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD STERN, HOST, THE HOWARD STERN SHOW: The audience at the Oscars gave Will Smith a standing ovation after the attack. That's the truth. It's on film. It ain't fake news. They didn't break Will's hands, they shook them, and it's wrong. And they all should be ashamed of themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STERN: People are getting nuttier and nuttier, and they're going to use these opportunities to attack people.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Howard Stern talking about this attack on comedian Dave Chappelle. Video from TMZ shows a man tackling the comedian in the middle of his show last night or Tuesday night in LA. The suspect will not face, we just learned, any felony charges in this case. Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt has all the breaking news on this today from Los Angeles. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. The 23-year-old man who police say attack Dave Chappelle on stage was originally arrested on suspicion of assault with a deadly weapon. And here is that weapon that police say Isaiah Lee had with him, a replica gun with a knife attached to it.\par \par \uc2 But the Office of LA District Attorney George Gascon has decided not to pursue felony charges against Lee apparently in part because Lee -- while Lee had the weapon in a bag, he did not actually use it during the attack on Chapelle. The DA's office told us a short time ago in an email statement, "after reviewing evidence, prosecutors determined that while criminal conduct occurred, the evidence as presented did not constitute felony conduct. Isaiah Lee will now face likely only a misdemeanor charge, an outcome which critics will likely say encourages similar attacks.\par \par \uc2 The very point Howard Stern, as you mentioned, was making about Will Smith treatment by fellow movie stars after he attacked comedian Chris Rock on stage at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STERN: Listen, you don't treat Will Smith any different than they did that Chappelle attacker. The audience at the Oscars gave Will Smith a standing ovation after the attack. That's the truth. It's on film. It ain't fake news. They didn't break Will'shands, they shook them. And it's wrong and they all should be ashamed of themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And already, many comedians are worrying that these responses by Hollywood stars or LA prosecutors to these attacks sends a message that it is open season on comedians who dare to offend anyone with their jokes. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You got to think twice in that business these days. Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, brand new episode of "The Untold Story" podcast, which we're so grateful that more and more of you are tuning into each week. I speak with Dave Rubin of "The Rubin Report." He just got a brand new book, it's called "Don't Burn This Country: Surviving and Thriving in our Woke Dystopia." Here's a bit of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVE RUBIN, HOST, THE RUBIN REPORT: We have had something so extraordinary in America for 250 years that has brought untold and unimaginable freedom to more people from all walks of life, from more places on Earth than any other place in the history of the world. There is a feeling that we are sort of giving it away right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Download that, you're going to learn a little bit more about Dave too, his personal story, which is a really cool, interesting story as well. And we'll also talk about the 2024 presidential election, what he thinks is going to happen. Foxnewspodcast.com is where you can download that.\par \par \uc2 That is "The Story" for today, Thursday, almost Friday, tomorrow. "The Story" goes on. We'll see you back here tomorrow. Thanks for joining us, everybody. Have a great afternoon and a lovely evening. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Crisis in Mariupol; Mortgage Rates Rising; Supreme Court Leak Investigation; Interview With Sen. Tim Scott (R-SC); Stocks Plummet\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Charlie Gasparino, Greg Palkot, Rich Edson, Susan Li,  Gerri Willis \par \uc2 8,236 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: About those gains yesterday, sorry, we reverse them all today, the Dow, the Nasdaq, all major market averages careening to levels we have not seen, or at least of this magnitude, for better the part of two years, interest rates backing up, and a reassessment of what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  intends to do moving forward on an inflationary problem that will not go away.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And what in the world is the real state of the markets? Yesterday's nearly 1,000-point run-up in the Dow based on view that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is on top of what is ailing folks when it comes to inflation or, today, maybe not on top of what is ailing people with inflation?\par \par \uc2 We have got you covered on this one, because remember yesterday, when they were all seizing on the view here that, well, actually the chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , is on top of this and he isn't looking forward to maybe hiking rates at the next meeting three-quarters-of-a- point?\par \par \uc2 But, today, people were saying, well, wait a minute, he is still talking about raising rates a half-point at a time, maybe for the next couple of meetings. And so they digested this and said, why the heck were we so happy yesterday? It's a wash, but a wash we can't really interpret to be which is the real direction of stocks and this economy, at a time when inflation itself remains very much out of control.\par \par \uc2 We got more reminders of that and a slowing indication on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the very latest right now from Gerri Willis at the Nasdaq market site, where anything technology was in a tailspin, I guess, huh, Gerri?\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely right, Neil. You understand it completely.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was a terrible day for the markets. I hoped you enjoyed Wednesday's rally, because it is over, it is long over. Listen to this, the Dow down over 1,000 points, 3.1 percent, the S&P down 3.5 percent, the Nasdaq down nearly 5 percent as tech stocks took it on the chin. We're seeing the worst day for stocks since June of 2020.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Dow and S&P continue in correction territory. The Nasdaq is in a bear market and continues there. Look, what's going on? You mentioned it before, a lack of confidence in \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to stanch this inflation, slow down this 40-year high in rising prices.\par \par \uc2 They say that there is no soft landing for this economy. In fact, some say \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  doesn't even know what a soft landing is.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at those tech stocks that are doing the worse, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , all these stocks with major losses for the day. There is one number, though, I think is important to focus on today. That is worker output, a big surprise there, that level productivity, an important number for the American economy, falling at its fastest pace since 1947, really historic those numbers coming in.\par \par \uc2 But I have to tell you, all of the gains from Wednesday are gone. And we are worse than at zero here. We're seeing major selloffs for the averages. We will have to see what Friday brings, always an interesting day for the markets -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a wash over the last couple of days as to where we stand. Sort of the rubber day good day tomorrow, to her point.\par \par \uc2 Of course, an employment report, a jobs report for the month just passed will be sort of teed up here.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on where we go from here. Susan Li, business correspondent extraordinaire, \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 , which, if you don't get, you should demand, especially today.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We could have helped you make sense of this.\par \par \uc2 Kenny Polcari\uc2  with us as well, Slatestone Wealth chief market strategist.\par \par \uc2 So, Susan, a lot of consternation over which was the reality, today or yesterday.\par \par \uc2 LI: And doesn't it seem like the Federal Reserve and \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  has pretty much lost most credibility on Wall Street?\par \par \uc2 I will be kind of say most, because, yesterday, you heard him in that press conference saying, we're not even thinking about a 75-basis-point hike. Today, Wall Street is still anticipating a 75 percent probability they're going to hike interest rates by 75 basis points in June.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And these are people who bet with their money.\par \par \uc2 LI: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they're saying, are they -- but, yesterday, they were celebrating what he said.\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, I think that was...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is today an indication they don't buy what he said?\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, I think absolutely, because he even said yesterday, we have a good chance of avoiding a recession.\par \par \uc2 Do people believe that with the sell-off today? No. By the way, that productivity number that Gerri just mentioned, yes, it dropped by the most since 1947. But that means the counterpoint of that is wages went up by the most since 1982.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very good point.\par \par \uc2 LI: And that is, by the way, more than double the Federal Reserve inflation target of 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 What does that tell you? It tells you that inflation is going to be above 2 percent for some time, if you're paying workers more.\par \par \uc2 KENNY POLCARI\uc2 , SLATESTONE WEALTH: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Normally, to address that, Kenny, you have to keep hiking interest rates to get north of that inflation level. We're nowhere near that right now, which would imply that we have to go a lot higher on rates.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you in that camp? And if you are, where to from here?\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: Well, I'm absolutely in that camp, because, if they really want to tamp it down, they're going to have to push rates higher than what the inflation rate is.\par \par \uc2 I think what's interesting, to add to what Susan said, the reaction today, because \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  didn't say, it's not on the table -- he just said, we're not actively considering it yet, which, to me, meant the door was wide open, that 75 is coming.\par \par \uc2 Even though he was trying to tell no, the market obviously thinks that not only is it coming, but it should come, because they are too far behind the eight ball. Here's the point. Inflation is now running at 8.5 percent already, maybe even 9 percent. Some people think it's closer to 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 And if the Fed wants to tamp it down, they're going to have to bring rates above that, the same way they did in 1981 with Paul Volcker, when he jammed rates to 21 percent. Now I'm not suggesting they should go to 21 percent. But we know what happened then, right? He sent the country into a big recession, inflation did come down, but it was painful along the way.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's ultimately the story is here, as people are thinking that the Fed has lost control. They're behind the eight ball. And they're not believing \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  for what he said yesterday today, after they dissected it, they pulled it apart sentence by sentence, they listened to the words, and they listened to his tone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Something happened, because it was a very, to your point, a civil-like reaction on this.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's interesting.\par \par \uc2 Susan, one of the things you really want to gauge is how the consumer is feeling, and he or she right now, we're told, feeling pretty good. But some of these e-tailers, while giving basically good numbers, are telegraphing trouble down the road.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Etsy\uc2 , \uc2 Wayfair\uc2 , \uc2 Shopify\uc2 , \uc2 eBay\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 LI: \uc2 Shopify\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... all near double-digit losses on the day, even when, in at least three out of four those cases, the numbers themselves, the revenues themselves were good, but signaling a consumer headwind here.\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, especially when you have interest rates projected to go above 3 percent this year. Mortgage rates are at their highest since 2009, at 5.27 percent, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 LI: And that's ultimately going to hurt the consumer.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's also a tough compare as well, when we were stuck at home, lockdowns, and buying things online. But it makes you really think about how priced these stocks are, how highly and richly priced these are, if they're guiding for slower growth in the future, because these are growth stocks, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 LI: Not all of them make profit. In fact, even \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , wouldn't you say, Kenny? I mean, in the last 10 years, they have made just a few quarters of profit.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't up until the last few years that we actually saw them in the black.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, by the way, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  sliding about 9 percent just today.\par \par \uc2 Kenny, was this overdone, though? Technology stocks themselves, when we're looking at some of the premier names, they were the ringleaders of the bull market. Right now, they're the ringleaders of the downdraft.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm wondering if it's a bit extreme here.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: I don't think it's a bit extreme yet, actually. And I hate to say that, because that just suggests that I think there's more downside, which I do think there is more downside.\par \par \uc2 There's got to be one of those days where it's complete despair, when they're throwing everything out the window, including the kitchen sink. That's when you know that you have hit the bottom, right?\par \par \uc2 And I don't think -- even though Nasdaq was down 5.5 percent today, it was fairly orderly. It wasn't -- didn't feel panicky to me, like they were throwing it out the window yet. And I hate to say that, so I do think there's going to be further downside pressure, because rates are going to go up, and they're going to go up faster than expected.\par \par \uc2 And the inflation story is around for a little bit. I don't see inflation peaking. I don't see it starting to bend over. I see it kind of just continuing gradually up. And then, if it peaks, it's going to peak, but stay elevated. And that's going to be the problem for the Fed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, guys, I want to thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's not only Wall Street worried about what the consumer is thinking. The consumers are worried about whether they will be surviving.\par \par \uc2 We talked to more than a few who are worried about what's happening as we speak.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Groceries are really expensive, meats and different groceries. Yes, it's been spiking. And inflation is going up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Lower savings, definitely. I mean, rent has gone up. I mean, gasoline prices have gone up. Everything has gone up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every time I go to the grocery store, everything is higher in cost.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Overall general sense, I feel like I'm seeing it everywhere, whether it's groceries, whether it's gas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm just hoping we can find ways to diminish those costs. And I know it's a natural thing for costs to go up, but it just seems a little too fast right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they was reducing the inflation, then how come things are getting more expensive?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, some real worries out there.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Westwood, The Washington Examiner, these are palpable concerns, justified concerns, because they're not going away. If anything, the price component we see and the deflator and all these other indices that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  relies on all point to prices continuing to surge.\par \par \uc2 That is not a very good environment for average folks, for any folks. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SARAH WESTWOOD, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Yes, you're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people are feeling pain across the board, whether it's heating their homes, filling up their car, going to the grocery store. And the Biden administration's response to all of this continues to just be kind of absolutely baffling.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this week, for example, you heard Biden out there touting the fact that this administration is on track to reduce the deficit this year. That's just not the sort of message that people want to hear from Biden right now. They have sort of mishandled the way they have talked about inflation from the beginning, whether it's calling it transitory, blaming it on a host of boogeymen, from corporate greed, to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 None of that has worked. And what the Biden administration has done that I think has really worsened this perception of how he's handling the economy is, they have talked about how good parts of the economy looks on paper, right? The Biden administration loves to talk about how good the jobs numbers are right now, the fact that wages are increasing, and they really fail to acknowledge the pain that's caused by prices rising faster than wages.\par \par \uc2 And I think you saw another example of that this week...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I think you're right.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: ... Biden talking about the deficit. People don't want to hear that right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 It's the last thing on their minds right now. Touche to the government if we come down our peak levels of spending during COVID a couple of years ago. But the fact of the matter is that those numbers should be coming down in this environment.\par \par \uc2 But, leaving that aside, we have seen a lot of retrenching, pivoting, whatever you want to call it, where a lot of customers, Sarah, are not only buying cheaper cuts of meat, if they go to the grocery store, they're just being cautious. Not so when it comes to vacation plans. The airlines, maybe minus \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 , signaling that it looks like a busy summer travel season.\par \par \uc2 But that might be a last gasp of activity. You hope not. But what is the sense you're getting about the fact that, little more than a couple of weeks ago, we were talking about, oh, we have hit the high in inflation, even if it stays around here, it won't get any worse?\par \par \uc2 Growing concerns today it will get worse.\par \par \uc2 WESTWOOD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I think the concern for the Biden administration is that people aren't going to have tolerance for these high prices forever. And, soon, they are going to start cutting down their spending, especially on goods that aren't absolutely necessary and goods that, by the way, are more a part of the way that the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  calculates inflation, right?\par \par \uc2 Gas, food prices aren't a part of core inflation. But when people stopped buying those other consumable goods, that number is only going to get worse. And I think that people aren't going to pay these high prices forever. So the Biden administration has to be worried. What happens after this summer?\par \par \uc2 COVID-free summer. People are excited to get out there. They're still willing to pay those higher prices. But when the interest rates go up on their credit card payments, and when that busy season is over, and the pain of those higher prices really start to take a toll, that's going to be happening right in the heat of midterm season.\par \par \uc2 I think, if you're the Biden administration, you're really worried about what happens when we hit that point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Westwood of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 We always like to posit here that a lot of people who hearken back to the '70s experience, a lot of people hope we don't bring that back, if for no other reason than we can't -- we can't see the value of leisure suits coming back.\par \par \uc2 But that notwithstanding, this is nothing like that. The underpinning from the bulls you hear and those optimistic about the economy is that we're in a much stronger starting position than we were back then. But, of course, the issue with inflation is, it gets to a point where it pinches on consumer activity, where the stag part kicks in, the stagflation, the slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, prices do come down, but at a terrible cost. And that is a worry of Tim Scott, the South Carolina Republican senator who warned people not too long ago on this very show that kind of stuff isn't transitory. it's here.\par \par \uc2 And he's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, even Republican Senator Tim Scott doesn't blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for all this inflationary mess, but he did tell me earlier today on FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand, that he should take responsibility for a big chunk of it.\par \par \uc2 This was from Senator Scott earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): President Biden's policies, economic policies, have led to the worst inflationary effects in our economy in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 That means that the average person in our country is experiencing an invisible tax that is eroding their spending power. That means our seniors who are on fixed income have to find a way to ration either their health care, their medicine, their energy, or food.\par \par \uc2 It means that people who are growing up in households like the one I grew up in, they're now having to make harder decisions than they have ever had to make. That's wrong.\par \par \uc2 Biden pouring so much money into our economy has caused our economy to explode in a negative way.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, you might not be surprised that the White House has a slightly different view of that, and that it has fingered other factors here, that, today, for example, a lot of people saying maybe part of this is the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's fault, that its move to aggressively raise interest rates comes too little, too late.\par \par \uc2 Others say just the opposite, that now some of the stark measures it appears to be taken to lift interest rates, it's going to slow the economy down and it won't address the underlying inflation. We got a hint of that that show labor costs are running at an 8 percent clip right now.\par \par \uc2 So, whatever people are making, they're paying out, and it's like a double whammy. Do you see anything changing that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I don't, Neil.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that this government has acted too slow too often. They created the inflation. Now they want to have credit for solving the problem, when, in fact, the problem may actually exacerbate the situation.\par \par \uc2 When your rates start going up, your economic activity starts going down. And that makes you get a little closer to a recession. The truth is that, while the inflationary effects cannot be stopped right now, the increase in the interest rates will slow it down.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think it's going to stop it, because it's too little too late. The Fed does not have enough tools in the toolkit for the economy that we have today, especially since, as you know, Neil, our unemployment rates are already significantly lower than is normal in this kind of environment.\par \par \uc2 Our labor force participation rate is not actually increasing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That combination is very concerning.\par \par \uc2 And so the interest rates will make it harder for people to buy homes. But for our seniors on fixed incomes, they will see a bit of a bump in their resources that they have.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Senator, it's interesting, but this does appear to be a worldwide problem. In Britain, of course, they just raise rates for the fourth time since just December. I think they're in and out of 13-, 14-year highs on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's running at 70 percent inflation rate, Argentina 75 percent, Brazil 50 percent, Europe collectively now in excess of 11 percent. Are we back in the '70s here?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I tell you what. I think we are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, literally, this feels like the Carter eras, when you think about the explosive price in gas, you think about what it costs to heat or cool your home if you're in South Carolina. It is drastic. But, more importantly, when you look at the global effect, you remember the days, Neil, when Venezuela was at a 900 percent inflationary effects because of the crushing decisions of their government.\par \par \uc2 What we have seen...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, why would you assume I'm that old, Senator? Why would you assume I'm that old?\par \par \uc2 Actually, I do remember it verbatim. But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That was only two or three years ago. We're both -- it was only two or three years ago.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. I hear you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: The truth is that we -- the whole globe responds to the type of economic decisions that we make here at home.\par \par \uc2 That's why our global leadership economically matters so much. We should be forcing decisions here at home like, why not restart our own energy economy, creating six-figure jobs here at home? Why not continue the conversation around the Keystone XL pipeline?\par \par \uc2 Why not have the PPE coming back home? Why not use Opportunity Zones to try insourcing back into America? We did it in South Carolina and we brought...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, assuming, Senator, that doesn't happen, let's say, politically, reading the tea leaves here, that doesn't seem likely, at least under this administration, you could argue right now they're really hanging their hats on maybe more spending.\par \par \uc2 They talked about college loan forgiveness and all of that. I just wonder, is their cure -- of course, it's far from your cure would be. It doesn't sound like anything will happen legislatively, if even that's an option here. And everything is hanging on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . Does that worry you?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: It does worry me, because, as we know, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can only increase interest rates. That's really their -- they could slow, of course -- they're starting the process of unwinding their balance sheet and reducing their balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That will have impact too. So they could slow that and increase interest rates.\par \par \uc2 But the truth of the matter is, our economy needs smart, reasonable leadership at the top. We're not getting that right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And just forwarding a little bit from what's going on in the markets, your name has been bandied about by quite a few folks as a possible presidential candidate, possible running mate, but that you're -- there's a lot of hopeful talk about you beyond the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When you hear that, how do you react to that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: It makes me chuckle. It also tells me that God has a sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: As a guy who darn near failed civics and high school as a freshman, the truth is that we live in the most amazing place.\par \par \uc2 What I will say, Neil, is simply this. America is the solution, not the problem. The more we invest in ourselves, the more we believe in ourselves, the better our future is. There's not a greater contrast today than the left leadership vs. the right leadership.\par \par \uc2 We have amazing national leaders from the state level throughout this country to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  on the right. So we have a lot of firepower coming to the table, and we have the solutions. They don't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, well, that's a very diplomatic and modest answer.\par \par \uc2 But finally, Senator, I do want to get your take on this idea Republicans are well-poised to do well in these midterm elections, take over the House, possibly the Senate, but that there's growing pressure...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... on the party to sit back and let it happen, and not help or do anything to disrupt this. What do you say to Republicans who say that's the strategy you should take?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I'm a former football player. So I always think that you should have your offense on the field as often as possible.\par \par \uc2 And our offense should contrast what we did when we were in leadership from 2016 to 2020 vs. what they have done in leadership. What we did while we were leadership was we created seven million jobs, two-thirds of those jobs going to women 70-year low, African-Americans all time-low, Hispanics all- time low, Asians all-time low, overall population 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 We were very compassionate. We focused on historically black colleges and universities when we were in the majority. And we funded them to the highest level possible. We worked on rare blood diseases like sickle cell anemia and many others.\par \par \uc2 We actually had an agenda that focused on the American people. So, we need to go back to the future, 2016 to 2020, and contrast that to the future that we're living right now. And that's foreshadowing bad news with higher inflation, higher spending, higher taxes vs. lower taxes, lower unemployment, and higher labor force participation rates.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, we know how to get it done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we shall see, the parties very, very different in terms of their approach to this. So we will keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we're getting some word out of the chief justice, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , and what he made up this leak of this draft opinion on Roe v. Wade by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , said that it was absolutely appalling, speaking at an Atlanta legal conference, and that one bad apple would not hurt the courts public reputation to decide issues of important political influence does.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is scheduled to address this same group tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember 3-something mortgage rates about a years ago, not even? Try 5-something right now, 5.27 percent, to be exact. That's the highest since August 2009.\par \par \uc2 And it's getting higher -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Roe vs. Wade was wrong the day it was decided.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Republicans have been on the wrong side of history and the wrong side of America.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): I don't know why some on the left want to lionize the leaker.\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): This is a life-or-death moment, and we need to fight like it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so the leak vs. the leaker, pick your poison and which party pursues which.\par \par \uc2 To Rich Edson right now at the Supreme Court on how all of that is going down -- Rich\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And there's still no indication yet who may have leaked this to Politico a couple of days ago. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , though, making his first public comments about all of this since that leak happened on Monday evening. He calls it appalling, absolutely appalling, the leak. And he says that the leaker will not affect the laws or the work of the court.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chief \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has tasked Supreme Court Marshal Colonel Gail Curley -- she's a former Army attorney -- with figuring out how exactly this leaked, working through theories, pointing to clerks, assistants, hackers, even justices and their families, a liberal trying to prevent Justice Alito's draft from becoming a final decision or a conservative boxing in and solidifying the justices' commitments to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 But Democratic Senator Chris Coons and Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  say they have introduced a bill to boost protections for the justices and their families, given the increasing focus on the court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): And it's not just an attack against the independence of the judiciary. This risks violence against members of the Supreme Court and their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Since this leaked Monday night, the Supreme Court in May of 2022 looks like the Capitol grounds of January 2021, with the same tall fencing designed to keep anyone from climbing over.\par \par \uc2 It's unclear what a marshal's investigation will even look like, whether Colonel Curley will request help from a private security firm or other government agencies. There has been no request yet for the Justice Department to get involved here, though there may be a reluctance to do so, having the executive branch, in effect, investigate the judicial branch -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rich Edson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 To Jim Trusty on all of this, a former DOJ prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Jim, we're learning separately -- and I do want to get into the leaker issue here -- but that Senators Sanders and Gillibrand and Warren have immediately called for abolishing the filibuster to get legislation that would enshrine the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that's more of a political issue here, whether they can succeed in doing that, but, in other words, to preempt whatever decision the Supreme Court comes down with later this year. That would be time a wasting there. How doable is that?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Oh, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, it's -- to me, it's a really disgraceful moment that anyone is wallowing in political exploitation, which is what you're really seeing, and avoiding an issue that actually should unite all of us. And we should be talking about how outrageous a breach this is of the ethics of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And it got about a minute's play. And then it's turned into this idea of let's use it to abolish the filibuster, or let's rant and rave and get emotional about abortion. So I don't need to add my voice to the emotionality. I think we should all be focusing on what a horrible breach this is.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, in terms of congressmen coming together and passing laws to protect the Supreme Court, how about also passing a clear federal law that protects the intellectual property of the Supreme Court? Because we're kind of struggling to find a criminal offense here. And it'd be nice to have something that basically says, if you engage in the unauthorized disclosure of the work product of the Supreme Court, that you face a serious felony for that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, for the time being, we catch the person who leaked this, no such punishment, maybe short of potentially losing his or her job.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But if they have the right people investigating it -- and you and I talked the other day about how the Marshals, parts of the U.S. Marshals Service are very adept at investigating. Like, the electronic surveillance guys are fabulous at tracking down fugitives, international or national. They know how to use technology.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be an investigation that's based on people and technology, people in terms of interviewing folks in the building and saying, hey, is there somebody that's been running their mouth, somebody that's an ideologue about Roe vs. Wade or that had tipped off that they might do something like this, or maybe someone actually steps up and confesses?\par \par \uc2 But, beyond that, you have got a lot of access to technology. You have got government computers with nice big banners that say, it's not your property. Probably have government-issued phones. These are easy things to scoop up and search for connections to Politico and search for documents that show that these folks, whoever it is, made the disclosure.\par \par \uc2 So I'm optimistic, with the right people investigating, it's a quick and easy leak investigation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, is it something that would be picked up by cameras in offices at the Supreme Court? In other words, if somebody was copying something, relaying something, or is it much harder than that?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, I don't know. I mean, I don't know enough about the security measures within the Supreme Court. Hopefully, none of us know on the outside about that.\par \par \uc2 But it's certainly conceivable. I mean, really, what you're looking at is, how sophisticated was the leaker? Is this someone who literally just sends an e-mail to Josh Gerstein at Politico? Or is it somebody who realizes that they don't want to leave an electronic paper trail or a video paper trail and does something that's much more like espionage, the drop-off in the city park kind of thing?\par \par \uc2 I'm optimistic, though, that one way or another, through technology in particular -- and someone that's capable of being this much of an ideologue should be kind of proud to announce their misdeed. But there's one other thing just to keep in mind, Neil. We don't really know where it's coming from.\par \par \uc2 And that means the investigators shouldn't eliminate anybody prematurely. If there's an associate justice that's involved in this that authorized a clerk, for instance, to do it, there needs to be serious consequences all the way up to that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You think that's possible?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: It's certainly possible. I hope not. I mean, it'd be -- it would make a dark day even darker in terms of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But, at this point, I don't think anybody should rule anything out. Again, most importantly, the investigators shouldn't rule it out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. Jim, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jim Trusty, following all of these developments here.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, following higher rates for that mortgage, whether you're trying to get a home or refinance the one you're already in. They like to say that your ship has come in when rates are low. Signs right now that it's leaving and you better move fast.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, for people of a certain age, hearing that 30-year fixed- rate mortgage is in the 5-something neck of the woods, and it was under 3 percent a year ago at this time, it still seems very, very low, especially for some of you who might know that, when my wife and I got our first mortgage, it was 13.5 percent. Again, I might have said that once or twice, but, but perspective and age is everything.\par \par \uc2 And for those of a certain age used to these very, very low interest rates, those are jarring backups. Now, how does it affect real estate and the financing activity that has kept it booming for so long?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask Katrina Campins, Campins Company founder, luxury real estate agent extraordinaire.\par \par \uc2 All right, so, Katrina, now you have some serious interest rate headwind for those of a certain age, I get it. But what happens now?\par \par \uc2 KATRINA CAMPINS, THE CAMPINS COMPANY: Hey, Neil. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 I do believe that the interest rate hike will cool the market. With that said, I think it's really important to note that we have a lack of supply. And I actually think an interesting thing to take note of is the fact that, with these interest rates rising, a lot of sellers are going to second- guess putting their house on the market because it's going to be more expensive for them to actually purchase.\par \par \uc2 I think another component is the fact that home builders are still experiencing labor shortages, supply chain issues. So that's going to continue. Do I think the market is going to slow down? Yes.\par \par \uc2 I do believe it's going to slow down. I think we're going to see less price appreciation. However, with that said, I do think we are going to see price appreciation continue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you're in a unique universe there in Florida, where it's just been propelled by people who like the tax haven environment and all the rest that supersedes a lot of this other stuff.\par \par \uc2 But, nevertheless, I'm wondering how it sorts out for those who are looking to buy a home. And, in this environment, do they seek out less of a home, a less costly home, because that's not been helping? Or does it create a rush that bids up the prices even higher?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Well, we're initially seeing a rush because interest rates are going up. So I'm beginning to see that rush now.\par \par \uc2 I do think, though, that we are going to see people take a step back with how much they believe they could afford as a result of interest rates going up. But, right now, what I'm experiencing is this rush to get into the market, to get a property under contract before interest rates continue to go up.\par \par \uc2 I think something else that's interesting is the fact that I believe cash buyers are actually going to reenter the market once some of these other buyers that require financing exit. So it's going to be really interesting to see.\par \par \uc2 But I do think, overall, the crazy frenzy that we saw during the pandemic is going to slow down. And more buyers are going to have an opportunity to find a property, even if it costs them a little bit more money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, keep us posted on all of that Katrina.\par \par \uc2 Katrina Campins, luxury real estate agent, very prescient on the boom in Florida before we even sensed that there could be a boom in Florida. And that apparent boom continues in Florida.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up, including right now word we're getting out a Mariupol that the Ukrainian deputy prime minister saying civilians will indeed be evacuated come tomorrow noon local time. No further details.\par \par \uc2 But, meanwhile, it's come, we're told, separately, to hand-to-hand combat among soldiers still in that facility, where they can't get out. And they're not being treated the same way as those civilians. Can you picture this getting uglier? It just has.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In this now better-than-two-month war in Ukraine, never have so many been so trapped for so long in one place, a metal facility that we're told has a network of tunnels underneath, where you have better than 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers holding out, and Russians threatening them they have got to surrender, and maybe just as many civilians who are caught up in the same crisis.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot right now in Kyiv, Ukraine, with the very latest they hope to start resolving, that is, tomorrow -- sir.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They're hoping, Neil, but we have heard that before.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the war here going to some extreme lengths. In fact, now it's into its 10th week. Let's talk about Mariupol that we have been talking about for a while, still hiding out beneath that massive steel mill in the embattled southern city, some 200 civilians, maybe 2,000 Ukrainian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 Now, some civilians got out earlier this week, Neil. The Russians were supposed to stop their bombardment today, allow some more to get out. They didn't do that. There's a lot of fighting still there. Supposed to happen again tomorrow. We will see. Tens of thousands really remain in that city, which is really, really beaten up over the past two months or so.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the east, Russian and Ukrainian forces are slugging it out. Remember, the east is the new goal of Moscow's aggression, Russian rockets striking several towns along the front line there, but Ukraine also making some gains, from what I'm hearing, including around the second biggest city in Ukraine. That's Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 This as a new report claims that intelligence provided by the United States was instrumental in Ukrainian forces killing as many as 12 Russian generals in the field. On the record today, the Pentagon says it won't speak to the details of that \uc2 New York Times\uc2  story, just that is providing intelligence for the defense of Ukraine, and, hey, they can use it however they want.\par \par \uc2 And while Ukraine steps up cross-border strikes against Russia as well -- it's hitting fuel depots and transportation hubs just inside Russia, even industrial targets deep inside Russia, including nearby Moscow, as Kyiv tries to keep the Kremlin off-balance.\par \par \uc2 Finally, Neil, a very unusual climb-down from none other than Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . According to the Israeli government, in a phone conversation today with Israeli prime Minister Bennett, Putin actually apologized for anti-Semitic comments made earlier this week about Ukraine and President Zelenskyy by Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov.\par \par \uc2 That's an unusual moment in these terrible last several months -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wonders never cease, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot, be safe, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot the middle of all of that.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, you have certainly heard from enough politicians on the Supreme Court leak, but is it me or not many companies? In this day and age with the \uc2 Disney\uc2  and the Florida dust-up, companies are very, very quiet on this -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, crowds that continue to gather, and very loudly, outside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , since the leak of this draft memo that could change Roe v. Wade as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Everyone's been weighing in, folks like that outside the court, politicians on both sides of the aisle, but, oddly enough, not much of corporate America, in fact, very few, if any, in corporate America.\par \par \uc2 Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, you got to know. What's going on? Operation crickets here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, listen, I think they have taken -- they have seen what happened with \uc2 Disney\uc2  when \uc2 Bob Chapek\uc2  came out against the Florida law that bans teaching of sexual education to children below third grade.\par \par \uc2 And they saw what Governor DeSantis did after that, which was essentially cut \uc2 Disney\uc2  out of all the corporate largess they were getting all the years. And I will tell you this, Neil. The Republicans in Congress have noticed. And I think that's what's seeping -- also seeping back to these CEOs.\par \par \uc2 Remember, during 20 -- since 2020, the CEOs didn't miss a chance to virtue- signal on everything, taking one knee, you name it, attacking the Georgia voter law. But they saw what happened in Florida. And they're hearing from their lobbyists what's going to happen in -- if the Republicans take the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The GOP has been very clear to lobbyists that, if they get the House and the Senate and if corporations want to be an addendum to the Democratic Party, there's going to be hearings left and right. And they're going to be at war.\par \par \uc2 I mean, right now, the GOP feels that corporations have basically been like a super PAC for the progressive wing of the Democratic Party. They have had enough of it. We're talking like open war here almost, Neil. At least that's what I'm hearing. And I think they're getting the message. If they want to go there, be prepared for more Ron DeSantises.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the GOP House and Senate can pass laws like take away corporate largess like \uc2 Disney\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But they can do other stuff, including hearings.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm wondering too, Charlie, if they're coming back to their senses. When you invest in, let's say, \uc2 Yum! Brands\uc2  and \uc2 KFC\uc2 , you just want them to make great chicken and leave it at that, and weighing in on these issues is not really important to either side, because you automatically, in a 50/50 country, and depending on the polls and surveys on this, you're going to alienate a large chunk of people who might not share your view.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering how much that's coming into play.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Oh, it's definitely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you think of it this way, Neil, the country I think it's more than 50/50 on wokeness. It's -- most people despise it. I mean, poll after poll shows that. There was a purple state named Virginia that went Republican based on teaching of -- what they're teaching kids in terms of Critical Race Theory largely and taking stuff away from parents.\par \par \uc2 And why these companies, who have to deal with everybody, get involved in politics, why Major League Baseball got involved into Georgia voting law is beyond me. It's just bad for business. And they -- I'm pretty sure baseball played -- paid some price for going against the Georgia voting law, which they said was racist, even though it was -- essentially forced people to show their I.D. to vote.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And \uc2 Disney\uc2  is going to face pressure here, but the political pressure, it could be pretty nasty.\par \par \uc2 I mean, think about it. Why does the -- CEOs represent shareholders. They work on behalf of shareholders.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Do shareholders want them to get involved in this?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Could I switch gears, if I can, my friend?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We are continuing to following the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  today, a lot of powerful people with a lot of money, including \uc2 Larry Ellison\uc2  of \uc2 Oracle\uc2  fame and a host of investment houses, now seem to be jumping on board, helping him out finance this, as if the guess who's the richest person the planet needs help.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of that, the fact that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock was up, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock was down, but where is this going?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I think he's going to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean, that's where it's going. He's going to -- he's going to win it.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the guy is a multibillionaire 200-plus times over. He has a lot of friends out there, a lot of really rich friends. A lot of people grew up in America aren't woke. They're libertarian and -- or they're conservative, and they liked what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 A billion dollars for \uc2 Larry Ellison\uc2  is not a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, I can't believe I'm saying that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's change under his couch cushions. That's like...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... money. Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, anyway, it's not bad. For a billion dollars, he's playing a part in the democratization of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And I can see other people jump in. I'm surprised Peter Thiel hasn't jumped in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: He's probably next.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The Republican billionaire who has helped pay for a lot of races there.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch very closely.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie Gasparino, the best in the business here.\par \par \uc2 All right, so we wrap up a crazy day at the corner of Wall and Broad, the Dow surrendering about 1,000-plus points, the Nasdaq, all of the major averages getting hammered. Tomorrow could be an interesting day, because we get the employment report, the jobs report for the month of April.\par \par \uc2 They are hoping for steady job gains in the vicinity of 400,000. You know the drill on Wall Street. If it misses that number or there are concerns that things could get worse, well, it gets worse, a lot worse. But maybe it doesn't.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. Ted Cruz (R-TX)\b0\par\par\uc2 Larry Kudlow \par \uc2 2,583 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: So, with great pleasure, joining me now, we bring in Senator Ted Cruz, as always.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cruz, it's wonderful to see you again. Sorry I didn't give you a better stock market today, but that could take a couple years, heavens knows.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I want to ask you this question.\par \par \uc2 I want to -- you're a good economic thinker, as well as other areas.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: With respect to the November elections, sir, in your judgment, what is going to be more important, inflation and a slumping economy or Roe v. Wade? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Well, if you look at polling right now, inflation is dominating the number one concern of voters. Right behind that is crime. Right behind that is illegal immigration. So, those right now are what voters are telling us are their top three concerns.\par \par \uc2 Look, Roe vs. Wade, there are a lot of Democrats and a lot of folks in the corporate media are hoping, if the Supreme Court overturns Roe vs. Wade, that it will magically prevent the red tidal wave that is happening in November. I don't think that is going to happen. I think they're getting the politics entirely wrong.\par \par \uc2 One of the things to understand, if the court does in fact overturn Roe vs. Wade, that doesn't mean abortion is immediately illegal all over the country. What it means is that the decision-making on abortion returns to the elected legislatures, primarily in the states.\par \par \uc2 That means, in bright blue states like New York or California, at least in the short term, it is certain that abortion will remain universally available on demand. And so, for many of the furthest left voters in states like that, they will find little to nothing change in their day-to-day lives.\par \par \uc2 For people in redder states, states like my home state of Texas, we will see meaningful restrictions on abortion. And those will vary state by state, depending on the values and the mores of the citizens of those states.\par \par \uc2 That's how the Constitution envisions these kinds of issues being decided, is having different resolutions, because the people of Texas want something different than the people of California.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, Larry, it is ironic. We're seeing Democrats engaging in histrionics about the prospect of Roe being overturned. And my favorite talking point they use is, they say it's an assault on democracy, which is richly ironic because it's precisely the opposite. It is a victory for democracy, because what the Democrats are saying is they don't want the voters to be able to set the rules on abortion.\par \par \uc2 They want nine unelected lawyers wearing robes to decree the answers for the entire country. And the reason Democrats are so upset is, they recognize that an awful lot of voters in America disagree with their radical and extreme views on abortion and, given the choice, will vote in different laws.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Going back to the inflation, economic slump, what did folks say?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you're from Texas. Texas is one of the growthiest states in the union, unregulated, by and large, no income tax and so forth and so on, the center of the oil and gas business, which is fabulous.\par \par \uc2 But what are you hearing back home? Because a day like today in the stock market is rough stuff.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: My concern -- I will be honest with you, Senator. And I'm not I'm not always right about this stuff.\par \par \uc2 But I think we have a long, tough road ahead of us because of mistakes made in the past year-and-a-half by the federal government, as well as by the \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2 . I don't think the market has bottomed. I don't think market interest rates have peaked, to be candid with you. And I think it's going to be hard to get inflation back down.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing? And what are you thinking about, as a senior member of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Look, that is very much what I'm hearing on the ground in Texas and all across the country.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is something that people see every day; they're confronted with every day. And the people that get hurt the most are those on fixed incomes, those with limited income. Particularly, seniors get hammered with inflation, because, if your income each month stays the same, and yet the cost of everything goes up, the cost of food goes up, the cost of rent goes up, the cost of electricity goes up, the cost of lumber goes up, the cost of buying a home goes up, and, most visibly, the cost of gasoline skyrockets.\par \par \uc2 Listen, right now in Texas, there's a mom coming home who's spending 100 bucks to fill her minivan. Right now in Nebraska, there's a farmer driving home who is spending 150 bucks to fill his truck. And that hits right at home. It hits. And there's a reason why, if you look at Biden's poll numbers and Democrats' poll numbers, that virtually every demographic you can think of is galloping towards Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Young people are galloping towards Republicans. Hispanics are galloping towards Republicans. We're seeing African Americans moving towards Republicans. We're seeing blue-collar working-class voters moving towards Republicans. And we're seeing suburban women moving towards Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And the reason is simple. All of those groups of people are feeling the pain, the disaster of Biden and Harris and the Democrats' failed socialist policies. And you and I have talked about this before. I think Biden is in many ways \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  2.0, with the same failures we saw in the '70s.\par \par \uc2 And that obviously led to stagflation. We have got half of the stagflation right now with the inflation. And I fear the stagnation is right behind it. That imposed an enormous amount of pain on Americans for several years. But the good news is, it also ushered in the Reagan Revolution, and it took \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  to give us Reagan.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- we may well name \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the Republican of the year...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: ... because, when all is said and done, I think Biden will have elected more Republicans maybe than any person who's ever lived.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: So, I shouldn't bring him before the Misinformation Government Board?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Because he's going to be the Republican guy of the year?\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you something, Senator, honestly, a serious point, though.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Coming back to the inflation issue, all right, predominantly, it's too much money printing. But, but, but, but, as you well know, it's also too much government spending and borrowing.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: So, I just have this dream. My dream goes like this.\par \par \uc2 There's a $350 billion corporate pork subsidies industrial policy handouts with a lot of Green New Deal. It's called the China Compete or some bloody thing.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Three hundred and fifty billion dollars, and 18 Republican senators voted for it in the go -- first go-around.\par \par \uc2 Is it possible and can we ask the Republicans to not vote for this, as a blow against inflation, as well as terrible industrial corporate welfare industrial policy?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we don't want to out-China China here.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: But we don't want to out-inflation inflation also.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: No, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 As you know, I was one of the senators who voted no, and enthusiastically voted no, because the last thing we need, with inflation galloping like it is, is to spend hundreds of billions of dollars more.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, we're not going to compete with China by having more and more government spending, and more and more debt, and more and more inflation, and hammering our economy. We're going to compete with China by unleashing free enterprise, by cutting taxes, by pulling back job-killing regulations, and letting the free market, letting free enterprise beat the communist economy.\par \par \uc2 That's how we won the Cold War. That's also how we're going to beat China. And listen, if you look at the volume of spending -- I was talking with just a woman in the airport the other week, who was talking about -- she was a single mom from New Jersey, was flying on a plane. And she was saying, inflation is just hammering me.\par \par \uc2 And she says, do you think Congress is going to do anything to fix it?\par \par \uc2 And I told her, I said, listen, this Congress isn't. As long as \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  are in office, they want to spend more, they want to tax more, they want to borrow more, and they want to print more money.\par \par \uc2 And it was interesting. This woman said, well, what is causing inflation? And, at the end of the day, it's not complicated. You and your viewers understand. Listen, prices are just a relative means of comparing the value of one good vs. the other. We started out in a barter economy, that if I had apples and you had bananas, and we knew that the exchange rate was two apples for one banana, I could bring two apples, trade you for a banana, and we would both have more fruit.\par \par \uc2 That got complicated carrying apples and bananas everywhere you went and knowing the relative exchange rates of everything. Money steps in and is a uniform comparison that, if you know that an apple is $1, and a banana is $2, it sets the relative value of everything.\par \par \uc2 But to use this very simple example, if you double the number of dollars on planet Earth...\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: ... then, roughly speaking, an apple will be $2 and a banana will be $4.\par \par \uc2 That's what's happening here, is, the government is massively increasing the money supply, and that drives the prices of everything up.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Both spending and Federal Reserve money creation, which is why I'm sort of begging the Republican Party regarding this bill and other domestic spending, let's not create any more bananas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a lot of bananas are going to add up to a big turkey.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I mean, if we're going to -- if the cavalry is going to come, Ted, Senator, if the cavalry is coming, which is great, I just want to make sure that we know, and the viewers know, and the voters know that the cavalry will be in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 You follow me?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: I...\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I mean, we got a signal here. And, so far, the signals are a little watered down. I think you would agree.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: I do follow you.\par \par \uc2 But I sure hope the Ministry of Truth doesn't have a metaphor police, because, after the bananas, the turkey and the cavalry, I think we'd both be in trouble there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Well, this -- the benefits or whatever of a Princeton education for both of us.\par \par \uc2 Let me move on. You have made a great statement. Senator Cruz blames Supreme Court leak on one little woke left-wing twit. That is fabulous. Really good.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to ask you a serious question. The other headline I'm seeing in the papers, White House refuses to condemn the betrayal at the Supreme Court. They seem, from Madam Psaki, but also others, completely unencumbered by the corruption of the leak of Justice Alito's report, memo, opinion.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: And that's -- I don't understand that. And I want to get your take on this.\par \par \uc2 Isn't the leak itself important? I know the policy is, but what about the leak?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Look, this is yet another consequence of the Democratic Party being radicalized and taken over by the extreme revolutionary socialist left, and they're willing to destroy institutions.\par \par \uc2 You're seeing Senate Democrats trying to blow up the filibuster, to destroy the filibuster in pursuit of their socialist agenda. This is that same agenda applied to the court. I have got to say, the fact that a draft opinion was leaked before the court decided the case is the gravest violation of the oath and the obligation that law clerks have to the court in the history of the Supreme Court and the history of our nation.\par \par \uc2 As you know, I started my career as a law clerk at the Supreme Court clerking for Chief Justice William Rehnquist. And the secrecy of deliberations and opinions is right at the heart of the court being able to do its job. It's never happened in over two centuries. And no one could even imagine it.\par \par \uc2 I got to say, when the news broke, I stared at my phone in disbelief, stunned, because some woke lefty twit decided, to hell with my obligations to my justice, to hell with my obligations to the Supreme Court or to the rule of law, and decided instead to try to use political pressure to politicize the court.\par \par \uc2 And this is the result of the Democrats' multiyear campaign to politicize the court, to attack the court. That's included \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  standing on the steps of the court, threatening justices by name, saying they have unleashed the whirlwind, and just wait to see what's going to hit them.\par \par \uc2 And to see \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  say, well, we don't have an opinion on the leak...\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: I know.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: ... my God, you have got a responsibility.\par \par \uc2 And you know what? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  used to be -- used to be the chairman of the Judiciary Committee. Somewhere, to the extent his brain is aware of what's going on, he knows how shameful it is that his White House is standing up there saying, we have no opinion on law clerks leaking draft Supreme Court opinions.\par \par \uc2 That undermines the rule of law. It is horrific. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be ashamed of himself and walk out and say, damn straight, we have an opinion. This is wrong. And we're not interested in burning the Supreme Court to the ground.\par \par \uc2 But he's too afraid to do that because he doesn't want to tick off the radicals and the leftists in his party.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes. And he likes to -- he likes to throw fear out there...\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: ... fear that things are going to happen that, as you said earlier, will not happen if Alito's opinion goes through.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, as you know, Biden was pro-life and was against Roe v. Wade for the first couple of decades of his career.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Senator Ted Cruz, thank you, sir. We appreciate it, as always.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Thank you, Larry.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: You bet.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020220505ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 America Drowns In Biden's Malaise; Kamala: Killing The Unborn Is Unpatriotic; Dems Have No Answer To Inflation Hur