ave Chappelle -- here we go again -- there's a slap heart around the world, that's now the tackle witnessed around the world. Dave Chappelle attacked on a stage during a live show. We're going to play the tape. Piers Morgan weighs in, as we continue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And tonight, shocking footage coming out of Los Angeles overnight where comedian Dave Chappelle was attacked on stage during a performance at the Hollywood Bowl. Now, video shows a man rushing onto the stage, tackling Chappelle before being surrounded by security and being detained.\par \par \uc2 Now, the 23-year-old assailant was reportedly carrying a replica gun with a knife blade inside it and he has now been charged with assault with a deadly weapon. Now, Chappelle's okay. He even joked about the attack right after it happened.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Chappelle tells FOX News that he is fully cooperating with police as they investigate what they call an unfortunate and unsettling incident. But footage from outside the incident shows the attacker being carried away on a stretcher with a pretty serious looking arm injury.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chris Rock -- you know, of course, the slap heard around the world, he was also in attendance last night, apparently joked about the incident, asking, was that Will Smith?\par \par \uc2 All right. Here with reaction, the host of "Piers Morgan Uncensored," which by the way, airs new episodes four times a week when you go to FOXNation.com.\par \par \uc2 All right. Across the pond, we'd love to see our friend Piers.\par \par \uc2 Look, when I watched the slap just watched around -- or seen around the world with Will Smith, a part of me was like, okay, if you're that sensitive, why don't you talk it out? It was an overreaction. Now, we see this attack against Chappelle.\par \par \uc2 I happen to think Chris Rock, Dave Chappelle, Larry the Cable Guy, every irreverent comedian, I love those guys. Why are people reacting this way to jokes?\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, FOXNATION HOST: Well, I think, Sean, it's a natural extension of what you see all day long on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and other social media platforms, where you have this canceled culture mob running around, who think that only their view about issues is acceptable. And anyone that differs from that view has to be shamed, abused, punished, cancelled, and now, we're seeing this manifesting itself in physical violence.\par \par \uc2 And it's a very sinister development if comedians can't tell irreverent jokes on stage without a fear of being smashed in the face or worse. This guy had a weapon that could have killed Dave Chappelle.\par \par \uc2 Then, where does that take it? Where does it take freedom of speech, freedom of expression? Where does it take comedy?\par \par \uc2 So I think it's all very unsettling and a direct consequence of what we saw at the Oscars, where I was a bit like you. I was kind of in a way, it was like a scene out of a bar and brawl between one guy this is another guy's wife and it ends up with a slap. But now, I kind of think, you know, actually I said a very bad precedent and the president is now being replicated.\par \par \uc2 And I think if I was a comedian right now like Chappelle or like these guys, with a big following in front of big audiences, you've got to be walking on stage now, particularly if Chris Rocks anywhere near you and worrying about where the next punch is coming from.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, you talked about cancel culture. So I've been doing radio 35 years, I've been on FOX, as you know, 26-1/2 years. In all my time on the air, I have never called for any person to be fired, any show to be canceled, any show to be boycotted.\par \par \uc2 One thing people seem to forget is audiences, they have a choice. They don't have to listen to a radio show. They don't have to watch a TV show. They don't have to go to a comedy show here.\par \par \uc2 What part of freedom in all of this are people missing because when I go to a -- if I'm going to watch a comedian, I want no holds barred. Say anything you want and if you don't, if you're going to be so easily offended, stay home. I'm not offended.\par \par \uc2 Chappelle is funny. He's irreverent. He's politically incorrect. I think he's one of the best comedians out there, along with Chris Rock.\par \par \uc2 And also, yeah, and also, I think what is also unsettling is that he's come under a lot of fire, Dave Chappelle, for some \uc2 Netflix\uc2  specials where he addresses this whole issue of transgenderism in America, and he raises some really interesting points about it, about the -- you know, inequality for example in sport that's arising out of these issues.\par \par \uc2 These should be debated in a free democratic society like the United States and you should better have a vigorous debate about it, and argue and reach points of consensus. But that's not allowed.\par \par \uc2 So, Dave Chappelle made some jokes about the transgender community. I didn't think they were transphobic. I think he was just having fun as he did as he does with everybody.\par \par \uc2 But in return for that, he got death threats. He had threats against his family. He had to up his security, and now, we see him being attacked on stage. And you know, it comes back to what I've said about this new cancel culture, is that in a way, it's a form of the very fascism that these so- called liberals who profess to be woke, it's -- it's a very form of fascism they say they hate most in life.\par \par \uc2 So they're going around trying to dictate what we can find acceptable, what is funny what we're supposed to allow to watch on television or movies you know what statues we can reveal what historical figures we can respect and so on. And if you don't toe the line, now, they're going to use violence to make you do it. That is fascism.\par \par \uc2 And it shouldn't be happening in modern day America or Britain or anywhere else. It's a so-called democracy.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm proud of the fact I can tell you in all the years I've been in media, not once have I ever been in the White House Correspondence Dinner. My record stands. I didn't show up this year, apparently turned out to be a superspreader event anyway. So I'm glad I didn't go.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But I've never been there once.\par \par \uc2 So I've never in my entire life thought that Trevor Noah was funny. Now, I watched the show it seems like every other comedian, late night comedian, is just too liberal. It's always one-sided.\par \par \uc2 But I noticed, I watched some clips after. He went after FOX News, CNN, fake news CNN, CNN plus.\par \par \uc2 He went after Republicans. He went after Democrats. He went after Trump. He went after Biden, he went after everybody. And I said, wow, for the first time, he's funny.\par \par \uc2 Why do these -- why are these late nights -- why these late night comedians take it on a political agenda? Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Well, I think -- I think the point about Trevor Noah, I thought he made a very good point. I say you're concerned about him over the years. But actually I thought he did a very good job that night, because he made this point that when you see what's happening in Ukraine, when you see what happens in Russia, in China, and North Korea, in totalitarian regimes, when you see all this going on, it's really about cancelling people's right to free speech and expression.\par \par \uc2 And when he turned to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and said, no, I'm allowed to insult the president of the United States sitting a few feet away from him, that was a powerful moment where I think everyone in America and around the world should look at that moment and reflect then on what happened with Dave Chappelle. How can it be that Trevor Noah is allowed to do what he did at the White House correspondents dinner and that is free speech at his best, and how 