as actually read it.\par \par \uc2 Partisans on television keep describing \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2  as a hater, somebody who just hates women and hates rights, a sadist whose only pleasure is extinguishing human freedom and happiness, both of which, of course, are predicated on abortion. That's what they're saying about Alito.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're not familiar with \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2 's soul, so we can't confirm or deny this either way. But we can tell you, having actually read his opinion, that there is no hint of any of this in what he has written.\par \par \uc2 In fact, there's not even an attack on abortion. He could be pro-choice, for all we know.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  merely argues that Americans have the right to vote on how abortion is regulated, and for nearly 50 years, Roe v. Wade has prevented voters from doing that. It has taken that right from them. That's it. That's the sum total of the supposedly controversial opinion by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , and if you don't believe it, here's how that opinion concludes, quote: "The Constitution does not prohibit the citizens of each state from regulating or prohibiting abortion. Roe and the Casey decision abrogated that authority. We now overrule those decisions and return that authority to the people and their elected representatives." End quote.\par \par \uc2 Now as a factual matter, not that facts seem to matter too much, but if you cared about them, you couldn't really argue with that claim, because the Constitution does not, in fact, prohibit American voters from banning abortion or for that matter, does not prohibit American voters from legalizing abortion because the U.S. Constitution doesn't mention abortion or allude to it in any way.\par \par \uc2 And anyone who claims that it does, as the Roe decision claims, is lying to you. Read the document for yourself. It's online.\par \par \uc2 So why exactly is everybody so mad at \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 ? It's strange because what Alito is proposing here is the very definition of democracy. We've got a bitter disagreement, in this case, over abortion. Both sides have a case to make. They can take that case to the public. In the end, the public gets to decide who's right by voting on it. That's called self-government. Since when is that scary?\par \par \uc2 Well, since one side decided that self-government is too risky and had to be eliminated. Now, they couldn't say this out loud, of course, because, as we told you, the alternative to democracy is tyranny and no one can be for tyranny.\par \par \uc2 So instead, they played yet another word game, because that's always how it starts. They inverted the definition of the word "democracy" to mean exactly the opposite of what democracy has meant for thousands of years since Athens.\par \par \uc2 In Washington at this point, the word "democracy" now means, "We're in charge of everything. Shut up." That's almost exactly how they're defining democracy.\par \par \uc2 This morning's "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " carried this headline, which, as usual, doesn't seem real, yet is real, quote: "In draft abortion ruling, Democrats see a Court at odds with democracy," because letting people vote on things is now quote, "at odds with democracy."\par \par \uc2 It's hard to believe anybody could say this with a straight face, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can say anything with a straight face.\par \par \uc2 Just today, Biden declared that people who insist on voting for things are not only wrong, they're the single greatest threat facing the United States. People who still believe in yesterday's understanding of democracy, where they have power, are more dangerous, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, than al-Qaeda, the Weather Underground, even the Nazi Party. These people are terrorists, and you know what we do to terrorists? Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is about a lot more than abortion. What happens if you have states c