hould we do about it? But this decision basically says: Hey, guys, go vote on it, because it's a democracy. Why don't you decide? And the response from a lot of people on the left is "Shut up, I'm going to hurt you." What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 ADAM CAROLLA, COMEDIAN AND AUTHOR: Well, it's been pretty effective. I mean, if you look at the last couple of years, you look at BLM, you look at all the riots, they've essentially got their way. I mean, they'd be kind of foolish not to go back to that playbook.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look at COVID, look at anyone who disagreed with anything they said, any doctor that disagreed with anything they said, anyone who tweeted something that was different from their normative views, they were thrown off.\par \par \uc2 So we have let their tactics be effective. I mean, I talk to comedians every day, and they're like, I think about the jokes before I say them. The audience, I worry about. It's been effective. So they're going to keep going with it as long as we let them keep going with it, and it keeps being effective.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, sure, I mean, if you let your kids spray paint your living room, they'd probably keep doing it. Why didn't someone think of that when we allowed a small group of angry people to torch our cities?\par \par \uc2 CAROLLA: I don't know. I mean, you know, if you really think about a stand up club, like a comedy club, it is the town square. You know, we always sort of talk about the digital town square that is \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but a stand-up club is a town square. A person gets up on a three foot riser and amplifies their voice and shares their opinions, and we somehow decided the town square was an antiquated idea or if whoever had the microphone said something they disagreed with.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I now do shows and people in the audience are disagreeing out loud. They're giving their opinion out loud, that never existed in the past. You were the audience, I was the comedian.