\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, their little tantrum, a little nonsensical like who said Black lawyers didn't matter? Like they are morons, but they're going to run our legal system and that's a huge problem.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Sibarium has covered the insanity at elite schools, including Law Schools more extensively than any reporter. He is with "The Washington Free Beacon." We're happy to have him join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot of speculation, again, we never want to impugn the integrity of any individual person, but that it was a radical law clerk who did this. Would that shock you if it turns out to be true?\par \par \uc2 AARON SIBARIUM, "THE WASHINGTON FREE BEACON": No, Tucker, it wouldn't shock me at all, because the students that you just showed are -- that is not an isolated incident. American Law Schools are graduating tons and tons of students like that every year.\par \par \uc2 It's not just UC Hastings. It's not just Yale Law School. It's not just NYU Law. It's not just Harvard Law. It's all the Law Schools. And it's not just law clerks, it is an entire generation of lawyers who are going to go on to work in white shoe law firms, who will go on to staff government agencies, and who will sit on Federal Courts, including potentially a Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 For all we know, the law clerk, whoever it is, will be a Supreme Court Justice one day. That is totally a possibility.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The aptitude levels seem to have fallen. It is not just that they're more political, they seem a lot dumber than they used to be. Is that my imagination?\par \par \uc2 SIBARIUM: You know, it is probably not totally your imagination, but I would caution against under estimating these students, right?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Fair.\par \par \uc2 SIBARIUM: It's easy to look at the craziness, but the reality is that in 20 years, you know, they chalked on the sidewalk "We are the law." That was one of the things they were chalking to protest Roe v. Wade -- the Roe v. Wade potentially being overturned. Well, you know, they are the law, that's the problem. They are or they are going to be the law.\par \par \uc2 There really is no law outside of the people who make and interpret it. And who are those people? It is graduates of Law Schools.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Two great points. Don't underestimate them just because their slogans are dumb doesn't mean they're not clever enough to wreck the country. And "We are the law." Isn't that like a fascist slogan? No person is the law.\par \par \uc2 SIBARIUM: No, well, it is certainly not a particularly democratic slogan.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, it is not.\par \par \uc2 SIBARIUM: Yale law students are not exactly representative of the average American, I think it's safe to say\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, they're not. Your reporting on this has just been great. Aaron, I appreciate you coming on tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIBARIUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the leak at the Supreme Court doesn't have a precedent in modern history except for the leak that got Justice Breyer out of his job, they thought he was too White to hold it, so they leaked that he was retiring, apparently, he didn't want to retire.\par \par \uc2 So two leaks during the Biden administration. It does feel like the Court is really changing, and not for the better.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet Dhillon is a Civil Rights attorney. She is also CEO of the Center for American Liberty, and well-placed to assess the changes.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet, thanks so much for coming on. So people are complaining about the leak. Well, tell us why this is important to the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, LAWYER, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: Well, it's very important, Tucker, for a couple of big reasons. Number one is what does this signal about the institution of the Supreme Court and the law? And that number two, what does it say about how Democrats are using the Court or abusing the Court to achieve their goal?\par \par \uc2 So with respect to the law, it is kind of an old-fashioned profession. People put "Esquire" after their names, and we have this sense of honor, when you're a lawyer and you get the other side's e-mail discussing something confidential by mistake, you're required to turn it back over to the other side.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, when you're a law clerk and I clerked in a Federal Court of Appeals in the Fourth Circuit, you're bound by an absolute code that what is internal is internal and it is not ready to be released until the Judges say that it is.\par \par \uc2 And so what happened here is to pick up on what Aaron said, today's young lawyers have learned to shout down their opponents, have learned that throwing tantrums and brute force sometimes wins over persuasion, and here, I think obviously, to me, a lawyer leaked this, probably one of the law clerks of one of the liberal Justices, were willing to say, well, I don't like where this is going, so I'm going to release this to the mob to get the result that I want. Pressure one of these Justices or two of these Justices, if it's six-three or five-four, to change their vote to perhaps avoid mobs outside their home threatening their families, hate mail, that sort of a thing.\par \par \uc2 And that could actually be effective here. And from now on, if a Justice is dealing with a controversial opinion, there will be a concern that somebody who doesn't agree with it within the building will release it and think about it if you're a lawyer. We've had so many important cases, I was on the winning side of three cases at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  over the last 18 months.\par \par \uc2 Imagine if somebody had tried to release one of those opinions in advance or try to pressure the Justices to vote a different way.\par \par \uc2 And so that's really what's at stake here. And the sanctity of the Court as this independent, deliberative body that really well bakes its 80 or so opinions a year has gone away if Judges are looking over their shoulder.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's tragic. I don't think that innocence is going to be able to be something that we can recover unless there is identification of who did this and punishment.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Now, from a political perspective, it's obvious that what has happened here is like you said earlier in your show, Democrats don't think they can pass these laws in the states and so they are using the brute force against the Court to accomplish these goals politically. That is incredibly evil and it attacks the very foundations of our tripartite system of government, Tucker, and so that's what's at stake here in this leak.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, it is mob rule with violence. It's always the same.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet Dhillon, one of the great -- one of the only Civil Rights attorneys left in this country. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you may have gotten the sense that there is something wrong with the economy, not just at the obvious level, like at the supermarket prices are up, but at a much deeper level like the United States national debt, never been higher, over $30 trillion dollars. So what do you do with that debt?\par \par \uc2 Well, there's only one way out of that debt short of default, and that's inflation and that's why they've created inflation.\par \par \uc2 We talked to someone who explained this better than maybe anyone we've ever heard. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if you've been to the grocery store, or the gas station or the hardware store, if you've bought anything from a house to a stock to a college education to a two by four, you know that inflation is out of control. The question is why?\par \par \uc2 Did Putin really do it? No, Putin didn't do it. The government did it and the government did it on purpose.\par \par \uc2 Our sovereign debt, our debt, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt level has got so high that inflation is the only way out. We've never heard this principle explained more clearly than by a macro economist called Luke Gromen who we just sat down and had an hour long conversation with for FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 The whole thing is worth listening to. Here's part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LUKE GROMEN, ECONOMIST: The U.S., the world is coming to -- I think we're living through the first bursting global sovereign debt bubble in a hundred years, right? And so if we look back in time, 1999, we had a stock bubble, it burst. Policymakers kicked it upstairs to the banking system and the housing market by virtue of creating a housing bubble to offset the lost demand from the stock bubble.\par \par \uc2 We have a housing bubble, it bursts. They kicked that problem upstairs to the sovereign debt level by virtue of the backstops put in place by the American government, foreign governments of their domestic economies around the world.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Tell us -- define the terms if you would, so sovereign debt is what?\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: Just debt written -- debt of a government. So Treasury bonds, bunds in the German.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That is not your credit card bill, it is America's credit.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: No. This is America's credit card bill and it's in its unsecured. This is full faith in credit. It is just bad full faith in credit, right?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right. So the good news is, we haven't pledged Yellowstone to the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: No, exactly. We've not pledged anything to anybody. It's all unsecured. But it's like the old "Superman" movie when we were growing up, right? It is with Superman with Christopher Reeves and Lois Lane where, you know, she's falling out of the building and Superman catches her and says, "I've got you." And she says, "Well, who's got you?"\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Good question.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: Right? So if you go, okay, if we've kicked it from stocks up to the banking system up to the sovereign, there's nowhere to kick this up to, you know, unless the Martians come in and write us a check. The only place to kick this up to is via the currency, via devaluation of the currency.\par \par \uc2 Anytime debt to GDP of a sovereign got to these levels, there's only three ways out. It is either restructuring your default, which is not going to happen. The United States has a printing press, we can -- the only way we would default is if we choose to default, it's not going to happen, in my view.\par \par \uc2 A sustained period of high inflation or a sustained period of financial repression, which is a little bit of the same thing, or even a hyperinflation. I don't think a hyperinflation is happening in the U.S. either.\par \par \uc2 So they've laid out the game plan where they have to do this.\par \par \uc2 How does this tie back to the Russian situation? I do think the Russian situation in terms of the invasion of Ukraine was a huge moment.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: To bottom line it again for the Humanities Majors in the audience, we've got too much debt. The only way out in real life is inflation.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: You have to inflate it away, and that's the only politically palatable option.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So when you see inflation, and God knows how you measure this really, we tried to even like -- what is inflation? It is pretty hard to define, it feels like.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But when you see it as a macro economist, you're not shocked?\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: No. We've been saying for a while in the aftermath of the COVID crisis that these factors had been building up, up to that point, and then when you see what debt did as a result, and I think it was a necessity that debt did what they did. They couldn't just stand aside in 2020 and let the system come unhinged chaotically, that doesn't serve anybody's interest.\par \par \uc2 But by virtue of that crisis, the steps they had to take to make sure the system didn't collapse boosted the debt levels to what sort of, you know, an irrecoverable --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's crazy.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: ... to borrow from the "Top Gun" metaphor, right? There's just -- there is no getting out of it. And so at that point, you've got to inflate it away.\par \par \uc2 And so I think you can see the steps of the serial stimulus, the stimulus in 2021 where maybe we didn't really need it. We're already recovering. We had the vaccines, but they did it again.\par \par \uc2 And you can see in the data, you can see last year we ran into the --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, this is the Congress and the Fed causing inflation as a way out of debt.\par \par \uc2 GROMEN: Effectively, it is the only policy prescription, the only politically and economically palatable policy prescription once you get the debt to where it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So as you get poor from inflation, remember they're doing this to you on purpose. That's on FOX Nation tomorrow, 7:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They say we only have 10 seconds left, but we have a lot more to say. Just kidding.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you tomorrow. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220505ei5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 J.D. Vance Wins Republican Senate Rave In Ohio; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Excoriates "Ultra-MAGA Agenda" Of Florida Senator; Federal Reserve Implements Biggest Hike In Two Decades; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Effect On Key Congressional Races; 77 Percent Of Voters Say Condition Is Only Fair Or Poor; Zelenskyy Reacts To Kremlin Nazi Allegation; Zelenskyy Accuses Russia OF Stealing Ukrainian Wheat; Investigation Continues Into Source Of Leaked Draft Of Supreme Court Decision Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Biden Administration Continues To Receive Criticism Of Immigration And Border Policy; Democratic Ohio Senate Nominee Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  Interviewed On Campaign Against Republican Nominee J.D. Vance; Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  Does Not Indicate He Wants President Biden Campaigning For Him In Ohio\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Alexandria Hoff, Peter Doocy, Brit Hume, Griff  Jenkins, David Spunt, Bryan Llenas \par \uc2 7,706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: May 4th, may the fourth be with you. I was always -- to tell you the truth, I love "Star Wars", but I'm really a "Star Trekky" person because that's when I had my first network show, William Shatner. That was my years.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Oh, I like it.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: That was my years.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, well that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Spock? What about Spock? (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 All right, thanks, Dana. OK. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Trump's favorite candidate wins the Ohio Republican Senate primary. J.D. Vance finished first in a crowded race. He will face Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in November's general election for the seat held by retiring GOP Senator Rob Portman. We will talk live with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says he thinks it's very important that President Biden come to his country. Zelenskyy spoke with Fox News Correspondent Griff Jenkins. We'll hear those exclusive comments from the interview a bit later.\par \par \uc2 The investigation is picking up into the leaking of a draft of \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  opinion on the Roe versus Wade abortion decision. The documents suggest that justices may overturn the landmark ruling. Demonstrations were held in cities across the country yesterday.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  announced a substantial half point hike in its benchmark short term interest rate. Additional increases are expected at the Fed's next two meetings, markets reacted positively with big gains today.\par \par \uc2 Now, to our top story, with the first big primary election day in the books, it appears former President Trump may still have a large influence in Republican Party politics. He's got juice at least when it comes to Ohio.\par \par \uc2 The candidate he backed in Ohio came up with a big win yesterday. And there's increasing focus on the unfolding contest in Pennsylvania, where the Trump machine is backing an entrant with an unusual resume.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff reports tonight from Homestead, Pennsylvania. Good evening, Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Yes, we are here in western Pennsylvania where Republican candidates will face off in a debate tonight. Six of the seven hoping to prove that they can win over Trump supporters without the former president backing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): It was a come from behind victory for author and businessman J.D. Vance in Ohio's Republican Senate primary aided in large part by Trump's mid-April endorsement.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF OHIO: They wanted to write a story that this campaign would be the death of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's America First agenda. Ladies and gentlemen, it isn't the death of the America First agenda.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In a statement following the win, the former president touted the success of all 22 candidates who backed in Ohio and Indiana. A spokesperson writing the power of President Trump's endorsement is undeniable, his dominance over the Republican power brokers in D.C. cannot be understated.\par \par \uc2 But in Pennsylvania, Trump's support may not have as large of an impact. Trump endorsed Dr. Mehmet Oz last month but his race is still a toss-up among seven candidates. The latest \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll puts Oz neck and neck with former hedge fund CEO David McCormack. We asked McCormick about Trump's influence.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMACK (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: He's very popular and Mehmet Oz isn't very popular. And the reason he's not popular is because his positions don't line up with primary voters.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: On the other side, Democratic Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman is holding a comfortable lead in his party Senate primary, dismissing that he's a socialist and accusation made by his closest competitor Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. JOHN FETTERMAN (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Neither he nor I are socialists, obviously. But I guess, you know, when -- you know, you have -- you know, trouble in polls and money, you know, you say things that are desperate.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In Ohio, a push from progressives, including Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  did not pan out. Former State Senator \uc2 Nina Turner\uc2  lost out to Congresswoman Shontel Brown in a congressional primary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Now, tomorrow morning, we expect to speak with Dr. Oz. He has taken heat from opponents on past comments he has made regarding masks and abortion.\par \par \uc2 And on Friday, former President Trump will be holding a rally here in the Commonwealth. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you. We'll talk with Democrats winner in the primary. Again, Ohio Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in just a bit.\par \par \uc2 The Fed announcement of an interest rate hikes sent stocks soaring today. The half point increase is the largest since the year 2000. The Dow gained 932 today, almost three percentage points. The S&P 500 was up 125, its best day in two years. The NASDAQ surge 401, more than three percentage points there as well.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is making another major push tonight for support of his struggling economic agenda. He's focusing on not just policies, but personalities and MAGA.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes against the backdrop of Tuesday's revelation about the coming \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  action expected on Roe vs. Wade abortions ruling.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me tell you about this ultra MAGA agenda, it's extreme.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Democrats fighting to control Congress having new target.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Who serves as the chairman of the National Republican Senatorial Committee. The president's team argues today's interest rate hike is all part of their projections that will lower inflation and keep taxes low.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Congressional Republicans want to raise taxes on half of Americans.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But that claim was given three Pinocchios by the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  last month. Now, Senator Scott is responding.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): What the Democrats are doing or what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing, he just keeps passing the buck and watching these plans go bankrupt, and he'll wait for the next president or the next Senate have to try to fix these things. So, what he just did was a complete lie.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president's comments about social issues even stronger.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: MAGA crowd has replaced mentions of Trump.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He's not on the ballot, a number of his of Republicans who have seemed to be under the whim of his predecessor.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This White House rarely comments on leaked materials but are making an exception for the leaked Roe v. Wade draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: What our focus is on is not getting our -- distracted or our eye off the ball of what is most important to people across the country here, which is not the leak and the story of the leak. It is the fact that women's health care is at risk for millions of people across this country.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The vice president has already given a rousing political speech about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're not going back.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Even as officials claim this issue is about more than politics.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Did I say it's not a political issue?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes. You actually said some called it a political issue, it is not.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, because the vast majority of the public believes that this should not -- that this should not be overturned. Meaning, I meant to say it's not a partisan issue, and I don't think it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): But it is a political issue. And it is a partisan issue based on the language the president and the vice president are using because they have both already said they think that this -- the substance of this leaked draft opinion concerning Roe v. Wade shows the importance of electing prochoice Democrats, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You just had a weekend with the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2 ' Dinner. Now, you have the Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  with COVID. What did the White House said?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House is telling us that he tested positive, he is not considered a close contact of the president and they have not seen each other in several days.\par \par \uc2 Blinken though one of many administration officials present at that Correspondents' Dinner. You can see some video there. The Pentagon announced today none of their attendees at the dinner have tested positive.\par \par \uc2 It has now been four days since that event. And they're telling us President Biden who was center stage tested negative yesterday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live on the North Lawn Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel early. Tonight, Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Amy Walter, publisher and editor in chief of The Cook Political Report, Jason Riley, Wall Street Journal columnist, senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, and Forbes media CEO Steve Forbes, co-author of the new book Inflation: What It Is, Why It's Bad and How to Fix It.\par \par \uc2 Steve, let me start with you with economic news before we turn to Ohio, we'll get Brit on camera here momentarily. This fed hike and the market reaction to it. All of these things seem to be baked in the cake usually, the market was seemed very pleased with this move today.\par \par \uc2 STEVE FORBES, CEO, \uc2 FORBES MEDIA\uc2 : While the market was anticipating what happened today, what they are not anticipating is that the chairman Powell or chair Powell was going to not raise interest rates as much as they thought he might in the next meeting in a few weeks. And so, that's why the market jumped up the way it did. The punchbowl is still out there.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  announced that they were also not going to remove some of the excess money it created at a pace that the markets thought might be possible.\par \par \uc2 But I think the market -- this market rally will be a brief one, because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  still believes that they were -- the only way you can ultimately control inflation is by slowing the economy. They hope not to tip it into recession. But by waiting so long as they did to finally confront the problem, they risked not a soft landing, but a very bumpy or even a crash landing with the economy into a full blown recession.\par \par \uc2 Ba OK, let's turn to Ohio now and the results of that Ohio primary and J.D. Vance with the winner there. The total votes about over a million and you've got J.D. Vance with the winning in crowded field but because essentially the former president's endorsement. Brit Hume, your thoughts on this in the fall out of it.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, this is a clear win for Trump in Ohio. Not because J.D. Vance won by such a big margin. It was still just a plurality down in what the 20s.\par \par \uc2 But Vance was trailing significantly. And when Trump endorsed him, that completely changed, and he shot to the top of the pack and won the primary. So, it's a big win for him, for Trump, a big win for J.D. Vance. It'll be interesting to see whether Trump's influence extends in other states, particularly Georgia, which is coming up soon. And whether his candidate -- his favorite candidate Dr. Oz wins here in Pennsylvania where I am now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy, your thoughts? Obviously, this was a big night for the former president, the first test really in this primary season.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, PUBLISHER AND EDITOR IN CHIEF, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: You know, when you look at the numbers coming out of that race, J.D. Vance's percent of the vote something like 32 percent, pretty much mirrors what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got here in 2016 as a candidate for president. He lost, of course to John Kasich who was then the governor.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that they both got the same percentage belies the fact that most of the other candidates in this 2022 primary though they didn't get Trump's endorsement, they ran as Trump like candidates.\par \par \uc2 So, J.D. Vance, I agree with Bret that it gave him some sort of rocket fuel in what was a very crowded, undefined race in the last few, basically the last couple of weeks of this election.\par \par \uc2 But whoever would have come out of that race, pretty much any one of those candidates would have been someone that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would have liked to see and was somebody that Donald -- that parroted much of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's language, style and policies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Washington Examiner Jason says Trump goes 55 and 0, no endorsements lost. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might want to consider reopening the Taj Mahal Casino in Atlantic City the way his luck has been holding. According to an early tabulation of his betting on the 2022 primaries, his endorsed candidates have all won or moved into runoffs. Significant?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, WALL STREET JOURNAL: I guess I'm with Brit and Amy a little bit here where I wouldn't read too much into the Ohio results. Ohio has been trending Republican in recent elections.\par \par \uc2 It's a state Trump won pretty handily in 2020. So, it's not too surprising that a candidate who ran in the mold of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  prevailed.\par \par \uc2 I'm more interested in seeing whether the Trump brand holds in Pennsylvania. And as Brit mentioned, in Georgia. I think those will -- those are two states that Trump lost. And we'll see what the impact will be in those states.\par \par \uc2 I do think the Supreme Court leak on the abortion cases has put some of these Democrats in a bind, particularly Democrats that are trying to run as moderates in the states.\par \par \uc2 They don't want to really talk about social issues. That's not something they had planned to have to discuss. They want to brag about their ability to win Republican votes. And I think they're going to be put on the spot here coming out to declare themselves as not just slightly, you know, pro- choice but vehemently pro-choice, almost abortion on demand pro-choice, and that is not what they want to do.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that presents a challenge for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it is a challenge, Steve Forbes, in an environment where the economy really drives a lot of decision making and thoughts about voting.\par \par \uc2 FORBES: Well, that's right. And the hope of Republicans is that this issue will be not superseded, but there will be voting on other issues come November.\par \par \uc2 And that's why that Supreme Court decision, we'll have to see what they finally come out with it. But it certainly it has given the democrat a lifeline that they didn't have before.\par \par \uc2 But I think ultimately, voters are going to go with the economy where Congress can make a real difference. And so, I think that the Democrats euphoria won't be long lived.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, the deficit is coming up now. President Biden is talking about the deficit and who gets the credit for knocking it down, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The bottom line is the deficit went up every year under my predecessor before the pandemic and during the pandemic. And it's gone down both years since I've been here, period. That's -- their the facts. And why is it important? Because bringing down the deficit is one way to ease inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget says no, President Biden has not implemented historic deficit reduction. The main source of falling deficits is the expiration of most COVID relief such as enhanced employment -- unemployment benefits, and recovery rebates.\par \par \uc2 The remaining decrease is largely the result of strong income growth and high inflation between the American Rescue Plan, the bipartisan infrastructure law and various executive orders. We estimated at least $2.5 trillion has been added to deficits through 2031 over the president's term so far, so just a fact check on some of that talk. It's interesting to hear though.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, it is. And when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , after all the spending that's gone on is talking about how he and his administration have significantly reduced the deficit. I don't think that's going to wash with very many voters.\par \par \uc2 And look at it this way, Bret. The deficit and the national debt are issues that worry voters. But they don't feel it. What they feel is inflation right now. And they all feel it. Every single voter in the country feels it. Even people who are rich enough that it doesn't really affect their standard of living still notice it.\par \par \uc2 And it is a -- it is always been politically about as corrosive an issues, you can imagine. A war going badly would compete with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: So, there is nothing they can say about the deficit or the economy right now, barring some striking decline in inflation or at least halting of it that's going to change the fact that, that issue is going to work very strongly against the Democrats in this midterm, and it will be the main issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy, last word here. Our latest Fox News poll on economy, and inflation, the condition of the economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Now, good, 21 percent. Only fair or poor, 77 percent in our latest poll. It's interesting to see how the abortion issue will play around the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): But boy, that number seems pretty upside down.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NATIONAL EDITOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Now, I agree with Bret here that -- Bret here that it is something that people feel, and when they feel it, you can't talk about anything else, or convince them that things are better than they are when this is what's going on.\par \par \uc2 I do think though in some individual cases, especially at the governor's level, this issue of abortion could be a much bigger factor. It may not be the number one issue but it is going to rank up there, simply because if this decision does hold true, the battle now goes on to the states and states -- battleground states, Arizona, Georgia, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, those -- Pennsylvania, those are the places where I think it will have a significant role.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it will be a factor. Question is how big in different places?\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you later on the show.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Up next, Fox News speaks exclusively with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  about the ongoing war with Russia and what the West needs to be doing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell me how this ends.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Only with victory. We have no way out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ukraine's president says President Biden should come to his country to see what the war with Russia has met. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  spoke exclusively with Fox News correspondent Griff Jenkins today about the 10-week long conflict. Griff reports tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): The Foreign Minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  compared you to Hitler. Russia now says that Israel supports the neo-Nazi regime here and Kyiv, referring to you. Your reaction.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We are used to these kinds of hits. And the reaction I think, is to weaken the world to these phrases of Lavrov. I think that the reactions must be more powerful, the reaction must be lightning like.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of the world, each year and the time of, you know, of this anniversaries or special days of holocaust, et cetera, and they always say that. Never again, we have to, you know, we have to fight. It's about diplomacy. It's about, you know, something very official, don't wait. That is my message. Don't wait official dates.\par \par \uc2 When we talk about the anti-Semitism, I have not heard from any sort of Jewish communities or societies on the territory of Russia.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: So, it's suggested western intelligence that Putin may officially declare a war. What do you think will happen on May 9th? And if it's a declaration of war, where would that change for you?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: For us, this is not going to change anything. For us, war is going on for eight years now. The full pledged war is going on for 70 days and whatever Putin is going to say, the possibility or the probability is there, they want to show what kind of czars they are.\par \par \uc2 They ruined the entire city. That will be like in the bad Hollywood movie. Is that the victory that they are striving for? For the civilized world, this is defeat. The May of 9th is going to be remembered in the world as the tragedy, as a bloody day, and certainly not the day to forget.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Do you have what you need? Do you need more? And what do you need?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: We have the key weapons. Was that which we could not de occupy the cities like Mariupol. We have a big issue -- Russia are blocked all the trade routes, and we cannot export our wheat, they occupied our ports and they're taking out our goods.\par \par \uc2 And I don't want to say this now, I don't want to name specific countries who is behind our backs, are making these deals to buy our grain from Russia that was stolen from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Can you tell me who is trying to buy this --\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Not now. But we, but we have the names of this countries.\par \par \uc2 But if, if they will do it, of course, we'll tell.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: How important is it that President Biden come to Kyiv to see Bucha or the outcome what has happened here?\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: It's important. I think it's very important. Because, you know, in our minds, in our society, the President Biden, the president of the biggest democratic civilization, for Ukrainians, for our understanding, yes.\par \par \uc2 That's it. That our partners, strategic partners, and I think that it would be a great signal, very import signal.\par \par \uc2 Johnson came, I just thinking that it's also very good for him. That is good for him, because United States supports us. So, I mean the president of the country, which support us, I think, is good for him to be here.\par \par \uc2 If it's possible, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Well, Speaker Pelosi came.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Yes, so, it's possible.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Tell me how this ends.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Only with victory. We have no way out.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Mr. President, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, thank you. You can catch more of Griff's exclusive interview, Saturday on "FOX NEWS LIVE" noon Eastern right here on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, trying to find out who leaked the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  draft opinion on Roe vs. Wade. It is a Washington who done it now.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Israel honors its soldiers and civilians killed in wars and militant attacks with sirens wailing nationwide for Memorial Day.\par \par \uc2 Ceremonies and poetry readings were held at cemeteries for more than 24,000 people lost in Israel's conflicts.\par \par \uc2 South Korean and Japanese officials say North Korea has launched a ballistic missile toward its eastern waters. It comes days after leader Kim Jong-un vowed to speed up development of nuclear weapons and threatened to use them against rivals.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 National Football League\uc2  announces its international schedule for the coming season. It features the league's first regular season game in Germany, with the \uc2 Tampa Bay Buccaneers\uc2  facing the \uc2 Seattle Seahawks\uc2  November 13th. There will be three games in England in October and one in Mexico in November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The search is intensifying into who leaked a draft of a \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  opinion concerning the nation's bedrock abortion law. Correspondent David Spunt is at the court tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In a city where information leaks are common, the Supreme Court stood above the scrum, until now. The marshal of the court, at the direction of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , is undertaking a high-profile investigation into a leak one court observer called the gravest, most unforgivable sin.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SMITH, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY LAW CENTER: It's truly shocking, truly appalling and is, in my experience, unheard of.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Paul Smith has argued 21 times in front of the Supreme Court and clerked for the late Justice Lewis Powell. He thinks this leak possibly came from only a few people, a clerk, a secretary, top court staffer, or a justice.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The oath of confidentiality that you take is one that is just on your honor as a law clerk or a justice or a secretary.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sources say the whodunit list of suspects could also include a disgruntled court employee or a justice's spouse. The court's marshal handles security for the justices and the building. There are growing calls for the Department of Justice and \uc2 FBI\uc2  to intervene. Neither is involved at this stage, though it's not clear if that may change. The draft opinion contains no classified information, so no crime may have been committed.\par \par \uc2 Before becoming a senator from Utah, \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  was a law clerk for Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , the author of the draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE, (R-UT) FORMER JUSTICE ALITO LAW CLERK: When they are finished with them, they don't throw them in a waste basket. They out them in a burn bag. They take the burn bag and they shred it twice vertically, horizontally, creating confetti.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The motive of the leaker or leakers is unclear, with speculation aimed at the chamber of both liberal and conservative justices. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt outside the court. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Actor Amber Heard took the stand in her own defense today, fighting the defamation lawsuit filed by former husband Johnny Depp. She talked about incidents of domestic violence which she says began after she made a comment about one of his tattoos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMBER HEARD, ACTOR: I just laughed because I thought he was joking. And he slapped me across the face. And I laughed. I laughed because I didn't know what else to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Depp contends an essay by Heard about the domestic violence ruined his career.\par \par \uc2 The judge overseeing the federal civil rights cases of four former Minneapolis police officers in the killing of George Floyd says he has accepted the terms of Derek Chauvin's plea agreement. The judge says he will sentence Chauvin to 20 to 25 years in prison. Chauvin pleaded guilty to violating Floyd's civil rights, admitting for the first time that he kept his knee on Floyd's neck even after he became unresponsive, resulting in the black man's death on May 25th, 2020.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration continues to take considerable heat over its immigration policy along the southern border. Much of the criticism is focused on the president's Homeland Security secretary now. Correspondent Bryan Llenas reports again tonight from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We have effectively managed a unprecedented number of non-citizens seeking to enter the United States.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas continues to say the Biden administration is successfully handling the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN HOEVEN, (R-ND): And 1.7 million illegal encounters last year, more than 1 million in the first six months of this year. Are you going to sit there and pretend that's not going to be and say that you are actually enforcing the law?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, we most certainly are, Senator.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE RICHARD CORTEZ, (D) HIDALGO COUNTY, TEXAS, COURT: There has to be a better answer than what we have today, because this is unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Hidalgo County Judge Richard Cortez, a Democrat, penned a letter to President Biden urging him to come see the crisis.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: What I'm asking the president is to come and visit us so he can have firsthand knowledge of what is happening in the border. All of us are very frustrated that the border issues continue with no solution.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: President Biden has never visited the southern border in his political career. Judge Cortez says the feds need to fix the unprecedented migrant surge because local resources are strained.\par \par \uc2 Large groups of migrants continue crossing daily, a dozen migrants crossed the Rio Grande this morning. We encountered nine immigrants including this pregnant mom from Cuba. Over the last few days, about 70 migrants were apprehended in Normandy, Texas, and 62 migrants found in a stash house in Laredo.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: We have a leak. And the leak is causing problems. I need a plumber to come and stop it. Instead of sending me a plumber, they are sending me people to mop the floor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Judge Cortez says that lifting Title 42, which is the COVID-19 deportation policy, will encourage more illegal immigration. He says the ports of entry would be overrun and overwhelmed by asylum seekers, further pressuring an exhausted Border Patrol. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas along the border in Eagle Pass. Bryan, it's the last night down there. Nice job on the border.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we will talk with Ohio Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  who will take on Republican J.D. Vance in November for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat in Ohio. And we'll tell you why this is a big day for "Star Wars" fans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: The opportunities in this state depend on us fighting and winning against \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in November, because as much as he will try to claim that he is one thing, his voting record and the evidence suggest that he is the very worst of modern American politics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tuesday's primary elections reinforce the juice that former president Trump has in the Republican Party now. As you heard there, J.D. Vance set to battle \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  for the Ohio Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Democratic Ohio Senate nominee Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Congressman, thanks for joining us. I want you to get a response to J.D. Vance there. The Vance campaign says how are you going to distance yourself from President Biden since have you voted with him 100 percent of the time?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN, (D-OH) OHIO SENATE NOMINEE: J.D. Vance has said America is a joke. And I don't think America is a joke. I think America is the greatest country in the history of the world. J.D. Vance said that he is not comfortable in Ohio, and there is no place I'd rather be than Ohio. I have a long record of fighting for working people, bringing resources back here, passing a bipartisan infrastructure bill, working to bring a high chip manufacturing company like \uc2 Intel\uc2  to Ohio. I'm about making Ohio be the world's manufacturing powerhouse. And I have got a long record on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Congressman, you look at the headlines in Ohio after last night, and pretty much across the board, it all includes one thing, and that is Trump the word "Trump." Former president Trump passes the test as kingmaker in Ohio, puts Vance over the top, all kinds of headlines dealing with that.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, you have President Biden delivering a speech today where he talks about the MAGA agenda and how it is so extreme. You come and represent a place where a lot of voters voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  both in 2016 and 2020. So how are you going to convince Ohio that President Biden and talking about the MAGA agenda being extreme is a good thing for your race in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I think first and foremost is we got to get out of this Democrat-Republican food fight. We've got start looking at each other as Americans. And look, I ran against \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . I have taken on Bernie Sanders. I have disagreed with Obama. I've even agreed with President Trump on renegotiating \uc2 NAFTA\uc2 , on being tough on China. So we've got to get away from which side are you on? You got the red shirt on or the blue shirt on. We need to all put the red, white, and blue shirt on and started thinking of ourselves as Americans so we can take on China, who is cleaning our clock, out-manufacturing us left and right all over the world. It's time for us to come together.\par \par \uc2 China wants us to keep fighting. Russia wants us to keep fighting. And so it's time for us to come together, to rebuild the great American middle class, which is the source of our power at home and around the world. And we can't do it if we keep fighting these old battles that we have been fighting for decades that get us absolutely nowhere.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've heard you on the stump, and you talk bread and butter issues, economy, kitchen table issues. Are you worried at all that this \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  leak, the draft opinion and whatever the ruling is going to be, somehow makes Democrats talk about abortion more on the national stage, and that effects somehow your pitch to Ohio?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I talk about freedom. I talk about freedom as a basic American value all the time. And I talk about it mostly in the context of economic freedom. I think economic freedom means you got enough money in your pocket to pay your bills, maybe go on vacation, and not have to work six or seven days a week, 12, 15 hours a day. That's economic freedom.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the Supreme Court decision, that's an issue of freedom. Do we really think as a country that the government should force someone who has been raped to have to bring the pregnancy to term? That is antithetical to a basic American value like freedom. So these --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As senator, I don't want to interrupt, but as senator --\par \par \uc2 RYAN: -- basic values are important all across the board.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As senator, would you have any limits on abortion?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Look, I think what we had established in Roe is something that we can continue to work with. And I think those can be the parameters. But then again, if you get rid of what was established law, which in many ways was conservative, to keep that to appreciate stare decisis and make sure we appreciate the law, if we move away from that, you're going to get state like Ohio that have some of the most extreme laws in the whole country where if you are a young girl, and you have been raped or there has been incest, that you can't -- you have to -- the state, the government is going to force you to bring that baby to term. And I just don't think that's a fundamental value.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I get all that, Congressman, but my question was about any limits to abortion at any point, late term, anything?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Look, you got to leave it up to the woman, because you and I --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So no is the answer.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You and I sitting here can't account for all of the different scenarios that a woman dealing with the complexities of a pregnancy are going through. How can you and I figure that out?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I appreciate your straightforward answer.\par \par \uc2 Last thing here, obviously in the campaign, when you were rubbing, as a presidential candidate, you talked about and were asked about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and how he reacts to things, how he talks about things. Here is from a debate. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: There's sometimes a lack of clarity, and I think that's what I'm hearing on the ground. I think that's what a lot of people are thinking. And we can't afford that at this point, and that's what these primaries are for, to figure out who is the best person to put forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You said he had a lack of clarity and asked about his stumbles. Do you think it's gotten better as president?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think there's a lot of people in public life that aren't as clear as I would like them to be, both Democrats and Republicans. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called-said J.P. Mandel was the candidate, mixing up both J.D. Vance and Josh Mandel just the other day. So look, these are sideshows.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I got it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: The American people need help. We've got to rebuild the middle class. We've got to beat China. These are the kind of stupid fights, quite frankly, Bret, that the American people are tired of.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hear your.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm representing the exhausted majority. The exhausted majority has a mommy in my campaign, whether you're Republican, Democrat, or independent, and we welcome everybody. We have got to heal, we've got to care about each other. We've got to move forward, and we've got to beat China. Rebuild the middle class, beat China.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, you welcome everybody, you welcome everybody, Congressman. But I heard you in an interview today really not answer if you want President Biden on the campaign trail in Ohio, the sitting president of the United States. I didn't hear an answer to that. Do you or do you not?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: My answer was I want to be the face of this campaign and I don't want any distractions. I'm tired of the Democrat-Republican thing. I want us to move forward together and start to heal and rebuild the middle class and beat China. And I want to be the face and the voice of this campaign, and I don't want any distractions. I don't need anybody to prop me up like J.D. Vance did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even from the sitting president of the United States of your party, right?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even from the sitting president of the United States of your party, who you voted with 100 percent of the time?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We'll make that decision as the campaign continues. But some of those votes are to bring a huge multibillion dollars intel project to Ohio creating thousands of jobs at $135,000 a year, or the bipartisan infrastructure bill that's going to create 600,000 jobs in the state of Ohio, or other initiatives that are going to get lead out of our pipes so our kids can drink clean water, or $1 billion that we're going to put into the Great Lakes Restoration Project.\par \par \uc2 So those things I voted for are for Ohio. And I don't have any billionaire like \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  pulling my strings or writing me a $10 million check. I'm here for Ohio -- 97 percent of my donations are under $100. And that's who is powering my campaign. And nobody is going to own me. I'm going to vote. As I told you, I have gotten in fights with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and ran against her and Bernie Sanders and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , and agreed with Trump. I'm down the middle. I play it straight, and I'm for Ohio. That's --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've covered all of those. We've covered all of those. And we appreciate you coming on, answering questions. We'll be out in Ohio to cover that race. Thank you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Just bring the orange tie with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You got it. You got it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you. J.D. Vance, by the way, will you be on TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT, and we will hear his reaction to the primary as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel returns. More reaction to the midterm primary elections and politics overall.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Capitol Hill, the Senate side. These primaries, we are less than six months to Election Day, midterm elections. Let's bring back our panel, Brit Hume, Amy Walter, Jason Riley with us. Brit, your thoughts of that interview with Congressman Ryan as he gets ready for this Senate race. A couple interesting things. He does not want President Biden, it appears, to campaign in Ohio. I didn't hear any limits on abortion. And overall, there is a distancing trying to happen with the administration.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. You can certainly see that, Bret. It was a good interview. I think Ryan is definitely an underdog in this race. He had a close call the last time out. And the state is trending Republican. And the fact that he is running away from the sitting president is telling. And I think no limits on abortion, ducking that was, I think he could safely come out and be for some, but he didn't. So I think he has got a serious uphill climb.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amy?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NATIONAL EDITOR, "COOK POLITICAL REPORT": Yes, this state is going to be a tough one with not just the way that the state has trended but really significant headwinds there. Look, I think that as you could hear from the interview, Ryan is trying to run as a populist blue collar Democrat. There is a successful Democrat in that mold in the state \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , Democratic senator there. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , though, has had one benefit that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  doesn't have. He has run in years that are good for Democrats -- 2006, 2012 when Obama was winning that state, and most recently in 2018.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes. This is a big problem for Democrats, and you heard it in the interview, particularly for moderate Democrats. And that is the key to Democrats holding on to the majority in the House, winning a majority, a real majority in the Senate, these moderates. And they have a real, real dilemma here.\par \par \uc2 And these people spinning this Supreme Court leak as overwhelmingly supporting Democratic candidates, I think they have it wrong. There are millions of women in this country who are pro-life. And the pro-choice movement tries to ignore them, tries to erase them, but they are there. And people like \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  know it, and that explains a lot of the hesitancy in his answers to your questions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's going to be an interesting race, Brit. We have a long way to go. But a lot of things will happen between now and then.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, I think so. But I think die, to some extent, is cast on what will be the major issues. A despite the fact that there's going to be a lot of noise about this Supreme Court decision if it comes down, as it appears it may, I don't think it will overtake the other major issues that we've been talking about for some time, the economy in particular, the border, crime in the cities, and so on are major concerns.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you so much. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, it is a special day. People around the world celebrating "Star Wars" day. This is May the Fourth, as in "may the force be with you," the fourth be with you. Creators of "Star Wars" announced Obi-Wan Wednesday is promote yet another spinoff T.V. series related products.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, fair, balanced, and unafraid. May the force be with Brian Kilmeade, starting "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220505ei5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Leak From A Supreme Court Draft Decision Over Roe v. Wade Fuel Outrage To Women On The Streets, While Democrats And The Liberal Media Using The Issue To Drive Atten