cks Eastern Ukraine; DOD Briefs After North Korea Tests Ballistic Missile; Ukraine: Russia Planning Mariupol "Victory" Parade; Dire Humanitarian Situation Unfolds In Mariupol; Trump Prevails In First Test Of Endorsement Power; Country Star Shared Inspiring Story Of Friendship; White House On Leaked Court Documents End Of Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, David Spunt, Joe Concha, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 9,420 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR: They've got to start paying attention to the issues that matter to the average American family.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Ambassador Haley, really appreciate you joining us and sticking around. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you so much. And thanks so much. To all of you for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . We'll see you again tomorrow. "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum at Fox News headquarters in New York. Moments away, former Attorney General Bill Barr, on the situation unfolding at the Supreme Court, as new speculation arises over the Supreme Court leaker, who he or she is, and what kind of consequences they may now face -- plus his reaction to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This MAGA crowd is really the most extreme political organization that's existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that was the president earlier today and taking a look at Ohio, where all of the Trump back candidates won their primaries in a big test of the former president's clout. We will speak with voters in Ohio on the ground there, find out what folks in that really important state are feeling and thinking today.\par \par \uc2 But first and very tough numbers for President Biden, lowest yet approval rating on his handling of the economy. 61 percent disapprove, the overall approval number is down nine points in the past year, he's at 45 percent. Vice President Harris also in the same neighborhood there, she's at 41 percent. From a year ago, Neil Cavuto, anchor of "YOUR WORLD" joins me now. Neil, great to have you with us this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As we take a look at the market, which is up strongly on the heels of this Fed rate decision. And I want to ask you about the president how he's doing on the economy. But you know, first Neil, to that question, because it's an effort to stem inflation, will it work?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's the only game in town, if you think about it. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , certainly more government spending isn't going to cure this. Many argue, we're in the in the position we're in because we've been spending like crazy. And that was even before the war Ukraine and everything else. But leaving that aside, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  seems to be doing what people thought it would.\par \par \uc2 It's hiking interest rate to half a, half a percentage point, we haven't seen a move like that, since 2000. If you think about it, Martha, when Bill Clinton was president, the idea seems to be as \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the chairman now talks to the press about this, that more hikes are on the way. They're OK with that, because the hikes even though they seem to be fairly dramatic, another half point now another half point, presumably, at the next Fed meeting next month, maybe successive hikes after that interest rates could go up pretty smartly and pretty quickly from here.\par \par \uc2 But since they're the only game in town addressing this, you have plenty of critics who say too little too late, but they are the only ones trying to stem this and then the only ones getting something done. Now, whether this is overdoing it or not, the markets certainly don't seem to think that's the case, right now that they're ahead of this, and they're wishing him luck fighting this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, it's fascinating to watch it. And I am interested in the way that you're putting it, which is the only game in town because you've got certain levers to try to get this thing under control. And this is one of them, and you can see in the bottom-right hand side of your screen, how the market has responded to that up 623 points right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to pull up this Gallup Poll actually confidence in President Biden to do the right thing for the economy down 17 points over the course of the last year. Your assessment on these numbers, they match up with the other number that I just showed, which was a Fox poll, Neil, what do you make of how Americans are looking at this presidency and the economy?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you know, when things are going well, you give a pass and you credit the President, when things aren't going well, the buck stops at the White House. And that's where it is right now. And a lot of people are getting angry, and angry, when they go by the gas station and see prices rising. They get really angry when they go to the grocery store, see prices rising. They get angry when they realize that the car they want to buy can't be had, and when they do get it, it's going to be? Well, again, a condition of prices rising so they get fed up.\par \par \uc2 And right now, they're literally exasperated with all of this and they don't understand oftentimes the connection between the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  than having to add insult to injury by making borrowing more costly as well. So, they do take it out on the president and they're not encouraged by a lot of the blame shifting that goes on. I think this would apply whether the President Republican or Democrat, if he's not owning up to at least his partial responsibility here, because long before the war in Ukraine, and long before these other developments, prices were moving up.\par \par \uc2 Now, you could make the argument as the administration as that we were coming out of an economy in part with the COVID lockdowns that everything else, that almost any movement after that, Martha, would lead to a pickup in inflation. But to sort of absolve yourself of any responsibility for this and say that maybe you reining in domestic oil production didn't bliss, get this fire going, then to turn around and blame it on the food companies is a cabal of rigging prices of the oil companies, you know, greedily taking advantage of this.\par \par \uc2 There might even be particles of truth to some of that, but not all of that. And I think sometimes we learn a great deal from a president, any president, who owns up to the problems and says, I'm on top of it, a lot of this is on me, but I'm going to do my best to correct it. There's very little of that going on here. And I think that only adds to the anger on the part of consumers of any political persuasion, who say enough, enough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's been a long time since I've heard anyone in that position, come forward and say, you know what, this was kind of partly our fault. And now we're going to switch gears and we're going to do something that we think is going to fix it. He really seems to be taking it on the chin with independents, who are very independent voters who are very concerned about inflation, 70 percent of them disapprove.\par \par \uc2 And that's kind of be a tough number to look at when you're looking at the midterms as they're coming up. So, today, the President came out to talk about the economy. And really the focus of his comments, which I know you were watching as well, Neil, was that we should feel great about the fact that the deficit has come down. Is that something that we should feel great about? And what's its impact on the overall economy?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I caught that too, that wasn't going down. That would be like me heralding, losing four pounds when I have to lose 40 pounds. And I get it and it is a little bit of an improvement. But it's still God awful high, and our debt is still over $30 trillion dollars. And it's getting a tad better, but fractionally, what you expected to do after we were so knee deep in COVID related spending that is eased up at least by the magnitude.\par \par \uc2 So, year over year, you are going to see some declines off those levels. But these would be record high levels at any other time. So, yes, we're off the worst levels that we were. But a lot of it simply has to do with Americans going back to work, to return from these lockdowns. I'm not taking anything away from the administration, but to clean it out and say, we are on top of the world again, it would be like me saying, I lost a few pounds here, I think I'm done.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think you are done. I think you look great, Mr. Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That (INAUDIBLE), but I think it has a little bit arrogance to it. But we see that a lot here and in sort of focusing on one thing. And believe me, I'm the first to bemoan excessive government spending under Republicans or Democrats. And that's, that's an issue, but to sort of Herald this and focus on this as an achievement when people are worried about the sky price at the grocery store, at the gas pump. I think that's done very, very little to ease their fears.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, as we said earlier, I don't know I have too many people who are sitting around the dinner table saying, but gee, the deficit felt good. I know my gas prices are soaring, that cost a hundred bucks my, my pick up --\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The cost of pasta, the cost marina sauce, the cost of sausage -- that's an everyday concern.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sometimes you know, you can be the smartest person in the room, all I.Q., but no E.Q. Americans are E.Q. focus right now. They're smart, but they also know what's going on and it's a pain -- address their pain.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Neil. Thank you for coming in. It's always good to see you. Thank you very much. Great to see you as well. So, as the Supreme Court investigates who is behind this leak, because you know what, there's an individual, a person who did this, and there's probably more than one person who knows exactly who it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): This lawless action should be investigated and punished to the fullest extent possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former Attorney General Bill Barr says the case could be made here for criminal charges. He's going to explain his thinking here, along with Joe Concha, who is also going to join us after that, as President Biden calls MAGA supporters the most extreme political organization in American history, they're next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The leaker of the Supreme Court draft opinion, this has never been done in the history of the Supreme Court is being slammed by critics as despicable and praised by supporters as someone who lifted the veil of secrecy on the court and several of the justices reportedly joining the opinion are labeled liars for what they said in their confirmation hearings by Democrats. Former Attorney General Bill Barr and Joe Concha on the legal and media follow up. But first, to Correspondent David Spunt, who joins us with the very latest on what's going on at the Supreme Court today. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  here at the Supreme Court has ordered a full-scale investigation by the marshal of the court. The mission may be accomplished to find a leaker or leakers, but with all due respect to the marshal of the court, the marshal of the Supreme Court is not experienced and well-versed specifically in finding leakers, especially when it deals with something this high-profile and this much technology potentially.\par \par \uc2 However, there have been growing calls from both sides of the aisle to get the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Department of Justice involved. At this point, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ are not involved. The reason is there's no classified information in that draft opinion that was leaked to Politico, the team at \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  blog, who monitors the court put it this way, Martha: "It's impossible to overstate the earthquake this will cause inside the court in terms of the destruction of trust among the justices and staff. This leak is the gravest, most unforgivable sin."\par \par \uc2 We see the impact this leak has had across the country, but just think about what it did in this building behind me as they point out at \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  bloc. Think about the raw tension between these nine justices. They may not agree on everything, but this is a close knit, insulated group as for who leaked the draft. It had to be a small group, including potentially clerks or secretaries, or even a justice. Although, experts say it's unlikely a justice. Of course, we don't know.\par \par \uc2 Georgetown Law Professor Paul Smith was a clerk for the late Justice Lewis Powell. And Paul Smith argued in front of the High Court, 21 times. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL SMITH, DISTINGUISHED VISITOR FROM PRACTICE, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY LAW CENTER: Not entirely clear there is a legal problem here that there's any law that was violated. The oath of confidentiality that you take is one that's just on your honor as a law clerk, or a justice, or a secretary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: As for the motive of the leaker, or leakers, I could spend the rest of the show here talking about it. I'm not going to do that into anybody's guess.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, it's a small pool of people that were able to get a hold of that draft. And the chief justice is hoping that the marshal of the court is able to find this person or people. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in former Attorney General Bill BARR, author of One Damn Thing After Another: Memoirs of an Attorney General, twice, in fact. Bill Barr, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, Bill, do you think that this person will be found?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I think there's an argument in some ways that they would like to be found eventually, because they could sort of build a cottage industry off of being the person that leaked this in some aspects.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, I hope they build it while they're in jail. Because this is a grave and unforgivable sin, and I think it is likely they're going to find out who leaked. That it's a relatively small group of people that could possibly have done that. And I think the full weight of the law has to be brought against them.\par \par \uc2 I disagree with the last lawyer who spoke, who felt this was just a violation of an honor system. I think there are a number of federal statutes that are potentially cover this, including, you know, fraud against the United States, which involves wrongdoing, intended to impede the functioning of the government.\par \par \uc2 They're also statutes on obstructing or trying to interfere or influence the due administration of justice by committing acts like this. And it could also involve using government documents for one's own benefit, converting government documents.\par \par \uc2 So, there are a number of criminal statutes that were potentially violated here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, I mentioned the potential for this person, because I've heard people talk about this person could write books, they could get a position at, you know, at a law university teaching, and sort of be someone who is held up for this leak, which I think a lot of people share your opinion about the seriousness of it.\par \par \uc2 But it just makes me wonder if the Department of Justice or the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is going to really work that hard to get to the bottom of it, given this political nature. Do you think they will?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Well, if they're -- if they're called on to do it, I think they will. The other option, at least initially, would, the court has virtually unlimited power to protect its own procedures and processes. And they can use compulsory power to do their own investigation.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, I think because criminal punishment, I think is called for here. I think at some point, it's going to have to go to the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that each person, as you say, it's a small group of people, each person will be asked, are you the person who leaked this? And they have to decide whether or not they're going to lie in the middle of that process or whether or not they're going to tell the truth. And that will likely lead to this person being found one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about this question of settled law, because everybody watches these hearings. And we watch the process where people who are being questioned about their ability to serve on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk about settled law. And here is a montage of the three most recent Supreme Court justices being asked about Roe v. Wade during their hearings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would tell you that Roe vs. Wade decided in 1973, as a precedent \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , it has been reaffirmed.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: That's settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court, entitled the respect under principles of stare decisis.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Roe is not a super precedent because calls for its overruling have never ceased. But that doesn't mean that Roe should be overruled.\par \par \uc2 It just means that it doesn't fall on the small handful of cases like Marbury versus Madison and Brown versus the Board that no one questions anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a lot of people who watch those responses and who watched the hearings believe that those individuals were saying that they believed that it would never be overturned or should never be overturned, based on any question that might come before the court.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain how you see those responses?\par \par \uc2 BARR: I don't think those responses could fairly be understood as saying it would never be overruled.\par \par \uc2 I think they all made clear that like any other precedent, it's subject to stare decisis, and Wade, and some degree of deference. But the whole doctrine of stare decisis also has in it.\par \par \uc2 Some factors that are consulted, that may lead a court to overrule a prior precedent. And the draft opinion that was circulated went into all of that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think we don't want to get ahead of ourselves and assume that, that draft had five votes locked in, I'm skeptical that it did. I think things could still be in flux.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Really? Why?\par \par \uc2 BARR: Personally. Did you say why?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Yes. Well, because I -- an opinion is not final until, you know, almost the day it goes out. Remember what happened in Obamacare, there is some last minute changes just in the last 24 hours before an opinion was issued.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there was an effort, obviously to galvanize a majority of the justices. But I think -- I don't think that the circulation of that draft necessarily means that there were five justices irrevocably committed to that position.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. There's going to be a lot riding on this. And when that decision, that final decision does come out, it is going to be so closely watched based on all the drama that's been created by this leak.\par \par \uc2 And we'll see where it goes with the Department of Justice in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigating it. Bill Barr, thank you very much. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BARR: Thanks, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe Concha joins me now Media and politics columnist at the Hill and Fox News contributor. Joe, good to have you with us today. I just want to put up on the screen. This is from our new Fox polling, because I think it's really important to look at where the American people stand on this issue, because you see that there is support to leave Roe v. Wade in place, right? When it's asked that way.\par \par \uc2 But look at this way. When -- what about a law banning abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy except in the case of medical emergency? Would you favor or oppose a 15 week -- after 15 week ban in your state? And 54 percent say yes, they think that that's a reasonable place to draw the line at 15 weeks, Joe.\par \par \uc2 And when you watch what's happening on -- in a number of places in the media, this is the kind of outrage that we're seeing. Let's play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON, HOST, NBC: It looks like the Supreme Court will overturn Roe vs. Wade. Apparently, they decided masks aren't mandatory but Mother's Day is.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN, HOST, \uc2 CBS\uc2 : And make no mistake, this is the biggest rollback of human rights in modern U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH, HOST, \uc2 COMEDY CENTRAL\uc2 : Gives us quite some time and you just wake up and find yourself back in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, HOST, \uc2 CBS\uc2 : Congratulations ladies, decisions about what you can do with your body are now being made by four old dudes and a woman who thinks The Handmaid's Tale is a romcom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Wow, late nights really hilarious these days, isn't it? And we're a long way from Carson, Letterman, and Leno.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, boy --\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's all -- it's like watching CNN.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes. It's just all political talk. It's quite funny, it supposed to be escapism. But anyway, neither here nor there.\par \par \uc2 The public sentiment on abortion, Martha is highly nuanced, as you just pointed out with that poll.\par \par \uc2 So, when you put the pulse of the country on this issue, you're trying to gather it, it demands precise context, right? So, overall, on the issue of abortion, the country is basically split right down the middle -- if you look at Gallup, between those who are pro-life and those who are pro- choice.\par \par \uc2 But then, to your point, when you ask that question, and that's the whole key here, because that's what this decision is based on. Should you ban -- or I'm sorry, should abortion be banned after 15 weeks of pregnancy? Then, the numbers go into decided direction, right?\par \par \uc2 As you said, 54 percent oppose abortion after 15 weeks. 41 -- I'm sorry, oppose abortion after 15 weeks, just 41 percent are go the other way. That's a 13 point difference.\par \par \uc2 And I don't see that particular poll being cited anywhere. You would think that by listening to the reporting, that we're a country that is something like 70-30, pro-choice or pro-life, and it doesn't seem that way.\par \par \uc2 So, meanwhile, by the way, while some see this potential decision as a political Hail Mary, that the Democratic Party was looking forward to stave off a red tsunami in November, I think it's more or less a wash, that this really doesn't move the needle.\par \par \uc2 Because again, inflation is still going to be a dominant issue 187 days from now.\par \par \uc2 Fox's new poll has the president, 25 points underwater on his handling of the economy, gas prices will still be at unacceptable levels, skyrocketing crime will still be an issue that transcends party, as well. As well the war in Ukraine, which will likely still be ongoing. So, those are the core issues for these elections.\par \par \uc2 Economy, inflation, gas prices, crime, Ukraine, abortion will be there, yes, but I think again, one side cancels out the other because this is so evenly, evenly split on this issue. What even goes more conservative anything once you bring the 15 week question into play?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it came up, I think, 11th, in terms of the priorities of where people -- you know, the issues that people are really interested in. It's also interesting to note, Joe, before I let you go that (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Abortions have fallen down -- fall -- have declined 20 percent over the past 12 years. So, the number of people getting abortions is down considerably.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: True.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the number of people who use an abortion pill to have an abortion is basically 40 percent of all the abortions in the country. And I would imagine that those pills will remain very accessible.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, it seems that the outrage machine is in overdrive when you look at what's actually happening in the country. Quick thought?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes, sure. I just hope that somewhere in this reporting, we talk a little bit about how hard it is. And I have some friends that are going through this. How hard it is to adopt a baby in this country, right?\par \par \uc2 And maybe if we incentivize people more to have children, instead of aborting them, more families could be happy, and those children could get a happy, solid safe home. I never hear about adoption, talked about that much in this conversation. I hope maybe a little bit more that, that could happen here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Aaron Judge, great video of him, going over to hug a little boy, the Yankee, and he was adopted on a day after he was born. Just a little fun fact.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: That's to get that 6'9" guy in your house? My God.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly. Thank you, Joe. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: All right, take care.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we're going to go over the Pentagon. John Kirby is answering questions, as there's a lot of questions about May 9th and what will happen in Russia on Victory Day, which is the day when they celebrate the victory over Hitler in World War II.\par \par \uc2 And you've also got this question of what's going on in the devastated city of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): An advisor to the mayor says that Russian forces are clearing away the wreckage of the theatre that they bombed. Do you remember when that happened?\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE) civilians back in March, he posted this video. We don't know exactly when this was shot. Ukraine's president telling Fox News, what he thinks will happen on May the 9th. This coming Monday, when Russia celebrates that Victory Day, marking the end of World War II in Europe. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): -- a parade, and this is this -- that city. And he is going to show some sort of victory, but they don't have victories. The loss of people, it's not a victory. Maybe even they want to conduct a parade in Mariupol and other cities that is temporarily occupied. They want to show what kind of czars they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Griff Jenkins interview from earlier with President Zelenskyy. Let's go to Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby, and listen in. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: -- that's the larger point.\par \par \uc2 So, we'll consult with allies and partners in the region. And we'll continue to assess what happened here. We continue to call on the north to stop these provocative tests and to be willing to sit down as we have offered, we would be willing to do without precondition and discuss a diplomatic way forward here to denuclearize the North.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And quick follow up. They've more than doubled the number of test launches since last year. Is the U.S., you know, twice as concerned that -- how concerned is the U.S. compared to last year?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We're -- we are still very deeply concerned about these tests and the provocative nature of their continuing ballistic missile program. And that is why, again, we have -- not just the United States, but the international community has condemned these provocations.\par \par \uc2 Where there are violations of \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  resolutions. That's why we have called for the North to sit down again without precondition and have a discussion.\par \par \uc2 And it's also why we continue to focus on the readiness of our alliance, our alliance in Japan, and meeting today with the minister of defense, and our alliances with our South Korean allies as well there on the Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Yes, (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you. I want to ask you about the secretary's meeting with Japanese Defense Minister Kishi. The Japanese government is devising its own national defense strategy. So, what input did the secretary gave to the Japanese counterpart to enhance the U.S.-Japan defense cooperation, and to make sure Japanese strategy is closely aligned with the U.S. national defense --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: They did have a discussion day about our national defense strategy and the Japanese national security strategy, and I think the secretary came away from the meeting feeling that they were very much aligned, and that is not a surprise because of our close alliance with Japan, and the close, deep collaboration and cooperation we have military to military.\par \par \uc2 So, that -- I think it was a very good discussion about that. And the secretary doesn't have any concerns.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For a while, Mr. Kishi said on top of the meeting that they will discuss extended deterrence to the Northeast Asia. So, could you tell us more about what they discuss in greater detail? And as the secretary said, there should be some additional steps to keep the extended deterrence robust and credible.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, I'm not going to get more detailed than what we've already read out. They certainly talked about the importance of integrated deterrence in the region. This is something the secretary has talked about since almost the day he took office. And integrated deterrence isn't just about integrating our joint capabilities. It's about integrating allies and partners capabilities in conjunction with ours to deter potential adversarial action. And that certainly applies when you're talking about the pacing challenge that China represents. It certainly was discussed today with Minister Kishi. But I'll let the Japanese speak to specific extended deterrence issues with respect to their home islands.\par \par \uc2 Again, it was -- the issue of deterrence and China's continued bellicosity and aggressive and course of moves was absolutely discussed, but I won't go any more detail than that. OK? Yes.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY, REPORTER: Liz from Fox kind of ask the question about it but --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: OK. Then we'll go to the next one --\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY: The minister today said that North Korea has become a greater and more imminent threat, and I'm wondering if the Pentagon agrees with that sentiment, that it's greater and more imminent. And also, if there's anything that you could flush out, then I know you're going to tell me that asked the Japanese about this. But I'm asking if there's anything that you could flush out about why particularly the recent tests, or even this test just yesterday about North Korea may make North Korea a more imminent threat.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think with each test, Court, that they learn even if it's a failure. And again, we're still assessing this one, so I'm not going to get into the details of it. But with each test, they learn, with each test they try to improve and sometimes they actually do. So when we talk about greater, I think that's what we're all talking about. And we would agree that the threat from North Korea continues to increase because they continue to test, because they continue to learn, because they continue to adapt their program. It would be foolish not to assess that the threat they're posing is actually increasing over time. Yes back there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you for taking my question.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We're going to bring in \uc2 Kurt Volker\uc2 , a former US ambassador to \uc2 NATO\uc2  and former US Special Representative for Ukraine negotiations. Ambassador, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting to know, you know, we heard General Milley yesterday talking about the potential for a war on two fronts and we're listening to John Kirby at the Pentagon and take questions not only on Ukraine and Russia, and what's going on there, but also on the concerns about North Korean missile tests and increased bellicosity as John Kirby just mentioned, and aggressive stance from China with potential to do something in Taiwan. Your thoughts on where we are, as we face a lot of threats in the world and potential geopolitical change?\par \par \uc2 KURT VOLKER\uc2 , FORMER US AMBASSADOR TO NATO: Well, we do face a lot of threats but we are the world's biggest superpower as well. And we are not fighting in Ukraine. We're supplying Ukrainians with arms. That's why it's important that we get our defense budget off, we get this the spending bills approved to keep resupplying our own military as we provided some of the Ukrainians that we replenish. And I think that we are fully capable of exercising deterrence in Asia for some time to come even as we continue to supply the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So, Chris Jenkins sat down with President Zelenskyy earlier today. And he was quite clear, President Zelenskyy, about what he sees happening at the steel plant in Mariupol. And that there's sort of a back and forth, there's a moment when 100 people are allowed out and then the bombing continues. Here's what he said about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translation): The United Nations together with the International Red Cross are doing this to help the evacuation of our people. However, I told them that Russia wishes to destroy everybody who is still in the Azovstal steel plant. And they may not agree to any evacuation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, the loss of life is just absolutely devastating in Ukraine, and there are children that are set to be in there still as well. What do you think about where this war stands right now and how much longer Russia can keep this up?\par \par \uc2 VOLKER: Yes. Well, first off on just on what we heard there, what the Russians are doing is absolutely barbaric. They have killed tens of thousands of people already. The mayor there estimated over 20,000. They're refusing to let people out and then they're shelling them again, and their intention is really to starve them out or bomb them out, killing everybody that they can. It is incredibly barbaric.\par \par \uc2 As far as where this goes, though, the Russians have strategically failed. They have not taken over Ukraine, they've not toppled the government, they are no longer in a position to threatened Kyiv other than by lobbing bombs at it, but no ground forces no opportunity to take territory there. And as we approach this May 9th Victory Day celebration in Russia, I think Putin is looking for something that he can say is a victory. And so, that's why I think they are doubling down on Mariupol to say that they created this landbridge from Russia to Crimea and take in more of Donbas.\par \par \uc2 But this is not going to last this way. The Russians are exhausting their military capabilities. Ukrainians continue to fight back. And even after May 9th, they will keep up the fight to take your territory back and this is not going to go well for Russia in the long run.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick last question for you. The news that the \uc2 EU\uc2  says that they're going to cut off their imports of Russian oil over the coming months, I guess, you know, maybe six month period. How do you think that's received at the Kremlin?\par \par \uc2 VOLKER: Well, I think it's received with disappointment and maybe a bit of skepticism as well. Maybe they're still going to try to block that implementation from the \uc2 EU\uc2 , but it is a very serious step.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  sends Russia, hundreds of millions of dollars every day, in order to buy oil and gas and shutting that down is critical. They've said it'll be by the end of the year. I really hope they do it by this summer or by the end of this summer. It's got to be faster to shut off the flow of funds to Russia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that's the fourth round of sanctions from Europe against Russia. Thank you very much, \uc2 Kurt Volker\uc2 . Ambassador, good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So on the heels of a big win for former President Trump's pick in the Ohio GOP Senate Primary, we asked Buckeye voters about the direction of the country, the job that the President is doing on key issues like inflation and immigration. Ohio always an important state to watch in the coming elections.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Big win last night for JD Vance in Ohio, also a win for former President Trump. His endorsement helped Vance come from behind to win the Republican race for an open Senate seat in the first big test of the foreign president's power as we head into the midterm season. Let's see what Ohio voters have to say about it.\par \par \uc2 Deb Ludwig is a registered Republican and co-owner of DITZ Designs in Norwalk, west of Cleveland, and Kimberly Brown is a registered Democrat from Cleveland. She is a hiring manager for a medical company. Great to have you both with us today from Ohio.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with a little bit of the victory speech last night from JD Vance, and then we'll get your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: We have such an opportunity here in the state of Ohio, because I think so many of our people, so many of it -- and again, it's not just Republicans, it's Moderates, it's Democrats. I think as much as I can't stand the leadership of the Democrat Party, most of the people across the state of Ohio, whatever they're voting for, are good people, ladies and gentlemen. And we in this party believe that we ought to fight for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Deb, what are your thoughts on last night and what's your reaction to what he had to say after his win?\par \par \uc2 DEB LUDWIG, REGISTERED REPUBLICAN: I actually didn't support JD Vance. I really liked Josh Mandel because he's kind of a homegrown boy and I've been watching him since 2012. And I like how he has addressed the financial responsibilities that that we have. But I'm hoping that JD Vance will take this -- I see it not necessarily as much of a Trump victory as an anti- establishment victory. So I'm hoping that he will grab a hold of the values that we have in Ohio, and we'll go forward with that.\par \par \uc2 I agree, it shouldn't be Republicans, Democrats, Independents, Conservatives, Liberals, it should be Ohio. We need to work together to make everything a little better.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kim, I know you said you pull the Democrat ballot at the election yesterday. What did you think of JD Vance has went on the Republican side? And he will likely run against a \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  for that seat in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY BROWN, REGISTERED DEMOCRAT: Totally, totally correct. You know, I'm not a fan, like I said it before, of Mr. Trump. So due to the fact that he received Trump's support, I, for one, we're not even looking at JD Vance. Now, I am looking at \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and I did, and I don't mind saying that I did vote for \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . I'm still going to believe in the middle class and the poor, and I also want Ohioans to work together.\par \par \uc2 So as I will always stay with me, it has to be people over party. I could care less about two Republicans, the Dems, Libertarians degree, it's really about how are we going to move, you know, our people forward, as far as, you know, people being human, and understanding certain issues that we have to endure in order to make life better tomorrow than what it is today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Kim, let me stay with you and then I'm going to go back to Deb for a moment. But we see in the polling that about 87% of Americans are very concerned about inflation. And say that that's their top concern, what it your top concern as you look at the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, as I looked at the midterm, I'm still concerned about post COVID-19. I mean, we're still in the midst of that and it appears to me that people have forgotten about it. But we still have a lot of small businesses that have closed down, and they have not gotten the necessary support help as far as finances and human capital to actually reopen a move forward. And nobody's really talking about that.\par \par \uc2 I'm also concerned about the infrastructure of our roads system. If you go through our basic neighborhoods, we know that we're on the verge of a lot of roads crumbling and nobody is talking about that. Nobody is talking about the inflation of gas prices. People are not talking about the war of Russia and Ukraine, they're not talking about the oil and what this is costing the American people. And people have to understand it that people go to work to make a living. We don't go to work to put gas in our car.\par \par \uc2 So sometimes we have to be more human and not to stand the fascist when it comes to our family units. And I don't think that really a lot of candidates are thinking about that. They still talking about stump speeches and feel good speeches. But at the end of the day, just about how, as Americans, how are we going to survive as people?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Deb, what do you think? What matters as you look towards the midterms and how do you think the country is doing on the whole?\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: I think inflation has to be number one. The egg prices here in Norwalk, we're about 79 cents before Easter, and they're $2.63 a dozen I think right now. Our gas, of course, absolutely our gas, but it's everything. We went to dinner the other night and dinners that were $60 in the fall or $112 now. It's horrible.\par \par \uc2 And I also know, we have a small business, trying to find people to work is very, very difficult. And you can, you know, the (inaudible) only so big, you can only pay so much. And we want people to want to be better but it's really hard when every penny that you have is going toward getting you to work for childcare or for food. You know, we don't want to just live on rice and beans, although they're delicious, but that is not what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you want all variety.\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, ladies, thank you very much. Deb Ludwig and Kimberly Brown from Ohio, I appreciate you both coming in today. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LUDWIG: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he is a huge country music star between the mega hit "Fancy Like" but now Walker Hayes is sharing his own story of struggle and hardship, and unlikely friendships that led him to a deep Christianity. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know who that is, right? That's country music singer Walker Hayes and he's here with us today to tell us about a friendship that changed his life and help the former atheist to find faith. He tells a story with his friend, Craig Cooper, in their new book, "Glad You're Here: The Story of Two Unlikely Friends Breaking Bread and Fences." In fact, the fence that divided their property, they took it down. Walker Hayes and Craig Cooper joins us now. Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 WALKER HAYES, COUNTRY SINGER: Hello, hey.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG COOPER, CO-ANCHOR, "GLAD YOU'RE HERE": Hey.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you. You know, so let me start with you, Walker. You know, there's a long story here but you have been sober for six years and you went from having a very tough time in your career, where you got a job at \uc2 Costco\uc2  to make things work with your six kids, to now you're at the top of the charts and filling arenas. And this man had something to do with that in terms of success.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh yes. This man led me to Jesus Christ. I mean, simply put -- God was merciful to show me his son through a human, you know. And Craig and his family loved my family unconditionally on another level, a level that we'd never felt, that welcome before.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, we went from complete strangers to now we're next door neighbors. The fence is ripped out and we just do life together. And I also know Jesus now, so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And, you know, the book is called "Glad You're Here." And you wrote a song about Craig. I just want to play a little bit of that song. And it's called "Craig." I'm not sure how you felt about a song named your name, but here it is.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To Craig, you reached out to him and said, hey, I'm glad you showed up at church.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Yes. Actually, I feel like God just blessed us and smiled on us when the Hayes walked into our lives. And I feel like it's a friendship from heaven and just one that has been a blessing, really, the both of us and our families. And so, I have always been encouraged, strengthen, built up anytime I hung out with Walker even before he was a believer. And so, you know, my deepest desire and heart for him was to come to faith in Jesus.\par \par \uc2 And so, when that happened, it blew me away. There's two versions of the song. One that he wrote as an unbeliever and one that he wrote after he became a Christian, and hearing both of them, for me, still blows me away. And it I'm stunned.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, what do you think when you listen to that, Walker, and you think about sort of the old you? You had an addiction which a lot of people out there who are watching this either, you know, have had themselves or they know somebody who's had. So what was the trigger for you? What changed? What changed?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, Christ. I mean just his salvation, you know, that he offers me. That's it. Not, you know, I'm still a sinner. I'm still broken. I need the Lord more and more, and more. And by His grace, I was able to, you know, quit drinking. The odds of that are not good, you know. There's some of that in my family and that's tough. And, yes, six years sober.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes. I mean, that's about it. A lot of my life has changed since but for the most, the most valuable thing that has changed is my soul has been purchased by the blood of Christ.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And simultaneously, you're selling out arenas. So you went from working at \uc2 Costco\uc2  and losing your car, and losing your record deal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To just selling out arenas. Quick thought on that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Some rags to riches stuff happening. But, I mean, again, it's all, you know, worthless compared to knowing Christ. And it'll all go away, you know, and we were enjoying some success, career wise, but I truly feel like all this success is so that we can share this message and just have a platform to stand on and say the name of Jesus,\par \par \uc2 I think it's a powerful message for other people for other friendships, to sort of open your eyes when you have a friend like this, who walks into your life and say, why is this person here and where that can take you both. So, Craig Cooper and Walker Hayes, thank you. Best of luck to you with the tour.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the book is called "Glad You're Here" and we're glad you guys are here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We're going to shift over here to White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , she's taking some questions from Peter Doocy. We're going to stick in right now\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: -- getting distracted or eye off the ball of what is most important to people across the country here, which is not the leak and the story of the leak. It is the fact that women's health care is at risk for millions of people across this country.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President said today, what happens if you have states change the law saying that children who are LGBTQ can't be in classrooms with other children? What is he talking about?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think, Peter, we've seen extreme laws that target LGBTQ families, their kids across the country. And I think what he's saying is we don't know what they're capable of given what they've already done today.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Which state is trying to segregate LGBTQ children in the classroom?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think we've seen laws that are incredibly discriminatory, that's what the President's referring to. And the fact that he doesn't know what additional steps could be taken by extreme wings of the party that would rather divide rather than work on issues that the American people actually are focused on actually impacting them.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So another one about abortion. Why is the President talking about the judgment to choose to abort a child?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: President's view on a woman's right to make choices about her own health care is well-known, well-documented, well-stated.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He said abort worth a child.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I understand, Peter, but what I'm telling you is, is what is positioned.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And how can you guys say this is not a political issue when the President's statement about this talked about getting pro-choice officials elected?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Did I say it's not a political issue?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, you actually said some called it a political issue. It is not, abort --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, because the vast majority of the public believes that this should not -- that this should not be overturned, meaning I meant to say it's not a partisan issue, and I don't think it is. There are many Republican and Independent women, men across the country who do not believe the Supreme Court should overturn a woman's right to make choices about her own health care. In fact, only 30% in recent polls thought they should. So that's what I'm referring to. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jen, thanks. A couple on thorough opinion. Earlier this year, President Biden made a speech on voting rights. He said they were under attack, it was important to do that. Will he make an address to the nation on abortion access?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't have anything to predict about -- address to the country. I can tell you that you've heard him speak about his views today and yesterday, and it's only been two days since we saw the leaked documents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the comments today and yesterday, when he was asked about abortion, the President really focused on privacy of children married couples, why isn't he speaking more directly about how this impacts women, poor women, women of color? How comfortable is he talking about this?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He has. He did yesterday both in his written statement and when he addressed this publicly. His point, as he's continued to talk about this over two days, is also that this brings into question our fundamental rights and the fundamental rights of people across this country, and who they marry, what choices they make about their own healthcare. And that goes to his own experience fighting against, again, Robert Bork in his nomination many years ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And did not use the word abortion.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It was in his statement yesterday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Until yesterday, that's my questions. He hadn't used the word --\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Breaking just moments ago, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  has tested positive for COVID. The State Department says he has mild symptoms and has not seen President Biden for a few days. He has not considered a close contact under CDC guidelines.\par \par \uc2 I also want to mention that the podcast with Walker Hayes and Chris Cooper will drop on Saturday. I spoke to them for a long time this afternoon, really interesting story about their friendship and his music. So I urge you to take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 That's THE STORY for today, I'll see you back here tomorrow. Have a great afternoon and evening. Neil is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220505ei5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  (R-FL)\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum \par \uc2 1,771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Senator Scott, you want to respond to that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Sure, I had the opportunity to listen to what the president said. First off, almost every sentence as a complete lie.\par \par \uc2 First of all let's think about what he did. He never talked about inflation. You know, since he's been in office, inflation is 8.5 percent. We just had a negative GDP, down 1.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 So you see people that are struggling all across this country.\par \par \uc2 Now, with regard to -- there's a complete difference between my view of the world and his view of the world. I am the biggest tax cutter probably in this Senate right now or in the -- even up here. I cut taxes a hundred times.\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got into politics, think of what's happened. Taxes have gone up on Americans constantly. On top of that, he's taken the debt of this country from under $1 trillion to $30 trillion. And his plan that he put out will take the -- take the debt of this country to $45 trillion and has massive tax increases on everybody.\par \par \uc2 My plan is: reduce taxes, but let's get everybody back to work.\par \par \uc2 With regard to Medicare, Medicaid and Social Security -- when I was governor of Florida, on top of cutting taxes 100 times and, by the way, reducing the debt of the state by a third, what I also did was I fixed the pension plan.\par \par \uc2 Here \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , since he's been around, made sure that the -- whether it's the Social Security or Medicare -- are in a worse position every year.\par \par \uc2 Medicare goes bankrupt in four years. He doesn't want to fix it. Democrats don't want to fix it. Social security goes bankrupt in 12 years. They don't want to fix it.\par \par \uc2 I want to have a conversation, let's fix these programs so they last. People have paid into these programs, they paid into Medicare, they paid into Social Security.\par \par \uc2 What the Democrats are doing and what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing, he just keeps passing the buck and watching these plans go bankrupt. And he'll wait for the next president or the next Senate -- have to try to fix these things.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: So what he just did was a complete lie. Go to rescueamerica.com. You can see exactly what I believe.\par \par \uc2 I want to fix this country. We've got to rescue this country, not take it down \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's horrible path.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let's show everyone at home the new polls that we have. And they really do match up with the numbers that we've seen from other polling outlets around the country.\par \par \uc2 And this is exactly why you have the president coming out today and sort of carving out this issue of the deficit and speaking about that and not speaking about inflation.\par \par \uc2 That's the job performance number: approval 45 percent, disapprove 53 percent. And I think we have some more of these numbers that show the breakdown.\par \par \uc2 So on the economy, he's at 36 percent approval; crime, 33 percent approval; immigration, 32 percent; inflation, he's at 28 percent approval on how he's handling this.\par \par \uc2 But, Senator Scott, explain to everybody at home why he is focusing on the cut that has been seen in the deficit of this country and calling that a big win?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, he's not doing anything. I mean the deficit's not going down. The amount of the deficit might go down a little bit because we were in the pandemic under -- when, you know, the last year of President Trump and so we don't have all that access spending right now.\par \par \uc2 But the deficit's not going down. This -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats want to spend every dollar they can. His plan takes the debt of this country from $30 trillion to $45 trillion. Tell me how we're going to pay for that.\par \par \uc2 Look at what's happening to interest rates. The 10-year's at over 3 percent today. How are we going to pay for this? We only collect about $4 trillion in federal revenues right now. The debt's at $30 trillion, so now it's almost $900 billion, probably, in interest expense.\par \par \uc2 I don't know -- I don't know how you can pay for this stuff when -- think about it, the Democrats won't fix deficit spending. They won't fix Medicare. They won't fix Social Security and they just have massive spending -- just constantly massive spending.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about a China bill that will be $250 billion with no return to the American public. This has got to stop.\par \par \uc2 When will -- the inflation will not stop until we balance the budget, we get people back to work, we figure out: how do you reduce inflation?\par \par \uc2 You create more competition. You reduce the permitting process --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- you reduce regulation. That's how you get inflation down. Stop this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We almost never hear Republicans or Democrats talk about balancing the budget and you would expect to hear more about it from Republicans. We'll see if we'll hear it in the next round of the midterm elections as they move along.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Republicans tearing into liberals after this leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion that could spell the end of Roe v. Wade, ending nearly five decades of federalizing the legal right to an abortion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 This is -- Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has ordered an investigation into what he calls an egregious breach of trust.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): And it is the cumulation of a multiyear campaign by Senate Democrats to delegitimize and to politicize the court.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DAN CRENSHAW\uc2 , (R-TX): It's one of the most egregious violations of the sanctity of the Supreme Court. And I hope there's serious consequences to it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): This was despicable. It's dangerous and it was dumb. To those who want to gain power through destroying institutions, you're going to get rejected at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, one of the things that strikes me here, Senator, is that both sides are sort of grabbing this narrative and figuring out how to wrestle with it as you head towards the midterms. And I just want to get your reaction to what President Biden just said. Because he was talking about how radical -- he called it the "Mega Agenda," and then he called it the "MAGA Agenda" and then he corrected himself and called it the "MAGA Agenda" again.\par \par \uc2 But what he just suggested was that this decision, if it goes through, by the Supreme Court could lead to the overturning of the Griswold case, which was about whether or not married couples can use birth control in their own lives and their bedrooms. And he said it could also lead to a situation where LGBTQ children are not put in the same classrooms with the rest of the children in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 Is that where we're headed here, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I don't -- I don't know where he's coming from. But I'll tell you what's radical, the Democrats want late-term abortion, they want to have abortion up to birth and they don't have any obligation to keep the child alive. That's what's radical.\par \par \uc2 We're going to see what the Supreme Court comes out with. I hope they find out exactly who did this and they lose their job. I mean this is ridiculous what these people are doing. Whether it's packing the Supreme Court, putting term limits on the Supreme Court, or now just doing everything they can to delegitimize the Supreme Court is not good for this country.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are start thinking what's -- talk about what's good for this country. Go win in a free and fair election. That's how -- that's what you should be doing. Don't discredit all these institutions that we have.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's Elizabeth Warren sort of giving us a sample of what this is going to look like politically. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This is what the Republicans have been working toward this day for decades. They have been out there plotting, carefully cultivating these Supreme Court Justices so they could have a majority on the bench who would accomplish something that the majority of Americans do not want. People want Roe v. Wade to maintain as the law of the land --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't want to dismember children in the womb, ma'am. No, we are not going to stand --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a taste of just what this looks like. Just what this argument looks like today, Senator. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I mean first off, let's find out what happened. Let's find out what the decision is. But I'll tell you what, I'm pro-life. I believe in the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 But what I'm going to fight is what the Democrats want to do, and that's when they (ph) say you can abort a baby right up until birth and not keep them alive. That's not right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We have new polls that show that people, 54 percent, of those polled favor a ban after 15-weeks. And I think what we're going to learn here, when you really look at where people stand on this issue is that that is a very common feeling across the country.\par \par \uc2 Now you're going to get people on both ends of the spectrum on that. But I think that, you know, that 54 percent reveals that they're pretty much in line with the Mississippi law that was at stake here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, I think -- and that's where Europe. It's, you know --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- it's around 15-weeks. You know there should be an exception for rape and incest. So I think that's where -- I think that's where the American public is. But they're not where the Democrats are. The Democrats are radical.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator Scott, thank you very much. Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  joining us today of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020220504ei540005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ukraine War Highlights Importance Of Free Press; Stunning Leak Shatters Secrecy Of Supreme Court; Biden Speaks As U.S. Sends Weapons To Ukraine; Biden Gives Update On Ukraine Military Aid; Biden: We're At An Inflection Point In History; U.S. Ramps Up Aid For Ukraine As Putin Wages War; United Kingdom: Must Push Russia Out Of Whole Of Ukraine; Leaked SCOTUS Draft Signals End Of Roe v. Wade; Voting Underway In Hard-Fought Ohio Primaries\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Marc Thiessen, Matt Finn, Shannon Bream, Jehmu  Greene, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 10,120 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we really hope Ukraine and other Eastern European countries will apply so we can support local --\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You're doing great work. Thank you for everything you and your team has been doing and continues to do. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for us here. Thank you so much for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2  , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  . Be sure to set your DVR so you don't miss a minute, of the minute of the program. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John, and Sandra. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum in Fox News Headquarters in New York right now on the story this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The story today is effort by someone on the inside to discredit the institution of the Senate, which continues a pattern that we've observed over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to talk a lot about that. What is the pattern that we have seen that dis, disrupts these institutions, and we've seen quite a bit of it. So, about to speak after the highest court in the land rocked by this unprecedented leak. The president really said nothing. He was asked about this earlier what he thought about the fact that there was this leak, this breach, he didn't comment on it. We'll see if he does a few minutes from now.\par \par \uc2 He's served for eight years as the chair of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  . So, we expect that no matter where he falls on this issue, and he sort of has been on both sides, in some ways, we can talk about that. But you would expect that he would feel strongly about the issue of this breach of this leak. Now, the court confirming that the document that was leaked is Justice Alito is draft opinion that was written in February. And in it, he talks about the majority overturning Roe v. Wade, and ordering an internal investigation now into this leak.\par \par \uc2 With that, we've got a great lineup for you today, we're going to bring in Marc Thiessen, first, American Enterprise Institute Scholar and Fox News Contributor; Senator Mike Lee is also going to join us in just a moment. So, Marc, you know, it did strike me that President Biden, who was the Chair of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  , he was asked, actually, what do you think about this leak? And he just said, well, if this is a decision -- if the decision holds, it's quite a radical decision, no comment at all, on the fact that this had been released.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it's amazing. The Democrats keep accusing conservatives of having politicized the court. But then here, you have what is likely a leak from a liberal clerk, who was trying to stop this decision from going forward. And everybody and he doesn't even have some of that -- it has nothing to say about it, if anything is bringing the court's credibility into question is something like this, which is completely unprecedented, and I think we're going to find out who the leaker was.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley was on earlier today. And one of the points he made is, \uc2 FBI\uc2  is going to come in and question every clerk, and they're going to ask him, did you leak it? And at that point, I don't know if the -- there's debate over whether leaking it was a crime, but saying no to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  would definitely be a crime so that that the leaker is going to have to make a decision. Do I lie to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and commit a crime to cover up what I did? Or do I admit it and own it, and now I get disbarred, and publicly shamed for credit this horrible breach of, of Supreme Court precedent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and right now, Chief Justice Roberts says that it's in the hands of the marshal of the courts. So, we'll see if it gets to the level of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , Mike Lee, who we're going to talk to in a minute has said that he believes that the court probably already has a pretty good idea where this came from. And I would also point out that there are some, there's some reports that the White House was aware of this story over the past few days. So, it seems highly unlikely that they would be aware of it if it had been leaked from, you know, from the conservative side, which some people have suggested, Marc.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, I don't think the Conservatives were the ones who were, who are, who are leaking this. You know, we're, conservatives are ready for it -- if from conservative side, this is great news. So, we'd be, I think they would be patiently waiting and making sure that they dotted their i's and cross their T's and got it done rather than trying to put it out there in the public sphere. There's no benefit. Cui bono is there to, is the legal term, who benefits -- conservatives don't benefit from leaking this at all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, OK. Marc, thank you very much. Senator Mike Lee joins us now. He clerked for Justice Alito. He says very few people would have had access to that draft opinion. And as I just mentioned, he's virtually certain that the court has figured out who this is, he's the author of the book, "Saving Nine," the fight against the left's audacious plan to pack the Supreme Court and destroy American liberty. Senator, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 You know, thinking earlier today, and I think we have some video of it, of what it usually is like when a Supreme Court decision comes out. And you know, interns and people who are waiting for the decision come tearing across the plaza in front of the Supreme Court, because nobody has had an inkling of what has been discussed and what the decision is, that's all been broken here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE (R-UT): It's been broken and it's significant. You know, in order for this to happen, a lot of protocols have to have been circumvented. Each law clerk and each justice have four law clerks each law clerk has access to these opinions as they're circulated. They typically don't print them out when they need to print them out, they follow and established set of protocols.\par \par \uc2 When they're finished with them, they don't just throw them in the wastebasket. They put them in a burn bag, they take the burn bag, and they shred it twice, vertically, horizontally creating confetti, then after they've shredded it, they burn it, and they turn it into slurry, all before that leaves the custody of the Supreme Court. So, these things had all been violated or overlooked, circumvented, perhaps deliberately, in order to release this. That's unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 The unfortunate part, the fortunate part about this, though, isn't the Supreme Court appears poised to correct a major slight a major overreach by the court nearly 50 years ago. That's good news for the American people regardless of how they feel about this issue as a policy matter. This was a decision made by federal judges, that was supposed to be made by state legislators who were shifting power back to the people where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want to show this comment by Chief Justice Roberts, who put out a very strong statement about what happened here, "To the extent this betrayal of the competences of the court was intended to undermine the integrity of operations, it will not succeed, the work of the court will not be affected in any way. Do you think that, that was the intention here of this leaker to try to sway perhaps Justice Roberts or anyone else who might have been a little bit on the fence? This was a February draft, and it's not expected out until the end of June, even early July?\par \par \uc2 LEE: That's right. No, no, look, well, I don't have first-hand knowledge of what happened or why it did. One could surmise as many have. And I think reasonably, that this was quite possibly an effort by someone to threaten, harass, intimidate, those justices planning to sign on to the majority opinion. I don't think that's going to work here. I don't think it's going to have its intended effect. And I think the effect that they've overreached this far in trying to denigrate the Supreme Court. Denigrate the Supreme Court, mind you, under circumstances when the Supreme Court was writing an historical wrong, an instance in which the Constitution had been manipulated by the court 48 years ago. It's not going to work this time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, Senator McConnell mentioned, the undermining of institutions in America that he said has been going on for the last couple of years. And you know, a few things come to mind the tearing down of statues, the teaching of the foundations of this country in a very different way than the founders intended it. You know, and I wonder what you think about that. If you believe that this shows that, and here you've got Brian Fallon, the Executive Director of Demand Justice, and he says, "\uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  leaks are good. Elite lawyers on both the left and right treating the court is precious all these years have just been given covered to an institution that is wholly unaccountable. Rip the veil off." Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LEE: It's a wildly inappropriate statement by Mr. Fallon. And it's not the sort of thing that we can allow to take hold. We have a good judicial system. The \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  , despite its flaws, is the envy of the entire world. It is a good deliberative body, even though it consists of fallible mortals. It's a good tribunal, it's not one that we should try to weaken. If we weaken the Supreme Court, we do so at our own peril, and that would be unfortunate. We're not going to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, the entire creation of the branches of government was designed so that the judiciary could be independent, could deliberate, could decide, and then release those decisions. Senator Lee, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lots to discuss on this front. President Biden is speaking right now. \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  , in Troy, Alabama, which is of course where the javelins have been built, which had been enormously successful in Ukraine's effort against Russia. So, let's dip in here and listen to this. We'll see if he has comments to make also about this huge decision out of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I mean, as a person firing can - - I know, you know, it's for anybody maybe listen, can change positions or take cover, and before that javelin strikes homes, strikes the target. In fact, they've been so important. There's even a story about Ukrainian parents naming their children, not a joke, their newborn child, javelin or javelina -- not a joke. So, the brave people in Ukraine, including the many civilians who have taken up arms to defend their country deserve every ounce of credit for pushing back the Russian assault and frustrating Putin's desire to dominate Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We're at an inflection point in history. For real, it comes along about every six or eight generations, where things are changing so rapidly, that we have to be in control. Folks, there's an ongoing in battle in the world between autocracy and democracy. \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  , the Leader of China, who I've talked with, spent more time with than any of the world leader has: over 78 hours on either in-person or on the telephone with him. And the fact of the matter is he just a straightforward about it.\par \par \uc2 He says that democracies cannot be sustained in the 21st century, not a joke. They cannot be sustained because things are moving so rapidly. Democracy require consensus, and it's hard to get consensus. Therefore, it can't keep up with an autocracy, one man rule. But that's not going to be the case. If that happens, the whole world changes. I mean, because of you, in this first, really, battle, if you will, for that to determine whether that's going to happen is because you're making possible, you're making it possible for the Ukrainian people to defend themselves, without us having to risk getting a third world war by sending American soldiers fighting Russian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 My dad used to have an expression he said, the only war worse than one that's intended is one that's unintended. You're allowing the Ukrainians to defend themselves. And quite frankly, they're making fools of the Russian military in many instances. A big part of the reason they've been able to keep up fighting and to make this war a strategic failure for Russia, is because the United States together, allies and partners have had their back. United States alone has committed more than 5,500 javelins to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You're changing the nation, you really are. Add to that significant supplies from our allies and partners as well as many thousands of other anti-tank and anti-air weapons, helicopters, armored vehicles, artillery, coastal defense systems. Before Russia attacked, we made sure Russia had javelins and other weapons to strengthen the defenses, so Ukraine was ready. Whatever happened. In the last two months, we continue to move even more resources and equipment at a rapid pace in the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We've made sure that there are no interruptions in the flow of equipment to Ukraine. Since Russia invaded Ukraine, just over two months ago, we have sent more than $3 billion in security assistance to Ukraine, alone, us not counting our allies. That money is a direct investment in defending freedom and democracy itself. Because if you don't stand up to dictators, history has shown us they keep coming. They keep coming. Their appetite for power continues to grow.\par \par \uc2 And every worker in this facility, and every American taxpayer is directly contributing to the case for freedom. And that's something we can all be incredibly proud of, in my view. Last week, I sent Congress excuse a point of personal privilege talking like an old senator, but sent a supplemental budget -- a fancy way of saying we need more money to -- make sure the United States can continue to send weapons directly to the frontlines of freedom in Ukraine, and continue to provide economic and humanitarian assistance help the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 And I urge the Congress to pass this funding quickly to help crank continue to succeed against Russian aggression, just as they did when they won the battle of Kyiv. And to make sure the United States and our allies can replenish our own stocks of weapons, to replace what we've sent to Ukraine. As I said, from the beginning, this fight is not going to be cheap. But caving to aggression would even be more costly. We either back the Ukrainian People's they defend their country or we stand by as Russia continues its atrocities and aggression.\par \par \uc2 I know what the answer is, and I think you all do too. But I know what the answer this plan is. There's something else here that to be understood. Being the Arsenal of Democracy also means good paying jobs for American workers. In Alabama and the States, all across America or defense equipment is manufactured and assembled. 265 people here at this plan are directly employed. Working on the Javelin program. All told, \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  has brought nearly 3,000 jobs to Alabama. The Armed Forces United States of America continue to be the best arm, most capable fighting force in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 In order to do that, we have to make sure our vital defense supplies are getting the inputs of supplies they need to produce and predict and provide the full capacity. I learned on the tour today that each of the javelins you produce includes more than 200 semiconductors. I've been a broken record, as the press will tell you on the R&D to be able to produce more semiconductors in United States. We invented to soccer going to the moon. We the United States. We're the one that modernize it. We've done more than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 But guess what? We stopped investing in ourselves. We stopped investing in ourselves. And so now, we're what back in the game, making sure that we become -- we become the primary producer of those semiconductors, computer chips, and power much of our modern lives, on our phones, our cars, almost anything that has an on off switch. And the semiconductor is critical to defense production capacity as you all know better than I do.\par \par \uc2 That's why we're making it as hard as we can for Russia to get hold of these semiconductors, and advanced technologies that they could use to upgrade its military during this conflict.\par \par \uc2 And why we're taking steps to make it easier to source what we need here in the United States during the global semiconductor shortage? And just one more -- there's just one more reason why Congress has to act quickly to provide the emergency funding, and the so-called CHIPS Act, by passing a broader bipartisan innovation act, so we can produce 10s of millions of these chips.\par \par \uc2 There's something we have to focus on, and something I've focused on from my earliest days of our administration, I'm determined to make sure the United States hold this technological high ground in competition with other nations, especially China as we move forward.\par \par \uc2 Folks, you know, we used to invest as a nation. Years ago -- 35 years ago, we invested two percent of our entire GDP in research and development. We do half of that now.\par \par \uc2 We do half of that. We used to be number one in the world. Now we're number 13 in the world. My administration, we're changing that. The United States used to own the innovation field.\par \par \uc2 In fact, within the Department of Research Program that are established \uc2 DARPA\uc2  . The first development of an anti-tank missile, with advanced infrared guiding systems that culminate in today's javelins.\par \par \uc2 The Bipartisan Innovation Act is going to help reverse decades -long decline in federal research and development and investment. And it should create and support entire families and expand U.S. manufacturing and strengthen our national security.\par \par \uc2 We're in God's name is written that the United States can no longer be a leading manufacturer in the world? We've created just in the last 17 months 465,000 permanent manufacturing jobs in America. We have the best workers, the most competent employees, the best science in the world, and by funding the CHIPS Act, we're going to ensure the semiconductors that powering the economy and our national security are made here in America again.\par \par \uc2 Today, all the world's most advanced chips are made overseas. But the events of the past few years have proven beyond a doubt that America security should never be held hostage to events overseas. Not a pandemic, not a war, not the politics of ambition, or other countries.\par \par \uc2 Fundamentally, there's an essence to this national security issue. This is one of the reasons why the Chinese Communist Party is lobbying folks to oppose this bill. And it's an issue that unites Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, let's get it done. Let's get it done. In introduction, Linda said she personally touched every single solitary, javelin -- 50,000 manufacturing this plant 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 I was worried to shake her hand, I thought you may might be electrocuted. But that's where they start, right here, with American skill, American craftsmanship, American patriotism.\par \par \uc2 And just a few days ago, the Wall Street Journal quoted a young Hungarian fighter saying and I quote, "Without the javelins, would have been very hard to stop the enemy pushing ahead."\par \par \uc2 So, these weapons touched by the hands, your hands, are in the hands of Ukrainian heroes, making a significant difference. That's something each and every day you could and should be proud of.\par \par \uc2 Once more urging Congress to quickly pass the supplemental funding bill, over $300 billion to help the Ukrainians, so they can keep -- they keep all of the very, very -- all do very, very busy for a while here.\par \par \uc2 So again, let me end where I began. I came from basic reason from the bottom of my heart to say thank you. Thank you -- thank you -- thank you in what you do, thank you what you continue to do.\par \par \uc2 Unless you go out in the field and see it, and not realize what a difference you're making. And God bless you all, and may God protect our troops. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): There you have it in Troy, Alabama, President Biden at the \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  plant there, where they have built 50,000 Javelin missiles over the course of that production there.\par \par \uc2 They really have been one of the most effective weapons that we have sent in this war. Taking out hundreds of Russian tanks and really putting a huge dent in Russia's capabilities and we've seen what happened when the Russians were pushed out of Kyiv. That war machine is under a tremendous amount of pressure.\par \par \uc2 So, now, the nation's top general, earlier today on Capitol Hill, warned, of potentially two theatres that could exist in war in our future. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2  , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: Now facing two global powers, China and Russia, each with significant military capabilities, both who intend to fundamentally change the current rules based order. We are entering a world that is becoming more unstable. And the potential for significant international conflict between great powers is increasing, not decreasing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now, Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby. Good to have you with us today, John Kirby. The president also touched on this. He said, we're at an inflection point, and we have to be in control.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of discussion about the potential for a two front war, a confrontation with Russia confrontation with China. Are we up to that task? Do we have what we need to supply these two areas simultaneously?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Yes, I think we actually are. There's no doubt about that. I mean, if you look at the work we put into, craft a new national defense strategy that looks at China as the pacing challenge, calls Russia out for being acute threat that it is.\par \par \uc2 And we tried to align those budget priorities with -- I'm sorry, with their strategic priorities with the budget that we submitted to Congress, and that General Milley was testifying too today. You know, that's a $773 billion budget that invests in more kinds of capabilities to help us deal with those particular kinds of threats and challenges.\par \par \uc2 There's a -- there's a record number of investment dollars in this budget, higher than the Pentagon's ever asked for in science and technology, investment research, and development.\par \par \uc2 So, we know we've got work to do, Martha, no question about that. But the - - but the heads are in the right place here at the Pentagon, and we're working hard on trying to make sure that we don't have to fight a war with Russia or China.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Because it's really about deterring those kinds of conflicts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we're hearing a lot and we're seeing a lot of different editorials about the need to catch up. And we have these reports also that equipment, how it sort of like equipment that is to be sent to Taiwan is now delayed and questions about whether or not it's delayed because of the effort to supply Ukraine with the weapons that they need. And can you tell me, are those going to be delayed three years? Is that accurate?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, without getting into the specifics of timelines here, what I would tell you is, we are definitely prioritizing making sure that Ukraine can defend itself. And we are well into the 90 howitzers that the president committed to Ukraine, almost all of them are actually in the country right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, we help Taiwan defend itself as well through the Taiwan Relations Act, which has been supported by both sides of the aisle now for many decades. And we're continuing to make sure that we are providing the capabilities to Taiwan to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to get into the shipment schedules for either place, but I can assure you that we are we remain committed to making sure that we are helping Taiwan with its self-defense needs across a range of military capabilities, not just artillery.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I meant that, that schedule is going to become important. We heard General Milley say that it's their goal, he believes to seize Taiwan by 2027.\par \par \uc2 So, if there is a three-year delay, that would bring them to 2025. And obviously, everybody's going to be watching that equipment, and that promise to help them to defend themselves, and potentially two fronts that we may be dealing with here.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this by Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  , and also by the British foreign minister, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  . And just to have everyone listen to the language that's being used now out of the U.K.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2  , PRIME MINISTER OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: The truth is that we were too slow to grasp what was really happening. And we collectively failed to impose the sanctions then, that we should have put on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  . And we cannot make the same mistake again.\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, BRITISH FOREIGN SECRETARY: We're doubling down. We will keep going further and faster to push Russia out of the whole of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With regard to that, you know, hindsight is always 2020. But we remember President Biden saying that the sanctions should go into place if and when Putin invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, would you say that it's the U.S. position when we look back now that we were also too slow to grasp what was happening?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, we were very careful at the time, Martha. And I think you and I talked about this actually before February 24th that if you pull the trigger on sanctions before an act has been committed, well, then, you've actually reduced the deterrent effect that it might have had to begin with. So, we made it clear --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, they weren't the deterrent, clearly.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We -- well, Mr. Putin made a choice here, he had diplomatic options on the table. He had the ability to avoid those sanctions. He chose to go ahead anyway. And now, the West, the United States, in particular, is doing everything we can to help Ukraine defend itself and win back its sovereignty. And they're doing that. They're working very, very hard at that every single day.\par \par \uc2 And as you just heard the president say, it's in no small measure, due to the kinds of weapons and systems that the United States is providing in record numbers. And I might add at record speed.\par \par \uc2 So, while we can all take a look back and think about things we could have done differently and better, and that's probably that's a healthy thing to do, Martha.\par \par \uc2 What we need to do right now, what we're focused on today, is what the president was talking about, which is helping them in the Donbas, which is a fight that is going to be reliant on artillery and what we call long range fires, and certainly a fight that is going to find more use for the Javelin anti-armor missile.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, on that point, because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  also appears to be looking forward and not backward, and he is looking potentially at annexing the Donbas region: Luhansk and Donetsk, we all watch May 9th, which is their big anniversary of the end of World War II and the -- and the loss of the Nazi forces -- victory over the Nazi forces.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess, you know, the question becomes, when I go back to \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  's statement, she said that we will keep going further and faster to push Russia out of the whole of Ukraine, is that our goal our Pentagon's goal as well? Out of the whole of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think, as you heard Secretary Austin say in testimony today, we are going to defer to Mr. Zelenskyy, the president of Ukraine to define what victory looks like, and what he wants.\par \par \uc2 And he's been very clear that he wants all of Ukraine to be sovereign and whole and free. But he gets to decide that. The Ukrainian people get to decide what victory looks like.\par \par \uc2 What we are doing is making sure that he has the tools and the systems, the information and the intelligence to do exactly that. And we're working on it again, at record speed.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we already have the bulk of the howitzers are -- in the country, from the time the president signed something as approved to transfer to Ukraine, we're getting it into their hands, oftentimes, just within a few days.\par \par \uc2 So, what we're focused on is making sure they have the tools to defend themselves and to decide for themselves what victory looks like? What a win looks like? That's really for the Ukrainians to speak to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And they have said it means the entire country, including the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, they have.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Including Crimea --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes, they have.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- which already got run over basically in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, real quick question. These reports that we're seeing about Putin undergoing surgery, is our intelligence confirming any of that?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I have not seen anything that corroborates those reports. I mean, it's -- lots of rumors that out there about his health. But I don't have any specific intelligence or information to corroborate any of that.\par \par \uc2 Look, we are focused right now, much more on Mr. Putin's actions and the decisions that he's making inside Ukraine. The people, he's killing, the city's he's destroying, the fighting that he has started, and that he needs to end.\par \par \uc2 And again, our focus is on helping Ukrainians resist that fighting. But our focus is on Mr. Putin's actions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and we hope that doesn't entail nuclear weapons, which we've heard a lot about recently. He needs to be stopped before that. Thank you very much, John Kirby. Always good to talk to you, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: You too, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, let's take you live to Lviv, Ukraine, where the mayor is saying that there was a new missile strike just a short time ago, and national correspondent Matt Finn, who is just under an active air raid alert there. Hi, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. About two hours ago, shortly before sunset, our crew heard loud blasts from inside the building where we are. We looked at the horizon and saw thick black smoke billowing across the city. Some of that smoke ended up settling in the air right behind us.\par \par \uc2 The mayor and state governor here now saying that three electrical power plants were hit. Substations, power has been knocked out across the city, and water supply is interrupted. One person so far reported injured.\par \par \uc2 And the Ukraine National Railway also now reporting six stations hit in central and western Ukraine with "severe damage" to infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Train stations and utilities have been major targets here. The air raid sirens were going on and off throughout the day today. We are under that act of air raid alert until about an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 This is now the third week in a row he that there have been missile strikes here in the Lviv region, sadly killing people.\par \par \uc2 The city of Lviv is swelled with an estimated hundreds of 1000s of refugees, many of those people escaping the East. Now, they are subject to missile strikes here in the West.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning, hopefully we'll be able to assess some of the damage from the missile strikes that we just saw a short while ago here in Lviv. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Matt Finn. Thank you very much, reporting live from Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, shock and dismay. That's the reaction from Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  today to an unprecedented leak from the highest court in the land when they were mid deliberation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): But I trust that the chief justice to clean his own House up. And if you need help from Congress, they'll be glad to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch is at the heart of this Supreme Court case and her state's ban on abortion after 15 weeks what she thinks about what has happened now in our exclusive interview coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A leak from inside the Supreme Court's inner sanctum sparking outrage today from both parties but for different reasons. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The integrity and independence of the Supreme Court is once again under attack.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The decision would be an abomination and abomination, one of the worst ever in modern history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chief legal correspondent Shannon Bream live in Washington. She is, of course, the anchor of "Fox News at Night" and author of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  bestseller "Mothers and Daughters of the Bible Speak." Shannon, good to have you with us a lot. A lot to cover today at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: There is, Martha. OK. Now that the court has actually confirmed that this draft that was circulated back in February is the real deal. We have a lot of questions. I mean, how much has changed since then, have both has changed, as the opinion itself change, when will we get it? There are plenty of legal ramifications, but there are political ones as well.\par \par \uc2 We're starting to hear from folks on Capitol Hill. GOP Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  is one of them. She famously, you'll remember, went to the Senate floor during the explosive Kavanaugh confirmation hearings to say, she was a yes vote. She's now reacting to what may be his vote will say to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Here's what she says. She says that the leaked draft opinion is the final decision and this reporting is accurate. It would be completely inconsistent with what Justice Gorsuch and Justice Kavanaugh said in their hearings and in our meetings in my office. Now, I couldn't get to what they said in the office but we do know what they said publicly. And as I recall, all of the Trump nominees were pressed on the issue of Roe and the role of president while they all expressed a deep respect for the court. And that principle, I do not recall any of them explicitly saying, at least publicly, that there was absolutely no possible scenario under which they would consider overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 The President says he had already directed his Gender Policy Council to prepare for any possible outcomes in this case, in response to what he calls a "coordinated attack" on abortion. And now some Democrats are again openly talking about doing away with the filibuster. Here, Senator \uc2 Patrick Leahy\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. PATRICK LEAHY (D-VT): I think that the Senate should eliminate the filibuster rule in order to codify.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Senator Markey, another Democrat, is returning to a position he's held in recent years, says it's time to add seats to the court critics call that court packing. As all that plays out, we now watch and wait to see what the ultimate opinion actually is from the court. And until it's signed off with names and votes, and officially issued by the court, we have no idea what this opinion will ultimately say, Martha,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right. This draft was written in February and they're expected to release the final opinion in end of June, beginning of July. We'll see. We know you'll be all over it and watching it for us. Shannon, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So with that, let's bring in Allie Beth Stuckey host of the podcast "Relatable" and author of "You're Not Enough And That's OK," and Jehmu Greene, founder and CEO of wedefendtruth.org and a Fox News contributor. Good to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 You know, I want to put a couple of numbers up here are, couple of polls here. This is the fox poll, and we're going to have new numbers actually coming out later this evening. Numbers who want to let Roe v. Wade stand, when that's the way the question is asked. This is how it breaks down. 63% let it stand, 31% overturn it.\par \par \uc2 When the question is asked do you want abortion to be legal, abortion should be legal 52, illegal 47. And there you see what we have more typically seen, which is a pretty close divide in the country, Jehmu, of people who think it should be legal and people who think it should be illegal. So, is this really so radical if the court decides that they want to just essentially what they would be deciding is that it's not a federal jurisdiction, it's something that should be decided by each state and the voters in that state.\par \par \uc2 JEHMU GREENE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Martha, it's more than radical. It's dangerous, it's unprecedented, and I think we have to start this conversation by recognizing that abortion access in this country has provided women the opportunity to pursue their education, to pursue the career of their choice to decide to have a family when they can be the best parent. And the Supreme Court is on the wrong side of not just history but the American people. There's a Gallup poll out there that shows 80% of Americans want abortion to be --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, it always comes down to the way the question is asked. What trimester do you think it's OK in? Over -- we see majorities who believe that anything past the first trimester, they are not so much in favor of.\par \par \uc2 So, Allie Beth Stuckey, let's bring you in here. Where do you think the American people are on this issue? And do they think that this is going to actually restrict a woman's right to find and have an abortion where in many states it will still be legal?\par \par \uc2 ALLIE BETH STUCKEY, HOST, "RELATABLE" PODCAST: I think there's a big misunderstanding on what overturning Roe would do. I think a lot of people, unfortunately, have adopted the misconception that this would abolish abortion across the board. According to Gallup, only 29% of people actually believe that abortion should be illegal in all cases. So the vast majority of Americans do believe there should at least be some restrictions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 As far as radical, I mean, let's talk about what abortion actually is. You're talking about killing a human being. If you want to talk about dangerous, I don't think restricting the killing of a human being should be described in that term. The brutal killing of unborn children is what should be described as radical and dangerous. And the court is absolutely not just on the right side of moral history, but on the right side of constitutionality as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jehmu, shaking your head. Obviously, this is, you know, a very hot button issue for a lot of people. But one of the things that I am struck by when I go through these numbers this afternoon, is that it is not so clear cut it to say that most Americans believe that this is a right that should be sustained. And the question that the court has taken up is not whether or not -- they're not banning abortion. They're saying this should never have been decided at the court level, because it is something you say most people want this right.\par \par \uc2 Well, then that is an argument in and of itself that most people should have the right to vote to decide, to elect officials who agree with them. So isn't that the proper venue for this in the state legislatures?\par \par \uc2 GREENE: Well, I certainly think that we are going to see a lot more activity in the state legislatures around this. We're already at a place, Martha, where 26 states, once this ruling comes down, 26 states are poised to ban abortion --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that's their prerogative, through their state legislature based on the wishes of the people who live there.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: As Senator Schumer made clear today, there are 100 million women who are about to lose access, to make a healthcare decision for themselves. In a country that has the highest maternal mortality rate, in a country that doesn't have universal childcare, in a country that has in accessible mental healthcare, the government wants to force women to have babies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well -- and a lot of people also can tell you the count of the number of babies that have been eliminated in the last 50 years. I'm guessing Allie Beth probably knows what that number is, because that's the other side of the story to be fair, Allie Beth.\par \par \uc2 STUCKEY: Of course. When you get down to again, what abortion is, it is not healthcare. When is healthcare ever been described as something that's a procedure where at least one person always dies? Abortion is the intentional killing of an innocent human being no matter how you want to look at it, that's what it is.\par \par \uc2 This is not forced birth. This is a government doing the bare minimum, which is protecting the right of a human being not to be murdered. That is what the debate is about. We can talk about different policies. I am all for making sure that we create a culture of life that is most of the pro- life work, making sure that women and their children, and fathers are all taken care of. But the lack of resources, the potential lack of resources for women after they give birth does not justify legally killing their children before birth.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: And there's no justification for forcing a woman to give birth. And quite frankly, there's nothing to be said about the just blatant line that we saw Justices Kavanaugh and Gorsuch do when it came to their confirmation process --\par \par \uc2 STUCKEY: It's wrong to kill a child, that's what it is.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: -- and women are going to turn out in November to right this wrong.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, there's no doubt that this will become a huge political issue. I would just point out again, and I know there's already fundraising happening on it across the board, people trying to get donations based on this. But what we will see is that if this decision stands, is that each state and each legislature, and the voters in those states will have the right to vote however they see fit on this issue across the country. So, Jehmu, thank you very much. Allie beth, good to see you. Thank you both for being here today.\par \par \uc2 STUCKEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And giving us your opinions, thank you both. So the case in Mississippi at the center of this now leaked decision on Roe v. Wade. This is just a huge seminal moment in the country for a hot button issue that has been on people's minds really since 1973 when Roe v. Wade was passed. The State Attorney General Lynn Fitch was the driving force in this Mississippi case. And she joins me exclusively when "The Story" continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH, MISSISSIPPI STATE ATTORNEY GENERAL: And it's in so abortion policy has been frozen since 1973. And it's time that our laws caught up because we're definitely making a change in our society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Yes. We are the court to overturn Roe v. Wade. And it's time, it's been nearly 50 years. The reality is this is a rule of law question. It's time to return it to the states because abortion policymaking should rest with the elected legislators and elected governors to make those decisions on behalf of the people because that's what they're elected to do. That's the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mississippi's attorney general here on "The Story" the day before her state defended its 15 week abortion ban at the Supreme Court. The case triggering the stunning leak of a draft opinion that would topple Roe v. Wade, potentially, after almost half a century as the law of the land. The court confirms the document is real, it's authentic, but says it is not the final opinion. It was written in February and it's expected to come out at the end of June.\par \par \uc2 Politico reporting "received a copy of the draft opinion from a person familiar with the courts proceedings in this Mississippi case along with other details supporting the authenticity of the documents." So with that, we bring in Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch, who brought this case. She joins me now. Good to see you, attorney General.\par \par \uc2 So, what was your personal reaction because this is so much -- you have worked so long and hard on this, when you learned, I assume you learned last night when the rest of us did. I don't know, maybe you can tell me if you learn before that this Alito decision opinion had been leaked.\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Great to see you too, Martha. Thank you for having me on. Yes, I learned last it night as with my team. You know, this is appalling, appalling that an institution like the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  that there's a breach of trust that happens. Certainly we are mindful of the justices, mindful of the opinion that will come down in a final form. And we'll certainly be anticipating and waiting for the final opinion. But it is, it's such an egregious act to have someone who is in a trusted capacity to walk out of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  with a precedent opinion like this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, do you think that there have been some discussion that perhaps Justice Roberts wanted a more narrow opinion that he didn't want to overturn Roe v. Wade, he wanted to rule on the case in Mississippi, and whether or not the 15 week ban on abortions after 15 weeks was constitutional. Do you think there's any way that that's the way this ends up going?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, I think certainly we're going to allow the court to speak to this. But remember that as soon as the argument occurred on December 1st, figure, that was all that was set in from the other side. They began fear discussions from the moment they argued all the way through.\par \par \uc2 So they've tried to bully our court. They particularly bullied the chief justice. They have gone after the echo chamber of let's pack the court or let's criminalize women who get abortions. So again, I think this is another push on the fear component, and they are again pushing very hard. And they're just -- it's a retread of the same arguments that they've made over and over again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So based on what you see in your own state legislature, and similar state legislatures, because we hear a lot of people saying this means the end of abortion in America. Is that the case? So your legislature passed a 15 week ban, meaning that after, you know, 15 weeks of pregnancy, which is, you know, deepened after four months, basically, there would be a ban. You couldn't select an abortion after that time. Do you think that that is what will hold in Mississippi or will Mississippi voters in the legislature go further and ban it altogether?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, the calls we could make, remember the whole nexus of our argument was a rule of law question, that this should always be returned to the states, every state should make a decision on how they would like to handle their abortion policymaking issues. Again, it is a rule of law question and they can each decide on their legislators. If they're not happy with them, then they can certainly remove them by the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 You know, we know that it'll be different in every state, Martha, that some states will codify abortion, some will put in stricter regulations. Certainly, that's going to be an issue as we see. But that's democracy at work, and we anticipate that will -- you know, happen in each state. But again, our argument is well as the rule of law who is always about the mission of empowering women and promoting life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. The case of Mississippi has jobs Dobbs versus Jackson Women's Health, and it is whether or not it will uphold a ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Lynn Fitch, attorney general in Mississippi, thank you very much. Lynn, good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Thank you. Great to see you too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. What -- didn't work went on today because you know what else, it's Election Day. A key test of former President Trump's power in the midterm elections is getting decided right now in Ohio, where people are going to the ballots and placing their placing their votes. There's just one lone person in that spot in Cleveland. There's another one. Let's go to Mark Meredith who is covering this for us in Cincinnati. Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha, good afternoon. From a very rainy Cincinnati and the bad weather may have an impact on the turnout you were just showing at different polling places all across the Ohio Valley. But those people that do go to the polls today will have plenty of different candidates to choose from. There are seven candidates running to be the Republican Senate nominee.\par \par \uc2 And right now we're looking to see if the results are going to match up what the polls we're showing, which was that author and businessman JD Vance was leading. For the longest time, Vance was really struggling in the polls falling behind the former Ohio State Treasurer Josh Mandel. And then in mid April, former President Trump offered up an endorsement in this race, and that really seemed to give Vance the lead that he had been looking for for a long time. It changed the race overnight.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mandel is still running as a pro-Trump candidate and he does have some state wide experience, so he certainly still in this race. Out on the trail, voters have been telling us they still questioned Vance's credentials considering back in 2016. He made multiple critical comments about Trump, as well as the MAGA movement. We caught up with him earlier today as he was casting his ballot here in Cincinnati, and he told us he's confident the polls will prove accurate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO US SENATE CANDIDATE: I feel good, I feel good. Look, we're in a very good place. We have to actually get the voters to turn out, the reins a little bit weird. But look, I think we're going to win. I think we're going to win by big margins.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: State Senator \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2  , who's running as a more traditional conservative and not embracing Trump's repeated claims of voter fraud is also reportedly seeing a late surge in support. Dolan was out there first thing this morning, Martha, trying to greet voters as the polls open. He also spoke with Fox about the race in this final stretch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT DOLAN\uc2  (R), OHIO US SENATE CANDIDATE: I think people as they began to pay attention to this race, and there are seven of us to choose from, they began to realize there's one candidate, me, that has been talking all about Ohio, going to Washington fight for Ohioans, and that's why I think the momentum is great.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: And when it comes to a primary, it's all about turnout. But turnout in early voting was fairly low, Martha, multiple campaigns have told us they were surprised at how many -- how few people had voted early on. We'll see what today brings but as I mentioned, the weather out here that hasn't been very busy as some of the polling places we've been to. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Going to look forward to see what the result is there tonight. Thank you very much, Mark. Meredith.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , National Journal Daily senior national political columnist who covers the campaign trail in his Against the Green Column. Great to see you, Josh. So this is fascinating to watch. You have this late endorsement by former President Trump for JD Vance. And then he kind of came up the left lane and this horse race, and moved into first place. But there's a lot of people out there and you could win with, you know, a smaller percentage of the overall vote, right?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , COLUMNIST, NATIONAL JOURNAL DAILY: It's a close race, primary polls are famously off. So any of the top three candidates I think, have a real chance of winning. And as you noted, Martha, this is the first big test of a Trump endorsement in a Republican primary. JD Vance was trailing in the polls. He moved into first place, but there's a question of whether that was sort of a short term sugar and whether that can last up until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 And the interesting candidate to watch, I think, is going to be \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2  , the state senator, who is the only Republican who didn't really embrace Trump as aggressively, didn't tie his campaign as closely to Trump as the other leading four candidates. And he has been gaining momentum, well, he may be coming up on the right side of that racetrack making a photo finish tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Listening to Pete Hegseth was at a diner. And I believe it was in Cincinnati this morning, and just listening to the people in there. You know, with Vance, all the way because I liked the Trump boost. I'll vote for whoever he tells me to vote for. And then, other people, I've been with Josh Mandel the whole time and, you know, mentioning a few of these other candidates as well.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised that, you know, what do you think about the former President's choice to even get into this race? I mean, it's early and you sort of put your name on the line in these races. And it's interesting in Georgia as well, when you don't necessarily have to in his position.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, Martha, it's a big gamble by President Trump. He's putting all his chips on the table and he could win big if he's the guy who propels Vance into the lead, into the nomination. But there's also a lot of reputational risk that if he doesn't win in Ohio, and Vance somehow doesn't come out on top. He's in bigger -- he got some bigger challenges with his endorsements in some of these other big states coming up in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 So getting a Vance win would be big for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . But if he loses in Ohio, he may have a lot of other losses with other candidates he really likes and his track record may look a little bit spotty after the month of May.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, I would expect that he's throwing his name behind several people out there. So if he wins more than he loses, that's going to be his story, right? You know, look, we're overall we're winning. The people who took his endorsement and one will be the big stars.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes. Dr. Oz keeping a close eye on the Ohio results because he also was endorsed by President Trump in Pennsylvania and the Senate race. And he also got a boost at first but kind of -- is now kind of still tied with not getting as much momentum as perhaps he anticipated. So this is going to be a big night to show whether Trump can capitalize on these big endorsements.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No doubt. And \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2  . has been playing a big role in this Ohio race as well, been out there campaigning with JD Vance. It's going to be very interesting to see what happens. Josh, as always, thank you. Great to have you with us tonight, hope to see a lot through these midterm races and get your insight. Thank you, Josh.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that's THE STORY for this Tuesday on a very big Tuesday with this leak at the Supreme Court and the Ohio GOP Senate Primary to watch for the results tonight. We're going watching that very closely throughout the evening. THE STORY goes on tomorrow, we'll see you back here then. Have a great day, everybody. Thanks for being with us. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par