ill not succeed. The work of the Court will not be affected in any way."\par \par \uc2 During December arguments over the Mississippi law at the heart of the case, which bans most abortions after 15 weeks, a majority of the justices seemed to be leaning in favor of upholding it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN G. ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE OF THE UNITED STATES SUPREME COURT: So, if viability, it seems to me, doesn't have anything to do with choice. But if it really is an issue about choice, why is 15 weeks not enough time?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What was much less clear was whether there would be five votes to overrule Roe v. Wade, half century of precedents.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: If we think that the prior precedents are seriously wrong, if that, why then doesn't the history of this Court's practice with respect to those cases tell us that the right answer is actually a return to the position of neutrality.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A draft majority opinion like this one authored by Justice Alito back in early February, is circulated to all the justices who then draft their own dissents, or concurrences.\par \par \uc2 The justices and their clerks are intimately involved with all the drafts which can change as Ken (PH) votes. The nearly 100-page draft is sweeping not only upholding Mississippi's law, but also overruling Roe and the subsequent K.C. opinion. "The Constitution makes no reference to abortion, and no such right is implicitly protected by any constitutional provision."\par \par \uc2 And later, "Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): If the official opinion does ultimately overturn Roe, each states will then be free to make its own decisions about abortion limits and access.\par \par \uc2 About two dozen states have laws that would institute more limits, while 16 other states in Washington, D.C. would 