m in their polling, of course, everybody's pulling including Fox News' polls, Bret, show very clearly inflation, immigration, jobs, and of course, national security, border security have been leading in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Last thing. This is -- this is a base issue for both sides. And I think you can probably expect that those who are pro-life, believing that you are killing a baby, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said today, aborting a child, and those who believe this is about women's health, they will be able to turn out the numbers in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But I think the left will take this a little too far and not realize how much people are hurting at the gas pump in the grocery store. How concerned they are about border security, national security as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, POLITICO writes of this way, "Democrats hope draft abortion opinion will jolt midterm elections: Privately, however, several Democrats acknowledge that campaigning on the Court's potential rejection of abortion rights would likely require them to do something they've typically struggled with in election years. Challenged the GOP on an issue that galvanizes its base." Your thoughts and positioning to that?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Yes. First, let me just say to Kellyanne's point, as an American and a patriot, I am worried about the leak. I am against Court packing or doing away with the filibuster, if that be what the Democrats are considering.\par \par \uc2 I also believe that the issue is dramatically being overstated by some in the Democratic Party. It certainly can help galvanize the base, Kellyanne acknowledge that. I certainly see it. Suburban women, younger people, people of color, I could see it helping. But the inflation issue, the issue of what's going on in our foreign policy, these are issues that people relate to day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 As you saw, Bret, from the numbers you put up, abortion is not one of the top five or six issues. And while I think it can help the party. The ActBlue fundraising numbers between last night and this morning we're not overwhelming as they were when \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  sadly passed away for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So, I worry that my party might overreact and I would also say this decision is not final. We all know that court decisions can and do change. And this decision which was I think, authored February 15th has a long history, even if it hadn't been leaked, before it became settled the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I want to put up very quickly, a Fox News poll, President Biden's job performance on a number of different issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: (voice-over): And Kellyanne, as you look at it, you know, coronavirus is really the only one, he's above water by two percent, but not by a lot. And everything else is underwater. Inflation, 67 percent disapprove. And to your point, that hits people's kitchen tables.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: It sure does. And that's not going to change. I mean, people really are hurting. They're complaining about that. And even much to my surprise, Bret, we don't see course correction in this White House. Americans have lost confidence in the competence of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I'm not sure he has the goodwill stored up.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, as people keep saying, gee, a majority Americans don't want Roe overturned. Two quick things, number one is we don't like words like overturn abolish, revolutionize, eliminate. That's a given.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we did a poll in March. Just ask the Americans please describe in your own words what Roe versus Wade provided in terms of the law and 26 percent get it correct as a quiz question.\par \par \uc2 I think that's benefited the Democrats quite a bit that people are a little confused and uncertain about what Roe provides. There are lies flying today that this would do away with abortion. That is not true. It would still be available. This goes back to the states where most health and welfare decisions are made.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Final thing, Doug, as you look at the primaries tonight, are you -- in coming days and weeks, are you looking on the Republican side about the juice that the former president has or doesn't have?\par \par \uc2 DOUG SCHOEN, FORMER CLINTON PRESIDENTIAL ADVISER: Yes, that is the key issue. Is this going to be an election as 2020 was about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Or will it be about inflation and immigration? Two issues where as I read those numbers, Bret, two-thirds or thereabouts of the American people were adverse to the administration and its handling of those problems.\par \par \uc2 I think we are looking at a wave election. My polling shows that the Senate and the House will both go to the Republicans, perhaps decisively, I don't think this will change it. Whether or not this becomes the settled law of the land with the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 And as Kellyanne said, I was part of the Clinton administration, we did of course correction, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  needs one desperately.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Doug, Kellyanne, we appreciate it. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Russian forces halt evacuations for Mariupol, storming last pocket of Ukrainian resistance in that besieged city. We'll have a live report from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Russian forces have halted evacuations from Mariupol, storming a steel plant held by Ukrainian defenders. It's the latest move by Russia to block civilians from an escape route in the ravaged city.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): More than a hundred civilians arrive in the Ukrainian town of Zaporizhzhia. The United Nations and Red Cross says they're coming from a steel plant in the besieged city of Mariupol. That remains under assault at this hour.\par \par \uc2 SVIATOSLAV PALAMAR, AZOV REGIMENT DEPUTY COMMANDER (through translator): Glory to Ukraine. Today on May 3rd, enemy naval artillery and barrel artillery shelled the Azovstal compounds, heavy air bombs were dropped. As a result of these criminal actions, two civilian woman have died.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: With heavy Russian attacks on going around the plant, the last Ukrainian resistance stronghold in Mariupol, calls are growing louder to save more than 200 Ukrainian civilians believed to be trapped underneath the facility.\par \par \uc2 Protesters marched through the streets of the Ukrainian capital this afternoon, demanding the safe release of those remaining in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 Across town, British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  spoke to the Ukrainian parliament over a video call announcing an additional $375 million in military aid that will include radar systems and drones.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: It's a conflict that has no moral ambiguities, or gray areas. This is about the right of Ukrainians to protect themselves against Putin's violent and murderous aggression.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Making similar comments today at a \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  plant in Alabama, President Biden called on U.S. lawmakers to approve his $33 billion aid request for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I urge the Congress to pass this funding quickly to help Ukraine continue to succeed against Russian aggression, just as they did when they won the battle of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Weeks after, Russian atrocities were committed near Kyiv. War crimes prosecutors are still digging up the dead like in Irpin were Valentina Popyl gives a statement about her brother who was killed during the Russian advance and buried along the side of the road.\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA POPYL, IRPIN RESIDENT (through translator): We had three incoming shells in our neighborhood, she explains. He was outside in his backyard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Today, we've heard air raid sirens multiple times across the Ukrainian Capitol, indicating Kyiv remains a target for Russian forces, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst live in Kyiv. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as President Biden considers eliminating some student debt, a new study reveals the move may help the nation's wealthiest households the most, we'll explain.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight, a heat wave in India has forced power outages throughout that country. India relies on coal for most of its electricity, but supplies at many thermal power plants are running low. The shortages are sparking scrutiny over India's need to diversify its energy resources.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Sydney, Australia. One of the big stories there tonight, \uc2 Australia's central bank\uc2  lifts its benchmark interest rate for the first time in more than 11 years. The hike was expected as the country deals with rising inflation there and around the world. The move could prove damaging for Australia's government which will seek reelection later this month.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Biden administration considers eliminating some student debt to help those struggling to pay it off. A recent study, though, reveals the nation's wealthiest households could catch the biggest break. FOX Business correspondent Grady Trimble takes a look tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: It's a campaign promise President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he'll keep -- student loan debt forgiveness, a popular proposal among progressives and many with student loans.\par \par \uc2 VINCENT VERTUCCIO, STUDENT ORGANIZER: It's an issue that is affecting working class Americans of all background, and it's a crisis that the president can fix with the stroke of a pen.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But a January report from the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  suggests the people who benefit most from such a policy would be the wealthy. The report says the wealthiest 20 percent of households owe almost a third of student loan debt. The next fifth owe right around 30 percent. The bottom 20 percent, the least wealthy, owe only eight percent of student loan debt. \uc2 Marc Goldwein\uc2  is with the nonpartisan Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget.\par \par \uc2 MARC GOLDWEIN\uc2 , COMMITTEE FOR RESPONSIBLE FEDERAL BUDGET: This is not a targeted way to provide relief. This is not reduce the wealth gap or reduce the income debt. If anything, it worsens economic inequality.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: But the White House says its intention is targeting relief. It's exploring the possibility of income caps for those whose loans will be forgiven.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president talked back on the campaign about taking steps or looking at steps to help people making less than $125,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The Biden administration insists the president can use executive action. If not and it requires Congress to act, there may be wiggle room from Republicans on student debt, but not on blanket forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2 , (R-OK): Allowing people to have greater grace to be able to pay off their debt at a slower pace may be one thing, but just telling people we are going to wipe this out, that's a whole different issue for me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: The Brookings report goes on to say the future value of a college degree is often left out of conversations about student loan debt. On average, it found the typical college graduate makes around $1 million more over the course of their career compared to someone with only a high school diploma. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Grady, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Arkansas Republican Governor Asa Hutchinson. Governor, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON, (R) ARKANSAS: Good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to ask you about all the abortion developments and the Supreme Court in just a bit, but coming off of that piece, what do you think of the student loan debate, wiping it away in the midst of the economy you're seeing in Arkansas?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, to wipe the student loan debt for our nation across the board is bad policy. But also, as your report pointed out, it's unfair. You look at Arkansas, and we have many high school graduates that go straight into the workforce, they get their training, they pay for it as they go, they get some assistance, but they might not go to college. They don't create that debt. And for them to carry the burden of paying off everyone's college debt is basically unfair.\par \par \uc2 I do believe that you can look at teachers, you can look at those that go into law enforcement, and provide some targeted relief. I think that's a fair debate. There's -- we want to be able to encourage those professions. But across the board, bad policy, and there is unfairness in it. We should not go down that path.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn to this draft opinion that was leaked, and Roe v. Wade, overturning Roe v. Wade. Here's three Democratic governors' reaction on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  today. Illinois Governor Pritzker, "Women's rights are under assault, and they won't stop there. Gay rights, birth control, fertility treatment, every decision that makes our country more free and more fair." New York's Governor Hochul, "Just so we're clear, banning abortions doesn't stop abortions. It stops safe abortions. In New York we will always protect your right to safe, legal reproductive care, period." Pennsylvania Governor \uc2 Tom Wolf\uc2 , "Abortion access in Pennsylvania will remain legal and safe as long as I am governor."\par \par \uc2 I know Governor Hutchinson, you have a different thought about abortion, and in fact if Roe v. Wade is overturned abortion would be banned in Arkansas. But your thoughts of this day, this leak, and where we go?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, and I think that's the first point is that we believe in the independence of the judiciary, and if you support that, then you have to be very concerned about this intentional attack on the court. And I do believe that the leaker will be caught. I think they will be identified. It's a small number of people. And with the techniques and interviews, the discretion of the investigation, they ought to be able to identify that one. And so that's something that the president and others should in a bipartisan way be speaking out against, that kind of unethical conduct at a minimum that we see in this leak.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the overall question, I think about my past, for 35 years I have been going to pro-life rallies, talking about the overturn of Roe versus Wade. And to think that it really emphasizes that engaging in the political side, engaging in who we elect to office, makes a real difference, and who is on the court makes a difference. This is a -- if the decision that has been leaked comes down in that fashion, first, it's a constitutional decision that says the right or privacy is not identified in the Constitution, Roe versus Wade was wrongly decided. And it goes back to the states.\par \par \uc2 I think there will be a robust debate in the states. States, just like in handling coronavirus, will come down on different directions on this. Arkansas is a pro-life state. We have already put a trigger law in place, and we're going to protect those lives if that decision comes down that reverses Roe versus Wade. We also have to be able to provide for the maternal health. We have to be able provide better adoption services. And I think that's going to be a part of the discussion as well as it should be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last thing, we talked about this with the pollsters earlier. Democrats believe that this decision, if it heads down this road, as we expect it to, will motivate their base exponentially heading into the midterms. You are a politician. You follow politics day to day. Do you believe that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I think it will motivate their base. I think they are intentionally trying to motivate their base. But it also is going to encourage the base of the Republican Party that understands the importance of pro-life values and legislation. And so, it's going to be affected on both sides. But I do believe that you're going to have a broader range of issues in the fall election, which will be from inflation, the supply chain, the economy, the worry about a recession. These are issues that are going to dominate as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Hutchinson, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we head to the southern border where migrants are preparing to enter the U.S. as the end of that COVID era immigration restriction looms, Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But first, as we head to break, we remember former Transportation Secretary Norman Mineta. Mineta was the first Asian American to serve as a cabinet secretary under both Presidents Clinton and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . His legacy includes ordering all commercial flights grounded after the September 11th terror attacks. Norm Mineta was 90 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, a new report from an internal watchdog finds the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  modified a report on Russian interference in the 2020 election to blunt the impact of its finding that Russia was aiding President Trump. Correspondent David Spunt has the details of this report. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Bret. The report from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 's inspector general found the acting secretary under President Trump altered and delayed an internal report on Russian interference in the 2020 election, worried it, quote, "will hurt POTUS. Kill it." That report concluded the acting secretary participated in the review process multiple times despite lacking any formal role in reviewing the product, resulting in the delay of its dissemination on at least one occasion. The delays and deviation from the I.A. Office of Intelligence and Analysis, standard process and requirements put the I.A. at risk of creating a perception of politicization.\par \par \uc2 The acting secretary at the time was \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2 , who was not mentioned by name. The I.G. found that DHS staff also made changes to its look into the foreign election interference based in part on, quote, "political considerations." There were also concerns about objectivity by senior DHS leadership.\par \par \uc2 Bret, this came during a turbulent time in the months leading up to the November 2020 elections when President Trump was running against former vice president and candidate, nominee \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The report said, quote, "Russian maligned influence actors were spreading rumors on Biden's mental health, rumors which were unsubstantiated." And the public release of a DHS intelligence analysis on those efforts was twice delayed by Wolf.\par \par \uc2 Bret, within the last five minutes or so we received a statement from the current DHS leadership under President Biden. That person said, quote, "This troubling report raises concerns over the prior administration's inappropriate interference in the review and clearance process for an intelligence product. Since January, 2021, DHS has renewed its commitment to providing accurate, timely, and actionable information and intelligence free from politicization and bias to the public and our partners across every level of government in the private sector and local communities."\par \par \uc2 Bret, this is breaking. We, of course, will continue to follow it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New video obtained by FOX News shows hundreds of migrants arriving in a city along the border on the Mexican side. A new FOX poll, meantime, showing 63 percent of registered voters want Title 42 restrictions to stay in place, 27 percent say they should end, meaning a surge at the border. Correspondent Bryan Llenas is in Eagle Pass, Texas, again tonight. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening. The National Guard installed concertina wire along the banks of the Rio Grande River in an effort to deter migrants from crossing illegally into Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 But, as you can see, some have found a way to get through by placing their wet clothes on top of those sharp raisers, protecting themselves from cuts so that they can make their way. It gives you an idea how difficult it is to contain illegal immigration and how desperate these people are. If you follow the trail of adults and children's clothes, it will lead you directly to the Rio Grande.\par \par \uc2 You're looking live at the river where another unprecedented surge of migrants is expected to illegally cross this summer as a result of the Biden administration's lifting of Title 42, the COVID-19 policy used to deport migrants immediately. This new video from today shows more Haitian migrants arriving Nuevo Laredo, Mexico, across from Laredo, Texas. We now know over the last two weeks, roughly 100 Haitian migrants are arriving per day. Large groups of migrants continue to illegally cross into the United States. This video shows Border Patrol and Texas DPS agents encountering dozens of migrants in the brush in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 You are looking at us live from above in our FOX News drone. This is private property, a paintball park owned by a young business owner. You can see the extent of the clothing and personal items scattered all over -- a wallet, a Converse tennis shoe, a pacifier, an infant onesie. The owner says he will have to pay for the clean-up. It's a familiar scene for ranchers and property owners up and down the border. Among the other things that are left behind, identification cards like these from Mexico and Chile. We are told some migrants prefer anonymity because it will help in their asylum cases. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan Llenas live along the border. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel reacts to the leak from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and what it all means, big picture.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: Never before, whoever committed this lawless act knew exactly what it could bring about.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2 , (R-OK): Whoever leaked this out clearly has an agenda that they are trying to be able to accomplish. We'll know at some point all the details and all the motivations on this.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: This is as urgent and real as it gets. We will vote to protect a woman's right to choose, and every American is going to see which side every senator stands on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All reaction to this draft opinion that was leaked from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , a shocking move because the court doesn't really leak, or it hasn't in many, many years.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, FOX News political analyst Juan Williams, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina. Trey, when you heard this, were you shocked? What's your reaction today?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: I was stunned. The judicial branch was always a cut above the executive and legislative branches. This kind of stuff is standard fare for Congress and the White House. But the judicial branch I kind of viewed as the last institution that was maybe immune from this. What it proves is that we are just a country full of relativists. The end justifies the means. It doesn't matter how we accomplish our goal, process be damned. If it means leaking something, breaking the trust of your colleagues, then the end justifies the means. I think it's sad. And I would think the same thing if it was an opinion that I disagreed with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A.P. says "What's next for abortion after the Supreme Court leak? Congress could move swiftly to enshrine a natural right to abortion, but that's unlikely. Such an effort has previously stalled in the Senate where Democrats have only a slim majority. If there is no legislative path to protecting abortion, it could take decades for the Supreme Court's decision to be undone."\par \par \uc2 And Katie, to that end, Senator Manchin told reporters today "The filibuster is the only protection we have of democracy right now. I have always believed in protecting women's rights with the filibuster, but the bottom line is we need checks and balances." So, that's not happening, the Manchin-Sinema vote is not materializing. And it's important to point out that abortions don't become illegal if this does get to the ruling, but it's sent to the states.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's really interesting to watch how Democrats jumped right over this issue of this egregious leak in the words of the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  today, and straight to the politics of this issue. Joe Manchin has also voted in the past against very extreme abortion bills put up by Democrats. It's not that they don't have enough votes to eliminate just the filibuster, but they don't have enough votes to pass the kind of abortion legislation that Democrats have come to support. They have gone from safe, legal, and rare abortion in the 1990s to not wanting to have any kind of discussion about any kind of restrictions despite the majority of Americans being in favor of abortion restrictions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 But if you look across the country at Democrats, and this has been the choice in the 2020 presidential election with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and 2016 election with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , when the Democratic candidate is asked do you support any limits on abortion, they won't answer the question. And we have seen Democratic state legislatures pass laws on abortion that allow abortion essentially up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 So when they accuse Republicans of being extreme on this issue, politically, Republicans are actually closer to the majority of Americans than Democrats would like them to believe as they push this false narrative that if Roe is overturned somehow abortion will be illegal everywhere. That's just not the case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And as you look at the map, Juan, there are 26 states that are likely or certain to ban abortions if Roe v. Wade is overturned. And there you see the possible abortion restrictions. We talked to Governor Hutchinson. They have a trigger in Arkansas already. It's a significant issue. But is it a motivating issue? We're in May ahead of a November midterm.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think it is potentially a motivating issue. I think the FOX News poll has 63 percent of Americans saying let Roe stand as it is, and that includes more than half of Republican voters. Now, Republicans typically have been motivated, the base, by the abortion issue. But as you can see from the crowds outside the court today, by the response from Democratic politicians, Democrats think this is going to definitely stir the Democratic base and maybe even extend to Democrats in the suburbs, independent women voters.\par \par \uc2 The leak, it steams to me, I just want to underline my agreement with Trey. I think this is a radical step. I have covered the court. I've never experienced anything like this. It undermines the credibility of the court as an independent branch of government. It's corrosive in that extent. Someone is trying to influence something. I'm not sure it's from the left. All indications are that there is a fight within the court about maintaining what looked like in February to be a five-vote majority. And it could be that people are trying to bargain and make deals, and think that this leak helps one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 But I think as far as what we know from what Alito has written, that undermines 50 years of precedent? That's pretty radical. We have never had rights taken away as Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a lot of speculation about the motives behind the leak, Trey, but the significance of what Alito wrote in at least that draft opinion is what?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: That it was -- I don't think it was ever going to be the majority opinion. I cannot see \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  signing that. I can't see Amy Coney Barrett. No offense to Justice Alito, but he has a different writing style, and I just don't see him cobbling together five justices. And if I'm wrong, which happens a lot, then the issue will be back before the Supreme Court, Bret, because you know some states are going to ban all abortions, including rape, incest, and the health of the mother, and some states are going to say you can have abortions until the child's first birthday. So it's going to come back to the Supreme Court unless they decide the issue of when life begins, and they are not doing that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's exactly right. There is another case in front of the court now on all of this. Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, it's Tuesday. That means it's time for "Tuesday Tweets." W.G. Fisk asks, "Do you believe \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  decision is a winning strategy for Democrats to run on for the midterms?" Well, we have been discussing a little bit of this, and you heard the pollsters earlier. Again, we are a long way away from November. We'll see how the actual ruling comes down, but, yes, it's motivating for the Democratic base 100 percent. It could affect things.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Barbara wants to know "What career advice would you give new graduates?" I say it's like driving on a road. You don't know where the road is going. You have your headlights, you can make out the road, eventually you see where you are going. Just keep on driving, even though it's sometimes tough.\par \par \uc2 Keith tweets, "Hey, Bret, if you could play with one PGA golfer, who would it be? And what's your handicap? No sugarcoating." Man, I get the handicap a lot. I am a 2.4 index. But I'm a walking wallet. I just hand money over. I would like to play with Tiger. I have not played with Tiger, and I think that would be pretty cool. You never know. The comeback continues.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll have results from the primary elections in Ohio and Indiana.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That is it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" with guest host Brian Kilmeade starts right now. What do you think, Tiger?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220504ei5300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 An Opinion Draft Overturning Roe v. Wade Was Leaked From The Supreme Court Making Democrats Criticizing The Ruling; Liberal Media Frenzied Over The Leaked Opinion Draft Claiming Fascism; Student Debt Madness: AOC Says Loan Forgiveness Is Good For Everyone; Celebrities, Dems Party At Lavish Met Gala; Tuned Out Teens: Teenage Stop Listening To Their Mothers At Age 13\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Joey Jones, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,664 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. And that this particular Supreme Court case doesn't cover that to your point here. So, but we'll be examining the survey closely. Senator Daines, thank you very, very much. I think Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  might have put this in perspective by saying that Roe was egregiously wronged from the start. Therein lies the debate.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Joey Jones and she buys all her scarves from Vick the local spider, Dana Perino, "The Five."\par \par \uc2 So, did something happen? Yes, an unprecedented leak at the Supreme Court sending Democrats and Republicans into a frenzy, but for different reasons. Chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  confirming the authenticity of a draft opinion that would overturn Roe versus Wade while pointing out that the Supreme Court has not made a final decision.\par \par \uc2 Roberts ripping the leak as an egregious breach of trust and is ordering an investigation. President Biden and Democrats ignoring the leak and are instead attacking Republicans and conservative justices on the high court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: If this decision holds, it's really quite a radical decision.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): This is a dark and disturbing morning for America.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): This is a five-alarm fire.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): We need to get rid of the filibuster and Roe is just exhibit A.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Part of this November's election, reproductive rights will be on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): This would appear to be an invitation to have, you know, hand maid's tale type antifeminist regulation.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): These justices are acting like this is somehow something that they have the right to change. They do not have the right to change this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow. This isn't coordinated at all. Democrats also telling their supporters to get out there and protest. Meanwhile, Republicans say the leak was meant to intimidate the justices into changing their vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The radical left immediately rallied around the toxic stunt making a last ditch Hail Mary attempt to cause a political firestorm and cause the court to reconsider.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): The Democrats really don't see the rule of law or the court's independence as principles that are above politics.\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS, GOVERNOR OF FLORIDA: That's a real significant breach of trust. You want to talk about an insurrection, you know, that's a judicial insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: If only we had a judge here. All right, judge. Who do you think leaked it and why and give us your assessment of this whole scene.\par \par \uc2 JEANNINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: I don't have the slightest idea who leaked it, but I'll tell you this. Whoever leaked it did something that is unheard of in American history. I mean, you just don't leak a decision by any judge, let alone a Supreme Court justice. I mean, there is -- and one of the things that we can be proud of is that the justices on the Supreme Court, they can be on the opposite side of issues, but you never hear about them not getting along.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we hear about Scalia, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , the best of friends although ideologically they were on opposite side. So whoever leaked this, we have to find out who the person is and charge them with a crime. I don't know what the crime would be, whether it's some kind of fraud on government property.\par \par \uc2 I've heard there is a statute fraud on a government that's very broad that could include this kind of act. But I'll tell you what's so interesting about this, is if they wanted to gin up the left who was in for alleged bloodbath in November by the Republicans, there is no better way to do it than with Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 But if you will just indulge me for a minute. I read the decision at length, the Dobbs decision as well as Roe again. Roe they say in this decision is an exercise of raw judicial power. In other words, it is a Supreme Court acting as a legislative body. And understand that if this decision is ultimately the decision of the court, it doesn't outlaw abortion. All it says is we are saying it's not a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Congress can pass a law, so \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is so fired up, pass a law, Chuck. Every state can pass its own law. They can -- they can, you know, negate it. They can ban it. They can promote it. Whatever they want to do. But this is what I think is so stunning about it. This decision says there is no right to abortion that is implicit in the constitution.\par \par \uc2 Understand there are three tests. Number one, is it in the constitution? Is it a constitutional right? Freedom of speech constitutional right. First amendment, the right to bear arms is a constitutional right, second amendment. But the right to an abortion? It is implied based upon what Roe says is the first, fourth, fifth, ninth, 14th amendment and I won't get into it, but they say we don't know which one for sure but it's through the due process clause.\par \par \uc2 In order for it to be part of due process and legal and constitutional, it must be a deeply rooted in our nation's history and tradition and it must be implicit in ordered liberty. Is abortion deeply rooted in our nation's history? No. In fact, at the time of Roe, virtually every state outlawed abortion.\par \par \uc2 And right before Roe, going back to the common law, it was murder and then it was reduced to a misdemeanor at some -- in some states. And it is -- is it implicit in the concept of ordered liberty? No. Even \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  said that. So, that is something that is based, I think, very well on very well argued. And, you know, in order for the left to get everyone fired up, they want someone on the right to cave to the mob. They want to intimidate some justice on the right to back up and support it.\par \par \uc2 But it's not a constitutional right, like same sex marriage, like interracial marriage, like contraceptive, Loving vs. Virginia. Griswold vs. Connecticut, all those cases. This involves a third party and that's the difference.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go. That was pretty clear. I feel like I could just -- I'm going to just say --\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you for coming to her Ted talk.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I know. So, the judge brings up something very interesting. The timing of this. And we talked about this that things are going so bad for the Democrats that some -- they need something and I mean, this is an amazing distraction before the midterms. Now, everyone's hair is on fire instead of talking about or focusing on inflation, crime, the border.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I have lots of different thoughts. One, I think that the people who supported Roe v. Wade were living on borrowed time and they knew it because this decision as the judge just pointed out, even \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  was like this is -- this thing was poorly decided. And so at some point that was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of claims today that this is the death of democracy when actually what happens is your state legislature will decide to bring it up again and some states like Colorado have a very, very permissible abortion. And then you might have another state like Mississippi that's going to go in a different direction. But the voters will get to decide and that is what happens in our republic. That's how it works.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. And Roe v. Wade was anti-Democratic because this report --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- in not a democratic institution.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Absolutely. And so when you have somebody like Liz Warren saying, well, we absolutely must kill the filibuster in order to pass this. Well, they couldn't pass it even in February. They got 46 votes for a bill that they wanted to try to codify Roe. It doesn't work. So you have for example, AOC attacking \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  today, but they don't even have the votes.\par \par \uc2 So, the internal Democratic fighting will continue. I also think that there's -- you're going to hear a lot about that the justices when they were in their confirmation process would have private meetings with senators and they would tell them what you thought because they always are asked, do you think that Roe v. Wade is settled law? And they'll say, well, they told me it was settled law.\par \par \uc2 Well, now you'll have some senators say they lied to me. And so the question is then why and I maybe -- when the decision actually comes out there will be a more fulsome discussion of that as to why this happened or maybe they'll do an interview. But remember, separate but equal was settled law until it wasn't as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So there was a case that was brought before the court. It's gone through the system. And even the liberals knew that Mississippi state law when it got passed they thought, oh boy, here we go. And I think that both -- my last point would be that both sides have gotten exactly what they said is coming for years, for however long.\par \par \uc2 How long has planned parent had been telling everybody that if Roe is overturned, women are going to die and be second class citizens, which is why you have a lot of people actually think that today. And then on the right, they've been saying if you elect Republicans presidents and Republican senators and you nominate conservative judges, then you will get to overturn Roe v. Wade. So now both sides are going to have to deal with the fallout on it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. You know, Joey, I think, you know, what scares me at this decision, it's obviously going to target the truly marginalized and I speak of pregnant men. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I have a lot of thoughts but I know that we don't have a lot of time. So, there are two issues here. One is the leak, which stands in itself as a controversy. The other is what might happen with Roe versus Wade and how abortion is handled in this country.\par \par \uc2 I think the one thing that I want to remind people is that people sitting at home watching right now aren't constitutional scholars and we accept that there is a religious right and there is a very liberal progressive left that are very passionate about this issue. But there is a lot of people in the middle that have an opinion on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Every agnostic person that's ever held their own child in their hand probably has an opinion about this issue. Every single mother that's ever been in a place that she felt like was between a rock and a hard place in the worse circumstance has an opinion on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And that's why the Supreme Court looks at this issue as unsettled as law in the land and that's why it's probably important that someone who is even pro-abortion but a constitutionalist might say, you know, Roe versus Wade probably overreached and didn't allow the people in this country to make this decision because it may be the most complex issue we ever decide as a nation.\par \par \uc2 We are asking the question what is life. Somewhere between conception and consciousness. That's a hard issue to explain or to define and science isn't there. That's why the Supreme Court struggled with this and that's why so many people struggled with Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jessica, last word to you.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: A lot of words. I was shocked by this last night. I really did not expect 9:00 that suddenly this would be what was happening. Obviously, it's something that liberals have warned of and that conservatives have long wanted so I guess it's not a complete surprise.\par \par \uc2 The timing is strange. I have seen feasible arguments for why it was a liberal clerk that leaked this and why it was a conservative clerk who leaked this because if it was a conservative, it might be someone who wants to keep all the five judges that are on the side of Alito's opinion in their box there.\par \par \uc2 And they don't want to leave any room for pushback probably on someone like Justice Kavanaugh who I think out of the group would be the most likely to maybe side with Roberts who it seems like is not with the group of five and is writing a separate concurrence. So, leaking is bad. It should not happen. There have been partial leaks before. Actually, the original Roe decision I thought that was fascinating. It was partially leaked.\par \par \uc2 But it's obviously a huge deal. The numbers speak for themselves, 63 percent of Americans believe that abortion should be legal in all or most cases. Most cases is where we get into trouble to Joey's point. And the people want to have some sort of conversation about a viability date. Right?\par \par \uc2 That I think we can all agree at least at this table that these laws that have come into place in conservative states like Mississippi where we're talking about six-week abortion bans. I was recently pregnant. I did not know that I was pregnant at the six-week mark. I wanted my baby. I am thrilled to have my daughter, but that doesn't mean that I should have my ability to make my own healthcare decisions stripped away from me because I happen to live in that state.\par \par \uc2 What Elizabeth Warren was talking about and everyone can make fun of her about her hysterical nature. She is absolutely correct. This law will hurt poor women. This law will hurt women who do not have access to good healthcare. I'm an upper middle class white woman. I will be able to get whatever healthcare I want for the rest of my life. That is not true of women that are struggling, people who are working two jobs.\par \par \uc2 And also the majority of women who get abortions guess what? They've already been mothers. They are people who making family planning decisions. This is not about teenagers running around and getting pregnant in a car and just saying, oh, I don't care, or people who think that abortion is the same as the morning after pill. It is not.\par \par \uc2 It is one of what -- the most carefully considered decisions that a woman makes. And that's why this law was so important. It was about privacy. That I should be able to go into a room with my doctor and make a decision.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That's why it's so tragically that there are people lobbying them to try to influence them to get an abortion. That's part of the problem. It is a nuanced thing that every specific individual has to make for themselves and that might be why states should decide as a culture and a sovereign state how they are going to handle it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Why should you be someone who can't live in the state of Texas, for instance, because you want to be able to make decisions about how to control your own body? And I'm so sick also of seeing on television people who were screaming that I was infringing on their rights because I asked them to wear a mask. Telling me that I have to have a baby that I don't necessarily want or that I can't --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You can even reverse that argument with the vaccines as well. So.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I would still argue that a shot in your arm is a little bit different than a commitment to a person for 18 years. And Republicans also, no support for the social services that they want to do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That commitment is a lot.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: They just say have a baby and deal with it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I think the assumption that the poor, you know, helping the poor by giving them more abortions seem pretty gross to me.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's giving them options.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, of course. That's what we call it. Up next, the media inflaming divisions and ramping up hysteria of the Supreme Court's abortion leak.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Welcome back. While Democrats are telling people to get out in the streets, the left-wing media is ratcheting up the tensions in the country with an almost apocalyptic take on the leaked Supreme Court opinion overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: In the event that we had a Republican president in 2024, that's where we'd be. We'd be at a, you know, South America style nationwide abortion ban in America.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST: What other cases have been decided based on these precedents that could now be in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 JAFFREY TOOBIN, CNN LEGAL ANALYST: Same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Honestly, I want to cry.\par \par \uc2 MIKA BRZEZINSKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: What does this story mean for the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC HOST: Well for the Supreme Court, in a word, illegitimacy.\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR, HOST, THE VIEW: This is just the beginning. Next, they will go after gay marriage and maybe the board, the -- what is it, Brown versus Board of Education. They already eroded our voting rights a little bit. So, I see some -- I see fascism down the line here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Greg, when Jeffrey Toobin is speaking out, have they lost the moral superiority on this --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, he's a big fan of abortion if you look into his history.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. So.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well. The question --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There are few things in his history that are pretty disgusting.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The question is are these media celebrities, are they truly passionate about this argument or is this just the next flame they can throw?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think it's the -- I think it could be a matter of both. I mean -- how dumb are these people to think that this is banning abortion. It's not, right? This is about sending it back to the states. And by the way, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," very, very pro-choices says that if this decision is made, the choice is made, you might see a 14 percent decline in abortions, 14 percent.\par \par \uc2 That's, I mean, if your hair is on fire about that and you know that if you wanted an abortion, even if you are poor, Jessica, somebody is going to get you that abortion. It's almost as though they have a quota, right? It's like people want abortion be as American as apple pie and hot dogs. It's like sorry, guys, there are still people in this world who do not see it as healthcare, who do see it as murder.\par \par \uc2 By the way, how about that gender diversity thing the Supreme Court that okayed Roe v. Wade, how many guys -- was it just all men, right? And this now, how many women? Three women on the Supreme Court? There is your diversity.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dana, that is one of the arguments that it kind of opens the door for a full ban across the country. Not necessarily to argue the legal premise of that, but it seems like the same people who tell us we don't have enough faculty over our children that are born to, I don't know, decide if they are male or female, and now tell us we have full control over children in a selfish way in some cases to end their life. And so where is the media coming from here? Like --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think that with the clips that you just saw and there's probably going to be a lot more of that, is that it doesn't have any nuance at all. It doesn't have any understanding of the actual facts and how this works and how state legislatures and your government that you elect at your state level will take this on and take it up.\par \par \uc2 And look, Republicans are going to have to answer the questions. Democrats are going to have to make their case, and that's how it is supposed to work. The other thing about these guys is, you know, they really don't like legal leaks, right? Remember the Hunter Biden laptop? That leak was terrible.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That leak was terrible. But illegal leaks like \uc2 IRS\uc2  tax information, the Steele dossier? What happened return to norms? I thought we were going to have all these return to norms? And also why are they so upset about the people's voices being heard? What about the people who believe that abortion is wrong, and since 1973 have lived with the Roe v. Wade decision?\par \par \uc2 And they worked. They made their voices heard. They got people elected that agreed with them and they were able to get a president elected who then was actually able to nominate three justices who have made a decision based on something as we established in the A block, a decision that was flawed from the beginning and now it's finally returned to the states if this decision is real.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Jessica, Dana said that -- you know, what's strong about people's voices being heard? There were some from Planned Parenthood on earlier today with Martha I think. And he said in the same sentence that the overwhelming opinion of Americans supports access to abortion under Roe versus Wade. And then he said that 40 states are pushing legislation to restrict access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Last I thought, state legislatures are representative governments of the people who live in the state. So how can that be true? How is it -- i this just really the media is pushing the narrative they believe in or do they really believe most Americans want abortions?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: They believe that a majority of Americans, 63 percent want some access to abortion and that might be -- there are a lot of people who believe it should only be in the first trimester or people believe it's up to 20 weeks as a very popular number of weeks --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Where is that --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- number from by the way? Do we have --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's a viability number of where the chances of --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Quickening. Modern day quickening that they referred to in the common law where the baby can live.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The 63 percent?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Still has a chance at living.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's obviously not a slam dunk at 20 weeks, but they've had a lot of success with it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I was going to say that the 63 percent number.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The 63 percent come (ph)--.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: More than 63 percent is saying that they want -- they don't want Roe v. Wade to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, and the purpose --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And the follow up question --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It depends on how you ask the question.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly, where that number came from.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: If you're going to cite it, I'd like to know the source.\par \par \uc2 JONES: People can say abortions should be legal --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's Gallup is the source.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- and still support the Mississippi law because it doesn't ban abortion. It puts an advance. It limits it.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: -- at eight weeks. But it limits it extensively. That's not something that is flexible.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But still legal.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It is -- it is still legal, but I want to say --\par \par \uc2 JONES: So now there are 63 percent of people could support the Mississippi law is the point?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: For that (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not 63 percent of people that are supporting a restrictive law and that law will --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I'm using your numbers not mine.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Okay. There are 20 states in the country right now that that have something called trigger laws, which means that if this decision goes into effect, which is expected at the end of June, immediately abortion becomes hugely restricted or outright banned in their states. That includes also going after people who assist in providing abortion that can be, remember in Texas we talked about your uber driver who takes you to an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But that never happened.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That could be your doctor.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No, no, no. There's got to be intent. Don't mislead the audience.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, I am not misleading the audience.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: There's got to be intent in order to --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: That's why Ted Cruz wouldn't even touch that law.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's called mens rea.\par 