d at times, both wings thought that the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  was a vote with them. There was an attempt at one point when he decided to go with the liberal wing, with the -- among the conservatives, to bring him back in to their side.\par \par \uc2 They had this meeting. They called back together to get together and when he showed up and they realized he was not with them, they thought there's nothing else we can do. We've lost him at this point. So there was some back and forth.\par \par \uc2 And that clearly could have happened in this case since this February draft. Like I said, I've been led to believe that this was legitimate as a February draft. A lot could have happened between now and then.\par \par \uc2 I have never as you said seen a leak like this at the court in the years that I've covered it. It is astonishing that somebody would release a draft opinion outside of the court. They are very tightly held. There are almost never any leaks from this one place in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Every place else leaks like a sieve around here. Not the court, and this is shocking actually.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. You know, and, Kayleigh, let me go to the political side of this. If true, it would not surprise me as a conservative and you as somebody that knows the law as well. We have often argued that Roe is bad law. It had -- it enumerated rights to people that were not in the Constitution, and it should be noted very, very clearly that if in fact it were to be true, and again, we're saying this very cautiously, this is on "Politico" tonight, and this is a pretty bombshell report.\par \par \uc2 I did have one person write me and says this is likely true and I had somebody else write me and say it's likely not true.\par \par \uc2 But what it would mean for the people in this country is it doesn't mean abortion would be outlawed or illegal it would be sent back to the states and the state representatives would decide within their state what the law should be, correct?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , "OUTNUMBERED" CO-HOST: That's right. And it would mean that we are no longer one of eight countries, Sean, and yes, you heard me correctly, eight countries in the world that allow elective abortion after 20 weeks. You're exactly right, go back to the states, a state like Texas or a state like Mississippi could you know say after weeks no elective abortions whereas a state like New York or New Jersey could take a different path.\par \par \uc2 It would also mean, Sean, we're not among the good company as Justice Roberts said during oral arguments of the people's Republic of China and North Korea, because right now, that is exactly where the United States stands with our abortion laws. We know 62 million children, 62 million, have perished because of this law. That's nearly one fifth of the United States population.\par \par \uc2 And you're exactly right to note that from the beginning, Sean, Roe was wrongly decided. Even Laurence Tribe, a liberal jurist, said that this is a verbal smokescreen -- that's what he called Roe -- the substantive judgment of which was nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , she thought that it was better left to the state. She was a critic of Roe. Though she was a proponent of abortion rights, generally, she thought the Democratic process was the way at least she made one or two comments intimating that.\par \par \uc2 This was bad law. This would put us on the side of life would allow states like Mississippi and Texas to decide one way, states like New Jersey and New York to decide another.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know put me down as very skeptical, Shannon, only because I've been doing this a long time. I have never seen anything like this ever leaked from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , not a single time.\par \par \uc2 The way it reads -- at points, it reads like it might be a partly an opinion, at other points, I read it and I'm like, this doesn't read like a Supreme Court decision to me. And you know, having covered the court as extensively as you have, I'm curious if you feel the same way. I really -- if I had to say what the odds are, I would say I'm leaning heavily against.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I do think we have to be as you've told your viewers very cautious about this. Again, I have not been waived off of this as being a legitimate draft at some point. Who knows how much? If it is legit, it may have changed in weeks since it was written. There are flourishes and there are expressions and the way that this is written that sounds like Justice Alito to me, and some of his other writings. So I think we have to take it with a grain of salt.\par \par \uc2 And again, until it comes out officially from the court, this means nothing. Votes could change and even if this is a legitimate draft, votes could have changed and this could be flipped the other way, and we just don't know but there are things in the writing that sound Alito-esque to me, not that somebody couldn't, you know, draft that of their own accord. But again, I can lead to believe that this was legit.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me throw this to Kayleigh -- why would "Politico" run the risk of basically -- let me run through the headline that they have here. Supreme Court has voted to overturn abortion rights, draft opinion shows. They're saying it definitively.\par \par \uc2 I have -- again, I've never seen that in my life and I'm skeptical.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Well, sometimes the headline overstates what is actually in the rest of the article I think this may be the case. To Shannon's point, you know, Shannon says she has not been waived off the fact that this was a draft from February, which I think is absolutely the case if they obtain this draft. That's more than pages. It likely was legitimate draft.\par \par \uc2 Whether that changes, we won't know, but I think it's too juicy of a story not to run with. I mean, how consequential this reporting is, Sean. If true, never in the history of the Supreme Court in modern history to have an opinion like this leak. I mean, it makes you wonder how it leaked.\par \par \uc2 Was it a clerk and maybe disagreed with the opinion and took this unprecedented step to put public pressure on justices as they're still making their decisions? I don't know, but someone it would seem to me with a motive clearly leak this in a way that is very unusual for the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Bream, we'll be watching you tonight. And, Kayleigh, as always, we'll be watching you tomorrow. So thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here with more on the breaking developments from the Supreme Court, Harvard law professor \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2 , along with FOX News legal analyst, Gregg Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 Gregg, we'll begin with you tonight.\par \par \uc2 I want to stay a little bit focused on -- we've not confirmed this. This is a political report. The writing -- what you think of it, what you read into it? Drafts are sent around many, many times in the process of forming the final opinion before it's released, usually in June. And I guess my main question to you, Gregg, is does this seem legitimate to you, does this sound Alito-esque to you? Do you believe that this is likely where the decision goes? And what would be the ramifications for people because I know it will be demagogued? And meanwhile it only would mean that the states would decide not the federal government.\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it is a rough draft. So it is not as let's say elegant in its prose as the final draft would be. And I'm skeptical too along with you. For example, let me just read one portion of this alleged draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak, the decision has had damaging circumstances or consequences. Far from bringing about a national settlement of abortion issue, Roe and Casey have inflamed debate and deepened division.\par \par \uc2 I must tell you that sounds very much like a politician wrote it. Not that I disagree with it. In fact I have long argued those very sentences that Roe was wrongly decided, inventing out of thin air this right to privacy and abortion. It was wrong then, it's been wrong over the last years and I think it's still wrong.\par \par \uc2 And I do suspect that the Supreme Court may in the end have enough votes to overturn Roe versus Wade. But right now, I'm in the skeptical category. Votes can change as the drafts go around. People have further conferences privately.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes minds are changed. There have been some very famous cases in which powerful Supreme Court justices have bent the arms of their colleagues on the high court and managed to shift completely decisions that went one way and eventually turned the other way.\par \par \uc2 So I'd be very cautious and skeptical. I think you're right, Sean, you know, this doesn't seem to me -- that portion I read doesn't seem to be written by an eloquent Supreme Court justice. Maybe it's a clerk.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. Professor, I'm really curious your thoughts on this because we might actually disagree. I'm not sure where you stand on Roe v. Wade, whether you think it's good law or bad law. Constitutionalist conservatives have argued it's bad law that this is a right not enumerated in the Constitution and that it actually is the role of states to make their own laws here and it wasn't the right of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious on your position?\par \par \uc2 ALAN DERSHOWITZ\uc2 , HARVARD LAW PROFESSOR: Well, I strongly oppose overruling Roe versus Wade after 50 years. But I think it will be overruled. I am less skeptical than you are.\par \par \uc2 I know Justice Alito. I know his writings. This sounds like a decision, a majority decision of the Supreme Court over ruling Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have a theory and it's only a theory. I think this was leaked by a liberal law clerk who is trying to change the outcome of the case, either by putting pressure on some of the justices to change their mind or by getting Congress to pack the court even before June, which is very unlikely or to get Congress to pass a national right to abortion law, which would apply to all the states and that would have to come to the Supreme Court to see whether that could be upheld under the Commerce Clause.\par \par \uc2 But I think this is real and I think that my theory is that it was leaked by somebody who wants to change the outcome. Look, I've been watching the Supreme Court for 55 years, and this has all the hallmarks of reality and it does not have the hallmark of a decision that's likely to be changed. Maybe justice -- Chief Justice Roberts will go with the minority, but I think they seem to have five votes at this point to overrule Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Look, it was always --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me interrupt you for a second. Let -- let's stay with your theory uh because it's a fascinating one. In all the time that you've been a constitutional professor at Harvard, I may add and you have taught some of the best and brightest people, do you -- I've never -- I can't think of an instance, not one, that a leak like this ever occurred. Can you?\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: Never has. It never has and it could only be done if somebody thinks this is an act of civil disobedience, an act which might get him disbarred, fired from the Supreme Court or her, if it's a law clerk. And yet, they want to go to the mat because they think there's one chance to try to preserve a Roe versus Wade, and that's by leaking this decision to "Politico". They didn't leak it to Fox. They leaked it to "Politico".\par \par \uc2 And so, it fits together. I -- it's just a theory. I have no information to support it, but it seems to me the most plausible theory as to who leaked it, why it was leaked, and how this does sound like it's extremely realistic.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Gregg, if in fact somebody did leak it, what would the consequences be?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Oh, disbarment. You know, I -- the professor has more courage than I do. I was thinking the same thing. This was leaked for a purpose, but I hesitated to say it because it is so serious, such an egregious violation of the solemnity and the confidentiality of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  proceedings.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- this is a terrible, terrible breach but the only explanation I can offer is consistent with the professor, that this is someone who is angry or upset at the way this is moving to eliminate Roe versus Wade as the law of the land and has decided that they're going to try to gin up opposition to it publicly in order to put pressure on the Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that will -- it might work with someone like \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , who may be on the fence here, according to "Politico". It hasn't yet committed to which side he'll be on.\par \par \uc2 But as for the other, you know, solid five justices, you know, public pressure will have the opposite effect. It will only steel their resolve to remain consistent, with fidelity to their initiation decision, if indeed this is real.\par \par \uc2 And again, I still remain skeptical. Maybe that's the journalist in me.\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: You may be right, but there's some history here.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember the switch in time that saved nine? Back during the Roosevelt time when there was the threat to pack the court, the court shifted its approach and upheld New Deal legislation in order to avoid being packed. And I could imagine that there could be that kind of pressure on a justice, not so much fear of anything, recriminations or anything, or political consequences but that they don't want court packing.\par \par \uc2 Nobody on that court wants court packing and I think the fear -- I do think that tomorrow we're going to see people proposing legislation to back the court. I think that's going to be the immediate impact of this leak.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I got to be honest because, Professor, you make one hell of a compelling argument. You really do and I think a very viable one and I think there is a great chance you might be right on the money on this. We all don't know -- we've all given our caveats.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: -- to let my judgments interfere with how I predict the outcome or analyze a case. I want Roe versus Wade to be upheld, but I am telling you this --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We understand. You were very clear. You're clear. We're losing your picture, Professor, thank you. Thank you, Gregg.\par \par \uc2 Here on the phone now, on the latest breaking news about Roe v. Wade is Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, I want to go back to this one more time and what they're saying in this political article is that the Supreme Court has voted to overturn abortion rights, according to a draft opinion, Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak. It's the decision has had damaging consequences, and far from bringing about a national settlement on the abortion issue, Roe and Casey have inflamed debate and deepened division. We hold that Roe and Casey must be overruled. It's time to heed the Constitution, return the issue of abortion to the people's elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 There's been a lot -- I have a lot of skepticism,, although I do find Professor Dershowitz's theory very fascinating, interesting plausible, all the above. So I really don't know what to tell this audience because I don't think anybody does know. What's your reaction to the "Politico" article?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO) (via telephone): My reaction is that the draft, Sean, reads a lot like Justice Alito. I clerked at that court. I've litigated at that court. I'm reading this leaked opinion tonight, and I think it's legitimate.\par \par \uc2 This to me in it -- there are details in this that would be very hard to know details in the reporting about when the justices voted about, how they voted at conference, it's pretty hard to make that up out of whole cloth and there's there are all kinds of indicators here that it sounds like somebody who really does know. And I can tell you, there's a small universe of people who would know the details that are in that report. Basically, we're talking about a justice or clerks or maybe, maybe an employee, you know, someone who works in one of the justices' offices.\par \par \uc2 But it's not very big, Sean, and I have to tell you, it sounds real to me and I think this is plainly an attempt by the left to try and change the outcome in this case and corrupt the process. And the court must not allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Senator, this has never happened in the history of the court, certainly not in my lifetime, Professor Dershowitz can't remember a single instance. What does that mean if somebody would leak what is only a draft it's not a completed document to potentially alter the outcome of a Supreme Court case? Well -- I've got to imagine, laws might apply there for the person responsible, no?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, certainly, the ethics rules for lawyers -- if this was a lawyer who did this a clerk, or anyone who practices law, who is currently member of the bar, they should be disbarred. And I have to say, I think the chief justice tomorrow morning is going to have a lot of work to do on this court.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning, the chief justice of the United States is going to have to convene the justices, he's going to have to convene the clerks, and all employees of the court and he's going to have to say that the leaker needs to come forward, and this is very, very serious that is an unprecedented breach of the court's confidentiality. And it is plainly meant to corrupt the process within the court.\par \par \uc2 It's an assault on the court, Sean, and it's got to be taken seriously and the chief justice is now going to be the one who has to do this inside the court.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let's ask you, Senator, one last question. As conservatives, as constitutionalists, we believe many -- and I've argued for years that this is bad law, an enumerated right not in the Constitution. And according to part of the draft, it addresses that question by saying the Constitution does not prohibit the citizens of each state from regulating or prohibiting abortion. Roe and Casey abrogated that the authority.\par \par \uc2 We now overrule those decisions, return that authority to the people and their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 Isn't that the art argument conservatives have made for a long time because I would anticipate if this were the final decision, that this would be an issue that would be demagogued to death, and we'd hear things like back alley abortions and coat hangers, you know, things, for example, that the late Senator \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  tried to say about Justice Bork, which were not true about him at the time.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, what this draft opinion says is that this is an issue for the voters. And that's exactly, right, Sean. The Constitution is silent on this issue. That means that it is up to the people to decide in the states. And it's really an issue that should be debated in the democratic process in the states, and that's where this opinion if indeed this becomes the majority opinion, that's where it would leave it.\par \par \uc2 And that is exactly right under the Constitution. The people should get to decide, not nine justices wearing robes who aren't accountable to anybody.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. We really appreciate it. Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , thank you for joining us last minute.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here with more, author of the big new book "Suppression, Deception, Snobbery and Bias: Why the Press Gets So Much Wrong and Just Doesn't Care," Ari Fleischer is with us, along with former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , and FOX News contributor Joe Concha.\par \par \uc2 Well, I know we got a lot going on with this story breaking here and you guys are free to talk about it. One of the things that America is dealing with, especially with what we call the ministry of truth issue, which seems to be a big part of the topic of your book, Ari Fleischer, your new book that's coming out soon. People can get a first edition copy at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or Hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is the media gets it wrong constantly. They get it wrong on purpose, they get it wrong because they have an agenda. They get it wrong the very things they accuse talk show hosts members of the press that do straight news investigative reporting and say they give opinion, they do themselves, don't they?\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, they did, particularly during the Trump era when they made the decision that their job as objective reporters was to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that the American people erred by electing him and he was a threat to the republic so they thought their job was to fix what the American people did instead of leaving it to us.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think in this instance so, Sean, I don't put this leak from the Supreme Court in that category. What really troubles me, not about the ruling itself, if that's the ruling itself, I accept and respect that ruling -- but the leaker.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake, Sean, this is an insurrection against the Supreme Court. I've already seen people on the left celebrating this leaker, calling him brave, trying to throw a Hail Mary to stop the ruling from being issued. Who at the Supreme Court will trust each other now if they know that their drafts are going to be leaked, just like everything else in Washington? The Supreme Court seemed to be the last institution standing that had internal integrity.\par \par \uc2 This is an insurrection against the court and it needs to be found who did it, and whatever legal means can be taken against them needs to be taken.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's a powerful statement.\par \par \uc2 Governor, I'd like your reaction to that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, I can't wait to read Ari's book. I know it's going to be amazingly good. But I am so glad that Ari used the term insurrection. That's exactly what it is.\par \par \uc2 But let's take it one step further, this is an insurrection not by some guy from some state who got hot under the collar and went to D.C. and got overheated at a rally. This is insurrection by a person who is paid for by the taxpayers and who has a duty under his particular job and employment to keep his mouth shut and he didn't do it.\par \par \uc2 So it's an egregious form, if you will, of insurrection, and I hope that everyone will use that term because if what happened January 6 was an insurrection, this absolutely is an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, that's a powerful statement.\par \par \uc2 Joe Concha, you cover the media. You understand the media as well like Ari and it's interesting that this came out of "Politico". What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It tells me that there are no accidents and there are no consequences. They went to "Politico" obviously for a reason here and documents and drafts like this, Sean, and guys are leaked because -- and Greg pointed out before and Professor Dershowitz and Ari and the governor, this was leaked to set off DefCon-1 among liberals, that this could happen and you better start mobilizing if you hope to stop it.\par \par \uc2 So, for this to come out now in an election year no less, this will also mobilize liberal voters that may have thought, I'm not going to go to the polls. I'm going to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, but, Joe, hang on. We knew this decision --\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: This will mobilize them, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We knew this decision was coming one way or the other, but I think it would mobilize both sides, wouldn't it? Both bases, those that have very strong opinions. Latest polls that I've been seeing on this show, America still is fairly split down the middle on the issue, are they not?\par \par \uc2 And again this would not end or make abortion illegal, it would return that decision to the states and let the people and their elected representatives decide. I think that's a very important distinction they have to make out, right, Joe? I'll let you finish.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Absolutely. And yeah, yeah, and Gallup, I looked at this not too long ago. And it's something like, 46-46 right now as far as pro-life and pro-choice in this country. You may think when you listen to many in the media that we are an overwhelmingly pro-choice country, but that number has stayed incredibly stubborn and static over many, many years as far as pro- life versus pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 But yeah, to your point, yeah, that this was going to happen before the election. For this to come out now though, it's very interesting. And \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  Blog, which is an independent source in terms of covering the Supreme Court, they said that this will be devastating in terms of within the Supreme Court. No one's going to trust each other and it's just something that is a complete dereliction of duty whoever did this, to Gregg Jarrett's point, will be disbarred and probably worse once that person is discovered, if he or she is discovered, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Governor, I saw you shaking your head. Go ahead, weigh in on your comment.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: I think we need to remind everybody that the liberals have used the big lie. We've heard that term before apply to conservatives. Here's the big lie -- the overturning of Roe v. Wade does not end abortion. That's what they have said and it's a big lie.\par \par \uc2 It simply takes it out of the hands of nine people wearing robes appointed for life and it puts it in the hands of the American people through their elected representatives, where it should have been all along. Some states will have more abortions, some states like my home state won't have any. That's what it will do.\par \par \uc2 And it's unfair to say it ends abortion. I wish it did, but it won't. It simply gives it back to the people.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You don't believe the state of Arkansas would have any exceptions at all?\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: We passed a constitutional amendment in 1986 that made life from conception as part of our Constitution. So the reason that we have waited is to see a court ruling from the Supreme Court that gave us the capacity to fulfill that. So that's exactly what it would be.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, I'll give you the last word on this, Ari Fleischer.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Yeah, I think it was wrongly decided. I don't see what in the Constitution created this so-called right to privacy that extends to the right to have an abortion. Like everything, these are matters that belong to the people and therefore to your state representatives. That's where health care, these type of issues are regularly settled.\par \par \uc2 And that's the healthy way republics breathe. That's the way we solve our disputes and we arrive at solutions. It never should have been decided by the Supreme Court in the first place.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And I think that's the real message, I know it will get demagogued to death and we will hear probably a lot of echoing the themes of the late Senator \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  against Robert Bork, the lies that he told about Robert Bork in the days ahead, especially if this decision does come down this way.\par \par \uc2 Ari, looking for to your book. Governor, good to see you, Joe, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 You went to West Point. You graduated one or two in your class, I don't remember, I forget, forgive me, I still love you. But you're also an attorney and some people might not know that. So add that to your list of incredible credentials and secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 I'd love to get your thoughts generally on, number one, the report, do you take it seriously? Do you think does it seem legitimate to you? And what it would mean if in fact this were the final decision.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Sean, I've read about the first two thirds of it now. It reads like a writing of a first draft from someone who believes deeply in the United States Constitution in the very way that I know Justice Alito does.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm going to give you a short odds that it's likely his writing from a first draft. It doesn't mean that's how it ends.\par \par \uc2 Second, a decision that reflected largely what is in that draft would be a glorious thing for the United States of America. It would in the first instance be a return to constitutional judging, right? We have a series of justices returning to our foundational roots, wiping off the book something that even liberal justices understood made no real sense. They had to defend it because of their own views on abortion.\par \par \uc2 But this is a good piece of judging, a good piece of constitutional work if it ends the way that this draft appears to be heading.\par \par \uc2 Second, the states will get to sort this out. That's the right place for this to be sorted out but I am also optimistic that when these rights are returned to people all across America, that we will save lives, save lives of the unborn all across this country and that would make a decision along the lines of what's in this potential draft a really glorious thing for the United States. Good constitutional law and protecting unborn children, a really good outcome, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay, and the thing is it would go back to the states. I doubt very much, Mr. Secretary, that New York, California, Oregon, Washington state, Illinois, are going to change the law in any way, shape, manner or form.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we now have states -- remember the infamous comments of Governor Northam. Well, first, the baby will be born and then we'll give the baby care and then we'll let the mother talk to the doctor and then they decide whether the baby lives or dies. At that point, that's called infanticide.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Oh, that's certainly true, Sean. There'll be states that have already put themselves in the business of doing precisely what Governor Northam described. They're not likely to change in the short run.\par \par \uc2 I'm convinced over time when people all across America have the opportunity to see the good that can come from changing that that. Maybe not New York, maybe not California, but a state that's now got Governor Youngkin, a state like Virginia, and some of the states that are much closer calls. Overall, I think this will have a really net positive effect on America's capacity, not only to have our right to privacy to find in the right way but also protect more children.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How do you respond to Professor Dershowitz's theory? And he said it's only a theory that this was leaked on purpose to instigate a packing of the court, which we know the left in this country wants to do badly?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I think it's a really -- a really well-thought out theory. I was a student of Professor Dershowitz a handful of years ago. I learned at his feet, and we didn't always agree on everything certainly politically, but it almost certainly seems like this was a decision by someone inside that court to undermine the integrity of that court, with an effort to substantively change how justices made their decision. I think that will fail, but there's no doubt that was likely the intent of the person who leaked this if in fact there was a leak from this court.\par \par \uc2 I hope that Justice Roberts gets his hands on the individual or individuals who worked on this and makes -- makes it very clear this is unacceptable and they pay a real price.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Times have changed certainly in Washington, the issue of privacy interestingly, I don't -- I would argue doesn't exist in America, especially for conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, always good to have you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Now with the very latest from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is "FOX News @ Night" host, our own Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, I'm sensing that maybe you talked to a few people since you were just on. What'd you learn?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. I am increasingly convinced that at some point, this was a draft that was circulated within the court. Again, what eventually ends up being the voted on, signed off on, released from the court official opinion could be wildly different than what I'm holding in my hands right now. But at some point, this was being circulated at the court for consideration.\par \par \uc2 And as we've talked about, this is what justices do. They trade this stuff back and forth. They modify opinions they try to gain votes get more people to sign on. So my gut says and what I'm being told is that this was at some point a legit draft that circulated at the court.\par \par \uc2 Now, here's the thing, if this is still in process, they're still writing concurrences, dissents, people are making decisions about their votes and where they're going to land. This could still take a while. But now, the chief justice has a real problem on his hands because this is floating around out there. Who knows what the final opinion is going to look like? He is never going to rush something especially as important as this case which could be potentially landmark.\par \par \uc2 But he's going to have to get something done. He cares very much about the integrity of the of the court, I can only imagine how furious he is about this because you'll hear him repeatedly when he does speak publicly about the institution of the court, how important it is for him especially as the chief to protect the court. So I got to imagine at this point he is not happy about this at all and will very much do what a number of your guests have suggested, pare it down, put the pressure on, figure out where this came from.\par \par \uc2 Somebody had a motivation. People will have their theories about whether it was on the left or the right. Somebody had a motivation to do this and he is most assuredly going to expend whatever time and energy he has to, to get to the bottom of that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It's interesting because we both acknowledged earlier the disclaimer that you had -- you had been our reporter at the Supreme Court for many years and you never saw this type of leak ever before. I can't think of an instance. \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  couldn't think of an instance, and then he put forth his theory, that this might have been done by an activist, A, to change the decision, or to begin the process of proposing and following through on the idea of packing the court, which we now know we've known for some time Democrats want to do. Any thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Listen, there are very few people who would have access to something like this.\par \par \uc2 No clerk in their right mind would leak it certainly without the knowledge or consent in some way of the justice they serve. It would be almost unimaginable because they wouldn't be employable. They would probably lose their bar license and I can't imagine they would ever be able to find a job again.\par \par \uc2 I can't believe a justice would do this. They have such great honor and integrity and commitment to the court. So that leaves very few options. The chief is going to spend time getting down to that, but again --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're suggesting that --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- to damage the --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, you're suggesting that a clerk would never do this without the blessing of the Justice? That that would take it to another level, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I have to wonder.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, right, it would, but I have to wonder because you -- if you decided to do this on your own as a clerk, you know you're blowing up your legal career because it's almost impossible that somebody will not get tagged with this at some point. And I have to wonder whether you would do that on your own. I mean your legal career is over.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you can go do other things. There are the things you can do. I used to be a lawyer, you can go to other things, but there's just so much speculation we have at this point about why you would ever dare to do something this dangerous, and it really chips away at the integrity of the court which is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But assuming that somebody did this --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- Washington institutions --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If we were to assume that this is true and I have the "Politico" report here, I'll put on camera. Assuming that it's true and somebody did do that, I can't imagine "Politico" would ever give up their sources.\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm -- I'm a talk show host, I do straight news, I do investigative reporting, I do opinion, I do culture, I do sports, I'm like a whole newspaper although people don't really seem to understand what my job is which cracks me up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 I say I'm a conservative. I give my opinion. I tell you who I support politically and I'm very, very outspoken about it.\par \par \uc2 But in this case, I would never give up a source. You'd never give up a source. I can't imagine "Politico" would ever give up their source. Do you?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And I bet they had to have more than one source before going with it, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. I agree with you, I don't think "Politico" would be the place we would get the name. But what happens is there is that very tiny universe of people who would have had if this turned out to be a real draft and we've been led to believe it is, there be a very small universe of people who have access to that and I wouldn't put it past the chief justice to sit down office by office, clerk by clerk, justice by justice and say, we're going to get an answer to this and I'm not going to let this go. This is not going to be an unresolved investigation. We are going to get an answer and a name.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the very integrity of the court is at stake and that is one of the top things that he cares about and he's talked about publicly and privately since he took on that mantle of chief justice.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's been one of my criticisms of the chief justice. I feel at times he's too political, you know, how will the Roberts court be viewed through the prism of history? And I think that might have weighed, I believe it's only my theory, that that might have impacted his decision on Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 But, Shannon, we'll be watching you tonight. As always, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We kind of put you backwards back to your old job, then you go back to your new job.\par \par \uc2 All right. Here with more analysis, former Florida Attorney General Pam Bondi, along with FOX News contributor Leo 2.0 Terrell, both attorneys in their own right.\par \par \uc2 First your thoughts, Pam Bondi, do you believe this is real? Do you believe possibly that there might be some political motives behind it? Maybe one of which as Professor Dershowitz was saying could be the idea of pushing forward the idea of court packing. Do you do you think this is real, first of all?\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI (R), FORMER FLORIDA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Sean, I do think it's real because it's "Politico". I've never seen "Politico" -- I can't believe that "Politico" would ever print something so important without checking their sources. They know who the leak is and the leak is credible.\par \par \uc2 And to your second point, I firmly agree with what Professor Dershowitz said. I believe this is the liberals. I believe this is someone as Shannon said who's risking their career, risking their career because of this opinion.\par \par \uc2 I think it's a draft. It's clearly a draft. It's not finalized, but hopefully it is true. You know, there is no constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 This should go back to the states. That's where it always should have been. And so I think this is a liberal who is against this and they are throwing away their entire career to leak this opinion. And they will be able to find out who it is.\par \par \uc2 I've been trying quickly to look through the federal statute and see if I can find any crime that would be attached to it and I can't. So far, there may be but at that level of practicing law as a Supreme Court law clerk, it's unbelievable that someone is throwing their career out the window.\par \par \uc2 And as Shannon said, they will be disbarred and I believe Justice Roberts and the rest of the justices will find out who it is. It's so easy to track things now especially that opinion was not out there publicly. It was very closely held.\par \par \uc2 And someone got it and someone leaked it and they will be held accountable. And the only thing I don't agree with, I think the final opinion may come out sooner rather than later because of this, because this was leaked I believe to pressure the conservative justices to scare them into not issuing this opinion.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And remember, this would go back to the states. It would not outlaw abortion, although I guarantee you that that demagoguery is next to follow. That's very predictable.\par \par \uc2 Leo, you've been a law -- civil rights lawyer for most of your career. Now, you're a TV star which took you away from your law practice, but in all seriousness, let's put your legal hat on tonight, the Leo 2.0 legal hat. Get your analysis of this. Does it seem real to you?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, let me tell you right now, 30 years of practicing civil rights law, constitutional law. So I was shocked when I heard it. Does -- is it real? I think it is very real.\par \par \uc2 Do I think there's a motivation behind it? Yes, I think it's political motivation. I think this is the type of draft to get the left fired up, to change the current narrative, to focus on women and say look what's going to happen. So I think there's a political motivation, not a court packing but to change the disastrous liberal agenda completely.\par \par \uc2 I also don't rule out the fact that somebody in the higher up allowed this to happen. They got in -- you know, this, you know, we want to believe that the Supreme Court is the last bastion of integrity. They're political animals, especially on the left.\par \par \uc2 And so I don't rule it out that they that leak occurred with the blessing of someone. I'm not going to guess who, but I think there's the blessing of someone because the Supreme Court, like it or not, is a political branch of government and this is an unfortunate, unfortunate situation. Pam, the criminal statute --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Leo, if in fact that would that turns out to be true and somebody maybe a clerk on the left, that slants solidly to the left, wouldn't you imagine that that person will be glorified and held up as a hero being, you know, you're heroic?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And it will then, of course, reignite this ridiculous debate about court packings. So I think that could be a very real political motivation.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Well -- as long as, as long as the current tenure of the -- the current Senate leadership at 50/50, he got Joe Manchin, I'm not worried about court packing. I worry about trying to change the narrative of the upcoming midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, that leaker will be glorified by the left, but he'll get employment. He may not be a lawyer he or she may not be a lawyer because they'll be disbarred but they'll get employment from the left. But I just find this totally motivated to change the narrative right now, that doesn't look good for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I mentioned earlier the late Senator \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  demagoguing Robert Bork during his nomination. You know, Robert Bork's America is going to be in America where back alley abortions take place, we can go back.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't that long ago when the coat hanger analogies were used, left and right, Pam Bondi, in spite of the fact that we all know that if this decision comes down this way, that the law would then be made by the states, by the people and their elected representatives if in fact that language is accurate, which by the way would be constitutional which is why conservatives have argued for years that this is bad law and it's a right not -- that given, that it was not enumerated into the Constitution. So -- but it would be demagogued I believe Leo is right on that point.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Of course, it would, Sean. But the law is the law and if this opinion is accurate, true, which I think it is, it will go back to the states which is where it's always belonged. We know that. That was bad law from the beginning, and if you want to live in Florida, South Carolina, Georgia, Texas, where it's going to be outlawed especially late-term abortions, then don't live in our states.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, let me go --\par \par \uc2 BONDI: You can go to California, New York, New Jersey, the liberal states --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have a few minutes left.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: -- where they will have the option. It's not an outlaw overall of an abortion ban but it's headed in the right direction for the states to be handling it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We have about 30 seconds left how many states would likely ban all abortions under all circumstances? I would say, not many if any, Leo? Real quick.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I was saying, if I was making -- if I was to give an estimate, anywhere between zero to three, ban all, zero to three.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And, Pam, what's your -- what's your estimate?\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Same. I would say three, but again, remember, Sean, this is dealing with late-term abortions as well. So I think it's going to make a tremendous difference in the pro-life movement, assuming this opinion gets issued legally.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, we got to -- we'll have more tomorrow. Pam, thank you. Leo, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That's all the time we have left this evening. Please set your DVR. Never miss an episode.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for making the show possible. Let not your heart be troubled. Laura is next. See you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220503ei5200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Expect Destruction Of Trust Within Court; Leak Has Potential To Irreparably Harm Court For Decades To Come; MSNBC's Maddow Ups The Outrage After Court Leak; SCOTUS Leak Threatens Credibility Of Institution; "Politico" Publishes Leaked Story That Supreme Court Will Overturn Roe Versus Wage Decision On Abortion; Pediatrician Interviewed On Controversy Surrounding Gender Affirming Treatment For Children; Protestors Gather At Supreme Court After News Leaked Court Will Overturn Roe Versus Wade Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Jonathan Turley \par \uc2 9,864 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 May 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, FORMER FLORIDA ATTORNEY GENERAL: It's going to make a tremendous difference in the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BONDI: Assuming this opinion gets issued legally.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Well, we'll have more tomorrow. Pam, thank you. Leo, thank you. That's all the time we have left this evening. Please set your DVR. Never miss an episode. Thank you for making the show possible. Let not your heart be troubled. Laura's next. See you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham. And this is a Fox News Alert. Tonight, Politico is reporting on a leaked draft of a Supreme Court opinion in that Mississippi abortion case, and that Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  has been assigned the majority opinion, which according to the draft, means Roe vs. Wade will be overturned.\par \par \uc2 Now, what does this mean? This returns the abortion issue to the states, which pre-Roe is where it always was. So, in other words, some states will allow abortion liberally, some states might ban it, some will find what they believe is a middle ground.\par \par \uc2 But I want to say this very clearly tonight. No matter what you think about this outcome, the leak itself represents a shocking and unprecedented breach of the courts confidentiality, which is sacrosanct. And it's key to the courts ability on any issue, to engage in the give-and-take and all the decision-making that's required to reach these decisions based on legal principles, based on the constitution, not because of political pressure. That's what the legislature does, not the court.\par \par \uc2 And those who clerk at the court, especially the law clerks, they are afforded a tremendous privilege. Think about to work at the court, to learn from the justices. But the inviolable deal that you strike when you accept that clerkship, which is the most coveted thing you can have as a young lawyer, is that you swear confidentiality regarding everything you see and everything you hear, and especially the internal deliberations of the court, especially that. It is not up to a law clerk to decide when the decision of the court will be announced. And it is not up to a law clerk or any employee of the court to leak a decision in what is a naked attempt to try to change the outcome before the final opinion is issued.\par \par \uc2 Now, why am I so passionate about this tonight? Actually, know what I'm talking about. A lot of people on television tonight don't know what they're talking about. I was a Supreme Court law clerk. And I know because I saw opinions go back and forth, drafts of opinions go back and forth. language changes. Sometimes even votes might change. Maybe a justice will see an argument, he or she didn't think of. It's rare, but it does happen. But this is all done confidentially. To encourage this free exchange of ideas. That's always deemed by everyone, doesn't matter what your political background is, doesn't matter. It's deemed essential to the proper functioning of our nation's highest court.\par \par \uc2 Now, Chief Justice Rehnquist, and our orientation session at the court said to all of us, there are nine justices, four clerks for each chambers usually, about 36 and then couple of the retired justices still have clerks that are still at the court. But it's not many, many young people, right? It's about 36, 40 young, young lawyers. He looked at us and he said, if you leak anything from this court, you can kiss your legal career, bye-bye. Done. Yes, we remember that. And through the entirety of the court's history, no matter how controversial or disruptive the court's decisions have been, and I'm talking decades, decades of decisions on freedom of religion, rights of criminals, same sex marriage, none of those draft opinions ever leaked. You know why? Because everyone in that building understood that was a line that could never be crossed.\par \par \uc2 And it is a sad commentary that someone in that building took it upon himself or herself to totally usurp the role of the justices and hijack the courts deliberative process. This was a bold faced attempt to tip the balance of the vote one way or another that we know which way. The leaker undoubtedly hopes, right, that this leaked opinion will drive activists to the court's doors, where they are desperately banging on the doors during \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's confirmation hearing. And they think that's going to have an impact, all the activists outside screaming and all people with all the colorful signs, that's going to have some impact. That will have one of the justices in the majority change his or her vote.\par \par \uc2 But once again, it looks like the Left will just smash an established government process to get their way, do anything in their power to stop a principled conservative majority from issuing a ruling on a pending case. This leaker is going to be sorely disappointed and should be immediately discovered and disbarred.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Jonathan Turley, \uc2 GW University\uc2  Law Professor, Fox News Contributor and Harmeet Dillon, Civil Rights Attorney and CEO for the Center for American Liberty. We also hope to be joined by former DOJ lawyer James Trusty in just moments. Professor Turley, I am - this is one of those things I thought I would never see in my life. I've seen a lot in my time in Washington, I've been here way too long.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is one of the most destructive things that I can ever imagine to happen to our legal system, take it away.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, we're all in disbelief. I've covered the court for years, and I teach a class on the Supreme Court. And I really didn't think this day would come and that's a strange thing to say, in a city that literally floats on leaks. The only island in that sea that did not have leaks was the Supreme Court. And it existed on a level of integrity that is all too foreign today in politics. And to see people celebrating today, this leak is perhaps the most disturbing thing of all, it just seems that there's no ethical rule or institutional value that can withstand this age of rage.\par \par \uc2 What happened with this leak, caused lasting damage to the Supreme Court - this, I think that the court will never be quite the same. Because it has always existed, as you said, Laura, on an understanding of people of good faith that believed in that institution, believe truly in each other, even though they disagreed wholeheartedly in opinions. This was a malicious act, and the only reason to leak it was to try to create external pressure to change the position of the court and maybe encourage Congress to pass this federal abortion law to try to federalize the abortion area.\par \par \uc2 What's really quite moronic about this, if at anything, the justices are more likely to dig in. The second greatest blow to the court would not just be - after the leak itself, would be if the justices did cave to external pressure. So, this has turned everything into a morass. And what this person did, cheap into the institution to a degree that is breathtaking. I don't know why I - maybe it was the last area of naivete that I have left after years in Washington. But I truly never thought we'd come to this moment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Harmeet, we've been hearing now for the last year plus from Democrats that they exist merely to really at this point to safeguard democracy. Right. The other cables have been spinning since January 6th, they're the true guardians of democracy, all the institutions, all the norms are being protected by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Left. What do we see tonight with this having happened?\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, Laura, I mean, there's so many thoughts going through my mind as a lawyer for the pro-life movement, as a constitutional lawyer, and also somebody involved in politics. This was a deeply political act, Laura. And I think it was aimed not only at legislation, like you mentioned at undermining potentially the courts' legitimacy and court packing, but also affecting the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: This is going to be a talking point and advertisements that are being drafted as you and I are speaking tonight, about how it is important to vote for Democrats to save women from back alley abortions, fill in the blank, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But all of that is a lie, Laura. I mean, this is an insurrection level event if you talk about really insurrection against the court. But when you look at what this opinion, if it turns out to be correct, and I agree with Professor Turley that in honor and dignity, the justices are likely to not change their votes, the ones who are honest and stick with this opinion, it returns to the states, a decision that always should have been there.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: And this draft opinion goes through the analysis very well that there never was a consensus in this country about abortion. It doesn't fit in the jurisprudence of the 14th Amendment. When you look at the Mississippi law itself, it provides exceptions for the life of the mother and for significant fetal defects. So, it isn't even an absolute ban after the 15th week. And so, you're going to have a look at what the Democrats have done on the political side in California, you have a bill in front of our assembly that talks about babies dying in a perinatal setting that is up to 28 days after birth.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: That being protected from prosecution. So, they have gotten, Laura, after--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They're fanatics.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Getting the Roe v. Wade more and more--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, they're fanatics.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Extreme. When the rest of the civilized world does not permit any of this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is about abortion on demand. This is utter fanaticism, and it's burned down the court in order to try to get your way, from the same crowd that claims to be the great protectors of our entire democratic system. To Harmeet's point, professor, the author of that political piece that leaked this supposed draft opinion. Again, we don't know if it's draft opinion, people think it is. We don't know.\par \par \uc2 But he spoke to MSNBC tonight. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH GERSTEIN, SENIOR LEGAL AFFAIRS REPORTER, POLITICO: This opinion that he's written, which is just a withering takedown of the Roe v. Wade, precedent. It pulls no punches at all, it's pretty brutal. And in the way it's structured, it kind of needs to be that way, because of the way Alito confronts this issue of a court precedent. Stare decisis is the legal term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A lot of people thought the original Roe opinion was brutal as well, given what happened to 50 plus million babies. But your response to his comment, professor?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, as you know, we - as a draft, it can change. This was a February draft. The votes can change, although whoever did this probably made that less likely. It does have elements of opinion that do sound like Justice Alito, he has spent his entire career questioning the basis for Roe and Casey. He was not alone, by the way. Professor Tribe at Harvard called Roe a smokescreen. The late Justice Ginsburg said that she had reservations about how Roe was written. So, Roe has really been a difficult path for the court. It has been really subjected to a series of five, four decisions often decisions that were quite fractured, nuanced beyond definition for some people.\par \par \uc2 But the court has never been on terra firma. What this court would suggest is that if it is in fact, a legitimate draft that they have a secure five justice majority, it would make sense that a leader would be picked, if Chief Justice Roberts was not initially in the majority, he might be going with a dissent or he - may have decided that he could not sign with the majority.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: But the decision itself has aspects to - of the type of analysis that we've seen from Justice Alito, who does not believe that this right is found in the constitution as opposed to resting with the states.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We have former DOJ Attorney James Trusty with us as well tonight. James, Bernie Sanders tweeted something moments ago saying, Congress must pass legislation that codifies Roe versus Wade as law of the land. And if there aren't 60 votes in the Senate to do it and there are not, we must end the filibuster to pass it with 50 votes. Well, this seems like the goal of the leaker. Jim, I mean, this seems pretty obvious at this point. I mean, they were ready with the politics. The second this thing dropped in Politico was like, it was almost like they had it planned ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Almost like - well, almost like they talked to each other about what's coming. But Laura, you've kind of stolen my thunder and so as Professor Turley. I mean, I've got this - heard this notion that there was a leaked draft opinion. And I didn't really care which way the opinion went, I thought to myself, I can't use all the words that came up at first, but I'll just tell you, this is an outrageous historic moment to have a leak out of the Supreme Court, a trial balloon or a warning shot, whatever you want to characterize it as. And I have two things that leap to mind.\par \par \uc2 One is, what if one of the Associate Justices was involved in a leak? I mean, that magnifies an already historic, horrific moment into something beyond belief. And maybe as a possible way of deflecting from that concept. This Supreme Court needs to speak as one tonight, or tomorrow morning and issue a statement that condemns the living hell out of what just happened. I don't really care.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: James, to your point, I want all of you to chime in here. To me, it seems like the court needs to come out like an hour ago. Immediately, the chief justice should come out and issue a statement. I mean, in the age of computers, you can do that, I understand that you can issue a statement saying, this is unauthorized obvious leak from the court and lay out the points as to why - how this leaker must be found. And there has to be consequences Harmeet for this individual, whoever it was, and we can guess their names floated out there. I'm not going to say any names on television.\par \par \uc2 But there are people who've been connected to the author of the piece, and who are current clerks. Again, I'm not going to get into it, because we have no idea. But they're going to find out who they are, does any of you on television tonight deny that we will find out who leaked this opinion. Any of you think we will not know who leaked this?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: No, we'll find out. We'll find out who did it, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Turley, what do you think? Will they find out?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I'm hopeful that we can. I know that Chief Justice Roberts is going to do everything he can. This is the greatest crisis not just of his career, but of any modern chief justice. I mean, this is the sort of original sin of judicial ethics, it does not get worse. And I think that when you mentioned what is the court doing? I think Chief Justice Roberts has been meeting nonstop, with the marshals and others to see how they're going to find the culprit here.\par \par \uc2 And God have mercy on that soul because the court will have none. This is the worst type of attack you can launch against the integrity of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, James, the chief justice, as most chief justices from Rehnquist, when I clerked to the current chief justice, they're there and they believe that their role is to safeguard the court for future generations. And we heard a lot about the legitimacy of the court after Kavanaugh, after Justice Thomas for whom I clerked, his confirmation, after the KC decision, after Lawrence v. Texas. All these cases, the legitimacy of the court is called - well, what about now?\par \par \uc2 So, the justice - chief justice, it's incumbent upon him to bring every law clerk before him, give me your phones, I'm sorry, or the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , give me your phone, we want all your accounts. We got to - we got to do our own - we have to do our own - look at every device that you've ever used and find out who did this. James, you're the \uc2 FBI\uc2  expert here. Tell me what could be done?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, well, I hope our \uc2 FBI\uc2  is not as politicized as it's been in recent years at the top. But look, I'm going to pose an alternative theory. We're talking about how I mean, there's a logical steps in the exact right, appropriately aggressive steps that the chief justice should take with federal law enforcement. My alternative theory here and this is very jaded, I admit, and it's ground zero here. But what if this person is treated as a heroic whistleblower by the Left.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think it's quite possible that this person is going to be front page of the newspapers, the big hero who tried to put the brakes on changes to Roe versus Wade, and there'll be great celebration, and great rewriting of history about how this was such a necessary moment to stand up against evil. I can see all of that happening, which is thoroughly depressing--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And that's the end of the court, that's the end--\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Institution.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, that's the end of the court - I mean - if that happens, and they're telling me, I've got to go to the next discussion. But, Jonathan, if that happens, if the leaker is celebrated, as I think James and Harmeet alluded to, clerks are never going to be able to have this role at the court that they have now. They're never going to be able to have access to opinions. I don't know what will happen at the court. If that's the case. Jonathan, real quick.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It's not just going to change, won't just change the court. It's going to change who we are as a country that we lionize someone who abandons every principle and value that they swore to uphold. If we've reached that point. Our problems go beyond the court itself.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's an excellent point--\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Well, I think that--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Panel. Yes, Harmeet, go ahead. Real quick.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: I was just going to say, we are at that point, I think that that is absolutely what's going to happen here. It is political. And if the chief justice doesn't come up with a second statement, a statement was already made tonight, just before we went on the air that the court had no comment. Yes. So--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: This is catastrophe for the court.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, this is so much bigger than - I mean, Roe versus Wade is itself a huge decision, obviously, and we'll get to the implications. But this - what happened with this leak, people have to understand, things will never be the same again in our judicial branch unless dramatic actions are taken. And it's got to happen fast. Panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is just the latest escalatory step taken by the Left, as I said to tear down our norms, our institutions, our traditions, and I don't think the voters are on board with what the Left is doing.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Chris Rufo, Manhattan Institute Senior Fellow and Attorney Kurt Schlichter. Chris, now this issue of - everyone - activism is fine, we celebrate activist, doesn't matter what you believe, you're out there - you're having legitimate exchange of ideas, no problem. What does this say to you about the level of desperation on the Left?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RUFO, MANHATTAN INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: Well, we've seen it for the last two years. I mean, we - the Left did a $2 billion in damage burned down entire city blocks after the death of George Floyd in 2020. And it's really been on a steamrolling effect that use any kind of justification, any kind of scapegoating mechanism to justify behavior that is pushing those edges that is going and transgressing those boundaries. This isn't a surprise to me.\par \par \uc2 But I think what really has to happen is that conservatives and especially those justices on the Supreme Court, have to hang tough, have to stick to their principles and cannot back down to this intimidation. We all have to come together, push back and reestablish those boundaries, if we want to keep our country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kurt, earlier tonight, Rachel Maddow seemed to suggest that this leak was worth it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: It is shocking, as you said, and as we started discussing here, it's shocking, both in substance. And it is also shocking in terms of what it means about the court and what it means about the stakes here that someone was willing to do this. It is just a remarkable thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remarkable thing. Imagine if this was a thought to be a conservative law clerk to leak something in a case that the liberals were all thrilled about. Imagine what Rachel and the crowd would be saying tonight, Kurt?\par \par \uc2 KURT SCHLICHTER, ATTORNEY: Oh, I can imagine, Laura, and I think we all know that they'd be rather upset. Look, we've been hearing for over - almost a year and a half about attacks on our democracy and insurrections. This did more damage than a bunch of elderly Trump voters taking selfies in the rotunda. We - the actual decision itself is huge. It's probably the most important decision since Plessy versus Ferguson was overturned by Brown versus Board. And as in that case, the Democrats are very, very upset about it.\par \par \uc2 But I think you're rightly focusing on the procedural aspects, the institutional aspects. And I don't - and frankly, I think there are a lot of former conservative voices we can't count on. I'm waiting for David French's, the conservative case for leaking draft opinions to come out and explain why we're wrong. This is a disaster on every level, the court needs to come together in unanimity against this, and we need to go back to basic principles and basic functions and norms of our institutions. And I'm afraid that's unlikely to happen at this point.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I was thinking about tonight is, Justice Breyer. Now, he's - obviously he's retiring. He's been - he's been a stalwart champion of a more liberal judicial activism on the court. I've had the chance to meet him a few times. He's always nothing, but pleasant and nice anytime, I speak to him, very genteel individual.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what is he thinking tonight? This is the court he's leaving behind, regardless of what he thinks about the outcome. I mean, there are tons of outcomes, Chris, over the last 40 years that conservatives were disgusted by, and I talked and wrote about most of them. But the idea that anyone would celebrate the smashing of the court protocol that goes back 100 years to the start of the court, a modern court. I can't imagine he's happy about this tonight.\par \par \uc2 RUFO: No, he's not. But this is something we've seen on the Left. As you note, for the last 50 years, a hard Left activists, including Marxist, intellectuals, have really run a long march to the institutions. And their theory is very simple. We get our people into the institutions; we change those institutions from within. we violate democratic norms. We go in an anti-democratic process, simply by dominating the bureaucracies. They believe they can do the same thing with the courts, as they've done to universities and other prestige institutions.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, to a certain extent, it's working. That's why conservatives have to adapt. We have to come up with new strategies and tactics to start pushing back on the Left's institutional dominance before they're really taking this democratic shell and then putting whatever they want inside.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. Well, it's become their pattern. And it's by any means necessary. They'll - whatever they have to do, whether it's ripping down a statue or smashing of Andy Ngo in the head or blowing up sex education in school and turning it into something very different. They'll do it and then they'll say, oh, you're an outlier for complaining about it. That's what's happening tonight with this leak at the court. And Kurt this, this 2019 New York Times piece reminded everybody that back in the 80s it was Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who's supposedly a devoted (ph) Catholic voted to let states overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 So that - just that is a pretty good indicator. Is it not Kurt of how far the Democrat Party has shifted to the most radical partial birth abortion, abortion on demand position on this one of many cultural issues?\par \par \uc2 SCHLICHTER: Well, I'm not surprised at all, Laura. They've been going more extreme and more extreme and more 