t is okay, because they're Black kids. We fight against that. We teach our kids that in America, everybody can win. Everybody can reach and achieve the goals that they set forth.\par \par \uc2 And so we are patriots, and I don't think that people like \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  are used to seeing an African-American young boys that are fatherless, that love this country, and understand really what their potential is, and that's what we teach and that's what we show.\par \par \uc2 And so I don't hate \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , I dislike her comments, but I pray for her because she should be on our side. If she really cares that America is the most fatherless nation in the world, the entire world on the planet, America has more fatherless kids, she should be fighting on my side over here.\par \par \uc2 I work and serve these children each and every day. They eat my food at my house, they sleep on my couch. When they need some new shoes, I buy them; when they need new clothes, I buy them; and so for her to come at me like that and to accuse me of child abuse when I've poured my entire life, heart, and soul into going out and serving the most underserved kids in our nation, I have a real problem with that.\par \par \uc2 She is going to have to apologize. I want MSNBC to apologize or otherwise, I'm going to have to pursue my legal strategy that my lawyers are putting together.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I've got to ask, and thank you because that is too much. Of all the things you can be mad about in this world and there are a lot to be mad about, I would say right now, she is mad because little kids without dads are getting help from somebody like -- why do you think that made her so angry?\par \par \uc2 BREWER: Because she's not used to seeing it, it doesn't fit her perspective and the left's perspective in this woke agenda that's taken over this country. The heart and soul of the most voiceless kids in our country right now are young fatherless African-American boys who are locked up to