people crossing. You know what I'm saying? But this is the thing, okay?\par \par \uc2 Performative stupidity like this is why \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is currently polling behind ISIS. Yo, okay. She is getting on TV and pushing the vaccine by telling everybody it didn't work, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 This is like me saying, my wife and I are expecting a baby. Thank God, we wore a condom or she'd be a lot more pregnant than she is right now. Come on, man.\par \par \uc2 And they do this again and again, like: Yo, if you want people to take the vaccine, don't tell them it doesn't always end good, like there's a reason \uc2 Carnival Cruise Lines\uc2  don't show scenes from Titanic during the commercials. Come on. It's stupid.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, you do kind of have to wonder. I mean, I guess they've already cashed out their \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  stocks, so there it doesn't matter them anymore.\par \par \uc2 But -- I mean, how dumb do they think people are? Do they think people believe it? Is this a ritual expression of contempt for the population telling you something they know you don't believe but forcing you to pretend you do? Like what is this?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Well, that's what I would attribute it to. I think at this point, they've become so entrenched in the fact that this has always been their cause that we're not living, Tucker, in something I consider to be the death of shame.\par \par \uc2 Like people will suffer any indignity if it means advancing their cause. So even if they have to get on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and be like, I got COVID, but unvaccinated like again, you're telling us it didn't work.\par \par \uc2 As far as all those tweets, I thought they were hilarious. Okay.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, I loved them.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  globbing on to the narrative. You know, she's doing that because she wants to run again, somewhere in the back of her mind. She'll never stop running.\par \par \uc2 Tucker, someday at Trump rallies, they are going to be chanting help her up. Okay, she will be running when she is 105. And as far as Elizabeth Warren, she's a dope. The only advice I would give there, Tucker, because you're good guy, never buy a used car of Elizabeth Warren. She sold my buddy a red Jeep Cherokee. We ran the Carfax, it turned out to be a white suburban, Tucker. Come on.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Really quick, in 10 seconds, if you were to run a national poll, and people could vote anonymously and you asked who is a bigger threat to you and your family? COVID or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? What do you think the results would be?\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: COVID or Kamala? Well, honestly, I actually think it would be Kamala. This is the first time she'd be doing good in the polls. I say we run it. It'll boost morale in the wing. Come on, man.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That was not a setup. You didn't know that question in advance.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: No.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Jimmy, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FAILLA: Appreciate you, buddy. Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 More news after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, there is so much going on in the world, but some of it for once is good. So take heart and above all, enjoy the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 A brand new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today," our interview with Glenn Greenwald tomorrow morning on FOX Nation, 7:00 AM, absolutely worth it.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow night. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220427ei4q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Many Democrats Call For Title 42 Extension; Texas National Guard Soldier Presumed To Have Drowned While Saving A Drowning Migrant; Kevin McCarthy Says Leaked Recording "False And Wrong"; Florida State Government Removes Special Tax Treatment for Disney World after Company's Criticism of State Law Prohibiting Teaching about Topics of Sexual Orientation and Gender Identity to Kindergarten through Third Grade; Talladega Superspeedway in Alabama hosting Large Racing Events; Some Democrats in Congress Urge Biden Administration Reconsider Lifting Title 42 Restricting Immigration Due to COVID-19 Health Emergency\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Lucas Tomlinson, Bill Melugin, Chad Pergram, Griff  Jenkins, Edward Lawrence, Alicia Acuna, Phil Keating, Madison Alworth, Juan  Williams \par \uc2 7,884 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FOX NEWS HOST: Why don't go into a hot air balloon in Melbourne, Australia. This guy was trying to save his balloon from crashing into homes and light poles and trees. 13 people, no one was injured. But that's why I don't ride hot air balloon.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: That's it for us tonight. We'll see you back here on Monday. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, I'm Gillian Turner in Washington tonight in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking now, a member of the Texas Army National Guard presumed to have drowned after going into a river to save a migrant. That soldier has been missing since this morning. We'll have all the details in a moment in a live report. We're on the ground at that location.\par \par \uc2 But we begin first with mounting concern from Democrats about President Biden's decision now to end COVID inspired asylum rules along the southern border. The White House is maintaining Title 42 provisions will end as planned next month, but now anxious Democrats fearing another illegal immigration surge as midterms approach are applying pressure.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson starts us off tonight from the White House. Hi, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Gillian, it's not just lawmakers sounding the alarm over the repeal of Title 42 ahead of the expected migrant surge this summer, but reportedly the president's own DHS secretary as well.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thanks for welcoming back to Washington State.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice over): President Biden traveled to the Pacific Northwest, but some lawmakers would like to see him go to the southern border ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): This is not good for Democrats in November. You know, in talking to some of my Republican colleagues, they're saying we can't believe the White House has given us this narrative.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Over a dozen Democrats, including many in tight Senate races this fall urging the White House to keep Title 42 in place.\par \par \uc2 A group of bipartisan senators have introduced a bill to delay lifting Title 42 for 60 days. The bipartisan House Problem Solvers Caucus offered its endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DHS says the Trump era public health measure has barred over 1.7 million migrants from entering the United States over the past two years since the height of the pandemic, a frequent critic of the White House says its policies are out of touch.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): The Biden administration's official policy whatever President Biden says is that we should force toddlers to wear masks on airplanes, but we should throw open our southern border to a quarter million illegal aliens every single month.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The Biden administration plans to repeal Title 42 next month.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY, HOMELAND SECURITY: The use of Title 42 will discontinue as of May 23rd. And we are in the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and throughout the government planning accordingly.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: But President Biden's DHS Secretary has reportedly told members of Congress he's concerned about the administration repealing Title 42 according to Axios.\par \par \uc2 It's been more than a year since Biden tapped his vice president to lead the White House effort on the southern border. She has visited once.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And on Title 42?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The president will meet the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  here at the White House on Monday and talk about immigration and Title 42, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Lucas Tomlinson at the White House tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's get an update now on that missing Texas Guardsmen who we are told is presumed drowned this hour after trying to save a migrant at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin is in Eagle Pass tonight with the details as they are developing. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Gillian. Good evening to you. Just a short time ago, I spoke to the local sheriff who gave us an update and as you look at seeing video here, we'll explain the latest as we know it.\par \par \uc2 That sheriff tells me the National Guard soldier disappeared at around 8:30 a.m. this morning after he jumped into the water into the Rio Grande in an effort to save a female migrant who was crossing over with another group of migrants.\par \par \uc2 What we've been told is that female migrant did survive and she is now in Border Patrol custody. However, that male soldier was never seen again. He never resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Texas Military Department put out a statement saying they cannot confirm that he died because they don't have a body yet. Only saying he is missing on a "mission related incident".\par \par \uc2 However, multiple sources are telling us this is now being treated as a presumed drowning of the soldier because he has been gone for more than seven hours and has not resurfaced after jumping into the water this morning. They did find his body armor, his radio and his cell phone near the river's edge. He took it all off and dropped it before he jumped into the water.\par \par \uc2 There had been boat searching all day long but still, that soldier's body has not been found yet in this presumed drowning, more updates as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Another thing we'd like to show you is take a look at this video. We are witnessing more Title 42 removals here at the International Bridge in Eagle Pass today. Watching these groups of migrants being expelled back into Piedras Negras, Mexico.\par \par \uc2 So, we decided let's go into Piedras Negras and talk to some of these migrants about Title 42 which is going to drop on May 23rd. It didn't take long to find these migrants. We've been told thousands of them are waiting for Title 42 to drop. Several of them told us they had already been expelled themselves because they're the Northern Triangle countries and that yes, they are waiting for Title 42 to drop next month.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what two Hondurans had to say that they do plan to cross illegally once that health rule drops, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): On May 23rd, there's another proposal from North America to help other countries, Honduras, El Salvador and Guatemala. The president has taken that measure to help us and support us in this moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): My hope is for now is that I get granted permission to cross with my son on the 23rd. That is my hope right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And, Gillian, back to that breaking news. I can tell you that multiple times this week, at least three times we've seen the bodies of three migrants in the Rio Grande here behind us. The currents can definitely get strong. National Guard soldiers will jump in and try to rescue people.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to that missing soldier, if in fact his body is found and he is confirmed to have died, it would mark the very first Texas National Guard soldier to die in the Rio Grande since Operation Lone Star was first launched in March of last year. More updates as we get them. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Bill, stay close. We'll come back to you if you get any new details on the missing Guardsmen. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy says a voice recording where he appears to tell other Republicans he planned to urge then President Trump to resign is "false and wrong". That audio was allegedly recorded shortly after last year's Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has the details tonight from the Capitol. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Kevin McCarthy may deny it but audio of conversations between McCarthy and other Republicans appears irrefutable. Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  asking McCarthy if former President Trump might quit, and McCarthy partly blaming him for the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): It would be my recommendation, he should resign. What he did is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: In another conversation, McCarthy ditches the former president.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I've had it with this guy.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy initially condemned former President Trump after the riot.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The president bears responsibility for Wednesday's attack on Congress by mob rioters. He should have immediately denounced the mob when he saw what was unfolding.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy and Mr. Trump screamed at each other on the phone during the riot. Yet, weeks later, McCarthy made a pilgrimage to Mar-a-Lago to make amend.\par \par \uc2 DARRELL WEST\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT, BROOKINGS INSTITUTION: That fast pivot on Trump in January is very noteworthy. Is he with Trump? Is he against Trump? How positive is he towards the former president? Those things are going to matter within that GOP caucus.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy aspires to be speaker if Republicans win the majority. At the leak shows the knives are out for McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: What you're dealing with in these calls, in these meetings is a team but very much a team of rivals.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy and the former president spoke late Thursday after the tape materialized. Mr. Trump hasn't trashed talked McCarthy so far.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: He's very accepting of people who were against him, but then turn for him. But what we don't know is when does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  potentially if he does put in the swords?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House Republicans often follow the former president's lead.\par \par \uc2 WEST: If Trump really goes after him, it could create problems for McCarthy's speakership.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McCarthy stumbled to become speaker before. He abruptly dropped a bid to succeed former Speaker \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  in 2015. McCarthy appeared to lack the votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Washington leaks are strategic. Cheney's office denies she leaked the tape. The same with Whip Steve Scalise, a leak could be designed to drive a wedge between Trump and McCarthy. And so far, that has not worked, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thank you, Chad. Georgia Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  says she doesn't remember liking or even posting on social media certain accounts of last year's Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 The hearing was held today in a challenge to her reelection candidacy. Greene herself says she never endorsed violence, but her critics though contend she aided and actually participated in an insurrection to obstruct the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 Greene is suing now, saying the law on which this challenge is based is unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street is worrying about rate hikes, the Dow had its worst day today since 2020 losing 981 points, the S&P 500 fell 122, NASDAQ dropped 335.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow lost almost two percentage points, the S&P 500 fell 2-3/4 quarters. NASDAQ dropped almost four.\par \par \uc2 And Ukraine's security chief says the main battles inside his country are taking place in the eastern industrial heartland and that Russia is now deploying more troops every single day.\par \par \uc2 This comes amid new claims of Russian atrocities near the southern Ukrainian port city of Mariupol, that's where we find Correspondent Griff Jenkins. He joins us actually from Kyiv, the capital tonight. And a warning, some of the images in his report may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Satellite images show what appear to be more than 200 new mass graves in the Ukrainian town of Manhush outside of Mariupol. The city council there says the graves could hold as many as 9,000 civilians. The mayor accuses Russia of trying to hide war crimes being committed on a widespread scale.\par \par \uc2 VADYM BOICHENKO, MAYOR OF MARIUPOL, UKRAINE (through translator): The lives that are still there, they are in the hands of just one person, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Smoke can be seen billowing out over the Azov stole steel plant, where Ukrainian fighters and many civilians are bunkered as Putin orders his troops to seal off the area declaring victory over Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy says Russia has rejected a proposed Easter truce that could have allowed desperate civilians to evacuate.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): This shows very well how the leaders of this state actually treat the Christian faith, but we keep our hope.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Now, Russian forces turn the focus of their attacks on the eastern Donbass region in the southern coastline, leaving the mayor of Mykolaiv of wondering if they're next.\par \par \uc2 ALEX SENKEVICH, MAYOR OF MYKOLAIV, UKRIANE: We are under bombardment almost every day. I ask all the people who still live in the city to leave the city.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Meanwhile, the Pentagon announces another $800 million military aid package that includes 72 howitzers, 144,000 artillery rounds, 72 tactical vehicles to tow howitzers, and more than 121 Ghost drones.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy's chief of staff Andrii Yermak says in an exclusive interview with Fox News, more is needed because the stakes couldn't be higher.\par \par \uc2 ANDRII YERMAK, PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY'S CHIEF OF STAFF: It's not just war in Ukraine, it is the war against democracy. It's the war for the freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Gillian, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s Secretary General wants to give diplomacy another try. He'll travel to Moscow narrowly next week and meet with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  but expectations for a significant breakthrough in peace talks are very low, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Griff Jenkins in Ukraine's capital tonight. Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 You can catch the rest of Griff's exclusive sit down interview with President Zelenskyy's chief of staff right here tomorrow. That's on "FOX NEWS LIVE" at noon Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Up next on this Earth Day, we look at whether President Biden's climate change agenda is actually making the economy worse.\par \par \uc2 But first, Beyond our Borders tonight. A \uc2 Taliban\uc2  official says a bombing at a mosque and religious school in northern Afghanistan has killed at least 33 people. No one immediately claimed responsibility for that bombing.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday, an ISIS affiliate said it was behind a series of attacks targeting Shiite facilities inside Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 French presidential contenders are upping the ante (PH) now with insults on their last day of campaigning before Sunday's run-off. President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  is facing a major challenge from nationalist rival Marine LePen. He accuses LePen of trying to divide France over Islam and she says Macron's plan for pension reform would mean the French would have to work forever.\par \par \uc2 And a nonprofit organization announces more land in the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo will be reserved for endangered Grauer's gorillas, the species has lost an estimated 60 percent of its population in the past two decades. These gorillas are sometimes hunted for food or trapped by snares set in order to catch other animals.\par \par \uc2 These are just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): The \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  is expanding its policies regarding pregnancy. The army says it will enhance paid medical leave for pregnant women as well as pregnancy losses for soldiers and their spouses.\par \par \uc2 This is a major first for the military, which also has duty allowances for birth, adoption, and even long-term of foster care placement.\par \par \uc2 And police in northeastern Spain, say a Russian former top gas executive is the main suspect now in the slaying of his wife and daughter. 55-year-old Sergei Protosenya was found hanged on a patio.\par \par \uc2 Investigators say the killings were not related to any criminal gang act or a similar case this week in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden is touting his climate change and environmental agenda on this Earth Day. But there are growing concerns in Washington, his priorities could be contributing to an economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Ed Lawrence, a Fox Business joins us tonight from 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue with the details. Good evening, Ed.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the president outlining what he's calling a bold climate agenda. He says that this point in time is a climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voice-over): The president today in Seattle signing an executive order that directing more than $2.4 billion that's already been allocated towards preservation and other projects related to forest.\par \par \uc2 The executive order includes a language to advance ecosystem resilience with no specifics on exactly what that is.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): Now, in pushing his climate agenda, the president talked about why many Democrats are not more vocal about the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The people who know better are afraid to act correctly because they know they'll be primary. I've had, I won't mention anyone, I promise to never wouldn't -- I won't but, up to six come to me and say Joe, I want to be with you on such and such, but I can't, I'll be primary. I lose my race -- I lose my race. So, folks, we got to -- this is going to start to change.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So, the president saying that these regulations -- the added regulations, he's doing that because he can. Some Republicans saying his climate agenda kills not only the economy, but actually boosts inflation.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): We know that the massive spending that we've had over the last two years -- two-plus years has added to our inflation woes, and higher taxes will also contribute to that. And all the money that \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is printing --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (on camera): Now, a new SEC report shows that companies will have to had an added cost of $10.2 billion, just because of the new climate regulations that are about to be enacted from this administration, that cost gets passed on to consumers. That's the fear at least.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, back to you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: $10 billion here, $10 billion there, pretty soon and we're talking about real money.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the possibility that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  could make changes to federal abortion law has many lawmakers scrambling to try and ensure the procedure stays legal in their states. One of those is Colorado.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, senior correspondent Alicia Acuna takes a look at what's happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO): Colorado has been and will be a pro-choice state. And what we're seeing in states like Texas and Arizona, in Kentucky, is an enormous government overreach.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Colorado's recent law guaranteeing the right to unrestricted abortion reads in part, "a pregnant individual has a fundamental right to continue a pregnancy and give birth or to have an abortion. A fertilized egg, embryo, or fetus does not have independent or derivative rights.\par \par \uc2 Supporters are relieved.\par \par \uc2 DUSTI GURULE, REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS ADVOCATE: I've cried multiple times. And because I'm just so happy --\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Opponents, devastated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My message to states across the country: Do not follow Colorado's lead. This is bad policy.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The legislative push in Colorado came after Texas passed its law restricting abortion last June.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): The life of every unborn child, who had the heartbeat will be saved --\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Several red states followed like Arizona.\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG DUCEY (R-AZ): I'm pro-life and I'm going to sign pro-life legislation.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: And more recently, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (R-OK): We want Oklahoma to be the most pro-life state in the country.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Blue states like Washington are going to Colorado route. But in the Mountain States region, Colorado stands alone, fast becoming a destination for abortions.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA COHEN, COMPREHENSIVE WOMEN'S HEALTH CENTER: What we're seeing is that on some days, up to 20 percent of our patients are coming from Texas. Look to the horizon and see the tsunami we know that it's coming.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Prompting all this, a looming \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision in the Mississippi case of Dobbs versus Jackson Women's Health Organization, which could impact the future of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 If the Court strikes it down what becomes of abortion law in America?\par \par \uc2 ERIC SONDERMANN, POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, we will become a Patchwork Nation, although another way of referring to patchwork is we become a Federalist nation, which is what we have always been.\par \par \uc2 The state legislatures including what the Colorado legislature just did become all important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is expected to rule in the Mississippi case by the end of June. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): Well, the Seattle area high school football coach lost his job over leading players in prayer will take his argument to the Supreme Court Monday.\par \par \uc2 Joe Kennedy\uc2  contends his First Amendment rights were violated. But School District officials say they had no problem allowing him to pray separately from his students, but that allowing him to pray with them at midfield immediately after games could be seen as a government endorsement of religion.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Republicans versus the Mouse House in Florida. The governor there, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says \uc2 Disney\uc2  needs to take a step back and listen to the people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Ultimately, this state is governed by the best interest of the people, not by anyone but corporations. We're not going to let them take over the state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Police say at least three people were injured in a shooting today in Northwest Washington, D.C. City officials are warning people in that area to stay inside right now because there is still an active threat.\par \par \uc2 You're looking at live pictures there on the screen. Dozens of law enforcement officers responded on the scene. It's near Howard University Law School. Police in tactical gear were seen escorting people out of an apartment building, and at least one person was seeing being led away by police in handcuffs.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is hitting Republicans tonight for stripping the \uc2 Walt Disney Company\uc2  of its long-held special privileges in the state of Florida. The moves part of the feud between the White House and Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  over parental rights legislation.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Phil Keating has the latest tonight from Miami.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in South Florida today, signing the Stop Woke Act into law, condemning critical race theory, and signing the anti- Disney Bill, taking away its self-governing status.\par \par \uc2 The stripping of its power was led by DeSantis after \uc2 Disney\uc2  vowed to fight in court the Parental Rights in Education law, known by opponents as the "don't say gay" law which prohibits teachers from discussing sexual orientation and gender identity with children between kindergarten and third grade.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I'm just thinking to myself, you are a corporation based in Burbank, California, and you are going to marshal your economic might to attack the parents of my state? We view that as a provocation, and we are going to fight back against that.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Still no public response from \uc2 Disney\uc2 , but President Biden has now weighed in, saying off camera, "I respect conservatives. There is nothing conservative about deciding you are going to throw \uc2 Disney\uc2  out of its present posture because Mickey Mouse should not be able to say, you know, gay."\par \par \uc2 Disney Reedy Creek improvement district was originally created in 1967 to bring Disney World to Florida and all the tourists and sales tax dollars. It works like a small government, managing all roads, permits, and emergency services. All of that will now have to be picked up by Osceola and Orange counties, where the mayor says this will be catastrophic for Orlando area taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 Florida's fight is also creating interstate competition. Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  tweeting "Florida's authoritarian socialist attacks on the private sector are driving businesses away. In Colorado, we don't mettle in affairs of companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2  or \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Hey, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , we're ready for a Mountain Disneyland."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEATING (on camera): \uc2 Disney\uc2  critics also point out that the company is expanding operations in communist but lucrative China where homosexuality and gay themes in movies are officially frowned upon.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Phil. It is one of the biggest weekends of the year at Talladega Superspeedway in Alabama, 100,000 fans are expected at Saturday and Sunday's big car events. FOX Business correspondent Madison Alworth is there with what it all means to the local economy. Hi, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Yes, we have seen fans that got here as early as Monday to enjoy the festivities at this racetrack. The two weekends that happen at Talladega, those races, they add $434 million to the local economy. That's huge for an area that relies so heavily on racetracks and the tourism around it.\par \par \uc2 And after two years of COVID precautions and restricted limitations to how many people could come in, having those 100,000 fans back really means a lot to the area.\par \par \uc2 And those fans, they really come from all over -- 70 percent come from outside the state of Alabama. All 50 states are represented, and fans come from over 20 countries as well. And the reason that people travel so far for this track is because it's fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL BRANHAM, NASCAR TRACK COMMUNICATIONS: This is the biggest, baddest racetrack on the planet. I love to say it's a palace of speech. And when Bill France Sr. went to build this racetrack, he wanted something a little bit bigger than what he had in Daytona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: I got a taste of that speed firsthand. Russell took me for a spin in the pace car. We hit 140 miles per hour. Gillian, it was at first fun and games, but then when we were inches, and I mean truly just inches away from the wall, not to mention the fact that we were riding that wall at 33- degree incline, it was definitely a little scarier. Once in a lifetime opportunity for me, and I say that because I think I will only do that once in my lifetime. I told Russell on the second lap that we needed to move back into the center lane. So if you want to catch the pros, which I'm very happy to do, to be stepping back not on the track this weekend, we're going to have those races on FOX Sunday. That's the big one, and lots of fans will be tuning in. It's been electric here, Gillian, seeing everyone back in action.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Great. Madison, thanks for taking us along on that ride with you. We appreciate it. You can see Sunday's \uc2 Geico\uc2  500 starting at 2:30 eastern on the FOX broadcast network on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the panel joins us on the southern border crisis. But first, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York, sources there say the handyman accused of killing a Queens woman and then dumping her body moved to the U.S. from Mexico illegally. David Bonola was arrested for the murder of Orsolya Gaal. Authorities say the two conducted what they call an intimate relationship when he started of working for her family.\par \par \uc2 And FOX 29 in Philadelphia there, as city officials reverse of the recent reinstatement of their indoor coronavirus mask mandate. The mayor's office says the move comes because case counts are leveling off and hospitalizations are decreasing.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look, finally, at Portland, Maine, from FOX 23. One of the big stories there tonight, the state launches a website to assist residents who want to claim their $850 relief payment following the signing of a supplemental budget. The program is expected it return half of Maine's $730 million budget surplus to an estimated 858,000 people.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We also take pride being not only a nation of immigrants but a nation of laws.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES, (R-TX): The situation on the border is very similar to war. Everybody is sucked up into this chaos. That's the part this administration hasn't seen. I don't understand how they can turn a blind- what is happening on the border. Migrants are dying at record numbers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Let's bring in our panel now, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen joins us, as well as Juan Williams, he's a FOX News analyst, and Jason Riley from "The Wall Street Journal" as he is a senior fellow as well at the Manhattan Institute. Gentlemen, thanks so much for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So Marc, let's start with you. There are increasing calls from Democrats now for President Biden to consider rescinding Title 42, most recently \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  and \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , and then the entire House problem solvers caucus. Let's pull this up so our viewers can see it. They say "The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  along with the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and other impacted agencies would need to submit a report to Congress within 30 days of the announcement ending the public health emergency with a plan showing how the administration will address the coming migrant surge. If a plan is not submitted within that timeframe, lifting Title 42 is automatically delayed and another 30 days until a report is submitted." What do you think?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: They are terrified. Look, when you have \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , the senator from New Hampshire, which is about as far as away from the border as you can possibly get, going down to Texas and filming a campaign video in front of Trump's border wall and saying we need to build physical barriers, you know Biden's policies at the border have backfired.\par \par \uc2 And what they realize is that Title 42 is the only thing that is protecting them from the consequences of Biden's disastrous border policy, because he has lifted all the other Trump policies on the border. He got rid of the remain in Mexico. He got rid of the safe third country agreements. He stopped deportations, which have dropped to the lowest level in history.\par \par \uc2 All that is preventing millions of illegal migrants from crossing that border is Title 42. And so if you lift Title 42, Americans -- his border policies are already terribly unpopular -- they will see the full impact of Biden's border policies, which have been artificially protected from because of Title 42 and the COVID emergency.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And Juan, what these Democrats in the House problem solvers caucus are asking for is really essentially just a plan from the Biden administration. They want an acknowledgment of what DHS has projected will be a migrant surge at the southern border. They want to know that the president is thinking through his own policies in advance. It's not so unreasonable.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Not at all. And I think you're going to see more of it. Speaking to Marc's point, I think one likely solution you're going to see is that Congress may do something. They certainly won't do comprehensive immigration reform, but I think you may see attached as a rider aide to Ukraine or money for COVID something that says Republicans and Democrats agree to extend Title 42 on the border.\par \par \uc2 Now, today a federal court, appeals court, said there is no evidence that what we see from Title 42 is preventing or protecting COVID from coming in the country or protecting even the immigration officials from contracting it from immigrants crossing the border. But they said it can continue for now. But I think you're going to have to see Congress take some action. They clearly have not within willing to do so.\par \par \uc2 The other part of this story is that you see Jim Jordan of the House Freedom Caucus putting out a memo saying we are going to use this to attack Democrats. We're all about making the immigrants into terrible people, sexual assault charges against them, and the like. I think you see exactly the power of this and why we have failed to see Congress take action.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, Juan, let's build on that point about the politicization here. Jason, "The Hill" is saying now that Biden is facing a mutiny over Title 42. Take a look at what they write in a new piece. They say, "There is also virtually no one on any side of the debate who sees Title 42 as truly rooted in public health concerns. It's always been seen as a policy primarily aimed at deterring migration. Just about everyone, whether on right or left, also agrees that the lifting of the measure is sure to cause an additional spike in unauthorized border crossings which are already at their highest point in recent decades."\par \par \uc2 Yesterday Health and Health Services Secretary Becerra basically said Republicans are using this as an excuse to try and get the president to shore up security along the southern border. I hazard to guess that at this moment Republicans would pretty much hang their hats on any policy move as an excuse to try and spur the White House into action.\par \par \uc2 JASON RILEY, COLUMNIST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": I don't think they need excuse for political reasons. We have legitimate crisis. You look at the numbers. The numbers in March were the highest in two decades, and a third higher than what they were in February. This is a legitimate crisis. This isn't something that's been manufactured by the right.\par \par \uc2 And I think Americans of all political stripes don't want to see this sort of chaos on the border. They want control of the border. They don't want the border erased. And their problem is that the left seems indifferent to this chaos on the border. And it's probably a reflection of their attitudes of chaos in the interior, the lawlessness that's occurring in our inner cities with crime. And their focus on these prosecutors who don't want to prosecute criminals or the supposed bail reform that isn't doing anything to address releasing violent criminals back into the population.\par \par \uc2 And that's a problem for Democrats. And I think it would be political malpractice for Republicans not to take advantage of this. This is a legitimate crisis. The American people want control of the border. They don't want the border erased. And Republicans, I think, are going to double down on this message, and I think they should.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, very fast to you for the last word here. It almost seems, critics say, that the president has grown complacent when it comes to the increasing number of deaths we are seeing along and around the southern border. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think that's true at all. I think the White House in fact, and you are seeing this from the Democrats, as we discussed earlier, Gillian is really worried not only about putting in place a workable policy dealing with amnesty and dealing as well with asylum claims, but they haven't found the answer. And that's what's going on. I don't think it's a matter of indifference.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right. Well, tonight we are standing by to hear more from our teams on the ground there. A Texas National Guardsman has been missing for hours now after he jumped in the river to save a drowning migrant. He was presumed drown earlier today. They are now saying that he is missing, so we're waiting to hear more on that. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round on the Ukraine war and COVID mask confusion. Plus, we've got winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KURT VOLKER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO NATO: As far as Mariupol goes, I think the only thing that could save them at this time would be a direct western military intervention.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: There are no plans right now to have U.S. troops involved on the ground in Ukraine. I think you know the president has been very clear about his decision on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And we're back with the panel now. Gentlemen, President Biden has said every which way from Sunday that he is not sending American troops to fight and die in Ukraine. But if we are still looking at images of corpses lying in streets and pregnant women being bombed a year-and-a-half from now, which is now something \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is predicting, surely the calculus will be reconsidered, maybe even changed. Marc, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: The problem hasn't been no one has talked about sending American troops. The question is we haven't been sending enough weapons. We're starting to send a little bit more, and hopefully it's not too little, too late.\par \par \uc2 But this is a big moment for Ukraine and also for President Biden, because the American people haven inspired by President Zelenskyy's leadership, and we've been amazed watching how they defended the city of Kyiv. And all of a sudden, our expectations have changed. They have won the battle of Kyiv, and now we know they can win. So if all of a sudden we start seeing images of Russian forces conquering cities in Donbas, taking capitals, doing all these things, they are not going to blame the Ukrainians for not doing enough job. Biden is not going to be able to do what he did in Afghanistan and say the Ukrainians didn't fight. He's not going to be able to say it's because of the Russian military being so strong and so powerful. As we've seen, that's not true. It will be because we didn't do enough. So his presidency is really on the line in how this fight unfolds.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jason, plenty of military analysts saying there may, may be a time in the future when the president would have to reconsider. We are already seeing the U.S. agreeing to train Russian -- excuse me, Ukrainian troops inside the country, which is something the administration previously said they wouldn't do. Could you foresee a possible turning point where the American public is simply exhausted and fed up and outraged to the point where Biden starts looking at this again?\par \par \uc2 RILEY: I think that's a longways off. I think what we see now in the polling is that the administration isn't doing enough. I think there's a lot of sentiment here in the U.S. for the administration to do a lot more than it's done. And I think Biden's real problem is that he made a bad first impression. The debacle of the withdrawal from Afghanistan continues to haunt this presidency. You can draw a straight line between his approval rating prior to that and what it is today. He has never really recovered. And I think he is very worried that this is going to track him and Democrats into the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 But I think the American people would be very much with the administration in doing as much as possible to help Ukraine defend itself against Russia. It is just a matter of political will on the part of Democrats. And as Marc mentioned, so far that will doesn't seem to be existent to the extent that it needs to be for them to do what they need to do to defend themselves. But I think the public would be with the administration if it continues down this path and gives the Ukrainians the arms they need.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, the president was asked how long we can keep up this pace of sending ammo and weapons to Ukraine, $800 million every two weeks at this going rate, and he basically said for as long as possible. We'll take a look at this release from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . They say American renters reported a sharp decline in their probability of owning a home in the future from 51.6 percent in 2021 to 44 percent -- 43.3 percent this year. This is a historically low rating here -- or high rating, excuse me. How long will American appetites to pay more at the pump, more at the grocery store and forego big homes in order to fight this war in Ukraine continue?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's great question, Gillian, because I think what you have to understand is support for Ukraine is not free for us, the American people. We do have to put up with higher costs in terms of gasoline and food and even, when you think about the economic, the overall economic impact, inflation here is about now the same thing that we are seeing in Europe. And they are playing even a greater cost in terms of the fact that they had more import of oil from Russia.\par \par \uc2 So keeping the alliance together, the international alliance is one thing, but also keeping the American people in support is another. I don't think there's any appetite on the part of the American people for sending our troops there, given what happened in other parts of the world, the 20-year long war in Afghanistan. But right now, the American people are with the Ukrainian people, and I think Biden understands that and is being supportive.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jason, I'm going to give you the last word here.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: Well, I agree with Juan. The American people are with the Ukrainians, and it's just a matter of political will on the part of the Biden administration, and projecting strength. I think really, again, Biden made a bad first impression in terms of foreign policy, and he's still trying to recover from that. And that could haunt the Democratic Party going to the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm going to stick with you here for a second, Jason. Tell me your winners and your losers of the week.\par \par \uc2 RILEY: My winner is Kathryn Kimball Mizelle, the federal judge, who vacated the mask mandate for public transportation.\par \par \uc2 My loser is the Justice Department who wants to appeal her ruling.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Marc, you next.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: My loser is \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who delivered another vapid, embarrassing street. First, we had her musings on the passage of time. Now we have space is exciting. It's really hard to screw up the vice presidency, but she seems to be managing to do it.\par \par \uc2 And my winner is Ukraine, because they are happy warriors. They've got a sense of humor amongst tragedy. And they just declared the Putin sunken flagship, the Moskva, a national underwater, cultural heritage site.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Juan, to you next.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That was great. I think Zelenskyy, President Zelenskyy of the Ukraine is the winner. He said this week he got just what he was waiting for in terms of that $800 million deal from the United States, more ammunition, more in the way of artillery and drones to stop the Russians on the eastern front.\par \par \uc2 As for a loser, I would say Kevin McCarthy, who said that it wasn't true that he had said Trump should resign, and then there is a phone tape of him saying just that. So he looks like a damaged political property at the moment.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Gentlemen, thanks so much. Great to see all of you. Have a fabulous weekend.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You too, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: It is Friday, so time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ready, set.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who knows who is under here. No more bunny business.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The much-hated mask mandate on the plane and on the train has been tossed out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You may remove your masks.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, should people continue to wear masks on planes?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: That's up to them.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: People are dying because of misinformation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm not a doctor. You are not a doctor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People have to assume their own individual risks and make that assessment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you considering delaying lifting Title 42?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There is going to be an appeal by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This administration cares nothing about border security, and it's going to hurt the American public.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: I think the president has richly deserved his approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We appeal and plead to all world leaders to help us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wager everything that when that tape is played, I will be right. The Ukrainians are going to win.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Their behavior has been atrocious, frankly, as a guest in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm going to get emotional about this issue, because it's horrible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One day you are a Cinderella, so to speak, and then in 0.6 seconds you are Quasimodo.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Space is exciting. It spurs our imaginations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we've got the latest on the war in Ukraine from the ground. And President Biden meets with the \uc2 Congressional Hispanic Caucus\uc2  on border issues.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much watching. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington. Judge Jeanine Pirro is in for Jesse Watters up next. Hi, Judge.\par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220423ei4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden's Historically Bad Presidency; Will Smith is Banned from the Oscars for 10 Years\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Peter Doocy, Sara Carter, Tammy Bruce, Joe Concha,  Christina Coleman, Rachel Campos-Duffy \par \uc2 7,717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Hi. Welcome to HANNITY. We have a busy news Friday night for you.\par \par \uc2 Our expectations for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they're already extremely low, but through 14 months in office, he has somehow he managed to even do worse than we predicted. Everything now is in crisis, it was all preventable, everything. Our schools, our cities, our borders, our safety, our security, our economy, war in Europe, America has abandoned in Afghanistan -- by the way, we're keeping track, it's days 237, and 250 since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he would not abandon them.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, millions are struggling to fill their gas tanks, heat their homes, and put food on the table. But tonight, it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who's full of complaints, today whining that Republicans weren't nicer to his Supreme Court nominee and he even accused them of verbal abuse. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I knew it wouldn't be easy but I knew the person I nominated will be put through a painful and difficult confirmation process. But I have to tell you, what Judge Jackson was put through was well beyond that. There was verbal abuse, the anger, the constant interruptions, most vile, baseless assertions and accusations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They pointed out a simple fact that she went easy in these child porn cases, in terms of sentencing. That's all they did. Did they filibuster her nomination like Biden did to Judge Janice Rogers Brown because he didn't want Republicans to ultimately nominate an African- American woman to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 ? Did they assassinate her character like then Senator Biden did to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  in 1991? Justice Thomas referred to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's smear campaign as a high-tech lynching. And did Republicans accuse Judge Jackson of gang rape without a single shred of evidence to support those claims?\par \par \uc2 You might remember this, Joe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did you consume alcohol during your high school years?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So you're saying there's never been a case where you drank so much that you didn't remember what happened the night before or part of what happened?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In your yearbook, you talked about drinking and sexual exploits, did you not?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So you were not as a sloppy drunk and so your roommate was lying?\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: From my standpoint, as a black American as far as I'm concerned, it is a high-tech lynching for uppity blacks who in any way deign to think for themselves, to do for themselves, to have different ideas. And it is a message that unless you kowtow to an old order, this is what will happen to you. You will be lynched, destroyed, caricatured by a committee of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  rather than hung from a tree.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you led that smear against Justice Thomas. And as far as I'm concerned, after trying to ruin the lives of both Kavanaugh and Justice Thomas, Democrats have no right to complain about the treatment of any nominee for any reason, especially \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who waged a full-blown witch hunt against two reputable justices.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, it's not totally clear that Biden even remembers what happened to either \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  or Justice Kavanaugh. Because we all know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in a very steep cognitive decline, it is becoming more and more evident, more obvious, more transparent every single day. Here's today's Joe video of the day. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My name is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Please sit down, I'm Jill's husband, and Naomi Biden's grandfather.\par \par \uc2 And folks, now, yesterday -- this is not only a sunny day. I mean this from the bottom of my heart. This is going to let so much shine sunshine on so many young women.\par \par \uc2 Her meeting with Judge Jackson, Senator Duckworth introduced her to 11- year-old -- is it Vivian, Vivienne? Vivian, I'm sorry, Vivian.\par \par \uc2 America is a nation that can be defined in a single word -- I was going to put him a foot -- excuse me, in the foothills of the Himalayas with \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  traveling with him -- I guess we've traveled 17,000 miles when I was vice president. I don't know that for a fact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Not only are the American people watching this, President Xi is watching this. Putin's watching this. The mullahs in Iran are watching this. Kim Jong-un are watching this. The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's watching this. The world's enemies are watching this.\par \par \uc2 This administration is nothing but a dumpster fire. You have a president struggling with even speaking, basic simple tasks, putting sentences together. The Biden syndicate, by the way, also facing a possible indictment.\par \par \uc2 The vice president -- well, she's got her own embarrassing moments and, of course, the never-ending giggling at inappropriate times. Our economy is imploding, food prices are soaring, gas prices at record levels, interest rates are now beginning to surge, a 40-year high in inflation. The Bank of America now warning of an imminent recession.\par \par \uc2 Things frankly couldn't get any worse but White House staffers, they're now openly complaining. The media is not sufficiently touting their historic success? What success? Exactly what are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 They take responsibility for nothing, 40-year high of inflation, record high gas prices, a disaster leaving the Islamic Emirates of Afghanistan, not being ready for war in Europe with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , oh, energy prices that he caused because he artificially reduced the world supply. Remember, the White House blames \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for everything, because of the war in Europe that happened under their watch.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, Putin's a thug. He's a criminal. The acts he's committing and his troops are committing a pure evil. He only responds to strength.\par \par \uc2 But, sadly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the weakest leader that I have seen in my lifetime. So now here we are months into \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's hostile invasion of a sovereign country and this week the devastating scenes on the ground in Ukraine are really hard to watch if you have a conscience and you have a soul.\par \par \uc2 Putin's brutality, evil knows no bounds. And earlier today, Russian forces -- they shelled a train station in eastern Europe, filled with innocent citizens, innocent civilians, they were just attempting to flee the war zone. And you got dozens and dozens that are now dead or seriously injured, including an untold number of dead kids.\par \par \uc2 But Biden is still blocking Poland from, what, delivering fighter jets to Ukraine. Why? In fact, most of the lethal aid the U.S. is sending to Ukraine is happening in spite of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  not because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He doesn't even seem to care about what is exactly going on in Ukraine and he doesn't really seem to care about anything that doesn't directly impact him or a member of his immediate family, because according to a new poll, Americans no longer think Biden is likable or and by the way for good reason.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a low-functioning, self-centered, corrupt career politician who frankly needed to retire 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, here with more from the White House is our own Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 Can you -- can we just go back to this explanation and this question and answer with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  on COVID? Just one second, I'm going to play it later in the show again but this really gets to me.\par \par \uc2 So we got \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  kissing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and breathing over his shoulder for a pretty significant period of time, but that's not considered close contact according to the CDC, Peter?\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: No, and I was trying to figure that out because what's closer in a professional setting like that than a kiss. But they don't seem to think that she could have possibly transferred any kind of a viral load over to the president.\par \par \uc2 But what we've seen all week long is that they want to talk about other stuff. Yes, COVID cases are on the rise, but they're not changing the schedule. Today, it was the confirmation hearing, the confirmation celebration. Yesterday, it was postal reform and on Tuesday, it was -- hey, remember when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was president? Let's talk about that and Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 And I think --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That didn't go -- Peter, that didn't go particularly well because Joe was wandering around alone while everybody gave their attention to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and even Joe tapping Barack on the back trying to get his attention, didn't work out particularly well for Joe.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And that one, I was in the room for that, standing a few feet away, I was surprised that so many of the invited guests were not giving the president -- the current president -- more of their attention.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Then I have a criticism of you. I mean, you saw the president wandering around lonely, why didn't you go over and talk to him?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: We were a little a little bit further away. But, you know, I -- for example, I did get a little bit closer when it was quieter to him the next day, and I asked him, are you worried about COVIDF coming back because all around the White House, in the cabinet, in the Congress, it is coming back, in Washington, D.C.? And he said, well, we just have to get the COVID funding bill done.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is something else that right now is not going to happen because I -- Congress just left for two weeks. Speaker Pelosi, Leader Schumer, they let the Congress leave for two weeks. And so, they want to talk about a lot of -- a lot of other things when I -- as you mentioned, gas prices remain high, near all-time highs in some places. Food prices are rising and that's something that affects everybody and it's not likely to change anytime soon because the most basic part of food production, fertilizer, comes from the Black Sea region, and they are not getting a lot of stuff in and out of there right now.\par \par \uc2 So these problems are mounting. The unofficial midterm push began this week with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  here. They're talking about healthcare. He said, hey, we've got a story to tell, we just have to tell it. But the story that a lot of people want to hear is not necessarily in line with the one that they are telling here.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Now, I'm trying to understand this because White House staffers were out there berating "Politico" for not touting all of Biden's successes. Now, you cover the White House every day -- I mentioned inflation. I mentioned record high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 I didn't mention that we ran out of COVID tests around Christmas and monoclonal antibodies around Christmas. I didn't mention the chaos at the border, I could have mentioned that. I did mention Afghanistan. I did mention Europe, the war that's broken out there.\par \par \uc2 I don't see what the list of successes are. Did they give you any background or detail about the things that they want "Politico" and reporters to tout as success?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yeah, they did. They gave us two things they think are a win right now. They think the sanctions they put on Russia and Putin are working the way that the world expected them to work, and they think that Russia getting kicked off the United Nations human rights council is a win because it's only the second time that that's ever happened. So that is where they want attention instead of on all the other stuff.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, I got to tell you something. Not exactly a long list, because I can give you the list of Trump successes, oh, including three of vaccines, monoclonal antibodies. Before COVID, record low unemployment for any demographic, and borders --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's another win they keep talking about, more jobs created than the first year of any president in history. So if you want to talk about what they want to talk about, it's that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Peter Doocy, keeping them straight and honest -- I'm kind of disappointed that \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  decided -- actually, she fits at MSNBC DNC because they don't often tell the truth and I've seen a lie or two, like they don't -- like immigrants don't need to be COVID tested because they're not going to be here very long, and 2:00 a.m. flights are early morning flights. Peter, I think that question was yours.\par \par \uc2 But it's too bad because it could have been a great show opportunity, Peter and \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  would have been a great debate show.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: All right. Well, let's just keep it going here in the briefing room for as long as we can. How about that?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. We turn now to another Biden crisis, another preventable one that is about to get really bad. Now, this week, our own Sara Carter was at the border where the situation on the ground is very concerning and this is about to get even worse than last year. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA CARTER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: We're using very high tech equipment here on this plane. This plane is set up -- I mean, you could see people climbing in the mountain. What are the national security implications because as you're out here. I mean, it's like looking for a needle in a haystack?\par \par \uc2 I'm still concerned about how wide open the border is, how much work is placed on your state and local level, and the federal government isn't backing that. There -- do you believe there's a national security element to what's happening here at the border and how bad is it?\par \par \uc2 STACY HOLLAND, ASSISTANT CHIEF PILOT, TEXAS DPS: I do and I've always felt that way prior to the predicament right now. The southwestern border is very porous. It is very open. If you can see, there's -- there's a lot of access, egress and ingress in Rio Grande River boundary where people can come across. It's just a fact, okay? It is a very open border.\par \par \uc2 If I were a country who wishes to do the United States harm, you got to ask yourself, would you come to the southwestern border where you can literally just come across with little to no pushback or would you go into like New York City, you know, where there's a big footprint of customs and law enforcement? And the answer to that is no.\par \par \uc2 Don't think for a minute these organizations aren't aware of what our policy is, okay? Their intel, their understanding and their comprehension of what our policies are, our policies aren't, is very good. Don't think for a second they're not going to take full advantage this yet new another policy of the removal of the title 42.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Joining us now with more, investigative journalists and reporter and FOX News contributor, Sara Carter.\par \par \uc2 All right. Title 42 rescinding, it's a CDC order for the pandemic that allows this country to return people to the country of their origin and that looks like it's going to be repealed and already, migrant caravans are forming to come to the border, maybe in larger numbers than last year?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Oh, much larger numbers than last year, Sean. They're expecting a tsunami, a wave of people coming from all over the world. I can tell you this. Since fiscal year, so we're looking from October, the fiscal year Border Patrol, October 2021 until now, there's been over a million apprehensions.\par \par \uc2 I was told by a senior DHS official that 600,000 gotaways on top of those one million apprehensions were expected this year alone and they're expecting more than 3 million people to be apprehended by the end of this fiscal year. It's absolutely incredible.\par \par \uc2 And I was up there with DPS Air Patrol, and I can tell you the thing they are most concerned about, Sean, are the people that are coming over the mountains where I was at in camo, people that did not want to be captured. There were narcotics.\par \par \uc2 We actually were able to fly over an area where three huge bundles of marijuana, over 300 pounds of marijuana had been left in the canyon ridge to be picked up later. Then the people disappeared, they disappear, Sean, onto I-90, on to I-10. There's resources are limited and it is a very, very dangerous situation.\par \par \uc2 These are not people that want to be turning themselves into border patrol. These are people that are wearing camo, they're trying to get away, they're trying to blend into the environment, they're bringing fentanyl and other drugs, and very dangerous, very dangerous situation for both our law enforcement officials, as well as Americans and those that are being trafficked.\par \par \uc2 All right. Sara Carter, thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, former senior advisor to President Trump, \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, Stephen, we had this border under your former boss, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , under complete control and it was done, "stay in Mexico" policy, he built the wall, almost finished it, over 500 miles the new wall, correct me if I'm wrong, and of course, he ended catch-and-release.\par \par \uc2 So the crisis was preventable and we could also solve the crisis by going back to those policies, but I don't see that happening with this administration.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's first week in office, Sean, he ordered the rescission and termination of almost every one of President Trump's unprecedentedly successful policies, and you just took through a few of them.\par \par \uc2 Under President Trump, in 2020, after we beat back all the court challenges, we had the most secure border in American history. There were zero discretionary releases. In other words, zero times would Border Patrol apprehend an illegal immigrant and have to release that person. It was sealed, as tight as a drum.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  undid all of that. And here's the scariest part, Sean, last year saw the highest number of illegal immigrants enter our country in recorded history. My sources on the ground in border patrol say this year puts last year to shame.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, and it's only going to get worse. If you look at every single problem we have, all preventable, all fixable. We were energy independent. We had secure borders. The president had and an orderly plan to get out of Afghanistan, certainly I truly believe that Putin wouldn't go near Ukraine if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were president, the economy was humming along and recovering nicely, everything with COVID wouldn't have happened because he believed in Warp Speed. Joe abandoned those policies.\par \par \uc2 So all these problems, all these crises are all self-induced but all fixable but they're not going to fix them.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: If they just -- if he just issued an executive order that just said go back and just put 