ide the womb. That little girl her neck had been cut, her brain had been suctioned out and she had been thrown into a medical waste bucket as well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, one little girl, another girl maybe again, 28 weeks old as well was dismembered, possibly while alive, torn into pieces. It is devastating and that is why these activists and Live Action and others are calling on D.C. Police to investigate because these children could have been born outside the womb, could have survived outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 There may be Federal laws that were violated here, laws that prohibit partial birth abortion, laws that prohibit leaving a born alive infant to die and Live Action has been investigating this abortion clinic in particular in D.C. that does these horrific late-term abortions, and we found the abortionist admitting on undercover camera that he would leave an infant to die in his facility if a child was born alive.\par \par \uc2 So this is happening in full sight of the authorities and yet no action has been taken.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, certainly moral laws are violated. Just in one sentence, do you have any idea how the ghouls who commit these crimes and support them have seized the moral high ground in our national conversation?\par \par \uc2 ROSE: I mean, it's the incredible dishonesty of media that is working to protect abortionist here, the killers of human beings. Children who deserve love and to be held instead of being thrown away like medical waste, and the media is covering for them. Authorities are covering for them.\par \par \uc2 Kermit Gosnell, an abortionist in Philadelphia operated illegally for 30 years, three decades before authorities finally did their job, and now, he is behind bars.\par \par \uc2 This abortionist in Washington, D.C. who killed these five children and thousands others should be behind bars as well. And so we're urging on the D.C. mayor to do her job as well as authorities.\par \par \uc2 Listen, Tucker, this is the American Horror Story. It is the American Horror Story that no one wants to talk about. But it is our children, our most vulnerable and they deserve love and protection.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I cannot believe the same who support this are the same ones who are agitating for war. They love to kill. It's amazing. Lila Rose, thanks so much for joining us. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the January 6th Committee is issuing subpoenas to people for attending the Trump rally on January 6, the people were nowhere near the Capitol. So apparently, it's now illegal to go to a political rally if the authorities don't like the candidate. Is that constitutional?\par \par \uc2 Well, the knives are already out for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , as predicted, guess what the saying about him now? We think you know. We've got the tape, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the world's richest man said online several months ago, you know \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is really important to our national conversation, it is the center of our political conversation, it is where political views form, so it must be free. Shut down \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , make it a one-party propaganda device and you kill democracy.\par \par \uc2 So now, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the guy who said that is the biggest shareholder at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And that has caused panic, not just at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  which is worried it won't be able to censor its political opponents anymore. But in the broader world of propaganda, as Reuters just reported, quote, "News of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  taking a Board seat at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  had some \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees panicking over the future of the social media firm's ability to moderate content." To censor things they don't like.\par \par \uc2 So we predicted that once Musk -- this was an obvious call, by the way -- bought the shares and tried to reform \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the media would of course have to destroy him and they would begin by calling him, can you guess? A racist. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is a massive threat to the way things are, so by definition it will not be long before they are calling him a racist. You can mark your calendar.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: All right, so that was a little self-promotion here. We are quoting ourselves, we're getting pompous in old age, but we couldn't resist because it was a pretty good call.\par \par \uc2 Within like hours, MSNBC rushed into the breach to confirm our worst suspicions. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANAND GIRIDHARADAS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: He is building in \uc2 Tesla\uc2  documentedly racist company that perhaps reminds him, gives him nostalgic memories of apartheid South Africa, where he grew up.\par \par \uc2 He is more than that. an embodiment of what I would say is \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's biggest strategic problem, which is a hostile, cruel, dangerous online environment, especially for women, especially for people of color, women of color in particular. Ask anybody if you don't -- if that's not your experience of it, ask someone who might know.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Oh, it is.\par \par \uc2 GIRIDHARADAS: And Musk embodies that bullying, that bro harassment, and his agenda has been telegraphed very clearly. Let's control.\par \par \uc2 At a moment when \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's greatest opportunity and need is for greater control of Nazism on the platform, of doxing and brigading of women and ruining women's lives for having opinions on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , controlling that, he wants the opposite and they have welcomed him to their Board.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So two of the single most privileged people in America posing as oppressed persons of color, and telling you that your free speech, your stupid Constitution must be destroyed, so they can be safe, when they're already safe because they're already in charge of everything. These are the people who will destroy your life for disagreeing with them. They're very worried about bullying all of a sudden.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  must be a racist. That was not hard to predict, by the way, at all.\par \par \uc2 So on January 6th, 2021, a man from New Mexico called Matthew Martin walked into the U.S. Capitol. The police waved him inside, "Come on in," they said with hand gestures. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Justice Department and then decided to prosecute Martin for being waved in by Federal employees. How does that work exactly?\par \par \uc2 Well, yesterday, a Federal Judge had that question and acquitted Martin. The Judge noted that, quote, "People were streaming by and the officers made no attempts to stop the people." Now, you would think a finding like that rooted in commonsense would prompt some reflection for the January 6th Committee. Wait a second, we're sending people to prison for being ushered in by Federal employees into a Federal building? People who are there to enforce the law ushered them in. Maybe we're on the wrong path. But of course, they're not saying that.\par \par \uc2 They are now issuing subpoenas to everyone who helped organize the rally, the political rally on January 6 for Trump because that's now a crime. Victor Davis Hanson is a Senior Fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 . He joins us to sort this all out.\par \par \uc2 Professor, thanks for coming on. What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HOOVER INSTITUTION\uc2 : Thank you. You know, almost immediately that riot was termed a conspiracy or coup and so the American people, they don't like people desecrating the Capitol, and so they went along for a while, then all of a sudden they started asking questions.\par \par \uc2 Officer Sicknick did not die violently, he died of natural causes, and the autopsy was suppressed for a while. That information was naught. Ashli Babbitt, who was shot was not some kind of armed intruder. She was a petite military veteran that was lethally shot for the high misdemeanor of going through a broken window and then when that happens in America, the suspect that's killed, the officer who did that, we get all the information, but we didn't get any information about the officer.\par \par \uc2 And then when we juxtapose it to 120 days of rioting and $2 billion of damage and 30 people killed and 1,500 police officers. We saw this asymmetry. They were militarizing the Capitol with 20,000 the next week with soldiers and then we thought, well, okay, maybe we're just paranoid. Let's get some information.\par \par \uc2 We have a January 6th Commission. So Kevin McCarthy will appoint his and Pelosi, and then all of a sudden, they said, no, these Republicans can't go and the only people that can serve are these Never Trump people.\par \par \uc2 And then we thought, well, there's 14,000 hours of video, let's just settle it and get the video in the e-mail. No, no, we're not going to let that so why if they're so worried, and then the coup de gras, Tucker, was this Matthew Rosenberg, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " marquee reporter, he had been telling us for months, that this was a coup and a conspiracy. On the anniversary, he said, "This is a right-wing denial. They're in denial." And all of a sudden, an operation Veritas unguarded moment, he says it was a festive occasion. Nobody was in danger at all.\par \par \uc2 All the guys and the reporters are you know, they're psycho dramatic. There was nothing there. And there were F.B.I. informants everywhere. We are thinking, wow, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 And so now, I think we had a buffoonish riot that was useful and never waste a crisis moment for the left.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think you're exactly right. The lying is just overwhelming which makes your commitment to truth telling no matter what all the more inspiring. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Victor Davis Hanson, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We've got still more news, the news avalanche continues after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, that's it for us tonight. We are still working on who wrote \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 's blog.\par \par \uc2 See you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220408ei4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Explosions Rock City Kyiv; Explosions In Kyiv, Aid Raid Sirens In Lviv; Report: Russia Might Target NATO Convoys Of Weapons; Biden Still Has No Policies To Take On China; New Fears Of Imminent Amphibious Assault On Odessa; Ingraham Guest Helps Americans Escape Ukraine; Man Smears Poop On Face Of Woman In NYC; President Biden's State of the Union Address to Congress Reviewed; Rep. Jim Banks (R-IN) Interviewed on President Biden's Economic Policies Mentioned in State of the Union Address\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Raymond Arroyo \par \uc2 8,892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 March 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left for this evening. Stay with the Fox News channel for continuing coverage of Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, set your DVR so you never miss an episode. And let not your heart be troubled, Laura Ingraham is straight ahead. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham and this is the 'Ingraham Angle'. The Russian onslaught in Ukraine continues. The first major city fell to Russian forces today as the civilian death toll climbs to more than 2000, according to officials there.\par \par \uc2 Now, further east, the port city of Mariupol underwent a 15-hour bombardment after being surrounded by Russian troops. Now, according to the mayor, the city is near a humanitarian catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 And already tonight in Kyiv, blasts are being heard all around the city. We're going to be updating you on the increasingly dire situation on the ground there. And we'll do that throughout the hour. But first--\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What you just saw on that video was verified by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Russian jets bombing a residential apartment complex just outside of the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Now, it reinforces Fox's own reporting that air strikes against the area have increased, including in spots where civilians are desperately trying to take cover.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Zhenya Mykhailenko, who is a business owner, who converted his own restaurant into a bomb shelters. Zhenya, when was the last time that you all heard sirens tonight?\par \par \uc2 ZHENYA MYKHAILENKO, RESTAURANT OWNER IN KYIV: Pretty much all night long.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Zhenya, the thought of this constant sense of being under attack, of hearing the sirens, of hearing bombs dropping, of hearing human screams. I mean, a lot of people are used to hearing that and seeing it in movies. But your people there are living through the horror of this.\par \par \uc2 And you decided to do what you could by opening up your restaurant to people most in need. What are you hearing from the people most affected on the ground? Obviously, they're terrified.\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILENKO: I think you very much underestimate Ukrainian people. Because we're started in 2014. I was in Mariupol in 2015 a year after. And that's what struck me the most was that the citizens of Mariupol, because back then people in the capital in Kyiv were used to hearing artillery fire. And neither was I.\par \par \uc2 Now, when I was in Mariupol in 2015, I was jogging along the street. And I heard artillery bombing for the first time. I was like, What the f*** is going on? But everyone around me who lived there for this entire year was just ok with it. It was like a normal day.\par \par \uc2 So it's not as dramatic as you try to describe it. Ukrainians are not panicking. We are not frightened by the artillery fire. In fact, if anything, it makes us more resilient.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There are a million people, Zhenya, who according to the \uc2 UN\uc2  and other sources, have fled the country, mostly women and children. Men who are fighting age are staying behind, except the severely compromised or elderly.\par \par \uc2 The spirit on the ground is something that's been written about and talked about for this - these last several days now. And in a country where we can all get pretty spoiled and pretty used to the comforts of home, it's amazing to see those people who are able to go on with their life as best they can under extremely difficult circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But obviously, we're not used to that here in the United States. We're used to being able to go to work and getting there safely and not worrying about where our parents are. But that is not the case and in major cities throughout Ukraine today.\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILENKO: Once again, I think now you're underestimating Americans. Because I lived in Boston, I lived in Los Angeles, I lived in Long Beach. And there's some pretty resilient people in Long Beach that I know.\par \par \uc2 And I know, in fact, that people in Dorchester and people in some areas of Boston are also very resilient. In fact, I often associate myself with a lot of Americans I met during my six years living in America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Zhenya, what would you like to say to the people of the United States, those of Europe, who are watching these scenes unfold, other than - apparently, we're underestimating the drama on the ground. What else do you want to say about what's happening there?\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILENKO: Oh, you're not underestimating the drama. The drama is real. You're underestimating how people react to it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. I don't think we are. But ok, but Zhenya, what would you like to say to the people in Europe about what your country needs and the support that you would like to say?\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILENKO: I want to say that, in fact, I just read an article about this is that Europe and the entire world was the witness of Russia since 2004 in Beslan. In 2004, when lots of children were murdered, because of Russian neglect, and many times over since 2004.\par \par \uc2 And we have not been doing anything since then. So today, while we're paying for this, Ukraine is paying for this, the price of having our cities bombed and women and children killed. And I hope that the entire world realizes and figures this shit out, because this is the last chance we'll get in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Zhenya. Thank you. Stay safe. We apologize, obviously, the viewers some of the language pretty rough, but one can sympathize given the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, we're learning of what would be an alarming escalation. Senior Czech defense officials are telling the Washington Examiner that Russia might target the convoys of weapons headed to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Czech Deputy Defense Minister Tomas Kopecny adding, "The consequences of launching a military attack on \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory are quite clear." Well, that's an understatement.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is John Ratcliffe, former Trump Director of National Intel. John, good to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN RATCLIFFE, FORMER DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, I realize the desire to get help to the Ukrainian people. But do you think the U.S. officials have really gamed out what happens if those \uc2 NATO\uc2  convoys are in turn attacked?\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: Well, I think what we're seeing is, you know, this is now day seven of what most experts thought including in our Pentagon was going to be a two or three-day invasion. And while that speaks to the courage of the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 It also speaks to the fact that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  miscalculated. He thought that conventional missile strikes and cruise missile strikes on command and control centers combined with his armored forces would lead to a quick victory, and that hasn't happened. And that's good news for the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 But the bad news is, we've seen what happens before when the Russians get bogged down. And Putin gets more desperate. What he did in Syria with saving Bashar Al Assad was to do what he's starting to do now, which is a siege mentality around cities where he literally has decided it's better to level entire neighborhoods, including civilians rather than risk any more Russian lives. And that's what you're seeing in Kyiv and Kharkiv right now.\par \par \uc2 And it also speaks to the fact that he has already warned that he does not want \uc2 NATO\uc2  interfering in this. And so, we need to be careful about escalating this. But, Laura, it really underscores why this was such a bad strategy from the Biden administration. The if-then strategy - if Russia invades then we'll do something about it, then we'll help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 All of these supplies and all of the weaponry and all of the things that we sent in the Trump administration like javelins and stingers and sniper rifles and all the things that the Russians need, they should have been there long, long time ago instead of the strategy that now leaves them on the outside looking in.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, here's the former \uc2 NATO\uc2  ambassador, John, under Obama. He's on MSNBC today on what he expects next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS LUTE, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO NATO UNDER OBAMA: I do expect to be more brutal. Look, we should appreciate that we're in the very early days, just a few days into this. And we should expect this to be a long, protracted, brutal campaign.\par \par \uc2 If given a choice, Putin will double down before he backs down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: John, realistically speaking, now that Russia is there, that Biden has said, we're not going to fight Russian troops. We're not going to send boots on the ground. What realistically can the U.S. do to push \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  out if we're going to keep buying oil from Putin and if we're going to keep supporting China, which is backdooring a lot of support? Obviously, to Russia.\par \par \uc2 RATCLIFFE: If you take out the options that would have worked the double whammy of this - the sanction that was the double whammy, which is actually sanctioning Russia's oil supply and increasing U.S. oil production. None of the other sanctions are going to work in a timely manner to help Ukraine. It is going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 Putin has not yet deployed. He's got 300 modern striker fighters, SU-30s, 34s, 35s. He's going to use those, use air to ground missiles to target Kyiv and Kharkiv and other cities, and then he's going to