ulting. I've got to tell you, it's insulting. She was on an African American podcast or radio show. It's insulting to this important base in the Democratic Party, and it's insulting to all Americans. I'm sorry. We're not little babies. Raise the discourse. Raise up the people. That's what leaders do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I have a television show, and it's on a network called FOX News, and you're Raymond Arroyo, and you have a role, too, to play, Raymond.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We got a huge reaction, by the way, to our Mardi Gras video last night. So where do people see more of your festivities there in New Orleans?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, FOX Nation shadowed me all throughout the Mardi Gras season. We just posted it. It is called "Mardi Gras All Access." It's a special that takes you behind the scenes and into the local side of the celebration. They also got to accompany my incredible ride as grand marshal in the biggest parade in Mardi Gras. It is lot of fun. It's on FOX Nation now.\par \par \uc2 And then there's a little special Laura Ingraham ditty coming up, which I guess we will tell people about later.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, throw my reference in at the very end. It doesn't matter. "America's Hidden Gems," "America's Hidden Gems," but I'm not going to spoil the secret.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: "America's Hidden Gems."\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Unlike you just spoiled all the secrets of yours.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, I didn't.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And we have Samantha in my video, I believe, in my FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, there's a hidden gem.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She makes an appearance, so I'm going to save that for later. But Raymond, I can't wait to watch behind the scenes with Mardi Gras with you. Thank you, Raymond.\par \par \uc2 And the architect of several foreign policy disasters has some thoughts on Ukraine. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 ? Uh-oh, she' next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She is not calling for American boots on the ground yet, but Hillary Clinton sure wants us to commit open-ended resources in Ukraine's fight with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That is a possible way out. If the Ukrainians with our help can impose enough economic pain on Putin and, sadly, the Russian people, combined with providing weapons, that that might be the only way that right now I can see us getting to a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A reminder, our effort to support the Mujahideen, fight the Soviets in Afghanistan, lasted nearly a decade. Up next, Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220302ei3200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Evacuation Preps For Americans In Ukraine; U.S., Russia Meet As Ukraine Invasion Fears Build; Why The GOP Is Resonating With Hispanic Voters; LA Motorcyclist Killed In High Speed Crash; Looking Back At Biden's Heated Press Encounters; AZ Blasts Biden Admin For Threat To Pull COVID Aid\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Benjamin Hall, Hillary Vaughn, Joe Concha,  Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,744 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: No official cause of death given the TMZ says he was sick with COVID. (INAUDIBLE) just 74 years old, what a sad day.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: He will be missed. Great to be with you, John. I'll see you back here Monday. I'll see at 4:00, I'm in for Neil again. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Looking forward to that, Sandra. I'm John Roberts. "THE STORY" with Martha it starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, thank you very much to you both. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And right now, on THE STORY, as President Biden says with troops amassed on Ukraine's border, Putin "has to do something." And his guesses that Putin quote will move in. Fox confirms that the United States is prepping plans to evacuate our people from the embassy there if needed. That is Secretary of State Tony, Antony Blinken, spends 90 minutes with Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov. Lavrov saying, well, he, he's going to wait for the U.S. response to the Russian proposals that they put forward and see what the U.S. says about them. For his part, Secretary Blinken says, "There is no trade space there, none."\par \par \uc2 OK. So, what happens now, as some see Europe on the brink of war, a moment not seen since World War II. Others question, why we would send billions of dollars to Ukraine for this effort? Former National Security Adviser under President Trump, Robert O'Brien, stands by Victor Davis Hanson also joining us in a moment. But first the State Department Correspondent Benjamin Hall in Geneva where this meeting took place. Good afternoon, Ben.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. Yes, there were no major breakthroughs here in Geneva today. And that was to be expected. The bar had been set very low. The one tangible thing that did come out of it seemed to be this U.S. concession, Secretary Blinken finally agreeing to give Russia those the response to his demands and his concerns. But just a few days ago, Secretary Lincoln had been saying he would definitely not give a written response because the concerns were not true. They were not based in fact. I asked him about this earlier. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALL: Secretary, Foreign Minister Lavrov sit top at you, looked you in the eye and tell you effectively tells you lies to your face. And if so, why human with a response --\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, SECRETARY OF STATE: When it comes to the conversations we have. I think the charitable interpretation would be that sometimes we in Russia have different interpretations in history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALL: Nevertheless, by agreeing to these letters, which will be delivered next week, Secretary Blinken has perhaps bought a little more time for diplomacy. And the two sides agreed to meet again in the coming weeks, perhaps leading up to another President Biden-Putin meeting. But as the two sides talk, Russia continues to mass its troops posturing for its invasion, while destabilizing Ukraine from within. And its economy right now is crumbling. And as talks go on cracks are also appearing between NATO allies as to how they should respond. But all in all, today's meeting went as expected.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Blinken offered the Russians a diplomatic off ramps saying again, there would be consequences if Russia invaded. Foreign Minister Lavrov then accusing U.S. of hysterics and provoking Ukraine. U.S. officials have told Fox News intelligence seems to suggest the Russians will invade. We don't know when, how, or where that will be. But suddenly, that seems to be the impression and the administration at the moment. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Benjamin Hall, thank you very much in Geneva. Robert O'Brien is Former National Security Adviser under President Trump and he joins me now. Good to see you, Ambassador O'Brien, thank you for being here today. So, what is your read on this? Because the words matter less than the actions. So, what do you expect that Russia will actually do next?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O'BRIEN, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: Well, though, we got here that when we have to look at the road that we took to get here, and the road is we gave Russia Nord Stream II for nothing. It was their number one foreign policy objective. We gave them an extension of new start, the nuclear arms treaty for nothing, something they wanted their number two foreign policy objective.\par \par \uc2 And then wanting more Vladimir Putin decided to put a gun to the head of the West by moving troops to Ukraine and threatening to invade if he didn't get more guarantees on which countries could or couldn't be eventually members of NATO, what arms could be placed in the current NATO member countries. And so, he's developed an appetite for, for us concessions and the time for appeasement to the Russians is over.\par \par \uc2 They're looking at our words carefully, but they're also going to look at actions and I think there are three things that we need to do. Number one, we need to move the 250 M1-A1 Abrams tanks that Poland has ordered. We need to get those to Poland immediately. Reassure allies and Poland, but also other allies and NATO that were behind them. We need to move a bunch of the troops.\par \par \uc2 So, we've got we've got over 30,000 troops in Germany, Germany's not paying their fair share for NATO. They're paying well under two percent. They're trending towards one percent of their GDP. And the American taxpayers are paying for Germany's defense. We need to move those troops, at least a significant Number of those troops into Poland again to reassure allies and NATO on the Eastern Front and to send a message to the Russians that they're not coming through our NATO allies in the East.\par \par \uc2 And number three, we have to make it very clear that if the Russians decide to invade even just a little bit, even a minor incursion, that the consequences to their economy will be devastating. They'll be cut off in the world banking system, the soccer teams, and Belgravia, apartments, and yachts that their oligarchs have will be seized. And they'll have no other choice but to become a junior partner to China, for the, for the foreseeable future. So, we need to take strong actions now. Though the words, unfortunately, the words now are not enough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's very interesting to me when you talk about that minor incur, incursion language. And then today, you have Biden's saying that Russia could reprise their gray zone tactics from 2014, which was deploying paramilitaries or Russian soldiers who aren't wearing Russian uniforms, right?\par \par \uc2 So, a little bit of move here, a little bit of move here, and a little bit of gray area in terms of who these folks actually are, which is very interesting. When you look at President Biden's language about what he would not tolerate, and he said he would not tolerate, quote, an official tank rolling Russian uniformed operations killing Ukrainians would not be allowed. What do you see in that?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, I think the President's comments were a mistake. I think he realizes that their mistake, they've sent Vice President Kamala Harris out to try and walk them back. They've sent Jen Psaki out to try and walk them back in. And I think the President made an honest mistake when he talked about a mi -- minor incursion into to Ukraine. The problem is, the Russians have watched what happened in Afghanistan, they watched the concessions we've already given them. They've watched us being somewhat weak on China.\par \par \uc2 And so, they think there's room to maneuver there. And the President's comments confirmed it. It reminded me of Ambassador April Gillespie back in July 1990, when she told Saddam Hussein that the US doesn't take a position on Arab borders. And Saddam read that as a, as a clear green light to invade Kuwait. From the US. It wasn't I don't think the President's statement was a green light to the Russians to invade. I think it was an honest mistake. But now we have to have actions, not words to declare it up for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, how far should the United States be willing to go in terms of military support? Because I think a lot of people look at this situation. And on the one hand, President Biden says, well, there's not going to be any entry to NATO anytime soon for Ukraine. So, if that is the fact, why would we even consider a mill, any sort of military support for Ukraine? A lot of people look at this and say, why, why do we care about their borders?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, we got to show a little more concern for our own borders. But, but having said that --look, the Ukrainians are friends, and they're not asking for us to fight for them. The Ukrainians are pretty tough people. I've been there. They want the tools necessary to fight the Russians. They want us to be the arsenal of democracy. I can't tell you how much they'd like that $80 billion of military equipment we left for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine if the Ukrainians had that? Unfortunately, they don't. But they'll fight for themselves. What we need to do, though, for our NATO allies, for Poland, for Romania, for Bulgaria, is we need to be forward deployed to let the Russians know that they're not coming into NATO, and that they're not this isn't going to be a slippery slope, like it was with in 1938.\par \par \uc2 When the Germans got sedate, and land in Czechoslovakia, and then took all of Czechoslovakia and then moved with the Russians, by the way into Poland and other countries. We need to let the Russians know that, that we're firmly behind our NATO allies. I don't think the Ukrainians are asking us to fight for them. I think they're asking for the equipment necessary for them to fight for themselves to protect their own country. We got to make sure they've got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What is the national security interest for the United States in this fight?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, the RNs our primary national security interests in Europe is making sure that NATO is defended. And, and so we don't want to have Russia over running non-NATO friends and putting more pressure on our NATO allies. That's number one. Number two, the Chinese are watching this very carefully. Xi Jinping is watching every move Vladimir Putin takes and is considering that as he decides whether to invade Taiwan after the Olympics or not. Now, Taiwan is a massive national security interest in the United States.\par \par \uc2 It's the cork in the Pacific. If Taiwan falls, the, the PLA Navy flows out into the Pacific, threatens Hawaii, threatens, you know, even California, the Aleutian Islands and Alaska cuts are allies in Japan off from, from Australia, New Zealand and the Philippines, our Thailand are treaty allies. So, we need to make sure that that the idea that you can just that nation states can invade their neighbors and take them over by conquest is not the new norm in international relations because it's going to have consequences far beyond Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Robert O'Brien, Former National Security Adviser to President Trump, thank you very much. Always good to see you, Ambassador. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Great to be with you, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Also, joining me now is Victor Davis Hanson, Senior Fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 . Victor, great to have you with us as well. If I may, I'd like to put up on the screen in New York post op-ed headline that reads, Biden acts much more like Putin's puppet than Trump ever did. And when you take a look at the treatment and all of the concern and so many corners that President Trump was cozying up to President Putin, when you look at the actual movements, in terms of what's gone on here, and also the fact that we now have twice as much Russian oil that we bring into this country and pay for than we did last year. How do you assess it?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, SENIOR FELLOW HOOVER INSTITUTION: Well, the beginning of wisdom is to see how we got here, as Mr. O'Brien said, and we've got to remember that Putin did this in 2014 when he invaded Crimea, and eastern Ukraine, and why did he do that? Because we had appeased him with reset. And we remember the Hot Mic conversation that Obama had where he said he was going to be flexible, and we dismantled Eastern European missile defense, and that encouraged Putin two and a half years later to go in. Then we have four years, where the President was called a puppet.\par \par \uc2 As you said, I think John Brennan and clapper variously called him a traitor and treasonous. But as you said, we flooded the world with oil that Russia didn't like. We took out so Khomeini Baghdadi, we destroyed ISIS, we killed 200 Russian mercenaries that attacked us in Syria. We got out of an asymmetrical missile deal. We stood up to North Korea, we got out of the Iran deal. And Putin came away with the idea that we were unpredictable as we beefed up us defenses and made NATO spend an extra 100 million.\par \par \uc2 So, he thought it was not a good thing in a cost benefit analysis. Then we had this administration, we went right back to the Obama and what did we do? We betrayed our allies and just scampered out of Afghanistan without giving them notice. That was not a big advertisement for NATO solidarity. And then in addition to that, Biden, you mentioned these conversations, he has in these press conferences, where he sees he seems on shore.\par \par \uc2 But people haven't pointed out that Putin watches the domestic scene, when he sees the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs call his Chinese counterpart and warn him that he might have to tip him off in case of a national emergency or when the Secretary of Defense and the Chairman of Joint Chiefs says their primary goal is to address climate change and white privilege or supremacy, rather than Bill a battlefield readiness. Putin gets encouraged. And he's back to where he was in 2014.\par \par \uc2 So, how do we determine the only thing I'm worried about? Martha, is he wants Ukraine not to be a NATO? And how do you what crane hasn't been a NATO for 30 years? It's the same we're five million people were killed in World War Two fighting for Russia. So. it's been a part of Russia? And is it in our interest to make it a NATO member? We don't want to say we're not going to make it because us tell us to, but we haven't made him a member for 30 years. And there's been reasons why.\par \par \uc2 I don't think people believe in Europe, and the coffee shops of Florence or an Amsterdam that they're going to fight Russians if they invade NATO Ukraine. And that's what would happen if, if Ukraine was a member right now of NATO, and it evoked Article Five at every single NATO country had to come to us defense to deter Russia, I think you'd see the end of NATO. So, an alliance is not just formal.\par \par \uc2 It's not just -- its actuality, until NATO members believe that Ukraine belongs in Europe, and they're willing to defend it with its life. If you emulate or fake that reality, then it's even more disastrous. And that's what we're doing. We're sort of saying, well, we're all going to protect Ukraine, and maybe someday we'll be part of NATO. But you Putin, given the history of our actions and those of Europeans, he doesn't believe that. And that's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 It would unwrap -- once you invoke Article Five, and people don't come to your fence, you have no alliances. And that would be the case right now, if Ukraine was in NATO, not that I would approve of it. But that's just a realist perception.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It great, great, great points, as always, Victor Davis Hanson, thank you very much. Nailed it. In terms of what's going on here and the dynamic and how Putin sees it, and why he's not that concerned in a lot of ways. Thank you, Victor.\par \par \uc2 So, think about this for a moment President Biden won 63 percent of the Latino vote in 2020. But recent polling shows that he now has only 28 percent of their support. So, what's going on there? That shift that is underway, could change the electoral map in this country. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, developing now, 1000s of pro-life activists at the Supreme Court as justices consider a Mississippi law that bans abortions at 15 weeks, with the potential to bring about the biggest change to abortion law since the precedent that was set by Roe v. Wade 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 A decision from the 6-3 conservative majority could come as early as June, but really no sign which way this is going to go at this point. That, of course, would be right in the shadow of the midterm elections as they come in November.\par \par \uc2 And in 2020, President Biden resoundingly won the Hispanic vote as a chain of Democrats behind him have as well in recent history got 63 percent to then-President Trump's 35.\par \par \uc2 We've seen those kinds of numbers shake out in many elections over the past 20 years.\par \par \uc2 But now, one year in, look at this shift that is going on. Only 28 percent of Hispanic voters say that they approve of the job that the president is doing. As Latinos, like the rest of those polled, reveal a shift to the right.\par \par \uc2 You saw the numbers that we've been showing to you. It was negative seven. Democrat -- positive seven. Democrat I.D. now positive seven, GOP I.D. across the country.\par \par \uc2 So, our panel is going to join us on this in a moment. But first, let's go to Fox Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn live in Washington. Hi, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Well, mayors really have a front-row seat how the -- how policies made here in Washington actually impact Americans every day. And we've had several Republican mayors tell us today, they think Republicans have a good shot at winning over even more Hispanic voters in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: What do you think is drawing that demographic to support Republican candidates when they haven't always in the past?\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ, MAYOR OF MIAMI, FLORIDA: I think it's because we have a very simple message, and the message is, keep taxes as low as humanly possible, making sure that the kind of economy that we have as an economy for our children and our grandchildren. And I think that's something that is a message that resonates with Hispanics.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (voice-over): A Wall Street Journal poll -- Journal poll found that right now, Hispanic voters have a negative outlook on the economy. Right now, 63 percent think it is headed in the wrong direction. And Republicans into 2022 are trying to capitalize on gains that former President Trump made in 2020.\par \par \uc2 A New York Times op-ed explains Trump's gain among Latinos. Saying this, "Donald Trump made inroads with Latino voters because he and Republican governors kept the economy open during the pandemic, cut taxes, distributed stimulus checks, secured the border, and expedited vaccine development."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): And Martha, the campaign arm of the Democratic Party is planning on spending $30 million to try to win back Latino voters in 2022. But all that money isn't going to do anything if voters are not buying into the Biden agenda. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): We will see. Hillary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now is Salena Zito, Washington Examiner, and national political reporter. And Jose Aristimundo, former deputy national press secretary of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOSE ARISTIMUNDO, FORMER DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'd like to start by putting up the percentage of Latino vote over the last several presidential elections, because with the exception of Mitt Romney, seen sort of a steady growth over the course of that period.\par \par \uc2 And after the Romney -- oh, he was pretty close to President Trump. But McCain, as you can see at 31 percent. So, after that they did an autopsy, sort of, of the Republican Party, and tried to figure out why they weren't reaching minority voters in a -- in a stronger way.\par \par \uc2 So, Jose, it would appear that, that, that seems to be working. What do you think about this really substantial shift that we're seeing in the Latino vote right now?\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNDO: Look, I think, first and foremost, Democrats have to recognize that we have to do a better job in messaging to Latinos to -- you know, to showcase what Biden has done for them.\par \par \uc2 We've created over 6 million jobs to put unemployment rate down to historic rates. With -- you know, we vaccinated over 208 million Americans. So, there are good things for Latino that are happened. We protected Venezuelan-American, Central Americans with TPS.\par \par \uc2 So, we've done good things. But I think, the DNC Democrats, we have to do a better job in messaging the Latino community.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, why -- so, Salena, why do you see -- I know you spent a lot of time across the country and in small towns and 