c2 ARISTIMUNDO: Latinos are American as anybody else. So, I agree with that 100 percent. But I think what we -- what we also have to recognize that you have to treat them respectfully.\par \par \uc2 Look at the way President Trump treat the Latinos from day one. These are gangsters, they're -- you know -- they're doing bad things, they're criminals. That's not the way Joe Biden has never treated Latinos. Latinos are very much of the American (INAUDIBLE). We're very proud of that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But they don't seem to have -- they don't seem feel that way, because look at the way they're going to -- they save their vote.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNDO: Martha, it's early -- it's early.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What the way you're putting on them isn't necessarily what they're telling us in these polls. They don't feel that way.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNDO: Look, look, if we learned anything in 2016, polls don't mean a thing. They go numbers go up and down. But what we can ignore is this (INAUDIBLE) over 6 million jobs, many of them Latino jobs.\par \par \uc2 That what matters. I'm not worried about the polls. They come up and down. Remember, it's a long way. In the political life, remember it's a long way. We're going to continue messaging Latinos that were here. This administration going to be here, we're going to be here to protect them, and to ensure -- and to continue to ensure that they're taken care of when it comes to the economy, when it comes to climate, and when it comes to creating jobs?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I think they want to be, you know, talked to like everybody else wants to be talked to in terms of the direction overall of the country and where we're headed.\par \par \uc2 But I really thank you both for coming because I think this is one of the big stories of the coming election cycles, both 2022 and 2024. In terms of where this goes.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jose, and thank you, Salena. Always good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNDO: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNDO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, after President Biden chuckles, and then calls a perfectly on-point question from our own Jacqui Heinrich stupid, we're going to take a look -- we'll show you that.\par \par \uc2 And then we're going to take a look at some of these other exchanges and kind of dive into this president's prickly relationship with reporters and talk to someone who covers all of the media quite closely.\par \par \uc2 Joe Concha about what he sees developing in this trend. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What the hell -- what do you do all the time?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the thing that always amazed me about the questions, and I apologize for having been short.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Wow video from Los Angeles, oh my goodness, motorcyclists crashing into a car after going upwards of 130 miles an hour. We have positivity of impact that sent the rider cartwheeling into the air more than 100 feet to his death.\par \par \uc2 Now the LAPD tells Fox that that motorcycle was a stolen vehicle, and that the officers were tracking it from a helicopter, but had determined that they should not pursue it behind it because it might be too dangerous. And you look at what happened, the two individuals who were just innocently driving this car at the time are also hospitalized. We're waiting for an update on their condition.\par \par \uc2 So media coverage of President Biden's time in office kind of taking a turn over the past couple of weeks as his agenda has stalled and crises have mounted at home and abroad, leading to some moments like these where the President kind of lost his cool.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, can I ask you a quick question on Israel before you drive away since it's so important?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: No, you can't. Not unless you get in front of the car as I step on it. I'm only teasing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why are you so confident he'll change his behavior, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not confident he'll change his behavior. What the hell, what do you do all the time?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Veterans Affairs is going to have a mandate for its healthcare.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You are suck a pain in the neck but I'm going to answer your question, because we've each other for so long.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think that that might incentivize more people to come over illegally?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you guys keep sending that garbage out, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, on Afghanistan --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to answer on Afghanistan now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in Joe Concha, Media and Politics Columnist for The Hill and Fox News Contributor. Joe, a lot of spicy back and forth, some of those go all the way back to May. We do see that from time, we saw it, you know, occasionally from President Trump as well. Your thoughts on how this relationships going, one you're in.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the problem is that this isn't grouchy Mr. Hollingsworth down the block that we're talking about, this is the president of United States who said to his staff on day one, Martha, "If you're ever working with me and I hear you treat another colleague with disrespect, talk down to someone. I promise you, I will fire you on the spot."\par \par \uc2 But time and again, as we saw again during his press conference this week, that wasn't in that montage, or with Jacqui Heinrich who asked a perfectly reasonable question. Jacqui Heinrich, by the way, who's highly respected and decorated, he's yelling at reporters or he's mocking them for asking these questions. And, by the way, the louder someone gets, the more defensive and insecure they appear. And why wouldn't he be insecure at this point when polls show that what, 7 in 10 Americans don't want him to run again in 2024. So overall, from a communications perspective, Martha, this president continually mistakes volume for decisiveness.\par \par \uc2 Think of the way Charles Krauthammer, the late great Charles Krauthammer used to make an argument, never raised his voice and yet was tremendously compelling and effective, even to those predispositions to disagree with him. But Mr. Biden, these situations comes across as awkward and angry, it ain't a good combination and it's only going to get worse as accountability rises from the press and his polling continues to crater.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe Concha, always good to see you. Joe, thank you very much. Great thoughts.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Happy weekend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Coming up still ahead, you too, the incredible story of a former Afghan Army soldier who fought alongside our US forces who has now received a warm welcome from his new neighbors in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAYLEE JOSEPH, FLORIDIAN: We were just imagining what it's like to lay in their new bed here in America and not hear bombs going off or be fearful for their safety overnight. And especially now to have a family just didn't know that America is offering that to him is so, I feel so proud of that and it makes me really proud to be an American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Arizona's attorney general says that he's not intimidated by the Biden administration's threat to pull COVID-19 relief funds to Arizona because they refuse to make masks mandatory in their school systems. So in a letter to Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen, the State AG writes, "It shouldn't surprise anyone that the \uc2 US Department of Treasury\uc2  is again trying to overstep its constitutional bounds and dictate Arizona how our state should run and fund our schools.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Republican Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  joins me now with some news to break on this story. Governor, great to have you with us. Good afternoon to you. So, what is the next move here between Arizona and the federal government on this fight?\par \par \uc2 DOUG DUCEY\uc2 , ARIZONA GOVERNOR: Good to see you, Martha, and thank you for having me. Arizona is going to file a lawsuit against the Biden administration, against this federal overreach and continued bullying and attacks on our state. We are going to use the dollars that Congress has appropriated to catch our kids up in school. And we want the dollars to go to the families that need to get their child inside a classroom that is functioning with a teacher in front.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden needs to focus on the border, or helping Ukraine or fixing Afghanistan, why doesn't he stay in his federal lane and let us educate the children in the state of Arizona?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what I find fascinating about this, governor, and I know you want that those dollars to be portable, so parents can say, well, this schools open and this is what their rules are, and that's where I want my child to go. But what I find fascinating about this is the $182 billion that went to school systems across the country, and they were supposed to use it for testing and mitigation, and all of these things. And it doesn't feel like. And then President Biden said, well, you know what, we weren't too happy with what's happened to some of the money. But once we give it to them, it's their money to spend the way they want.\par \par \uc2 So why is it that the only place where it seems like they're actually watching where the money goes, and now they're trying to smack your hand over is Arizona.\par \par \uc2 DUCEY: There's been no accountability to the unions for the amount of money that they've been given. But yes, they continue to focus on Arizona. And for some reason, this president is obsessed with masks and with mandates, and he has a very little focus on math, or reading, or writing. Those are the things we're trying to get our kids caught up with along with American civics, so they can truly understand federalism. This president needs a lesson in it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, this is the issue that I have been really focused on because I don't understand why the Department of Education's number one priority isn't catching these kids up, because they're all over a year, you know, months behind in some cases, and perhaps as much as a year and others. So are you going to have summer school or are you going to have longer school days? What's the plan to try to actually make sure that your kids are where they need to be, so they don't get anxiety over all of this?\par \par \uc2 DUCEY: What I believe the Department of Education's number one priority is trying to please these government unions. If you look at their decision making and the lack of accountability, that's what you see. But I want you to know in Arizona, we are going to have a summer camp. We're going to launch it in the June, July, August timeframe. And the focus is going to be catching our kids up.\par \par \uc2 We want to lead the nation in eliminating learning loss. And we're going to focus on math, reading and American civics with quality teachers inside flexible settings to accommodate our families across the state. Because the last couple years have been tough on our kids, and especially our kids of color, our kids in poor and low income areas, the pandemic has not been good for them, distance learning has not been good for them.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, we're getting them back in the classroom with a teacher at the head of the classroom. And if a school system will not work with us, we're giving families flexible dollars so they can find a school anywhere in the state that will serve their child well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know what I think what you're doing is fascinating, and we're going to follow the progress in Arizona and see how these children do, you know, in the coming years. And whether or not they're more caught up in Arizona than they are in other places because of what you're doing to give people choice and also some extra schooling to catch up over the summer. Nobody likes to go to school in the summer but you're giving them a gift to get caught up, which I think is going to relieve a lot of anxiety that these kids have over slipping behind as well.\par \par \uc2 Governor Ducey, thank you. Very good to talk to you today.\par \par \uc2 DUCEY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll be watching that program.\par \par \uc2 DUCEY: You too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, sir. So an Afghan soldier who fought alongside our US forces, evacuated the country on one of the very last flights out, with his wife. His baby was born on board the plane. And now their American Dream is beginning in Florida. This incredible story, an Afghan war veteran, Congressman Michael Waltz, whose district they're from, joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAIHAN, FORMER AFGHAN INTERPRETER: We are still thinking about those people, like what if I was there, if I were them, stuck with my family, with my newborn baby, having nothing for it and still just go hide and save yourself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This breaking moments ago, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has just released details of a notebook that they found with the body of Brian Laundrie, which they say revealed written statements by him claiming responsibility for the death of Gabby Petito in August of last year. Brian's body was later found in October after an extensive manhunt.\par \par \uc2 In late August, one of the final US military evacuation flights departed from Afghanistan carrying \uc2 US Army\uc2  ally Kaihan and his pregnant wife. As the plane touched down in Qatar, Kaihan's wife gave birth on that transport plane to a baby girl. And now thanks in part to a chance conversation with Fox, the family is settling into their new home in Florida. National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin reports.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Martha, it started with a conversation I had with a good Samaritan in Jacksonville, Florida who wanted to help Afghan evacuees. I connected her to the Independence Fund who matched her with this amazing Afghan family. Here's their story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Baby Dunya, whose name means world in Dari, was one of the Afghan babies born onboard US military evacuation flights last August.\par \par \uc2 KAIHAN: I was sweating in the aircraft like shooting an aircraft like, so like gosh, please don't make it happen here in the airplane. She feel pain and she said like it's getting worse and right the aircraft landed so was the right time.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Dunya was born as the C17 landed in Qatar. Five months later, she and her family arrived to a warm reception at a VFW Hall in Jacksonville, Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just amazing what they probably been through.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Now one step closer to achieving the American dream, thanks to the generosity of Carol Williams of Jacksonville, Florida, she committed herself to helping refugees when she lost her baby daughter in 1968.\par \par \uc2 CAROL WILLIAMS, KAIHAN FAMILY SPONSOR: I want us to stay together as Americans, and show what America really stands for.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Williams rallied her community to welcome Kaihan and his family providing a fully furnished house and paying their rent for a year, including utilities. They donated a used car and started a college fund for Dunya. In another twist of fate, Williams and Baby Dunya share the same birthday.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Kaihan is going to run circles around most of us. He is going to be the American dream.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Kaihan graduated from Afghanistan's West Point and was first brought to the US in 2017 by the US military to complete an English Immersion Program at Lackland Air Force Base in Texas. He was certified as a Blackhawk helicopter pilot.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH: What he did is greater than anything we could ever thank him for.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Kaihan has the following wish for his new daughter.\par \par \uc2 KAIHAN: I love like she be a F35 pilot.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: He and his wife, Shamila, are now adjusting to their new life in Jacksonville, grateful now that they are safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The kind of good news story we all need on this Friday, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jennifer, I'm so glad we're able to connect those people and start that ball rolling. What a wonderful, wonderful story. Carol is a remarkable person and so is his family. Thank you very much, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 So Florida Congressman Michael Waltz serves a district that is not far from where this family resettled. He's a former Green Beret commander and serve multiple tours in Afghanistan, has a deep personal connection to these stories. Congressman, it's great to have you with us and great to share that story with our viewers.\par \par \uc2 You know, we've had a lot of tough stories, people who didn't get out. But this is a good one.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Right. No, it is a good one. I'm so proud of my hometown of Jacksonville, Florida for opening their arms and opening their great city to our allies. I think every American was just so appalled at this betrayal. And the way this withdrawal was done. And it just warms my heart, and I think yours too, to see so many people stepping up with their own money in their own resources.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, in a bigger picture, these veterans groups that have done so much to get Americans out and to get our allies out are still at it, Martha. Many of them have quit their jobs. They've left -- they've exhausted their savings. I just spoke with one this morning. They have a flight over there now. There are still Americans over there that they're getting out and legal permanent residents, not to mention the thousands of our Afghan allies. And they're doing it despite the State Department, Defense Department with very little help from their own government.\par \par \uc2 They're not going to give up. I'm not going to give up. And I'm just really thrilled to see that our great Floridians opening, you know, again, opening their hearts and homes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, imagine what we could accomplish if these great veterans groups could work in tandem with the State Department leaning on each other, especially when it comes to all of this money that's going over there to help people. I know that you feel strongly that they would do a better job of it.\par \par \uc2 Just a couple seconds, but you're hoping for a fast citizenship program for people like Kaihan, right.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: I'll be introducing legislation. He was a Blackhawk pilot. Many of these pilots and well trained Afghans want to join the US military. And if they're willing to step up yet again and serve with us, they shouldn't be at the bottom of the pile when it comes to citizenship. I'll be introducing that this year.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, the State Department should be in a public private partnership with these veterans groups, working with them and giving them a part of those hundreds of millions of aid not working against them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Congressman, thanks you thank you so much. Always good to see you. Michael waltz from Florida.\par \par \uc2 So we are awaiting an update from law enforcement officials in Texas on what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is now calling an act of anti-Semitism after an attack on a synagogue there in which four people were held hostage. We're going to break the news out of Colleyville, Texas on that story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just one of the many Meat Loaf videos that I watched this morning when I heard this sad news, that the Grammy Award winning singer Meat Loaf had passed at the age of 74. "Two Out of Three Ain't Bad," it's just one of his many hits. It's off the "Bat Out of Hell" from 1977 which was the soundtrack of a generation of teenagers. And it's one of the bestselling albums of all time still to this day.\par \par \uc2 We're told that he died surrounded by his wife and two daughters and that the cause of his death was not released. This statement says, from his heart to you souls don't ever stop rocking. Meat Loaf 1947-2022.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" of this Friday, January 21, 2022. We will look forward to seeing you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend. In the meantime, thanks for joining us today. Your World starts right now. Take care, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220122ei1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Daily Cases Spike Amid High Demand For Tests; Actress Betty White Dies At Age 99; Colorado Wildfires Destroyed Hundreds Of Homes; Biden Warns Putin Of Possible Sanctions Against Russia; Republicans Say Biden Needs To Be More Aggressive; Chief Justice Roberts Calls For Judicial Independence; New York City Preparations Underway; New Year Celebrations Preparing Across U.S.; Airlines In U.S. Facing Increasing Cancellations And Delays Due To Staffing Shortages; Media Coverage Of January 6th Capitol Hill Riots, Biden Administration, And Trials Of Kyle Rittenhouse And Jussie Smollett Examined; Biden Administration Facing Criticism Over Inflation, Immigration, And Foreign Policy; House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  States Version Of Build Back Better Legislation May Still Pass Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Jeff Paul, Nicole Saphier, William La Jeunesse, Rich  Edson, Laura Ingle, Mark Meredith, Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 7,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 December 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: 2022.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Mike Emanuel in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, as we come on air this evening, it's now Midnight in Paris and Berlin and people across Europe are already wishing good riddance to 2021. We will have a live report from New York's Times Square and what promised to -- promises to be a toned-down ball drop.\par \par \uc2 But first, health officials warning the new spike in daily COVID cases is also fueling a surge in hospitalizations in some cities across the nation. The vast majority of those hospitalizations among the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And as many Americans look forward to the new year they find themselves having a hard time finding COVID tests, a problem that could last into 2022.\par \par \uc2 And now, many public-school districts say they will require COVID testing for students and staff. Let's go to our experts that we have with us this evening Dr. Nicole Saphier, Fox News contributor and former assistant Health Secretary Admiral \uc2 Brett Giroir\uc2 . Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SAPHIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: From the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , the U.S. breaks its single day virus case record hospitalizations and deaths. However, have not followed the same dramatic increase in the past two weeks. Deaths are down by five percent with a daily average of 1,221 while hospitalizations increased by just 15 percent to an average of 78,781 per day.\par \par \uc2 While you obviously want to track cases, Dr. Saphier, our hospitalization and fatalities, really more meaningful metrics.\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: Well, absolutely. And as of right now, as you can see, we need to really change our mindset and stop focusing on these daily new case counts.\par \par \uc2 Because as we know, the virus is everywhere, it is impressive how highly transmissible and contagious this is.\par \par \uc2 But what is more important at this point is how many people are being hospitalized and how many people are dying from this virus.\par \par \uc2 And while there is still a very high number, unfortunately, what we haven't been able to do throughout the entire course of this pandemic is have true figures, a good idea of which people are in the hospital because of COVID. And which people are in the hospital for various other reasons who are just testing positive for COVID. We still don't even have that. And that has been something that's been skirted around a lot.\par \par \uc2 But right now with Omicron, highly contagious variant. But the good news is it does still seem to be having more mild effects on people and that should be the messaging going forward as we head into 2022.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Here is a clip from CDC director, \uc2 Dr. Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CDC: We know a PCR test would not be viable because that PCR can stay positive for up to 12 weeks. We don't know how this antigen performs with regard to predicting transmissibility towards the end of your period of infection.\par \par \uc2 So, with a negative antigen test, we'd still ask you to wear a mask. With a positive antigen test, we'd still say you could wear a mask. And since it wasn't going to make a difference in our recommendations, we did not recommend an antigen at that period of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Admiral, did the CDC just further confuse everyone with these updated isolation guidelines?\par \par \uc2 ADM. BRETT GIROIR\uc2 , FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY HEALTH & HUMAN SERVICES: Well, they've certainly confused me and they themselves are confused with the whole testing situation.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think it's very appropriate that they finally are balancing just pure infection control with the needs of other public health, mental illness, kids getting back to school, cancer screenings and keeping the economy and society intact.\par \par \uc2 So, I am fully supportive of decreasing the amount of isolation time. What I'm not supportive of is they're trying to finesse their lack of testing, their lack of preparation for testing by saying the test really don't predict, that's entirely wrong.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's part of the argument is that if you have COVID, after five days, if your antigen test is positive, you are fully transmissible to other people. That's what they're trying not to tell you. But that are -- that is the facts, and I think it just belies the whole underlying problem with testing that they let sink for eight months before they restarted the machine.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Dr. Saphier, there's obviously an abundance of cases. What's the responsible thing to do if somebody you know is had COVID in terms of family and friends?\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: Well, you know, as the Admiral is saying, first of all, with those rapid antigen tests at home, if they're negative, but you have symptoms and you've been around people with COVID, you can -- you can certainly have COVID.\par \par \uc2 A negative rapid test doesn't mean that being sick, you should just go out and be with other people. If you're sick, stay home.\par \par \uc2 We had that mantra pre-COVID, we certainly need it now with COVID. If you have symptoms, if you've had close contact with others, then make sure that you either get tested, stay home and do not go around others when you are having symptoms, whether you have a positive rapid antigen test or not.\par \par \uc2 I agree with the Admiral that while the rapid antigen test has had a very positive predictive value, meaning the ability to whether or not it's positive or not with someone positive, by saying that the \uc2 NIH\uc2  saying now maybe it's not going to be as sensitive or it's not going to be able to predict the positive.\par \par \uc2 You know, I do think that's backtracking because of their lack of available testing. And I think rapid antigen tests are our way through becoming -- out of this pandemic and allowing this virus to be endemic. We need them, and we need them now.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Admiral, you were the testing point person for the Trump administration, a lot of schools are saying they want kids to get tested, probably their staff too, are they going to be able to get those tests?\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: So, I don't think they're going to be able to get the test. But of course, we have new transparency from the administration, when are the tests going to be here, how many, where they're going to go.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I think it would be a poor prioritization. Not that I don't care about school children. But if we're only talking about 500 million rapid tests over a few weeks, or maybe a few months, we really have to make sure that the people who are at high risk, those who could benefit from the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and the Merck drug, have tests available to them so they can test and get -- actually get on therapy.\par \par \uc2 And remember, one test going into school, it only tells you you're negative that one day, you could be positive the next day or the next day. So, I don't think entry testing for school is a wise strategy where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Dr. Saphier, we're up against the clock, but they're talking about boosters for 12 to 15-year-olds, to be honest, I've got two of them. Is the science there for that?\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: No, the science is not there. In fact, that's why the expert panel decided against -- they voted against generalized boosting for the population. But unfortunately, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  went ahead -- went forward with it without the approval of their expert committee, they reconvened. They said, OK, you know what, we are going to just boost the people despite the fact that the external review committee voted against it. And now they're doing it again, it sounds like they're not going to even look at their expert external committee, and they are now looking at boosting this younger population.\par \par \uc2 They need to change their mindset, we need to start focusing on the high risk and the vulnerable, there will be negligible positive clinical effect by boosting these younger, healthier individuals. We should be sending those vaccine doses to other parts of the country and the world that are lacking the vaccines because that is how as a global society, we're going to move forward.\par \par \uc2 It is a detriment to continue to focus on the lowest risk start targeting with high quality mask, with testing and with boosting and vaccines those that are most high risk.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Admiral \uc2 Brett Giroir\uc2 , Dr. Nicole Saphier, thank you so much. Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 SAPHIER: Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Breaking tonight, the death of an icon. Actress Betty White, a beloved fixture on T.V. shows and movies for more than 60 years is dead at the age of 99. White was just weeks away from celebrating her 100th birthday.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden sent his condolences calling White a cultural icon who will be sorely missed.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent William La Jeunesse joins us from our West Coast newsroom with more. Good evening, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 White passed away overnight at her home here in Los Angeles. She was the longest running woman on television, jumping from "The Mary Tyler Moore Show" in the 70s to the "Golden Girls" in the 80s and 90s, receiving Emmy Awards in both.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETTY WHITE, ACTRESS: I'm sorry, Billy (PH), but I cannot do a chocolate souffle with only two cameras.\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, Lou, you're stronger than I am, press your advantage.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: White began her career in 1939. Her first big break came a decade later on a variety show followed by a sitcom and then, a job that changed everything for her.\par \par \uc2 On the game show "Password", she met the love of her life host Allen Ludden. Along with acting, White was passionate about animal welfare, winning a Lifetime Achievement Award from the \uc2 Jane Goodall Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHITE: My life is divided absolutely in half, half show bizness and half animal business and I'll do anything I can to make life better for these guys.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Even as she aged, White stayed young appearing in a Super Bowl commercial and later hosting "Saturday Night Live".\par \par \uc2 WHITE: When I first heard about the campaign to get me to host "Saturday Night Live", I didn't know what \uc2 Facebook\uc2  was. And now that I do know what it is, I have to say, it sounds like a huge waste of time.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: This is the latest issue of People Magazine which obviously went to press before her death. As to her upbeat nature, she said I got it from my mom and that never changed. I always find the positive.\par \par \uc2 She also said the key to happiness is a sense of humor. "You better realize how good life is while it's happening. Because before you know it, it will be gone."\par \par \uc2 Betty White passed away at 99, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A cultural icon, William La Jeunesse, thanks very much. The scenes in Colorado look like something out of a movie. Just North of Denver, fast moving flames engulf entire neighborhoods as tens of thousands of people flee their homes.\par \par \uc2 In less than 24 hours, the fire have already consumed thousands of acres, burning hundreds of homes to the ground.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul has the very latest on the ground in Boulder, Colorado. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Mike, just a day before this entire area was on fire. Now, it's essentially freezing over with the first major snowfall of the season. That's a welcome sign for this devastated community that right now can use this help more than ever.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice over): Running for their lives, thousands in the cities of Superior and Lewisville, Colorado had mere moments to get out before the fast-moving wildfires swept through.\par \par \uc2 VALERIE LAURIZIO, EVACUEE: This is the first time I've really been involved in anything like this. And I'll tell you, it is really scary.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: The largest fires are now smoldering and snow was helping calm the flames. But the damage is enormous. More than 500 homes destroyed and officials say they wouldn't be surprised if that number reached 1,000.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS\uc2  (D-CO): Many families having minutes, minutes to get whatever they could, their pets, their kids into the car and leave. The last 24 hours have been devastating.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: God help us.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: It's really hard to put the destruction into words, you have to see it. You know, there was a home here. Behind that, a home there. This was a massive neighborhood, home after home. A home there, a whole line of them right here, burned down to their bricks and then, you look off into the distance and you see burnt trees, and yet, more homes burned down to the ground and you have to think about the families.\par \par \uc2 Each one of these homes tells a unique story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would imagine this is what a nuclear attack would look like.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my God. The cause of the fire is still under investigation. And while it will take some time to rebuild, there's thanks in all the lives that were saved.\par \par \uc2 JOE PELLE, COUNTY-SHERIFF, BOULDER COUNTY: It's unbelievable when you look at the devastation that we don't have a list of a hundred missing persons and that's a miracle because it would be given the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, there was some confusion surrounding the cause of this fire. Officials originally thought maybe a downed power line caused a spark that causes massive wildfire. That turns out not to be the case but they say still the drying conditions and strong wind gust made this an extremely large and dangerous fire, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Devastating. Jeff Paul live in Boulder, Colorado, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Stock slipped on the final trading day of the year on Wall Street, the Dow lost 60, the S&P 500 fell 13, \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  dropped 97.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up 1-1/10. The S&P 500 gained nearly nine-tenths and the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  dropped a fraction.\par \par \uc2 For the year, the Dow and S&P had their largest one-year point gain on record, the Dow closed 2021 up 18-3/4 percent. The S&P 500 added nearly 27 percent and the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  gained 21-2/5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Up next, details on the lines drawn following President Biden and Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's phone call and where things go from here.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 29 in Philadelphia, as one woman and five men are wounded after more than 60 shots were fired during a shooting in Germantown Thursday night. It's believed that the 21-year-old woman may have been the intended target of the shooting. She is currently in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 Fox 31 in Denver as the truck driver sentenced to 110 years in prison for his role in a deadly crash that killed four people on Interstate 70 receives clemency from the governor.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  announced Rogel Aguilera-Mederos' sentence has been reduced to 10 years. He will be eligible for parole on December 30, 2026.\par \par \uc2 And the big story in Charlotte, North Carolina tonight, South Carolina coach Shane Beamer gets a mayonnaise bath, capping the Gamecocks' celebration after their victory over North Carolina in the Duke's Mayo Bowl Thursday. The cooler was filled with 4-1/2 gallons of mayo. No calories if you use the mayo that way, right?\par \par \uc2 You're looking at the Capitol Dome in Washington and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: With the New Year comes new laws for many states, some notable ones include California becoming the first state requiring a $15 an hour minimum wage for businesses with more than 25 employees.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire will prohibit abortion after 24 weeks of gestation, with exceptions for the mother's life or physical health. But Democrats there have already drafted legislation seeking to repeal the new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 And Montana will legalize recreational marijuana use after state voters approved the measure in November 2020. 17 states in the District of Columbia have already legalized marijuana for recreational usage.\par \par \uc2 We're getting a clearer picture on the substance of yesterday's phone call between President Biden and Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . A call coming amid escalating tension between the world powers and the Russian military buildup near the border of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson is traveling with the president in Wilmington, Delaware. Good evening, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 And just a short while ago, the United States, Australia, Canada, the E.U., U.K., all released a statement condemning Russia for closing a Human Rights Center and a human rights group this week. In that statement, they say that Russia has had a pattern of deepening and systemic repression in that country, just the latest point of contention between the West and the United States and Putin's Russia.\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice-over): Following a late lunch on the Wilmington, Delaware Riverfront, President Biden says he's willing to address Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's concerns on one condition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I made it clear that they only could work if in fact he deescalate.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Russia has massed tens of thousands of troops on its border with Ukraine. If Putin invades Ukraine again, the Biden administration has threatened unprecedented sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin response --\par \par \uc2 YURI USHAKOV\uc2 , KREMLLI AIDE (through translator): That could lead to a complete breakdown in ties between our countries.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Many Republicans say the administration needs to be more aggressive providing weapons to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): They got away with it with the Obama-Biden team in 2014. He thinks he can get away with it now.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Ukraine's foreign minister tweeted, "If the West falters and chooses appeasement, we will still defend ourselves, our right to exist and choose our own future."\par \par \uc2 Russia, China, a global pandemic that won't quit and a series of domestic challenges faced the president heading into 2022. 2021, closes with an Omicron powered surge infecting more Americans than ever, and long lines for COVID tests.\par \par \uc2 The President acknowledges his administration was slow on testing and says he's trying to secure 500 million kits to begin sending out next month. The Supreme Court will also decide whether his administration may require vaccines or weekly COVID tests for workers at larger companies. With Republicans looking to make the Biden COVID response, an issue for the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAUDIA TENNEY\uc2  (R-NY): The lockdowns, restrictions, vaccine mandates, people are recognizing and I think it's going to be the defining issue next year and why the Republicans will take back the House at least.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The White House says its vaccine campaign will continue keeping Americans out of the hospitals and alive. Democrats are also touting securing personal protective equipment and passing trillions in COVID rescue and infrastructure spending.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Our country could not be more -- it could not be better served than with this most experienced capable hands than yours, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Democrats had hoped to close the year by passing their social spending plan. One to address education, healthcare, and climate change, though with the thinnest of majorities in the Senate, and the objection of Senator Joe Manchin. Democrats say they'll have to try to cobble together a less ambitious plan in the New Year. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Rich, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has a New Year's Eve message. In a year-end report he just posted, what's the message?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Well, the message here Mike comes as there has been significant criticism of some of the courts' decisions recently. He is asserting the chief justice the independence of the court.\par \par \uc2 In his message that just posted, he writes, "The judiciary's power to manage its internal affairs insulates courts from inappropriate political influence, and is crucial to preserving public trust in its work as a separate and co-equal branch of government."\par \par \uc2 This also comes at this time that some Democrats are pushing to add seats to the highest court. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Rich Edson, live in Wilmington, Delaware. Rich, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the travel nightmare at airports across the nation continues. Is there an end in sight?\par \par \uc2 Plus, Times Squares welcome backs -- welcomes back patrons to celebrate New Years, but it still won't look like years past.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight, at least 12 Yemeni troops were killed in an apparent friendly fire airstrike in Yemen. Authority say Thursday's airstrike by a Saudi-led coalition also injured eight soldiers and burned three military vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Fighting is escalated in recent months between Iran-backed Houthi rebels and Yemeni government forces. Armed attacks and looting at three warehouses in northern Darfur have prompted the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  to suspend aid deliveries across Sudan.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . agency says the theft of nearly 5,000 metric ton of food will endanger the nutritional needs of as many as 2 million people.\par \par \uc2 And easy come easy go for 2,000 bank account holders in Britain, the bank Santander is blaming a technical issue for the accidental deposit of $176 million across 75,000 transactions. The bank says the duplicate deposit mistake was rectified but not before some of the payments went into the accounts of rival banks.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Power preparations are underway in New York City for tonight's scaled-back ball drop in Times Square. It's a welcome sight after crowds were banned altogether last year.\par \par \uc2 But the return of revelers means added safety measures. Senior correspondent Laura Ingle joins us tonight from Times Square. Good evening, Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Mike, you know, those who want to bring in the New Year here in Times Square have to follow some pretty strict rules if they want to be a part of the big countdown.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, you know, it was no crowds last year, this year, we're finally seeing some numbers in this area, let show you what is happening.\par \par \uc2 INGLE (voice-over): This will be a scaled-back celebration tonight, with only 15,000 screened revelers allowed to take part in this historic event.\par \par \uc2 Now, that number is down from the usual 58,000 headcount that we have seen in years past. In addition to being checked for weapons, New Years, partiers will have to show proof of full vaccination, of photo I.D., and wear a mask to make it through that screening process.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I've come here specific for the -- for the New Year's Eve, I'm so excited.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am not worried at all. I'm vaccinated, right? I'm vaccinated. So, I'm ready to go.\par \par \uc2 INGLE: And while the warmer temperatures and high rates of vaccinated people has made the mood pretty merry here, there is the uncomfortable news that New York City has just seen its highest number of COVID cases recorded yesterday -- 44,000 In just one day.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): And so, just to remind New Yorkers, that we're not unique in this, we are being hit very hard without a doubt, but this is also a national phenomenon, global phenomenon effect.\par \par \uc2 And also, we are testing more. That's one of the reasons we're seeing high numbers.\par \par \uc2 INGLE: Now, others are following New York's lead. The show will go on in hotspots like city like Las Vegas, and Chicago. In fact, the Las Vegas Strip is prepping its big firework show. And estimates 300,000 people will be there to celebrate.\par \par \uc2 Still, many cities are pulling the plug on their plans, including San Francisco, Atlanta, and Miami for the second year on a row where the Big Orange drop has been halted once again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGLE (on camera): And of course, you don't want to miss a minute of all the action tonight, right, Mike? So, just keep it on Fox, because at 10:00 p.m. Eastern, we have got the All American New Year's Eve show.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be in Nashville, we're going to be here in Times Square. So, tune in, we've got you covered. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Laura, glad to see you're not freezing. Be safe and have fun, happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 INGLE: Thank you, happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Well, it's another tough day for air travelers this New Year's Eve. More than 1,400 flights have been canceled so far as the COVID surge and winter weather caused chaos nationwide, and experts say the trouble could continue into the New Year. Correspondent Mark Meredith shows us tonight from Reagan National Airport in Arlington, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: America's airlines are ending 2021 with mounting delays, cancellations, and confusion about what lies ahead. And for thousands of passengers, the friendly skies are becoming anything but.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's a panic, and then you go online and then they want you to reschedule and find a different airline. I was like, no, I'm not going to try to wait.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Coming into the airport there has been a lot of shortage of staff. So we stood in line for quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: A surge in COVID cases is adding to staffing shortages at airports nationwide, and there may be more turbulence ahead. New York based \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  is scaling back its schedule through mid-January, canceling nearly 1,300 flights. An airline spokesperson telling FOX Business "We expect the number of COVID caves in the northeast where most of our crew members are based to continue to surge for the next week or two."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says it too expects staffing shortages due to COVID, and it's warning delays during the busiest travel times are possible. In Seattle, winter weather is wrecking travel plans for thousands.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My plane was supposed to land in Seattle at 6:00. It was canceled. We have been trying to get home since then.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: SeaTac's largest carrier, \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 , is now reducing its flights by 20 percent, Alaska calling it the perfect storm of severe winter weather, coupled with crew members and aircraft being displaced around the country.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Americans hoping to escape the cold on cruise ships are being told to stay home. On Thursday, the CDC urged people to avoid cruise travel regardless of vaccination status. Health officials say ships are reporting a spike in COVID cases, but the cruise industry is pushing back, calling the warning, quote, "particularly perplexing." The industry argues the majority of people testing positive are either asymptomatic or have mild cases.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: For travelers, the federal mask mandate remains in effect until mid-March. It could always get extended past then. Federal health officials say they are also still debating whether or not they are going to try to require people prove their vaccination status in order to fly. And Mike, this is a debate that is only expected to take up even more ferocity in the New Year. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: No doubt about that. Mark Meredith live at Reagan National, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look back at the news stories that captivated the nation in 2021 and how those stories were presented in the mainstream media.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You are looking live at the Wild Horse Saloon in Nashville, Tennessee, where, as Laura Ingle mentioned earlier, FOX News Channel is ringing in 2022 in an all-American way. FOX's special New Year's Eve coverage begins tonight at 10:00 p.m. eastern. Throughout night there will be a line dancing competition, New Year's resolutions from FOX News personalities, and a comedy workshop with the Gutfeld team. Sounds like fun.\par \par \uc2 Before we look ahead to the New Year, let's take a look back at the stories that dominated 2021 and how the news media portrayed them. Here is FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: The dominant story in American politics took place at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  on the 6th day of the year, and there was a rare degree of unanimity across the media spectrum.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: It pretty clearly seems to be President Trump who is encouraging a mob, an insurrection to storm the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I don't think there is much doubt, Bret, that the president's actions and his entire conduct post the election are what led us to this point.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But that quickly faded as media partisans did battle over the importance of the Capitol riot, who was to blame for the violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And the fairness of a Democratic-led probe with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and other Republicans defying subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Many major news organizations downplayed or ignored the initial allegations of sexual harassment by someone they lauded, New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , even when "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " interviewed accuser Charlotte Bennett.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe that he was propositioning you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sex.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: The miscalculation seemed even worse when Cuomo resigned.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 , (D) FORMER NEW YORK GOVERNOR: The best way I can help now is if I step aside.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And CNN later fired his brother Chris Cuomo --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CUOMO, CNN ANCHOR: I am truly sorry.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: -- for crossing ethical lines.\par \par \uc2 President Biden drew far more sympathetic coverage than his combative predecessor, but took far fewer questions, and sometimes bristled at routine inquires.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why are you so confident he will change his behavior, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not confident he will change his behavior. What the hell -- if you don't understand it, you are in the wrong business.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think that might incentivize more people to come over illegally?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you guys keep sending that garbage out, yes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: A turning point came with the president's chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan, which virtually all news outlets harshly criticized as a fiasco for Biden.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You could also look at this as a tremendously humiliating moment of American humiliation, leaving -- forced to leave on the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 's clock.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: While the press often hammered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over his uneven handling of COVID-19, Biden drew largely positive coverage, until the pandemic dragged on, another 400,000 people died, and the record breaking Omicron surgery revealed a severe shortage of tests.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Empty shelves, no test kits, is that a