ear in the population. They're using omicron to try to make people scared. If you're a leader in government, you're making people calm, and you're implementing what you talked about -- testing, treatment, therapeutics. That's government at its best. What we're seeing is government at its worst.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, and what's a shame is testing is not a new phenomenon. For the president weeks ago to promise that it was going to be free and available, where have they been for the last year? If not for helping to perfect testing and making sure that it's readily available, one of the most important things people need to know is if they're infected.\par \par \uc2 Again, our thanks to Dr. Nicole Saphier who's with us before the commercial. And Congressman Zeldin, thanks for joining us tonight. We do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, switching gears, the defense rested its case in the manslaughter trial of former Brooklyn Center, Minnesota Police Officer Kim Potter today. Now, remember, Potter fatally shot Daunte Wright back in April while he was resisting arrest and Potter says she meant to grab her Taser but pulled a gun by mistake. Today, she took the stand and broke down in tears describing the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Here to break it all down, FOX News legal analyst Gregg Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 Gregg, you've been following this case closely. How do you read what's happening now and this emotional testimony that was given today?\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: It's always a risky decision, Jason, to take the witness stand if you're the accused. There's no telling how the jury will read your testimony.\par \par \uc2 I watched. I thought she was sincere and genuine, incredible. She broke down and sobbed and apologized repeatedly I'm sorry, I'm sorry, I never meant to hurt anyone.\par \par \uc2 She reiterated that she mistook her gun for a Taser. Prosecutors say that's impossible for an experienced veteran police officer who is a highly trained in these things every year the use of a gun and a Taser.\par \par \uc2 The side-by-side pictures of the two may tell a story to the jurors. They have a different look, size, different feel, different weight. One is yellow and the other is not. And they say her mistake is more than just an accident. It is gross negligence and recklessness which constitutes manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 The defense on the other hand it tries to may much of the blame on Daunte Wright himself. He's the one who resisted arrest, who fought with police, who jumped in the car and attempted to flee the scene as Sergeant Michael Johnson was leaning in, trying to handcuff him. Johnson took the witness stand and said, I fear for my life. I thought it was good to be dragged to my death as he pressed the accelerator of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 And he further testified that Kim Potter was justified in using her gun to shoot Daunte Wright, albeit mistakenly. That was also confirmed by use of force expert and the chief of police, both of whom said she was justified in using her gun.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, there is a fine line between an accident and that negligent conduct resulting in death. The law struggles to differentiate between the two and as a consequence, jurors also agonize over that decision.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it's going to be very difficult when the case goes to the jury next week.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, always tough cases when you look at it in real-time and adrenaline flowing, even a veteran, it's a difficult, difficult situation. It's sad for the loss of life.\par \par \uc2 Greg Jarrett, thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrat-run cities or from coast-to-coast are seeing crime surges. Cities crumble and the chaos ruling the streets. For example, 12 cities this year broke homicide records, all run by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And you're seeing the video. Smash and grab thefts continued to plague retailers, as defund poli