e to abortion on demand by mail shows how far to the left they have swung in this country. And they are talking about -- let's remember, these are the same Democrats who talked about things the governor of Virginia talking about possible infanticide, when we are talking about protecting life here in this country.\par \par \uc2 We need to make sure that every life is protected and anything other than that is actually going to be an issue. And you should absolutely talk to a doctor. It's unbelievable that they want to be air dropping abortion pills on demand to people. It's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah, it truly is. Congresswoman out of -- Pressley, out of Massachusetts, she was quoted today as saying -- she said today that student debt is violence. That's what she said. Student debt is violence.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I read it. It's crazy. This is at the Squad. This is the progressive extreme left. And they want to use it terms it to scare and intimidate.\par \par \uc2 Policy violence? It makes no sense, Jason, whatsoever. But you know what? The progressives have hijacked the Democratic Party. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lied to the American public. He's run as a moderate.\par \par \uc2 He's not in control, and when you hear this type of nonsense, this rhetoric from the squad, it's just tell you how far left this -- the Democratic Party has gone. I mean, I tell you right now, 2022 is a very critical to this country to bring us back to what it's about, liberty and freedom.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Erin, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  owes you a little bit more time next time.\par \par \uc2 But thanks to both of you and Leo for joining us tonight, because we got to go.\par \par \uc2 But straight ahead, amid a number of ongoing crises, the Obamas have decided to focus their attention on asserting themselves into the world of pop culture. We'll explain as \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2  joins us up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 As the Biden administration continues to wear down our nation's economy, Biden's old friends, the Obamas, are continuing their foray into pop culture. Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  just announced his annual list of favorite songs. This year, it includes Montero, "Call me by your name", by rapper and singer Lil Nas X. The song's music depicts the artist descending into a hell on a stripper pole and giving the devil a lap dance.\par \par \uc2 Viewer discretion is advised.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: All right. So, he's not following in the footsteps of \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , I can tell you that.\par \par \uc2 So, here with reaction, former California gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Larry, I got to tell you -- thanks. First of all, thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt that the Obamas will continue to go Hollywood, but I want to focus your attention on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , because she's supposed to be the rising star. She's supposed to be the one that's the wing man there for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She's supposed to be the future of the Democratic Party, but she can't even organize her office, let alone try to weigh on on weighty issues like immigration.\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER\uc2 , NATIONALLY SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: You know , it's interesting you're finding all of this subtle talk about them throwing her overboard for the 2024 election. Jason, it is not going to happen I'll tell you why.\par \par \uc2 Black female voters constitute the strongest demographic for the Democratic Party there is. And while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , his poll numbers are like 28 percent or so in the nation, she enjoys, I just read, a 72 percent approval rating among blacks. It wasn't broken down by gender but my suspicion is among black female voters, her popularity is even higher.\par \par \uc2 And when the CNN article surfaced with all the comments about how the Biden administration is not happy with her, there was an article in the black publication called "TheGrio" where this black female was quite angry about the treatment of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , pointed out what I just pointed out about how loyal black females are. And I think the Democratic Party will fear their wrath in the event that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , assuming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not run again in 2024, and the prospects of that are quite likely, that she's not the person that would be running as a president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 They are stuck with her and the reason they chose her, of course, is because she checked all the boxes. She's black, she's a person of color, all of that. It wasn't because she was confident. She didn't win a single primary. In fact, she pulled out before the first caucus in Iowa. She wasn't popular among California. Couldn't raise any money.\par \par \uc2 That's irrelevant. She checked all the boxes and now, they're stuck with her. So, this list of possible replacements are all mostly -- most of them are white men. That's just not going to fly. So, they are stuck with her.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Larry, you know about hard outs. We only got about 20 seconds here, but there's a lot of talk that say, hey, if Biden gets a Supreme Court pick, maybe that's the way to get out of this?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Would you do that if you are \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to be president. She doesn't want to sit on the Supreme Court. She ran for president. She wants to be president.\par \par \uc2 She's this close to being president assuming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can't make it, assuming she can beat her Republican rival, which I doubt.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ELDER: But, you know, between now and 2024, there's a lot of time.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yeah. \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us. I hope you have a wonderful holiday and a merry Christmas. Thanks for joining us tonight on HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 More of the special edition of HANNITY after this break. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Welcome back to this special edition of HANNITY. Many thanks to Sean for letting me sit in his seat. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening.\par \par \uc2 Make sure to check out my podcast, "Jason in the House". Just type in "Jason in the House" anywhere you do podcast. This week, I have an interview up with Bill Melugin, rising star at FOX News. You'll love that interview.\par \par \uc2 Again, thank you for joining us. I hope you have a wonderful holiday. Great merry Christmas. And stay tuned, because Laura Ingraham is up with "THE INGRAHAM ANGLE." That's up next. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020211218ehch00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Former Officer Testifies About Killing Daunte Wright; Former CDC Dir On Omicron Emergence As Winter Begins; Reports Of TikTok Threats Put Schools On Alert; FDA Permanently Allows Abortion Pills By Mail; US Bracing For Omicron-Fueled COVID Wave; Warrant Issued For Baldwin's Phone In "Rush" Probe\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Garrett Tenney, Leo Terrell, Joe Concha, William  La Jeunesse \par \uc2 7,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Happy Holidays to you and your family. Merry Christmas. And that does it for us. Thank you so much for joining us here on "AMERICA REPORTS." I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: And I'm John Roberts. Hope you all have a Merry Christmas. We'll see you again very soon. THE STORY WITH MARTHA MACCALLUM starts right now. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS HOST: All right, thank you, John. Merry Christmas to you. Sandra, thanks. Good to see you. So good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. And this has been truly riveting testimony that we have been watching throughout the day. And it is all coming really primarily from the back and forth with the former Minnesota police officer, Kim Potter.\par \par \uc2 The day was April 11th, 2021, it was roughly one year after the George Floyd murder, and that is when Kim Potter says that in a traffic stop when she was training another officer she mistakenly pulled her gun instead of her taser.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIM POTTER, FORMER POLICE OFFICER: And I remember yelling, taser, taser, taser, and nothing happened. And then he told me, I shot him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Potter pleaded not guilty to manslaughter. She is facing two -- first degree and second degree manslaughter are the options that this jury will be considering. She could face as much as 15 years in prison. Correspondent Garrett Tenney has been ably following all of this for us from the trial site in Chicago and he joins us now. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha, this clearly it was not easy for Kim Potter as she became overcome with emotion repeatedly throughout the day, anytime that she was forced to relive this tragedy. Starting with this morning when Potter testified the encounter with Daunte Wright turned chaotic as the officers tried to take him into custody and he broke free, getting back in the car and trying to drive away while one of the officers was leaning into the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 POTTER: They're still struggling, and I can see Sergeant Johnson and the driver struggling over the gearshifts because I can see Johnson's hand and then I can see his face. He had a look of fear on his face. It's nothing I've seen before. We are struggling. We're trying to keep him from driving away. It's just went chaotic. It -- and then I remember yelling, taser, taser, taser, and nothing happened. And then he told me I shot him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: On cross-examination, prosecutors laid out much of the training Potter received during her 26 years as an officer, including on the use of force and when and how it's appropriate to use force, while suggesting someone with her experience should have handled the situation better.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIN ELDRIDGE, PROSECUTOR: And ultimately, was Mr. Wright able to get back in the car?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You never saw a weapon on Mr. Wright, did you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: Never saw a gun?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: He never threw a punch, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: Never kicked anyone?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: Never said, I'm going to kill you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Potter is facing first and second degree manslaughter charges and we are expecting to have closing arguments on Monday. The jury will be heading home later this afternoon after getting their jury instructions. The final images, they will be taking home with them over the weekend, though, are those visuals of Kim Potter on the stand being overcome with emotion as they head into closing arguments on Monday. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Garrett, thank you very much. Let's bring in Leo Terrell, civil rights attorney and Fox News contributor, and attorney Brian Claypool. Gentlemen, good to have both of you with us. This is obviously very emotional testimony. I should point out that we have seen emotional testimony from Daunte Wright's family as well over the course of this trial.\par \par \uc2 Leo, culpable negligence is what this jury would need to find she had, right, in order to charge her with manslaughter and to find her guilty of manslaughter. What really stood out to you today in terms of the moments that you think this jury will be pondering the most as they go into that decision making room?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And thank you for that question, Martha. I'm going to give you both sides as an attorney for both sides, if I was playing both the prosecutor and defense. I think Potter was very believable. I think she's tremendously sympathetic. I think she's honestly truly hurty and she was expressing a lot of remorse for her mistake, her error. She made a mistake and she knows it, and I think those tears were real.\par \par \uc2 If I'm the prosecutor, I have to be very happy for a couple of reasons. The worst thing a witness should do is say, I don't remember. And I think a couple of times when the prosecutor asked her questions, and we said, I don't remember, that brawls into her credibility, her credibility comes into play.\par \par \uc2 I also believe the issue of her expressing remorse is going to be very important, but she's a veteran police officer. So the question here is, what threat does she see and was the accident something that a veteran police officer would have done?\par \par \uc2 I don't think this prosecutor was out of line, Martha. She had to get these point across to the jury, because she can make these points in her closing the argument, but I think Potter is a believable witness.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I think you touched on exactly the right point, and that is was Sergeant Johnson in danger. So was that merited for her to pull either the taser, which she says she was planning to pull, and that is a lot of what we saw Erin Eldridge going at. And if people recognize these people, Erin Eldridge was also involved in the Derek Chauvin case, as was the other attorney who's in the room, as well Mark Frank.\par \par \uc2 So, let's watch this. This is a little bit of a long exchange, but I think it's worth watching. And I'd like to then get Brian's take on where we are with all of this. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You'd agree that as a police officer, you have the duty to render aid and communicate information to other officers, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: And it's part of your job to assist those who are hurt or injured, true?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: And to communicate to other officers what you know about a particular scene, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: Give them whatever information you can to help them do their jobs to help render assistance, things like that, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: But you didn't do any of those things on April 11th, did you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You stopped doing your job completely. You didn't communicate what happened over the radio, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You didn't make sure any officers knew what you had just done, right?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You didn't run down the street and try to save Daunte Wright's life, did you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You didn't check on the other car that had been hit, did you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: No.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: That all happened just down the road from you?\par \par \uc2 POTTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELDRIDGE: You're focused on what you had done, because you had just killed somebody.\par \par \uc2 POTTER: I'm sorry it happened. I'm so sorry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brian Claypool let me bring you back in, and just correct myself, as Matthew Frank who is the attorney general in this case. I also want to let everybody know that the jury has been sent home, they will begin their deliberations on Monday, that's another thing that everybody will have to take into account, that they're going to have a really 48 hour cooling off period between this final testimony and when they begin deliberations.\par \par \uc2 So Brian Claypool, your thoughts on that interaction that we just watched?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, ATTORNEY: Yes. Hey, Martha, thanks for having me back, and Merry Christmas. I think Leo agree with me, we both handled shooting death cases in California involving police officers. And the argument -- if you're making the argument, gee, the police officer should have rendered medical aid, and a police officer should have tried to save the life of somebody they just shot. That's not going to work with a jury because these police officers aren't paramedics.\par \par \uc2 But with that said, I think both sides score points. Let's talk about the prosecution for a moment. I think the prosecution did a good job of letting the jury see the totality of the circumstances. So where's the starting point here? One of the most powerful pieces of evidence the prosecution got out today, she got Potter to admit, I would not have pulled this car over. That makes this case bigger than just the state of Minnesota versus Kim Potter, Martha.\par \par \uc2 That brings into question training and police practices across the country. In other words, if you as a police officer create an unreasonable risk, are you then justified in relying, gee, I made a mistake in using deadly force. And when she says, I wouldn't pull them over, I mean, this encounter probably should have never happened.\par \par \uc2 And another quick piece of evidence that I think is really powerful was when the prosecutor showed the picture of a taser right next to the gun, Martha, it was profound. I mean, the -- when you grab the handle of the Taser, it's like that big, the gun is like that big.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: And then she showed a picture. She showed a picture of Kim Potter holding the gun. So there was there was time that was lapsed between holding the gun and shooting the gun, that is powerful because that establishes I think that Potter knew she had a gun in her hand, and I think that does potentially rise to culpable negligence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. She -- at one point Erin Eldridge said, you know your left from your right, right, to Kim Potter, and she said, yes. I got to go, but I want to get one more quick answer from you, Leo. She said she had never fired her weapon before, never fired a gun before, never fired her taser before. Quick thought before we head out.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh, you know, all that is irrelevant to what she did on that given day, what she knew at that time, what threat did she see, and what actions does she take. It's what happened that day is going to be the determining factor. I'll say this, the jury is this a case on facts, the jury is going to deal with this case and we don't know what the outcome is going to be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, complicated and complex to be sure, a lot of emotion on that stand today from Kim Potter. And as I said, we've seen a lot of emotion throughout the course of this trial from Daunte Wright's family as well, and also from the officer who she was training. Gentlemen, thank you. Brian Claypool and Leo Terrell, always good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 CLAYPOOL: Yes. Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So also breaking today, the CDC now backs what its former director predicted here on the story four months ago. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT REDFIELD\uc2 , FORMER CDC DIRECTOR: Sadly, I'm going to predict that, you know, within, you know, two, three, four months, we're going to have another variant, and that variant is going to be more infectious than the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And indeed, that is exactly what happened. Exclusive with \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  on Omicron and whether this is a wave that we need to be very concerned about or not, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY, CDC DIRECTOR: Omicron is increasing rapidly and we expect it to become the dominant strain in the United States as it has in other countries in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: CDC Director Rochelle Walensky making that prediction earlier today as Omicron now pops up in 39 states. The emergence of this latest variant is something that \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  saw coming four months ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Sadly, I'm going to predict it, you know, within, you know, two, three, four months we're going to have another variant, and that variant is going to be more infectious than the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  led the CDC at the beginning of the pandemic and is a virologist. Doctor, welcome back, it's always good to have you with us. I guess, given that prediction that you made at that point, is there anything that the country should have done to prevent what we're seeing right now?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: You know, Martha, this is just the reality of what we're up against with this current virus. That it's going to continue to evolve. And if that virus evolves to the point that it has a replication advantage over the dominant variant, then it will replace it.\par \par \uc2 So when I suggested that we'd have a new variant that would replace Delta, I really thought there'd be a new variant that will have a replication advantage, particularly in people that have already been infected or had been vaccinated, and their immunity is waning. And unfortunately, that's what we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 And I think the most important thing that we could have done then and can do now and still can do is to get people vaccinated. More importantly, than just being vaccinated, making sure you stay vaccinated. So this is why additional vaccinations are going to be required, because the immunity to this virus does wane, whether you are naturally infected, or whether you're vaccinated, you constantly go from a point of being protected and then susceptible, and protected and susceptible. So, again, that vigilance in getting the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I would argue that we could do and we need to do more of is we need to get more availability of testing so that you can determine whether or not you're asymptomatically infected and pull yourself out of the transmission pool. And that's going to be even more important, I think, with the Omicron variant, because --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: -- more of us that get infected are going to have less symptoms.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I want to play this soundbite from Dr. Fauci where he opened the door to potentially more restrictions in the way we live our life. And we're going to dig into this more in a moment, but the NFL has just postponed three games. So here's Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, BIDEN CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: If the counts keep going up, and the test positivity keeps going up, we may need to be more restrictive. But for right now, people who are vaccinated and boosted should feel reasonably comfortable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so I want to take the second part first there and get to the restriction part of the question after that. But he says if people who are vaccinated and boosted should feel reasonably comfortable, so if you have two shots of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Moderna and a booster, should you go about your daily life?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Well, again, it depends on the timing of that third shot. I do believe that it turns out that with the Omicron variant, if I wish to take blood from someone that's vaccinated and tested against Delta and Omicron to see how well that blood can neutralize the virus, the blood that neutralizes the virus Delta, if you do it against Omicron, it's about 40 to 50 times less able to neutralize the virus. So that this virus is really able to get around our immunity as it wanes.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, before we were saying you needed to be boosted, I'd say at six months, it's not clear to me whether that's going to need to be sooner, obviously, for those that are older and have a more weakened immune system, obviously those are immune suppressed. But I do think if you've gotten two injections and you've been boosted with a non-J&J vaccine within the last 60 to 90 days, I think you're going to be largely protected. But in --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: -- you know, in four or five months, you know, you're not getting protected.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So are you saying that we're going to have to get boosted -- we're going to have to get boost -- yes, so we're going to have to get boosted every few months? For how long, for another year, another two years?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: I think one of the -- yes. I think one of the most important things we need, and hopefully the private sector will come up and develop it for us, we need an objective measure of whether you still have evidence of immunity or lack of immunity, this is what I call an immunity test, because right now we're giving vaccine based on time, but we're all different.\par \par \uc2 My wife and I recently got tested for our antibody against spike protein, we got vaccinated the exact same time, mine was less than a hundred, hers was over a thousand. We're all different. But I do think until we develop a better durable vaccine, we are going to probably have to have recurrent boosters.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. So, but if you have all of that, and you have good antibodies, can you go about your normal life?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: I think we have to start doing that, Martha. And we're going to - - this virus is going to be with us for the duration. We've got to start learning how to live with it. I don't agree that the answer is to shut things down, shut things down, shut things down. I've always felt that we need to look at situations and see how to do them in a safe and responsible way.\par \par \uc2 CDC director, I never advocated shutting down the schools. I felt that that was not in the public health interests of kids, K through 12. I thought what we needed to do is learn how to keep the schools open in a safe and responsible way. That's the way I feel now.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that we can use to help do that is expanded access to testing. You know, we need to be able to understand who's infected and who isn't. And if I'm infected, I pull myself out of the transmission pool. Unfortunately, as we sit here today, we don't have the number of tests that we need to have simple, reliable, weekly or twice a week testing, to be able to understand how to pull yourself out of the transmission pool.\par \par \uc2 I just got back from the UAE. I was there for eight days, I was tested four times to make sure that I didn't have the virus and that I was not entering into the different environments that I went into. And I think testing combined with vaccination, and then some smartness about mitigation steps. But we got to go along with it, we got to move on. We got to move on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to move on, we're going to have shots and arms every few months and get tested every few days, that's a tough kind of move on environment.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, sir, I just want to give you an opportunity to respond to this story that came out. The House Oversight Committee has released a report that they say shows evidence that the Trump administration and officials, including you, were muzzling people in the \uc2 HHS\uc2  and CDC who wanted to speak out. Dr. Nancy Messonnier is one of the more prominent voices here. And she says that she was basically told to be quiet after she spoke out on February 25th about the risks of the virus. Do you want to respond to that oversight report Committee?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Well, I think Nancy Messonnier is a really an important -- enormous public figure, done great service to our nation. When she basically told the truth of what she felt was coming, there were some people that weren't really happy about her comments. But I think as everyone knows, her comments were right on.\par \par \uc2 There was some decisions after that, unfortunately, that limited CDC's ability to communicate effectively to the American public, I was very disappointed in that. But I will say people argued the reason for that is we had a task force now. And as you remember, almost every night, the task force did a press conference in the White House press room and people felt that that was adequate. So, you know, I think it was --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you would -- you think it's true that she was told to sort of tamp it down?\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: I think -- Well, I think the agency, in general, no longer was able to give its weekly -- or daily briefings that we routinely did and they were replaced by White House briefings. So I mean, that's just -- that is a true reality, and I think people owe Nancy Messonnier a great deal of gratitude, and thanks for her courage to just speak out the truth.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I appreciate your candid answers, as always. Dr. Redfield, thank you. Good to have you here today, sir.\par \par \uc2 REDFIELD: Thanks, Martha. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're going to continue our conversation on all of this. God bless you as well. Dr. Amesh Adalja will be with us shortly to talk more about this. Obviously, it's on everybody's minds as we head into the holidays and try to navigate all of this. Also an update from TikTok after schools across the country shut down in response to threats that went viral. Joe Concha is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Schools across the country increasing security after posts on TikTok warned of shootings and bomb threats nationwide today, some districts even closed their schools for the day based on this. Official say they do not think that these threats are credible, but in some cases, they decided not to take any chances.\par \par \uc2 TikTok tweeting, quote, we have exhaustively searched for content that promotes violence at schools today, but I've still found nothing. What we find are videos discussing this rumor and warning others to stay safe. Media reports have been widespread and based on rumors rather than facts. We are deeply concerned that the proliferation of local media reports on the alleged trend that has not been found on the platform could end up inspiring real world harm.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for the Hill and a Fox News contributor. Joe, obviously there's a number of sides to this story. I mean, any parent that hears that there's a possibility at their school is going to be concerned, especially given what we just watched in Oxford, Mississippi, and we always worry about copycat sorts of attacks.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, you've got the TikTok element to all of this and their role. So in a challenge, you know, please challenge everybody to go out and do this, it's just so disturbing. So take whichever side of that you want first, sir.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Sure, I'll take it as a parent, right? It's such a helpless feeling. You send your kids off to school and not worrying whether, OK, are they going to do well in their studies? Are they going to make a certain team? Are they going to get along with their classmates? Now, it's are their lives in danger?\par \par \uc2 And these social media threats make school districts and administrators helpless, Martha. If they don't take it seriously, and something horrible and deadly happens, then it's like that recent school shooting at a high school in Michigan, right? Why didn't you do more to stop this? All of the signs were there on social media.\par \par \uc2 But given how viral these threats have become, and how easy they are to make on platforms like Tiktok, the hope is that these threats don't become the new normal. But TikTok needs to step up here and moderated site a thousand times better than it is now. But its owners are based in China. And that's who we're dealing with. And they may not care very much what impact its platform, the negative impact it's having on Americans, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I always think that this really has to come not from the companies ultimately, but from parents and families deciding that they're not going to let their kids use these, these forms of social media, that they have a lot of dangers. And to give up that response, it's almost impossible. I'm very sympathetic to this, to keep your kids off this stuff because they have a phone, because they can find a lot of ways to do it. But what do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: I think I remember a segment I did with Martha McCallum, I think it was three or four years ago. It just hit me now when you're just saying that, where we've talked about how you went away Memorial Day with your family, and you left the phones at home, with the exception of a phone that you need to make an emergency call on. But you stayed off it for the weekend, and you're probably a lot happier for it.\par \par \uc2 And at least in my house, you know, my kids are six and eight. I'm not dealing with things on the high school level yet, but they get their iPad for one hour over the weekend and that's it. Because we've learned that if they have more access to it, we could give them all the educational stuff they want. They'll find the bad stuff and stumble upon it if you give them enough time.\par \par \uc2 So you're right, this happens, maybe not at the school level or the national level, but every parent in every household understanding that social media is as addictive as smoking was for kids and teenagers 20-30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, I think that's the only thing that's going to ultimately make some of these companies wake up and realize that they have to put a sharper parameters in. And on TikTok side, we told their side of the story here as well. They claim that this is sort of amped up outside of their social network. Joe, always good to see you. Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Good to see you, Martha. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Coming up, I will speak with \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . I'll speak with Amesh Adalja, Dr. Amesh Adalja, who breaks down what you need to know about omicron spread as we head into the holidays and also huge news from the NFL cancelling games. So where is this going? Are we doing this all over again? We'll talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Now, the FDA has cleared the way also for abortion pills by mail ahead of the Supreme Court ruling that could shake up Roe v Wade. This is a huge story and a debate you will only see here on "The Story" right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The FDA permanently lifting a major restriction on abortion pills allowing women to receive the medication now by mail, instead of requiring patients to pick up the pills in-person and to have an in-person doctor visit. Now, you can do it by telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 The move coincides with one of the most consequential challenges to Roe v Wade in our lifetime. That is now before the Supreme Court. The State of Mississippi is