ut the mother's health, then you would make sure that there is a safe way to have abortions because abortions are going to happen. They have always happen. If you care about the mother, then you care about her safety, and you need to create a pathway to do so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lila?\par \par \uc2 ROSE: I would say what about the two dozen women that these drugs have killed, and how can they be safe when they're designed to kill? They are lethal drug and they're killing. They've killed 5 million children already. This is politics. The Biden administration --\par \par \uc2 KONST: First off, they're not children, so let's start off with the definition of the Supreme Court's working --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROSE: At just three-and-a -half weeks. And even doctors from the Mayo Clinic who are pro-abortion themselves say this is a terrible decision, and say that they need to have (inaudible) with women to confirm pregnancy, because it's very dangerous to be shipping these pills via mail to women and girls. Sexual abusers could order them to continue to victimize young girls --\par \par \uc2 KONST: (Inaudible) do that.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: There's no in-person visit for these women. So if you care about women and certainly if you care about justice for the lives of children in the womb, this is a devastating decision. And it's pure politics in the most cynical way.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Yes, it's pure politics and you're spewing a bunch of falsities. First off, this is --\par \par \uc2 ROSE: No, what do you about the mail --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KONST: I let you speak. I let you speak. You're picking one doctor from --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Nomiki, it's your turn, Nomiki. But I -- hey, guys? Hold on. Nomiki, wait.\par \par \uc2 I just want to point out -- I just want -- I'm going to give you your chance to respond. But I think you also need to be respectful of the way that Lila looks at this situation, and the way that she speaks about it. And we need to do the same. Go ahead, Nomiki.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Well, I mean, the reality here is Lila is saying that she cares so much about women, thousands, hundreds of thousands of women have died because they do not have a safe access to clinics, doctors. You care so much about seeing a woman -- having a woman see a doctor then maybe you shouldn't be advocating for the funding being cut for access to clinics .There are several referees --\par \par \uc2 ROSE: So are you saying they should have in-person visits with doctors?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KONST: One click -- hang on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I just have time for a quick final thought from Lila. Lila, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Listen, at the end of the day, it's again 5 million deaths because of the abortion pill. This is a big boon for the abortion industry. They're going to make major bank because they don't even have to see the woman. They don't have to confirm her pregnancy. And women can die because of this. So for everybody's sake, this needs to be reversed and I look forward to when this very pro-abortion administration is no longer in office and we hopefully have a change in political leadership --\par \par \uc2 KONST: You're anti-women's health.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nomiki? Nomiki, stop it.\par \par \uc2 KONST: She's lied. That's a false.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's not fair. No, it isn't.\par \par \uc2 KONST: I'm not going to allow her.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It isn't.\par \par \uc2 KONST: She's lying on air. Lying on air and putting women at risk.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, I got to go. All right. You guys are not willing to discuss this in a respectful manner then we're going to go. Bye. See you both later. Lila Rose, thank you very much. Nomiki -- we gave you more than your fair share, OK.\par \par \uc2 So let's get over to this topic now for a moment. Breaking right now, the NFL has announced that they will postpone three games now that 100 players and coaches have tested positive for COVID-19. Dr. Amesh Adalja joins us right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, COVID outbreaks have now forced the National Football League to postpone three of this weekend's games, Las Vegas at Cleveland has been moved from Saturday to Monday. The Sunday games have Seattle at the Los Angeles Rams and Washington at Philadelphia are now going to be played on Tuesday. They're giving those players enough time to get past their COVID period that they are on after they had more than hundred coaches and players diagnosed with positive test results.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is Dr. Amesh Adalja, senior scholar at the Hopkins Center for Health Security. He's focused on pandemic preparedness and infectious diseases. Doctor, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there's a lot of tension and nervousness right now around this omicron. I don't think any buddy in this country thought we were still going to be dealing with this in Christmas of 2021. What's your take?\par \par \uc2 AMESH ADALJA, SENIOR SCHOLAR, HOPKINS CENTER FOR HEALTH SECURITY: I think we still don't know a lot about Omicron. We still don't understand it severity. But we do know we have to be prepared for it to increase the number of cases. Just looking at the data from New York City, it is going to be something that eventually will displace Delta. And for those people who are unvaccinated, we are going to see severe debt from Omicron, and it might happen faster than it would have occurred with Delta.\par \par \uc2 So this is something that we're not helpless against. We've got vaccines, we've got tests, we've got monoclonal antibodies, we have an anti-viral. But I think we need to take it seriously and make sure that our hospitals are ready.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So speaking of that anti-viral, how quickly can we get that into people's hands?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: It's something that's kind of now mired up in FDA bureaucracy. They're likely going to have a meeting where they discuss it, and then the FDA acting commissioner will decide whether to approve it. So we're talking about a matter of weeks. But I think if we could get this today, if there is anything left on the shelf -- if they have stuff on the shelf, to be used compassionate use -- in a compassionate use manner, that would be helpful. Because we know that this works on the Omicron variant, we know that this has really high efficacy, probably 90% of preventing deaths and hospitalizations. And that was an unvaccinated individuals. So this is a real game changer and we need it today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I'm going to ask you the same question that I asked Dr. Redfield, and I'm thinking about what President Biden said. For the unvaccinated, we're looking at a winter of severe illness and death. And we certainly hope that that darkest scenario that he presented does not come to fruition. But for people who have two vaccines and a booster, should they go about their normal lives.\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: I think it all depends on your risk tolerance. If you're someone who's fully vaccinated, even if you get a breakthrough,, whether it's Delta or Omicron, it's likely to be mild, likely not to even cause you to call your doctor. So for those individuals, I think it's up to their own risk tolerance. And I think we've got to learn how to risk calculate because this virus isn't going anywhere. And the best thing we can do is reduce the harm the virus causes by getting ourselves vaccinated so that when we get our inevitable COVID-19 infection, it's a mild one.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And do you agree with Dr. Redfield that we're going to be getting vaccines or recommendations for vaccines every five or six months because these vaccines and the immunity don't last nearly as long as we thought they would?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: Like you think it's a little bit early to say that for sure. But what we're seeing with the mRNA vaccines is, yes, they do tend to wear off quicker than other types of vaccines, but there are a second generation and vaccines that are in the works. So I think we may be able to avoid that and it's also dependent upon the goal we're trying to achieve. We're not going to eradicate or eliminate this. We're not going to drive cases to zero.\par \par \uc2 And if our goal is to prevent severe illness and hospitalization, I think we can do that with our current vaccines. But maybe there will be a second generation vaccine that covers SARS-CoV-2 and some of the other coronaviruses that cause our common colds. And maybe it will be more like the measles vaccine. We have to see what science brings us with the second generation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Dr. Adalja, thanks. Great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have breaking news just moments ago. Ghislaine Maxwell says that she will not testify in her own defense. She's not going to take the stand at her sex trafficking trial here in New York City. She's accused of recruiting girls as young as 14 years old, who got on the stand and said that they were sexually abused by billionaire Jeffrey Epstein and some of them accused her as well.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell has called the claims absolute rubbish. She says prosecutors have not proven their case beyond a reasonable doubt. And so she will not take the stand in her own defense.\par \par \uc2 So text messages, calls and content from Alec Baldwin's phone is now fair as prosecutors dig in now -- as they dig into the deadly shooting on the movie set. So what took so long for them to ask for this phone when he was always central to the activity and the horrific actions that happened that day when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Almost two months after the deadly shooting on the set of Alec Baldwin's movie "Rust," investigators want to get a closer look at his phone. Cinematographer Halyna Hutchins was killed when the gun Baldwin was holding went off. He claims he did not pull the trigger. That was in October. So why are investigators now issuing a search warrant for his phone all these months later? Attorney Brian Claypool is back with us but first to national correspondent William La Jeunesse live on this in LA. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Basically, police want to see everything on Baldwin's phone, but there's something else in this document that is stunning. Baldwin says he did nothing wrong. The court record says something else despite what he said happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: Now in this scene, I'm going to cock the gun. I said do you want to see that? And I she said, yes. So I take the gun and I start to cock the gun. I'm not going to pull the trigger. I said do you see this. She (inaudible) down and tilt it down a little bit like that. And I cocked the gun and go, can you see that? Can you see that? Can you see that? (Inaudible) and I let go of the hammer of the gun and the gun goes off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Well, Baldwin says he cocked the Colt .45 and release the hammer. But according to the armorer, Hannah Gutierrez Reed, Baldwin was not supposed to cock the gun saying, "We had the gun the whole time before the incident and nothing happened. And they, meaning Baldwin and the director, we're not even supposed to be pulling the hammer back.\par \par \uc2 Why? Well, many gun movies are adjusted to reduce the force necessary to pull the trigger. In doing that, the notch that holds the hammer back can malfunction and fire. Secondly, Reed says she loaded five dummy rounds but one round would not go in so she cleaned the gun and grabbed another round from what, in her words, had been a wonky set of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 As for the iPhone, police want to see any and all pictures, movies, emails, texts, and he's deleted material and passwords to the cloud. Finally, Martha, please say Baldwin requested a bigger gun. He denies that. The phone is likely to tell us who is telling the truth. Back to you..\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. William, thank you very much. All right. Let's bring in attorney Brian Claypool once again. Brian, I want to start by playing the soundbite from December 2nd, the interview that Alec Baldwin did with George Stephanopoulos. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS, HOST: You're not worried about being charged?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I don't. I've been told by people who are in the know in terms of even inside the state, that it's highly unlikely I would be charged with anything criminally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that's the case, Brian?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CLAYPOOL, ATTORNEY: Hey, Martha, great to be back with you. Alec Baldwin is a nightmare for any criminal defense attorney. I love the guy as an actor and a family man. But I got to tell you, when you go out and do a national TV interview, and you start saying I've talked to some insiders, I'm not likely to be charged and oh, by the way, I just cocked it. I didn't really push the trigger or anything. It just went off after that, when you've already allegedly told the sheriff member, the sheriff, Martha, when he did that press conference. One of the first things he said was, we know for a fact that Alec Baldwin is the person who pulled the trigger. He said pull the trigger at the press conference. How did the sheriff get that information? Was that during the first interview with Alec Baldwin. And now after he goes on a second interview, and now we're going to get text messages.\par \par \uc2 But here's the important part. The sheriff is also looking for browser information. Why is that important? Because was Alec Baldwin potentially browsing to find out to learn more, like William said, about a Colt .45? What are the mechanics? Maybe he can then go in and say, Hey, I just cocked it back and tilted it down and it went off. So the browsing information is going to be important. What else is important?\par \par \uc2 Well, now they're looking for possibly deleted messages, Martha. And also this is important, any messages that Baldwin sent while others were being interviewed in the investigation, that's important because I think this is all leading to a potential investigation of another crime, which is obstruction of justice. Did Baldwin try to coerce or intimidate or suggest to some ways during interviews to talk -- exactly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No, it's very, very interesting. And you wonder why they didn't ask for this phone sooner, why they didn't get a warrant for this phone sooner, and whether anything came up in the course of investigating that made them want to circle back and definitely get the phone now. Brian, thank you very much, Brian Claypool with us today.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY for this Friday, December the 17th. But as always THE STORY goes on so we thank you very much for being with us today. We hope you have a fantastic weekend. And we will see you back here on Monday at 3:00. Stay tuned though, "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020211218ehch00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden 