ou, President Trump repeatedly suggested or insisted that I lead the charge to change the filibuster rule. I had a one-word answer, no. Changing the structure of the Senate in order to try to achieve a partisan advantage is a mistake for the Senate and a mistake for the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McConnell also says that Democrats ignored bipartisanship in a split Senate this year. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were off today. The Dow fell 30. The S&P 500 lost 41. The NASDAQ plunged 385.\par \par \uc2 One of the most controversial proposals from the Biden administration is apparently off the table tonight at least for now. The President's team had proposed massive financial compensation to illegal immigrant families separated at the border. That prompted outrage among many people up on Capitol Hill and elsewhere. Now that deal is falling apart. White House Correspondent Peter Doocy tells us why tonight, live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. When President Biden dismissed reports about this last month. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  quickly came out to accuse him of not knowing what was going on in his own administration and then these negotiations continued with families of illegal immigrants suing the federal government for weeks quietly until now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: Folks, I'm not going to take questions today.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  is mad at the Biden White House tweeting, after our government deliberately abused young children by separating them from their families. Today they've walked away from negotiations to help those who are traumatized heal. White House officials have defended cash payments to people who came here illegally.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND\uc2 , SENIOR BIDEN ADVISER: We took children, the President Trump took children from their parents, and some children have never been returned. Do we think that's OK?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And President Biden doesn't.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If in fact, because of the outrageous behavior of the last administration, you coming across the border, whether it was legal or illegal, and you lost your child, you lost your child, it's gone. You deserve some kind of compensation, no matter what the circumstance.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Under no circumstance, have these payments been considered for people who waited in line to come?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why would we be giving people who are coming here to wait for your (ph) money?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why are you giving people who came here the wrong way (ph)?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean, but I don't understand the question.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And President Biden has never feared that settling with illegal immigrants for six figures could be a lure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think that that might incentivize more people to come over illegally?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you guys keep sending that garbage out? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): The Justice Department says today, while the parties have been unable to reach a global settlement agreement at this time, we remain committed to engaging with the plaintiffs and to bringing justice to the victims of this abhorrent policy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Did he change his mind? Does he no longer think that these people need compensation?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: So, Peter, it's ongoing litigation. So, I would refer you to the DOJ. I can't speak from it from here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): The first time I asked the President about this on an escalator in Rome, he literally scratched his head. We later came back to D.C. he called the reports garbage and then officials had to clarify that the garbage part was just that the settlement amount was up to $450,000 per illegal immigrants. But it seems that as of tonight, that amount is all the way down to zero. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow this, Peter. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The latest numbers indicate another rise in illegal immigrant encounters along the U.S. Mexico border last month. We have new exclusive video tonight from the Rio Grande Valley, the epicenter of the southern border crisis. Correspondent Bill Melugin is in La Joya, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): This is a path that 1000s have walked before during this unrelenting border crisis. And this morning, you're in La Joya, Texas, this very familiar scene played out yet again.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over) Large groups of migrant families crossed illegally into the U.S. and they walk in search of border patrol to give themselves up, an immigration crisis that was once described as seasonal by the Biden administration back in the spring, has shown no signs of slowing now in the middle of December.\par \par \uc2 While official CBP numbers have not been released yet, the Washington Post now reporting that there were 173,600 migrant encounters at the southern border in November. That's a 5% increase over October and a 140% increase over November 2020. It's also the first monthly increase since July.\par \par \uc2 In Laredo, Texas Tuesday night, Texas DPS troopers found 100 illegal immigrants being smuggled inside of a suspicious tractor trailer. They were all locked in from the outside and there was no source of ventilation. Troopers arrested a 17-year-old in connection with the incident.\par \par \uc2 In the Rio Grande Valley last night, Border Patrol stumped a pickup truck on highway 281 and found migrants crammed into the bed of the truck being smuggled. The driver was arrested.\par \par \uc2 Also, in the Rio Grande Valley yesterday again off of Highway 281, a source tells us Border Patrol arrested this suspected human smuggler who had dressed up as a fake oil worker and had actually cloned an oilfield truck to blend in while smuggling four illegal immigrants inside of that truck. And perhaps the most bizarre of them all, border patrol near Tucson Arizona found six illegal immigrants dressed in camouflage being smuggled inside of a hearse, two us citizens were arrested for smuggling them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, so far just this week just here in the Rio Grande Valley sector, Border Patrol reports they've arrested multiple criminal illegal immigrants including three gang members, a rapist, a child molester and a Mexican national with a warrant out for his arrest in the state of Utah for aggravated sexual abuse of a child. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The latest Fox Business poll shows just 36% of registered voters surveyed approve of the President's crime policies, 57% disapprove. The number one reason cited for the crime spike, the breakdown of more values coming in second weaker criminal penalties followed by the availability of guns and decreased police funding.\par \par \uc2 There's growing criticism tonight about what many lawmakers perceive as a lack of urgency from President Biden about the rash of smash and grab robberies throughout the nation. Correspondent Gillian Turner takes a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, \uc2 GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Rico was a game changer.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Legal experts say the Justice Department could bring charges against smash and grab criminals, under what's known as Rico. The Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations statute, Rico has historically been used to prosecute the mob.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: This isn't organized crime. It doesn't look that way, smashing grabs look everything but organized. But if you look more closely, you'll see that it is quite organized.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Perpetrators across the nation are stealing millions of dollars of goods from brick-and-mortar stores, reselling them and getting away with it. Senate Republicans say they'll come up with a nationwide solution if the White House won't.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY (R), LOUISIANA SENATOR: The first responsibility of government is to protect people and property. It's government's job to do that. And if any public official tries to blame it on the victim, shame on them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But it's not just Republicans who are desperate for a national solution.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: It must be stopped. And it's not just San Francisco. It's in our entire country. And that's evidenced by the appeals that we are receiving from across the country for the legislation.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The Justice Department says they are looking for federal level solutions to the crisis. But the White House so far is leaving it to city and state leaders to fend off thieves and vandals.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: What our focus is on is making sure that the local leaders, the police officers and departments who know what they need for these communities have the assistance and the funding they need. And that's what we're working around the clock on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not over.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: DOJ has already used similar federal statutes to go after parents at school board meetings, and experts insist they could also use them to crack down on thieves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): The White House was rolled out two new initiatives just this week alone targeting 15 criminal organizations overseas and they say more action is coming. So, the question now is really, when is the White House going to turn their attention to organized crime that's exploding here at