aking access to the abortion pills more difficult.\par \par \uc2 The coronavirus pandemic is not the only health crisis this country is now facing. We're also getting new information about the epidemic of drug overdoses. Here's Correspondent David Spunt from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We want justice right away.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Frustration and pain outside the Department of Justice, victims of another pandemic overdoses calling on Biden administration officials to take action. More than 100,000 people died from an overdose between April 2020 and April 2021. The highest level recorded.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA GODSEY, MOTHER: Finding your daughter on the floor with a needle still in her hand is an image I'll never get out of my head.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Patricia Godsey lost her 34-year-old daughter, Cassie, to an overdose. She says Cassie became addicted to opioids after a surgery then switched to heroin.\par \par \uc2 GODSEY: It's gut wrenching and you can't do anything. I mean, we tried, we tried recovering. We tried interventions.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Godsey and others want DOJ officials to continue taking on drug makers and prosecute the sucklers. The billionaire family who ran Purdue pharma, makers of OxyContin just today, the \uc2 DEA\uc2  out with yet another warning about overdoses.\par \par \uc2 ANNE MILGRAM, \uc2 DRUG ENFORCEMENT ADMINISTRATION\uc2 : This is an existential threat to our communities, bringing harm and violence and shattering families.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Last year DOJ settled with \uc2 Purdue Pharma\uc2 . Now department attorneys must decide by a court-imposed January 5 deadline whether to oppose a drug injection site in Philadelphia called Safehouse were addicts get high under the supervision of staff to similar places recently opened in New York City advocates say, it will help to control the overdose pandemic.\par \par \uc2 RONDA GOLDFEIN, SAFEHOUSE VICE PRESIDENT: I understand that people who are complaining about what's happening in their neighborhoods but everything that we're trying now isn't working. All we see are the numbers increasing. And so, we have to try something different.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The Trump administration sued to block the Safe House in Philadelphia from opening if the Biden administration sides with the State House. Brett, it will go ahead and unlock the gates potentially for more of these safe injection sites to open across the country. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden's approval numbers go down over his pandemic response. We'll take a look at that. First, beyond our borders tonight, the Kremlin says it has submitted draft documents outlining security arrangements it wants to negotiate with the U.S. and its \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. The proposal comes amid spiraling tensions over Ukraine. The Kremlin says a senior Russian envoy stands ready to immediately depart for talks in a neutral country on that proposal.\par \par \uc2 A powerful typhoon slams into the southeastern Philippines toppling trees, ripping tin roofs and knocking out power as it blows across the island provinces. Nearly 100,000 people have been evacuated there. Coast Guard personnel are rescuing residents stranded by chest deep waters in a southern province where pounding rains and -- or swapping villages in brownish water. Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Omicron is here. It's going to start to spread much more rapidly beginning of the year. And the only real protection is to get your shots.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden warning the new coronavirus variant, Omicron, is spreading tonight here in the U.S. as concerns over Omicron rise. New booster mandates are surfacing around the country. Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has that story tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In mid-January, New York's Metropolitan Opera and \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  will join a growing number of organizations requiring proof of vaccine boosters. The move follows growing evidence that additional doses of existing vaccines reverse some of their waning effectiveness against new variants.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Our booster vaccine regimens work against Omicron. At this point there is no need for a variant specific booster.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: A new Fox Business poll shows voters worry about the pandemic with only 8%, believing it's completely under control the same figure as a year ago. But when it comes to mandates, the results are mixed, with 68% of voters favoring mask requirements for travelers, but only 51% approving a vaccine and testing requirements for larger businesses. CDC officials estimate the Omicron variant accounts for 3% of COVID cases nationwide and expect that figure to increase rapidly.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , CDC DIRECTOR: In some areas of the country. The estimates of Omicron are even higher, including in New York and New Jersey, where CDC projects that Omicron could represent about 13% of all cases.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: A University of Hong Kong study awaiting peer review found Omicron is 70 times faster than previous strains, infecting and multiplying and human air passages leading to the lungs, but much slower infecting the lungs themselves. Researchers say this may explain why Omicron appears to spread rapidly while causing less serious illness. Spikes in mild cases among students have prompted several universities including Princeton and Cornell to move final exams online.\par \par \uc2 And the military continues to mandate COVID shots as a matter of force readiness, with the Marines reporting 94% of their ranks are fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: And late this afternoon a CDC advisory panel voted in favor of new language recommending mRNA vaccines such as \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna over the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  shots for adults. This meeting came amid ongoing concerns over extremely rare yet somewhat elevated levels of blood clots in some \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine recipients. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Johnson Serrie in Atlanta. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Dr. Martin Makary, Johns Hopkins Health Policy Expert and Fox News Contributor. Doc, let's start there with the J&J, how concerned should J&J shot takers be and should they get this other shot?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: While the other vaccines are so impeccably safe? The CDC advisors are basically saying, look we know about a blood clot complication that's one in 100,000 women between the ages of 30 and 50. So given that incidence and observation, might as well with go with the safer vaccine.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We talked about that Hong Kong study and what they're finding about the severity of Omicron. How do you characterize it?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: It's really a remarkable study. It shows that the Omicron variant is not infecting the lung tissue itself. It's infecting the bronchos and the nose passages, the upper respiratory areas of the body, not the lungs where the severe infections form. And this really tells us a lot. It's laboratory confirmation of the epidemiological data that it's not overwhelming hospitals and the length of stay is shorter and fewer people are hospitalized. And, also, it's affirming the bedside observation that these are very mild infections. It's telling us that Omicron will probably be the fifth seasonal coronavirus. There's four others that have circulated seasonally for decades. This will become the fifth. Those other four constitute about 25 percent of the cases of the common cold. This is also going to cause a common cold.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Take a listen to President Biden tonight, how he characterizes Omicron at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For unvaccinated we are looking a winter of severe illness and death for unvaccinated, for themselves, their families, and the hospitals they'll soon overwhelm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Saying unvaccinated, but if you are unvaccinated and you've had it before, are you destined to severe illness and death this winter?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: No. There is absolutely no evidence that those with natural immunity, their body's t-cells are active and they're working, and that immunity is effective against Omicron. That's an \uc2 NIH\uc2  study that recently came out. They should feel good about their immune protection. It's people who are adults with a risk factor that we worry about with no immunity, no vaccinated immunity and no natural immunity. We are worried about Delta and Christmas with those individuals.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So what would you say to the folks who have not been infected, who still are unvaccinated, they are on the fence, what would you tell them right now?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: I would say get vaccinated because we have a lot of people who are adults with a risk factor like obesity, they come in very sick with Delta, and they have a lot of regrets. They wish they would have gotten vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And should people try to get tested before they get together with their family at Christmas?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: It really depends if you've got high risk vulnerable people in that setting. If you do then maybe it makes sense. But if you're around low risk people, people who have immunity, you can feel good about that immune protection.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This "Washington Post" op-ed really perked my ears. And it basically says "The Biden administration has been sidelining vaccine experts. Many people were appropriately outranged when the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , under the Trump administration, made appropriate decisions under what appeared to be extreme political pressure and without advisory committee input. We should apply the same standards to the Biden administration. As a country we are weaker when we bypass our scientific experts. Although some may find it convenient to make policy without their input, the long-term consequence also hurt public health." Do you think this is a big deal?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Yes, this is a scathing op-ed. These are two of the top \uc2 FDA\uc2  scientists who have since left. And they point out that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  bypassed their technical experts. They didn't convene that expert panel that they always convene when voting on a recommendation, and they went ahead and pushed the boosters without convening them. A lot of doctors have concerns about boosters in young people. There's almost no benefit right now based on the current data. That could change. But there are known risks of adding an extra dose to, say, college students, and now a lot of colleges are going to mandate boosters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously COVID overall is very scary. We've lost more than 800,000 people in this country. That's a huge number. But what I'm hearing from you is that Omicron is not the big killer, people should still take it seriously and get vaccinated or do their due diligence.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: That's right. Vaccination is an amazing impeccable, strong barrier for low risk people, and for older people a booster can help. The data really supports people over 65 or if you have had J and J, the single dose, probably should have been designed as two doses. And if you had COVID, you probably don't need that booster. At least there is no data to support it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, are we eventually going to get to a place where this is going to be an annual thing, just like a flu shot, that you're going to have a flu and COVID shot that's going to be able to provide protection?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Many of us think there has been too much of a focus on the antibody levels. You can boost them and get them up. But normally they down over a few months. But your t-cells, that strong immunity, it takes a couple days to kick in once you get the infection. That protects against severe disease. And that's probably durable, long-lasting. Many studies support that, and that's why we feel good about once people get immune protection --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we as a country are not taking that into that much of account when you look at public policy, right?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: That's right. That's right. We could keep giving people boosters forever, but we can't boost our way out of any endemic virus. It's already in the water system in Florida when they have tested it. Omicron is already ubiquitous, and it will be ubiquitous like meningococcal is in 10 percent of all noses of people in the United States, but it's not causing severe illness.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Dr. Makary, we really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, honoring America's bravest heroes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Each of you know what it means to stare down danger as someone with strength in a moment of trial. We're grateful for all that you three have done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The remaining members of a missionary group who were kidnapped two months ago in Haiti have now been freed. Ohio based \uc2 Christian Aid\uc2  Ministries issued a statement saying that the 12 remaining hostages are free. Neither that group nor Haitian police are giving any details. The leader of the kidnappers had threatened to kill the hostages. Authorities said the group was demanding $1 million per person.\par \par \uc2 Senators have given final congressional approval to the Uyghur Forced Labor Protection Act, barring imports from China's Xinjiang region unless businesses can prove they were produced without forced labor. Press Secretary at the White House \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  says the president supports this measure after months of the White House declining to take a position.\par \par \uc2 President Biden meantime has awarded three Medals of Honor to some of this country's greatest heroes. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has the story from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today we honor three outstanding soldiers whose actions embodied the highest ideals of selfless service.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  awarded the Medal of Honor to Master Sergeant Earl Plumlee for heroism in Afghanistan. Sergeant First Class Alwyn Cashe and Sergeant First Class Christopher Celiz received the medal posthumously.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Outnumbered, with no regard for his own safety, at times armed with only a pistol, Staff Sergeant Plumlee attacked the insurgent forces, taking them on one by one.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Ten insurgents wearing Afghan Army uniforms and suicide vests blew a 60-foot hole through the base wall with 400-pound car bomb in Ghazni Province in 2013. Plumlee, an active duty Green Beret, killed one Afghan insurgent using a grenade, and another suicide bomber with precision sniper fire.\par \par \uc2 MSGT EARL PLUMLEE, (RET) MEDAL OF HONOR RECIPIENT: It's a life and death profession we are in. You may only get one chance to get something right. So, put your all into it and do it to the best of your ability.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Sergeant First Class Alwyn Cashe is the first black recipient of the Medal of Honor since Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Alwyn Cashe was a soldier's soldier, a warrior who literally walked through fire for his troops.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: He pulled six fellow soldiers out of burning Bradley fighting vehicle in Iraq in 2005, even though he himself was drenched in fuel. He died in a \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  hospital with burns over 72 percent of his body. Three of the six soldiers survived. He was the youngest of 10 children.\par \par \uc2 KASINAL CASHE WHITE, SISTER: I tell anybody, he taught me to live, enjoy it, make the most out of every opportunity, because you don't always get a do-over.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Sergeant First Class Celiz put himself between the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and a rescue helicopter during a fierce firefight in Afghanistan three years ago.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Sergeant Celiz used his body as a shield for the aircraft and his crew against the heavy incoming fire.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: He waived off the pilots who wanted to return for him after he had been shot, knowing they would all likely die if they did.\par \par \uc2 KATIE CELIZ, WIDOW: He didn't believe in awards and everything. He believed that you did good for the sake of doing good.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: At the Pentagon, Jennifer Griffin, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing stories.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel, Marc Thiessen, Mollie Hemingway, Juan Williams on what's happening to President Biden, his agenda, why one publication says no one is listening to him, and the list of potential Democratic candidates by another. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): I think Build Back Better is dead, not for just 2021, but forever.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): The agreement among all the Democrats was that we would push those two things forward together. Obviously, we are having a problem pulling the second half of that across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 RON WYDEN\uc2 , (D-OR) SENATE FINANCE COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: We continue to push ahead with the parliamentarian. This is a unique kind of procedure.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I'm not going to have a postmortem on something that hasn't died.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, one thing has died, that the Senate parliamentarian minutes ago earlier today says the plan to help millions of immigrants, the immigration portion of Build Back Better needs to fall out of the bill. That is not going to work for the Senate parliamentarian. That is a blow to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the president has released a statement. This legislation is stalled. He said "My team and I are having ongoing discussions with Senator Manchin. That work will continue next week. It takes time to finalize these agreements, prepare the legislative changes, and finish all the parliamentarian procedural steps needed to enable a Senate vote. We will advance this work together over the days and weeks ahead. Leader Schumer and I are determined to see the bill successfully on the floor as early as possible." It's not before Christmas. And there are questions about where it goes after the new year.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "Washington Post" columnist Marc Thiessen, Mollie Hemingway, senior editor at "The Federalist," and Juan Williams is a FOX News analyst. Marc, this is not a good day tore Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": No, it's not. And look, if the Democrats want to win in 2024, maybe they ought to pick a Manchin- Sinema ticket because they're the only Democrats who seem to be in touch with what the American people are thinking -- 64 percent of Americans said in a "Washington Post" poll that they are concerned that Biden and the Democrats are giving us too much government, getting government too involved in our lives, 46 percent to 15 percent they are concerned that this bill is going to fuel inflation and that's one of their number one concerns. And in West Virginia, where Joe Manchin, people vote for Joe Manchin, those are the people he cares about, 59 percent oppose the bill, 64 percent say it will make inflation worse, and 75 percent say now is not the time to pass major spending bills that could make inflation worse. So I don't know what kind of pressure they think they can put on him, but his people are backing him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, Senator Manchin has made clear from the beginning what his stipulations are, under $1.75 trillion for Build Back Better. If you do a program, you've got to go the whole way to 10 years, not one year. And yet, people seem surprised that he is holding the line here. It's probably not only him, but that's the one getting the attention.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think the primary concern at the moment is the childcare tax credit, because he had previously said he was at least for that for one year, and you know some of that is going to expire or expiring as we speak, Bret. So the question is, is he a man of his word on that point?\par \par \uc2 But the talks continue, as you saw in that statement from President Biden. And for all the talk about 2024, look, I think first, let's deal with 2022 next year in the midterms. And Democrats know history is not on their side when it comes to those midterm elections. The party that has the White House often loses, and given the reality of the moment, it doesn't look good with President Biden's low approval numbers.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, that's a year away, even the 2022 election, much less 2024. And I think Democrats at this point are strongly behind Biden having passed the COVID relief bill, having passed infrastructure. They see Biden as the likely nominee going into 2024. If he doesn't run, they see Harris as the likely one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I guess questions come from some of these left leaning organizations, let's say CNN, for example, does this thing, who could replace Biden in 2024, and has a list of possible candidates. And everybody does stories, but how about "Slate"? "Slate" is not exactly a right leaning organization, but it says "Why isn't anyone listening to Joe? Biden's delivery can unintentionally make him seem unconvinced of his own proposals. That doesn't reassure. At worse it provokes unease. Lack of fire may be costing him an audience he needs to convince of his effectiveness. And it must be said that nostalgia for a lost and better America is strangely reminiscent of Trump's. The president's messaging is stuck in a black hole. His party's hopes depend on his ability to break out."\par \par \uc2 And Mollie, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " says he should say right now he is not running.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, SENIOR EDITOR, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, let me just first say, I would like Republicans not to celebrate the defeat of this spending package prematurely. If you remember, Republicans thought that they had defeated Obamacare, they elected \uc2 Scott Brown\uc2  to the Senate, and then a few months later, even though it was a deeply popular package, it was pushed through, and Democrats were able do that. So they should be cautious and make sure they see this to the end.\par \par \uc2 But the problems that Democrats are saying about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , obviously they are struggling in the polls. They are deeply unpopular. But I don't think they are particularly unlikeable candidates. The reason why they're unpopular is a Democratic Party wide issue. It's the policy. Yes, it's partly that we were told that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would be a uniter and a moderate, and he has governed as anything else but that. But the entire Democratic Party shares his views on rolling back what was working on the border, or destroying the pipeline so that our gas prices go up, or putting trillions of dollars into the economy and having inflation and costs rise.\par \par \uc2 All of these things aren't unique to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . In some regard, it might even be worse when you go to outside that field. And so Democrats need to really be honest about why they are unpopular, and it's not the personality of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marc, on the flipside, it's a big deal that they got through COVID relief, $1.9 trillion. It's a big deal that the Biden administration led the way and got through the bipartisan infrastructure bill at almost $2 trillion that administrations have been trying to get for a long time. But they tied it to this Build Back Better bill, and that seems like it's falling apart. And they maybe are not getting the benefits off the positivity off of the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: The problem with the COVID relief bill, while it's a political victory, is they poured $1.9 trillion into a $300 billion hole in the economy and unleashed inflation and a supply chain crisis and a massive labor shortage. So that hasn't been a net positive for them because that's what is driving down President Biden's popularity. It's not the passage of the COVID relief bill. It's the effect of the COVID relief bill.\par \par \uc2 And as for the bipartisan infrastructure bill, one, he held it up. He asked his own party in the Congress to hold it up as leverage for this multitrillion dollars Democrats only bill. And he should have had four or five things like this. This is what he promised. He campaigned on a promise not to bring socialism to America. He said I'm not the socialist. He said I'm going to unite the country. I'm going to bring Republicans and Democrats together and get things done. That's what I did for 40 years in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 All he has got is the bipartisan infrastructure bill. He could have done police reform, on China, on a whole bunch of different things, and so that's why he's in trouble.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm just saying come March, come April, if you see the president at a bridge building in someplace that's a swing state, it could be a different deal.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, tomorrow's headlines with the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a look at tomorrow's headlines with the panel. Marc, first to you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Biden signs a bipartisan defense bill he didn't want. At a time when the China threat is rising, President Biden sent a defense budget that actually cut defense after inflation. Republicans and Democrats in Congress united and overwhelmingly raised the defense budget by $24 billion more than he wanted. So Biden is delivering on promise to unite the country, just not on the way he intended.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Tomorrow's headline, I think, is going to say that Senator Manchin rejects a ban on offshore drilling, Bret. The coal state Democrat, as you know, from West Virginia really is siding with the oil companies despite increased concern about climate change. But he is who he is. He's Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Mollie.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Americans are wondering whether football games will be played as the milder COVID variant works its way through teams.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thanks guys.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME" hosted by Will Cain starts right now. Hey, Will.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020211217ehcg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Tornadoes Hit Multiple States; Interview with New York Mayor \uc2 Bill De Blasio\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Wallace, Steve Harrigan, David Spunt, Guy Benson, Juan  Williams \par \uc2 9,036 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 Deadly tornadoes slammed six states and killed dozens of people. We'll have the latest.\par \par \uc2 And the White House under increasing pressure for a federal response to rising crime across the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have concerns about what we've seen. Serious concerns about retail theft in a range of communities across the country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice-over): The Biden administration promising new resources to stop smash-and-grabs and support law enforcement in the nation's cities, as mayors play defense over fallout from protest, bail reform and spikes in violence.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (D), NEW YORK CITY: Still, New York, the safest of the top 20 big cities in this country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask outgoing Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  about that and resistance to his sweeping new vaccine mandate for all businesses.\par \par \uc2 Then, prices surge as inflation hits highs not seen in decades, consumers facing higher costs for gas, food, and clothes.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says more government spending is the solution.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My plans do not add to inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But Republicans blast what they say is the real cost of Build Back Better?\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The truth is it adds $3 trillion to the debt.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll speak with \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , the top Republican on the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus -- a Supreme Court ruling keeps in place a Texas ban on most abortions. We'll ask our Sunday Panel what it signals for Roe v. Wade ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And our Power Player of the Week. The doctor hoping to create an unlimited supply of life-saving organs.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We begin with breaking news. Emergency crews are searching for survivors and digging through the destruction after a trail of deadly tornadoes hit six states, the largest and most devastating, tearing across more than 200 miles with Kentucky bearing the brunt.\par \par \uc2 The governor saying 70 are dead but that could grow to more than 100.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Steve Harrigan live on the ground in Mayfield, Kentucky, with the latest -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, ordinarily, when you see the aftermath of a tornado, you can see the path, one street gets hit, maybe the next street over does not get hit. That is not the case in this town where the destruction is almost complete.\par \par \uc2 The candle factory was one of the largest employers here. At midnight Friday, a tornado brought the building down, and 110 workers inside, including a trapped woman broadcasting a cry for help.\par \par \uc2 Go on with the houses, the businesses, the trees, the landscapes, are fathers, daughters, coworkers, friends.\par \par \uc2 EMILY EPPERSON, \uc2 AMAZON\uc2  DRIVER: One of my best friends is inside there right now. I'm assuming. We haven't heard from him since the tornado hit about 8:30 last night.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Unfortunately, her friend was one of six killed when the tornado collapsed the wall at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): When you talk to people who survived, some of them appear stunned. And just one day, some of them have lost their house, their car, their job, their business, their records, their photographs, everything -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Steve Harrigan reporting from Kentucky -- Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the spike in crime across the country. At least a dozen major cities hitting all-time homicide records already this year. Meanwhile, major retailers are pleading for help as smash and grab robberies are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 The White House says more guns and the continuing pandemic are behind the crime surge but critics point to changes in cash bail and more lenient prosecutors, calling on the federal government to do more.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll talk with the mayor of the country's biggest city, New York's \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to David Spunt at the White House for a look at how the Biden administration is responding -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, the White House and Justice Department are pumping millions of dollars into fighting crime but city leaders on the ground say it's not working fast enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: This bothers me very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  sounding the alarm on a nationwide spike in crime, from smash-and-grabs in California, to more than 1,000 murders this year on the streets of Chicago and surrounding areas, America's cities, small and large, are under the microscope and breaking records.\par \par \uc2 DERMOT SHEA\uc2 , NYC POLICE COMMISSIONER: When you have people that have no regard for others, expecting them to change their behavior dramatically, it's not working.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The White House fending off criticism from the \uc2 National Sheriffs' Association\uc2  over just how much the Biden administration is helping.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The president and members of our administration have been longtime advocates for supporting and funding the cops program, something where the president proposed almost $300 million in additional assistance through his budget from what it was last year.\par \par \uc2 VERNON STANFORTH, NATIONAL SHERRIFS' ASSOCIATION PRESIDENT: Three hundred million dollars across every jurisdiction across the country would equal about $18,000 per jurisdiction. How is can help? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: On the streets of the Big Apple, it's not only crime grabbing headlines. Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 's new private sector mandate will require everyone 12 and older to show proof of two vaccines, ages 5 to 11, proof of at least one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The mandate, Chris, begins December 27th. It affects nearly 200,000 businesses -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: David Spunt reporting from the White House -- David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is the mayor of New York City, \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 . Mayor, welcome back to "Fox News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (D), NEW YORK CITY: Thanks so much, Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to start with something you said this week. Here you are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Even looking back on the difficult year of 2020, it was one of the most difficult years ever for this city, but still New York City is the safest of the top 20 big cities in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, according to the \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2 , through last Sunday, six of the seven so-called index crimes are up this year, including murder, robbery, and felony assault. The only one that's down is burglaries.\par \par \uc2 Mayor, you know what Disraeli said about lies, damned lies, and statistics. In fact, doesn't your city have a serious crime problem?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Chris, we've got a lot to do. There's no question about it. But let me tell you first of all, in the eight years I've been mayor index crimes, major crimes in New York City are down 11 percent over eight years. And we did it bringing police and community closer together.\par \par \uc2 So the key is to overcome this horrible patch we've had in the COVID era all over this country. And we bond police and community, get those guns off the street. And the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has been doing a fantastic job. The most gun arrests we've seen in over two decades.\par \par \uc2 So yes, there's been problems, for sure, and more to do, particularly in certain parts of the city. But I will guarantee you this -- and our police commissioner, \uc2 Dermot Shea\uc2 , said this this week very clearly, we are going to go back to where we were pre-pandemic and then get safer still. And we have been the safest big city in America and we will remain.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But again we get to statistics. Murderers are up 45 percent over the last two years. Robberies are up 4.5 percent just in the last year. And I want to take a look, want you to respond to your police commissioner, \uc2 Dermot Shea\uc2 , and what he said just this week about people who are arrested being allowed out without even having to post bail.\par \par \uc2 Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DERMOT SHEA\uc2 , NEW YORK CITY POLICE COMMISSIONER: When you have mass amounts of people put back on the streets that have traditionally been held in jail, you're seeing some of that permeate here as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: When you say that New York is the safest big city in America -- and I'm sure you can find statistics to back it up -- don't you risk looking out of touch? I think there are an awful lot of New Yorkers who don't feel that.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Chris, look, we've got to make sure that not only are we safe but people feel safe. There's no question, both are important. But, look -- look around this city right now, this city is thriving. We have come back strong from COVID. And the bottom line is that there is -- today in New York City, a much safer city than we were eight years ago. There's no question about that.\par \par \uc2 The two years of COVID have created immense problems and I agree with Commissioner Shea. There's some changes that we need in the laws in Albany. We need our court system back and running, which it hasn't been over these last two years.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is doing their job and they're doing it with the community. And this is crucial, Chris, that bond between police and community got really hurt deeply last year. But that's being fixed and healed now. And it's making a difference. We're getting guns off the street. We are going to turn this situation around.\par \par \uc2 I have confidence and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  has confidence that we're going to get back to the pre-pandemic level soon and then surpass and become even safer. And the statistics show it in most of the city. There are a few places where we're struggling. There's no question we have more work to do, for example, in the Bronx. But in most of New York City, the statistics show we're getting back to the pre-pandemic levels and then going better and further.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But Eric Adams, who takes over as mayor on January 1, ran on a tough-on-crime platform. And one of his main tenets was he said that he's going to restore an anti-crime unit that you disbanded.\par \par \uc2 Here is Mr. Adams.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR-ELECT OF NEW YORK CITY: This is going to be a city where we're going to be safe, we're going to have effective policing that's not heavy-handed, we're going to have the backs of our police officers, but we're going to hold them accountable to do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: When voters elected Eric Adams, I mean, of all the candidates running for mayor, weren't they in a sense rejecting you?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Eric Adams is someone I've worked really closely with, Chris. And it's pretty well known in New York City I supported him and helped him in every way I could to get elected. And I think he's going to continue what we started and build upon it. He was a police reformer as a police officer. This is well known. And he believes that we need to improve the relationship between police and community as key to safety. That's what we did with the strategy of neighborhood policing.\par \par \uc2 So I think there's a tremendous amount of continuity. You know, Commissioner \uc2 Dermot Shea\uc2  believed we needed to change that plain clothes unit and I agree with him. Those officers are now in uniform and they're taking more guns off the street this year than we've seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 So I want to say Commissioner Shea had a view strategically of what would work and he proved it and I backed him on it. I believe that was the right way to go. But I'll tell you, I think Eric Adams is going to take everything that has been done and take it to the next level of safety for this city. I have a lot of faith in him.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let's turn to COVID. You just imposed a new mandate that all private businesses must have all of their employees vaccinated by December 27, no exception for -- well, if you're not vaccinated you can get tested regularly instead.\par \par \uc2 Why impose what may well be the most dramatic and sweeping COVID vaccine mandate in the country just four days before you're going to leave office, sir?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Because my job is to protect New Yorkers. And, look, the bottom line is we've gone through the worse crisis in our history, we've lost tens of thousands of people in this city, it's been incredibly painful -- I've talked to a lot of New Yorkers who lost a grandparent, a parent, someone they loved and I have to keep them safe and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Excuse me, sir. Why not allow the new mayor, who's going to come in four days later, to impose that mandate if he's going to do it? You're sticking him with it.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : No. Every time we've put a mandate in place, Chris, it has worked. We're at 71 percent right now of all New Yorkers. All New Yorkers. Seventy-one percent fully vaccinated. We're leading the country.\par \par \uc2 And that's because we use incentives and mandates and every single mandate we've put in place has greatly increased the number of people vaccinated. Since the first mandates back in August, we've had over a million more doses, and that's why the city is open and thriving.\par \par \uc2 Chris, really vaccination equals freedom because it allows people to get back to work, get back to their lives, be safe wherever they are, school, a workplace. It works and that's why we're continuing to deepen it.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: This week New York City became the biggest city in the country to allow non-citizens, people that are in this country legally for as little as 30 days, to vote in municipal elections, non-citizens to vote, for instance, for mayor of New York City going forward.\par \par \uc2 You expressed some concerns about the legality of this law but you said you're not going to veto it. Why not?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : I have mixed feelings. I've been very open about it on this law and I think there are big legal questions. But I also respect the city council. They made a decision -- look, the bottom line here is we've got a lot of -- this is an important issue, of course. But what we just talked about is a central (ph) issue, defeating the Omicron variant, defeating COVID so we can go back to life in the city. That's where my focus is.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, let's talk about your focus going forward. There is some talk and you are apparently openly considering running for governor next year. I want to take a look at this recent poll. Let's put up the numbers.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who replaced \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , now has 36 percent support. You're way back in the field at six percent. And when voters statewide were asked about you, 28 percent had a favorable opinion, 55 percent unfavorable. Mayor, do you really have a shot?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Chris, that poll you showed, that horserace, that reminds me of pretty much every election I've ever been in. I've been the underdog many, many times. It's not where you start, it's where you finish. And elections are a chance to tell people your vision and what you've done.\par \par \uc2 My vision is that we've got to change a lot of things in this state, including helping families. Working families are struggling in the State of New York. They need a lot more help.\par \par \uc2 Parents need a much better situation for their kids then they have right now. The school day does not account for parents' schedules at work. Summer is tough for parents. I proposed a plan to change that by asking those who have done very well to pay a little bit more so families can have a decent life in New York State.\par \par \uc2 So I think people respond to ideas and vision and accomplishment. And I'll be going all over the State of New York talking to people about just that.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So, in 10 seconds, you sound like you're running.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Well, I'll have more to say on that soon. Right now, for the next few weeks, finishing my term as mayor, my goal and my focus, keeping this city safe from the Omicron variant, turning us -- you know, getting away from the COVID --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : -- era. And, again, this is why a tough, strong approach with mandates is what has kept us safe. This is one of the safest places in America when it comes to COVID and we intend to keep it that way.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mayor \uc2 de Blasio\uc2 , thank you. Thanks for your time this Sunday. Good to talk with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO\uc2 : Same here, Chris. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss what's happening across the country with both crime and COVID.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): These are Democrat prosecutors who are deliberately refusing to enforce the law, and they're deliberately saying that when we have crimes, we're just not going to prosecute them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Missouri Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  laying responsibility for the spike in crimes across the U.S. squarely on Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group: Guy Benson of FOX News Radio; Julie Pace, executive editor for "The Associated Press", and Fox News political analyst, Juan Williams.\par \par \uc2 Julie, certain crimes are up dramatically. Murders are up, although not nearly at the level they were in the `90s and we've seen these shocking images, you know, just every time you see `em, it's kind of stunning, of these organized smash-and-grabs, oftentimes in high end stores.\par \par \uc2 How big a problem for President Biden and Democrats both in terms of the politics? And also, is there anything substantively they can do about it?\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, THE ASSOCIATED PRESS: Well, I think in terms of the politics, you know, crime is one of those things that can really overshadow basically any other issue for people. If you don't feel safe, if you don't feel like your family is safe in a community, then there's almost nothing else that matters. So, from a purely political perspective, you know, there are the statistics and then there's how people feel. And if people feel like they're living in an era where crime is increasing, then they're going to perhaps react at the ballot box there.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to actual actions, you know, there are some things that the administration is considering in terms of federal assistance, federal advisory groups for local police officers. But often, this is really a state and local issue. But for Democrats, obviously, this trickles down, if people like Democratically elected officials are not taking crime seriously enough are not doing enough to prosecute as some of what we're seeing, then, certainly, I think there could be some blowback there.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Guy, Republicans are hammering Democrats on the national level, even on a few points, no bail for non-violent crimes, prosecutors in some jurisdictions not even enforcing laws. And, of course, there's defund the police.\par \par \uc2 How legitimately an issue is this? Clearly, it is on the local level. How legitimate an issue is it on the national level?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS RADIO: Oh, it's a big issue, and I think Julie's right. I would call it trickle up is the effect here, where it might be happening at local levels because, as you mentioned Chris, some prosecutors and DAs have effectively decriminalized low level crime or even mid level crime.\par \par \uc2 But if that's seen as a Democratic issue, that could trickle up to the White House, and certainly two congressional races coming up, in 2022. I see Republicans hammering on this -- on this general issue set virtually every single day, and I think a big element of it, Chris, is the recidivism, the re-offending of people who in many cases get out with no bail or very low bail from that horrible series of killings up in Waukesha, Wisconsin, at the Christmas parade, all the way to the guy who burned down the FOX News Christmas tree in New York. He was out within hours because of so called reforms in New York.\par \par \uc2 There are people who are dangerous who should not be on the streets. And far too many of them are and I think people feel that.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Juan, even in a city as liberal as New York City, and I just had this conversation with Mayor \uc2 De Blasio\uc2 , you see a former cop, Eric Adams, elected mayor largely on a law and order agenda.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Eric Adams represents most Democrats on this issue. You know, the issue is trying to have safe streets at the same time, and here I'm pretty much using Adams's language to hold police accountable when there's misbehavior.\par \par \uc2 You know the thing about this. Chris is, I spoke to Adams during his campaign, and he told me that if working class people in New York are told by any candidate that they were that they plan to defund the police, he said, that candidates going to need intense police protection.\par \par \uc2 This is not a Democrat problem. I think that it's California Governor Newsom who said this week that Texas has a higher rate of violent crime than California. And if you look at the charts right now, I think it's like five of the top states in terms of murder rate per capita have Republican governors.\par \par \uc2 So when you look at this, I really think it boils down to a gun problem. And right now, close to 80 percent of all these murders are being committed with guns. And Adams wants really tough attitude towards getting guns off the street.\par \par \uc2 You know, in New York, there's a big issue about stopping and frisking, especially minority youth, and he says he's afraid in favor of stop, talk and frisk, not ending stop and frisk, stop, talking and frisk. So, I think he doesn't want Starsky and Hutch on the streets. But he wants safe streets, and I think that's what most Democrats want.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let's turn to COVID and President Biden's vaccine mandates. His requirement for federal contractors to get shots now blocked in court. Same with a mandate for health care workers and for businesses with more than 100 workers.\par \par \uc2 Here's Democratic Senator Jon Tester.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JON TESTER (D-MT): In the state of Montana, folks are saying, business people are saying this, you know, even though the owners think it's a good idea to get vaccinated, they're saying this puts me in a bind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Julie, Tester and Senator Joe Manchin, two Democrats voted with all 50 Republicans in the Senate to defund, to block President Biden's mandate for big businesses.\par \par \uc2 PACE: And this is a huge frustration for the Biden White House because they look at the state of the pandemic in the United States, and they say there is one way and science backs this up. There is one way to get control of the spread of the virus, and that's for more people to get vaccinated and in places where you have had vaccine mandates that have gone into place, you've seen vaccination rates rise, you've seen COVID rates come down.\par \par \uc2 But if those -- if those mandates continue to be blocked by the court, the Biden administration looks at this and says, how are -- how are we going to get those vaccine rates up if we're not mandating it? What is the other method for doing so?\par \par \uc2 And then they want they worry about the economy. They worry about our ability to get back to sort of life as normal if we continue to have rates, where they are.\par \par \uc2 So again, deep frustration at the fact that the one thing that they see as our solution for getting out of this pandemic continues to be blocked.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Guy, I mean, Julie is entirely right. COVID cases are up. COVID deaths are up, and now we have the Omicron variant that we really know very little about. Is opposing mandates is a good politics is a good public health?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Well, it's good enough politics to have two Democrats joining with Republicans in the Senate to vote to block what Biden has done, and I would point out that President Biden himself and top members of his team insisted to the American people months ago that there would be no mandates from the federal government. And then they instituted some anyway.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But wait, wait and fairness, Guy, that's before the Delta variant and you know, the science changed.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Correct, but I think people hear from the president, we're not going to force people to do something. And then yes, situations on the ground change. They say, well, never mind. We are going to try to force people to do something. I think that won't sit well with many voters and has not in many cases.\par \par \uc2 And I also was struck by something that Mayor \uc2 De Blasio\uc2  said, and he has said this in previous comments as well regarding his very heavy-handed mandate, which is he has cited Omicron as the reason why it's sort of like the triggering of the instigating effect here that brought this about.\par \par \uc2 Based on what you just said Chris, it's true. We are still waiting for a lot of good data on Omicron. But thus far, it does appear to be significantly less virulent. No deaths that we have seen so far around the world. The numbers have fallen significantly in terms of hospitalizations and certainly deaths in South Africa, where it's been circulating for a month. Those are good signs.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right, panel. We have to take a break here. We'll see you later in the program.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  in what he says is the real cost of President Biden's Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: On Friday, the CBO estimated the true cost of President Biden's social spending bill is not $1.75 trillion, as advertised, but a staggering $4.9 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, South Carolina's \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , the top Republican on the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2 , who says the bill could be paving a path to socialism.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You commissioned the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  to project how much Build Back Better will cost over the 10 years, assuming that the programs that are in it, the spending programs that are in it, go on for 10 years and are not as in the case with child care just for one year.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The CBO found, instead of adding $200 billion to the deficit, it will add $3 trillion to the deficit. But, Senator, the White House says that that's fake because if the programs are extended, they'll find ways to pay for them.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, give me a plan to pay for them then. President Biden said the bill was fully -- fully paid for. Vice President Harris said it was paid for. Schumer, Pelosi, Secretary of Treasury Yellen. The CBO says it's not paid for. It's $3 trillion of deficit spending. It's not $1.75 trillion over 10 years, it's $4.9 trillion.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean? The House should re-vote. The vote in the House was based on a fraud. This bill doesn't cost $1.75 trillion, it costs almost $5 trillion. It doesn't add $300 billion to the deficit, it adds $3 trillion.\par \par \uc2 There is not a plan to pay for it. If there is, I missed it. So, give it to me. Give it to the American people. Before we vote in the Senate, show me how you pay for this bill.\par \par \uc2 And you know why I wrote a letter to CBO, because Joe Manchin came to me and he said, I think this bill is full of gimmicks, that these programs won't go away, Lindsey, and if you score them for 10 years, I think the bill will double. Well, it didn't double, it was almost 2.5 times.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope that this will be a showstopper for Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 But, in 2017, President trump and all Senate Republicans voted for the tax cuts then that added $2 trillion to the economy.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And, in fact, you talk about budget gimmicks, it used the same budget gimmick there. For instance, saying that individual tax cuts we're going to end in 2025.\par \par \uc2 Here's White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The plan they proposed and passed for and passed into law, the 2017 tax cuts, were $2 trillion, which were not paid for in any way, shape or form. That didn't seem too upset them at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator, does she have a point?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Oh, no, not really. I like giving money back to the taxpayer. I don't like spending more money than we did in World War II. And that's what we're doing right now.\par \par \uc2 This bill, Build Back Better, it's $5 trillion. It's not 1.75. It will add $3 trillion to the deficit. When they tell you the bill is paid for, their lying. When \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  brought it to the House with the CBO score of 1.75, it was a lie. When she said it was 300 billion to the deficit, is was a lie.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, sir, respectfully, what she has -- what \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is saying, and a lot of Democrats are saying is, that when President Trump and you -- you collectively --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: What's that got to do with anything?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let -- let me just finish passed the 2017 Trump tax cut --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: That was a lie. It wasn't paid for.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: No, what happens is you can't go beyond 10 years in terms of the budget window.\par \par \uc2 We voted knowing that cutting taxes we believe would be good. I never said that cutting taxes -- I voted for cutting taxes. I'm against expanding the government.\par \par \uc2 They're telling you it doesn't cost anything, Chris. Well, the CBO says it costs $3 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin, if you're watching, you were right, Joe. You got this right. It is a lie. It's $4.9 trillion, not 1.75.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: It's $3 trillion. So Joe Manchin was right.\par \par \uc2 Now, what the hell are you going to do about it? If you say it's going to be paid for, give me the offsets. Do you raise taxes by $3 trillion? Do you cut the government by $3 trillion?\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about what's in the Build Back Better plan. Families in your state of South Carolina with two children, on average, now spend 21 percent of their income on childcare. The Build Back Better plan would cut that to 7 percent of their income on childcare, pre-pandemic 14 percent of children in South Carolina lived in poverty. By blocking Build Back Better, you'd ended a child tax credit of up to $3,600 per year per child.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator, just as a practical matter, won't that hurt a lot of families in your state?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: As a practical matter, my -- families in my state are getting crushed by inflation. A 6.8 percent inflation number came out Friday. It's the highest inflation rate in 40 years. So it makes your paycheck go less. It's crushing people who are working.\par \par \uc2 So, if you pass Build Back Better, it declares war on fossil fuels. It gives tax credits to buy electric vehicles made by union plants, which hurts \uc2 BMW\uc2  and Volvo.\par \par \uc2 This bill, Build Back Better, will be gasoline on the inflation problem. It destroys the ability to extract fossil fuels. Gas prices went up 58 percent. The last thing I'm going to do is add more burdens to working families in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And here's my message to the Democratic Party, quit lying about this bill. They should revote it in the House. And, Senator Manchin, you were right. I hope you will stand up and stop this madness. We need to stop Build Back Better before it destroys this country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right, let me talk to you about another issue.\par \par \uc2 This week 14 Senate Republicans voted with the Senate Democrats to set up a process, not to vote to increase the debt limit --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But to set up a process of the Democrats by themselves could vote to increase the debt limit. You were not happy about those 14 Republicans, the way this played out, and you blasted Senate Leader McConnell for setting the GOP up to, quote, get shot in the back. And then you said this, quote, it's pretty obvious to me that this will not be received well by the Republican faithful, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, are you that worried about former President Trump and his base?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: What I'm worried about is that for four months the Republican Senate said we would not lift a finger to help the Democrats raise the debt ceiling. We would make them use reconciliation, which is -- that they can do by themselves to raise the debt ceiling because that's the process they used to spend the money. At the end, we did not make them use reconciliation, which changed the rules of the Senate in a House bill. I don't like that a lot. I like Senator McConnell.\par \par \uc2 But here's what happened, 70 percent of Senate Republicans voted against this and every House Republican but one voted against it. What we did is promised one thing and deliver another.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- but -- but --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: It was a mistake.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- but, sir, I mean, in the end, the Democrats are going to end up raising the debt limit by themselves. All we're talking about here is setting up a process so they can do it. Is that really such a big deal?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: It apparently was to every House Republican. It was apparently a big deal to 70 percent of the Senate Republicans. It was a big deal to me.\par \par \uc2 The way we did this is we literally changed the rules of the Senate and a House bill. We did not use the reconciliation process.\par \par \uc2 But here's my point, if we're going to be successful in 2022, we're going to have to work together as a team. And here's what I would say to every Republican, if you want to be a Senate Republican leader in the House -- excuse me, a Republican leader in the House or the Senate and you don't have a working relationship with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you cannot be effective. So, I hope we'll get on the same page here.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, you know, what we're talking about here is something a lot more serious than the process to set up the debt vote. If you -- you don't raise the debt limit, the country defaults.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: If that happens, it tanks the markets. It has -- it probably puts us in a recession. It means that things like Social Security checks and veterans benefits don't go out.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I understand all that.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Do you really want to play politics with that?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Every time we raised the debt ceiling, we get something for it, generally speaking. I wanted to -- to have some budget reforms. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , the last time we raised the debt ceiling on Trump's watch, wanted more spending.\par \par \uc2 What we said as a party is, we're going to make them use the reconciliation process to raise the debt ceiling. The Democrats caused this problem. They needed to fix it. If you do it -- if you help them, you're legitimizing this spending. So it really is not about the process as much as it's about keeping your word.\par \par \uc2 We let a lot of people down. Senator McConnell's been a great leader on many things, but we're going into an election cycle where the wind's to our back. We can't do this again.\par \par \uc2 But when you look forward to this party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the most consequential Republican in the entire Republican Party, maybe in the history of the party since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . And if you're going to lead this party in the House and the Senate, you have to have a working relationship with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or it will not work.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator Graham, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Thanks for coming in today. It's always good to talk with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next we'll discuss the Supreme Court's Texas abortion ruling and what it means for Roe v. Wade, when we come right back with our Sunday panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I'm going to continue to fight for the constitutional right of all women to make decisions about their own body without interference by some legislative group of people that think that they can replace their judgment with hers.\par \par \uc2 MARJORIE DANNENFELSER, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  LIST PRESIDENT: The law will remain in effect, as it has for quite some time, already saving thousands of lives and we hope more to come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Two very different reactions to the Supreme Court's decision Friday to leave in place Texas' tough abortion law while allowing for limited challenges to it.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Julie, the Supreme Court allowed the Texas abortion law to stand. And while it said that providers can sue, it -- there's a real question as to whether they can sue in a way that they could actually stop the law.\par \par \uc2 Which raises the question, what does this -- it's a separate decision, largely procedural, but what does it indicate about what the Supreme Court may do later this year, later this term in the Mississippi law, versus when it comes to Roe v. Wade and whether it's allowed to stand?\par \par \uc2 JULIE PACE, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, THE ASSOCIATED PRESS: Well, I think that's exactly right. The ruling on Friday left this narrow opening to sue, but it is extremely narrow. And I think that raises a lot of concerns among people who -- who would like to see abortion rights continue in this country about what the court will do in this larger case involving the Mississippi law. And I think we already saw in the arguments, you know, some of the justices really signaling, you know, pretty clearly that they are willing to either substantially restrict abortion rights or perhaps even overturn Roe completely. And I think that that decision on Friday, again, is just going to raise that concern about where that broader ruling is going to go in the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Guy, if the cord ends up in the Mississippi case, and we'll probably hear it in late June or early July, if it ends up sharply limiting Roe v. Wade, or perhaps even overturning it, how big an issue does abortion become in the 2022 midterms?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, TOWNHALL.COM, "THE GUY BENSON SHOW" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, it will certainly be an issue. And, of course, should that happen, should Roe be curtailed or supplanted, that issue, abortion, would be restored to the states for people to make decisions based on their legislative representatives on those policies.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the national picture on abortion, I know some Democrats have said, oh, this would really energize our base. It could really turn the election. I think polls suggest that Americans are focused on a lot of other big issues, not just abortion. It could very much fire up or motivate certain people out there. There's no question about that.\par \par \uc2 But I did see a poll this week in Texas, which is Ground Zero on this fight, with that -- with that restrictive, new law. It's been in place for weeks now. And Governor Abbott, who signed that bill into law, is leading his leading Democratic opponents, his top would-be opponent, by 15 points, including by eight points on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's a guessing game how much it would actually affect races. I think there's sort of spin on both sides so far.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Juan, I think it's just a fact of political life in America that for the last 50 years pro-life advocates have been an extremely powerful force and certainly a big force in the Republican Party. Let's assume this all flips and Roe v. Wade goes away. How effective, how powerful do you think the pro-choice forces would be in politics as we head into the 2022 midterms?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think you're right, Chris, I think for the last 50 years, since Roe v. Wade was put into law, abortion opponents have been on the march. They've been on the offensive to try to end Row v. Wade. Now, if it flips, as you describe it, I think what you'll see is that people who are for abortion rights in this country will be on the offense. They'll be marching and they'll be saying that they are protecting women's rights.\par \par \uc2 I mean they also will have a powerful message to take the voters because right now, according to Pew, 59 percent of Americans favor keeping Roe v. Wade as it is. The status quo. And 75 percent of Americans, according to "The Washington Post, "think abortions is a matter between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the number one issue in all midterms is turnout. And what you get, of course, is the economy is the number one issue. But in states where you have governors races, legislative races, as governor -- as Guy was referring to, this is going to be a big issue, and it's going to lead to increased turnout, I think, especially among one very critical group, suburban white women. And Democrats are absolutely thinking that's to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right, let's turn to the Biden/Putin phone call earlier this week about -- about 100,000 Russian troops massing at the Ukraine border. After the phone call here was National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: I will look you in the eye and tell you, as President Biden looked President Putin in the eye and told him today that things we did not do in 2014 we are prepared to do now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Julie, what do your sources at the White House tell you, do they think that President Biden made headway, and, if so how much, with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Well, they feel good about a couple of things. One, they do feel good about the message that Biden delivered. They think he was extremely tough. They think he was very clear, as Jake Sullivan said, that there are measures beyond what the Obama administration did that the Biden administration would be prepared to do. They also feel really good about the unity among western allies. And that's been something we haven't seen over the last couple years, unity on Russia among the United States and -- and the European. So they feel like they're in lock step with those European leaders.\par \par \uc2 But the big question is how Putin responds. And that's really how they'll know if they made any progress here. If he backs off of his posture in Ukraine or if he moves forward, and that is, in some ways, going to be out of Biden's hands.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, I want to talk to you about that, Guy, because, you know, there's been some criticism of President Biden because he didn't block the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  natural gas pipeline from Russia to Germany, and there was some question would he bring that up unilaterally. And the answer we heard from the white House was, well, if Putin were to go ahead, that they believe Germany will support them and cut off the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, which is something, as well as Ukraine, that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  cares a lot about.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, and it's only one thing. There are other major factors at play here and other basically threats that appear to be on the table that go far beyond just the pipeline, which I do think was a mistake by Biden. I was pleased to see yesterday he's starting to sound a little bit tougher in some of the things that he's saying Biden is.\par \par \uc2 The question here is the -- the psychology of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . What does he think he can get out of an invasion potentially of Ukraine and is the deterrent effect of all these other threats that are now being put out there and mentioned, are those powerful enough to change his calculus because over the last 10, 15 years we've seen that what the west has done has not stopped him. He continues to do what he feels is in his interest. Is this time really different? Are the threats that are being made explicitly or implicitly enough? And I don't think we have a great answer to that yet.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Finally, Juan, you got some big news this week about your nephew, Chris Williams. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Chris, I can't -- this is out of the world news for my family. We're so thrilled. Chris was one of 12 people -- one of 10 people picked out of 12,000 to join \uc2 NASA\uc2 's 2021 class of astronauts.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, who will go into training in Houston. So, you know, we were thrilled with Chris when he graduated from Stanford, MIT and \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 . Now he's going into space. He's going to be an astronaut.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations to my brother, Roger, his wife, Ginger, but the whole family, we're -- we're all out of space on this one. This is too much.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I got 30 seconds left. I have always thought that I, and, frankly, you, have the wrong stuff. When did you know that Chris Williams had the right stuff?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: When he was a little boy, and he was wearing a onesie that had astronauts on it. That's how much this kid -- I mean he's always had an ambition to be an astronaut, and it's come true.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And -- and was that onesie the aspiration of his parents or him?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it was him, Chris. That's how you get to MIT and Stanford and \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Thanks panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our "Power Player of the Week," the leading surgeon who's made a remarkable breakthrough in organ transplants.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right now there are more than 100,000 Americans on the waiting list for an organ. And each day we lose 12 of them. But what if there were a way to unlock the tragically short supply of organs?\par \par \uc2 Here's our "Power Player of the Week."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT MONTGOMERY, DIRECTOR, NYU LANGONE TRANSPLANT INSTITUTE: We're only able to supply about 25 percent of the organs that are needed for the people on the waiting list each year.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): Dr. Robert Montgomery of the NYU Langone Transplant Institute on the urgent need for organs. It drove him to a medical breakthrough, the first ever pig to human kidney transplant.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Xenotransplantation is using a non-human organ and transplanting it into a human.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The genetically engineered pig kidney was attached for 54 hours to a deceased donor who had been kept on life support.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: The transplant went really well. In fact, the kidney behaved and looked just like a human kidney.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: It was a major test of whether xenotransplanted organs may be a solution to the crisis.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): What was the key to genetically engineering the pig kidney so that the human recipient wouldn't reject it?\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: We knocked out a particular gene that has been lost during evolution from pig to man. And so we can avert rejection.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): This is personal for Montgomery, who was just 15 when he lost his father to heart disease.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: One of the doctors, at one point, mentioned a transplant but said that, you know, at age 50, he was too old. And that stuck in my head my whole life.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Montgomery went on to become a world renowned transplant surgeon. But he also faced heart troubles.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: I've had seven cardiac arrests and have been resuscitated from them. All of this has just really heightened my resolve and my resilience to really try to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: In 2018, Montgomery needed help. He turned to the team he had created.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): How did it feel when the team you put together was going to give you a heart transplant?\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Well, you know, I hired them because they were the best in the world.\par \par \uc2 I'm doing great.\par \par \uc2 You know, I had complete confidence in them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You're doing fantastic.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): Montgomery is married to legendary opera star Denyce Graves.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): How is it to be married to someone who is literally a diva, a prima donna.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: You've met Denyce. She's a very down to earth person.\par \par \uc2 I understand why we refer to opera singers as divas.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): Montgomery's xenotransplant made global headlines, but he reminds us it's just a first step.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: We're probably a year or two away from the first in living human trials.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: He hopes putting his heart and his history into this research will save lives.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: I've been both a surgeon and a patient my entire career, and that's kind of a unique thing and I need to -- it's my duty to make the very best of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Montgomery says if the trials go well, pig kidneys could be available for regular transplants to humans within 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Finally, a personal note.\par \par \uc2 After 18 years, this is my final FOX NEWS SUNDAY. It is the last time, and I say this with real sadness, we will meet like this. Eighteen years ago the bosses here at Fox promised me they would never interfere with a guest I booked or a question I asked, and they kept that promise. I have been free to report to the best of my ability to cover those stories I think are important, to hold our country's leaders to account. It's been a great ride. We've covered five presidential elections, interviewed every president since George H.W. Bush, traveled the world sitting down with France's \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  and Russia's \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I've gotten to spend Sunday mornings with you. It may sound corny, but I feel we built a community here. There's a lot you can do one Sunday mornings. The fact you've chosen to spend this hour with us is something I cherish.\par \par \uc2 But after 18 years, I have decided to leave Fox. I want to try something new, to go beyond politics to all the things I'm interested in. I'm ready for a new adventure. And I hope you'll check it out.\par \par \uc2 And so, for the last time, dear friends, that's it for today. Have a great week and I hope you'll keep watching FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020211212ehcc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Inflation Rises At Fastest Rate In 29 Years; Impact Economic Concerns May Have On 2022 Midterms; Severe Consequences If Putin Invades Ukraine; DOD: Still "Sizeable" Russian Force Near Ukraine; White House: "Severe Consequences" If Putin Invades Ukraine; Texas Six-Week Abortion Ban Remains, Challenges Allowed; SCOTUS Leaves Texas Abortion Law But OK's Challenges; White House: Abortion Ruling Shows Women's Rights At Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Juan Williams, Mercedes Schlapp, Jennifer  Griffin \par \uc2 8,375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS HOST: I'll see you at 6:00 for "SPECIAL REPORT."\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, thank you very much, you guys. Both of them are off the market, by the way. All right. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Here is THE STORY: New York City's incoming mayor. This is very interesting. Eric Adams, says he will not surrender this city to violence, and to those who would "burn it down," his words. He has been as you may have seen at odds with the local BM leader, BLM leader I should say in New York, who has warned the new mayor, not to put the plainclothes police which were pulled off the streets in 2020 back on the streets, which the new mayor has promised he will do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If they think that they're going to go back to the old ways of policing then we're going to take to the streets again, there will be riots, there will be fire, and there will be bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, today right here, the debate between these two men continues as Hawk Newsom joins us with his reaction to Eric Adams, that's coming up this hour. But first, have you seen the shocking inflation numbers that came out this morning? This is not what anybody was hoping for, but these numbers keep creeping higher as the country plunges into territory that we really haven't been in in 39 years, with some prices popping as much as 58 percent. That takes a bite out of your wallet since last year.\par \par \uc2 So, this is obviously a major concern for U.S. voters and for families across the nation, as these very real concerns, including crime and labor shortages, as well are prompting some really surprising shifts in party loyalty as we look ahead to the midterms. Take a look at this poll, which is understandably getting quite a bit of attention for people who watch politics and the voting groups very closely. According to the new numbers from the Wall Street Journal: Hispanic voters are now evenly split in their allegiances between Democrats and Republicans. That's a significant move, given that President Biden beat President Trump with Hispanic voters by 30 points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, what's going on out there? Mercedes Schlapp, former White House Strategic Communications Director; and Juan Williams, Fox News Political Analyst, great to have both of you with us today. So, hi to both of you. Obviously, economic concerns -- Juan, I'll go to you first, they don't, it doesn't matter what color you are, what you know, what your background is, these are hitting everybody across the board, and we know that, that is one of the biggest concerns of all voters, regardless of their background.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Absolutely, right, Martha, and that was a fascinating poll. And it showed that there was a particular drop off for Democrats among Hispanic men. And again, when you talk to them in the focus groups, according to the Wall Street Journal/NBC Poll, what you see is they're talking about the economy. And of course, anybody talking about the economy, especially today is talking about inflation. And about I would think, with some particular regard to high gas prices now.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the whole thing about this being inflation, being transitory, I think, transitory, it just went out the door. I think that right now, people are concerned, you know, but in all honesty, it's troubling to see this inflation. But I think from the Democrats point of view, it's not something that they see as alarming, not drastic, because you still have strong job numbers that came out last week, we have very low unemployment. We have wages going up, but people don't like inflation. And I think that's why people react by saying they don't feel good about this economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, one of the big issues here at Mercedes, is that, you know, politicians and political parties take different groups for granted. And, and with the --\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FORMER WHITE HOUSE STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And with the assumed population, increasing population of Hispanics in this country, I think Democrats have always just figured, look, all those voters are going to be ours. But they're finding out that that isn't necessarily the case. Listen to this, from Representative Richie Torres, a Democrat from New York, here's what he's saying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RITCHIE TORRES (D-NY): We have to be careful not to treat the Latino vote as a model and not to take historical constituencies of the Democratic Party for Brexit, because we can lose those constituents and we have lost those constituencies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mercedes, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Yes, the Democrats have been very comfortable in that they thought they were making inroads with the Hispanic community. But the problem is, is when the Democrats and the leftist are pushing for example, even simple things like changing the Spanish language, going from Latino and Latina to Latin-X, which Hispanics don't even connect to that word, when they're pushing a radical agenda items like Critical Race Theory, gender confusion at the age of five years old, that for Hispanic parents is very alarming.\par \par \uc2 Add to that the economy as Juan brought up. This is critical because what's happening is, is that the Hispanic families they want to make ends meet. You know, they're working hard to make sure that they're able to achieve this American dream and what are we seeing? Rents going up. Housing prices going up. Food prices, prices going up. They can't afford it and it's putting an enormous amount of stress on so many working families out there.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's why you're seeing the Hispanic community, basically saying, wait a second here, I came here for a better life. I'm raising my kids and all of a sudden, I can't afford to live in America, and they're going to blame it on the party that's in charge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you see the sharpest decline for President Biden among, among racial groups. The sharpest decline is among Hispanics, Juan. Texas Hispanics, obviously a state that blue voters would love to see, do them do, themselves do better in. 54 percent disapprove of how this president with the job that this president is doing? Does this trend concern you, Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, look, you know, I'm a Democrat. But here is your political analyst, Martha. I think the Democrats have every reason to be concerned, not just in Texas, and not just among Hispanics. You know, when you look at President Biden's poll numbers, and people talk about them now being I think, in the Real Clear Politics average, are in the mid to low 40s, that drop off is not because Republicans have suddenly turned against the president. Republicans weren't with him in the first place.\par \par \uc2 What you're seeing his black voters and Hispanic voters and to some degree, the independent voters say they don't like this economy. And I think that's a really important point given that right now, the President is still out there pushing the Build Back Better plan. And you have people saying, hey, could this add to inflationary pressures in the country? The contrary point would be the policies, things like lower taxes for the middle-class childcare, help, help with childcare expenses, are popular programs. And so far, the economists say they will not add to inflation, but it's a delicate dance and it has real political consequences if not handled well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it sure does. Yes, in the other polls that show only one in four voters say that they think that Build Back Better will actually improve the economy. So, that, that's a big issue for Democrats as they look forward to the midterms. Juan, good to see you. Good to have you here. Mercedes, good to see you as well. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, still ahead on THE STORY, the Black Lives Matter leader who is at odds with New York City's crimefighting mayor-elect and president -- and Secretary, excuse me, Mike Pompeo and Ambassador Robert O'Brien join me in their first joint interview as President Biden is put to the test on the world stage. They'll be here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking right now, roughly 100,000 Russian troops are masked on the border of Ukraine, no movement since President Biden's call with Russian President Vladimir Putin this week, Fox News had obtained these satellite photos that you see in the middle of that screen that show a clear Russian military buildup along that border over the last several weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden on the call with President Putin said that there would be "severe consequences, economic consequences and others," was the phrase that was used, and he said that would be like none President Putin had ever seen. But no more specifics beyond that. In moments, we're going to talk to former Secretary of State Mike Pompeo and former National Security Adviser Robert O'Brien, but first to National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin, who was just out of the briefing at the Pentagon. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, it became very clear listening to Press Secretary John Kirby here at the Pentagon just moments ago, we questioned him about the build-up on the Russian forces on the border with Ukraine, it became very clear that there has not been any change since the President spoke to Vladimir Putin earlier this week. The status quo seems to be holding, and Russian forces steam seem to be along those borders. Here's what John Kirby said, when I asked him about those forces just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: I would say that there still is a sizable amount of Russian forces in the western part of their country around the borders, while the border with Ukraine, to the, to the north, to the east, and to the south of that, that extreme eastern part of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We also asked John Kirby about a statement that came out from the \uc2 Russian Foreign Ministry\uc2  earlier today in which the Russian said that if NATO would rescind its potential offer down the road to Ukraine and Georgia to join NATO, then possibly there could be a reduction in tensions and, and those forces could be removed. I asked John Kirby about that, and he really kind of tapped danced a bit.\par \par \uc2 And it's really not clear what NATO stand is in terms of the offer to Ukraine and Georgia. Clearly, that's something that's been dangling out there for some time. But it's not clear that the administration or NATO is willing to take that off the table. So, right now, we are really still in a very serious standoff on the border with Ukraine, all eyes on what Vladimir Putin plans to do next.\par \par \uc2 Also, at the Pentagon briefing, there were questions about future training between the U.S. military and Israel when it comes to potential military options should Iran, the talks with Iran fail in terms of the nuclear program there. Kirby said that, in fact, those are routine exercises, even though reports from Israel suggest that may be more than that. Back to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the President certainly has a lot on his plate. And Jennifer, thank you very much, Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Let's bring in former Secretary of State Mike Pompeo, a Fox News Contributor and Former National Security Adviser Robert O'Brien. Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us, obviously on these really big topics.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Great to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, thank you. So, Secretary Pompeo, let me start with you. Do you believe that Vladimir Putin is going to cross the border with more of his troops that are masked on the border right now into Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Martha, I think that's still up in the air, and depends on the behavior not only of the United States, but the European countries that have the most important interest in this.\par \par \uc2 Remember, there's a lot of history here, Martha. Vladimir Putin thinks that the breakup of the Soviet Union was the greatest catastrophe of the 20th century. He then watched a pipeline be attacked by people from his country inside of Russia, \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Remember, they shut down the southeast corner of gas, and he watched the United States do virtually nothing under President Biden. And he remembers it was President Biden who wouldn't even provide anything more than sleeping bags and lemonade to the Ukrainians the last time he took one- fifth of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We need to establish that deterrence. If we get it right, the Europeans get it right, we can prevent Vladimir Putin from engaging in this kind of coercive, thuggish behavior.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mr. O'Brien -- Ambassador O'Brien, excuse me, you know, when you hear Jennifer Griffin, reporting about the back and forth on the Russians, and Vladimir Putin wanting us to rescind any discussion of NATO inclusion for Ukraine and Georgia, as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 And that there's not an unequivocal, you know, that invitation will not be rescinded. Does that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT O'BRIEN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: No, it doesn't surprise me. And then, Secretary Pompeo laid out the context exactly right. When Mike and I were in office, the Russians had two very primary foreign policy goals.\par \par \uc2 One was the, excuse me, a clean extension of the New START nuclear treaty, where they didn't have to do anything, but could continue to violate the treaty and build up nuclear arms outside the treaty.\par \par \uc2 And the other was to have \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  completed. We had -- we had stopped both those things during the Trump administration. They were both given to the Russians in exchange for nothing.\par \par \uc2 And so, those tools that we had are -- those chips are now taken off the table. And now, Vladimir Putin's mass troops on the Ukrainian border, and is now got a new demand. And that is that we preemptively take off the table, the ability of Georgia and Ukraine, and free people in both those countries, at least the portions of those countries that the Russians don't already occupy to join with the free world.\par \par \uc2 So, again, we got nothing for the earlier goodwill. And now, he's coming back to the table and saying, what have you done for me lately, and he wants more. So, we've got to be very careful how we handle this situation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And now, Secretary Pompeo, you have Ukraine commanders really sounding the alarm. They're saying we cannot hold off a Russian invasion on our own. We'll be overwhelmed, were their words.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Martha, I've seen the statements. Look, the Ukrainians are capable fighters. Have been down in the southeast part -- have been down in the southeast part of the country in the Donbas. I think Ambassador, O'Brien is been down there as well.\par \par \uc2 They are real warriors, but they need support. Not only from the United States in terms of ammunitions and armaments, but from their European partners as well. Remember, this is European sovereignty that's at risk here, and they need to be first. First in line to make sure they get it right to protect the Ukrainians from this coercive behavior from Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is there any sign that, that will happen, Ambassador O'Brien? Obviously, Germany has a lot invested in this \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, they went ahead with that deal over the objections of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that Germany and other European allies will present a real military threat potentially in any form to the Russians in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Well, Secretary Pompeo is absolutely right in it. And Martha, it's not just been a military threat, it's got to be a diplomatic and economic threat to the Russians as well.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, we haven't seen much from the new German chancellor, there's been very little coming out of Berlin. And they've been, frankly, quite soft on Russia for several years now. Was -- and that's something we need to see change. Unless we want to see European borders start being changed by the use of force, as we saw in the 1930s.\par \par \uc2 Now, there was some good news that came out of Paris, President Macron made some very strong statements on Ukraine -- the Ukraine sovereignty and, supporting Ukraine and, potentially even supporting Ukraine militarily.\par \par \uc2 So, it was interesting to see that the Paris has taken a harder line so far than London or Berlin on this, and so, that was a promising sign. But we -- it's not alone -- it's not enough to have Macron alone sounding the alarm and support for Ukraine. We need the new German chancellor, we need Prime Minister Johnson and others do, to get involved, as Mike pointed out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it was interesting to listen to Jake Sullivan, Secretary Pompeo, talk about the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, essentially saying, well, we could turn it off again.\par \par \uc2 You know, I mean, it has the -- oil has not started flowing through it, but the construction is largely done. And so, he sort of presented that as we can still hold that over their heads. We can -- we can withdraw that. Is that -- do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Martha, they probably can. But it gives me no joy for them to have learned the lessons that we told them during the transition. The things we knew that mattered to the American people to protect our security interceptor. All this is what this is really about. It's about making sure that we have the rule of law, things that matter to American businesses and jobs here at home.\par \par \uc2 You know, they probably can do things to shut it down although it's a lot further along than it was -- what 10-1/2 months ago when we left office. I regret that they're now even thinking about allowing that pipeline to continue to be built. They should shut that down immediately. It's not in the best interest of Europe. It's not in the best interest of the United States of America to allow that pipeline to be completed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, it's tough to see any real barriers that Vladimir Putin would see to moving forward with his intent as it appears on the border there. So, we'll see what happens. Gentlemen, terrific to have both of you on this today.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ambassador O'Brien, Secretary Pompeo, great to see you both as always.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Great to be here, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 O'BRIEN: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 So, New York City's mayor-elect puts a BLM leader on notice for talking about bloodshed in the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAWK NEWSOME, LEADER, BLACK LIVES MATTER, NEW YORK: Because we believe in defending our people. So, there is no way that we are going to let some Gestapo come in here and harm our people. Right? We prayed for peace, but black opportunities, prepare for the worst.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, how to Hawk Newsome respond to the latest from the incoming mayor of New York? He joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I watched you, you know, talking on a bunch of different interviews today. And you said, burn it down. You said burn it down. It's time. So, that makes me think that you want to -- you want to burn it down.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: I didn't say, it's time. I said, if this country -- if this country doesn't give us what we want, then, we will burn down this system and replace it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A decision today by the Supreme Court that keeps a controversial Texas abortion ban in place, but allows challengers to that ban to go forward. The Texas law bans abortion after about six weeks when a fetal heartbeat is detected. And it is up to private citizens, not state officials to bring lawsuits against clinics and doctors who perform them beyond to those dates.\par \par \uc2 Remember, just days ago, the Supreme Court heard arguments in a landmark case out of Mississippi directly challenging abortion law in this country.\par \par \uc2 Dan Patrick is the lieutenant governor of Texas, and he joins me now. Good to see you. Lieutenant Governor. Thank you for being here this afternoon. What was your reaction to this decision out of the court today?\par \par \uc2 DAN PATRICK, LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF TEXAS: Martha, I've been on with you a number of times over the years. Never has there been a greater day for babies. Babies in the womb, if you could hear them are celebrating today, because innocent life now has a chance to live because of this Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 This is a total victory for the heartbeat bill. When I was a senator, I wrote the Sonogram Bill many years ago back in '07, finally passed in '11, which cut abortions down by about a third in our state when women were entitled to see a sonogram, to see it's a real baby with a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 This bill will further reduce the number of abortions in Texas dramatically as it already has. And I want to thank Attorney Jonathan Mitchell and Bryan Hughes, our senator who carry this bill, Senate Bill 8, along with our legal staff.\par \par \uc2 This was a very creative way and legal way and constitutional way to save life. And the court upholding it today, Martha, is just a great day for life in America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. I just want to put a couple of things up on the screen. This is a poll from the Washington Post on this Texas abortion ban. It was done, November 7th to the 10th. It says the state law in Texas authorizes private citizens anywhere in the country to sue anyone who performs or assists an abortion in Texas after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Do you reject 65 percent want the Supreme Court to reject it? 29 percent want of them to uphold it. And here is the reaction to the news today from the White House press briefing. This is Jen Psaki.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The ruling this morning is a reminder of how much these rights are at risk. And how women across the country who have now in Texas, especially. It's been 101 days since this was put in place. 101 days, where their health -- their health, their access to what has been law of the land for many decades now has been questioned and put, and in challenge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, those are both -- those two examples that I just put up are both national reaction.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But one of the things that I think is really at the heart of this debate right now is who should decide right? Whether states should be able to pass their own laws with regard to this. And that's what we see at play in the Mississippi case as well.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the indication that you see the three justices who were appointed by President Trump all voted in favor of this today, and you had a dissent from Justice Roberts. Is there anything to be read into what they decided today that may give us an indication of where the Mississippi case is going?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Well, Martha, I think it looks very positive for that Mississippi case based on today's ruling. But I don't want to focus on that, I want to focus on what happened today and something that Jen Psaki just said.\par \par \uc2 We have reduced abortions in Texas since this law took effect September 1st, saving 1000s of babies -- 1000s. So, is she celebrating? Is she celebrating the saving of innocent life? It doesn't sound like it. It sounds like she would have preferred those babies not to have been born.\par \par \uc2 Look, we care about mothers that are in crisis pregnancy positions. We personally -- I personally put in the budget this last session $100 million, so that we can provide health care, diapers, all types of care for mothers who want to put their babies up for adoption, we've had over 100,000 women take advantage of that -- of that program.\par \par \uc2 But this is about life. This is about a heartbeat. And in Texas, 55 percent of Texans -- forget the national polls, support this bill. 59 percent of Hispanic voters support this bill.\par \par \uc2 And at -- and a -- and a large number of African American voters support this bill. So, we get back Martha to the basics of what we're talking about here. And this decision by the Supreme Court said, hey, this goes back to precedent set by this court for 100 years of state's rights and sovereignty? Not just wiping away laws.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the justices said we're not a roving commission to take away laws we do not like. And so, but at the heart of this, let's get -- let's put the legal issues aside. This bill has now been confirmed to be constitutional and every other state can pass it, the Supreme Court said.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: But it's about innocent life of a baby with a heartbeat in a womb. It's no question it's a life, and that baby has a right to live the American Dream, and this is why it's a great day, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lieutenant Governor Dan Patrick, thank you very much. We'll watch this case closely. We're watching the Mississippi decision closely as well. And we thank you very much for being here today, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. All right, switching gears here. After threats like these --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAWK NEWSOM, CO-FOUNDER, BLM GREATER NEW YORK: If they think that they're going to go back to the old ways of policing, that we're going to take to the streets again. There will be riots, there will be fire, and it will be bloodshed because we believe in defending our people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I should say he says he's not threatening that. He's saying that's what he believes will happen if these plainclothes officers come back onto the scene in New York. And New York City's incoming mayor is putting the Black Lives Matter New York organization on notice saying the city will not back down, will not surrender, says Eric Adams, to those kinds of pressures. Hawk Newsom response here only on "The Story" right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: There will be riots, there will be fire, and it will be bloodshed. I am not threatening anyone. I am just saying that it's a natural response to aggressive oppression, people will react.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Black Lives Matter of Greater New York co-founder Hawk Newsom last month after meeting with New York City mayor-elect Eric Adams about his plan to crack down on crime by returning the plainclothes officer unit to New York City streets. It was disbanded in the summer of 2020. So, Adams is a retired \uc2 NYPD\uc2  captain and he reportedly fired back with this, "We are not going to surrender to those who are saying "we're going to burn down New York." Not my city, he said.\par \par \uc2 Pete Hegseth is standing by with his response. But first, let me bring in Hawk Newsom to respond to that, Hawk. Thank you very much for being here. When you listen to the words of Eric Adams, and I know we covered the meeting that you had with him not that long ago, what goes through your mind? What's your response to him on that?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Well, the mayor is engaging in a political circus and my parents didn't raise a clown. So I'm not here to play these circus type political games with him. I'll say this, the mayor took a trip to Africa, which wasn't covered by the press, his appointees wasn't covered by the press, so he threw my name out there so he can get into media.\par \par \uc2 Look at what happened after we engaged in this back and forth. He was on Colbert, he was on Anderson Cooper, he was on Bill Maher. I made him famous, right, for the spat we had. And if the mayor truly cared about what's best for the city, he would have talked about the things we agreed upon in that meeting. But he didn't want to discuss that, Martha. He wants just hype and attention.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's what he says. He says he wants to bring this unit back. Listen to what he said. He said I was elected and this is what I ran on, and I'm going to follow through on that because that's what the people of New York City have asked me to do. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, NEW YORK CITY MAYOR-ELECT: I said I was going to reinstitute an anti-gun unit in plainclothes. Voters voted on that. They're going to have their body cameras on on every interaction. We're going to use precision policing to identify the gang members. We removed the precinct level plainclothes unit. That was a bad decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So since that unit was disbanded, we've seen homicides, almost double in New York City. So he's saying I want to save city, and a lot of New Yorkers respond to that really positively. They want to save city. They want to be able to go out on the streets. They don't want people breaking the windows and stealing stuff. They want to see children be safe on the streets. What is wrong with anything that he just said there?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Well, here's the problem. The man does not have a plan. He's about to be though. Yes, he does have a plan.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, he does have a plan.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: He's going to put the anti-crime police unit back on the street.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Wait, can you listen to listen? Can you listen to listen? Are you going to yell at me? You're going to let me make my point. You just talk, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Don't do, OK? Don't do that, all right? Because I can interject, and I can say -- you said he doesn't have a plan.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Let me make a statement.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I (inaudible) Africa and Anderson Cooper that no one cares about.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: That these things that he's saying, precision policing?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: -- focusing on gangs, these are all things that de Blasio said. Crime isn't just up in New York City, it's up around the country. So what is he going to do?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He's the mayor of New York City.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: He's had more time. He's had more time\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm telling you. He want to put that unit back on the street. I spoke with the New York City detective said to me back in June of 2020 --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's take turns here, OK? I let you answer. Let me ask you a new question.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And then I'm going to let you respond, OK? I spoke with the New York City detective when that unit was disbanded. He said you mark my words, there are going to be kids dying, this is going to be really bad. Let me put up on the screen. 21 children have been killed in the streets of New York mostly in gang violence. The purpose of this unit that he wants to put back on the street. This is his plan, tou asked what his plan was, was to clean up illegal guns, violence and hard drugs on the street. So I'm asking you what is wrong with putting this unit back on the street if it keeps children of New York City and adults safer?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: OK. There's this thing called the Constitution, and the Fourth Amendment protects us against illegal search and seizure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Secondly, this unit has been responsible for the most high profile deaths in the city. He isn't saying what he's going to do differently. For your journalistic integrity, just ask the right questions What is his actual plan? He's just talking and drumming up new stories so that we could get this back and forth going to get -- between two black men, instead of talking about the things we agreed upon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's not what it's about. No, that --\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: Which was feeding people good food, right? But you want to talk about us being in the streets. We have a school where we teach kids how to be safe every single day. Today, I spent the day looking for lost 11-year- old. I don't get paid by the city to do this. I'm not --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, God bless you for that. And that is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: You know what, just one last thing, Martha, one, one, one last thing, OK? Nobody wants our communities to be violent. But there are other ways to do it. He's not saying what he's doing differently, though, the Democrats have been talking about body cams for the last four years. People have poor. You just reported that if inflation is up. People don't have money, people are starving.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: People need to be safe, Hawk. They need to know that the police are going to have their back.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOME: The employment run up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They need to know that when they go out on the street --\par \par \