r \par \uc2 You see it, you live it, you breathe it, you see it right now in New York City. And the reason it's across the country, the argument he wanted to make is because the same DAs and the same type of Democrat mayors are making the same kind of defund the police moves and bail reforms, which are letting the gangsters and the criminals out of jail to commit repeat offenses. So his logic doesn't make sense to begin with.\par \par \uc2 He's dealing in trafficking in grievances, and he's pointing the finger at Eric Adams who ultimately is right to say no one will burn down my streets, no one will riot, no one will loot, and ultimately we need businesses to come back, tourists to come back and average people like kids in tough neighborhoods to be safe. And if you really care for that, you would do everything possible to take out the gangs who are responsible for most of this violence.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Overwhelmingly, the murders that happen in New York and we see the murder numbers have almost doubled since 2018. Our children under the age of 21, mostly in the toughest neighborhoods in New York.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So those are the kids who are dying. That is -- they are the victims of, you know, drug gangs, of gangs, all of it. So those are the people who need to be protected more than anyone else, because they're the ones who are dying. Eric Adams, the stakes are so high for him, Pete, as you and I both know in this city, because he's promised a lot. And he has the promise of being someone who I think can make people on both sides of the aisle feel better about living in the city, feel safer living in the city. So the stakes are very high and he is going to have to follow through on his plans and it's not going to be easy. So we do wish him well across the board as --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Sure do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- New Yorkers, as he heads into January 1 when he will take over as mayor. Pete, it's always good to get you to weigh in, so we appreciate you coming here today. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. Great interview, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. So also breaking right now the White House pressed on whether the President rushed to judgment in defending Jussie Smollett Jessie small at now that we know that his hate crime was a fake.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Both the president and vice president tweeted at the time of the attack. President tweeted, what happened to Jussie Smollett must never be tolerated in this country. We're with you Jussie. The vice president called it an attempted modern day lynching. Since the guilty verdict, are there any lessons learned here in rushing to judgment when a crime is alleged?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, those tweets didn't go too well, did they? The response with Leo Terrell next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking moments ago, the White House saying that it respects the jury's decision in the Jussie Smollett case. The actor was convicted on five counts of lying to the police about being a victim of a hate crime. Fox News White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich asked press secretary Jen Psaki if there are any lessons to be learned about rushing to judgment and speaking out about this case, long before it ever went to a jury. Here's part of that response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: There are lessons learned perhaps for everybody who commented at the time, including former President Trump. I wish April Ryan was here because I think she asked him the question. Do I see your scorn or no? OK. She just left where he said I can tell you that it's horrible. It doesn't get worse in response to her question about Jussie Smollett at the time.\par \par \uc2 (END 