ar \par \uc2 We catch somebody illegally with a gun. And it's pled down to a misdemeanor, and they're out. We've had people, you know, that have been charged with gun crimes three and four times. I believe the surest way to get a handle on holding people accountable when they're doing things illegally with a gun is to punish them. And that's not happening in many communities across America today.\par \par \uc2 There is a NICS background check when you go in to attempt to purchase a firearm that requires you to go through this. There's a huge number of people that are prohibited from buying that firearm that are caught by the system that are never prosecuted for trying. We have gun laws in place. We're not holding people accountable. We have felony firearm in Michigan. I'd like to see anybody that's caught illegally possessing and using a firearm in a criminal manner, should get felony firearm and do hard time. If we're going to hold people accountable, let's hold the criminals accountable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is there anything in the father's history that should have precluded them from being able to buy that firearm?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Not that I'm aware of.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, how frustrating is it to -- I know you mentioned that you guys have been trying to talk to the suspect and his parents are telling them not to talk, how frustrating is that for you and your deputies and your investigators?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I believe regardless of what the parents or the individual suspect does, our investigators are well on the path to create a very, very good case to hand up a time to the prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, I have one more question about the actual shooting. In a crowded hallway of school and (inaudible) the initial victim even know what was going on (inaudible). The reason I'm asking, (inaudible) kids in school.\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I know how kids walked through a hallway. We always tell the kid, you know, keep your eyes open, be careful. But if a kid is walking next to the (inaudible) there's not a whole lot you can do. The initial victims who was shocked, did they actually know what the heck was going on when it happened?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I would have to say no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So there's all that there was a female retired deputy who was helping out at the school yesterday. Do you have more information on who she is and how she was able to come out?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: There private security, is employed by the school district in addition to us having school resource officer that covers acts for schools. I know some of them were there yesterday. I'm not sure all the specific ones. So we really don't want to get into identifying personnel at this point. But there were private security, people employed by the school and I know at least one or two were there yesterday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, the prosecutor said, I'm sorry, she said it's being considered or looked into but where do you sit with the evidence you've found? Do you see any potential criminal negligence on behalf of the parents?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I think that would have to be a TBD as the investigation to be determined as the investigation unfolds. And we present it to the prosecutor, and then she'll make the decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, how the gun get inside the building?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: You know, it's speculative, obviously, but my guess is he carried it in, either in his backpack or in his waist.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, I apologize for the repetitive question. Can you explain again the meeting of that morning, and the day before? He was meeting with his parents about his behavior?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: The day before it was a meeting with school personnel about some concerning behavior. And the meeting the day of was with school personnel and parents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: About the same issue?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: About a different issue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What was that issue?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I really can't go into the specific issues on either day, but two different meetings happened.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you give us any insight into his mental state, there was obviously suicide watch is moved to the county jail. Have they continued? Are there concerns about that?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Well, typically, anytime we have somebody with active participating role in a tragedy such as this, they're put on suicide watch just to ensure that nothing happens. So that that's kind of SOP, standard operating procedure and, absolutely, if he gets transferred to the Oakland County Jail, that will happen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There have been a number of copycat threats throughout the county today --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Sadly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- these are already non-busy but I assume that is on the texture, the resources --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Yes. Spot on, Ross (ph), yes. There are some -- some weird anomaly. Every time something like this happens in the country, there's a whole bunch of copycat threats and texts, and pictures, and it really burdens law enforcement all across the nation. We're actually in schools today investigating what we think are part of that incredibly disturbing trend.\par \par \uc2 And that's, again, it raises the level of anxiety of parents, students and teachers in schools that had nothing to do with this individual incident, but are now feeling very much anxious and tied to it. And that's how it broadens this whole anxiety and depression that many parents and students are feeling. And it's incredibly disturbing. And so, when those kids or whoever we find out, call in or do these texts, false threats, you know, as you've seen us in the past, we attempt to get charges on them.\par \par \uc2 Even if it's not a credible threat, the mere threat is a crime. And if you make a threat, we're going to seek charges.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Again, apologies on the redundancy, no evidence of bullying from where you sit right now, correct?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I specifically myself asked the school district, they had no information in any of their records that he had been bullied, and we have had no information brought to our attention from any other source that he had been bullied.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sheriff, the prosecutor said that this looks like he had been planning this for a while. Do you know if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was aware of any of this prior to --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: At this point, I'm not aware of any law enforcement agency had him or the school on their radar for this particular incident at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For this particular incident, what about previous incidents?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Any incident involving him. He wasn't on any law enforcement radar that I'm aware of. Certainly not ours, we had no communication about him or about anything that was pending. And I haven't heard from any law enforcement agency that they did.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was the suspect a gamer? Did he have any video games?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Apparently, he was interested in games. I can't really comment on what those were at this point, that obviously is something that will be looked at as well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, is the belief that he took the gun illegally or was given the gun illegally?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: That's part of the investigation. In any event, he had the gun illegally, took the gun illegally into the school, and use the gun in an illegal and murderous fashion. So we've got a whole bunch of charges already regardless of how it came into possession.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any clarity can give on his familiarity with weapons? Perhaps gun range use, etcetera, in terms of his background and familiarity with guns?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I don't know much beyond what we saw on evidence in the school, and that will be part of the investigation obviously. With this particular weapon, it's our understanding was purchased four days ago. So this particular weapon, probably not a huge amount of familiarity with.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But over the years, other weapons?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: that's part of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What's your perspective on the terrorism part, the impacts to that message s (inaudible) --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I agree, 100%. You know, if you weren't hit by a bullet doesn't mean you weren't terrorized that day, and will have nightmares about the rest of your life. Whether you're a parent, a teacher, or a student in that class, you know, going through that building in the wee hours of this morning, looking at the disarray in the classrooms and the backpack strewn across the floor, that had to have been an absolutely terrorizing moment in anyone's life. I don't care if you're an adult or a child.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How much more time do you expect them processing at the school itself? Are you pretty much done?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: We're pretty much wrapped up with the physical processing of the school and not necessarily the evidence that we obtained from it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's a search warrant executed at the parent's house. So there are additional properties that you're looking at that require similar searches?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: We did see some property at the house and our detectives are going through it. So I think I've mentioned this before. We've got a lot of different bundles here that we're fortunate to have under our own roof. We have our own crime lab. So they're processing things as it relates to the scene and items that were seized there. We have computer crimes, looking at any kind of digital evidence, including his phone. We have detectives looking at any potential documents or other things that may contain information or things relative to, you know, some of his thoughts, maybe, maybe contained in some of that.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of those things are kind of spanned out to all of our different specialists. And then, they'll be brought together under the officer in charge of the case, and relay to the prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you give us any details on what was seized, the volume of material that seized?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I can't get into the details of what was seized. I can just tell you we have a voluminous amount of stuff and things to do, stuff to go through and things to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, I just have one more question, I believe you answered it previously so I apologize for that. Well, your investigators, were they have a follow up conversation with the school in regards to why you guys were not informed about that meeting or is --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: certainly our investigators will be in our -- in close contact with that school and we're in close contact with all schools in Oakland County. And we encourage all schools, public or private houses of worship, businesses, anyone that has any concerning behavior, they should report it. Allow law enforcement the opportunity to make the situation safe to say there's nothing to it, or we need to look deeper. That's what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 It's not a burden, so we encourage people, wherever they are, if they're concerned about something, let us know, or let your local police know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The term concerning behavior, does that constitute threats, any specific?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I don't want to get too awfully into what was concerning, because I didn't get into the actual content. But, you know, it's one of those things, if someone looks at it, and you go, oh, that doesn't seem right. Whether it's threatening or disturbing, or maybe they're worried about, you know, someone being bullied or suicidal.\par \par \uc2 We have obviously a huge problem in America is suicide, especially teens. If they see something that may cross into any of those realms, it needs to be shared. So we can figure it out, a, if it's a threat to anyone else or themselves and, b, where to get help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And, Sheriff, have they indicated why they didn't share the concern in your office?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: That's all part of our discussion with them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sheriff, did he know about the meeting with the school and his parents that morning?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Did who know?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This defendant, I'm sorry, (inaudible). Did he know that he was going to be meeting with school officials and parents/\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: No, he was brought into the meeting and his parents were called.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, OK. So I'm asking because did he ever leave the school after that meeting?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that was already in school -- so the gun was somewhere in the school.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Again, it's still moving. We believe it was. I can't definitively say 100% that it was but we believe it was. Because we began the process of tracking his movements and saw at what point and where he came out with a weapon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sheriff, do you think that some type of red flag law could help prevent this crime at all? Or I don't even know, something like that even exists for a minor could have been taken away from home or?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: You know, I don't necessarily think so. I think the most important thing is to share information. Because, I mean, for anything, whether what you're talking about or a different process, you have to share, you have to say, this seems concerning, can you look into it? And with or without that, we would and say, hey, what's going on, what are you seeing, what are you hearing, what have you seen, and try to determine where it's coming from and where it might lead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As I mentioned about the security, previously, (inaudible) of maybe that affiliated with the school or?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: After the meeting in the morning with the school, the parents, do the suspect go back to class and they released back to --\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: He was released back into the school, from the meeting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have any video of where he retrieved the weapon from, like a locker or somewhere?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Again, the first time we saw the weapon, the weapon actually in evidence was when he came out of the bathroom. So, you know, every other moment in time it wasn't observed. So whether it was concealed in his purse or in a backpack, or hidden somewhere in a restroom, right, that is all part of the investigation if we can determine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's been some discussion of metal detectors in schools and whether that would have prevented them from being able to smuggle weapons inside of the building. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: The vast majority of schools in America do not have weapons or metal detectors. And, you know, that's a determination for each district about what kind of environment they want to be operating in. But, you know, I know for a fact, one of the last debriefs I got on a school shooter, he came in through a backdoor, you know, somebody who was going out and he came in, walk in with a firearm and began shooting.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, there's tragically nothing foolproof in a world where you have people that want to hurt other people. But again, the best preventive path that we've seen, that has worked is to make sure everyone talks about something they've seen, heard or witnessed so it can get looked into, to put training and protocols in place. In fact, that situation that I just mentioned, where somebody came in a door, that was a non-traditional door, they had radios as a school system all around the building, and anyone in the school was empowered to put the school in the lockdown.\par \par \uc2 And so a groundskeeper, a janitor, I can't remember his actual position, saw the person with fire and go into the school, and called it out on the radio school went into lockdown. They still had, I believe one or two students killed but everybody else was in lockdown. And the police were able to get there before anymore perish. So processes, training,, communication are some of the best paths that we've seen in terms of success.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: sheriff, are you going to be releasing any video footage from inside the school?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: No, at this point, that would become a handoff to the prosecutor for evidence.\par \par \uc2 A recent update, the 14 year old with a gunshot wound to the hand and jaw has been discharged.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Any update on the young girl described yesterday on a ventilator after a chest wound surgery?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I think she's still --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She's off the ventilator now.\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: She's off the ventilator but she's still critical.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible) arraignment?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: Probably happening almost as we speak --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: (Inaudible) right now.\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: It's going to be a video arraignment. So the detectives driving in person to the district court where he's going to swear to the warrant, then they're going to connect via video and arraign the suspect to that court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Bouchard, can you relate the names of the folks that are still hospitalized and their conditions?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: No, we're not naming the victims that are hospitalized. I mean, even the ones that are deceased we're sad to hear that they've been negatively impacted, you know. We hope that it be uplifting and supportive rather than focusing attention that they don't want.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At any point in the video, did anybody try to disarm him or people just running from him?\par \par \uc2 BOUCHARD: I don't -- nothing I saw tells me that and I'm not sure that we have any information on that front that has come to me. So the part I saw there was no attempt. But obviously, when you've got somebody very close firing a weapon, that's not almost anybody's natural response to go towards it other than police and military. That's an act with extensive training. Anybody else? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, sheriff.\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Quite a bit of breaking news in that news conference, Michael Bouchard, the sheriff in Oxford County, Michigan. Probably the biggest piece of news from this news conference was that the parents of the suspected shooter were called to the school the morning of the shooting, and it was a second time they had been called in for a meeting about behavioral issues with their son.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff said that it was not directly related to a threat about a shooting, but that the parents were there and that he, the suspect, was brought into that meeting. And as far as the sheriff could tell, at this point, he never left the school again after that. He was sent back to the classroom. So that opens up a whole new sort of scenario and timeline for what happened.\par \par \uc2 He also talked about the charges, seven counts of assault murder, 12 counts of possession. He is going to be arraigned via video momentarily. So obviously, this is a tragic, tragic situation. You have the loss of four young people, four high school students, Justin Shilling, 17 years old, Hana St. Juliana, 14 years old, Madisyn Baldwin 17, and Tate Myre who, according to reports, died in the sheriff's vehicle on the way to the hospital. But one young person was released, so that's good news, and the young woman taken off a ventilator. And we hope that that will continue to be better news for her prognosis going forward.\par \par \uc2 What a horrific, terrible story. He will be charged as an adult in this case. So we continue to cover that for you throughout the day here on Fox.\par \par \uc2 So I also want to get in before we leave you today on "The Story," this truly riveting day at the Supreme Court. We listen to the arguments and one of the most important cases of our time since 1973 different questions about whether the court is the place where abortion law should be decided. We got a window into the thinking of the justices and the attorneys from both sides.\par \par \uc2 Here is some of the intense back and forth today, starting with the solicitor general for Mississippi, and then you'll hear for the attorney for the Jackson Women's Health, and that's the on the DOJ side, and then the justices. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT STEWARD, SOLICITOR GENERAL, MISSISSIPPI: Roe and Casey have failed, but the people, if given the chance, will succeed.\par \par \uc2 JULIA RIKELMAN\uc2 , CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: Eliminating or reducing the right to abortion will propel women backwards.\par \par \uc2 SONIA SOTOMAYOR, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: What has changed in science to show that the viability line is not a real line?\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: What constitutional right protects the right to abortion?\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: It's been 50 years of water under the bridge 50 years of decisions, saying that this is part of our law.\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: You say that the existing framework accommodates that your word both the interest of the pregnant woman and the interest of the fetus, you can't accommodate both interests. You have to pick.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's comment especially interesting today. We're going to continue on that story in a moment. But I want to take you back to the Michigan story. This is the arraignment of the 15-year-old suspect shooter. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 12:52 pm, Sheriff's Office deputies were dispatched to Oxford High School located at 745 North Oxford Township, County of Oakland State of Michigan for the reporter multiple gunshots being fired in around the school. As deputies were responding to scene they witnessed numerous students and staff fleeing from the area.\par \par \uc2 The first few deputies (inaudible) deployed to the area, high school, taking a 15-year-old student into custody later identified as Ethan Crumbley, 15-year-old, Oxford Township resident. (Inaudible) on the scene, the victims identified were a 16 year old male who was deceased, a 14-year- old female who is deceased, a 17-year-old female who was deceased, a 17- year-old male who later was deceased at the hospital, 14-year-old male gunshot wound, 17-year-old female gunshot wound, 15-year-old male gunshot wound, 17-year-old male gunshot wound, 47-year-old female gunshot wound, 14-year-old female gunshot wound, and a 17-year-old female gunshot wound.\par \par \uc2 As a result of this, Ethan Crumbley was lodged at Children Village on homicide and attempt homicide with the unduly referee approval. There was a weapon recovered from Ethan. It was a 9 millimeter SIG Sauer automatic handgun. The additional investigation revealed that school security video of the shooting incident perpetrated by Ethan, the school security video shows Eth