am, Singapore, Canada, the Netherlands, Iceland, and Guinea-Bissau. Most developed countries today allow elective abortions only during the first trimester, or up to about 12 weeks. And only 59 out of 190 nations allow elective abortions at all.\par \par \uc2 Today, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  reiterated this reality.\par \par \uc2 JOHN G. ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE OF THE UNITED STATES SUPREME COURT: When you get to the viability standard, we share that standard with the People's Republic of China and North Korea. And I don't think you have to be in favor of looking to international law to set our constitutional standards to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Republican senators call the U.S. a liberal outlier.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): It's not the company you want to be in when you're talking about China and North Korea. We can do better, we must do better.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: \uc2 Julie Rikelman\uc2 , a lawyer arguing against Mississippi's six-week ban before the Supreme Court today, said comparisons with other countries are misleading.\par \par \uc2 JULIE RIKELMAN\uc2 , SENIOR DIRECTOR, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2 : Some countries, Your Honor, have a nominal line of 12 weeks or 18 weeks, but they permit legal access to abortion after that point for broad social reasons: health reasons, socioeconomic reasons. So, their regimes really aren't comparable.\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN CROWN: We are the pro-life generation. We are the pro-life --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: But pro-life activists say Mississippi's law is actually more in line with the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BILLY, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, CHARLOTTE LOZIER INSTITUTE: 47 out of 50 countries in Europe limit abortion prior to 15 weeks. Over half of the European countries limit abortion prior to 12 weeks, and eight of them don't allow elective abortion at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): In today's case, the courts been focused on Mississippi's 15-week ban, specifically as the chief justice made abundantly clear. So, whether Roe v. Wade remains in place nationwide or not, may very well hinge on this standard. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was fascinated to see the perspective of other countries.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Put it all in contact.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Another prominent congressional Democrat has decided not to run for reelection. Also tonight, a government shutdown looms if there's not quick action in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is on all of that from Capitol Hill with an update. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 We've seen fiscal cliffs before, but lawmakers appear to be getting dangerously close to a shutdown this time.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Bret. The House doesn't even have a bill yet. There is no agreement on how long a short- term bill should last. But the problem is the Senate. Some conservatives want to slow down the bill over vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE, (R-UT): Our military service members deserve the right to make this medical decision for themselves without the threat of losing the ability to care for themselves and provide for their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The House Freedom Caucus asked Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell to, block, quote, timely passage of the spending bill unless it defunds vaccine mandates. But most GOP members disagree with Lee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): No. No. There's no appetite, nor should there be on either side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McConnell doubts the government will close, but Democrats are preemptively blaming the GOP if the government does shut down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: Our Republican colleagues, meanwhile, can either work with us to move the process quickly through the chamber, or they can engage in obstructive tactics that will make a government shutdown almost a certainty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The GOP could drag out passage of the bill in the Senate for days even if most senators want to pass the bill before the Friday deadline. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, what about this latest Democrat to announce retirement. This is not some back bencher.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right. \uc2 Peter DeFazio\uc2  of Oregon will not seek a new term. He chairs the Transportation Committee. This is a blow to Democrats. DeFazio is the 19th Democrat to retire. The chairman of the Budget Committee, \uc2 John Yarmuth\uc2  also announced recently that he would retire. The house GOP Campaign Committee says, quote, committee chairs don't retire unless they know their majority is gone. And Bret, when Democrats lost the House in 2010, 25 of their members retired. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I didn't tell you I was going to do this, but government shutdown casino, you have $100 on chips. You've got to put it on either it shuts down or doesn't. What do you got?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Probably 55 to 60 percent because we don't have a bill. And if they stretch this out through the Senate, they would have to run the traps. It probably could take until next Tuesday or Wednesday to pass the bill if the opponents don't relent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sixty bucks on shutdown. All right, Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Atlanta have selected a new mayor. Democrat Andre Dickens easily defeated City Council President Felicia Moore with 64 percent of the vote. The campaign was dominated by concern over rising violent crime in the city of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts Republican Governor \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  says he will not run for a third term. That's 